inl note_of_bend_backend_7 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-10-16T10:03Z", "main-thread", "06:03 main: pack-1r installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-053218, 6660 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-050109, Swift 050650, Bend 051447 against the pack-1q bless: 0 changes on listed rows). Union matches over several scrutinees (union structural equality) as nested matches, mismatched tags to the fallback; Bend 364 -> 388 (wgsl_nullary_union, wgsl_payload_union + 22 rows that already agreed). Open: typecheck_long_let_body disagrees (exit 1, unlisted).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_8 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-10-16T10:08Z", "main-thread", "06:08 main: typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 chained inl ~tN lets, C exit 236) exits 1 on Bend after 5.3 s (probe single-flight-20261016-051447): looks like a Bend toolchain depth limit on the generated let chain rather than wrong arithmetic; not listed. Next: read the Bend stderr for that row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_9 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-10-16T10:30Z", "main-thread", "06:30 main: typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 chained lets) Bend exit 1 is the Bun/JSC default stack limit (bend2: the machine stack overflowed), not codegen. bun.exe reserves 18 MB; BUN_JSC_maxPerThreadStackUsage=16777216 gives exit 236 = C. Harness fix staged as tmp/pack-s/bend-stack.ps1 for pack-1s, then list the row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_10 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 10u32, "2026-10-16T11:39Z", "main-thread", "07:39 main: coverage census from probe single-flight-20261016-051447, rows where C runs but Bend does not compile: 101. Macros 37 (11 are samples/core/sm.spi byte_to_i32's Bend macro arm; value macros staged as tmp/pack-u/bend-macros.ps1, unit macros stay unsupported), f64 19, i64 13, u64 8, mutable-layout defaults 7, BackendSwitch without a Bend key 13. bend2's numeric primitives are U32, F32 and Nat only (comp.ts ops table): i64/u64 could be emulated on Nat mod 2^64 or U32 pairs (Lua spiral_w64 precedent); f64 has no primitive.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_11 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 11u32, "2026-10-16T11:57Z", "main-thread", "07:57 main: pack-1s installed both modes (07:23/07:31) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-073131, 6,685 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1r bless 053218 overridden by probes 062511 (Kotlin), 061551 (Swift), 064814 (Python); the one extra row, Bend typecheck_long_let_body, exits 236 like C. ts_int_wrap Python Known -> agree.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_12 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 12u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_13 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 13u32, "2026-10-16T18:05Z", "main-thread", "14:05 main: next grain recorded, not started: i64/u64 (22 samples, 3 C-only) need a pair representation; bend2 has U32/F32/Nat only and Nat is Peano (BendTT 1n+add), so pairs of U32 like Lua's spiral_w64: add/sub with carry, 64-bit mul via 16-bit limbs, long division, signed compare, shifts, decimal show, conversions. f64 (19 samples) has no primitive at all.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_14 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 14u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: Bend 2 u64 prototype (U32 & U32 pairs: add with carry, 32x32 to 64 multiply via 16-bit limbs) passes 4 BigInteger checks (job tmp/night/bend64). Syntax facts: pairs pass linearly, destructure with (+h,+l), match only on variables, shift counts are Nat.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_15 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 15u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread lab cleanup: the u64-as-U32-pairs prototype (w64_add with carry, w64_mul32 via 16-bit limbs, w64_mul, 4 self-checks exiting 0) moved from lab/bend-backend/main.bend to apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend. Its final home is the compiler's bendPrelude (emitted helpers, the way luaW64Helpers serves Lua) when the i64/u64 grain is done; that grain then deletes the lane file.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_16 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 16u32, "2026-10-19T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:19 lap 1 grain: today's pack 5 review (single-flight-20261019-072348): Bend 400 rows agree with the C oracle plus 1 Known (bounds), 0 disagree; the 10-14 next step (add the 20 new agreeing rows to the Bend list) is long done. Left: i64/u64 (the w64 U32-pair prototype in tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend moves into the bendPrelude) and floats (Bend 2 has F32 only, no formatter) Next: list the matrix samples whose Bend row is missing only for i64/u64 so the w64 grain has a target count")
inl note_of_bend_backend_17 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 17u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: target count for the w64 grain: of Bend's 130 missing C samples only 2 are blocked by i64/u64 alone (other tags: string 68, macro 45, array 41, f64 31, union 28), so i64 support unlocks rows mainly together with strings and f64, not by itself Next: count Bend-missing samples whose only blockers are i64 and string (Bend 2 strings exist), to see if strings are the better next grain")
inl note_of_bend_backend_18 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 18u32, "2026-10-19T18:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:14 lap 4 grain: blocker combinations for the 130 Bend-missing C samples (accept 125623): none 17, string+macro 15, string 15, f64 11, i64+string 6; of the 17 untagged ones 12 are C compile-error negatives (no Bend value) and only 5 run: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred (exit 0), package_default_int_i64 (exit 7), zig_exit_wide (exit 305, exit-width semantics), mega_omniledger runtime_native; the string-only 15 are mostly negatives too, so strings are not the cheap win either Next: probe the two frontier_hopacsota samples on Bend (a -Probe Bend row restricted to them), then add them to the Bend list if they agree")
inl note_of_bend_backend_19 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 19u32, "2026-10-19T18:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:47 lap 5 grain: probe of the untagged Bend-missing samples on the installed compiler: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending and frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred compile and run on bend2 with exit 0 like C; package_default_int_i64 needs i64 (the w64 grain), zig_exit_wide exits 49 vs C 305 (bun truncates to 8 bits, the same exit-width class as the Linux F# CI row); staged via the generic lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/add-to-list.py Bend <samples> (dry run clean) Next: apply with the pack after 8b, review, accept")
inl note_of_bend_backend_20 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 20u32, "2026-10-19T21:20Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:20 main: pack 9 ACCEPTED 17:20 (single-flight-20261019-161549: 7,057 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 155055); Bend gained frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending and frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred, both agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_21 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 21u32, "2026-10-20T03:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:41 main: estimate corrected for WSJF: 97 percent overstated the rest; what remains is i64/u64 (move the w64 U32-pair prototype from tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend into the bend prelude, unlocking about 21 rows) and f64 (Bend 2 has F32 only: emulate or keep Known); progress 85, estimate 2,400 min total, so about 6 h remain.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_22 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 22u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "main", "18:46 w64 prelude extended and proven on Bend 2 (lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64.bend = the tmp/lanes prototype's add/mul32/mul/eq + new w64-ext): w64_not, w64_neg (two's complement), w64_sub, w64_ult and w64_slt (sign bit flipped with xor 0x80000000), w64_shl, w64_lshr and w64_ashr for n in 0..63 (n = 0, below 32 and 32+ branches), w64_or. 10 new checks (neg 1, sub across the word boundary, unsigned and signed compares both ways, shl by 32 and across the carry, lshr across words, ashr of the sign bit by 63 and of a positive value) all pass: exit 0 in about 3 s. Bend 2 lesson: match may only scrutinize a parameter, so every condition goes through a w64_pick or w64_pick_bool helper. Still missing before the codegen switch: div/rem (signed and unsigned, toward zero), conversions to and from i32/u32/f32, and printing (decimal of a U32 pair). Next: those, then codegenBend emits i64/u64 as U32 & U32 pairs plus this prelude when a program uses a 64-bit op (the Lua spiral_w64 pattern), unlocking about 21 rows.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_23 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 23u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "main", "18:50 w64 division proven on Bend 2 (lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-div.bend.txt, tested by w64-div-test.bend): w64_udivmod is restoring shift-and-subtract over 64 bits in ONE self-recursive def (w64_udiv_rec) with a Nat fuel counter (64n; Bend 2's termination checker needs a structurally shrinking argument and rejects a U32 counter and mutual recursion), plus w64_udiv/urem and signed w64_sdiv/srem (toward zero, like C: sign of the quotient = xor of the operand signs, remainder takes the dividend's sign). 8 checks pass in 1.3 s: 100/7 = 14 r 2; 2^32/3 = 1431655765 r 1; -7/2 = -3; -7%2 = -1; 7/-2 = -3; (2^64-1)/1. Bend 2 rules learned (for the codegen): match and destructuring only on parameters; + (reusable) parameters must be Data, so pairs are passed as their U32 halves; arguments are eager, so recursion branches only through a match on a parameter; scalar bindings (+x = expr) are fine. Still missing: conversions (i32/u32/f32 <-> pair) and printing; then the codegenBend switch.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_24 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 24u32, "2026-10-21T22:54Z", "main", "18:54 w64 prelude complete in Bend 2 and proven: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-prelude.bend.txt (one file for codegenBend to embed when a program uses a 64-bit op, like Lua's spiral_w64). Contents: add, mul32, mul, eq, not, neg, sub, ult/slt, shl/lshr/ashr (0..63), or/and, udivmod/udiv/urem/sdiv/srem (toward zero), of_u32/of_i32 (zero and sign extension), show_u/show_s (decimal; Nat-fueled single recursion with a stop flag). Tests: 4 prototype checks + 10 (w64.bend) + 8 division (w64-div-test.bend) + 7 conversion/printing (w64-show-test.bend: 0, 2^32, 2^64-1, -5, INT64_MIN, i32 sign extension, u32 zero extension), all exit 0. Remaining for the about 21 rows: codegenBend maps Int64T/UInt64T to U32 & U32 pairs (type at 166769, zero value at 166913, literals as (hi, lo)) and routes TyOp arithmetic, compare, shift, conv and show for 64-bit operands to the w64_* calls, and float conversions (F32 only on Bend 2; f64 stays Known). That is the next compile-side slice.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_25 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 25u32, "2026-10-21T22:59Z", "main", "18:59 codegen slice staged: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-codegen.ps1 (9 anchors, dry run matches each once; shared codegenBend section, so pair-drift is clean). Int64T/UInt64T -> (U32 & U32) (high, low); zero ({0 : U32}, {0 : U32}); LitInt64/LitUInt64 split into words; TyConv 64<->64 identity, 64<-i32/i8/i16 w64_of_i32, 64<-u32/u8/u16/char w64_of_u32, 32<-64 w64_lo; Printf through w64_show_s/u; TyOp Add/Sub/Mult/Div/Mod/LT/GT/LTE/GTE/EQ/NEQ/And/Or/Xor/Complement/Shl/Shr/Neg go to the w64_* calls (signed by Int64T); the 43-def prelude (lab w64-prelude.bend.txt, whole-file Bend typecheck plus 32 checks green) is embedded as bendW64Prelude and appended only when the emitted defs call w64_, like spiral_narrow_i8. Not covered: 64-bit float conversions (F32 only on Bend 2) and 64-bit array lengths, which stay unsupported errors. Gate in PACK-AFTER-NIGHT: add the i64/u64 samples to the matrix Bend list (about 21 rows per the 10-19 23:41 note), each must agree with C, and every other row must be unchanged (programs without 64-bit ops emit byte-identical Bend).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_26 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 26u32, "2026-10-21T23:00Z", "main", "19:00 matrix side staged: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-matrix.ps1 adds the 15 i64/u64 samples with a C output that the Bend list lacks (array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, closure_pair_i64, heap_i64_real, heap_i64_text, heap_u64_text, i64_scalar, lua_wide_integers, mut_i64_text, native_closure_union_rec, portable_console_lines, printf_portable, ts_int_wrap, union_i64_text). Excluded by kind: the 3 abi_external_*i64 C-shim samples and rust_static_closure_chain. Proven on a copy: Bend list 405 -> 420, no other key changed. In the pack, any of the 15 that still hits an unsupported shape (heap_i64_real needs float conversion, for example) goes to Known with its reason, not silently dropped.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_27 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 27u32, "2026-10-21T23:11Z", "main", "19:11 dropped samples/heap_i64_real from w64-matrix.ps1: it pairs an i64 with an f64 (1.5f64), and Bend 2 has F32 only, so it would fail on f64, not on the w64 path; it stays off the Bend list with that reason. 14 candidates remain (printf_portable and portable_console_lines also print an i64 via %d and console.write_line, which the patch routes to w64_show_s).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_28 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 28u32, "2026-10-22T18:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:09 main: lab drained. w64-codegen.ps1 and w64-matrix.ps1 landed with PACK-AFTER-NIGHT: bendW64Prelude is in the core, and every definition line of the 5 lab prelude parts is found there (w64-ext's 13 missing lines are only its inline check harness). In the 07:53 review run, 11 of the 14 new Bend rows agree with C, and the other 3 are Known with reasons: lua_wide_integers (f64, Bend 2 has F32 only), portable_console_lines (unit macros), union_i64_text (bend2 SOME PROOFS FAIL: expected Data, observed Type). The 32 prelude checks (w64-*-test.bend) are deleted, since the matrix rows run the same helpers against C. Still open for Done: unit macros, and the union_i64_text codegen gap (a union case carrying i64 text, where the payload type is emitted where Bend expects data).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_29 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 29u32, "2026-10-22T18:10Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:10 main: union_i64_text hypothesis, read from samples/union_i64_text/main.bend (no run yet): the union is emitted as type U0 is Data: U0_Both{f0: (U32 & U32), f1: String}. Every other w64 use is a local or a parameter (heap_i64_text, mut_i64_text and closure_pair_i64 agree), so the one new shape is a sigma pair type as a field of a Data constructor, which matches bend2's \"expected Data, observed Type\". Test after the block, about 5 s of bend2: a 6-line file with type T is Data: T_A{f0: (U32 & U32)}, then the same file with the field split as T_A{f0_hi: U32, f0_lo: U32}. Rule: if the first fails and the second passes, codegenBend flattens each 64-bit union field into _hi/_lo U32 fields, packing at construction (via a let destructure) and repacking on unbox, and union_i64_text leaves Known. If both pass, the cause is elsewhere: bisect the file by deleting defs.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_30 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 30u32, "2026-10-22T19:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:06 main: union_i64_text hypothesis CONFIRMED with bend2, about 5 s each (scratch bend-sigma-field): type T is Data: T_A{f0: (U32 & U32), f1: String} fails exactly as the sample does (expected Data, observed Type); with the field split into f0_hi/f0_lo U32 it typechecks; is Type passes the declaration but IO.pure then rejects the value. The flattened shape the codegen will emit (w64_hi/w64_lo at the box, +v_hi, +v_lo binders, the pair rebuilt as the arm's first do statement) runs to exit 42 = 1 + 41 as predicted. Patch lab/BEND-BACKEND/union-w64.ps1 (6 anchors: declaration, unbox binders and rebuild, ruled-out arity, box, union default value, prelude w64_hi/w64_lo) rides PACK-NEXT-3, rule (f) in PACK-NEXT-2 note 9. Unit macros remain the other open row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_31 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 31u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:48 main: Bend prelude on demand, staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\bend-prelude-on-demand.ps1, 3 anchors on the next3 core). Every main.bend started with the same 183-line bendPrelude (33 defs, 12 of them @unsafe), 26.7% of all Bend output, and bend2 typechecks each def on every row. bendUsedPrelude keeps only the defs the program reaches, transitively; a decorator line stays attached to its def; the w64 and narrowing helpers already were conditional. A Python mirror (scratch bend_prelude_mirror.py) over the 417 samples: 286,189 -> 234,399 lines (18.1% fewer); most programs drop 20-26 of the 33 defs (the f32 conversions in 416 files, i32 show in 415, string slicing in 409). The pack review checks mechanically that each changed main.bend equals the old one minus prelude defs, and native results must match next3 row for row. The Bend median run is 810 ms, so the typecheck saving is measured there: native_ms of Bend rows next4 vs next3.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_32 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 32u32, "2026-10-25T13:27Z", "main", "10-25 09:27 main groom: the last noted step SHIPPED. bendUsedPrelude (prelude on demand, from PACK-NEXT-4) is in the installed tree core and has passed every accepted review since (latest 5j accept, DISAGREE 0). The item stays open for the rest of its Feature scope (the not-supported list in tmp/AGENTS.md: unit macros and the rest).")
inl compiler_upstream_onion () = Task ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", "Move the compiler into the upstream fork's repo with our changes as onion layers over mrakgr's files (PR-ready), one file per backend, splitter adapted for parallel builds; 2026-10-08 first upstream-ready patch: CodegenC (user globals reach C output; (void)x in empty decref bodies, 211 -Wunused-parameter -> 0 apart from one stale output), made line-local so scripts/upstream.py exports it cleanly (4 hunks): python scripts/upstream.py export deps/The-Spiral-Language HEAD <out> --since HEAD writes The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs; opening the PR on the fork is the user's call; 18:2x readable-names + C patch material now durable at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\upstream-pr\\names-and-c (CodegenC, CodegenFsharp, CodegenPython, CodegenUtils); both CodegenPython conflicts resolved by intent (fork already carries the string option source name in join_point_method); upstream.py export fix: an addition after the last line of a section goes to the end of the target file (it was spliced between codegen_env and its members); generatedMethodName uses System.Char (CodegenUtils opens only System.Text); verified by a differential build of the fork (unpatched vs patched, external includes restored from history): 0 patch-only errors, with a canary type error proving fsc reaches the codegen files past the unrelated lib/fsharp/Common.fs errors; 21:1x CodegenC now emits the backend standard includes before user globals (Seq.append globals env.globals |> Seq.distinct): user globals using bool/size_t no longer precede <stdbool.h>; dev review: only the include order changed in the 2 samples with C globals (Lean exit=-1 rows were load kills, parity alone); deployed dev32 both cores; patch material CodegenC.fs refreshed (export --since 98492a63, since the 18:34 commit moved HEAD past the names work); 19:4x two fresh candidate exports exist for the next curation pass, both exit 0 against the fork clone at polyglot/deps/The-Spiral-Language: names-and-c-194526 (export --since HEAD, so only tonight's uncommitted edits, 45634 bytes, carries the closure void-cast fix at lines 616-617 but not the committed globals fix) and names-and-c-full-194526 (export --since 98492a63, the documented PR base, 46185 bytes, carries all three: the void-cast fix, the Seq.append globals env.globals fix from C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED and generatedClosureName from the readable-names work); both report conflicts the fork has since moved into (14 and 20 hunks, mostly CodegenCpp plus 2-3 CodegenC hunks in the closure and string-compare areas), so the curated 10-08 names-and-c material stays canonical and opening the PR remains the user's call; 2026-10-12 split: non-defacing changes go upstream as a PR series (test-block parser skip, canonical package dirs, DeadPureLetElision, hermetic BackendSwitch, the new backends, bug fixes), each with its sample rows; edition-3 syntax stays fork-only (SPIRAL3-EDITIONS); 08:13 input from the water-oil re-shape probe: to make shared Graph/ServerUtils/WDiff upstream-shaped, hopac's supervisor must stop requiring interned path ids in shared code (42 compile errors when ServerUtils takes single-flight's text); plan: hopac keeps ids in its own layer and converts at the boundary of the shared functions; 2026-10-13 22:59 the spiral-split gear splitter (scripts/gear-dev.ps1, bench-split.ps1, build-splitter.ps1, tmp/splitter, lanes/splitter) was removed at the user's request: idle since early October (234 runs Sep 26-Oct 1, then 21), built for gpt5parallel's 159k lines; use build.ps1 -Configuration Debug -CoreSource <copy> for candidates; 2026-10-13 23:41 user decision: after hopacsota promotion (HOPACSOTA-P6); the splitter it mentions was deleted 2026-10-13", Epic, P1, D8, 10u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P6,SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL,UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA,CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "compiler,upstream,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_upstream_onion () = Planning ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", Minutes 3000u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:35 touch: upstream.py status vs the fork: 37,259 differing lines, 31,923 of them in Utils (shared infra), then CodegenCpp 404/253, CodegenC 400/128, SignalRSupervisor 357/248, BlockParsing 315/164; Parser/LineParsers/BlockSplitting near zero")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: core now 185,945 lines with 14 #if pairs (was 186,572 / 16 before the 10-15 unifications). Each pair unified removes a section the upstream sync would otherwise need twice. Next grain: rerun upstream.py status to see whether Utils is still 31.9k of the 37.3k differing lines.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: found and fixed a tool bug. upstream.py still looked for '#if SPIRAL_CORE_HOPAC', but the core was renamed to SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL (spiral d94dc380, 10-13). Since then sections() saw no pairs and read gpt5parallel's side as single-flight text, so status/import/export were wrong. The 10-14 status (37,259 lines, Utils 31,923) was that artefact. Fixed the marker (scripts/upstream.py lines 13, 68; LF). Real status against spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language: 5,543 differing lines; largest CodegenCpp 404+253, CodegenC 400+128, SignalRSupervisor 355+248, BlockParsing 370+171, Prepass 226+181; Utils only 19+17. The onion is ~7x smaller than recorded.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night drift (upstream.py status, 27 mapped sections): 6,014 differing lines (ours 4,089, upstream-only 1,925); 5,543 on 10-16.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:44Z", "main-thread", "15:44 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: migrating the compiler from spiral/apps/compiler into the upstream fork stays here as the backlog home. Candidate split PRs named by the user: the sorted-names indexing change (the user's own, separate PR), the de-duplication of generated names that came from the single-file build (first done in the old dib notebooks, kept when hopac/gpt5parallel dropped the dib), union/nominal private constructors (SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS, Done since pack-1e), and Export (ours: no Export op exists upstream; history is squashed so the author is unknown). StringSlice is upstream's own op (BlockParsing.fs, CodegenC.fs), not ours. For hopacsota the end state is a neat structure in the fork, ready for review, not commit; the split into PR branches is decided later. Pause before any repeated destructive git operations by the agent until that flow is stable.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 6u32, "2026-10-18T05:47Z", "main-thread", "01:47 main-thread, from the user's forked study session (software history of the last 50 years): do not move the whole spiral repo into the fork. Move the language (apps/compiler: both cores, editions, formatter, harness, samples, oracle tables in matrix.psd1; and lib/spiral as the standard library) into i574n/The-Spiral-Language, keeping our changes as layers over mrakgr's files for PR-ability; keep the ecosystem in spiral (cells, the spiral CLI, wasm, lisp, zed, CI glue for dice/alphabet/polyglot), the way tibia and eoie already live apart. Rationale: the active fork that keeps history becomes upstream (EGCS to GCC, Neovim, LibreOffice, MariaDB; history-dropping forks like XEmacs drifted for good), and language repos hold language plus stdlib only (CPython, Go, rustc with cargo outside). Fork last merged mrakgr/master 2026-10-04. Timing: not now (history rewrite; destructive git paused until hopacsota stabilizes). Safe first step: a one-way sync keeping the fork's compiler source identical to apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs before anything moves.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:19 lap 1 grain: the bite-sized upstream post series is this epic's first layer in practice: UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (base branch host_cpp_and_cuda_backend + corebase as core + 5 proven fixes, make-post.ps1, upstream.py export --since/--until) then UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL; each post is one PR-shaped onion layer over mrakgr's files Next: record the post series as deps once post 1 lands in the fork (user's commit)")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:21 lap 2 grain: the core switch (CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE) lands today 11:45-13:30 with eoie, tibia and x lanes aligned (dry runs: eoie 0 source edits, tibia 44, x 0), which makes the fork's core = post 1's corebase-as-core layer in the tree; recorded as a dep Next: -")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 9u32, "2026-10-19T18:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:41 lap 4 grain: drift measured with tmp/scripts/upstream.py status against the fork clone (deps/The-Spiral-Language HEAD, the core v1 commit not yet committed there): 6,377 differing lines over the upstream-mapped sections; largest: Infer 422+238, CodegenC 413+140, CodegenCpp 404+253, BlockParsing 385+173, SignalRSupervisor 356+248, CodegenUtils 354+26, WDiff 341+90, PartEval 334+85, Prepass 294+199; smallest real layers: RefCounting 5+6, BlockSplitting 6+7, VSCTypes 7+0, ParserCombinators 0 (identical); the 30 new backends live in our own sections and are outside this count Next: first onion layer candidates are the near-zero sections (RefCounting, BlockSplitting, VSCTypes): export each as its own layer with upstream.py export --since, and check the fork file round-trips")
inl hopac_speed () = Task ("HOPAC-SPEED", "Measure hopac vs single-flight (real-work rows 3-22x slower, fresh process per job), split startup from evaluation, profile and iterate to single-flight speed; stats 2026-10-08 09:5x (probe.ps1, fresh process per compile, F# backend, mean of runs 2-3, net = total - frontier_hello baseline): startup sf 1.0s / hopac 1.45s; net eval brzozowski 0.65s / 0.8s (1.2x), zeta 0.95 / 1.4 (1.5x), omniledger 2.65 / 5.0 (1.9x), lean_cic 1.8 / 5.5 (3.1x), spiral_proves 1.5 / 4.65 (3.1x); the suite-level 126x median compares warm single-flight workers with fresh hopac processes and overstates the gap; next: profile lean_cic; profile lean_cic hopac (probe -Profile 12, SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=1), innermost compiler frames: term 36%, installBridge 7.4% (thread-local tail-result bridge save/restore per declared JP step), ty_core_impl 6.8%, buildFreshApplyState 3.2%, DiagSidecar.emitReentryKeySample 2.6% (runs even when quiet: ConditionalWeakTable + 2 AddOrUpdate per re-entry; counts may feed hot-key fallback, check before gating), DiagJson runner/loop/emitRecord 5.5% (background thread, CPU not wall), EvalWorklist replay tick 2.3%; candidates: gate sidecar sampling when quiet if counts are diagnostic-only, make the bridge scope a struct/stack instead of ThreadLocal round trips; each change needs an A/B probe on lean_cic/spiral_proves; 11:3x A/B (tmp/hopac-speed/ab-threadlocal.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, runs 2-3): the 4 hot-path JP slots AsyncLocal -> ThreadLocal gave identical outputs on 4 samples but lean_cic 6.15/6.50 -> 6.15/6.20 s, spiral_proves 6.10/6.15 -> 5.95/6.10 s, brzozowski and hello within noise (A alone varies 5% between rounds): no proven gain, reverted; the installBridge 7.4% is not the AsyncLocal write cost; next: allocation profile (gc-verbose) of term on lean_cic; 11:4x runtime knobs on the unchanged binary (ab-runtime*.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, baseline noise about 0.3 s): the shipped config (server GC, concurrent, TieredPGO off) is already best: gen0 64 MB +10%, gen0 8 MB within noise, TieredCompilation off +15%, workstation GC +20-25% on lean_cic/spiral_proves (the hopac core allocates from several threads); GC tuning is ruled out; remaining levers: startup JIT (ReadyToRun, hello is 1.6 s of mostly startup) and algorithmic work in term; 11:4x gen0: test.ps1 sets DOTNET_GCgen0size=256 MB for every run; fresh-process probes (ab-gen0.ps1) show it 4-13% slower than the runtime default on lean_cic/spiral_proves in BOTH cores; but an A-B-A single-flight suite (aba-gen0-suite.ps1, warm workers, 236 C rows) shows no difference (summed 62.5 / 67.6 default / 67.4 s); test.ps1 left unchanged: switching it needs an A-B-A hopac -Suite all (fresh process per job) showing the gain; ReadyToRun is already applied by build.ps1 since 2026-10-05; 12:3x hopac harness default now warm-recycle with deferral (HOPAC-WARM-SERVER): suite 2.35x faster at identical rows; 2026-10-12: hermetic BackendSwitch validation made the hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validating every arm, per-build type cache); candidate: type-check other arms without evaluating their join points; ladders 2026-10-12: hopac 67 min vs single-flight 27-35 min on -Suite all -Native; user decision 2026-10-12 19:05: hopac remains the intended default once it matches single-flight (raised to P0 with HOPAC-WATER-OIL); user decision 2026-10-12 19:18: water-oil first, then speed: every speed fix after unification is written once; 06:22 suite wall times tonight (same machine, varying load from parallel lanes): single-flight 19-23 min per -Suite all -Native; hopac 64, 41, 47, 48 and 29 min (pack-0005, least contended: hopac 29 vs single-flight 23); user decision 2026-10-13 07:08: hopac becomes the default core when its -Suite all -Native wall time is within 5% of single-flight on an idle machine with identical results (tonight's least-contended run: hopac 29 min vs single-flight 23 min); 2026-10-13 16:07 main, early estimate from today's suites (paired ok rows, compile_ms; the two runs had different machine load, so an idle measurement follows tonight): contracts hopac/sf sum ratio 15.2 (median 8.5, p90 50.6), examples 5.1 (median 7.1, p90 19.3), frontier 2.4, mega 1.8; worst samples are the mega roots on native backends (runtime_bench sf 7.8 s vs hopac 249 s, indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 8.6 vs 173 s); the user's switch rule (hopac default at equal suite time, within 5% idle) is far from met: hopac stays the candidate lane; 2026-10-13 18:36 main, interleaved A/B measurement (hopac_speed_ab.ps1 in main's job tmp: per sample, single-flight and hopac Release alternate in fresh --batch processes, 2 rounds, best of each, C backend; background load hits both modes alike): 14 frontier+mega samples sum sf 56.3 s vs hopac 149.0 s = 2.65x, median 1.83x; fixed startup overhead ~1.4 s (frontier_hello 2.5 vs 3.9 s); the gap grows with evaluation size: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench 5.3 vs 36.6 s (6.9x), mega_zeta 4.8x, omniledger 3.4x; next target: profile runtime_bench on hopac (probe.ps1 -Profile) for the evaluation hot path, and the startup cost separately; switch rule (equal suite time within 5%) far from met; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, sampled profile (probe.ps1 runtime_bench -Mode hopac -Backend C -Profile 30; logs in spiral-bin/probes/mega_brzozowski_derivatives__runtime_bench): innermost compiler frames ~37% evaluation (term 12.0, body.Invoke 23.1, peval 1.7) vs ~30% join-point coordination bookkeeping (jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 9.1, jpTryReserveDeclaredBodyLane 4.3, jpNoteDeclaredResumeEvidence 3.2, jpSetCurrentDeclaredSafeContinuationAtDepth 3.1, jpMaybePruneDeclaredResumeSnapshots 3.1, jpPersistDeclaredBodyCheckpoint 2.4, observer lane 1.5, continuation ordinal 1.3, retire snapshot 1.2) and ~8% diagnostics/sampling (DiagJson runner+loop 6.2, JpNativeThreadCpuClock 1.9; probe.ps1 sets SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET: yes); first target: the declared-body resume-evidence/checkpoint machinery (race-34 replay store) on the hot path - make it lazy or batch it, measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, caveat on the profile above: the shares are thread-stack samples, not CPU time; DiagJson.runner/loop is the progress HUD ticker, which in quiet mode only sleeps in 25 ms slices, so its 6.2% is a sleeping thread; re-run the breakdown on the evaluator thread only (python scripts/profile-thread.py <run.speedscope.json> [thread]) before optimizing the join-point bookkeeping; 2026-10-13 18:39 main, evaluator-thread CPU profile (profile-thread.py run1.speedscope.json, busiest thread 29.1 s busy, inclusive): jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 22.4-23.3%, buildFreshApplyState 17.8%, jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation 7.6% (the rest is evaluation: term/apply/if); ~half of hopac's evaluator CPU is per-step replay/resume bookkeeping single-flight does not have; next: make jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation and the let-continuation store lazy (record only when a suspension can actually resume) and reuse apply state instead of buildFreshApplyState per apply, then re-measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x, runtime_bench 6.9x); 2026-10-13 20:02 main, next-session plan from reading jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation (spiral_compiler.fs ~145856, 23% of hopac evaluator CPU, 2 callers): per evaluator step it interns shapeText (OperationalReasonIdOps.create, a string hash per step) although only 1 in 1024 steps uses the counted shape, does a ConcurrentDictionary GetOrAdd for the job profile, takes a lock to write five last-seen fields, and polls DiagJson.terminalFailureExitRequestedNow; behaviour-neutral fixes: keep the raw shape text and intern only on sampled/read paths, cache the job profile in the owner's AsyncLocal slot instead of GetOrAdd per step, replace the per-step lock with owner-local fields published at sample points; then buildFreshApplyState (17.8%) and jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation (7.6%); measure each with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x overall, runtime_bench 6.9x) and keep hopac's full suite at 0 behaviour differences; 2026-10-13 22:47 naming: the core this item calls hopac is now gpt5parallel (user decision; SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL, -Mode gpt5parallel, lanes/gpt5parallel); the id keeps HOPAC until eoie can rename ids (EOIE-AGILE-RENAME-ID); the planned rewrite is HOPACSOTA; scope now: the measured baseline (2.65x overall, 6.9x runtime_bench, ~half of evaluator CPU in replay bookkeeping) is hopacsota's target to beat, rather than a patch list for gpt5parallel; closed 2026-10-13 23:34 as superseded by hopacsota (user decision): goal inherited by HOPACSOTA-P3 (speed) and Phase 1/2 speed gates", Epic, P0, D7, 100u32, Done, "HOPAC-WATER-OIL", "compiler,hopac,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopac_speed () = Planning ("HOPAC-SPEED", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl portable_primitives () = Task ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", "Replace C-only macros with portable ops: StringConcat, Floor/Ceil/Round/Atan2, string search/case, a char model, Zig/Lean/Bend arms in lib backend switches; 2026-10-08 13:1x lib/spiral math (edited in math.livemd via mix spiral.edit, math.spi re-exported and compared): floor/ceil/atan2/round gain C (__builtin_* with _Generic for f32/f64), Cpp (<cmath>), Delphi (Int/Frac/Ord, ArcTan2) and TypeScript arms; Lua and Gleam round fixed (gave 2 for 2.7 and 4 for 3.2; now the shared round_half_to_even); lib backend.spi dispatches C and Delphi (before: no lib function could reach a C arm); new sample portable_math_rounding: 10 backends, 9 native runs exit 0, all agree with the C oracle; 13:0x accepted the lib-using samples (30 rows; review and accept runs identical): portable_math_rounding new on 10 backends; format_any x2 C/Delphi now fail at the C/Pascal compiler with backend_switch_has_no_<backend>_arm_in_lib_spiral (BackendSwitch evaluates every arm, so a lib fallback cannot raise a Spiral error for one backend only, same as the Zig/Lean fallbacks); 7 F#/Rust/Zig rows changed only in variable numbering (proved by normalizing v<n>); next: console write_line/write for C and Delphi via the portable !!!!Printf with a typecase, which needs one agreed bool/float text across backends (F# True vs Rust true); 17:1x lib console (console.livemd via spiral.edit, console.spi re-exported): write_line/write gain C arms (printf via real typecase helpers write_line_c/write_c: string %s, i32, i64, u32, u64; floats and bools stay out: no shortest round-trip float text in C, F#/Python print True) and Delphi arms (Writeln/Write); TypeScript write_line moved from console.log to process.stdout.write(String(x)+newline): the matrix runs TS in a Worker where console.log and process.stdout.write reorder, and console.log printed i64 BigInt as -7n; new sample portable_console_lines: 8 native backends, stdout identical to C; lib samples re-reviewed (zig_lib_console changed only in numbering); 19:5x lib convert (convert.livemd): convert_i32_base gains C (strtol) and Delphi (FPC radix prefixes ##36/##37/##38, base 2/8/10/16) arms; convert_i32 gains C/Delphi/TypeScript typed dispatch (string -> base 10, float -> round half even via nearbyint/Round, int -> cast); try_convert from string gains C/Delphi/TypeScript/Lua arms through parsed_integer (strict whole-text validation, never-throwing value expression, target range check via integer_fits) and Gleam trims like Rust; Lua previously returned the unparsed string, TypeScript had no convert_i32; lib console Gleam write_line/write print ints via int.to_string (io.println took only String); new sample portable_convert_parse: 7 native backends print 255/11/-42/123/none/none/7 like C; portable_console_lines now has a Gleam row; 19:1x triaged what is left: the lib backend_switch fallback in lib/spiral/backend.spi already carries Zig and Lean arms (they only compileError or name the missing arm for non-unit types) and math (5/5/5), console (3/3/3) and optionm (5/5/5) are fully covered, but the switchable backends run_target_args' and the backend union itself know only Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Cuda, Cpp, Rust and TypeScript, so a Zig or Lean arm there needs a design call on extending a union that every consumer matches exhaustively; the lib files with not one zig or lean arm are date_time (28 switches), listm (10), resultm (10), seq (9), trace (9), runtime (4), async (3), crypto (3), env (3) and the single-switch common, guid, platform, reflection and testing, while base (23 switches, 5/5), am (13, 3/3) and sm (27, 12/11) are partly covered; nothing is blocked on this today because the matrix matrix runs only the C and Gleam backends (the 19:0x restore rung: 610 rows = 483 C + 127 Gleam, no Zig or Lean row exists to fail) and a new arm cannot be validated without a sample, which tonight's scope forbids; StringConcat, string search and case ops and a char model remain compiler-op additions that need every backend and both cores", Feature, P1, D6, 82u32, Paused, "DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", "compiler,lib,portability,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_portable_primitives () = Planning ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", Minutes 720u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_portable_primitives_1 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:53 touch: lib/spiral coverage: 26 files have backend switches; 18 have no Zig arm, 18 no Lean, 24 no Bend; largest gaps date_time (42 switches, 0 Zig/Lean/Bend), resultm 11, listm' 11, seq 10, trace 7, async 5; sm' 12/11/0, base 5/5/0; next: resultm/listm' arms (pure Spiral, no host API needed)")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_2 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: recount 10-15: 35 lib files contain backend switches; 26 of them have no Zig arm and 26 no Lean arm (10-14: 26/18/18 by a narrower match). This is the Zig coverage blocker for DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION; raised P1 -> P0 because it now gates the default switch.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_3 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 3u32, "2026-10-15T11:55Z", "lane-b", "07:55 lane-b: Zig arms written in a lib copy for base, sm' + sm'_real (string_builder family, concat, split, convert_to_utf32), convert (convert_i32_base, convert_i32, try_convert, unbox), optionm' of_obj, listm' (list' as []const t: empty, cons, rev, fold, fold_back, filter, map, distinct, to_array), am' to_list_base', seq (seq' as []const t), resultm (result' as union, from_try, get/unwrap_err text), testing _throws, reflection union fields, backend union + run_target Zig case, env + trace (need the zig-environ compiler patch). Zig snippets compile-checked; proof runs (F#/C/Rust/Gleam capture before/after + forced-Zig Kino sweep) in progress")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_4 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 4u32, "2026-10-15T13:32Z", "lane-b", "09:32 lane-b: Zig batches 1-3 proven and handed to main for pack-1k (patches/zig-arms-batch1.ps1, zig-arms-batch2-backend.ps1, zig-arms-batch3.ps1, about 60 Zig arms; lib-only). Kino-free proof (Kino barred during the Tibia live block): 165 lib test cells captured through Kino before 08:20; a one-process --batch rebuild reproduced 650 of 650 outputs. After the arms, F#, Rust and Gleam outputs are byte-identical; 26 C outputs differ only by the added field in the C rendering of type-level backend_switch records that have no C key and were already non-compilable. On Zig, 139 of 168 cells build and pass (zig build-exe -fno-llvm). Remaining failures are backend-specific tests plus run_target-based helpers (listm'.box, console colors, format_exception), which batch 5 guards with is_zig. Lesson: a Zig case in union backend breaks exhaustive run_target matches on every backend, because BackendSwitch validates all arms.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_5 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 5u32, "2026-10-15T14:36Z", "lane-b", "10:36 lane-b: batch 5 (is_zig guards on try_unit/try_finally, substring, format_exception, am' skip/sort, the 9 console colors, listm'.box) proven: F#/C/Rust/Gleam outputs 650/650 identical to batches 1-3; Zig cells 145/168 pass (up from 139). Batches 1, 2, 3 and 5 are in the tree (main, pack-1k), with zig-environ and zig-empty-macro in the core; the gpt5parallel and single-flight Debug builds of the pack-1j core plus both Zig compiler patches pass. Batch 4 (env/trace): probe program identical on F#/Rust/Gleam/Lua; on Zig, trace prints time, count, level, text and locals, env reads PATH, and TRACE_LEVEL=Info filters Debug. The Kino corpus and Zig test-block run follow after the 12:45 arena block.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_6 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 6u32, "2026-10-15T18:59Z", "main-thread", "14:59 Zig coverage progress today (lane B): batches 1-3, 5 installed in pack-1k (base, sm', sm'_real, convert, optionm', listm', am', seq, resultm, testing, reflection, console guards, backend is_zig); batch 6 (sm' whitespace/lastIndexOf, file_system) in pack-1l review; batch 4 (env, trace) proven for pack-1m. Remaining without Zig: async, common, crypto, date_time, guid, networking, platform, runtime, threading (+ rust-only and bench files).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_7 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 7u32, "2026-10-15T19:09Z", "lane-b", "15:10 lane-b: zig-arms-batch4-env-trace proven in Kino after fixing a private-copy trap (Kino lib_rels prefers deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral, so a Kino copy with a deps junction runs the tree lib): trace+env on Zig 0 -> 4 passed / 0 failed; rust/fsharp/lua 4/4 before and after; fsharp/c/rust/gleam captures 18/20 identical, 2 differ only in line numbers of a pre-existing C backend_switch error. file_system batch6 captures 58/58 identical. Batch4 queued for pack-1m (main).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_8 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 8u32, "2026-10-15T22:24Z", "lane-b", "18:2x lane-b: zig-arms-batch7.ps1 (guid Zig field + new_guid/new_raw_guid/hash_guid + zig_random_u64, platform is_windows, common random', crypto hash_text): 12 test programs fsharp/c/rust/gleam 43 identical + 4 C diffs only the known guid_switch tuple field; Zig hash_text, hash_to_port, hash_guid, get_executable_suffix, retry_fn build and pass on zig 0.18-dev and 0.17.0 (-fno-llvm); probe prints valid distinct v4 guids and correct sha256. Not covered: common disposable, crypto file hashing (Rust-only).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_9 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 9u32, "2026-10-15T22:25Z", "main-thread", "18:25 main: lane B Zig batch 7 in the tree for pack-1n: guid (Zig field, lowercase new_guid, v4 new_raw_guid via a xorshift zig_random_u64, hash_guid), platform is_windows, common random', crypto hash_text (std sha256). Left: common disposable, rust-only file hashing; async, date_time, networking, runtime, threading.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_10 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 10u32, "2026-10-15T23:13Z", "lane-b", "19:2x lane-b: zig-arms-batch8-testing.ps1 (testing __assert_contains is_zig branch): 6 testing programs fsharp/c/rust/gleam 18/18 identical; all 6 build and pass on Zig -fno-llvm incl. assert_contains(_2). Lets the testing rust pins move to Zig next pack after a Kino agreement run.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_11 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 11u32, "2026-10-16T07:38Z", "main-thread", "03:38 main: SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY's final design is in the tree lib: union backend now has Zig/C/Lean/Delphi/Wasm/Bend cases and run_target_args' maps every key, so a run_target | _ => default really reaches every backend (before, those 6 could not reach any default). Lib Zig work is now | Zig => arms in run_target matches, never is_zig () guard chains.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_12 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 12u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_13 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 13u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night lib fixes in the tree (uncommitted, tests added): guid.new_guid lowercases on Gleam, Lua and Python and guid.to_string exists (sm obj_to_string of a guid printed a byte list on Zig and a quoted string on Gleam); convert.try_convert parses into any integer type on Gleam; Lua format_debug of records folds fields (one-field records crashed, wide fields printed halves); file_system Zig arms for directory_separator_char and get_current_directory and a Gleam arm for file_exists (tested); file_system.(</>) default arm is now a plain Spiral join instead of F#-only failwith text (tested on gleam, zig, lua), and the get_temp_path / get_file_name defaults use the portable failwith form (lib has no F#-only fallback text left outside F# arms except one parsing test helper); date_time timestamp guid pair in portable Spiral (last 2 rust pins gone).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_14 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:36 lap 1 grain: lib/spiral recount (2026-10-19): 32 files dispatch per backend (backend_switch or run_target); 11 have no Zig arm (date_time 50 dispatch sites, async 17, near 16, runtime 13, threading 7, rust 6, bench_kit 5, networking 4, ...), 22 have no Lean arm; near, rust, async, threading are host-only by design, so the portable targets are date_time and runtime Next: date_time Zig arms (it already carries the DATE-TIME-PORTABLE TODO block): pick its smallest switch group and add | Zig arms with a ///> zig test")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_15 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:02 lap 2 grain: macro texts in the 65 C samples without a Zig row, by frequency: DynamicArrayCapacityN(!x) 11, DynamicArrayReserveN 10, DynamicArrayResizeN 8, DynamicArrayRefCountN 4 (one C-runtime family, 33 uses, the dynamic_array_* samples), then plain assignment/compare/call shapes (!x = !x 4, !x == N 3, (!x)() 3), libc ABI shims (strlen, memset, memcmp, lldiv 8) and Rust-only text; a portable dynamic-array primitive (capacity, reserve, resize, refcount as compiler ops with arms per backend) is the single biggest unlock, ahead of date_time Zig arms Next: draft the op set (DynamicArrayCapacity/Reserve/Resize/RefCount) with their C semantics read from the C runtime, as a design note in lab/PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_16 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 16u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:02 follow-up on the DynamicArray family: the 25 dynamic_array_* samples call DynamicArrayResize/Reserve/Capacity/RefCount<N> as macro text, and the names are defined only in a C shim header the matrix's C flags include (the generated main.c defines Array<N> as a fixed struct with a flexible array member, and main.rs just pastes the macro text), so these samples test a C-runtime ABI experiment rather than portable Spiral semantics; a portable version needs a growable-array type in core (capacity separate from length, resize keeping aliases valid) designed per backend first, which is a language feature, not an op swap. Lower priority than it looked; date_time Zig arms stay the next cheap grain.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_17 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 17u32, "2026-10-19T18:10Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:10 lap 4 grain: the Zig-first accept (single-flight-20261019-125623) leaves 32 C rows with oracle no-zig: 10 dynamic_array_* and 10 abi_external_* (C ABI experiments), 8 rust_* samples whose C row has no Zig peer, wgsl 1, native 2, and 2 plain samples; Zig try: qualified_union_case_no_payload needs its module files (not a single-file probe) and python_macro_annotations fails with Cannot find the backend Zig in the backend switch op (line 16), a sample-side backend_switch with no Zig arm Next: give python_macro_annotations a Zig arm (sample edit between runs, with the matrix Zig row), and run qualified_union_case_no_payload on Zig through a -Probe Zig rung row")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_18 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 18u32, "2026-10-19T18:58Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:58 lap 5 grain: python_macro_annotations needs no Zig arm: it tests Python annotations of macro types (Result, CancellationToken), and its C and Bend arms are constant placeholders 24i32, so a Zig arm would only add a trivially agreeing row; it joins the by-nature C-only set (C ABI, eBPF, Rust, Python samples) that AGENTS.md lists; so of the 32 no-zig C rows none is a portable-primitives gap; the real remaining scope stays compiler ops: StringConcat, string search and case, a char model, each needing every backend Next: pick the first compiler op: string search (index_of) already exists in lib as sm index_of with per-backend arms; check which backends lack an arm there")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_19 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 19u32, "2026-10-19T19:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:12 lap 5 grain: string search arms fixed in lib sm.spi (three real bugs): Lua index_of used string.find with pattern semantics (. and % were magic) and nil - 1 on a miss, now string.find(s, search, 1, true) or 0; Lua replace used gsub patterns on both sides, now escapes the needle (%p) and the replacement (%%) and truncates gsub to one value; F# index_of, starts_with and replace were culture-sensitive, now StringComparison.Ordinal (F# stays UTF-16 indexed, consistent with its substring; making it byte-based broke substring_cuts_by_start_and_length, so reverted); new test search_and_replace_treat_text_literally on lua, fsharp, rust, gleam and lua added to the utf8 byte test; Cells gate of sm.spi 43/43, 0 type errors Next: string case (to_upper/to_lower) arms: check Zig allocUpperString is ASCII-only vs the others Unicode, and C/Kotlin/Swift/Delphi lack string search arms entirely")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_20 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 20u32, "2026-10-19T23:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:22 lap 6 grain: the char model is a semantics decision before it is code: CharT maps to a byte on C (char), Zig (u8), Delphi (AnsiChar) and Wasm, to a Unicode scalar on Lean (Char), Swift (Character via String) and Rust, to a one-character String on Gleam and Lua, and to an i32 code on WGSL, Kotlin, OpenQasm, Ebpf and Calyx (spiral_compiler.fs CharT arms 49788-172278); lib has no char predicates at all (no is_letter, is_digit, code point), which is also what blocks the self-hosted tokenizer (its F# is_var_char uses Char.IsLetterOrDigit, Unicode-aware); typed .spi gives char no ordering and no i32 conversion Next: decide byte vs scalar for char (recommendation: Unicode scalar as u32, matching Rust/Lean/Swift and the tokenizer; byte backends then decode UTF-8 at string index), then add char_code and is_letter/is_digit with per-backend arms")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_21 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:22 correction to note 20: Swift maps CharT to Int32 (an i32 code, spiral_compiler.fs 171289), not a Unicode scalar; the scalar group is Lean (Char) and Rust; the i32-code group is WGSL, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf, Calyx.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_22 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 22u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 main: P0 -> P1 in the 23:1x reprioritization (user order: XSD first, then full hopac SOTA); the char-model decision and remaining ops wait behind those.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_23 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 23u32, "2026-10-21T23:16Z", "main", "19:16 cross-link from XSD (which the user ranked first): SEFAZ-XSD-DELPHI-BENCH note 3 needed a byte or code point read from a string on Delphi, and there is no portable way. lib never converts char to a number (json_scan's digit_of compares chars one by one), so lab xml.spi gets a Delphi-only byte_at macro (UInt32(Ord(s[i + 1]))) with a Spiral UTF-8 decoder on top. The deferred char-model decision would remove that per-backend macro: a portable byte read (StringIndex returning u8, or a Conv from CharT to u32 with each backend's char group: byte, i32 code or scalar, per note 20) makes xml.spi's code-point helpers plain Spiral on every backend. The XSD Delphi slice is the first real consumer, so this is the evidence for picking it up after XSD's step 1.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_24 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 24u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl zig_float_format () = Task ("ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT", "Zig build time: std.fmt float printing adds ~0.4 s per program; a lean shortest-round-trip formatter matching the C oracle; 2026-10-10 measured on zig 0.17: the float arm is compiled only when a float is printed (an i32 print and a program that prints nothing both cold-build in about 9s; f32 and f64 cold-build in about 10-11s; a warm f64 after an i32 cache is about 2s). Printf still rejects a float argument, so no current program pays that cost. The printer is still unwritten.; 2026-10-13 18:05 Kino lane: Zig floats now print canonically (shortest round-trip via std.fmt {d}, .0 for integral values, NaN/Infinity/-Infinity) through sm'.show/format, asserted in sm' show_floats on zig; the build-time part (a lean formatter instead of std.fmt, ~0.4 s per program) is still open", TaskKind, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,zig,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_float_format () = Planning ("ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl gleam_parity () = Task ("GLEAM-PARITY", "Gleam: closures inside recursive unions, BEAM has no inf/NaN; 1 DISAGREE left is Known; done 2026-10-11: native_closure_union_rec runs exit 70 agreeing with C (TyConv keys on the source primitive) and native_float_infinity joins the Gleam list as the Known row (BEAM floats are never infinite or NaN: a 0x7FF0000000000000 float bit-pattern match is nomatch, binary_to_term raises badarg), ladders single-flight-20261011-174150 / hopac-20261011-174720 / restore single-flight-20261011-175313 each ran C,Gleam examples with native: 610 jobs, oracle agree 126, known 1, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, script exit 0, restore 0 of 610 rows differ", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,gleam", "current", "current")
inl clean_generated_output () = Task ("CLEAN-GENERATED-OUTPUT", "Zero toolchain warnings and no comments in generated code across backends (Lean partial-def warnings suppressed by linters); 2026-10-08 13:3x measured C: the harness builds C with -w (all warnings suppressed); gcc -Wall -Wextra -fsyntax-only over the 208 committed main.c: 126 files warn (211 -Wunused-parameter, 180 -Wunused-but-set-variable); the 31 hard errors are expected (27 C-ABI samples linked with harness shims, 2 format_any lib arms deferred to the C compiler, 2 POSIX poll.h cube samples); fixes ((void)param for unused parameters, no set-but-unused temporaries) are in CodegenC = upstream section, so they go with COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION; then drop -w from the harness so warnings fail the native tier; 13:4x C -Wunused-parameter fixed: empty refcount bodies (ArrayDecrefBody, ClosureDecrefBody, heap DecrefBody) emit (void)x; (114 C outputs changed only by that line and one blank line; native unchanged); left: 180 -Wunused-but-set-variable (match results stored and never read: dead stores from peval, needs dead-store elimination), the // case comments on C switch labels (go with READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES); 18:4x fresh gcc -Wall -Wextra -fsyntax-only sweep over all 462 committed main.c: 382 clean, 31 hard errors (15 abi_external, 10 dynamic_array_growth/resize, the 2 format_any lib arms, the 2 POSIX cube samples, 2 native_managed_array_scc), 213 warnings in 49 files = 204 -Wunused-but-set-variable (was 180, new samples landed), 8 -Wunused-parameter, 1 -Wtautological-compare; two corrections to the record: the // case comments on C switch labels are gone (0 comment lines in the 462 main.c, and 0 across main.rs 402, main.pas 391, main.fsx 516, main.kt 246, main.swift 246, main.lean 135, main.wat 147, main.ts 117, main.lua 135, main.gleam 127, main.bend 120; the only comments left anywhere are the 11 main.zig build-header lines), and -Wunused-parameter is not fully fixed: the 8 remaining are closure body runtime parameters that the body never reads (closure_array v1, closure_depth v2, closure_heap v1, closure_mut v1, closure_return_heap v2, closure_return_text v2, method_depth v1, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned x), unlike the refcount bodies that already emit (void)x; the one -Wtautological-compare is ebpf_conv compiling its own m <= m source comparison (main.spi line 23), not a codegen defect; 19:4x fixed the closure-body half of the leftover: the C closure printer now void-casts exactly the domain arguments the body never reads (it collects the body's free variables once through the existing refc_used_vars walk and emits (void)vN; only for arguments absent from it), a three-line change in the shared CodegenC section that both cores build; the full-suite run single-flight-20261011-190603 regenerated the outputs with 7 files each gaining one (void)vN; line (closure_array v1, closure_depth v2, closure_heap v1, closure_mut v1, closure_return_heap v2, closure_return_text v2, method_depth v1) at 3774 jobs exit 0, native 2749 ran with 13 build-fail, oracle agree 2284, DISAGREE 0 and missing|regressed 0; re-sweeping the same 462 main.c gives 389 clean, 42 warning files, 206 warnings = 204 -Wunused-but-set-variable, the ebpf_conv tautological compare and 1 -Wunused-parameter, and that last one is the stale mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned output that predates the 13:4x (void)x; fix (425 of 426 empty decref bodies carry it), and it did not clear in tonight's three full-suite runs: that sample is only in the suite as a Fsharp contracts job, its main.fsx refreshed at 20:47 with the same residual b00257ff342f6cce in rung 1 and in the restore, while its main.c still sits at 10-06 16:36, so no suite run regenerates its C file and clearing it needs a targeted C regeneration of that sample; 5 mega samples also drifted in that run (Rust impl Default blocks and the (v5)->refc++ parenthesization of earlier changes), pre-existing and benign; codegen proof ladder 2026-10-11 evening: rung 1 single-flight-20261011-190603, rung 2 hopac-20261011-195057, rung 3 the single-flight restore single-flight-20261011-204644, each 3774 jobs (contracts 669, examples 3052, frontier 48, mega 5), every run script exit 0 with native 2749 ran, 13 build-fail, oracle agree 2284, DISAGREE 0 and baseline missing|regressed 0; the restore reproduces rung 1 exactly, 0 of 3774 rows differ, so hopac left no residue behind and the void-cast fix reads back identically from both cores; across the cores 84 of 3774 rows differ and only in the emitted-text hash (83 rows over 32 samples and 13 backends, portable_math_rounding 11, portable_console_lines 11, portable_convert_parse 9) or in that one compile status (runaway_inline_match_recursion flips timeout in rung 1 to error under load in rung 2, non-ok in both), while compile, native, exit and stdout stay identical throughout; left: the 204 dead stores, which need dead-store elimination in peval; 2026-10-12: the gcc census used -fsyntax-only, which skips flow analysis: a real -O0 -c -Wall compile of 80 sampled main.c finds -Wreturn-type (control reaches end of non-void function) in 18 files, 42 of 48 warnings, from union switches whose cases all return; fix: the last case of a match with no fallback and one scrutinee is emitted as default (exhaustive in C, no builtin). Dead stores: new backend-agnostic DeadPureLetElision pass in CodegenUtils (backward liveness, drops TyLet of pure ops whose vars are dead, never Div/Mod/index/slice/macros/calls; memoized per body because RefcDecrTable keys statements by reference), applied to the main body and the CodegenAdapter method/closure bodies of both cores. Both unbuilt; verifier checks every changed output is a line subsequence of the old one; 2026-10-12 built and proven (single-flight-20261012-062458, 3,768 rows, DISAGREE 0, no regression): DeadPureLetElision + exhaustive default changed 268 rows, every one verified as a line deletion of the bless-time snapshot modulo the documented knock-ons (case->default relabel, (void)vN for a now-unused closure parameter, Gleam _v unused-parameter prefix, Delphi tmpN and Wasm $tN temp renumbering, blank-line placement); about 3,000 dead lines removed (C 1,199, Delphi 606, F# 535, Zig 554, Rust 298, TypeScript 96, Kotlin/Swift 46 each); gcc -O0 -c -Wall on the same 80-file sample: 48 warnings in 18 files -> 2 in 2 (-Wreturn-type gone, 2 dead stores the purity whitelist keeps); ladder single-flight-20261012-062458 / hopac-20261012-070019 / restore single-flight-20261012-080719: 3,768 rows each, 0 rows differ across cores or on restore (compile, residual hash, native, exit, stdout, oracle, baseline), DISAGREE 0; bless dev41 started 08:36 (main cache, then a reproduction pass); samples/string_ord joins the Wasm list (exit 0 like C, a $str_cmp forced to 0 exits 1); blessed dev41 2026-10-12 08:36 (single-flight-20261012-083637, 3,769 rows incl. the new string_ord Wasm row, agree 2,287, DISAGREE 0) with reproduction single-flight-20261012-090246: 0 of 3,769 rows differ, parity 3,769; 2026-10-12 compiler lane: Op.Dyn treated as pure, pureBlock/pureBranchingOp drop dead if/unbox/int-switch with pure branches; gcc -O0 -Wall set-but-unused over 464 main.c: 46 warnings in 12 files -> 31 in 2; remaining: folder_index_module macro let (won't fix: macro text opaque), omniledger runtime_native multi-destination let components (needs per-component rewrite) and single-variable chains kept live by something in the IR (next: IR dump); 2026-10-14 01:55 DeadPureLetElision now prunes dead components of multi-destination lets: a pure branching TyLet whose binder has some dead components keeps only the live ones in the binder and in every branch return (nested branch returns included), then re-elides the branches; mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native gcc -O0 -Wall -Wextra warnings 29 -> 6; the 6 left are runtime_i64_add's raw C macro in the sample itself (opaque by design, like folder_index_module); outputs changed only on omniledger (F#, C, Rust, Delphi, Zig; native identical, stdout same); rung 1 single-flight-20261014-004129 clean; 2026-10-14 05:09 blessed with the Bend pack (single-flight-20261014-043715 reproduces rung 1, 0 of 4,094 differ); the pruning pass now elides each branch once (prune the unrewritten op, then rewrite) instead of twice, output-identical (proven by the bless reproduction); left: the two samples whose warnings come from their own raw C macros (folder_index_module, omniledger runtime_i64_add), opaque by design", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,codegen", "current", "current")
