inl note_of_wgsl_backend_6 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:16 lap 4 grain: first union slice sized (accept 125623, C compile-ok samples without a Wgsl row, excluding strings, arrays, closures, heap, mut, 64-bit, recursive unions): 18 samples, 5 with nullary-only unions (match_multiline_scrutinee, nominal_union_case_pattern, op_args_application, tagged_union_enum, union_case_record_field_order) and 13 with scalar payloads (tagged_union_scalar, tagged_union_three_case, union_i8, union_call, qualified_union_case_pattern ...); so WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS buys 5 rows, and payload unions as a tag u32 + per-case scalar fields add 13 more Next: WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS: lower a nullary union to u32 constants and match to switch; prove with the 5 samples via a -Probe Wgsl candidate")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_7 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:46 lap 5 grain: correction to lap 4: the union and array lowering already exists (codegenWgsl: tag-only unions are u32, payload unions a SpiralUnionN struct, arrays live in a spiral_heap buffer; WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS, WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS and WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS are all Done), so what the item lacks is matrix rows; probe on the installed pack 7 compiler: 49 candidates compiled with --backend Wgsl, 35 compile, and all 35 run on spiral-wgsl (wgpu 30) with C exit codes (10 union samples + 25 ebpf_* scalar samples, empty stdout on both); the 14 failures are i8, heap layouts, closures (YFun) and 6 sample backend switches without a Wgsl arm. Staged lab/WGSL-BACKEND/add-proven.py (adds the 35 to the Wgsl list, dry run clean) for the pack after 8b Next: apply add-proven.py after pack 8b accepts, review (35 new Wgsl rows must agree), accept; AGENTS.md Wgsl paragraph still says unions and arrays are unsupported: fix the sentence with that pack")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_8 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:20Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:20 main: pack 9 ACCEPTED 17:20 (single-flight-20261019-161549: 7,057 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 155055); the Wgsl list grew from 50 to 85 rows (10 union samples, 25 ebpf scalar samples), all agreeing with C; AGENTS.md Wgsl paragraph now describes the union and storage-array lowering.")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_9 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-10-19T21:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:26 lap 5 grain: batch census (lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/blockers.ps1, one --batch compiler process, seconds instead of minutes) of the 413 C run-ok samples still without a Wgsl row on the pack 9 accept: strings 160, COMPILES 72, closures 38, sample backend switches without a Wgsl arm 38, layouts 21, f64 18, i64 18, recursive unions 14, sub-word 19; of the 72 that compile, 55 run on spiral-wgsl with C exit codes and empty stdout (staged in lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/wgsl-next.txt, dry run clean) and 17 fail WGSL validation (exit 2): non-tail recursion (frontier_fib, recur_sum, the managed_array scc samples, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending), C-ABI externs (abi_external*) and dynamic_array macros, a codegen bug, since codegenWgsl should raise its unsupported error for non-self-tail recursion instead of emitting a shader the validator rejects Next: add the 55 with the next pack; then make codegenWgsl detect non-self-tail recursion (a call to a join point on the current stack) and raise unsupported, proven by the 17 turning into compile errors")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_10 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 10u32, "2026-10-19T21:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:35 lap 5 grain: recursion guard staged and proven on a private-cache Debug candidate: lab/WGSL-BACKEND/recursion_guard.py tracks the methods whose bodies are being emitted (method_memo registers the tag before emitting, so a recursive call sees it) and raises unsupported recursion that is not a self-tail call (WGSL has no call stack), the guard Calyx already has; the 5 recursive samples (frontier_fib, recur_sum, the two managed_array scc samples, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending) turn from invalid shaders into compile errors, all 85 listed Wgsl samples still compile, pair-drift clean (shared section); the other 12 invalid shaders come from C-only macro text in the samples (abi_external*, dynamic_array_* C shims), not codegen Next: pack 11: recursion_guard.py + the 55 staged rows (wgsl-next.txt), review, accept")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_11 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 11u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); Wgsl list 85 -> 140 rows all agreeing (dynamic_array_bounds_negative on Wgsl is Known under the Zig oracle: robust buffer access reads unchecked like C); codegenWgsl now rejects non-self-tail recursion at compile time.")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_12 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 12u32, "2026-10-22T04:13Z", "main", "00:13 lab drain: recursion_guard.py landed: codegenWgsl tracks methods_being_emitted and raises the recursion that is not a self-tail call error (the script's anchor drifted, the code is in). Removed spiral/lab/WGSL-BACKEND (1 file); the item stays open for its remaining work.")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_13 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 13u32, "2026-10-22T13:58Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:58 main: next WGSL feature drafted without a build: sub-word integers. %TEMP%\\wgsl-subword\\patch.py (dry run by default; 5 anchors, each matching once on the tree core and on PACK-NEXT-2's core; CodegenWgsl is a shared section, so pair-drift stays clean; no new comments). Design: i8/i16 live sign-extended in an i32 and u8/u16 zero-extended in a u32, the same promotion C does. narrow re-establishes the range after Add/Sub/Mult/Div/ShiftLeft/Neg/BitwiseComplement: (x << 24u) >> 24u for i8 (arithmetic shift), << 16 >> 16 for i16, & 0xFF / & 0xFFFF for u8/u16. Div narrows for i8 -128 / -1 -> 128, which wraps to -128 as C's store does. Mod, comparisons and and/or/xor of in-range values stay in range. Conversions between any two integer words bitcast i32<->u32 when the words differ and then narrow to the target, which is exact modulo semantics (u32 -> i8, i32 -> u16, i16 -> u8, ...). f32 -> sub-word goes i32()/u32() then narrow, sub-word -> f32 is f32(), and bool <-> sub-word uses select. Literals LitInt8/16 -> Ni, LitUInt8/16 -> Nu. Heap words, zeros and shift counts need nothing new, because tyv maps the four types onto i32/u32. Matrix side: %TEMP%\\wgsl-subword\\matrix.ps1 adds 9 samples to the Wgsl list (small_int_scalar, small_int_ops, array_i8, union_i8, method_i8, array_u8, array_i16, array_u16, ts_int_wrap). They come from the 17 Kotlin-list sub-word samples not on Wgsl, minus the heap/closure/string/printf ones that WGSL rejects for other reasons. Any of the 9 that still hits another unsupported shape goes to Known or off the list with its reason, never silently. Rides the next pack after PACK-NEXT-2.")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_14 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 14u32, "2026-10-22T15:30Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:30 main: ranking of the remaining WGSL gaps by the C-oracle samples each one blocks (Python regex census over the 314 Kotlin-list samples that are not on the Wgsl list; heuristic, so counts are upper bounds). Strings: 42 uses, 38 sole-blocker (WGSL has no string type; out of scope by design). f64: 21 uses, 19 sole-blocker. WGSL core has no f64, and a double-float (f32 pair) emulation cannot reproduce C's binary64 results bit for bit, so these stay unsupported. Closures: 19 uses, 13 sole (no function pointers; out of scope). i64/u64: 17 uses, 12 sole-blocker, most of them heap or text samples that also need heap layouts. The genuinely unblocked ones would be i64_scalar, array_i64 and array_u64. failwith: 2 uses, 1 sole (runtime_bench, which is Known elsewhere anyway). Next WGSL feature by this rule: exact i64/u64 as vec2<u32> limbs with helper functions emitted on use (add with carry, sub, mul via 16-bit partial products, div/mod by long division, shifts, compares, conversions), mirroring Lua's spiral_w64. Its own pack, after PACK-NEXT-2 lands sub-word ints.")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_15 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 15u32, "2026-10-22T15:34Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:34 main: exact i64/u64 for WGSL drafted for the pack after PACK-NEXT-2: %TEMP%\\wgsl-w64\\patch.py (10 anchors, dry run matches once each on the composed PACK-NEXT-2 core, because it stacks on the sub-word patch; no comments added). Representation: vec2<u32> (x = low word, y = high word) for both signednesses, signedness carried by the op. A helper block spiralWgslW64Helpers is emitted only when a program touches a 64-bit value (uses_w64): add with carry, neg/sub, 32x32 -> 64 wide multiply from 16-bit partial products, 64-bit mul (low words only), signed/unsigned less-than, shl/shr (signed shr keeps the sign word; counts mask & 63 like C on 64-bit), unsigned divmod by 64-step long division, signed div/mod truncating toward zero with the remainder signed like the dividend, and f32 conversions both ways. Codegen: tyv/zero/literals (LitInt64/LitUInt64 split into words), array words (two words per element through element_words/element_value, so heaps and union fields carry them too), conversions (64 <-> 32-bit and sub-word words via .x plus bitcast/narrow, i32 sign-extend, u32 zero-extend, f32, bool), ops (+ - * / % < <= > >= == != via all()/any(), << >> with a 64-bit or 32-bit count, negation; & | ^ ~ work componentwise on vec2 already). Verified without a GPU: a bit-exact Python mirror of the helper WGSL (32-bit wraparound) agrees with exact integers on 20,000 random cases for add/sub/mul/lt/shifts/divmod/signed div-mod, and on i64_scalar's exact values (5e9 / -4 = -1,250,000,000; 1e6 * 1e6 = 1e12; -8 >> 1 = -4; 2^32 >> 1 = 2^31). Matrix candidates for its pack: i64_scalar, array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64 and ts_int_wrap (needs sub-word as well).")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_16 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 16u32, "2026-10-22T15:35Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:35 main: matrix correction before PACK-NEXT-2 reached its matrix step. ts_int_wrap also uses u64/i64, so it moves from the sub-word list (PACK-NEXT-2: now 8 samples, Wgsl 140 -> 148) to the w64 list (next pack: i64_scalar, array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, ts_int_wrap).")
inl note_of_wgsl_backend_17 () = Note ("WGSL-BACKEND", 17u32, "2026-10-25T13:27Z", "main", "10-25 09:27 main groom: the last noted step SHIPPED. The w64 sample list (i64_scalar, array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, ts_int_wrap) is in tests/matrix.psd1's Wgsl list. Open scope per tmp/AGENTS.md's WGSL paragraph: f64, sub-word integers, recursive unions, strings, closures, heap/mutable layouts, globals, failwith, non-tail recursion.")
