inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_17 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 17u32, "2026-10-22T00:00Z", "main", "20:00 suite time before/after (lab suite-time.ps1, reruns from the same data): matrix single-flight-20261021-031858 on the 74 converted samples x c,zig = 148 rows, 146 s serial (compile 22 + native 118 + run 6), 0.99 s per row. Cells gates of 7 families = 122 test rows, 646 s serial (compile 361 + target 285), 5.29 s per row: 5.4x the matrix. Compile does not grow with file size (mut_family 413 lines has the lowest mean, 3.3 s); its floor is 1.2 s per cell against 0.15 s per matrix row, so the gap is fixed per-cell overhead plus contention (gates ran beside tibia-build-pace2 and eoie-release; queue averaged 15 s per cell). probe-lib sets SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 by design, so a warm rerun (mut, 48.6 s vs 66.6 s cold) gains only from a warm box. method_family ran 10/10 too: 75 tests, 74 unique samples in 11 families. RULE (fixed now, before more data): no more families and no removal list for originals until suite-time.ps1 shows Cells at most 2.0x matrix per row on a quiet box (no heavy slot held); the lever is the per-cell floor (KINO-HOT-PATHS resident server, CELLS-PACK-NEXT); re-measure after that pack installs. Converted lab families stay as the fixture.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_18 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 18u32, "2026-10-22T00:52Z", "main", "20:52 suite-time.ps1 now reads archived gate data (lab gates-20261021, 20 files) including the three earlier gates (tagged_union, tuple, string): 82 cells, 144 test rows, 767 s serial (compile 420 + target 347), 5.33 s per row against the matrix's 0.99: still 5.4x. Correction to note 17: the compile floor is 457 ms (one cell of the 19:29 tagged_union gate, the quietest run), not 1.2 s, so part of the per-cell cost seen tonight is load and queue, not fixed overhead; the floor is still 3x a matrix row's 0.15 s. The 2x rule stands and is judged on a quiet box.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_19 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 19u32, "2026-10-22T01:48Z", "main", "21:48 lab folder moved whole to spiral/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21/SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS (LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL note 4): 11 family files, to-family.ps1, gate-family.ps1, suite-time.ps1 and gates-20261021. When the 2x rule clears, move it back to spiral/lab (suite-time.ps1 reads gates-20261021 relatively, so the folder moves intact); the attic keeps days for 14 days.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_20 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 20u32, "2026-10-22T01:52Z", "main", "21:52 REVERSED: the attic was drift (user via x: target folders get deleted often to free disk). All 160 files moved back from spiral/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21 to their original spiral/lab paths (sha256 equal for every file), and the attic folder is removed; ignore the attic path in the earlier note. Healing is finishing the items that hold these folders (user 21:5x).")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_21 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 21u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 20 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS until the item lands.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_22 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 22u32, "2026-10-22T07:12Z", "main", "03:12 main: repaired references my drains broke. (1) suite-time.ps1's default -NotebookDir now points at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS\\gates-20261021, where the 01:36 output move put the gate notebooks. (2) gate-string.ps1, gate-tagged-union.ps1 and gate-tuple.ps1 called lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1 (promoted to apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 on 10-21); they now call lib_gate.ps1. (3) lib_gate.ps1's new -Lib guard accepts any folder with lib/spiral at or above it, as this lab folder inside the spiral repo has. Checked: lab family dir, tree lib and TEMP copies pass; a path with no lib above it throws.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_23 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 23u32, "2026-10-23T18:03Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:03 main: the matrix row kind for test blocks exists now, as its own script rather than inside test.ps1 (1,143 lines, already past the 1k limit; and test.ps1's native code is the native-memo key): apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test-blocks.ps1 [-Lib <package dir> -Files <spi...>] [-Reports <*.tests.tsv>] [-Out <run dir>]. It runs Cells lib_gate per file (or takes existing reports) and writes a results.tsv in the matrix schema: id testblocks/<file>/<test>, backend = target, suite testblocks, exit 0/1, oracle agree / DISAGREE against the test's inline expectation, not-run-<status> otherwise; exit 1 on any DISAGREE. Dry-proven on 4 existing family reports (80 rows, agree 80), and compare-runs.ps1 reads two such run dirs: a flipped test shows as exit + oracle cells and 'DISAGREE rows on right: 1'. The real -Lib run over the 11 lab/SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS family files is queued at the chain end (job-1792778604345, after the float wiring gate).")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_24 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 24u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main-test-blocks-families finished 0 at 22:05: test-block rows 150, agree 150, DISAGREE 0, not run 0 (run dir spiral-bin\\runs\\testblocks-20261023-215838).")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_25 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 25u32, "2026-10-24T06:48Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:48 main: families moved toward their real place. The 11 family files (150 test-block rows, all agreeing in run testblocks-20261023-215838) are copied, hash-equal, to apps/compiler/tmp/tests/families with package.spiproj's packageDir rewritten to ../../samples (same core). Nothing in apps, scripts or .github referenced the lab path. After tibia's block, test-blocks.ps1 -Lib that folder must reproduce 150/150; then the lab copies and its gate-*.ps1 runners (superseded by test-blocks.ps1) go, and to-family.ps1 (the converter for further families) moves to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts. Retiring the folder samples these families replace is a separate rule-based step.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_26 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 26u32, "2026-10-26T13:31Z", "main", "10-26 09:31 main: test-blocks families run (job main-test-blocks-families, 07:41-10:15 incl. a slot wait): test-block rows 150, agree 150, DISAGREE 0, not run 0; run dir spiral-bin\\runs\\testblocks-20261026-074135. Ran on the 5m' compiler path (queued before the 5m' install; the rows agree across backends on the installed compiler).")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_27 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 27u32, "2026-10-27T08:25Z", "main", "10-27 04:25 main: 10-27 04:25 main: benchmark status and a LOAD-ROBUST re-measure rule, registered before any code or run (user via compiler 04:2x: wary the samples-to-cells migration is slower; wants pending benches iterated to SOTA before the self-hosted rewrite; standing rule tonight: benches must give value on a fully loaded CPU, never gated on a quiet box). STATUS: the only measurement is 10-21/22, wall clock on a loaded box: Cells test blocks 5.33 s/row vs matrix 0.99 s/row = 5.4x over 144 rows; the gap is a fixed per-cell overhead (compile floor 457 ms/cell, quietest run, vs 0.15 s per matrix row), not file size. It is STALE: the Cells pack landed since (memo, compiler pool, unit-first), and no re-measure followed. The 2x rule has held the migration since. RULE for the re-measure (the method is fixed now): (1) Same work on both sides: the 11 families' 150 rows (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/families) vs the matrix rows of the same samples and backends (c, zig). (2) Interleaved A/B rounds, matrix then Cells then matrix..., at least 5 rounds, so both sides see the same load. (3) Primary metric: CPU seconds of each side's whole process tree (sum of user+kernel over its descendants), not wall time, since CPU time is far less sensitive to contention. Wall time and load (box CPU% sampled each 10 s) are recorded beside it. (4) Verdict: the median over rounds of the per-round CPU ratio Cells/matrix; the migration resumes iff that median is <= 2.0. The wall ratio is reported but does not decide. (5) Per-cell breakdown (startup, compile, native build, run) for the Cells side, so a RED names the cost to attack. Runs on a loaded box by design; only a memory-pressure abort (free < 1.5 GB) voids a round.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_28 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 28u32, "2026-10-27T08:53Z", "main", "10-27 04:53 main: 10-27 04:53 main: n27's benchmark is WRITTEN and QUEUED. apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1 (unstaged, its proper place). It runs each side inside its own Windows Job Object and reads JobObjectBasicAccountingInformation: user+kernel CPU over every process that ever joined the job, exited children included, so short-lived compilers aren't missed the way polling misses them. The matrix side is test.ps1 -Suite all -Filter <the samples named by the families' failwith strings> -Backend C,Zig -Native (no -Reuse flags, so no memo replay). The Cells side is test-blocks.ps1 -Lib tests/families -Files <the 11 families> -Compiler process, with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0. Round order alternates matrix-first and cells-first. Box load is sampled every 10 s; a round is VOID only if free memory < 1.5 GB at its start; per-phase Cells totals come from the notebooks' phases_ms. The verdict follows n27 (median per-round CPU ratio <= 2.0). Prerequisite change: test-blocks.ps1 gained -Compiler process|daemon (default daemon, so existing callers are unchanged), passed through to lib_gate. Without it, every Cells compile would run in the shared long-lived daemon, outside the measured job, and Cells would look far cheaper than it is. Job: job-1793091102350 (heavy, 4 h, 5 rounds), watched.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_29 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 29u32, "2026-10-27T13:44Z", "main", "10-27 09:44 main: BENCHMARK VERDICT RED (job-1793091102350, bench-cells-vs-matrix-20261027-062533; n27's rule: the samples-to-Cells migration resumes iff the median per-round CPU ratio cells/matrix <= 2.0). Measured under full load (box 98-100% every round, per the user's run-anyway rule): matrix 187.8 to 223.6 CPU-s per round over 670 processes; Cells 4,038 to 4,958 CPU-s over 1,041 to 1,333 processes. Counted rounds 1, 2 and 4 give ratios 22.18, 22.28, 22.72 (median 22.28; wall median 7.63, reported only). Rounds 3 and 5 don't count: Cells exited 1 with DISAGREE 3 / not run 3 and DISAGREE 20 / not run 20 of 150 rows. That's a reliability finding of its own: the same blocks agree in other rounds, so under load Cells rows flip, which is likely timeouts; read those rows before blaming tests. Cells phase totals over all rounds: compile 23,204 s of 29,214 s (79%), target 5,269 s, cleanup 409 s, mount 282 s, slot_wait 0.05 s. DIAGNOSIS: the cost is the compile design, not the tests. With -Compiler process every test cell starts cold compilers (about 9 processes per cell: 1,333 over 150 rows), and each cell's program is its whole file up to the block plus the body, so a family file with N tests compiles its prefix N times. NEXT, pre-registered before measuring, same harness, same rule (<= 2.0 median CPU ratio, VOID rounds excluded): E1, Cells with a PRIVATE warm compiler daemon started inside the measured process tree, so its CPU is counted (the shared daemon is outside the job, which is why process mode was used), measuring warm compiles. E2, a design item, not a flag: compile each family file once with every test block as its own entry point, removing the N-fold prefix recompiles. If E1 alone reaches <= 2.0, the migration resumes on E1; if not, E2 gets drafted as the SOTA fix before the self-hosted rewrite, per the user's ask to iterate Cells-vs-matrix to SOTA first.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_30 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 30u32, "2026-10-27T13:48Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27 E2 design sketch (building on main's 22.28x Cells/matrix measurement + the E1/E2 pre-registration; the PROTOTYPE stays queued post-5n/5o per the lane split -- this is the build-independent design only).\n\nGoal: compile a family file's SHARED PREFIX once and run each test block as its own prefix-sharing entry, so N blocks cost 1 prefix compile + N block deltas instead of N cold \"prefix+block\" compiles (the 79%-compile cost + ~9 cold compilers per cell that the measurement found).\n\nModel: today parse_spi makes each test block a cell = \"the file up to the block + the block body\", compiled cold per cell under -Compiler process. E2 = a host mode (e.g. --family <file>) that:\n  (1) parses the file once and splits it into the prefix (top-level defs before the first test block) and the ordered test blocks;\n  (2) compiles the prefix ONCE -- parse + typecheck + PartEval of the prefix -- into a REUSABLE prefix result: the evaluated top-level environment (typed defs, globals, the join-point dictionaries);\n  (3) for each test block, evaluates the block body SEEDED by that prefix result, then emits/runs its entry per its ///> targets.\n\nSoundness crux (the hard part, flagged to main): PartEval is stateful -- join-point dicts, globals, the replay store. The prefix result must be a READ-ONLY SEED: each block forks a fresh evaluation context from it and must NOT mutate the shared prefix state, else block N sees block N-1's leakage. This is exactly gpt5parallel's warm-process per-build leak (tmp/AGENTS, FRONTIER fix 57: \"some per-build join point state leaks into the next build\"). So E2 needs: snapshot the prefix's PartEval state immutable; per block, fork block-local mutable state; discard after the block. Single-flight gets this isolation today the crude way -- a fresh process per compile; E2 must get the same isolation WITHIN one process off a shared prefix. That isolation (a snapshot/fork of the PartEval environment) is the core compiler work; the backend codegen per block is unchanged.\n\nCells side (main / E1): one host invocation per family (not per cell), against a warm in-tree daemon; Cells maps each block's result back to its cell and applies the same memo/agree logic. E1 (warm daemon) removes the cold-start; E2 removes the prefix recompile -- together they target the 22x. Validation when prototyped: same family, same pass/fail/agree per block as the current per-cell Cells gate, with a CPU ratio approaching 1x (the n27 threshold is 2.0). Prototype + the PartEval-seed/snapshot API: post-5n/5o.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_31 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 31u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:55 x (for main): the cause of the flipped rows in bench-cells-vs-matrix-20261027-062533 (read-only analysis of its round*-cells .tests.tsv, .gate.txt and .ipynb files, and the lib's file times). None of the 23 failing rows is a cell timeout, a compiler-start lock timeout or an out-of-memory kill. Every one is a REAL type error from a lib file that another lane was editing while the round ran, which Cells picked up from the live lib (the mount is cells-mounts\\66a0f2ea464aa5dd\\spiral, refreshed from lib/spiral). Per row: round 3 (08:05:37-08:34:06), tuple_family mixed/nested/pair_returns_zero: c failed in 20-21 s, against 50-66 s for a passing cell and a 300,000 ms cell timeout, with 'FatalError: File main has a type error somewhere in its path. xsd_emit.spi:2014:18 ETY-UnboundVariable simple_aliases / 2016:16 inherited_kinds (module_parts)'. lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi was last written at 08:36:18, two minutes after round 3, so round 3 compiled it mid-edit, and round 4 (08:37-09:04) passed 150/150. Round 5 (09:12:25-09:40:36): 7 array_family, 10 closure_family and 3 ebpf_family rows, c failed in 22-37 s with 'schematron.spi:80:25 Unification failure Got: list xml_node Expected: string (read_schematron); 81:7 xml_node does not have the Some case'. schematron.spi has not changed since 10-26 23:53, but its import xml.spi was written at 09:15:40 (+100 lines, unstaged), inside round 5. Today's xml.spi has read_xml : string -> list xml_node and single_root : list xml_node -> option root_view, which type-check schematron's call, so it was a transient mid-edit signature. Every zig row is not_run only because a test stops at its first failing target, and c runs first; so c 'recurs' through order, not through any backend weakness. Families: whichever ran while a lib file was mid-edit (tuple in round 3; array, closure and ebpf in round 5); none recurs across rounds. RULE for Cells: neither a longer cell timeout nor a retry fixes this. Take ONE lib snapshot per run (hash the mounted lib at run start, compile every cell against that copy) and report a lib file that changes during the run, so a benchmark round is never compiled against a half-edited lib; one cheap retry of failed cells only after the snapshot rule is in place.")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_32 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 32u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "main", "10-27 09:55 main: E1 queued (bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1 -CellsCompiler private-daemon -Rounds 3; written before it runs). The Cells side is a generated cells-private-daemon.ps1. It starts mix spiral.compiler_daemon on a private port (14000 + 100 x round) as its own child, so the daemon's CPU is inside the measured Job Object; waits until the port accepts a connection (10 min cap; exit 3 if never); runs test-blocks.ps1 -Compiler daemon (the Cells client reads SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT through CompilerDaemon.port(), and any pool daemons it spawns land on base+offset inside the same tree); then stops its own daemon tree plus every daemon --list shows in its port range [base, base+100), so nothing lingers. 3 rounds instead of 5: the 5-round run's counted rounds spread under 2.5% (22.18 to 22.72). The rule is unchanged: median per-round CPU ratio <= 2.0 resumes the migration on E1, and rounds where either side exits non-zero don't count. Bench script fixes in the same edit (unstaged, apps/compiler/tmp/scripts): the per-round progress lines now go to Write-Host, so they reach the log (before, they went into the $rows pipeline and the run printed no progress).")
inl note_of_samples_to_test_blocks_33 () = Note ("SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", 33u32, "2026-10-27T18:22Z", "main", "10-27 14:22 main: E1 RED by rule n27 (resume iff the median per-round CPU ratio is <= 2), but a large step: Cells with a private compiler daemon costs 9.89x the matrix's CPU (median; per round 9.89, 9.52, 10.10), down from 22.28x in process mode. The daemon alone cut Cells' CPU by 2.25x. Job job-1793109054829; out spiral-bin runs bench-cells-vs-matrix-20261027-120434. 11 family files, 74 samples, 3 interleaved rounds, each round on its own lib snapshot tree (1,150 files), so mid-run lib edits can't flip rows as they did before. Box load was 100% throughout, and the rounds agree within 6%, so the bench holds up under load. Matrix about 210 CPU-s per round over 671 processes; Cells about 2,070 CPU-s over about 1,360 processes. Cells phase totals over all rounds (ms): queue 5,619,150, compile 3,722,146, target 3,522,365, cleanup 264,459, mount 205,297, slot_wait 20. Queue is time waiting for a cell slot, summed across parallel cells, so it isn't CPU. Of the work phases, compile and target dominate about equally. Next lever per the plan: E2 (compile each family file once instead of once per test block), which attacks compile directly. Owned by the compiler lane after 5n and 5o. The target half (each block's own native build and run) is the matrix's job too, so E2 plus a shared target build per family is what can approach 2.")
inl test_expect_persist () = Task ("TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST", "Expect-test mode for test blocks (user 2026-10-12): a run may write each block's observed result into the source (toggleable dev mode, ppx_expect / insta style) and clears it after a successful run; needs a result syntax under the test header that the parser skip already ignores; user decision 2026-10-12 18:53: rewrite the source, opt-in: a dev-mode flag (--accept) rewrites the _assert_eq literal in the .spi in place after a run, under a lock; without the flag a mismatch fails; git shows the diff, the user commits; 2026-10-12 19:22: dependency on SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS removed (it applies to the test blocks lib already has; the user picked it for the next session's lanes); 20:07 Kino lane (uncommitted, Spiral.Kino.Accept): --accept (.spi only, also via the warm daemon) rewrites, for each failed test whose output holds __assert_eq { actual = A; expected = E }, the single _assert_eq E literal in that test's block to A (type suffix kept), under <file>.accept.lock; rows report status accepted (counted as executed), the run exits 0 when nothing else failed; refusals per test (accept_refused: none or several matching literals, non-plain actual); without the flag a mismatch fails and the file is untouched; real run: plain exit 1, --accept exit 0 accepted 1 (_assert_eq 5i32 -> 4i32 in place), re-run 3/3 passed with memo_hits 2", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "TEST-RUN-REPORT", "tests,kino,samples", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_expect_persist () = Planning ("TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl configurable_oracle () = Task ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", "The C oracle is configurable (user 2026-10-12): a matrix/test-block option names the reference backend (C today; Zig once ZIG-HUB-SPEED reaches C speed), with agreement computed against it", Story, P0, D4, 80u32, Paused, "SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS,ZIG-HUB-SPEED", "harness,oracle,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_configurable_oracle () = Planning ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_1 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:39 touch: the oracle stays C until ZIG-HUB-SPEED; the h1 classifier fix landed today (failure-class exits only), which the configurable oracle reuses unchanged")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_2 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: oracle remains C. Switching to Zig needs Zig rows to cover C's 513 (now 146). Lowered P2 -> P3 behind BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_3 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 3u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: Zig rows now 441 against C's 5,016 total harness rows (pack-1o).")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_4 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 4u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: correction of the previous note (5,016 was every backend's rows together): in the pack-1o bless single-flight-20261015-194349 C has 497 native rows and Zig 441.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_5 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 5u32, "2026-10-16T18:26Z", "main-thread", "14:26 main: implemented: test.ps1 -Oracle <backend> (C default; Zig, Rust, Delphi, Wasm, Lean, Gleam, Lua, TypeScript, Python, Kotlin, Swift allowed): the oracle column compares every other native row (C included) against that backend's row; a sample without one is no-<backend>. Neutral by default (pack-1x bless 140122 reproduced its expectation with it). Coverage measured on 125241: Zig rows exist for 454 of the 493 samples with a native C row; the 39 others are C-only (22) or written for one backend (17: ebpf_*, rust_*, ts_float_nan, ts_while_loop, wgsl_parallel_lanes, python_macro_annotations); Zig arms for ts_float_nan/ts_while_loop staged in pack-1y.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_6 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: full review scored with -Oracle Zig (single-flight-20261017-043743, candidate pack): agree 5,354 (5,383 under C), known 3, no-zig 67 (C 37, Bend 12, Rust 8, Ebpf 6, Python 2, Delphi 1, Wgsl 1), DISAGREE 5, all dynamic_array_bounds_negative: Zig exits 3 on the negative index like Lean and Bend while C, Delphi, Wasm, Kotlin and Lua read out of bounds and exit 0. Zig is the stricter oracle; a switch needs Zig rows for the 67 samples and a bounds-check decision for unchecked backends.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_7 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T19:55Z", "main-thread", "15:55 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: Zig becomes a SECOND oracle next to C (not a replacement): reviews compare against both C and Zig rows (C fallback where no Zig row exists), roughly two native tiers per review. Evidence: today's -Oracle Zig run agreed on 5,388 rows with DISAGREE 0. Next: a -Oracle C,Zig mode in test.ps1 (both oracles scoped in Known), a Zig bless, then make it the review default; native memo keeps the second tier cheap on reruns.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_8 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the first Zig bless runs in the next overnight run, after the -Oracle C,Zig harness change.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_9 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:07Z", "main-thread", "19:07 main-thread done (unstaged): test.ps1 -Oracle takes a list and now defaults to C,Zig; the second oracle is a pass over rows that agree with the first (DISAGREE on mismatch, or known via a Known entry scoped to that oracle). Full suite with memos in 117 s: DISAGREE 0, Zig ran 458 rows, the 4 dynamic_array_bounds_negative unchecked-backend rows are known vs Zig. No extra native time (Zig rows already run). Documented in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. The next bless runs under C,Zig by default, which is the first Zig bless.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_10 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T12:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:38 helper-lane lap 1: verified the matrix half: test.ps1's -Oracle default @('C', 'Zig') is committed (a2abbe52, 10-18 01:03), so every accept since (123705 on 10-18, pack 3, pack 4 whose EXPECTED.tsv is dated 10-19 00:28) ran under C,Zig: the first Zig bless happened. Test-block half: Cells has no named reference backend; with agree on, cells.ex disagreement (lines 186-211) requires all targets of a test to print the same value and names the first two that differ (targets disagree: X printed ..., Y printed ...), with --no-agree reason as the escape (notebook.ex:120). All-agree already subsumes C-as-oracle for test blocks; the only missing piece is an expected-disagreement entry scoped to a reference backend (the matrix's Known with Oracle), e.g. dynamic_array_bounds_negative's checked vs unchecked split. Progress 65 -> 80.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_11 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:24 helper-lane lap 2: test-block form specified against the code it would touch: document.spi already parses header modes into ///- options (test_mode/agree_mode at 312/337, the no_agree reason at 450), and cells.ex disagreement (208-215) compares every target's shown output with the FIRST non-empty chunk. Spec: a header mode reference <backend> (test name reference zig =, cell option ///- --test --reference zig) makes disagreement compare each chunk with the reference backend's chunk instead of the first, names the reference in the message (targets disagree with the reference zig: ...), and still marks only the differing target as disagrees; combined with no_agree it stays off. Default without the mode: the first target, i.e. today's behaviour, so no existing test changes. Two code points: document.spi header parsing (one more agree_mode-like word) and cells.ex disagreement (pick the reference chunk). The matrix half stays as is (C,Zig).")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_12 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:56 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the Cells half as lab/CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE/patch.py (anchors --check OK on today's apps/cells, LF; not applied, lane 2 edits Cells): notebook.ex run options gain :reference from a cell option ///- --reference <target> (reference_target/1 beside build_only?/1, next to the existing :agree put at line 932); cells.ex disagreement (210) compares every non-empty chunk with the reference target's chunk when one is named and present, else with the first as today, and returns {reference, other}, so only the differing target is marked disagrees; the message becomes targets disagree with the reference zig: ... only when a reference is set, so the existing notebook_report_test assertion at 429 (targets disagree: lua printed ...) is unchanged. Left: the .spi header mode (test name reference zig =) in spi/document.spi beside agree_mode, and one ExUnit case (lua vs gleam, reference gleam: the lua target is the one marked).")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_13 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:37 helper-lane lap 4: the .spi half staged as lab/CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE/document_patch.py against apps/cells/spi/document.spi (4 anchors, --check OK): a header mode reference <target> (test name reference zig =) is accepted by flags_valid, becomes the cell option ///- --test --reference zig in test_options, and the reverse export (test_block_go) writes it back through reference_mode, so .spi -> cells -> .spi round-trips; word_after reads the target word with text.take_unicode_ident. Plus lab/CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE/reference_test.exs: round trip, the cell option, and a lua/gleam disagreement with reference gleam whose message names the reference and marks lua as the target that disagrees (helpers as in notebook_report_test: exit_when, a target callback). Both patches (cells.ex/notebook.ex from lap 3 and this one) plus the test are ready for one Cells change; lane 2 edits Cells, so they wait for a coordinated apply + mix test.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_14 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: patch.py (cells.ex and notebook.ex) and document_patch.py (spi/document.spi) dry runs both match apps/cells today. Applying them touches Cells files that lane 2 also edits, so that waits for coordination; reference_test.exs is the proof after apply.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_15 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T04:15Z", "main", "00:15 landing path found during the lab drain: neither half had landed (document.spi has no --reference; cells.ex has no reference message). document_patch.py's second anchor had drifted because the formatter split the header expression across lines; re-anchored to the current layout (++ reference_mode head as its own line), and its dry run matches again; patch.py (cells.ex, notebook.ex) still matches. Both, plus reference_test.exs inserted at the end of notebook_report_test.exs, join tonight's CELLS-PACK-NEXT run in an apps/cells copy (full mix test); they land in the tree with that pack if green.")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_16 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 16u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): Cells patches staged; coordinated apply with lane 2 pending -> next: apply + mix test | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: both patches dry-run clean on apps/cells -> next: Cells window + reference_test.exs")
inl note_of_configurable_oracle_17 () = Note ("CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE", 17u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl kino_run_one_test () = Task ("KINO-RUN-ONE-TEST", "run_notebook.ps1 --test <name>: run only the prefix of a .spi up to that test block plus its body, report pass/fail and output; prerequisite of ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES and of fast single-test loops; done 2026-10-12: run_notebook --test <name>[,name] runs only those test cells, each carrying its definition prefix and earlier static prelude tests (regression: --test square_2 alone keeps open testing); evaluate/2 tests: [names]; per-test planning for .spi (plan: :tests) measured neutral on time (sm' 195 -> 38 steps, 374 -> 369 s) and kept", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,zed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_run_one_test () = Planning ("KINO-RUN-ONE-TEST", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl notebooks_to_spi_siblings () = Task ("NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS", "Repeat the lib/spiral migration in dice (lib/dice, contract, fsharp), alphabet (documents, hangul) and polyglot notebooks: to_spi_tested export, build scripts run .spi, Kino tests move from livemd round trips to parse_spi; mirror every dice change in alphabet; 2026-10-14 02:38 lane 1 (uncommitted): dice end to end: lib/dice.spi and contract/dice_contract.spi re-exported with their test blocks (run_notebook --path x.livemd --export-only --with-tests --spi-path x.spi: 24 and 8 test blocks); the compiler emits identical code with and without the blocks (dice.spi F# 325574 B and Rust 511961 B, dice_contract.spi Rust 184859 B, before/after compiled byte-identical); lib/build.ps1 and contract/build.ps1 run --path x.spi --output-path x.spi.ipynb and drop the livemd export step; runs from the .spi (private daemon, Invoke-Heavy): dice.spi 23/23 + prelude passed (397 s), dice_contract.spi 7/7 + prelude passed (489 s); lib/fsharp/dice_fsharp.livemd is an F# notebook (--no-spi, exported to .fs), nothing to migrate; README links -> dice.spi.html / dice_contract.spi.html; alphabet mirrored: apps/documents/documents.spi re-exported (2 test blocks, F# 634232 B and Rust 1014128 B byte-identical with/without), documents/build.ps1 runs the .spi, run 1/1 + prelude passed (198 s), README link -> documents.spi.html; apps/hangul is F# (--no-spi), nothing to migrate; Kino tests moved from livemd round trips to parse_spi for these sources (document_test: dice and alphabet .spi load and write back byte for byte via to_spi_tested, test counts; the CLI export comparison keeps only the F# notebooks), document_test 16/16; i574n.github profile README links updated; left for the user: git rm dice/lib/dice.livemd, dice/contract/dice_contract.livemd, alphabet/apps/documents/documents.livemd (lane rules forbid deleting tracked files; nothing reads them any more); polyglot in progress; 2026-10-14 02:49 polyglot (uncommitted): lib/math/math.spi (13 test blocks) and apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi (7) re-exported with tests, Rust output byte-identical with/without the blocks (292284 B, 86607 B); lib/math/build.ps1 runs Invoke-Notebook math.spi --output-path math.spi.ipynb, chat contract build.ps1 runs the .spi, both drop the livemd export; runs from the .spi: math 13/13 passed (197 s), chat_contract 6/6 + prelude passed (237 s); apps/spiral/temp/cube: cube.spi already equals the export, no tests, build.ps1 no longer re-exports it (the .spi is the source); README links -> math.spi.html, chat_contract.spi.html; not migrated, with reason: apps/perf/Perf.livemd and apps/scheduler/Tasks.livemd have no test cells but run output cells (benchmarks; Tasks has six-target ///! run cells) whose outputs a .spi cannot carry, so moving them means turning those run cells into test blocks (a design call: output-only tests), and their .spi files are already in sync with the livemd; the F#/pwsh/Elixir notebooks (lib/fsharp, parser, scripts, exercism) have no Spiral cells; left for the user: git rm of the migrated .livemd files (dice lib + contract, alphabet documents, polyglot lib/math, apps/chat/contract, apps/spiral/temp/cube); 2026-10-14 03:54 mirror check (dice -> alphabet): dice lib/build.ps1 and contract/build.ps1 (run the .spi, drop the livemd export) = alphabet apps/documents/build.ps1, same diff; dice README notebook links = alphabet README documents link; no counterpart: dice has two Spiral notebooks (lib, contract) against alphabet's one (documents), dice lib/fsharp and alphabet apps/hangul are both F# notebooks left as they are, and neither repo's scripts/ or .github/workflows reference .livemd or .ipynb names; reverse direction for information: the alphabet-only init.ps1 NanumGothicCoding install and the documents hangul code-fence fix have no dice counterpart (dice builds no PDFs)", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "CI-AFTER-SPI-MIGRATION", "dice,alphabet,polyglot,kino", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_notebooks_to_spi_siblings () = Planning ("NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS", Minutes 360u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl spir_interleaved_livemd () = Task ("SPIR-INTERLEAVED-LIVEMD", ".spi + _real.spir -> .livemd places real-mode cells before the rest, so sm'_real.spir does not round-trip byte for byte (the other 38 do); interleave by heading so the Livebook view of sm' matches the old notebook; done 2026-10-12: real-mode cells interleave by heading; repo-wide check sm'.spi + sm'_real.spir -> --livemd-path -> --export-only --with-tests gives both files byte-identical", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,livemd", "current", "current")
