inl note_of_spiral3_editions_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T15:16Z", "lane-a", "11:16 lane-a: semicolon give-back patch verified on an isolated Debug build: '{ verbose = !-; strict = !+ }' fails to parse on the 04:35 core, compiles on the patched core to the same C as its edition-2 twin (9A1D33CBCAD1). Ready for main's pack.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-14T17:22Z", "lane-a", "13:22 lane-a: real-tokenizer converter over current lib (77), apps (14), eoie src (482): 21,156 lines to edition 3, 0 rejected, every file parses to the same blocks and round-trips 2->3->2 byte for byte (the '!-;' case did not occur outside the user snapshot).")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 4u32, "2026-10-14T21:09Z", "lane-a", "Semicolon give-back patch installed in pack-1e (e3 '!-;' lexes as '!-' then ';', twin of e2 residual 9A1D33CBCAD1); converter round trip lib/apps 91/91 re-verified 16:30.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 5u32, "2026-10-15T02:04Z", "lane-a", "22:04 measured in spiral_compiler.fs: the edition-3 symbol table (edition3_symbol_token) is consulted by the tokenizer in both editions, and DefaultEnv.edition is read in one place only (the parser's '*' layout index, edition 2 only). So mixed-edition imports already work except for '*'; the package.spiproj 'edition' field (pack-1e) sets SPIRAL_EDITION process-wide from the entry's package, not per package. Next step for true per-package editions: carry the edition per package into BlockParsingEnv (only '*' needs it today)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 6u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: measured: the edition setting only changes how the parser treats '*'; edition-3 symbols tokenize in both editions. EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE gives byte-identical edition-2/3 Rust and C.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night trace: four independent edition readers: compiler config (CLI --edition or SPIRAL_EDITION only), package schema edition (applied in the package default env used by file_default_env), the formatter editionOf line scan and x-compat xCompatPackageEditionThree. The positional host path never calls file_default_env, so a package edition has no effect there. Fix: one resolver (nearest package.spiproj, then env, then 3) used by all four.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:19 styles-survey: closest established terms for typefeatureset (from knowledge, no web search). No single standard term covers all five. Candidates: System F-omega (HKTs, polymorphism, existentials by encoding; no GADTs or type classes); System FC (GHC Core, Sulzmann/Chakravarty/Peyton Jones/Donnelly 2007: F-omega + type-equality coercions, built to host GADTs, existentials, HKTs and type classes via dictionaries) = best fit for the first four; prototypes alone = type classes / ad hoc polymorphism (Wadler-Blott 1989); typecase and union_to_record reflection = intensional type analysis (Harper-Morrisett 1995); inl/real = a two-level language (Nielson-Nielson) / multi-stage programming with partial evaluation (Jones-Gomard-Sestoft); type-directed partial evaluation (Danvy 1996) is a specific NbE technique, not a fit; type-level programming is the practice, not the feature set. What is distinctive in Spiral: the typing features are resolved by the partial evaluator at compile time, not by a typechecker over a core calculus, so the honest gloss is System FC-style type features plus intensional type analysis, resolved by staged partial evaluation. Suggestion: keep typefeatureset as the house word and define it once in docs with that gloss; the shortest accurate phrase for outsiders is staged type-level programming.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "styles-survey", "15:20 styles-survey correction to note 8: Spiral does typecheck top-down code (GADTs, HKTs, prototypes, forall are checked there); what the partial evaluator resolves is the reflection part (typecase, union_to_record, record folds in real) and the specialization of inl code. So the gloss is: a System FC-style typed surface plus intensional type analysis in a two-level (staged) language.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 10u32, "2026-10-17T19:43Z", "main-thread", "15:43 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: when edition 3 is ready, migrate package.spiproj autonomously to the chosen manifest (spiral.toml was the last pick), supporting both workspace layouts during the transition. Also research whether edition 3 can absorb the need for .spir files (the real dialect) by drawing on current languages with benchmarks (staged/comptime designs: Zig comptime, Mojo parameters, Lean 4 elaboration, Scala 3 inline/macros), so real-mode code lives in ordinary modules.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:21 lap 1 grain: fresh evidence for the edition-3 candidate '#' inside macro strings: on 2026-10-19 a Gleam tuple pattern Ok(#(a, _)) inside a lib $'...' arm failed to parse (# is MTermInline, a splice starter with  ! @), and the x lane hit the same wall; the gotcha list already records Pascal #36 and Gleam ##(a, b) escapes, so # as a splice costs every backend whose syntax uses # (Gleam tuples, Pascal chars, C preprocessor, Rust attributes) Next: count # occurrences inside lib/sample macro text that are escapes (##) vs splices to size an edition-3 change (e.g. #{...} only)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: sized the # question on lib: inside $'...' and $\"...\" macro text, 17 ## escapes vs 65 single # splices across lib/spiral, so # as a splice is used 4x more than escaped; an edition-3 change should narrow the splice syntax (for example #{expr}) rather than drop it Next: count the 65 single # uses by following character to see whether #( or #name dominates")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:43 lap 3 grain: exact macro rule from the tokenizer (spiral_compiler.fs 43394-43443, macro_body): after any of the four starters (backtick type, ! term, @ type literal, # inline term) a ( opens an expression splice, the same starter twice is the literal char, an identifier is a variable splice, and anything else is a parse error; so Gleam's tuple syntax #( collides by construction with the # expression form (and Rust's format!( with !( ), and my lap-2 regex counts (17 ## vs 65 #) were unreliable because they also matched raw strings; an edition-3 fix that removes both collisions is a splice form no target language uses, for example #{expr} and !{expr}, with bare #( and !( left as literal text Next: check how many lib and eoie macro texts use #( or !( as splices today (they would need rewriting under that edition-3 rule), using the tokenizer's own TokensCli output rather than a regex")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:21 lap 4 grain: macro splice usage measured (macro-text lines in lib/spiral and eoie/src): #( expression splices 11 lines in lib (base 2, convert 2, lustre 1, listm 6) and 0 in eoie; bare !( splices 50 lines in 18 lib files and 148 in 57 eoie files; so an edition that frees #( for Gleam tuple text costs 11 lib lines (mechanical #( -> !( or a named splice), while touching !( would cost about 200 lines Next: edition 3 rule proposal: drop # as an expression starter inside macro text (keep ## as a literal for compatibility), migrate the 11 lib lines with a rule script, and let Gleam #( pass through")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 15u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: finish edition 3 and migrate everything to symbols (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION), getting closer to x sim compatibility, then split everything to 280 (EDITION3-280-SPLIT): standardization is near; the name stays edition 3 (LANGUAGE-NAME closed); raised to P0.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 16u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 main: back to P1. I had raised it to P0 at 22:3x from the user's also-need list; the user's order is XSD first, then full hopac SOTA, so it waits behind those.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 17u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-20T03:25Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 35%.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 18u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: migrate everything destructively once edition 3's symbol version is ready, with backups, always forward when not drifting (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 19u32, "2026-10-24T19:05Z", "main", "main 15:1x (user 15:0x: the compiler must compile projects mixing edition-2 and edition-3 packages, because core is hosted upstream). Checked in the core: already per package. SpiProj schema reads edition: 2|3 per package.spiproj (schema ~178537/178661); file_default_env (~182996) resolves each file's owning package and package_default_env applies its edition, so the edition-2 parse rule (prefix * = RawLayoutIndex, ~45256) follows the package, not the process (--edition / SPIRAL_EDITION is only the default). The lexer applies edition3_symbol_token in every file by the 10-12 18:44 decision (per-package lexing only on a real conflict); conflict check today with the compiler's own tokens CLI over the 18 core files: 0 tokens spelled as an edition-3 symbol (positive control: a scratch file with := ? ?> |: gives keyword tokens). So an edition-3 project over the upstream edition-2 core needs no compiler change; the converter must skip edition-2 packages (FormatterConvert already reads editionOf per file), and the core stays words. A lexer flag becomes necessary only if upstream code starts using one of the symbol runs as an operator.")
inl edition3_golden_sample () = Task ("EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE", "The chat's worked program (canonical_form_certificate/main.spi at 31d26ec4, the geandra_v0_4.py fixture: unions, nominals, prototypes/instances, lambdas, records, GADT signatures, existentials) as the first edition-3 golden test: same residual as its edition-2 original on C and Rust; the Python prototype's 116 tests become acceptance cases for the real compiler instead of a separate implementation", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "edition,samples", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_edition3_golden_sample () = Planning ("EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl test_run_report () = Task ("TEST-RUN-REPORT", "The test command reports discovered/executed/passed/failed/skipped/unsupported per file and in total; a deliberately failing test block fails the test command while an ordinary build stays green (the compiler skips blocks, so a green build says nothing about tests); from the 2026-10-12 review of dc7b955c; done 2026-10-12 (Kino lane, unstaged in apps/kino): Notebook.evaluate/2 in-memory entry point with per-test verdicts and a report (discovered/executed/passed/failed/skipped/unsupported/filtered/not_run); run_notebook prints one `spiral-tests file {json}` line per source file (a .spi with real tests also gets its _real.spir line) plus `spiral-tests total {json}`; non-zero exit on any failed test or cell error; lib/spiral/build.ps1 prints per-notebook and total counts and the failed test names from those lines; Kino mix test 151/151", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tests,kino", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_run_report () = Planning ("TEST-RUN-REPORT", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl test_identity_i18n () = Task ("TEST-IDENTITY-I18N", "Test names are derived through test_name_set (ASCII letters, digits, _), so Korean/Chinese/Hindi headings collapse to generic names: separate a stable test identity, a localized title, and a backend-safe symbol (three fields, not one string); done 2026-10-12: report rows carry \"symbol\" (ASCII backend-safe, e.g. test_u441_...) and \"title\" (the Unicode heading); a Unicode --test сложение passed through pwsh -> mix -> erl in a real run; lib .spi files stay byte-identical through the round trip", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tests,i18n", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_identity_i18n () = Planning ("TEST-IDENTITY-I18N", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl source_boundary_authority () = Task ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", "Kino finds test blocks with line heuristics (spi_walk/header_of: column-0 test header, 4-space body) while the compiler skips them in BlockParsing: make the compiler the one authority on source boundaries (expose test-block spans/bodies from its tokenizer), reuse it in Kino, the formatter and the notebook renderer; add tests for multiline strings, (* *) comments, malformed headers, tabs; record the indentation convention per edition (4-space extraction is a compatibility rule, not the edition-3 rule); 2026-10-14 01:47 lane 1: compiler truth extracted without touching the compiler (an fsx over a scratch copy of SpiralCompilerCore.dll: wdiff_tokenizer_all, then state.blocks whose first non-comment token is TokVar test, body = the block's lines after the header with trailing comment/blank lines dropped, since comment tokens never start or end a block in block_at); Kino truth = Document.parse_spi + Document.tests; corpus (582 .spi in spiral lib+apps, eoie src, dice, alphabet, polyglot lib): before and after 373 compiler test blocks = 373 Kino test cells, 0 count and 0 body disagreements (2 name rows differ only because the extractor reads keyword names test forall / test exists as non-TokVar); 11-file edge suite (col-0 doc and line comments inside a body, test b = 1, test c without =, test<TAB>d =, unknown flag, test i=, col-0 continuation of a triple-quoted string and of a (* *) comment, tab-indented body, words starting with test): before 9 of 10 test-bearing files disagreed (5 tests the compiler skips that Kino silently compiled as code, 4 bodies cut short); after 10/10 counts agree, the comment cases are identical, the 5 malformed headers are now failing tests (body failwith 'malformed test header: <line>' : (), a run panics with that text) instead of silent code; left 2 disagreements that need the compiler's tokenizer (col-0 continuation lines of a multi-line triple-quoted string or (* *) comment inside a test body; a line-level approximation broke parsing.spi, which has triple quotes inside a dollar-quote macro); Kino change (uncommitted, proven in a private copy first: document_test 16/16 incl. 3 new tests, notebook_report_test 23/23): apps/kino/spi/document.spi spi_walk keeps a column-0 comment line (// or (* ) in a test when an indented line follows before the next column-0 statement (resumes_test), and test_like lines that header_of rejects become Malformed test cells; for the main thread (no spiral_compiler.fs edit made): (1) one authority: a host CLI mode, e.g. SpiralCompiler.dll --test-blocks <file.spi> printing name, header line, body first/last line from wdiff_tokenizer_all + state.blocks + blockParsingIsTest (exactly what the probe does, so it can live in host Program.fs over existing public functions), then Kino's spi_walk takes spans from it and the 2 remaining cases close; (2) blockParsingParse skips every block that starts with test (blockParsingIsTest, spiral_compiler.fs ~45394) without checking the header, so a malformed header silently disappears from compilation; it should return a parse error (e.g. ExpectedTestHeader) unless the block is test <ident> [static|print_code|ignore|no_agree 'reason'|build_only] [fails ...] = with an indented body, the same grammar as Kino's header_of/modes_valid", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,kino,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_source_boundary_authority () = Planning ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl multilingual_presentation () = Task ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", "One resolved program, several human presentations (Korean/Chinese/Hindi/Russian/Arabic and the original names): binding-aware alias tables that rename bindings only (never literals, JSON keys, FFI/external protocol names), checked by identical compile results; a pinned UAX #31 identifier profile with NFC for names only (string contents preserved); agents propose names and docs, tooling verifies; prototype mapping exists for the certificate sample (66 spellings, 4 presentations round-trip); 2026-10-14 02:32 lane 3 typed model (not compiled; C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\multilingual\\model.spi, 255 lines of Spiral unions/records + operation types), built from the geandra_v0_4.py prototype (per-locale bijective alias tables, NFC/identifier check, constructor marker for scripts without case, structural-AST equality after each translation): bindings are keyed by canonical name + kind (term, type, nominal, union case, record field, module, type variable, pattern variable, prototype, test name) + definition site, each with an exposure (internal or reaching JSON keys, foreign symbols, external protocol names, macro text, generated public names) and the fixed spellings that are never aliased (string and macro text, keywords per edition, symbols used as keys); alias tables per language profile (direction, letter case, constructor marking, Edition2Words or Edition3Symbols keywords, UAX #31 profile with NFC) are verified against one program digest; conflicts are a union: two aliases for one binding, one alias for two bindings in scope, shadowing and capture, keyword or edition-3-symbol clashes, non-NFC/non-UAX31, alias on a fixed spelling, unmarked constructor, unknown binding, table for another program; presentation maps presented spans to canonical source ranges, measures width in East Asian display columns for the formatter's 120 rule and reports overflow lines; edits map back to canonical patches or alias renames, and are rejected when the structure or residual hash changes; Zed/Kino/terminal/X surfaces carry display-only bidi isolates and a hover with the canonical name; 2026-10-14 02:34 lane 3 correction: model.spi is now 267 lines (not 255) after adding a surface_warning union on the presentation (bidi controls stripped, grapheme count differs from columns, hover unavailable, alias not renderable: the X-compat warnings), CanonicalNeedsAlphaRename (binding_key * edition) in round_trip_error for a canonical name that is a keyword in the other edition (the prototype's 'requires alpha-renaming before Spiral export'), and a separate resolve_failure for resolve_program; record-key decision: record field symbols are aliasable bindings (RecordFieldSymbol) unless a symbol reaches output as a key (SymbolLiteralUsedAsKey / ExposedBinding with JsonKey), which stays a fixed spelling", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "TEST-IDENTITY-I18N,SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "i18n,edition,agents,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_multilingual_presentation () = Planning ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", Minutes 3000u32, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_1 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:46Z", "lane-a", "08:46 lane-a: width check for the formatter's 120 rule: 302 corpus lines are non-ASCII, 260 have display columns != UTF-16 length (lib 237), 0 change the over-120 verdict today. Decision: measure in East Asian display columns so presentations get the same limit. measure\\display-width.txt")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_2 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: unchanged; the formatter's 120-column rule still counts code points, not display width (302 non-ASCII corpus lines). P3.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_3 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom correction: the formatter does use display width where it matters. Layout.fsx fits (248) and Check.fsx (106) call Lexed.displayWidth (text elements). Only the over-120 tallies in Format.fsx (236, 247) use String.Length (UTF-16 units), so the reported counts can over-count non-ASCII lines. Next grain: switch those two tallies to displayWidth (lane A's files).")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_4 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 precision: String.Length differs from display width in both directions. Wide CJK characters count 1 but display 2, so it under-counts; combining sequences count several but display 1, so it over-counts. The tallies are approximate for non-ASCII lines; the layout itself is not affected.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_5 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:53Z", "main-thread", "06:53 groom: done for the tallies. Format.fsx over120-out, over120-out-outside-fences and the over120-left listing now use Lexed.displayWidth instead of String.Length (3 edits, LF). Smoke run on lib/spiral/sm'.spi (format, no --split-literals): exit 0 in 45 s, same counts as before on ASCII lines (3 long literals > 120, which --split-literals handles). So layout, checker and tallies all measure display width now. Remaining scope of this item: presentation of non-Latin identifiers and docs.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_6 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 6u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17 scope for edition 3: symbols plus presentations in each language's native writing, battle-testing edition 3: English (default), Portuguese, Chinese, Korean in hangul, Russian, Arabic, Spanish, French, Hindi, German, Japanese, Italian, Polish, Turkish. Goal: the canon programming term per function, code piece and notebook block in each culture's own developer jargon, not a literal gloss. Spend tokens once: a content-addressed translation cache keyed by (source text hash, language, translator id), so a name is translated once and every later run is a cache hit, like the compile memo. Benchmark the translators before choosing: a weak model, a SOTA model, and a dedicated MT system, scored by back-translation agreement, identifier validity (UAX 31 + NFC) and spot checks.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_7 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 7u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread: English stays the default, and the translations feed back into picking it (user flagged this may be a stretch). A deterministic rule that fits the cache: for each candidate English name, take every cached round trip (English -> language -> English); the score is the number of languages whose round trip returns the same name. The highest score wins, ties go to the shorter name and then lexicographic order. Same cache, same choice; a new translator id invalidates only its own rows. Feeds LIB-CANON-NAMES.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_8 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:39 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix: the 10-14 typed model existed only in job tmp (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\multilingual\\model.spi, 268 lines, LF, comment-free: nominals for names/tags/digests, source_range, binding keys by kind, exposure, alias tables, conflict and round-trip unions). Copied it into the repo lab as spiral/lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/model.spi so it survives job-tmp cleanup and stays reviewable; the title's job-tmp path is now historical (original left in place). Deps unchanged: TEST-IDENTITY-I18N and SPIRAL3-EDITIONS.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_9 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:25 helper-lane lap 2: the rescued 10-14 model still type-checks on today's compiler: lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/model.spi as a module under a trivial probe_main.spi (try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy) compiles with exit 0, so its 268 lines of unions/records (binding keys, exposures, alias tables, conflicts, round-trip errors, surface warnings) are valid Spiral against the samples core after two weeks of compiler packs. That makes it usable as the typed spec when TEST-IDENTITY-I18N and SPIRAL3-EDITIONS open.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_10 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:57 helper-lane lap 3: first data instance of the model: lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/certificate_aliases.spi gives samples/contract_end_to_end_execution_certificate four alias tables (Korean, Chinese, Russian, Arabic) over its four user bindings, keyed by exact byte ranges in main.spi (certificate type 618-629, Certificate case 665-676, certify 924-931, render 1227-1233). Each table carries its language_profile: Korean, Chinese and Arabic are ScriptWithoutCase, so their constructors use ConstructorPrefixMark and the type and the case can share a spelling (인증서, 证书, شهادة) while Russian keeps UppercaseInitial (сертификат / Сертификат); Arabic is RightToLeft. Origin is AgentProposed, so check_alias_table must still verify them. Probe (try.ps1 C with model.spi + the table module, bounded): compiles, exit 16 = 4 tables x 4 entries, so non-Latin localized_name literals pass through the compiler and C intact.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_11 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:37 helper-lane lap 4: first check_alias_table rule as code, lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/check_rules.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): two entries of one table that share a spelling are an OneAliasForTwoBindingsInScope conflict only when their bindings live in the same Spiral namespace (terms: term bindings, union case constructors, pattern variables, fields, prototypes; types: type, nominal and type-variable bindings; modules; test names). That is why the certificate tables' shared type/case spelling (인증서 for both certificate and Certificate) is legal: the type and the constructor are in different namespaces, so constructor marking is a readability rule, not a uniqueness one. Expected exit 1: the four certificate tables give 0 conflicts, and a Korean table that spells certify and render both 표시하다 gives exactly 1.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_12 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: check_rules.spi exits 1 as predicted - the four certificate tables have no same-namespace spelling conflict, and the Korean table spelling certify and render alike yields exactly one OneAliasForTwoBindingsInScope.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_13 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): check_rules.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): check_rules PASS (1) -> next: AliasIsKeyword rule | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: check_rules.spi 1 -> next: next rule")
inl query_cache_compiler () = Task ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", "Persistent incremental compilation in the Hopac model, not a salsa-style query system (user decision 2026-10-13 23:34): results persisted per specialization key as hopacsota fill-once cells (parse per file, typecheck per package, specialization per join-point key, codegen per method), reused across builds when the key's inputs hash equal; return-type hints from the previous build feed D16.3 (checked, restart on a wrong guess); contract = lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P2", "compiler,perf,cache,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_query_cache_compiler () = Planning ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", Minutes 6000u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_1 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:45Z", "lane-a", "08:45 lane-a: parse-cell cost measured in fsi over the real core (1st pass, JIT incl.): lib/spiral 77 files 699 KB 2,373 blocks: tokenize 3.0 s + parse 3.3 s; eoie 482 files: 2.9 + 4.0 s; wdiff re-tokenize after a 1-line edit: lib 67 ms. lane-a\\measure\\parse-cost.txt")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_2 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: relevant measurement since: tonight's cold-rebuild matrix (146 owners) spends ~5-7 s per fresh compiler process mostly on the core-lib re-parse that this item would cache. That is the strongest case for it so far; raised P2 -> P1.")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_3 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:21 lap 1 grain: today's data point for the cache case: the Cells --gate of lib sm'.spi (41 tests) took 9 min (06:42-06:51), dominated by cold compiler processes each re-parsing the core and lib; the 10-14 measurement (lib tokenize 3.0 s + parse 3.3 s per process) is the per-process share a parse cell would remove Next: count compiler process launches in one sm' gate run to turn it into a seconds-saved estimate")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_4 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:05 lap 2 grain: measured the per-process floor this item would remove: a cold SpiralCompiler --backend C on a 2-line program that imports lib (lab/QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER/main.spi, packageDir ../../lib) takes 5.7, 6.0 and 8.1 s (under concurrent blog-build load), i.e. startup + core/lib parse and typecheck; the 09:51 Cells gate of lib sm'.spi ran 146 (test, target) compiles in 9 min (about 2 at a time), so roughly all of its wall time is that floor; a persisted parse/typecheck cell for core + lib would turn each into a fraction of a second (the 10-14 fsi number of 6.3 s for parse alone overstated it) Next: split the floor: time --backend C on an empty main that imports only core (no lib) to separate runtime startup from lib parse/typecheck")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_5 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:05 floor split (cold processes, installed single-flight DLL): no packages 0.90-0.92 s (runtime + host startup), core only 1.42-1.51 s (core +0.55 s), lib-importing 2-line program 5.7-6.0 s (lib +4.3 s); so about 75 percent of every lib-backed compile is re-reading lib/spiral, and the first cache cell to build is the lib package's parse + typecheck (keyed by the lib .spi hashes, which Cells' memo already computes), not per-specialization results; probes in spiral/lab/QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER (main.spi, core-only/, bare/).")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_6 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:06Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:06 the cheap win before any cache cell: Cells already has a warm compiler daemon (--server-tcp, CompilerClient) that keeps lib parsed across requests, but gates run SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process (one cold process per compile, by choice for isolation). probe-lib.ps1 gained -Compiler process|daemon; HEAVY QUEUE 2 runs the same sm' gate on the daemon to compare wall time (cold 537 s) and results (42/42), since KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE already showed warm and cold outputs identical (17/17).")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_7 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:51 warm-gate A/B (main heavy queue 2, 10:46-10:50): Cells gate of lib sm'.spi on the warm compiler daemon (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER not process) gives the same 42/42, 0 type errors, in 217 s vs 537 s cold (2.5x); so for gates the existing daemon already removes most of the per-process lib floor; probe-lib.ps1 now defaults to the daemon. The cache-cell work stays for cold one-shot compiles (CI, the matrix's fresh-process mode).")
inl note_of_query_cache_compiler_8 () = Note ("QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER", 8u32, "2026-10-19T18:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:22 lap 4 grain: scope narrowed with numbers: the matrix already amortizes the floor (test.ps1 runs one compiler --batch process per batch; pack 8 review compiled 7,020 rows in 219 s, about 31 ms per row), and Cells gates use the warm daemon (2.5x); the 4.3 s lib parse floor still hits every cold one-shot compile: CI notebook builds, eoie regen (168 owners), lib.ps1 Publish*, and a first Cells cell Next: measure eoie regen -Publish wall time vs compile count (168 owners) to price the cold floor there, the largest one-shot consumer")
inl hopac_coordination_kernel () = Task ("HOPAC-COORDINATION-KERNEL", "A reference state machine for the hopac model (choices with losing alternatives, shared result cells, cancellation scopes, owned evaluation contexts; cancellation requested != task finished != state reclaimable), with the fork's documented failures as the regression corpus (shared fresh-ID counters, duplicate parent replay, incomplete placeholders, cross-build contamination fix 57); then Rust (crossbeam, arenas matched to protocol lifetimes) and Gleam (subjects) implementations compared by event histories, clean vs incremental, sequential vs parallel; related: HOPAC-WARM-SERVER (fix 57 is one of the corpus failures) and KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM (the Gleam side shares the subject-based runtime); 2026-10-13 22:47 naming: the core this item calls hopac is now gpt5parallel (user decision; SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL, -Mode gpt5parallel, lanes/gpt5parallel); the id keeps HOPAC until eoie can rename ids (EOIE-AGILE-RENAME-ID); the planned rewrite is HOPACSOTA; closed 2026-10-13 23:34 as superseded by hopacsota (user decision): goal inherited by HOPACSOTA-P2 (D3/D4 fill-once cells, wait-for server)", Feature, P2, D7, 100u32, Done, "", "hopac,concurrency,rust,gleam", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopac_coordination_kernel () = Planning ("HOPAC-COORDINATION-KERNEL", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl executable_atlas () = Task ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", "A small redacted executable atlas from the espiral-comum repos (gh-pages: HPOW.md, EC2025*.md, KMLs, PDFs): source objects (exact bytes, revision), claims, checks (programs with declared parameters), results (with limitations), presentations (prose, map, notebook, translation); first checks: arithmetic claims (1943 = 29 x 67 is not prime), distance under declared models (sphere 6371 km vs WGS84 differ: ~816.92 vs ~817.68 km), KML metadata lint (dragon alpha24 file names itself alpha23), PDF signature verification as a separate layer (RFC 3161); keep private coordinates and identifiers out of any public demo", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "TEST-RUN-REPORT", "atlas,kino,tests,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_executable_atlas () = Planning ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", Minutes 1200u32, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_executable_atlas_1 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:34Z", "lane-a", "09:34 lane-a: first check written as a Spiral program, lane-a\\repro\\atlas_1943 (smallest factor of 1943 = 29, cofactor 67, exit 0 iff the 'not prime' claim holds); queued behind the heavy gate for C compile+run. Distance checks need the declared coordinates.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_2 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:44Z", "lane-a", "10:44 lane-a: first check executed: repro\\atlas_1943 compiled to C (04:35 core) and run with gcc: exit 0, so 1943 = 29 x 67 (not prime) holds, checked by a program rather than prose.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_3 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: the first repro (lane-a repro/atlas_1943) is still in the job tmp; unchanged; P3.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_4 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: repro atlas_1943 still compiles to C and runs exit 0 (lane A, 10-14); no further step without a design call. P3.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_5 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:39 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix plus re-verification: the first check (1943 = 29 x 67, claim not prime) lived only in job tmp (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\lane-a\\repro\\atlas_1943); copied its source into spiral/lab/EXECUTABLE-ATLAS/factor_1943.spi and re-ran it on today's installed compiler via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy: C compile 0, run exit 0, and Zig compile 0, run exit 0, so the claim check holds on both oracle backends. The distance check stays blocked on a design call: the 816.92 vs 817.68 km pair uses coordinates from the private repos, so a public demo needs substitute public coordinates under the same two declared models (sphere 6371 km, WGS84).")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_6 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:26 helper-lane lap 2: second check, with public coordinates instead of the private pair: lab/EXECUTABLE-ATLAS/distance_models.spi computes the haversine distance Sao Paulo (-23.5505, -46.6333) -> Rio de Janeiro (-22.9068, -43.1729) under two declared sphere models, mean radius 6371 km and equatorial 6378.137 km, and passes only if they land at 360.7-360.8 km and 361.1-361.2 km (Python reference 360.749 and 361.153: the declared model moves the answer by 0.4 km, the atlas claim shape). The samples core has sin, cos, sqrt but no asin or atan2, so arcsine is 8 Newton steps on sin/cos. Runs on the installed compiler via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy: C exit 0, Zig exit 0. WGS84 (ellipsoid, Vincenty) still needs atan2, which neither core has; that is the third check's prerequisite.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_7 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:59Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:59 helper-lane lap 3: third check landed: lab/EXECUTABLE-ATLAS/vincenty.spi computes Sao Paulo -> Rio on the WGS84 ellipsoid (a 6378137 m, f 1/298.257223563, Vincenty inverse, 30 lambda iterations) and passes only inside 361.26-361.27 km; reference lab/EXECUTABLE-ATLAS/vincenty_ref.py (python -I) gives 361.260861 km. So the three declared models give 360.749 (sphere, mean radius), 361.153 (sphere, equatorial) and 361.261 km (WGS84): the model choice moves the answer by 0.5 km, which is the atlas claim made executable. The missing core ops are now Spiral code instead of a blocker: atan by two half-angle reductions plus a 40-term series, atan2 by quadrant cases (atan2(-1,-1) checked against -3pi/4), tan as sin/cos. try.ps1 bounded runs: C exit 0, Zig exit 0. PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES input: these three could become lib math functions with test blocks.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_8 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:39 helper-lane lap 4: turned the three checks into one typed atlas claim, lab/EXECUTABLE-ATLAS/claims.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): union earth_model = MeanSphere | EquatorialSphere | Wgs84Ellipsoid, place = {name; latitude; longitude}, distance_claim = {from; to; model; low_km; high_km; reference}, distance_km dispatching to haversine (6371 / 6378.137 km) or Vincenty WGS84, and holds claim. Main states the three Sao Paulo -> Rio claims plus one deliberately wrong one (the WGS84 range claimed for the mean sphere); expected exit 111 (the three hold, the wrong one does not). The claim record is the atlas unit: the model is part of the claim, so a figure without its model cannot be stated.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_9 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: claims.spi exits 111 as predicted on C - the three Sao Paulo -> Rio claims hold under their declared models (mean sphere, equatorial sphere, WGS84 Vincenty) and the deliberately wrong claim does not.")
inl note_of_executable_atlas_10 () = Note ("EXECUTABLE-ATLAS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): claims.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): claims PASS (111) -> next: claims as test blocks | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: claims.spi 111 -> next: next claim")
inl ml_labs () = Task ("ML-LABS", "Revive mrakgr's Spiral-s-ML-Library work as two bounded labs before renting GPUs: Tensor Lab (typed op graph, dimension propagation, storage planner with a CPU reference, then selected GPU kernels) and Leduc Lab (pinned rules/observations, the actual CFR/CEM variants, independent checks); separate same-model-better-execution from different-model results; keep seeds, environment, failed trials with each result", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "ml,benchmarks,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ml_labs () = Planning ("ML-LABS", Minutes 3600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_ml_labs_1 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:55Z", "lane-a", "08:55 lane-a: inventory of Spiral-s-ML-Library 80d6a1a (2026-02-05): 174 .spi/.spir; Leduc Lab = game/leduc (base, cardm, deckm, game, model_cfr, model_cem, play_*), 1,148 lines; Tensor Lab = corebase/tensorm + corecuda/tensor_cuda, 613 lines. Real parser per block in measure\\ml-parse.txt.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_2 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: source still present (C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library). Unchanged; P3.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_3 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: the ML library clone is still at 80d6a1a (2026-02-05); no upstream movement to track. P3.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_4 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:40 helper-lane lap 1: scoped the Leduc Lab's real dependencies (read-only greps of C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library, still 80d6a1a of 2026-02-05; git fetch shows no upstream commits since): game/leduc opens corebase, corecuda, coreext, corepython, serializerm, tensorm, game, model_cfr, ml.cem_models and ml.cfr_models, and its qualified calls lean on refm 32, arraym 20, random 20, rangem 11, deckm 9, optionm 8, sa_listm 8, tensorm 8, plistm 5, jsonm 4. So the Leduc Lab is not CPU-standalone: it reaches corecuda and corepython (CUDA kernels through Python), and the CPU reference needs those opens split out first. corebase itself is what CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is adopting as the slim core, so that migration is the natural first step for both labs.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_5 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:26 helper-lane lap 2: per-file split of game/leduc for a CPU-only Leduc Lab: corebase-only today are cardm (7 lines) and base (2); game (73 lines) needs corecuda only for corecuda.random.shuffle (one call), and deckm (24) opens corecuda for its random module; model_cfr/model_cem (78/80) add tensorm plus ml.*_models; types, full, play_*, train_* (63-190 lines each) pull corepython and serializerm (the Python/CUDA runtime). So the CPU rules core (cardm, deckm, game) is 104 lines that become CPU-only by giving them a CPU random shuffle (a seeded Fisher-Yates over corebase arrays) in place of corecuda.random; the CFR/CEM models are the second slice.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_6 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:00 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the CPU replacement for the one GPU dependency of the Leduc rules core: deckm.draw_card uses corecuda random (u32_ubound over intrinsic.popc_u32, then sample_masked_bit_u32); lab/ML-LABS/cpu_deck.spi does the same draw on a CPU - a seeded xorshift32 step, popcount by clearing the lowest bit, and the k-th set bit of the 6-bit deck mask (k = rng mod popcount) - with cardm's u32_to_card mapping (0-1 King, 2-3 Queen, 4-5 Jack) and draw_all emptying the deck. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded): exit 111 = every one of the 6 cards drawn exactly once, the same seed gives the same sequence, the next seed a different one. Next: drop it into a lab copy of deckm.spi in place of corecuda.random/intrinsic and compile cardm+deckm+game with the ML library's own corebase (default int i32, f32).")
inl note_of_ml_labs_7 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:40 helper-lane lap 4: before porting deckm/game, checked which backends corebase itself can reach, and it narrows the Leduc Lab's CPU target: corebase's static arrays and lists are backend_switch macros over a C++ static_array<el,dim> (sam.spi 4-7: CppHost, CudaHost, CudaDevice, plus a Python static_array) and sa_listm's push/pop/unsafe_set_length are C++ method macros (sa_listm.spi), so game.spi's table (sa 2 int pots, sa 2 card) and random_action's sa_list 3 compile only for CppHost/Cuda/Python, never for C, Rust or Zig. corecuda.random.shuffle (random.spi) is a plain Knuth loop over index/set/length with int_range on u32_ubound, so only the bound draw is GPU-specific. Plan for the CPU slice: target CppHost (C++ host build, no CUDA) with a CPU rng (the xorshift draw from cpu_deck.spi, already exit 111 on C) behind u32_ubound and popc; a C/Rust/Zig Leduc would need sam/sa_listm arms for those backends first, which is a corebase port, not a Leduc one.")
inl note_of_ml_labs_8 () = Note ("ML-LABS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): CppHost package assembly script not yet written -> next: write it; run after main's go | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: corebase arrays are C++-only macros, so the Leduc CPU target stays Rust/C via lib arrays -> next: port deckm onto lib arrays | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: unchanged -> next: port deckm onto lib arrays")
inl package_registry_design () = Task ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", "Package manager design: exact caller-pinned locks (the prototype's plan format), declared capabilities checked at install, index separate from blob storage (crates.io model: index + object storage/CDN; Cloudflare R2 candidate), signatures; social platforms (X/Bluesky/Mastodon) only as optional mirrors and discovery, never the source of truth; 2026-10-14 02:20 lane 3 typed model (not compiled; C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\registry\\model.spi, 290 lines of Spiral nominals/unions/records + operation types), built from the prototype's planner (geandra_v0_4.py validate_lock/plan_install: schema, caller-pinned root, digest-keyed nodes, iterative DFS order, cycle/unreachable rejection, grants vs declared capabilities, offline/budget): exact locks (exact_version, exact_requirement carries the dependency's package_id, RootDiffersFromCallerPin), declared capabilities as a union (files by path scope, network host, process, env, foreign library per backend, GPU, clock) checked against granted ones at install (CapabilitiesNotGranted lists package*capability), index separate from blobs (signed append-only index_checkpoint chain of Released/Yanked entries vs content-addressed blob_origin ObjectStore bucket/key e.g. R2, social platforms only as MirrorOnly with a required authoritative digest), detached Ed25519 signatures for releases and checkpoints; PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS: ModuleDirectory packages live as immutable directories in a content-addressed store and are linked into the workspace by DirectoryJunction (files by hard link or copy; errors JunctionTargetNotDirectory, StoreEntryMutable); editions and default numerics are per release (edition UpstreamEdition2|ForkEdition3, numeric_defaults) and travel to the compiler as one compile_unit per package, which needs the compiler to hold DefaultEnv per package instead of per process; correction: model.spi is 284 lines, not 290", Story, P1, D5, 75u32, Active, "", "packages,registry,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_package_registry_design () = Planning ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Pair)
