inl note_of_package_registry_design_4 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom correction: the +5 since 10-14 comes from files committed at 10-14 21:31, not from tonight's additions. The new sample and eoie_dev_spiral_tests are untracked and not in the 1,528.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_5 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user direction 2026-10-17: the Rust/cargo ecosystem (rayon, many small crates under the 1k-line gate, compiled in parallel) is the end-product target to the end, not Zig and its package manager, and no dynamic languages for end products. So the registry design builds on cargo: Spiral packages map to crates, locks pin crate versions and Spiral package digests together, and the 1k-line crate split is a design input the registry must make cheap (many tiny crates, one lock).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_6 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:40 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix: the 10-14 typed model lived only in job tmp (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\registry\\model.spi, 285 lines, comment-free); copied into spiral/lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi (original left). Drift found against note 5 (user 10-17: build on cargo, Spiral packages map to crates): the model has 0 mentions of cargo or crate; its versioning is exact_version + exact_requirement over sha256 package_id/blob_id with its own index_checkpoint and blob_origin (R2 bucket/object_key) and social mirrors. Under the cargo direction, release_manifest needs a crate mapping (crate name, crate version, features, Cargo.lock pin) and the own index/blob store becomes optional (crates.io or a cargo alternative registry index).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_7 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:27 helper-lane lap 2: brought the lab model in line with the cargo direction (note 5): lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi gains nominal crate_name and cargo_feature, type cargo_crate {crate; crate_version : exact_version; features}, union lock_pin = SpiralSource : exact_requirement | CargoLock : cargo_crate * sha256_hex (the Cargo.lock checksum), and release_manifest gets rust_crates : list cargo_crate and pins : list lock_pin next to its Spiral requires. Spiral sources keep sha256 path identity (exact_requirement with package_id); Rust dependencies are pinned the way Cargo.lock pins them. Both the 10-14 model and this revision type-check on today's compiler (try.ps1 C with a trivial probe main, exit 0).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_8 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:00 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the index decision into the model (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi, type-checks via try.ps1 C, exit 0): union index_backend = OwnContentAddressedIndex url | OwnIndexWithStaticMirror url * url | CargoSparseRegistry url, union rust_resolution = CratesIoWithLock | AlternativeRegistry url, a sparse_index_path union mirroring cargo's 1/2/3/prefixed name layout, and registry_choice. Decision recorded in chosen_registry: Spiral packages keep the own sha256 content-addressed index served as static files with a mirror (a GitHub Pages copy plus a raw-git copy of the same tree), because their identity is the source hash (exact_requirement, package_id) and cargo's sparse index keys by name+semver, which cannot express that; Rust crates resolve the normal cargo way (crates.io plus the Cargo.lock checksum pins already in lock_pin), so no alternative registry is needed. CargoSparseRegistry stays a case only for the day Spiral packages ship as Rust crates by name.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_9 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:40 helper-lane lap 4: added read_index's path function to the model, index_path name (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi): the own index uses cargo's sparse layout so the same static-file tooling and caching rules apply - 1/name, 2/name, 3/first-char/name, otherwise first-two/next-two/name - over sm.slice (inclusive end) and sm.length from the core. Probe path_probe.spi checks a, ab, abc and spiral (expected exit 15: 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc, sp/ir/spiral); unrun until main's freeze all-clear. Cargo lowercases names for the path; Spiral package names are already lowercase identifiers, so no case folding is modelled.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_10 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: path_probe.spi exits 15 as predicted - index_path gives 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc and sp/ir/spiral.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_11 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 11u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): path_probe.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): path_probe PASS (15) -> next: index entry line format | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: path_probe 15 -> next: index format")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_12 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 12u32, "2026-10-25T20:32Z", "main", "10-25 16:32 main: the typed model is respelled to edition 3. lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi, path_probe.spi and probe_main.spi were all edition 2 (76 heads; the 10-24 respell skipped lab/). The pack-spelling candidate's 'format --spelling symbols' converted every head (343, 12 and 1 lines, all unchanged in count), and 'format --spelling words' turns each back into its original byte for byte, which is respell-repo's GREEN rule. Originals kept in %TEMP%\\registry-respell\\original. Next, per the 10-19 lap trail: the index entry line format (one line per release in the sparse-layout index), typed in the model.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_13 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 13u32, "2026-10-25T20:41Z", "main", "10-25 16:41 main: index entry line format, designed and typed (lab model.spi, appended; probe queued as job-1792960858607). Decisions, written before the probe: (1) Encoding is JSON Lines in RFC 8785 canonical form (JCS), so cargo-style sparse-index tooling applies and the signed bytes are deterministic (index_line_encoding = JsonLinesRfc8785). (2) One file per package name at index_path, one line per index_entry, append-only (index_file record). (3) A yank is a NEW line, not cargo's in-place 'yanked': true rewrite, so the checkpoint chain stays append-only. A release's state is a fold over its file's lines in order: index_event (PublishedEvent id | YankedEvent id * reason), package_state (NotPublished | Published | PublishedThenYanked reason | YankedBeforePublish reason), after_event and state_after. A republish of the same content hash is ignored (a yank is final); a later yank replaces the reason (last word wins). Gate: the model type-checks and status_probe.spi exits 15 on C (republish of another id, last reason wins, unknown id, yank before publish).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_14 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 14u32, "2026-10-25T21:17Z", "main", "10-25 17:17 main: the node probe (kit2) caught a parse error in status_probe.spi:10: a nullary constructor nested in a pattern needs parentheses (memory spiral-macro-gotchas). Fixed to '| YankedBeforePublish (AuthorRequest) =>'; rerun requested. The model itself was not reached, because the probe's block failed first.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_15 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 15u32, "2026-10-25T21:25Z", "main", "10-25 17:25 main: the index-line-format slice is GREEN by n13's rule (node run by kit2, the stuck local job offloaded). The edition-3 model type-checks, and status_probe.spi exits 15 on C. So the state fold behaves as specified: a republish of another id is ignored; the last yank reason wins (PublishedThenYanked (BrokenBuild _)); an unknown id stays NotPublished; a yank before publish gives YankedBeforePublish. Next per the lap trail: the JCS encoder for one index line (index_entry to canonical JSON text) over lib json, plus a round-trip test.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_16 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 16u32, "2026-10-25T21:28Z", "main", "10-25 17:28 main: encoder design for the index line, written before drafting. lib has no canonical JSON writer (json.spi decodes; json_scan.spi and json_schema.spi parse into json_value, which holds JsonNumber as a digit string), so the gap goes into lib (lib-first) as json_schema.canonical_text : json_value -> string, not into the model. Scope: the RFC 8785 subset the index needs. (1) Object members sorted by key. Keys are compared by UTF-16 code units per the RFC; for ASCII keys (every index field) that equals byte order, and non-ASCII keys are refused, to stay exact without a UTF-16 transcoder. (2) Numbers: integers only, canonical decimal (no leading zeros, no plus sign, -0 as 0); a fraction or exponent is refused, since ECMAScript float serialization is out of scope. (3) Strings escape only the quote, the backslash and U+0000-U+001F, the short forms for b/t/n/f/r and \\\\u00XX lowercase for the rest; everything else is raw UTF-8. (4) No whitespace. Gate: Cells tests on rust + gleam for key order, -0, a control-character escape, a nested array and object, a refused float, and parse(canonical_text v) = v round trips over json_schema's parser.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_17 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 17u32, "2026-10-25T22:09Z", "main", "10-25 18:09 main: the lib slice for n16's encoder is DRAFTED in landing form: %TEMP%\\lib-json-canonical\\apply.ps1 appends json_schema.canonical_text and its helpers (hex_nibble, control_escape, canonical_string, ascii_from, canonical_integer, insert_member and sorted_members as an insertion sort with duplicate-key refusal, joined) plus 2 interleaved tests (rust, gleam: key order, -0 to 0, newline and quote escapes, a U+001F escape as \\u001f). Dry-applied to a copy: 312 -> 399 lines, no new line over 120. Gate: the lib gate kit on json_schema.spi (locally, or on the node once the LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST node trial validates). Unverified until the gate: string < for key order, failwith in expression position, and the recursive call from the local member_text closure.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_18 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 18u32, "2026-10-25T22:31Z", "main", "10-25 18:31 main: the encoder slice TYPE-CHECKS (node job-1792966428412: candidate json_schema.spi, 0 type errors), so string < for key order, failwith in expression position and the recursive call from the local member_text closure are accepted. Its node GREEN is VOID (no test rows on either side; the gate-kit hole fixed today), so behaviour is unproven. The real gate and land are queued locally as main-serial-json-canonical, after the new_vec chain, on the fixed gate.ps1.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_19 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 19u32, "2026-10-26T00:44Z", "main", "10-25 20:44 main: the planner core drafted against model.spi's own interface types (validate_lock, check_capabilities), ported from the prototype's validate_lock/plan_install checks: %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\planner.spi (119 lines, module planner, opens model). validate_exact_lock trusted lock: (1) RootDiffersFromCallerPin when lock.root is not the caller's pin; (2) check_nodes: every requirement's required_id must name a node whose name and version equal the requirement's (else MissingDependency or RequirementIdMismatch with the owner); (3) visit_list, one self-recursive DFS where the path argument is the stack: an id already on the path is DependencyCycle (the path, root first), finished ids are skipped, post-order prepend then reverse gives deps-first order; (4) unvisited nodes are UnreachablePackages (no package smuggled into a lock). check_lock_capabilities: every declared capability not structurally equal to a granted one becomes (package, capability) in CapabilitiesNotGranted. Plain list recursion only, the state_after style, so no library function this core may lack. Probe %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\planner_probe.spi, exit code by bits: 1 order [b; a; r] for r->a->b; 2 cycle [r; a; r]; 4 unreachable [c]; 8 missing d; 16 root differs (other vs r); 32 b's NetworkTo not granted with exactly that pair; 64 granted when listed. GREEN = 127 (run.ps1: C backend + gcc, the status_probe pattern). Queued in the block-end batch (main-registry-planner-probe). If GREEN, planner.spi joins model.spi as the item's typed core; next would be plan_install's fetch/offline/budget half over install_request.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_20 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 20u32, "2026-10-26T00:48Z", "main", "10-25 20:48 main: plan_install's second half ported from the prototype (C:\\Users\\i574n\\Downloads\\geandra_v0_4.py, plan_install lines 1001-1039, read as reference only; untrusted download, never run), appended to %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\planner.spi (now 217 lines, 0 over 120). plan_exact_install read_cost request follows the prototype's order: validate_exact_lock (a lock_error becomes LockInvalid); scan_packages in install order; for each package's module blobs (SingleModule or ModuleDirectory), a known-revoked blob is BlobRevoked, a cached blob must match the declared content_bytes (BlobSizeMismatch id wanted found; the content-addressed blob_id stands in for the prototype's text hash), and an uncached one joins the missing set once; a package whose blobs are all cached is verified_from_cache. Then capabilities, then MissingWhileOffline (offline and anything missing), then BudgetExceeded when missing x read_cost passes fetch_budget (the prototype's len(missing) x read_usd). Accepted gives package_order, verified_from_cache, one fetch_batch of the missing blobs (sources Nil: resolving index sources is fetch_blob's job), estimated_cost, and Nil links/compile_units (materialize's job). The read cost is a parameter, as read_usd is in the prototype: install_request has no field for it. Second probe plan_probe.spi, bits: 1 cold [b; a; r] at 750 micros; 2 warm offline budget-0 plan verifies all 3 from cache with no fetch (the prototype's warm test); 4 offline cold is MissingWhileOffline; 8 revoked; 16 size 1 vs 2; 32 BudgetExceeded (750, 500). run.ps1 now compiles both probes: GREEN = 127 and 63. Same block-end job (main-registry-planner-probe).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_21 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 21u32, "2026-10-26T04:44Z", "main", "10-26 00:44 main: the planner's first real compile (node, after the core-path fix) caught a genuine bug: planner.spi:77 '? lock.root = trusted |> not' parses as lock.root = (trusted |> not), not applied to a package_id (ETY-TermError: Got package_id -> package_id, Expected bool -> bool). Fixed to '(lock.root = trusted) |> not'. Both probes rerun locally as main-serial-probes-3 (the node is over-subscribed). The first node run was RED for environment reasons only: no SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR on the node, so |core- resolved under the user home; the probe scripts now set it from env.ps1's Get-SpiralPackageDir.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_22 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 22u32, "2026-10-26T05:19Z", "main", "10-26 01:19 main: the planner core is GREEN (node job-1792991586904, after the precedence fix). planner_probe exits 127 (deps-first order [b; a; r] for r->a->b; cycle [r; a; r]; unreachable [c]; missing d; root differs from the caller pin; b's NetworkTo not granted, as exactly that pair; granted when listed). plan_probe exits 63 (cold plan fetches [b; a; r] at 750 micros; warm offline budget-0 plan verifies all 3 from cache with no fetch; offline cold is MissingWhileOffline; revoked blob; cached size 1 vs 2; BudgetExceeded (750, 500)). validate_exact_lock, check_lock_capabilities and plan_exact_install (%TEMP%\\registry-planner\\planner.spi, 217 lines) are now the item's typed, proven core beside model.spi. Next: materialize (links and compile units from a plan) and fetch_blob over index sources.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_23 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 23u32, "2026-10-26T08:20Z", "main", "10-26 04:20 main: materialize drafted. The prototype has no counterpart (its plan_install ends at the fetch plan), so these rules were written before the code. Walk package_order deps-first. Each package gets a compile unit rooted at store_root/<package_id hex> (content-addressed, so immutable), carrying its release's edition and numerics, with the requirement ids as dependencies. Each package gets one DirectoryJunction link deps_dir/<name> pointing at that root. A second package with the same name would collide on a link path, so it is rejected with the new install_error LinkPathTaken. A package id the lock lacks is LockInvalid (MissingDependency). model.spi changes (lab): LinkPathTaken added, and the materialize signature fixed to materialize_request -> install_plan -> outcome install_plan install_error. The old install_plan -> list workspace_link could not know editions, requires or the store root; materialize_request = { lock; store_root; deps_dir }. Code: %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\materialize.spi (59 lines, 0 over 120). The probe builders moved into a shared fixtures.spi (plan_probe now imports it, byte-identical helpers). materialize_probe composes the real plan_exact_install warm plan with materialize_plan: bits 1 links deps/b, deps/a, deps/r -> store/<id> deps-first; 2 compile units' dependencies, roots and edition; 4 two packages named a -> LinkPathTaken deps/a; 8 missing id. Expected exit 15. run.ps1 covers all three probes (127, 63, 15) and is queued after kit's rename. Not covered: fetch_blob over index sources, and the junction-target check (JunctionTargetNotDirectory needs the file system, so it belongs to the executor, not this pure planner).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_24 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 24u32, "2026-10-26T08:22Z", "main", "10-26 04:22 main: fetch_blob's pure half drafted: %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\fetch.spi. The executor does the download; the planner decides order and verifies. Rules, written first. Order: LocalStore first (free), then ObjectStore, then social mirrors, stable within a rank. A mirror is usable only for the exact blob it says it mirrors (mirrored_blob = wanted). No usable source gives MirrorWithoutAuthoritativeDigest when only non-matching mirrors exist, otherwise MissingWhileOffline [wanted]. Verify: the blob id is the content digest from the signed manifest, so every source is checked the same way: digest <> declared -> BlobDigestMismatch, byte count <> declared -> BlobSizeMismatch (declared, found), else a cached_blob at the stored path. New record fetched_bytes = { digest; byte_count; stored_at } is the executor's report. fetch_probe expects 63: 1 ordering, 2 non-matching mirror dropped, 4 mirrors only rejected, 8 verified -> cached, 16 digest mismatch, 32 size mismatch 1 vs 3. run.ps1 now covers 4 probes (127, 63, 15, 63), queued after kit's rename. With validate, capabilities, plan, materialize and fetch, the planner's pure surface from the model's operation types is drafted. publish_release, read_index and follow_checkpoints remain.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_25 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 25u32, "2026-10-26T08:25Z", "main", "10-26 04:25 main: publish_release drafted: %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\publish.spi. Hashing and signature crypto stay with the executor, so the digest function is a parameter and the planner only checks that the signer key is a known key of the manifest's author. Rules, written first, checked in this order: digest(manifest) <> id -> ManifestDigestMismatch; signer not a known key of the author -> AuthorKeyUnknown; name and version already Released in the index -> NameVersionTaken. A version is never reused, even after a yank (the crates.io rule): released_as scans Released entries, which stay in the append-only log after a Yanked. A module blob not in the uploaded list -> ContentNotUploaded (deduped, in layout order). Else Accepted (Released release). New record author_key = { key_author; key_text } (a model candidate once it compiles). EditionNumericsUndeclared needs no check: release_manifest makes edition and numerics required fields, so the type rules the case out. publish_probe expects 63 (fresh, forged id, stranger key, taken, unsent blob, yanked still taken). run.ps1 now runs 5 probes (127, 63, 15, 63, 63) after kit's rename. Left on the model's operation list: read_index and follow_checkpoints.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_26 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 26u32, "2026-10-26T08:27Z", "main", "10-26 04:27 main: the model's last two operations are drafted: %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\index.spi. The functions are named find_release and follow_index_checkpoints, because model.spi uses read_index and follow_checkpoints as type names (the planner already keeps that split: validate_exact_lock vs validate_lock, plan_exact_install vs plan_install). Rules, written first. find_release returns the first Released entry with that exact name and version, else UnknownRelease (name, version); whether it is yanked stays state_after's question. follow_index_checkpoints starts at a trusted checkpoint id and walks the given checkpoints in order: each previous must equal the id before it, else CheckpointChainBroken at that checkpoint; each must pass the verifier parameter (signature crypto stays with the executor), else IndexSignatureInvalid; the result is the newest id, or the trusted id for an empty list. index_probe expects 63. run.ps1 now runs 6 probes: planner 127, plan 63, materialize 15, fetch 63, publish 63, index 63. Every operation type in model.spi has a drafted pure implementation; compile + run is queued after kit's rename (post-rename item 11).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_27 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 27u32, "2026-10-26T10:25Z", "main", "10-26 06:25 main: registry probes run 1 (job main-registry-planner-probes, 06:10): planner_probe 127 and plan_probe 63 still pass after the fixtures refactor; materialize, fetch, publish and index are RED. The probes are modules of one package, so a parse error in one fails them all. (1) publish_probe.spi:19 (0-based 18) has a record update on an application, `{ signed \"a\" \"k1\" ?| id = id \"b\" }`, and the parser wants a name before ?|. Fixed by binding `genuine` first. (2) materialize_probe.spi:46 got a field unification error (.unit_dependencies vs .unit_edition, list package_id vs edition) in a comparison chain mixing field access, = and &&; every comparison is now parenthesized. (3) `materialize_plan` unbound and `Unbound module: open materialize` are probably a cascade from the package's parse failure. Rerun queued as job-1793010278411-main-registry-planner-probes-2; any error that remains in materialize.spi itself shows up there.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_28 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 28u32, "2026-10-26T10:31Z", "main", "10-26 06:31 main: registry planner GREEN locally (06:2x, --check plus a gcc build of each probe in a fresh package): materialize_probe 15, fetch_probe 63, publish_probe 63, index_probe 63, all as expected; planner_probe 127 and plan_probe 63 passed in run 1. Every operation type in model.spi now has a compiled, probe-verified pure implementation: validate_exact_lock, check_lock_capabilities, plan_exact_install, materialize_plan, fetch_order + verify_fetched, publish_release, find_release + follow_index_checkpoints. The last cause was a name clash, not code. The probe opens model first, model declares a type alias named 'materialize', and the later ':< materialize' then resolved to that type, hence 'Unbound module'. materialize.spi itself compiled clean as an entry (only 'no main'). The module is renamed workspace.spi (it builds the workspace links and compile units); run.ps1 and the probe follow. Rule for this model: no module named after one of model.spi's operation types (materialize, plan_install, validate_lock, ...). The two other fixes from run 1 (the publish record-update parse and the parenthesized comparisons) stand. The queued job-1793010278411 reads the updated run.ps1 and records all six formally.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_29 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 29u32, "2026-10-26T10:32Z", "main", "10-26 06:32 main: stopped the queued job-1793010278411 before it started: the 06:2x local --check plus gcc run already verified all four new probes (15/63/63/63) on the installed compiler, and run 1 verified the other two (127/63). The heavy slots go to work that still needs a verdict. The probes rerun as part of the next change to the planner, through %TEMP%\\registry-planner\\run.ps1.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_30 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 30u32, "2026-10-26T14:32Z", "main", "10-26 10:32 main: registry planner LANDED in the repo (unstaged, in the untracked lab folder beside model.spi): spiral/lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN now holds planner, fixtures, workspace, fetch, publish and index, the six probes, and run.ps1. run.ps1 now reads every module from its own folder and builds in a TEMP work dir, so no build output lands in the repo. Proof from the landed copy (run.ps1, 10:2x, C backend + gcc): planner_probe 127, plan_probe 63, materialize_probe 15, fetch_probe 63, publish_probe 63, index_probe 63, all as expected: VERDICT GREEN. The TEMP draft (registry-planner) was removed after a byte check (LF-normalized) showed all 12 landed files equal to it, so this is the one record. Earlier notes' TEMP paths now mean this lab folder. Next for the item: the executor half (downloads, hashing, Ed25519, the junction calls) behind the pure planner, starting with materialize's junction step over lib file_system.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_31 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 31u32, "2026-10-26T20:07Z", "main", "10-26 16:07 main: 10-26 16:1x main: the executor's first step (materialize's links) is designed by reading what exists; no code changed yet. The planner emits workspace_link { link_path; target; kind = DirectoryJunction } in package order (workspace.spi materialize_packages, proven by materialize_probe 15). Lib has directory_create_symbolic_link (file_system.spi:892: Windows symlink_dir, unix symlink), but NO junction primitive. On Windows a directory symlink needs developer mode or admin, while a junction needs neither, which is why the planner's kind is DirectoryJunction.\nDecision, by rule (no new crate, since the crate gate keeps lib std-only; one host call per macro; flow in Spiral): a new lib primitive file_system.directory_create_junction (target) (path) : result () string.\n- Rust/Windows: ONE host call spawning cmd /d /c mklink /J <path> <target> through std::process::Command, returning (exit code, stderr). mklink is a cmd builtin, not an executable (kit's launch model lists it as BuiltinMklink), so cmd /d /c is required, and /d skips AutoRun.\n- Rust/unix: the existing symlink call (a junction is a directory symlink there).\n- Spiral decides Ok/Error from the exit code and returns stderr text on failure.\n- F#/others: a backend_switch fallback that defers to the target compiler, per the lib convention.\nThe executor module (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/execute.spi) then walks plan.links in order: refuse if link_path exists (mapping to the planner's LinkPathTaken), create the junction, return the first error.\nProof plan, written now: a lib test that creates a junction in a temp dir, checks file_system's rust_is_symlink-style probe sees it, reads a file through it, removes the link and checks the target is intact (the same reparse-point safety as the cube proof). Plus an execute_probe in the lab over materialize_probe's 3-link plan.\nOrdering: file_system.spi is also in Nil batch 2 (5 sites), so the primitive is drafted against the post-batch-2 text, or packed into the same gate.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_32 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 32u32, "2026-10-26T20:12Z", "main", "10-26 16:12 main: 10-26 16:2x main: CORRECTION to n31's placement, found by reading before drafting.\n(1) The lib-reuse route I preferred (file_system calling runtime.execute, 'cmd /d /c mklink /J ...', no new host code) would leak crates. runtime's Rust command path (execute_with_options_rust, runtime.spi:1005; its tests declare '-d chrono encoding_rs encoding_rs_io regex sha2') isn't std-only, so every file_system consumer's Rust build would inherit those crate dependencies, the crate-gate problem. Module order would allow the call (runtime 57 < file_system 58), but it is still wrong.\n(2) runtime.execute has only Rust and F# arms, while the lab's probes build with the C backend (run.ps1: C + gcc). So the executor can't be probed on the planner's current backend at all.\nRevised decision, by the same rules (std-only lib primitives, one host call, flow in Spiral): the junction stays a std-only lib primitive in file_system. On Rust/Windows it is one host call to std::process::Command::new(\"cmd\") with args /d /c mklink /J <path> <target>, returning the exit code; Spiral maps a nonzero code to Error. Unix reuses directory_create_symbolic_link. Other backends get the deferring fallback. No runtime dependency.\nThe executor (download, hash, Ed25519, link creation) is the first Rust-backend half of the registry, so it gets its own Rust-backend probe runner instead of the C-backend run.ps1; the pure planner stays on C. model.spi needs one new install_error case for a failed link (LinkFailed of relative_path * i32 exit code) before execute.spi can report it. Order unchanged: draft against file_system's post-batch-2 text.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_33 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 33u32, "2026-10-26T20:22Z", "main", "10-26 16:22 main: 10-26 16:3x main: lib file_system.directory_create_junction drafted and PROVEN BY EXECUTION on a lib copy (TEMP lib-fs-junction/apply.ps1). Shape per n32:\n- refuse if the path exists (Error, the planner's LinkPathTaken idea);\n- Rust/Windows: ONE host call, std::process::Command cmd /d /c mklink /J <path> <target> with stdout nulled, returning the exit code; Spiral maps it to Ok, or Error with the code;\n- Rust/unix and other backends: the existing directory_create_symbolic_link;\n- std-only, no runtime dependency.\nProof: a probe package calling it compiled to Rust (exit 0, no tokenizer/parser/type errors, mklink present in main.rs), rustc built it, and the run created a real junction (LinkType=Junction, target the probe's a), a file read through it ('through the junction'). A second run refused, exit 1. Deleting the link left the target intact. Stdout is nulled because the first run printed mklink's 'Junction created' line into the caller's output.\nThe slice adds the Rust test directory_create_junction_links_reads_and_refuses_a_taken_path (create, refuse on the second call, read through) right after link_file's test. Serial gate (kit gate on file_system.spi, running that test) and land are in the post-rename requeue (main-serial-fs-junction).\nCAUTION found while reading: lib's existing link_directory (file_system.spi:1346) deletes path RECURSIVELY (directory_delete !+) when it is a real directory rather than a link, then links. That is fine for build scripts resetting deps folders, but wrong for an installer: the registry executor must use directory_create_junction's refusal, never link_directory.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_34 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 34u32, "2026-10-26T20:30Z", "main", "10-26 16:30 main: 10-26 16:3x main: registry executor, first step GREEN by execution. New in lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN: execute.spi (create_links over plan.links: JunctionTargetNotDirectory when the store target is missing, LinkPathTaken when the link path exists, else lib file_system.directory_create_junction, mapping a failure to the new model case LinkCreateFailed of workspace_link * string); execute_probe.spi; execute-run.ps1 (a Rust-backend runner: lib copy with the junction slice applied, a package of real lib plus the lab modules, compile to Rust, rustc, run in a fresh dir, reparse-safe cleanup). Result: execute_probe exit 7 = 1 two junctions deps/a and deps/b created and read through + 2 a taken path is LinkPathTaken + 4 a missing target is JunctionTargetNotDirectory; ws/deps/a LinkType=Junction. BUG found and fixed on this path: the first run's mklink printed 'Parameter format not correct - deps', because cmd parses /deps in ws/deps/b as a switch. My earlier single-segment probe (a, b) could not see that. The lib primitive now converts path and target to backslashes in Spiral (sm'.replace) before its one host call; every real install path (deps/<name>) would have hit it. Follow-ups queued after the rename: the C planner probes rerun for the model union change (main-registry-planner-probes, expect 127/63/15/63/63/63), and the slice's kit gate on file_system.spi, which also re-checks the 3-line layout of the host call (layout only, made after this run).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_35 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 35u32, "2026-10-26T20:32Z", "main", "10-26 16:32 main: 10-26 16:4x main: the executor's next step (verify a fetched blob) is designed by reading lib. The pure side exists: fetch.verify_fetched (module_entry, fetched_bytes {digest; byte_count; stored_at}) gives Accepted cached_blob, or BlobDigestMismatch / BlobSizeMismatch. The executor's job is only to measure: hash the stored file and count its bytes, then call it. Lib has crypto.get_file_hash path (crypto.spi:85; a buffered sha2 over the file, hex string), with two constraints that shape the step. (1) It unwraps file_open (resultm.unwrap', crypto.spi:87), so a missing blob would PANIC: the executor checks file_system.file_exists first and maps a miss to a typed error (MissingWhileOffline for cache entries) before hashing. (2) crypto declares '-d chrono regex sha2' and std has no SHA-256, so the hashing half can't use execute-run.ps1's plain-rustc mode. It gets a cargo mode: a generated Cargo.toml pinning the crate versions lib's own builds use, then cargo build --offline. The link step stays std-only and plain rustc. Probe plan: write two blobs, one matching its entry and one with a flipped byte; expect Accepted for the first, BlobDigestMismatch for the second, and the typed miss for an absent file, with the panic case shown not to occur.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_36 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 36u32, "2026-10-26T20:44Z", "main", "10-26 16:44 main: 10-26 16:4x main: registry executor step 2 (verify a stored blob) GREEN by execution. execute.spi gained verify_stored (entry, stored). It checks file_system.file_exists first, so a missing blob is the typed MissingWhileOffline [content] instead of crypto.get_file_hash's unwrap panic, then hashes with lib crypto.get_file_hash (sha2), measures with file_system.rust_file_length, and hands both to the pure fetch.verify_fetched. hash_probe over 'hello' (sha256 2cf24dba...938b9824, 5 bytes): exit 15 = 1 matching blob Accepted with 5 bytes + 2 a flipped digest is BlobDigestMismatch + 4 a missing file is MissingWhileOffline, not a panic + 8 the right digest at the wrong size is BlobSizeMismatch (6 vs 5). lib's hex output matches the standard lowercase digest. execute-run.ps1 is now one runner for every executor probe: -Probe/-Expected/-Meaning, plus -Cargo, which generates a crate with lib's own requirements (spiral/workspace/Cargo.toml: sha2 ~0.11.0-pre.4, regex 1.10, chrono >=0.4,<1), copies the workspace Cargo.lock, and builds with cargo --offline. The link step stays plain rustc. Its error filter now prints the ETY messages; the first RED (sm'.slice 1 63, ETY-ValueRestriction) was hidden by the old filter. Next executor steps: Ed25519 signature checks (no lib primitive yet; needs a crate decision) and the end-to-end install (plan, fetch from a local store, verify, link) as one probe.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_37 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 37u32, "2026-10-26T20:47Z", "main", "10-26 16:47 main: 10-26 16:5x main: Ed25519 crate DECIDED by the pre-written rule (no new version enters the build; offline-resolvable): ed25519-dalek 2.2.0. It is already in lib's workspace lock (spiral/workspace/Cargo.lock: ed25519-dalek 2.2.0, ed25519 2.2.3, curve25519-dalek 4.1.3, signature 2.2.0; pulled in transitively) and in the local cargo cache, both as .crate and as extracted src under CARGO_HOME (scoop persist/rustup/.cargo, not ~/.cargo, which is why a first search found nothing). So execute-run.ps1's -Cargo mode builds it offline with the copied workspace lock. ring 0.17.14 is also locked, but dalek is the ed25519 API the lock already resolves; no second signature stack. Shape of the next step, by the granular rule: a lib primitive crypto.ed25519_verify (public_key : array u8) (message : array u8) (signature : array u8) : bool, ONE host call (VerifyingKey::from_bytes then verify_strict). Byte lengths (32/64) are checked in Spiral, which returns false on a wrong length rather than letting a host try_into panic. Hex decoding happens in Spiral before the call. The registry's publish/index signature checks (SignatureInvalid, IndexSignatureInvalid, today pure booleans in the C probes) then get a measured executor twin, as verify_stored did for digests. Probe plan: the RFC 8032 test vector 1 (empty message) must verify; a flipped signature byte and a wrong-length key must not.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_38 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 38u32, "2026-10-26T20:53Z", "main", "10-26 16:53 main: 10-26 16:5x main: registry executor step 3 (Ed25519) GREEN by execution, with the crate from n37 (ed25519-dalek 2.2.0, already in the workspace lock, cargo --offline). New lab module signing.spi: decode_hex in Spiral (nibble lookup by sm'.index_of in '0123456789abcdef', so no char ordering; odd length or a non-hex digit is None); 32- and 64-byte length checks in Spiral; then ONE host call, VerifyingKey::from_bytes(...).map(verify_strict(...).is_ok()).unwrap_or(false). The host's try_from().unwrap() can only see correct lengths, so it can't panic. signing_probe exit 15 = 1 RFC 8032 test vector 1 (empty message) verifies + 2 a flipped last signature byte does not + 4 a 3-byte key is false, not a panic + 8 odd-length hex is false. So the executor now has three steps proven by execution: links (7), blob verification (15), signatures (15). Next: an end-to-end install probe (plan, then fetch from a local store, verify, check signatures, link), and promoting decode_hex and ed25519_verify into lib crypto as a gated slice.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_39 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 39u32, "2026-10-26T21:31Z", "main", "10-26 17:31 main: 10-26 17:3x main: END-TO-END install GREEN by execution, first run. install_probe.spi chains the pure planner and the measured executor on real data. Two stored modules with REAL sha256 digests (module a body cddea6d0...af1b, module b body 813119b2...867f, 13 bytes each; computed independently with .NET SHA256 before writing the probe). A lock where a requires b, a cache pointing at the stored files, offline, a zero fetch budget. Then: plan_exact_install (the pure planner accepts the cache), materialize_plan (links deps/b, deps/a), verify_stored on both blobs (lib sha2 hash plus size against the lock), execute_links (real junctions through lib file_system.directory_create_junction), and a read through ws/deps/a and ws/deps/b. Exit 7 = 1 both blobs verify + 2 the installed workspace reads 'module a body' and 'module b body' through the junctions + 4 after the stored b is tampered ('module b BODY'), verify_stored reports BlobDigestMismatch. The executor's proven steps now compose: plan, materialize, verify, link. Signature checks (signing.spi, GREEN 15) are proven separately and join the end-to-end path when publish/index records carry real Ed25519 signatures. Next for the item: that join, then promoting execute/signing helpers into lib as gated slices (crypto Ed25519 and file_system junction are already queued).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_40 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 40u32, "2026-10-26T21:41Z", "main", "10-26 17:41 main: 10-26 17:4x main: DESIGN GAP found and fixed in the pure planner. publish.publish_release accepted a release when its signer key was one of the author's known keys (known_key), but never checked that the signature VERIFIES. A forged signature under a known public key passed. That was a real registry hole, since public keys are public. Fix, on the seam index.spi already uses (an injected verifier keeps the planner backend-neutral): publish_release gains a parameter verify : detached_signature -> package_id -> bool, checked right after the known-key check, rejecting with the new publish_error case SignatureNotVerified of package_id. C probes: publish_probe passes a trusting stub to its 6 existing calls and adds a 7th case with a distrusting stub (bit 64), so its expected value goes 63 -> 127 (run.ps1 updated; the C-probe rerun is in the post-rename requeue, main-registry-planner-probes). Executor: execute.spi gains ed25519_author_signature, the real verifier adapter (Ed25519 algorithm: lib-to-be signing.ed25519_verify over the package id's hex text; other algorithms false until implemented). signed_publish_probe (Rust, RFC 8032 vector 2, whose message is the byte 'r' and so equals the fixtures' package id text for 'r') checks the real path; its result follows.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_41 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 41u32, "2026-10-26T21:42Z", "main", "10-26 17:42 main: 10-26 17:4x main: signed publish GREEN by execution (n40's fix on the real path). signed_publish_probe: publish_release with execute.ed25519_author_signature as its verifier, the author's known key = the RFC 8032 vector 2 public key, release id = the fixtures' id 'r' (text 'r', the vector's one-byte message). Exit 3 = 1 the genuinely signed release is Released + 2 the same release with its last signature byte changed (...0c00 -> ...0c01) is Rejected SignatureNotVerified (id 'r'). Before n40 the second release would have been ACCEPTED: same known key, no verification. The registry's proven executor now covers links, blob digests, signatures (standalone and inside publish) and the end-to-end install. Open: the C planner probes rerun for the two model/publish changes (expected 127/63/15/63/127/63; queued post-rename as main-registry-planner-probes), and the lib promotions (fs junction, crypto Ed25519) whose gates are queued.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_42 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 42u32, "2026-10-26T21:48Z", "main", "10-26 17:48 main: 10-26 17:4x main: the six C planner probes rerun GREEN after today's model and publish changes (new install_error LinkCreateFailed, new publish_error SignatureNotVerified, the injected verify parameter on publish_release): planner_probe 127, plan_probe 63, materialize_probe 15, fetch_probe 63, publish_probe 127 (was 63; the new bit 64 is a signature the verifier rejects, SignatureNotVerified), index_probe 63. All exact, VERDICT GREEN (run.ps1, C backend + gcc, installed compiler). The pure half and the measured Rust half are consistent: 6 C probes plus 5 Rust executor probes (execute 7, hash 15, signing 15, install 7, signed publish 3), all GREEN. The queued post-rename rerun (main-registry-planner-probes) was dropped from the requeue as redundant.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_43 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 43u32, "2026-10-26T21:56Z", "main", "10-26 17:56 main: 10-26 18:0x main: progress re-scored against milestones registered now from the item's own title (before scoring). (1) exact caller-pinned locks: DONE (planner_probe 127). (2) declared capabilities checked at install: DONE (planner_probe, CapabilitiesNotGranted/grants). (3) index separate from blob storage, signed append-only checkpoint chain: DONE (index_probe 63, fetch_probe 63). (4) detached Ed25519 for releases AND checkpoints: HALF. Releases are verified with real crypto (signed_publish_probe 3, after today's fix of the missing verification, n40). The checkpoint chain verifies only through its injected stub, with no real-Ed25519 checkpoint probe yet. (5) social platforms only as MirrorOnly with a required authoritative digest: DONE (fetch_probe bits 2/4). (6) PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS, a content-addressed store linked by DirectoryJunction: DONE (materialize 15, execute 7, install 7; StoreEntryMutable not yet exercised). (7) per-release editions/numerics as one compile_unit per package, with the compiler holding DefaultEnv per package: compile units DONE in the planner, the compiler change NOT done, so this one isn't counted. (8) real object storage (R2) fetch/publish: NOT done (local store only). Score 5.5 / 8 = 68%, rounded down. Status Active. Next by value: (4) a real-Ed25519 checkpoint probe (the adapter exists; one more probe), then (8).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_44 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 44u32, "2026-10-26T21:58Z", "main", "10-26 17:58 main: Correction to n43: its prose time '18:0x' is wrong. It was written at 17:56 local (its stamp, 21:56Z, is right).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_45 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 45u32, "2026-10-26T22:03Z", "main", "10-26 18:03 main: 10-26 18:03 main: milestone (4) complete, Ed25519 for releases AND checkpoints, GREEN by execution. execute.spi gained ed25519_checkpoint_signature (trusted_key) (checkpoint). Writing it showed a second gap of n40's kind: a verifier that checks the signature only against the key the checkpoint NAMES would accept a fake checkpoint signed with any attacker key. The adapter therefore requires signer_key = the registry's trusted index key BEFORE verifying, over the checkpoint id's hex text. signed_index_probe through the pure index.follow_index_checkpoints: exit 7 = 1 a checkpoint (id 'r', previous 't') signed by the trusted key (RFC 8032 vector 2) extends the chain to 'r' + 2 a tampered signature is IndexSignatureInvalid + 4 a checkpoint (id '') carrying a cryptographically VALID signature by an UNTRUSTED key (RFC 8032 vector 1, empty message) is IndexSignatureInvalid. Milestones now 6 of 8 (1-6 done; 7 the compiler's per-package DefaultEnv and 8 real R2 object storage remain) = 75%.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_46 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 46u32, "2026-10-26T22:23Z", "main", "10-26 18:23 main: 10-26 18:23 main: StoreEntryMutable (modeled in the planner, never exercised) needs primitives lib lacked: file_system had no read-only check or setter (0 matches for readonly/permissions). Packed into the queued junction slice (TEMP lib-fs-junction/apply.ps1, same file and same gate, so no second gate run): file_is_read_only path : bool (one host call, std::fs::metadata(..).map(|m| m.permissions().readonly()).unwrap_or(false)); file_set_read_only (read_only) path : bool (one host call, set_permissions, true when applied); non-Rust backends return false. New Rust test file_read_only_round_trips: a fresh file is writable, locking succeeds, it reads locked, unlocking succeeds, it reads writable, so (false, true, true, true, false). Dry run: 0 new lines over 120. Executor wiring, next: verify_stored locks a blob after Accepted, and create_links refuses with StoreEntryMutable when the stored module is writable. The slice lands after kit's rename (main-serial-fs-junction).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_47 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 47u32, "2026-10-26T22:30Z", "main", "10-26 18:30 main: 10-26 18:30 main: StoreEntryMutable exercised for the first time, GREEN by execution. execute.spi: verify_stored now locks a blob read-only (lib-to-be file_system.file_set_read_only) ONLY when verify_fetched accepts it; new check_store_locked (stored) is Accepted when the entry is read-only, else Rejected (StoreEntryMutable stored). store_lock_probe exit 7 = 1 a stored blob not yet verified is StoreEntryMutable + 2 after verify_stored accepts it the entry is locked and passes + 4 an entry made writable again is StoreEntryMutable. install_probe now unlocks store/b before its tamper step, since the verified file is locked; its rerun follows. The primitives ride the queued junction slice (lib file_system, main-serial-fs-junction).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_48 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 48u32, "2026-10-26T22:39Z", "main", "10-26 18:39 main: 10-26 18:39 main: install_probe rerun GREEN (exit 7) with the store lock in place: 1 both stored modules verify and are locked; 2 plan -> materialize -> junction links install ws/deps/a and ws/deps/b, readable through the junctions; 4 a tampered stored module (unlocked first, so the lock is a real barrier) is BlobDigestMismatch. All 8 executor probes are GREEN by execution: execute 7, hash 15, signing 15, install 7, signed_publish 3, signed_index 7, store_lock 7, plus the publish 127. Probe cleanup now also clears the read-only flag before removal.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_49 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 49u32, "2026-10-26T23:15Z", "main", "10-26 19:15 main: 10-26 19:15 main: registry lab cleaned and the whole suite re-proven on the current files. (1) Nil -> [] everywhere in lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN: 114 sites in 13 files (memory list-sugar-not-cons), 0 Nil/Cons left. (2) Lines over 120 re-laid where they can be split (store_lock_probe record, status_probe's events list and fourth match, signing.spi's Rust macro); the 2 left are 128-char hex literals in signing_probe, which can't be split. (3) Verdicts after both, by execution: C backend (run.ps1) planner 127, plan 63, materialize 15, fetch 63, publish 127, index 63; Rust/Cargo (scratchpad registry-cargo-suite.ps1, serial) signing 15, execute 7, hash 15, install 7, store_lock 7, signed_publish 3, signed_index 7, status 15. All 14 GREEN. This supersedes n48's 'all GREEN' claim, which was written before the non-lock probes were rerun.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_50 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 50u32, "2026-10-27T07:21Z", "main", "10-27 03:21 main: 10-27 03:21 main: both registry lib primitives LANDED in lib (unstaged). fs-junction (directory_create_junction, file_is_read_only, file_set_read_only, with their tests) and crypto-ed25519 (decode_hex, ed25519_verify, RFC 8032 test): gate and land exit 0 for each. The lab executor's execute-run.ps1 still applies them onto its copied lib; once the user commits, that step can be dropped, and the executor runs on plain lib.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_51 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 51u32, "2026-10-27T23:43Z", "main", "10-27 19:43 main: Groom 10-27 19:4x: the next step is blocked only on the user's commit. Both lib primitives landed unstaged on 10-27 03:21 (fs-junction, crypto-ed25519). Once they're committed, lab execute-run.ps1 drops its apply-onto-copied-lib step and the 14-probe suite re-runs on plain lib, as the proof that the design needs no lab-only lib. The rest of the 25% is design text (declared capabilities, the publish flow) and needs no CPU.")
inl social_transport_sim () = Task ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", "X-like simulator as Kino's battle test (user 2026-10-12: deferring it was a bad call; it truly battle-tests Kino): a deterministic, seeded event simulator of many humans/agents publishing Spiral cells as posts (post, reply = downstream cell, quote/link = upstream dependency, edit = new revision, delete, like = demand/interest, unlike = withdrawn demand), with delayed, duplicate, reordered and stale deliveries and cancelled consumers, driving Kino's real compile/run path (warm server, concurrent cells over shared prefixes); invariants checked on every schedule: each visible result equals the sequential re-run of its exact revision, stale revisions never surface, shared prefixes compile once (cache hits counted), withdrawn demand cancels work and frees memory, no cross-request contamination (hopac fix 57 class), equivalent reorderings commute; phase 1: event log format + replay runner + 5 invariants + a 100-cell scenario from lib notebooks; phase 2: scale (10^4 cells), memory ceilings, agent personas; later: transport frames (280-char, twitter-text v3 weighting, NFC) and optional real X/Bluesky/Mastodon mirrors; 2026-10-12 phase 1 built (apps/xsim, untracked, Elixir, path dep on apps/kino): Scenario (cells of any .spi via Kino parse_spi -> posts: definitions as a reply chain, tests replying to the latest definition; seeded likes/unlikes/edits; deliveries shuffled in a window with duplicates), World (idempotent per event index, causal per post+user, replies/edits/likes before their post buffered), Runner (demand-driven: wanted posts -> prefix chain key -> one task per key, cancel on withdrawn demand or edit, results in completion order), Invariants (sequential equivalence vs a fresh baseline, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand hidden, commute across schedules), fault knobs (naive_visibility, cancel: false, rerun_completed, leaking executor) each proven to trip its invariant; mix test 8/8 x5 seeds; mix xsim.run on lib math/date_time/sm' with the fake executor: up to 195 posts, 1,068 deliveries, 104 duplicates, 80 buffered, all invariants ok, commute ok; a leaking executor fails sequential equivalence and commute; next: --executor kino on Kino's new in-memory Notebook.evaluate (agent B), then scale and memory ceilings; 11:55 first runs on real Kino (Notebook.evaluate): two genuine findings, KINO-SOURCE-DIAGNOSTICS (temp paths make outputs nondeterministic) and TEST-PRELUDE-SEMANTICS (tests depend on an earlier static test's open); xsim now models Kino's linear prefixes (tests on the trunk, --branch tests optional) and normalizes temp paths; 12:15 simulator made incremental (prefix key = H(parent key, source), edits re-key only their subtree, the runner only touches posts each delivery changed, refcounted prefix keys, lazy prefix sources): full lib corpus (36 notebooks with tests as interleaved author threads, 1,651 posts, 5,916 deliveries, 529 duplicates, 4 edits + 2 breaking edits per thread) 626.5 s -> 17.5 s simulator time with the fake executor, ~linear (228/535/1,651 posts: 2.3/5.1/17.5 s), every invariant and commute ok; tests 10/10 (new: root edit re-keys the subtree); multi-notebook corpus (--spi a,b,...) and --breaking edits added; 12:25 transport frames: Xsim.Frames splits a cell into numbered frames within 280 weighted characters (twitter-text v3 ranges: Latin weight 1, CJK/Hangul 2; NFC; split at grapheme boundaries; header \"<post> k/n\"), World buffers frames per post and applies the rebuilt post at its earliest frame index once complete (parent missing -> pending), --frames in mix xsim.run; tests 12/12 (Hangul cell -> several frames, each <= 280, shuffled rebuild exact; framed corpus with 30% duplicates and a 20-wide shuffle window keeps every invariant and commutes with the unframed run); 12:20 real-Kino finding KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE; 12:40 --churn N (N edits cycling the root of each thread, woven through the stream: agent-rewrite storms re-key every prefix and cancel in-flight work) exposed an over-strict invariant, not a runner bug: cancelled-then-restarted prefixes counted as double compiles (14 cancels, 5 keys restarted); one_compile_per_prefix is now starts - cancellations <= 1 per key (the rerun_completed fault still trips it); --save/--replay delivery logs (JSON lines) for exact bug reproduction; full lib corpus with frames: 1,651 posts -> 6,643 events, ~8,000 deliveries incl. ~1,330 duplicates, all invariants + commute ok in 20-24 s per schedule; tests 14/14; 12:50 phase-2 scale reached with the fake executor: lib corpus x6 as 216 author threads (--spi-file, since mix.bat caps the command line at 8,191 chars), 9,906 posts -> 40,585 events -> 46,604 deliveries incl. 6,019 duplicates, all framed, with edits, breaking edits and root churn: every invariant + commute ok, 118.9 s simulator time, peak BEAM 110 MB, 300 s wall incl. the sequential baseline; next: real-Kino scenarios at growing size, memory ceilings of the daemon side, agent personas; 13:20 in CI (uncommitted): apps/xsim/build.ps1 (mix test; the framed lib corpus with edits, breaking edits and churn over 2 schedules + commute; unless -fast a cold 2-concurrent real-Kino scenario that guards KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE, then the daemon is stopped) runs from spiral scripts/build.ps1 after the lib notebooks; local -fast run 190 s wall incl. deps and compile; 14:20 semantic oracle added after the KINO-C-FALSE-PASS report: breaking edits are known-wrong revisions, so on the real Kino path any visible ok result for a post whose current revision is a breaking edit is a violation (breaking_edits_fail); a lenient executor that passes everything is caught, an honest one is not (test); the concurrent-vs-sequential invariants alone cannot see a false pass because both sides are wrong the same way; tests 15/15; 18:53: CI run 38312827285 (spiral 17:47 commit) failed in apps/xsim mix test: the churn test asserted map_size(results) > 6, which depends on timing (a fast runner cancels every stale revision, leaving exactly 6); replaced with a deterministic check (the root ends at the last churn edit); 15/15 pass on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42, 1234; uncommitted; 2026-10-14 01:14 agent personas (uncommitted): Xsim.Personas, mix xsim.run --personas lurker=N,fickle=N,editor=N,breaker=N; lurkers like about half the posts, fickle users toggle like/unlike 5 times on one post (withdrawn demand, cancel and restart), editors rewrite one definition 3 times and then revert to the original source (ABA: the prefix key returns to an earlier value), breakers push a breaking test edit and then fix it back (the broken source joins scenario.broken); persona streams interleave round-robin and weave with churn; fixed an off-by-one: --edits 0 produced one edit; tests 25/25 on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42 (new: 4 schedules with 30% duplicates and a 10-wide window, every invariant, breaking_edits_fail with an honest executor, every post ends at its original source, the schedules commute; an unknown persona raises); full lib corpus (36 notebooks) with personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: 1,662 posts, 8,467 events, about 9,730 deliveries, 5,690-5,745 prefixes, 47-54 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 17.5-21.6 s per schedule, peak BEAM 70 MB; next: the real-Kino size ladder with daemon memory ceilings (script ready at the job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: isolated port 13977, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP), waiting for the heavy gate; 2026-10-14 02:04 real-Kino size ladder (job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: its own daemon on port 13977 with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN=3, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP, users 2, 2 schedules, concurrency 2, through the heavy gate): math.spi 3 cells: 3 prefixes, 52 s cold + 10 s, daemon tree peak 1,464 MB; 6 cells: 5 prefixes, 69-101 s + 22-34 s, 1,735-1,858 MB; 12 cells: 10 prefixes, 201 s + 103 s, 2,353 MB (one pool compiler reached 1,116 MB); 8 cells with --memo and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1: 8 prefixes, 43.8 s then 0.8 s (memo hits 15 then 43 cumulative, misses 13), 2,331 MB, every invariant and commute ok; daemon tree = erl 58 MB + 3 pool compilers (dotnet 374-1,116 MB) + 3 pwsh compiler_bridge relays at 101-114 MB each (about 320 MB, filed as KINO-BRIDGE-OVERHEAD); genuine finding: the 6-cell rung failed sequential equivalence and commute only because a failing Rust cell prints the OS thread id of its panic (thread '<unnamed>' (52388) panicked), so two runs of one revision differ; xsim's Invariants.comparable now normalizes the id as it did temp paths (test added, 26/26), rerun passes; Kino-side normalization filed as KINO-PANIC-THREAD-ID; apps/xsim/build.ps1's CI run now passes --personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 (that exact command: pass, 102 s wall); 2026-10-14 02:17 phase-2 scale with personas (fake executor): lib corpus x6 (216 author threads) with frames, edits, breaking edits, churn and personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 per thread: 9,972 posts -> 50,647 events -> about 58,160 deliveries incl. about 7,510 duplicates, 22,638-22,888 prefixes, 63-64 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 89-93 s per schedule, peak BEAM 147 MB, 467 s wall; xsim tests 26/26 on seeds 0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026; daemon-side ceiling observed on the real path: each pool compiler recycles past about 1.2 GB (logged at 1,228-1,318 MB, KINO-DAEMON-MEMORY policy), so with 3 compilers plus 3 pwsh relays and erl the daemon tree is bounded near 4.3 GB; 2026-10-14 02:21 full math.spi on real Kino with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 22 posts, 80 events, about 92 deliveries, 21 prefixes, 2-3 cancels, schedule 1 291 s then schedule 2 5.2 s (memo hits 179 then 390 cumulative, misses 32), daemon tree peak 3,115 MB (compilers 788/803/1,087 MB, 3 recycles at 1,228-1,318 MB), 32.7 min wall including the sequential baselines; sequential equivalence, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand and commute all ok, but breaking_edits_fail flagged f0p4: an xsim oracle bug, not a Kino false pass: the breaking edit bumped the last digit run, turning 7.3890560989306495 into 7.3890560989306496 (the same f64, and inside _assert_approx_eq's tolerance), so the edit was not breaking; Scenario.break_source now bumps the whole literal's integer part (7.389... -> 8.389..., 9 -> 10), shared by --breaking and the breaker persona, with a test (27/27 on seeds 0, 1, 42); 2026-10-14 02:48 fix verified on real Kino: math.spi 5 cells with --breaking 1 (the edit now turns the f0p4 test's 7.3890560989306495 into 8.3890560989306495) on its own daemon: 5 prefixes, 53 s + 18 s, every invariant ok including breaking_edits_fail (the cell now fails honestly), commute ok, daemon tree 1,796 MB; xsim tests 27/27 on 69 seeds (0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026 and 100-159); 2026-10-14 03:00 the --edits 0 fix changes build.ps1's cold real-Kino race guard (math.spi 3 cells, users 2, edits 0, 1 schedule, concurrency 2) from 8 to 7 events; that exact scenario on a cold isolated daemon still passes with peak_inflight=2 (3 prefixes, 32.7 s, every invariant ok, daemon tree 1,226 MB, 47.8 s wall); churn-heavy persona stress over math+date_time+sm' (319 posts, 2,000+ events, 40-50% duplicates, churn 6-12, fickle 6-8, breaker 4-6) passes on seeds 1-16 at concurrency 1, 3 and 8; 2026-10-14 03:26 simulator-side memory ceiling: Runner evict: true / mix xsim.run --evict frees a prefix's result when its last demand goes (withdrawn like, edit, re-key), recomputes on renewed demand, counts evictions in one_compile_per_prefix (starts - cancels - evictions <= 1) and adds the invariant unwanted_results_freed (every held result belongs to a wanted post); summary gains peak_results and evictions; full lib corpus with personas, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: results held 1,608 instead of about 5,700 (-72%), about 4,000 evictions per schedule, starts 5,657-5,689 vs 5,744-5,792 kept (no extra compiles at this scale), every invariant and commute ok; on math+date_time+sm' with churn 6: 316 held instead of 1,548-1,599 at +1.4-4% starts; tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 (new: eviction commutes with keeping, never holds more results, evictions happen); the default stays off so earlier numbers compare; 2026-10-14 03:37 second notebook on real Kino: date_time.spi 20 cells with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 20 posts, 70 events, about 80 deliveries, 20 prefixes, schedule 1 94.5 s then 3.1 s (memo hits 131 then 306 cumulative, misses 44), every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail and commute ok, daemon tree peak 2,181 MB (one compiler at 1,190 MB, 5 recycles in the daemon log), 11 min wall; my daemon port 13977 is free again; next: multi-notebook real-Kino corpora (math+date_time interleaved threads) and --evict against Kino's memo; 2026-10-14 03:39 eviction at phase-2 scale (lib corpus x6, same options as the 9,972-post run): results held 8,232 instead of about 22,700 (-64%), but 24,250-24,798 starts instead of 22,701-22,952 (+7-8% recomputes from renewed demand after churn and fickle toggles), 148-188 s per schedule instead of 89-93 s, peak BEAM 133 vs 147 MB; every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and commute ok; so dropping results on withdrawn demand trades about 8% more compiles for a bounded result store, which is the policy question for Kino's memo (keep, evict, or evict with a grace window); 2026-10-14 03:44 correction: the 4.3 GB daemon-tree bound above is an estimate from the recycle threshold (3 compilers at about 1.3 GB, relays, erl), not a measurement; the highest measured peak is 3,115 MB (full math.spi); 2026-10-14 04:09 first multi-notebook real-Kino run (math.spi + date_time.spi, 10 cells each, as two interleaved author threads via --spi-file; a comma list through mix.bat had silently kept only math): 20 posts, 91 events, about 105 deliveries, 16 prefixes, with --memo, --evict and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: schedule 1 224 s then 21 s (memo hits 52 then 121 cumulative, misses 18-19), 0 evictions needed, every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and breaking_edits_fail ok, commute ok; daemon tree peak 3,398 MB (compilers 877/981/1,104 MB), the highest measured so far; 16.6 min wall; evict stress on 3 notebooks passes on seeds 20-35 at concurrency 1 and 6; xsim tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 and 200-259; next: a daemon-memory sampler in xsim itself (today the ladder script reads the tree after the run), larger multi-notebook corpora on real Kino when the gate is quiet, and Kino's memo policy under eviction; 2026-10-14 04:14 final code checked as CI runs it: pwsh apps/xsim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (mix test, then the personas corpus run) exit 0 in 117 s: 1,662 posts, about 9,730 deliveries, every invariant and commute ok; 2026-10-14 04:26 review fixes: --breaking and the breaker persona now only pick test cells whose source Scenario.break_source actually changes (a tail like pair.1 was picked, left unchanged and then flagged as a false breaking_edits_fail violation), and the fickle persona returns no events on an empty thread instead of crashing; test added, 29/29 on seeds 0-19", Epic, P0, D7, 100u32, Done, "", "social,simulator,cells,stress,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_social_transport_sim () = Planning ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl language_name () = Task ("LANGUAGE-NAME", "Pick the edition-3 language name once SPIRAL3-EDITIONS parses real programs (votes 2026-10-12: Espira 835, Spile 790, Geandra 610 - Geandra as a release codename); prefer a name that keeps .spi/package.spiproj; run a real clearance search before announcing; user decision 2026-10-12 18:53: decide later; keep 'Spiral 3' / 'edition 3' until it parses real programs and a clearance search is done", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "naming,edition", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_language_name () = Planning ("LANGUAGE-NAME", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
