inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_3 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:42 helper-lane lap 1: recount: apps/compiler/tmp/samples has 867 dirs (849 on 10-15, so still growing while SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS sits at Active 5%); dep TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS is Done, so only SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS gates this. Compaction input found on the way: the accepted oracle (EXPECTED.tsv, 1,178 ids) holds 19 samples/ ids with no folder anywhere in the repo (native_layout_* x16, native_fptr_basic, native_fptr_reuse, native_closure_managed_branch; only NEXT.md still names two of them), i.e. accept keeps rows of deleted samples forever; and samples/contract_fsi_adversarial is an empty untracked dir. Both belong to the first cleanup pass by criteria (details go to SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS).")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_4 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:28 helper-lane lap 2: compaction work list by family (first underscore segment of the 867 sample dirs): contract 332, native 85, closure 48, array 46, heap 46, union 34, ebpf 31, dynamic 25, method 21, managed 15, abi 15, mut 15, ts 14, frontier 13, rust 11, wgsl 9, recursive 6, multiline 6, plus 56 smaller families (74 in all, 34 of them single samples). contract (38 percent of all dirs) is the family CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG is measuring right now, and ebpf (31) is the family SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS starts with, so both big cleanups already have their first slice in flight; the 34 singleton families are the cheapest to fold into test blocks next to an existing family.")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_5 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:03 helper-lane lap 3: ranked the families by signal per second from the fast-tier fold cache (spiral-bin/fast-tier-fold/single-flight-0.2-7.json, 8,527 rows mined over the single-flight runs up to 10-17; signal = residual changes + 2 x outcome flips, cost = median row seconds); table in lab/SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP/family-signal.tsv. Bottom (compaction first): contract/contracts rows 944 with signal 4 in total and 2,445 s of median cost (the largest cost of any family, 2 flip histories), then c 1.1/s, mega 1.6/s, folder 2.4/s; the mid band (array, heap, union, closure, method, mut: 3.0-3.6/s) is the bulk of rows but cheap; top: tagged 15.0/s, recursive 12.3, managed 11.1, portable 10.4, rust 9.3, dynamic 9.1. So the contracts suite carries almost no development signal for its cost, which supports CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG's switch (faster native) and argues for compacting contract samples into fewer typed-negative test blocks next; high-signal families stay as they are.")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_6 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: first compaction cut listed (lab/SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP/duplicate-negatives.tsv): of the 251 ExpectedErrors pins, 95 samples fall into 34 groups that pin the identical Contains text (largest groups 7, 6, 6, 4, 4, 4, 4 - nearly all mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency negatives on exact_linear_indexed terms, plus mega_lean_cic sort mismatches and mega_zeta child-accessibility mismatches); 236 of the 251 pins are mega-family negatives. Samples whose first error text is identical prove the same rejection at the type checker, so each group can shrink to one representative row or become one typed-negative test block that lists the variants, about 61 rows fewer before looking at error locations. Caveat: equal texts can still come from different source sites; the review that drops rows should compare the error spans too.")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_7 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 7u32, "2026-10-19T22:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:26 helper-lane lap 6: correction to lap 4's compaction cut. The 34 groups of identical pins are not proof of identical rejections. Every pin in lab/SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP/duplicate-negatives.tsv, and 205 ExpectedErrors pins in matrix.psd1 overall, stop at Expected: with nothing after it. So two negatives with the same Got side but different Expected types share one pin. Shrinking a group to one representative could therefore drop a distinct rejection. The safer first cut is the opposite: make those pins discriminating by carrying the first Expected line, then re-group, and only collapse what is still identical. Matrix inputs are main's, so this would be a lab script that reads each negative's current first error from a review and proposes the longer pins.")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_8 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 8u32, "2026-10-22T06:37Z", "main", "02:37 main: lab drained. duplicate-negatives.tsv and family-signal.tsv (the 10-19 lap analyses quoted in notes 4 and 6) moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP (sha256 checked). Folder deleted; the item itself is unchanged (Planned).")
inl note_of_samples_out_of_tmp_9 () = Note ("SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:04 (subagent): priced the duplicate-negative cut from the fold cache: 60 removable rows with cost data, about 46 s of median compile time per full suite (mega negatives are cheap; the win is fewer rows to review, not time) -> next: span comparison per group before any removal | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: discriminating-pin plan recorded -> next: lab script proposing longer pins")
inl cross_backend_benchmark () = Task ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", "Performance modes and a cross-backend benchmark (in-session task since 2026-10-07, never a backlog item): the same programs timed on every backend that runs them (C as baseline; Rust, Zig, Wasm, Kotlin, Swift, Lean, Gleam, Lua, Bend, WGSL), optimization modes per backend, results as a table in a published page; feeds ZIG-HUB-SPEED and DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", Feature, P1, D5, 70u32, Paused, "", "backends,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cross_backend_benchmark () = Planning ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_1 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:52Z", "main-thread", "08:52 design: reuse the harness's per-backend build and run logic, with no separate bench runner. A staged patch (job tmp/rotation/patches/run_ms.ps1, all 5 anchors dry-run clean, applied after the current bless) adds results.tsv run_ms, the last native process of a job: the program run for compiled backends, elaboration plus run for interpreters. The benchmark is then a quiet '-Filter' run over the megas, runtime_bench and cube with -Parallel 1, repeated 3 times, and a table of median run_ms per backend vs C, published as a page. A first rough cut (native_ms, mixed build+run, loaded) is on ZIG-HUB-SPEED.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_2 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:25Z", "main-thread", "17:25 run_ms is in results.tsv (installed today). First table from single-flight-20261014-164608: median run_ms vs C on the same rows: Zig 0.96 (145 rows, sum 11.1 s vs C 10.8 s), Rust 1.16 (409), Swift 1.54 (248), Wasm 2.66 (149), Lua 4.07 (137), Kotlin 9.4 (248, JVM start), Bend 28 (344), Lean 89 (137, lean --run elaboration). The quiet mega/cube/bench pass (-Parallel 1, 3 repeats) is the next step toward the published page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_3 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:49Z", "main-thread", "17:49 method fixed: suite run_ms is too noisy under load, so time the built binaries directly (min of 7, Process.WaitForExit; PowerShell Start-Process -Wait adds a ~1 s floor). First clean table: min of 7 direct runs of the harness-built binaries (System.Diagnostics.Process, every exit correct; C gcc as built by the harness, Zig ReleaseFast, Rust release), ms C/Zig/Rust: brzozowski runtime_bench 333/87/346, indexed_dfa 78/30/79, inventory_dfa 14/13/27, runtime_native 13/11/14, lean_cic 11/11/11, omniledger 18/10/14, spiral_proves 16/13/19, zeta 15/13/22, cube 14/10/18. Next: extend to Swift/Lua/Kotlin/Wasm/Lean/Bend binaries or runners, then the published page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_4 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:47Z", "main-thread", "06:47 groom: input added 10-15: compiled F# baseline (Release exe, min of 7): runtime_bench C 266 / Zig 63 / F# 319 ms, indexed_dfa 53 / 23 / 170 ms (job tmp/fsbase). Method settled (direct Process timing, not suite run_ms). Next grain: put the 9-program table into a script with the F# column.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_5 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:52Z", "main-thread", "06:52 groom caveat: the 10-15 F#/C/Zig baseline (tmp/fsbase) built Zig with scoop/apps/zig/current = stable 0.17.0, not the harness's zig-dev. The Zig-vs-C ratio matches the harness-built 10-14 numbers (runtime_bench 0.24x, indexed_dfa 0.43x), so the conclusion holds. A scripted rerun should pin the harness's zig-dev.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_6 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: new apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/bench-native.ps1 (uncommitted) times harness-built natives directly, min of N, exit checked against C; quiet run on 9 programs all match C.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_7 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (samples alpha705, Sep 2026): the fresh-pair protocol that finally gave honest A/B numbers: a unique graph identity for each side, session teardown before EVERY measured compile (two different graphs in one session degraded 4.5 s -> 12.7 s whichever went second), both orders, and a symmetric cache nonce on both sides (a predecessor SHA that hit the cache made a candidate look +4.7% while fresh-vs-fresh was -14%). Run compiler-family jobs serially (4 concurrent supervisors timed out) and parallelize inside them. Record workload cardinality (active_calls) and the runtime profile (one-shot FSI vs a persistent daemon differed enough to straddle a 10 s gate) in each receipt.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_8 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:08Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:08 helper-lane lap 1: bench-native.ps1 (committed in dec17e05) timed only main.exe dirs (C, Delphi, Rust, Zig). Extended it (uncommitted) with interpreted runners: Wasm via wasmtime run -W max-wasm-stack over samples/<s>/main.wat and Lua via tests/native-shims/run_main.lua, same min-of-N Process timing, exit checked against C; parse-checked. HEAVY QUEUE: C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab\\CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK\\run.ps1 (9-program table with Wasm and Lua columns into lab tsv, ~5-8 min). Next: Kotlin (java -cp classes MainKt) and Fsharp (dotnet fsi) runners, then the published page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_9 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:01 helper-lane lap 2: bench-native.ps1 (uncommitted) now also times Kotlin (java -cp kotlin-stdlib.jar;classes MainKt from the native dir's classes, stdlib beside the resolved kotlinc) and Swift (the native main.exe with SwiftRuntime prepended to PATH; it was silently failing before, swiftCore.dll not found), on top of lap 1's Wasm and Lua; interpreted runs carry PathPrefix null (env.ps1 is strict). Smoke via Invoke-Heavy, native_cube_direct, 1 run each, every exit 42 = C: C 18.1 ms, Rust 9.7, Zig 6.7, Swift 19.3, Delphi 31.2, Wasm 57.5, Lua 62.6, Kotlin 165.5 (JVM start). Single runs only, not the table; the queued lab run.ps1 (min of 5 over 9 programs) now picks up all 8 columns.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_10 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:45 helper-lane lap 3: ran the table myself (it took 38 s under Invoke-Heavy, far below the queued estimate; queue row marked done) and published it: https://claude.ai/artifact/AKLHUKRWhc4QKuU6491ota (private page, Spiral Backend Run Times). Min of 5, 9 programs, every exit equal to C. Median ratio to C: Zig 0.77 (9 of 9), Rust 0.82 (9), Swift 1.59 (1, only one native Swift build), Delphi 2.14 (9), Wasm 3.48 (8), Lua 8.27 (8), Kotlin 20.0 (9, JVM start included). runtime_bench: C 159 ms, Zig 63, Rust 212; cube: C 14.5, Rust 8.2, Zig 6.6. Data: lab/CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK/bench-runs5.tsv. Still missing for the title: Lean, Gleam, Bend and WGSL columns (interpreters with their own runners), the F# compiled column, and optimization modes per backend. Progress 40 -> 70.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_11 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:29 helper-lane lap 4: bench-native.ps1 (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts, uncommitted, parse-checked) gains three interpreted columns exactly as test.ps1 runs them: Wgsl (the spiral-wgsl runner on main.wgsl), Lean (lean +leanprover/lean4:v4.35.0-rc4 --run main.lean, so the time includes elaboration, about 2 s) and Bend (bun bend2/main.ts main.bend with BEND_NO_TELEMETRY and BUN_JSC_maxPerThreadStackUsage=16777216, run from the bend2 folder); Measure-FastestRun takes an optional environment table for that. Gleam stays out for now: it needs a built gleam project (the Cells warm package or the harness shim project) rather than one file. Not run (core-switch freeze); HEAVY QUEUE row added, then republish the same artifact page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_12 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:49 helper-lane lap 5: bench-native.ps1 also gets the last two interpreted columns, mirroring test.ps1's native tier: Python (run_main.py shim, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, SPIRAL_CUDA=0) and Gleam (the harness shim project copied once to <cache>/bench-gleam, main.gleam copied in, gleam build outside the timed region, then erl -noshell -pa <every build/dev/erlang/*/ebin> -eval spiral_exit:halt(main:main()). is what gets timed, so BEAM start-up is included but compilation is not). Parse-checked; the queued run.ps1 row now yields all columns except F# (fsi start dominates) and Calyx/OpenQasm (simulators).")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_13 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 13u32, "2026-10-22T06:36Z", "main", "02:36 main: lab drained. The 3 bench outputs (bench-20261019-1022.tsv, bench-runs1.tsv, bench-runs5.tsv) moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK (sha256 checked). run.ps1 was a one-line wrapper and is dropped; its command is: . apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1; Invoke-Heavy -Label 'bench-native' { pwsh -NoProfile -File apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/bench-native.ps1 -Runs 5 -Out <run cache>/bench-<stamp>.tsv }. bench-native.ps1 (in the tree since 10-19, with the Wgsl, Lean, Bend, Python and Gleam columns) is the tool. The next full run writes into the run cache.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_14 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 14u32, "2026-10-22T16:58Z", "main", "2026-10-22 12:58 main: scheduling rule. The next bench-native.ps1 -Runs 5 run goes after PACK-NEXT-2 is accepted (so the Rust concat fusion, or-join v2 and WGSL sub-word numbers are in it) and only on a box with no other lane's heavy or compile window, since timings under load mislead (heavy-gate-lessons). Output goes to the run cache, then the published page is updated. Not today before tibia's live block (about 13:40-14:45).")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_15 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 15u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 18:26 (subagent): bench run not started for the same reason (timings under load are noise) -> next: idle window: run.ps1 with Wgsl/Lean/Bend columns | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: still no idle window (pack 11 review + eoie Cells runs hold both gate slots for 20+ min) -> next: idle window")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_16 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 16u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
