inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: the D16 chain table source still exists (job tmp/hopacsota-p0/r3_simulate.py, 13 KB); R4 measured 636 deferred methods + 579 closures on apps/spiral with max queue 1,215, the fork points P3 shortens.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:17 inherited from HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH: the R3 instrumentation (lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH/r3_instrument.ps1 + r3_simulate.py) no longer applies since pack-1v moved its PartEval anchor behind PartEvalSeams; re-home it into PartEvalSeamsHopacsota (it sees every deferred body) before measuring the cutoff.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 lap 2 grain: blocked on P2's engine (no worklist trace hook yet); inputs ready: lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH/r3_instrument.ps1 + r3_simulate.py and PLAN.md's LF result (longest remaining chain first within 4 percent) Next: -")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: still blocked on the P2 engine slice; new tool for when it lands: packs can now be gated with pair-drift -Tracked on a candidate copy and reviewed on a private Debug build, so P2/P3 code is measured without touching the shared DLL Next: wait for P2")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:16 main: the R3 instrumentation is re-homed, since the old r3_instrument.ps1 and r3_simulate.py are gone with the drained lab. %TEMP%\\hopacsota-census\\census.ps1 patches PartEvalSeamsHopacsota's Session; it's inert unless SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT is set, and works under the queue policies (dfs, bfs, random), where a deferred body runs only after its parent returns, so its measured run time is its self time. Each body gets an id, its parent (the body running when Defer was called; 0 is the root), its spawn offset (the time since the parent's start) and its self time. The root is a row too, and the file is appended when the session ends. Timing uses System.TimeProvider.System.GetTimestamp (high resolution; lint G2 bans only the Stopwatch and DateTime tokens; the identifiers avoid G2's record/replay words). Lint: G1 619 lines, G2 0 hits, parse clean. simulate.py does list scheduling over the spawn tree (a child is ready at its parent's start plus its offset) and gives the critical path, the total/critical bound and simulated speedups at 2, 4 and 8 workers; checked on a hand-computed tree (4.3 ms path, 3.49x bound, reached at 4 workers). Limits of v1: only spawn edges, so claim waits are not edges yet, which makes it an upper bound. Rides pack-next8 (core BF7EE0AD76C18036). Measure after it builds: SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=bfs plus the census on the 5 mega roots and apps/spiral, which gives P3's starting table.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 1 08:26 (main): inherited the R3 instrumentation re-home (lab/HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH scripts, anchor moved behind PartEvalSeams) from the closed research item -> next: re-home r3_instrument.ps1 into PartEvalSeamsHopacsota once P2's engine exists")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 9u32, "2026-10-28T07:10Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: P3 build-free start (P2 done, unblocks P3). Read PartEvalSeams.Session.Defer (:54036): each deferred body allocates a child ResizeArray + a \"globals\" marker + Children registration + a globals append + with_globals env + a body closure -- a FIXED per-deferral overhead paid for EVERY type-known body under a deferring policy. At w1 (my measure 206s vs single-flight 86s) no parallelism amortizes it -> the ~2.4x base cost IS this overhead. So the granularity cutoff (P3 core) = defer a body only if its census self-time > this per-deferral cost; inline cheaper ones. The census hook is already built into the Session (:54024 census_out, SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT), so no patch -- the existing P2-composite hopacsota DLL (AADA8236) works. NEXT (build-bound run): SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_CENSUS_OUT + bfs on the 5 mega roots + apps/spiral -> self-time table -> set the cutoff + the D16 span-shortening fork points.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 10u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 attribution RULE (pre-measure, main steer): target = the w1 SERIAL gap (206s vs 86s = 2.4x), NOT granularity (w8 only 0.70-0.83x w1, so parallelism isnt the gap). Profile w1 hopacsota vs single-flight on spiral.spi; attribute the ~120s delta across (A) per-body deferral alloc (Job/Ch/Session; engine bodies/spawned counts), (B) D4 wait-for (engine bridges/waits), (C) per-key globals merge (Session.Finish), (D) GC/alloc + MVar contention (sampled profile). RULE: lever = the largest single contributor >=40% of the delta; top two each 20-40% -> take both; ignore <15%. Engine stats first; add a dotnet-trace sampled profile if counts dont localize the TIME. Record attribution BEFORE picking the lever.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 11u32, "2026-10-28T09:54Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 w1-profile (node job-1793180917498): SF 120.2s, w1 hopacsota 180.2s, delta 60s (node under load; box was 206/86). Engine stats (LOAD-INDEPENDENT): bodies=1515, claims=372147, ready_hits=325376, bridges=80862, waits=0, conflicts=0, cycles=0, abandoned=0. RULED OUT at w1: bucket B (D4 wait-for)=0 (waits=0); MVar contention=0 (1 worker, conflicts=0). Overhead = the PER-OPERATION machinery: ~372k family/memo claims + ~325k ready-hits + ~80k Hopac Ch bridges, vs only 1515 bodies (bucket A per-body modest). At w1 every deferred eval still crosses the Ch worklist (pure overhead, no parallelism). PRELIM lever: a single-worker fast-path that skips the Ch bridge + direct-calls the claim path. NEXT: dotnet-trace sampled profile for the TIME split (Ch.give/take vs memo/family claim vs globals merge) to apply the >=40% rule.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 12u32, "2026-10-28T09:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 lever rule REFINED (main): DONE RULE = w8 vs single-flight (NOT w1) -- a w1-only bridge-skip cant earn P6 promotion (needs w4/w8 to beat SF, which still pay every claim+bridge). Lever must cut per-op cost at EVERY worker count. Candidates: (1) READY-HIT INLINE -- resolve a claim inline when immediately ready, no Ch round-trip (325k of 372k claims are ready-hits = 87%); (2) batch claims per body; (3) memoize family lookup per key. The dotnet-trace TIME split picks which per-op cost is biggest -> the lever. Measure EACH candidate at BOTH w1 AND w8; drop any that helps w1 but not w8.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 13u32, "2026-10-28T10:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 engine-code CONFIRMS mains arithmetic (Claim :53878-53906): a ready-hit resolves INLINE (ctx.Ready/published TryGetValue -> ValueSome, NO bridge); only the slow settle path (:53896) bridges. So 325k ready-hits DONT bridge; 80k bridges = ~47k non-ready claims + Family/Fill/Announce. ready-hit-inline lever MOOT. Real per-op overhead: EVERY claim (372k) does let id = box (table, key) (object ALLOCATION) + 1-2 dict lookups (Ready, published) -> 372k boxings+lookups at ALL N. Refined levers: (A) kill the per-claim box alloc (372k GC pressure, all N) via a struct/cached key; (B) settle/Ch bridge (80k). dotnet-trace TIME split picks A vs B (not installed on node; install or probe.ps1 -Profile + profile-thread.py, both present).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_14 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:04Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 profiling setup: probe.ps1 -Profile is SAMPLES-only + shells to dotnet-trace via Get-DiagTool (:56, not installed -> auto-installs). BUT profile spiral.spi (machinery-exerciser: 1515 bodies, 372k claims), NOT a mega root -- mega roots are ~0-join-point (MEGA-PARALLEL n1), barely defer, wouldnt exercise Claim/box/Ch. So: install dotnet-trace + profile the spiral.spi w1 compile DIRECTLY (dotnet-trace collect -p <pid> --profile dotnet-sampled-thread-time --format Speedscope) -> profile-summary.py -> frames -> split box(table,key)/GC (lever A) vs Ch give/take (lever B). Gated on a quieter node window (clean frames; node ~99% CPU). Then prototype the winner, measure w1+w8 vs the w8-vs-SF done rule.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_15 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 15u32, "2026-10-28T10:20Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 bridge machinery (Engine :53786-53827): bridge = ctx.Bridges++ then Scheduler.run control op -- a SYNCHRONOUS hop onto the CONTROL scheduler (NumWorkers=1 :53775). ask = bridge + MVar.modifyFun on the Table guard. settle (non-ready claim path :53822) = ask ClaimOp. So the 80k bridges each = Scheduler.run through ONE control worker + an MVar modify -> the 1-worker control scheduler SERIALIZES all table ops. At w8, 8 body threads funnel table ops through 1 control worker = an Amdahl serial fraction = the likely reason hopacsota cant beat SF at w8 (invisible at w1). Lever B (batch/reduce bridges, or widen control) raises the w8 ceiling AND cuts per-op at all N. Lever A (372k box Tuple allocs) is per-op alloc only. Profile frames: Tuple..ctor/GC=>A; Scheduler.run/Hopac/MVar=>B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_16 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 16u32, "2026-10-28T10:24Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 DONE RULE for any control-path lever (main): re-run P2s FULL determinism gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = the single-flight hash), NOT just the w8 speed check -- the 1-worker control scheduler (:53775) + single Table guard MVar (:53780, all ask/settle do MVar.modifyFun guard) may be WHAT makes per-key merges + claim decisions linearizable (deterministic); naively widening control could reopen the nondeterminism P2 closed. PREFER levers that do LESS control work while KEEPING it serial: (a) resolve more claims without settle (fewer bridges), (b) batch several ops per hop, (c) SHARD the Table/guard so independent keys dont share one guard (per-key serial -> still linearizable, cross-key parallel). TWO serial points: the 1-worker control SCHEDULER and the single guard MVar; the 2-point measurement (bridges/sec + control busy% at w1 vs w8, alternated; ~100% busy at w8 = the ceiling) says which saturates first.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_17 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 17u32, "2026-10-28T10:36Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 STATE (groom): Active, measurement stage (was mis-marked 0 Planned). Serial gap attributed (per-op claim/bridge machinery; wait-for=0). w1 profile: machinery NOT CPU-hot (compiler frames dominate) -> box-alloc lever A ruled OUT as CPU-dominant. bridge = Scheduler.run through a 1-worker control scheduler + MVar (lever B) is the likely cost AND the w8 Amdahl cap. Determinism gate: any control-path lever re-runs P2s full gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = SF hash). NEXT: contention job (SF/w1/w8 wall+CPU+bridges) decides bridge-latency vs redundant-work + the ceiling; then pick + prototype a determinism-safe lever (fewer bridges / batch per hop / shard the table guard). See notes 10-16.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_18 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 18u32, "2026-10-28T10:53Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 CONTENTION RESULT (clean, job-1793184486226): SF wall 26.5s cpu 42s; w1 hopacsota wall 26.4s cpu 43.6s (== SF! the earlier 120/180 +60s was NODE LOAD, not real -- w1 has NO overhead); w8 wall 30.8s cpu 76s (SLOWER than SF + ~1.8x CPU), bridges 103k vs w1 80k. bridges/sec w8/w1 = 1.1 (control worker SATURATED: 8 workers dont raise throughput); wall speedup w1->w8 = 0.86x (w8 slower than w1). DIAGNOSIS: the 1-worker control scheduler + single guard MVar cap throughput (Amdahl) AND w8 adds contention (more bridges, ~1.8x CPU, no speedup). LEVER = SHARD the Table guard (determinism-safe): per-shard serial -> still linearizable, independent keys parallel -> raises bridges/sec + cuts contention bridges. Done rule: w8 beats SF AND P2 full det gate (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = SF hash). Prototype next.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p3_19 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P3", 19u32, "2026-10-28T11:02Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 P3 w8 STATS (job-...486226) confirm SHARD. w1: ready_hits=325376 bridges=80862 waits=0 conflicts=0. w8: ready_hits=306234 (-19k) bridges=103002 (+22k) waits=903 conflicts=0. w8 slowdown is NOT key contention (waits=903 = 0.24% of claims, conflicts 0, DAG highly parallel) -- it is LOST READY-HITS: parallelism claims ~19k keys before their value is computed -> miss cache -> settle -> +22k bridges that serialize on the 1-worker control (bridges/sec capped ~3300). So the control IS the bottleneck + the DAG has the parallelism to beat SF. SHARD design (vs real Table :53637): shard entries by stableHash(id)%N (pure fn of id); per-shard named/published/conflicts/abandoned; widen control so per-shard Decides run parallel; per-shard markers re-ordered depth-first by Session.Finish (tags dont leak to output); graph (cross-key waits) stays global 1-worker (rare). Done rule: w8 beats SF + P2 full det gate. Prototype at the 11:30 box slot.")
