inl note_of_eoie_spiral_tests_21 () = Note ("EOIE-SPIRAL-TESTS", 21u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl eoie_style_checkpoint () = Task ("EOIE-STYLE-CHECKPOINT", "Decide eoie's restart style from Tibia evidence, not before (user 2026-10-14 06:44: we still don't know if the tibia style will pay off, and it is advancing without the 1k loc restrictions); compare the two experiments on the same measures, read from git and the backlog, never from either session's opinion: lines and files per shipped feature, compile and test time per change, defects found after landing, agent edit success (edits that compiled first try), rust_global and hand-written Rust counts; eoie keeps its 1k-loc crate gate meanwhile as the control; the checkpoint runs when Tibia has shipped enough features to compare (the Tibia session owns that signal), then decides between restart in the Tibia style, restart keeping the gate, or incremental migration", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "none", "eoie,dogfood,spiral,decision", "current", "current")
inl eoie_agile_formatted_writer () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", "eoie's agile writer emits the formatter's canonical layout so state/agile.spi never has lines over 120 (decided 2026-10-14 06:54): multi-line task records, notes as their own rows, long strings split with the core concat operator (formatter Q1); one-time reformat of the existing file; gate: agile check passes and 0 lines over 120", Story, P1, D4, 40u32, Paused, "SPIRAL-FORMATTER", "eoie,agile,formatter", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:20Z", "lane-a", "08:20 lane-a: check: agile.spi 510 lines, 362 over 120 (288 strings). Prototype layout: 470 records, over-120 372->2, 0 field mismatches on read-back. Blocker: eoie reader is line-based (state.rs line.find Task). Design lane-a\\agile\\design.md")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 2u32, "2026-10-14T16:33Z", "lane-a", "12:33 lane-a: the formatter itself now emits the designed layout for agile.spi (Task ( / one field per line / ), union cases one per line): parse identical, 0 layout lines over 120 (76 before), 432 over 120 left, all string literals waiting for the Q1 concat op.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 3u32, "2026-10-14T23:25Z", "lane-a", "Formatter on state/agile.spi (19:05 code, split literals): same parse, 0 guard blocks, over-120 324 -> 4. Blocker unchanged: eoie's agile reader is line-based (state.rs line.find), so the formatted file must wait for a block reader.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 4u32, "2026-10-15T04:59Z", "lane-a", "00:59 formatter v9 on state/agile.spi (808 non-blank lines): same parse, idempotent, G7.2, 0 guard blocks, 0 lines over 120 (12,508 lines out). The formatter side is ready; the blocker stays eoie's line-based agile reader (lane B's verbs)")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: still waits on lane B's block reader (lane A handback 10-15); the formatter keeps agile.spi stable (G1) with 0 lines over 120.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement on a copy: SpiralCompiler format turns state/agile.spi (1,300 lines, 1,160 over 120) into 19,426 lines with 0 over 120 and eoie agile check still passes, but eoie reader is line-based: agile list sees 0 of 298 tasks and agile note fails with no task with this id. Order: reader and writer learn the multi-line record layout first, then the one-time reformat.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_7 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:16Z", "eoie", "09:16 reader design, measured first: 19 .spi files read state/agile.spi by line (line finders for 'inl task_', 'inl note_of_', 'inl planning_of_', tuple_field): about 12 readers (agile_task_text model, task_next, task_listing, task_inspection, planning model/tally/rollup/migration, note model, state_runtime terminals, task_census, views, lease) plus their CLI tests. Cheapest robust design: normalize at load. One agile_task_text function, records_as_lines(text), joins each multi-line record back into one line (it tracks parentheses outside string literals and honours the core concat operator used for split literals), and every load site calls it before its existing finders; the parsers themselves stay unchanged. Rule before building: done when, on a copy of the shared backlog formatted by the 5e formatter, agile list/show/next/note/set and agile check give byte-identical results to the unformatted copy, and all agile CLI notebooks pass. The writer (emit the canonical layout) and the one-time reformat come after that, in that order.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_8 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:24Z", "eoie", "09:24 reader normalization drafted and passing (scratch f5/src/agile_records_model): records_as_lines joins a record whose parentheses stay open across lines (paren depth counted outside string literals, UTF-8-safe via lib code points), also joins an inl ... = binding head with its body, adds no space after an opening or before a closing paren so the result has the single-line form the finders expect, and merges the formatter's split literals (a literal, then ^^ and the next literal, becomes one literal). Tests: a single-line record with a paren inside a string passes through unchanged; a formatted Task record (head, fields, split literal, closing paren on its own line) joins back into one line. Next: call it at each of the ~12 load sites, then run the rule's byte-identity check on a 5e-formatted copy of the shared backlog.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_9 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:24Z", "eoie", "10:24 eoie: reader slice drafted, scratch agile-records/apply-agile-records.ps1. New HostFree crate eoie_agile_records (scratch f6/src/eoie_agile_records) is called at 18 load sites: 14 agile verbs, todo scan, memory links, resume, handoff. A dry run on copies changed 39 files, each only where intended. Three defects in the 09:24 model were found while tracing the writers, before any build: (1) each read added a trailing newline, so every agile write would have grown the file by one line; (2) building with acc ++# line is quadratic, gigabytes of copying on the 5 MB state; (3) writers compare the text they read against the file before writing (state_written / text_equals), so normalized text would have failed every write on a formatted file as changed-since-read. Fixes: lines pushed into one growable array and joined once (byte identity now tested, plus a 1,000-record test); writers keep the raw text for the compare and write the single-line form; agile add's duplicate check reads the normalized text. The lib had no linear join or in-place push: lib-gaps/apply-lib-gaps.ps1 (baseline-guarded) adds am'.growable/push_back and a Vec::join Rust arm for sm'.concat_array, beside process.try_capture_combined_list for EOIE-CI-PARITY. Checks wait for tibia's live block to end (notebooks: records model, ci parity model, lib process/am'/sm'; standards). Then the byte-identity rule on a 5e-formatted backlog copy in the candidate build.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_10 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 10u32, "2026-10-23T15:31Z", "eoie", "11:31 eoie: the byte-identity gate's positive control found that the 5e formatter cannot format state/agile.spi. format fails after 92 s with 'Found a high surrogate char without a following low surrogate' because the file holds one 😀 (line 4288) inside a literal longer than 120 columns. Minimal repros (scratch agile-records/fmt-probe): every long literal with an astral character fails wherever it sits, the same literal with é formats, and a short line with 😀 is fine. Reported to main with the repros (literal splitting probably cuts at a UTF-16 index). Rule, set now: the reader slice ships only when its gate can run on real formatter output, so it leaves release M and waits for a formatter that formats agile.spi (main's 5f or later), then joins the next release with the gate unchanged. 6,163 of 6,446 lines are over 120 today.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_11 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 11u32, "2026-10-23T19:53Z", "eoie", "15:53 eoie: note 10's blocker is lifted. Main's installed formatter-only core 19F5F752 (spiral-bin\\bin\\single-flight\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll, installed about 15:5x) formats a copy of state/agile.spi in 69 s with exit 0: 6,541 lines become 93,941, with 0 over 120. byte-identity.ps1 now formats with that installed DLL (the formatter the writer will call; the 5d refusal stays), while the release train keeps formatting with 5e until N, so landed layouts stay consistent. The positive control against today's eoie is running. N now waits only on O and on the lib landing (am' push_back, sm' nonempty_line_at, concat_array via lib-gaps after main's lib land).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_12 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 12u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "eoie", "18:30 eoie: writer spec, read from the installed formatter's source (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs literalChain about line 180435 and displayWidth about line 178769), so the writer can emit the formatter's layout byte for byte instead of shelling out to a 69 s format. A record that fits in 120 columns stays on one line. Otherwise the constructor opens with ( and each argument goes on its own line at indent+4. A string argument that doesn't fit becomes a ^^ chain: room = 120 - indent - 6. Units are text elements of plain text, plus each escape sequence as one unit. Width per element: 2 for runes in 1100-115F, 2E80-A4CF, AC00-D7A3, F900-FAFF, FE30-FE4F, FF00-FF60, FFE0-FFE6, 1F300-1FAFF, 20000-3FFFD; 0 for non-spacing marks; else 1. Accumulate until width >= room; cut after the last escaped newline if it lies past room/4, else after the last space if past room/2, else at the current length. The remainder carries over (width recomputed) and the marks reset. Piece 0 is written as a plain literal and later pieces as ^^ literals, all at the same indent. Test plan, data-driven: the formatter's own output of today's backlog (scratch agile-records/fmt-probe/agile.spi, 93,941 lines from 6,564 records) is the oracle; the writer's layout of each record must equal its formatted text, and the gate is 6,564 of 6,564. Order: after N installs the reader (byte-identity proven 8/8 discriminating today), the writer slice comes next.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_13 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 13u32, "2026-10-23T22:36Z", "eoie", "18:36 eoie: the cut algorithm is drafted as a pure model (scratch agile-layout/model.spi, package f6/src/eoie_agile_layout). Units are records of text, width, utf16, space and newline. Positions are kept in UTF-16 counts, so the room/4 and room/2 thresholds match .NET's current.Length exactly, while splitting happens on unit boundaries, so it is backend-agnostic. The marks reset after each cut, and the remainder's width is recomputed, as in literalChain. chained_lines lays the pieces as a plain literal then ^^ literals at the same indent. Tests: backlog note 9 at indent 8 must split into exactly the 8 pieces the installed formatter produced (copied from fmt-probe), plus a short-literal case and a chain-layout case. Typecheck after tibia's block. Still to write: the host unit builder (text elements and widths from the formatter's rune table) and record layout (one line if it fits, else one argument per line at indent+4).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_14 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 14u32, "2026-10-23T22:38Z", "eoie", "18:38 eoie: record layout facts read from the oracle (fmt-probe): the definition line stays 'inl <binding> () =' and the body sits at indent 4 as '<Constructor> (', one argument per line at indent 8, closed by '    )' at indent 4, with no blank lines between definitions (authored breaks only). An argument chain that is not last carries its comma after its last piece (^^ \"...follow-ups in EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT\",); the -6 in room = 120 - indent - 6 covers the ^^ prefix, both quotes and that comma. Of the first 400 note definitions none stayed on one line, so the writer can always use the multi-line form for Note and Task records, and the oracle comparison will show whether any record needs the one-line form.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_15 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 15u32, "2026-10-23T22:40Z", "eoie", "18:40 eoie: unit-builder decision, measured on today's backlog (.NET StringInfo over all of state/agile.spi): 0 text elements span more than one rune, and there are 47 distinct non-ASCII runes (é x11, Cyrillic, → x4, ’, —, 😀 x3, wide Hangul 가). So code-point units with the formatter's width table reproduce .NET's text elements exactly on today's data, and Rust std chars() is enough with no grapheme crate. Edge rule for future notes: a non-spacing mark or a rune after a zero-width joiner merges into the previous unit with width 0, matching .NET clusters for the common cases; the oracle comparison over all records stays the gate. The model also gained record layout (record_lines, a plain/literal argument union, comma placement), with an oracle test generated from the formatter's 15 lines for note 9.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_16 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 16u32, "2026-10-23T23:27Z", "eoie", "19:27 eoie: the unit builder needs character codes, and lib has none (no char-to-code and no char-to-string in sm' or convert; checked by grep). Drafted lib additions (scratch lib-gaps-2/apply-char-code.ps1): sm'.char_code (char -> u32: Rust 'as u32' code point, .NET UTF-16 unit, Python ord) and sm'.of_char (char -> string), with an _assert_eq test in sm's own idiom. With them, the unit builder is pure Spiral: sm'.to_char_list, then char_code against the formatter's width ranges, with of_char for the unit text and utf16 = 2 above U+FFFF. Gate: sm'.spi lib gate (about 35 min; the baseline reds show_floats and replace_regex pre-exist), landed together with console.read_to_end after main's next lib land.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_17 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 17u32, "2026-10-23T23:57Z", "eoie", "19:57 eoie: oracle census of record shapes: 5,561 Note and 614 Task definitions use the multi-line form, and 248 definitions stay on one line (Planning records such as inl planning_of_bend_backend () = Planning (\"BEND-BACKEND\", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)). Task arguments are literals and plain words (Story, P2, D1, 60u32, Active), all covered by the model's PlainArgument and LiteralArgument. The model gained the one-line rule: one line when 'inl b () = Ctor (a, b, ...)' fits in 120 columns, else the multi-line form. It also gained a test from that Planning record, making 6 oracle-backed tests in all.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_18 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 18u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 150m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_formatted_writer_19 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER", 19u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl wgsl_parallel_lanes () = Task ("WGSL-PARALLEL-LANES", "2026-10-14 07:35 WGSL programs run as one GPU thread (codegen @workgroup_size(1), wgsl-run dispatch_workgroups(1,1,1)), so the GPU gives bit-exact results but no throughput; apps/tibia gpu_sim.spi proves the multi-creature simulator is bit-exact on WGSL (10 ticks, a 3000-tick troll hunt, 20 arena-spot cyclops clears) but one thread took ~74 s vs ~23 s on C (both incl. compile); design: (1) a lane primitive (e.g. !!!!GpuLane() returning global_invocation_id.x, or main taking a u32 lane) and @workgroup_size(64); (2) wgsl-run dispatches ceil(lanes/64) workgroups from SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES, with one i32 result slot per lane and an aggregate (sum, or all-zero for tests); (3) the shared spiral_heap is partitioned per lane (base = lane * heap_words / lanes) so bump allocation never collides; test: a C-vs-WGSL lane sweep where lane i simulates seed i and the per-lane results match C; serves: GPU training/evaluation of the Tibia simulator to take load off the CPU (CPU is the night-run bottleneck)", Feature, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,compiler,gpu,tibia", "current", "current")
inl tibia_hunting_ground () = Task ("TIBIA-HUNTING-GROUND", "Hunting-ground scenario after the P5 stop rule: respawning camp (2 cyclops + 4 trolls, 60 s respawn) on the real arena map (terrain ArenaHunt), patrol routes and a spawn-sweeping rule; route choice as the new decision", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,simulator,scenario", "current", "current")
inl tibia_party () = Task ("TIBIA-PARTY", "Several characters on the hunting ground (the next scenario design after TIBIA-HUNTING-GROUND)", Feature, P3, D5, 85u32, Paused, "", "tibia,simulator,scenario", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_party () = Planning ("TIBIA-PARTY", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_party_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 09:59 written before any code. Order: (1) a hunter record and a party band of size 1, accepted only when every existing pin is unchanged, gpu_sim's WGSL twin included; (2) then a second knight. Decision rules are not expected to express well: splitting the six-cyclops ground into territories vs moving together (one tanks while the other kills, pulls staggered so no knight faces more than two). Hand rules to beat: both sweep the nearest spawn and avoid each other's target; both follow one leader. Stop rule: if the best hand rule lands within 5% of the best evolved party tactic on held-out experience at equal or lower cost, stop; the party lever is then saturated too. Not before P4 has nExBot and the random control live (PR-3).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 10:40 step 1 done: hunting_pack.hunter record nested in pack (world state stays in pack; knight changes through with_hunter/hunter_steps); pack_sim 23/23 and gpu_sim 6/6 unchanged, so every pin is bit-exact including the WGSL twins. Step 2 (second knight) waits per the note above.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 3u32, "2026-10-14T15:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 11:02 step 2 (probe, C only; Kino gate pending because a suite next to the live PR-4 block got both reaped): pack gains ally : hunter, camp.party (1 or 2); the second knight acts through turned (swap ally and hunter, run the unchanged code, swap back), monsters chase and strike the nearer knight the same way, call_sign offsets its RNG entities by 100; solo hunts unchanged (seed 1001 still 18 / 1140 / 20). Six cyclops, 100 held-out hunts, party XP (death -5000) / deaths / profit: solo cavebot sweep 135300 / 0 / -241191; duo cavebot standing 191400 / 0 / -98649; duo cavebot shared sweep 236350 / 1 / -172914; duo territories (border 119) 234750 / 0 / -228350, (115) 232350 / 0 / -221823; duo retreat standing 156450 / 0 / -91826; duo retreat shared sweep 232350 / 0 / -177360; duo retreat territories 229650 / 0 / -207419. Splitting the ground costs ~30% more for no XP: alone, each knight faces the cyclops; moving together (both on the same rule) is the strong hand rule. Next: evolve the duo rules for the stop rule (search waits for memory).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 4u32, "2026-10-14T15:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 11:13 stop rule reached: rules evolved for the duo on training seeds (search seed 91, 12 gens x 12 offspring, objective profit at the shared-sweep cavebot's training XP 237100) return heal 117 + 6 per attacker, never retreat: held-out 238300 XP / 1 death / -170319 vs the shared-sweep cavebot 236350 / 1 / -172914, +0.8% XP and +1.5% profit, inside the 5% bar. The party lever is saturated; the code (ally, turned, call_sign, SweepsTerritory) stays as scenario capability. Kino gate for the ally change still pending (run when the live box is idle).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 5u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: unchanged state (duo views, ally via swap, shared-rule result); stays paused behind POTION-FIDELITY because two-knight live runs need the potion model first.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 6u32, "2026-10-22T03:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:38 Resume condition (retirement grooming): TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY is Done, or Paused with its own resume condition met, because two-knight live runs need the potion model to hold first.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 7u32, "2026-10-22T15:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:59 RESUMED mechanically: its condition (note 6) is met, TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY is Done (PR-23 closed the potion gap as noise). The pending Cells gate for the ally change (note 4) is covered by today's scripts/build.ps1 job (tibia-libmig-after-land runs Cells over every src file, pack_sim included). Next steps: (a) read-only feasibility of a duo in the live harness: a second character in the local world, two OTClient instances on the off-screen desktop, per-client parity, run-arena support for a party size; (b) pre-register a duo live block against the model shared-sweep prediction before any run; (c) the live block itself in a window.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 8u32, "2026-10-22T16:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:01 Step (a) done, read-only. Live duo is feasible with four harness changes and no code in the probes beyond a party flag. (1) World: canary config.lua has maxPlayersOnlinePerAccount = 1 and all sample characters (Knight Sample id 5, Paladin Sample id 4, ...) sit on account 1, so the scenario either sets the limit to 2 for the run (restored after, like spawns) or adds a second account; the second knight is a sample character set to vocation 4 with Knight Samples stats by the same UPDATE. (2) Client: init.lua takes setCompactName(otclient) for both the user write dir and the rc script (/<compact>rc.lua), so a second client folder (otclient-src copy, 182 MB, 139 MB of it data) with compact name otclient2 gets its own write dir, probe log and rc file; run-probe.ps1 gains a client root parameter. (3) Launch: run-offscreen.ps1 starts both clients on the off-screen desktop; parity stays per client. (4) Rows: run-arena writes one row per knight plus the party sum. The model rule to mirror is the shared sweep (both knights run the same cavebot rule, no coordination), so no client-to-client channel is needed. Next: pre-register a duo hunting-ground block against a fresh model prediction on the adopted bundle (the 10-14 duo numbers predate it; a compile job), then build (1)-(4) outside a live block, then a smoke duo in a window.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 9u32, "2026-10-22T16:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:03 Correction to note 8, point (2), from OTClient source (main.cpp, resourcemanager.cpp): no folder copy is needed. The client takes --user-dir=<path> (isolated profiles, upstream #1540), which moves the write dir (configs, bot storage, tibia-probe.log). The default write dir is getUserDir() plus the compact name (APPDATA otcr/otclient/otclient), so only the last segment is the compact name. Trap: setWriteDir appends the write dir at the END of the search path, so an otclientrc.lua in the client folder (the scenario restores otclientrc.lua.orig there) shadows one in a user dir. Duo design: both clients start from the one folder with their own --user-dir, each holding its own otclientrc.lua, and the folder copy of otclientrc.lua is moved aside for the duo run and restored after. Harness changes: run-offscreen.ps1 gains an -Arguments pass-through to CreateProcess, run-probe.ps1 an optional -UserDir (rc and probe log there; unchanged without it). A first edit to run-probe.ps1 assumed both path segments were the compact name; it was reverted unchanged (git shows no diff).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 10u32, "2026-10-22T16:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:05 Harness step 1 landed in live/ (no live block running, defaults unchanged): run-offscreen.ps1 takes -Arguments (appended to the quoted exe; empty gives the old command line exactly), run-probe.ps1 takes -UserDir (rc and tibia-probe.log in that dir, launch with --user-dir; without it everything is as before). Both parse; the default command line was checked equal. Still to do: the scenario side (second knight in the DB update, maxPlayersOnlinePerAccount 2 for the run, moving the folder rc aside during a duo, two run-probe processes in parallel, rows per knight), the competitor's character from the environment (an apps/arena edit, after the build job), and the pre-registration.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 11u32, "2026-10-22T16:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:08 Harness step 2 in live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 (parses; every addition gated on -Ally, solo runs unchanged except a no-op check for a stale config.lua.kept): -Ally <character> -AllyAt x,y set that character to a knight (vocation 4) with Knight Samples stats, position and copied items; config.lua gets both per-account limits raised to 2 for the run (refuses if the text is absent) and restored in finally like the spawns; the ally runs the SAME probe file in a parallel run-probe with -UserDir tibia-local/ally-profile and TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER set, its stdout to ally-probe.out, and the scenario prints '== ally log <path>' after both end. Same probe file means the rc search-path shadowing (note 9) cannot pick a wrong script. The ally sample stays a knight in the local DB after a duo (world data). Left: the probes read TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER (apps/arena, after the build job), run-arena makes the ally row, the pre-registration, a smoke duo in a window.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 12u32, "2026-10-22T16:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:10 Harness step 3 in live/run-arena.ps1: -Ally/-AllyAt pass through to the scenario; the row block is one function Write-ArenaRow(lines, who), called for the knight and, when the scenario printed '== ally log <path>', for the ally as competitor '<name>-ally' with its own log '<stamp>-<label>-<competitor>-ally-<run>.log' (post-block row matching by competitor and run still pairs them). Checked by extracting the functions and running them on synthetic lines: the solo row has the old 15 columns and values, the ally path is read, a solo run finds none. Left: TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER in the probes (apps/arena, after the build job), pre-registration, smoke duo.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 13u32, "2026-10-22T16:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:11 RULE P1, written before any duo prediction or run. Question: does the model predict a live duo on the hunting ground? Scenario: PR-23s hunting-ground setup (Canary spawns, 300 s hunts, level 30, axe 60, heal below 160, never retreats) with Knight Sample at 1936,1336 and Paladin Sample made a knight at 1937,1336, both running spiral-policy, which is the model shared sweep (same rule, no coordination). One smoke duo first (not counted: both log in, both act, both rows written); then 10 duo hunts in one live window, a crash rerun once then missing, no runs added after a look. Predictions: the adopted bundle with camp.party 2 shared sweep on the hunting-ground camp, 100 held-out hunts, from one score job run BEFORE the window; the numbers are written into a BENCHMARK pre-registration (PR-24) before any duo row exists. Tests: party kills per hunt (sum of both rows Loot lines) and party potions per hunt (sum of both rows), each a one-sample t against its prediction, critical 2.26 (9 df). Decision: both hold, the duo model matches live, and note 5s saturation finding (best hand rule within 5 percent of the evolved duo) stands on a live-checked model; either fails, the gap is named on this item before any candidate. Descriptive: kills split between the two knights, deaths, each knights lowest health.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 14u32, "2026-10-22T16:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:17 Block runner drafted in scratch (block-libmig-duo.ps1, parses): LIB-MIGRATION smoke (note 19 criterion there) -> duo smoke (rule P1 smoke: 2 rows, both in-game lines, loot by either) -> PR-24 through live/block.ps1 with -Ally Paladin Sample -AllyAt 1937,1336, 10 hunts, -Minutes 70; each stage stops the block on failure. live/block.ps1 -Ledger is now optional (default empty; every existing caller passes it), since P1s tests are per-hunt party sums: eoie table query --group run --sum kills,potions then eoie table test --test one-sample --arms all, chain checked on synthetic duo rows. Window: main proposed 13:45-14:15 after its PACK-NEXT-2 review; D37 and duo-predict run before it.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 15u32, "2026-10-22T16:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:48 Predictions in, PR-24 pre-registered in BENCHMARK (12:47, before any duo row): job tibia-duo-predict (adopted bundle, shield 11, camp.party 2, 100 held-out hunts): 15.36 party kills, 7.56 party potions per hunt (knight 7.14, ally 0.42), 0 deaths; solo arm 13.19 kills, 14.84 potions. The solo self-check first looked failed (14.84 vs 14.34), but 14.34 is score.ps1s shield-12 table and the live scenario resets shield 11 since PR-16; kills match at either shield. Caveat added to BENCHMARK PR-23 (verdict unchanged; live 14.8 is t -0.05 against the shield-11 prediction). Sharp model claim to watch: the ally drinks almost nothing (monsters chase and strike the nearer knight, note 4; why the first knight is nearer so often is not traced).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 16u32, "2026-10-22T16:57Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:57 Probe side landed on the tree for the duo (the lib migration stays restored, see TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note 23): probe.knight reads TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER (default Knight Sample) and bot_profile joins _1511 through lua.joined; random, path, competitor and observer use bot_profile. Job tibia-party-probe-build (scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua) green: every probe compiled and parses, format check clean, lua.spi 5 of 5. The generated competitor, random and path Lua each read the variable once. Harness complete; the block runner (scratch block-libmig-duo.ps1, now duo smoke then PR-24) waits for main's go.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 17u32, "2026-10-22T17:47Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:47 BLOCK START 13:45 on main's go: job tibia-block-pr24 (scratch block-libmig-duo.ps1: duo smoke, then PR-24 through live/block.ps1). The live-gui request was held 52 s by x's heavy-build pre-commit until x stopped it at 13:46; window granted 13:46:17. Every compile job of mine was stopped by on-go.ps1 and is recorded for requeue.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 18u32, "2026-10-22T17:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:50 Live duo confirmed during the smoke hunt (13:48-13:49): two OTClient processes from one folder, the second with --user-dir tibia-local/ally-profile; the ally log reads 'in game as Paladin Sample health 515' and 'policy starts at 1937,1336,7'; both knights approach, kill and loot. Method check for rule P1's party sums, read from the live logs: each 'Loot of a' line appears only in the looter's (corpse owner's) log, with different contents per kill; the other knight sees only '<name> gained N experience points', which the registered regex does not match; 'Using one of' lines are private to the drinker. So summing both logs counts each kill and each potion once.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 19u32, "2026-10-22T17:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:53 DUO SMOKE PASSED (rule P1 smoke, not counted): two rows at 13:53:36, spiral-policy 721 experience, lowest 141, 2 potions, 26 gold and spiral-policy-ally 939 experience, lowest 129, 5 potions, 73 gold; both in game, loot 5 and 12. Descriptive only: the ally took more of the fight than the model's split predicts (model ally 0.42 potions vs knight 7.14 per hunt). PR-24 started 13:53:39 (live-gui window of 70 min, 10 duo hunts).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 20u32, "2026-10-22T17:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:56 Source lead found during the block (Canary 3.6.1 src/creatures/monsters/monster.cpp, read-only; prompted by the uncounted smoke's split, before any PR-24 data). The model gives a foe to the ally only when the ally is strictly nearer (hunting_pack ally_is_nearer, chebyshev <), so every distance tie goes to the first knight, and adjacent knights sweeping together tie constantly; that is why it predicts ally 0.42 vs knight 7.14 potions. Canary: searchTarget's NEAREST keeps the first creature of targetList and replaces it only when strictly nearer, but onCreatureEnter calls onCreatureFound(creature, pushFront = true), so the knight that most recently entered a monster's view sits first and wins ties. Cyclops also use strategiesTarget nearest 70 / damage 30 (the knight that dealt the most damage) with changeTarget every 4000 ms at 10 percent; trolls are nearest 100. So live splits should be far more even than the model's. Rule P1's tests are party sums and do not depend on the split; the split stays descriptive. A candidate (ties by most recent view entry plus the cyclops damage strategy) gets its own rule after PR-24's verdict.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 21u32, "2026-10-22T17:57Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:57 Candidate design from the note-20 lead (design only; its rule and score come after PR-24's verdict). The model's foe record has no target memory: foe_acts re-picks the nearer knight every tick. Canary keeps a monster's target until searchTarget runs again: on target loss, on changeTarget (cyclops: every 4000 ms at 10 percent; trolls: chance 0), or when a follow completes. So the candidate (duo camps only, a static camp flag like the ranged one so solo pins stay bit-exact) adds to foe: target_knight (none, first, second), last_entrant (the knight that most recently came into its view, from views.sighted-style screen checks per tick), and damage taken from each knight. searchTarget at acquisition and on each retarget event rolls the creature's strategiesTarget (cyclops 70 nearest / 30 damage; trolls 100 nearest): nearest with ties to last_entrant, damage to the knight with the larger damage total. Between events the foe keeps its target. Expected effect: an even split between knights; party totals near the current prediction. To pre-register: the split as the primary (each knight's share of the party's hits taken against PR-24's live share), totals held.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 22u32, "2026-10-22T17:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:59 Strategy table for the note-21 candidate, from the local Canary data (data-canary/monster): cyclops nearest 70 / damage 30, changeTarget every 4000 ms at 10 percent; troll nearest 100, changeTarget chance 0; hunter nearest 100, changeTarget every 4000 ms at 10 percent. So trolls never retarget while their target lives, and only cyclops use the damage strategy.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 23u32, "2026-10-22T18:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:07 Candidate code drafted as a scratch patch flag (party-target-patch.ps1 -Work <copy>, dry-applied: every anchor matched the expected count, 1810 -> 1885 lines, widest 120), behind camp.party_targeting (builder targeting_like_canary; default false, so every pin stays bit-exact). Foe gains aims_at, entrant, in_view, per-knight damage and retarget_at; knight hits credit damage by call_sign. aimed_by_canary ports searchTarget: the most recent view entrant first in the list (onCreatureEnter pushes front); retarget when no target or on the changeTarget roll every 4000 ms (cyclops and hunter 10 percent, troll 0); strategy roll nearest (ties to the entrant) or damage, where Canary's loop never records the first element's damage, so with two knights the damage strategy picks the second-listed knight whenever it dealt any damage (ported as is). Purposes 40 and 41 are new (11-23 used). Its rule and score come after PR-24's verdict.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 24u32, "2026-10-22T18:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:10 Prediction job ready for the candidate: duo-predict.ps1 -Targeting applies party-target-patch to the work copy and opts the duo camp in (targeting_like_canary; without the switch the same main uses id, so the PR-24 predictions reproduce), writes duo-predict-targeting-*.tsv, and now also reports hits taken per knight, the quantity a split rule would test. Edited with one explicit replacement per change (memory: no pair loops in PowerShell; a first attempt with a pair loop refused safely and was dropped).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 25u32, "2026-10-22T18:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:13 Harness reviewed end to end during the block (git diff of live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 and live/run-arena.ps1), no defects. The ally gets the knight's stats including shield 11 (matching the shield-11 predictions) and the copied items including 100 potions. The per-account limits are guarded, restored in finally, and recovered from a stale copy at the next start. TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER is cleared right after the ally starts, so the main client keeps its default. Each row reads only its own lines (the ally's output is a separate file), so the knight's experience and potion counts cannot pick up ally lines. The one lasting side effect, Paladin Sample staying a knight in the local DB, is documented (note 11).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 26u32, "2026-10-22T18:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:22 Correction to the note-21/23 candidate after reading Monster::onThinkTarget (monster.cpp 1259-1313): a changeTarget event does NOT re-run the strategy roll. When changeTargetChance >= uniform_random(1, 100) fires on the changeTargetSpeed period (4000 ms, then a cooldown of the same length, so one event per 4 s), a monster with targetDistance <= 1 (melee: cyclops, troll) calls searchTarget(TARGETSEARCH_RANDOM) and a ranged one calls NEAREST. The default strategy roll (cyclops 70 nearest / 30 damage) applies only to the initial acquisition. The patch is fixed: acquisition uses the strategy roll, a changeTarget event picks at random for melee and nearest for ranged; re-dry-applied clean, widest line 120.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 27u32, "2026-10-22T18:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:23 Second correction from source: a monster's view range is not the client screen. Creature::canSee defaults to MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X/Y = the client viewport (8, 6) plus (3, 5) = 11 by 11 tiles (map_const.hpp), so a knight counts as entering a monster's view at Chebyshev 11 in x and y, not 7 by 5. The candidate's on_monster_screen now uses 11; re-dry-applied clean.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 28u32, "2026-10-22T18:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:24 Note 27 follow-up: the model already encodes Canary's range as geometry.sees with monster_view_range () = 11, so the candidate reuses geometry.sees instead of its own helper (one definition of a monster's view). Re-dry-applied clean.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 29u32, "2026-10-22T18:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:25 Source check of the entrant mechanism (Monster::onCreatureMove, monster.cpp 405-421; onCreatureAppear 320-330): a creature that moves into the monster's view triggers onCreatureEnter (push FRONT), and so does one appearing (login, spawn) in view. But when the monster's own move brings a knight into range, the monster runs updateTargetList, which adds missing spectators through onCreatureFound with the default push BACK. The candidate treats every view entry as push front: a known approximation, to be refined (entry by the knight's move vs by the monster's move) only if the split test points at it.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 30u32, "2026-10-22T19:02Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-24 RESULT (BENCHMARK, 15:00): rule P1 fails on both metrics. Party kills 16.6 vs 15.36 (t 3.10), party potions 5.1 vs 7.56 (t -5.10), 0 deaths as predicted. GAP NAMED: who the cyclopses attack. Live, the hits split 188 knight vs 203 ally over 10 hunts and potions 28 vs 23 (the ally used more in 4 of 10 hunts); the model gives the knight 7.14 potions per hunt and the ally 0.42, because ally_is_nearer breaks every distance tie toward the first knight and a foe keeps no target. Spreading the same pressure over two knights lets the 160 threshold and regeneration absorb more of it, which fits fewer party potions live. RULE P2, written before the candidate is run: the candidate is camp.party_targeting (scratch party-target-patch.ps1, built at 14:0x from Canary monster.cpp before this result was read: the creature that entered view goes first, the cyclops damage strategy 30 percent, changeTarget every 4 s at 10 percent with RANDOM for melee). Calibration on PR-24 (post hoc, never adoption by itself): its 100-hunt duo prediction must sit inside PR-24 95 percent CIs (party potions 4.01-6.19, party kills 15.70-17.50) with the ally potion share in 25-75 percent (live 45). Pass: pre-register PR-25, 10 fresh duo hunts at the candidate predictions, and adopt party_targeting as the duo default only if both hold at abs t <= 2.26. Fail on either: name the remaining gap on this item, no candidate block. Job tibia-duo-predict-targeting is queued behind x run 8.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 31u32, "2026-10-22T19:14Z", "tibia-lane", "Schedule (agreed with main, x and eoie at 15:1x): after x run 8 ends (~16:20), job tibia-duo-predict-targeting runs first in a compile window beside main PACK-NEXT-3 review; rule P2 decides by ~16:45. If it passes, PR-25 (pre-registration drafted in scratch pr25-draft.md, filled with the candidate numbers before any run) starts after BOTH main review end (~18:00) and eoie release F end (~18:30-18:50), via scratch on-go-pr25.ps1 then block-pr25.ps1, about 65 min. Latest start 19:40, else PR-25 moves to tomorrow. If P2 fails, no block today.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 32u32, "2026-10-22T19:17Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-24 descriptive, post hoc (no rule; it does not change the candidate or rule P2): every one of the 391 hits the two knights took came from a cyclops (eoie table logs --values on the 20 logs, target/pr24-cyclops-hits.tsv). The first cyclops hit per hunt (knight vs ally, ms): 15249/125173, 28583/14524, 141515/10360, 123274/12127, 18310/20356, 120946/10255, 17935/134517, 36524/10084, 127117/19413, 140593/16712. The ally was hit first in 7 of 10 (binomial p 0.34 against a coin, not a finding). In 10 of 10 the knight hit first took at least as many hits as the other (2 ties), and the other one often took its first hit only at 120-141 s, i.e. from a later cyclops. That is target persistence: a cyclops keeps the knight it first attacks, which Canary does (target kept, a 10 percent change roll every 4 s) and the model does not (ties to the first knight, no memory). The candidate keeps the target by construction; whether it reproduces the split is what P2 checks.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 33u32, "2026-10-22T19:21Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P2 FAILS (job tibia-duo-predict-targeting, 15:19-15:21, 100 held-out hunts, same job reran the baseline duo bit-for-bit: 15.36 kills, 7.56 potions). Candidate camp.party_targeting: party potions 6.26 (outside 4.01-6.19), party kills 15.28 (outside 15.70-17.50), ally potion share 90 percent (outside 25-75; knight 0.63, ally 5.63), hits taken knight 8.0 vs ally 32.1 (live 18.8 vs 20.3), 0 deaths. No PR-25 today. REMAINING GAP NAMED: the first target when both knights enter a cyclops view in the same tick. The candidate always ranks the ally as the entrant (entered_second is checked first), so with target persistence every cyclops picks the ally; live picked the ally first in 7 of 10 hunts, and the other knight was hit mostly by later cyclopses (first hits at 120-141 s, note 32). The persistence itself looks right (each live hunt is lopsided toward the first-attacked knight), the choice is not. Next, CPU-light: read how Canary fills a spawned monster targetList (the spectator scan order at spawn and onCreatureAppear order for two players already in view) before any new candidate; a candidate must take that order from the source, never from fitting PR-24.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 34u32, "2026-10-22T19:24Z", "tibia-lane", "Source facts for the named gap (Canary monster.cpp, mapsector.cpp, spectators.cpp; read 15:2x, before any new candidate). (1) A creature that enters a monster view is pushed to the FRONT of targetList (onCreatureAppear -> onCreatureEnter -> addTarget pushFront), so for the hunting ground cyclopses that exist at server start, the knight who logs in LAST is first. (2) When the monster itself appears or moves, updateTargetList APPENDS spectators in Spectators().find order: 16x16 sectors scanned row by row (y outer, x inner), then each sector creature_list in insertion order (emplace_back; removal swaps the last element into the hole). x 1936 is a sector start (121 x 16), so both knights at 1936/1937 share a sector and keep their login order there; a knight standing at x 1935 or less is scanned first. (3) NEAREST is Chebyshev with a strict less-than over targetList order, so a distance tie keeps the first in the list. (4) Harness: run-policy-scenario starts the ally probe first but in a fresh pwsh, the knight in-process, so the knight usually logs in first (hunt 10 world.log: Knight 14:51:31.821, Paladin 14:51:32.877; the other hunts overwrote world.log). Prediction from these facts alone: initial cyclopses go to the ally (last login, at the front), respawned or approaching cyclopses go to the knight (scan order, login order in the shared sector). Live fits that: ally hit first in 7 of 10, the knight hit mostly at 120-141 s. The race in (4) is a harness defect: login order must be deterministic before PR-25 (start the ally only after the knight logs in game).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_35 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 35u32, "2026-10-22T19:27Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P3, written before candidate 2 is built or run. Candidate 2 = candidate 1 plus targetList order from the source facts of note 34, nothing fitted: (a) both knights newly in a foe view in the same tick at the hunt start (login, now under 1000 ms) -> the ally first (last login at the front; login order knight then ally, which the harness now enforces); (b) both newly in view in the same tick otherwise (a respawn or the foe own move) -> spectator scan order: the knight whose 16x16 sector comes first in row-major order, the knight on a tie (sector insertion order = login order); (c) one knight newly in view: if the foe moved this tick or just appeared it is appended (the current first stays first; it becomes first only if the list was empty), otherwise (the knight walked in) it goes to the front. Known approximation, kept: a knight that leaves the shared sector and returns is not moved to the back. Calibration on PR-24 exactly as P2 (party potions in 4.01-6.19, party kills in 15.70-17.50, ally potion share in 25-75 percent), with the caveat that PR-24 logins were racy (usually knight first). Pass: PR-25 pre-registered at candidate 2 numbers on the fixed harness (ally probe now starts only after the server logs Knight Sample in; live/run-probe.ps1 -AfterLine/-InLog), run in the first free window. Fail: name the next gap, no block.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_36 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 36u32, "2026-10-22T19:35Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P3 FAILS on kills only (job tibia-duo-predict-source-order, 15:31-15:34, 100 held-out hunts; the same job reproduced the baseline duo and solo arms bit for bit). Candidate 2: party potions 5.83 (inside 4.01-6.19), ally potion share 56 percent (inside 25-75; knight 2.58, ally 3.25), party kills 15.32 (outside 15.70-17.50), 0 deaths; descriptive: hits taken knight 19.05, ally 21.92 against live 18.8 and 20.3. So the source-derived target order closes the split and potions gap, and no PR-25 runs on this candidate per P3. NEXT GAP NAMED: kills. The model kills 15.28-15.36 per duo hunt whatever the targeting, live 16.6; the solo arm has the same sign (model 13.19, PR-23 live 14.2, t 1.89, inside its band), so it is a kill-rate shortfall common to both, not a party effect. Live PR-24 kills by kind (Loot lines, target/pr24-kinds.tsv): cyclops 5.8, trolls 10.8 per hunt (knight 3.3 + 6.4, ally 2.5 + 4.4); PR-23 solo 4.8 and 9.4. Next, a compile job printing the model kills by kind for duo (candidate 2) and solo, descriptive, to locate the shortfall before any rule.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_37 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 37u32, "2026-10-22T19:41Z", "tibia-lane", "Kills gap located (job tibia-duo-kills-by-kind, 15:39-15:41; kinds derived from party experience, cyclops 150 and troll 20: cyclops = (experience - 20 x kills) / 130). Cyclops kills match: model duo 5.60 (candidate 2) and 5.68 (baseline) vs live PR-24 5.8; solo 4.75 vs PR-23 4.8. Trolls fall short by about one per hunt in both: duo 9.72 vs live 10.8, solo 8.44 vs live 9.4. So the shortfall is a troll mechanism the duo shares with solo (respawn cycle, troll approach from its spawn, or trolls fleeing at run_health 15 and escaping), not targeting or the party. It belongs to the hunting-ground model, not this item; candidate 2 targeting stays the party-targeting candidate, and the kills metric of a future PR-25 waits on the troll fix. Next, CPU-light: read Canary for troll flee and respawn behaviour against the model troll path, and count fleeing trolls in the PR-23/PR-24 logs (server text for a troll that flees is absent, so the proxy is a troll hit by the knight that never shows a Loot line), before any rule.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_38 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 38u32, "2026-10-22T19:54Z", "tibia-lane", "Troll kill timing (job tibia-troll-times, solo arm, 100 held-out hunts, a troll kill = a tick where party experience rises by exactly 20; live PR-23 from the 10 hunt logs, Loot of a troll lines, smoke hunt excluded), troll kills per hunt by time: 0-30 s model 1.99 vs live 2.0; 30-90 s 0.05 vs 0; 90-130 s 2.05 vs 2.2; 130-200 s 1.42 vs 1.2; 200-301 s 2.93 vs 3.9. The early waves match (the 94.2 s respawn of canary_respawn_ms fits the 90-130 s wave), so the shortfall is all in the last 100 s: about one troll from the second respawn wave of the near spawns, or the far spawns (1953,1355 and 1941,1354, 18-19 tiles from the start, outside the 11-tile monster view) reaching the knight later in the model. Next CPU-light: count live first kills per spawn by the knight position at the Loot line; a model trace of where each late troll comes from needs a compile.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_39 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 39u32, "2026-10-22T19:58Z", "tibia-lane", "Troll respawn timing ruled out from source (spawn_monster.cpp 276-338, monster.cpp:366): troll, cyclops and hunter are isBlockable = false, so a player near the spawn never blocks it; a death starts the spawn check (onCreatureDisappear -> startSpawnMonsterCheck), the first check sets lastSpawn (cleanup), a later check passes lastSpawn + interval and schedules the spawn 3 x 1400 ms later. That is the model canary_respawn_ms (death-relative, check grid plus 4200 ms), and the 90-130 s live wave matches it. Live late kills (200-301 s, 40 in 10 PR-23 hunts) all happen with the knight at x 1948-1951, y 1341-1349 (between troll spawn 1950,1345 and cyclops spawn 1953,1346); job tibia-troll-where prints the model knight position at each troll kill to test whether the model knight stands elsewhere late (the remaining hypothesis).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_40 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 40u32, "2026-10-22T20:18Z", "tibia-lane", "Knight position at late troll kills ruled out too (job tibia-troll-where, 16:0x-16:17, solo, 100 hunts, src with F34; troll bins unchanged: 1.99, 0.05, 2.10, 1.36, 2.94). Model positions are in the ArenaHunt frame: player_start 103,86 = live 1936,1336, offset (1833,1250), which maps all six spawn_points exactly onto live/hunting-ground-spawns.xml. Converted, the model knight at late troll kills stands at 1952,1345 (25), 1949,1344 (15), 1951,1345 (12), 1951,1344 (11), 1951,1346 (10), the same ground as live (1948-1951, 1341-1349). Ruled out so far: respawn timing (note 39), knight late position (this note), targeting (note 36). Left for the about one missing late troll per hunt: which trolls reach that ground (wake by view at 11 tiles: troll spawn 1941,1354 sits 10-11 tiles from it, so a knight 2-4 tiles further south, as live often stands, wakes it and the model knight may not) and troll kill time. Next step needs a model trace of per-spawn troll wakes; not started today.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_41 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 41u32, "2026-10-22T20:56Z", "tibia-lane", "Troll gap localized per spawn (job tibia-troll-spawns, solo, 100 hunts; each troll kill attributed to the slot whose returns_at was set that tick, by its spawn tile): kills per hunt 0-90 s spawn0 (1943,1334) 1.00, spawn1 (1950,1345) 1.00, spawn2 (1953,1355) 0.01, spawn3 (1941,1354) 0.03; 90-200 s 0.99, 1.00, 0.69, 0.79; 200-301 s 0.88, 1.00, 0.45, 0.60 (total 2.93). With a 94.2 s respawn a spawn yields at most about one kill per 100 s, so live 3.9 late kills need about one from each of the four spawns, while the model far spawns give 0.45 and 0.60. The shortfall is the FAR spawns: their first kill comes in 90-200 s (0.69, 0.79 per hunt), so their respawn lands late and the second kill often misses the 300 s end. Live cannot be attributed per spawn from the logs (Loot lines carry no position), but the late live kills all happen where the near spawns and the second cyclops spawn meet. Next candidate question for the source: when a far troll first wakes and walks to the knight (view range 11 vs the 10-tile distance from the late ground, and the first think after the knight comes into view), not fitted. Checked 16:59: view range is not it, Canary MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X = 8 + 3 and _Y = 6 + 5 (map_const.hpp), the same +-11 square as geometry.sees.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_42 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 42u32, "2026-10-22T21:06Z", "tibia-lane", "Measurement limit for the far-troll question: the probe seen-at lines come from the knight client, whose viewport is +-8 x and +-6 y (Canary MAP_MAX_CLIENT_VIEW_PORT_X/Y), smaller than a monster view of +-11. From the late ground the far spawns are 9-10 tiles away in y, so trolls near them are invisible to the client (in 10 PR-23 hunts a troll within 2 tiles of 1953,1355 was seen once, of 1941,1354 never). Live far-troll wake times cannot be read from client logs; they need a server-side trace (Canary log or a server probe), or a model-side trace compared only against live kill totals. Canary logs Spawning <name> at <pos> only at trace level, which config.lua says exists only in debug builds, so the server-side route needs a debug Canary or a server-side probe script.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_43 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 43u32, "2026-10-22T21:46Z", "tibia-lane", "Model change at 17:45 (TIBIA-FIDELITY note 115): F34 adopted, monsters flee at health <= run_health as in Canary. Every duo prediction above (PR-24 pre-registration, candidates 1 and 2, kills by kind, troll times and spawns) was made before it; from now on predictions include it, starting with job tibia-troll-wake. Expected direction for trolls (runHealth 15): slightly fewer kills (the troll-only current-first pin went 330 -> 303 over 20 seeds), i.e. the open late-troll shortfall can only widen, so the far-spawn question stays the one to answer before any party rerun.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_44 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 44u32, "2026-10-22T21:59Z", "tibia-lane", "Troll wake times, model vs live (job tibia-troll-wake, solo, 100 hunts, src with F34; live from the 10 PR-23 logs, knight position from seen-at/player-at lines carried with the latest @t stamp). Model: near spawns see the knight at once (1943,1334 median 0 s, first death 4.4 s; 1950,1345 0.6 s, 10.9 s); far spawns late (1953,1355: 88 of 100 hunts, median 139.6 s, first death 175.2 s in 80; 1941,1354: 92 of 100, 137.8 s, first death 169.4 s in 87); cyclopses 1953,1346 1.5 s / 41.8 s and 1958,1336 7.4 s / 72.1 s. Live: the knight first comes within 11 tiles of 1953,1355 in 9 of 10 hunts at median 149 s, of 1941,1354 in 10 of 10 at median 149 s. So the far trolls wake at about the same time in both: wake timing is ruled out too (with respawn timing, knight position, view range, notes 39-41). Left: what happens after the wake, i.e. how fast a woken far troll is killed and how often trolls escape by fleeing at runHealth 15 (now in the model too, F34). Next model output: escapes per hunt and wake-to-death time per far spawn; live escape counts need a server-side trace.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_45 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 45u32, "2026-10-22T22:00Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P4, written before any build or score. Source fact (Canary troll.lua: no healing spell; monsters do not regenerate without one; monster.cpp updateIdleStatus: a monster with no target away from its spawn walks back with isWalkingBack, keeping its health). The model instead turns a fleeing foe that gets 8 tiles away (escape_distance) into fresh_foe at its spawn with FULL health (hunting_pack foe step, escapes + 1). Candidate F35 (scratch d20-patch -F35): the escaped foe still returns to its spawn tile (the walk-back, kept as a jump) but keeps its current life, so a troll that fled at 15 or less dies to the next hit. Score on the current bundle (with F34): d21-score -W 0 -Flags F35 (100 held-out runs, the D37 criteria: arms, hunter clears 57-85, hunter hits 19.37-24.93, hunting-ground kills d_kills, retreat (b)) plus troll-times -Flags F35 (solo troll kills by phase vs live PR-23 9.4 total, 3.9 in 200-301 s). Adopt (B-style, as F34) only if every criterion the bundle passes today still passes AND solo troll kills move toward live without passing it (more than 8.44 and at most 9.4 + its live 95 percent half-width from PR-23); then pack_sim pins refresh under the D38B rules. Otherwise F35 stays a flag and the result is named here. Known second approximation in the same path, left for after P4 (one change at a time): the model calls a flee an escape at 8 tiles (escape_distance), while a Canary monster drops its target only when the knight leaves its +-11 view (updateTargetList canSee), and then walks back to its spawn over time instead of jumping.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_46 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 46u32, "2026-10-22T22:01Z", "tibia-lane", "P4 band computed (no rule change): PR-23 solo troll kills per hunt 10 8 10 10 7 8 10 9 11 11, mean 9.4, sd 1.35, 95 percent half-width 0.97, so the P4 troll condition is more than 8.44 and at most 10.37 solo troll kills per hunt.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_47 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 47u32, "2026-10-22T22:09Z", "tibia-lane", "P4 VERDICT: F35 REJECTED (stays a scratch flag), and escapes are ruled out as the troll-gap mechanism. Jobs tibia-p4-f35-score and tibia-p4-f35-trolls (18:01-18:08, src with F34, F35 verified in every work copy): every output is bit-identical to the bundle, i.e. arms (t -1.02 .. 1.61), hunter clears 76, hits 20.93, hunting-ground kills 13.18, retreat (b) 11.73 t 0.45, and solo troll kills 8.44 per hunt with the same phase bins (1.99, 0.05, 2.10, 1.36, 2.94). So in the scored scenarios no fleeing foe ever reaches the 8-tile escape, and the reset-to-full-health path never runs; the P4 troll condition (more than 8.44) fails on equality. The escape-distance approximation (8 vs Canary view 11) is moot for the same reason. Ruled out for the late-troll shortfall: respawn timing, knight position, view range, wake time, escapes. Left: the time from a far troll wake to its death (walking from the far spawn, the knight target order among the late crowd, and the second cyclops spawn at 1953,1346 sharing that ground). Next model output: wake-to-death per far spawn with the knight target at each tick.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_48 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 48u32, "2026-10-22T22:13Z", "tibia-lane", "Far trolls wait behind cyclops fights in the model (job tibia-troll-chase, solo, 100 hunts, src with F34): wake-to-death median 31 s (1941,1354, 87 hunts) and 36.4 s (1953,1355, 80 hunts), of which the knight targets a cyclops for a median 26.7 and 26.4 s; near trolls die in 4.4 and 10.3 s with no cyclops in between. Live check (10 PR-23 logs, every competitor target change, approach <name> when out of reach and strike <name> when adjacent, 204 changes): 1 switch from a cyclops to a troll before that cyclops loot line in 10 hunts, so no evidence that the live knight breaks off cyclops fights either (current-first targeting in both). Left for the late-troll shortfall: how long a cyclops fight lasts once two monsters stand on the knight (the cyclops kill rate per adjacent second was matched on PR-15, notes on POTION-FIDELITY, so a mismatch would have to be in how often a late troll is adjacent while the knight fights the cyclops, i.e. ordering, not damage). Stopping here for today; the next trace is per-tick target and adjacency for the late ground.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_49 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 49u32, "2026-10-22T22:34Z", "tibia-lane", "Late target changes (after 200 s; model job tibia-troll-late, solo, 100 hunts, src with F34; live 10 PR-23 logs, every strike/approach line timed by the latest @t stamp before it): cyclops engagements match (live 1.2 per hunt, model 1.30), troll engagements do not (live 7.1 per hunt, model 3.09), against late troll kills live 3.9 and model 2.94. So the live knight engages about 1.8 trolls per late troll kill and the model about 1.05: live trolls are re-engaged after the knight loses them (a troll that flees at runHealth 15 and is picked up again is the likely pattern, untested), and live also reaches more distinct late trolls. Next session: in the live logs, whether consecutive troll engagements repeat the same troll (same tile neighbourhood within seconds) vs new trolls, and the same count in the model, before any candidate. Live half done 18:36: of the 71 live late troll engagements, 33 follow another troll engagement within 2 tiles with no troll loot between (likely the same troll re-engaged), 38 do not (3.8 distinct trolls per hunt, matching the 3.9 late troll kills); so the re-engagements are a side behavior and the gap is the number of distinct late trolls the knight reaches (live about 3.8, model about 3.0 per hunt). Also ruled out 18:37: a hunt-length artifact (live loot after 300 s once in 10 hunts, at 301.1 s; last loots 221-301 s).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_50 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 50u32, "2026-10-22T23:26Z", "tibia-lane", "Distinct late trolls, model side (job tibia-troll-distinct, 19:2x, solo, 100 held-out hunts, src with F34; a troll life = slot plus deaths since 200 s, an engagement = a change of the knight target after 200 s): 3.09 troll engagements per hunt, 3.08 distinct troll lives, 0.01 re-engagements of the same life, 1.30 cyclops engagements. Live PR-23 (note 49 method): 7.1 troll engagements, 3.8 distinct, 3.3 re-engagements, 1.2 cyclops. Only the distinct count is comparable: the live competitor writes the same strike line several times at one stamp (hunt 110328: each late engagement logged as 4 identical strike Troll lines), and note 49's grouping puts those copies in the same-troll bucket, so the 3.3 live re-engagements are mostly duplicate log lines, not behavior, while the distinct count 3.8 stands. Distinct late trolls 3.08 vs 3.8 is the same 0.7 to 1 per hunt as late kills (2.94 vs 3.9): the model knight reaches fewer different trolls after 200 s and kills nearly every one it reaches. Descriptive; no rule changed. Next: per spawn, when each far troll is first targeted after its wake (model), against the live knight's position track, before any candidate.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_51 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 51u32, "2026-10-22T23:31Z", "tibia-lane", "Late troll kills come in clusters live, rarely in the model (job tibia-troll-gaps, 19:3x, solo, 100 held-out hunts, src with F34, a troll kill = experience +20 in a tick; live from the 10 PR-23 logs, Loot of a troll @t stamps; same definitions both sides, kills from 200 s on). Per hunt: late troll kills model 2.94 vs live 4.0 (live includes the one 301.1 s kill of note 49); kills within 10 s of the previous troll kill model 1.15 vs live 1.9; the others model 1.79 vs live 2.1; median gap to the previous troll kill model 33.2 s vs live 16.0 s; kills at 280-301 s model 0.83 vs live 1.1. So about three quarters of the late shortfall (0.75 of 1.06) is back-to-back kills: live, several trolls stand at the knight at once and die 2-8 s apart (one hunt: 288, 292, 296, 298 s), which the model produces less often. Kill speed per troll is not the gap (combat matched on PR-15, and a 2-8 s back-to-back gap needs the trolls already adjacent); arrival together is. Live has only 10 hunts, so read the split as direction, not size. Next model output: how many trolls are within 1 tile of the knight when each late troll dies, against the live seen-at lines at the loot stamps.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_52 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 52u32, "2026-10-22T23:35Z", "tibia-lane", "Crowding at late troll deaths (job tibia-troll-adjacent, 19:3x, solo, 100 held-out hunts, src with F34: live trolls within 1 tile of the knight in the tick before each kill after 200 s; live from the 10 PR-23 logs, trolls within 1 tile in the last seen-at line before each late troll loot). Model, 294 deaths: 0 trolls 1, 1 troll 230, 2 trolls 61, 3 trolls 2, so two or more in 21 percent (0.63 per hunt). Live, 40 deaths: 0 in 10 (seen-at lines older than the death), 1 in 19, 2 in 8, 3 in 3, so two or more in 28 percent of all (1.1 per hunt) and 37 percent of the 30 with a current line. Same direction as note 51: live trolls reach the knight together more often, and the back-to-back kills follow from that. 10 live hunts, so direction only. Open for the next session: why more trolls arrive together live (far trolls waking while the knight fights a near one, their walking pace, or the model's single-file approach on the late ground), with the per-troll arrival trace before any candidate.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_53 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 53u32, "2026-10-22T23:42Z", "tibia-lane", "Where the late clusters come from (19:3x-19:5x; model jobs tibia-troll-clusters and tibia-cyclops-fights, solo, 100 held-out hunts, src with F34; live from the 10 PR-23 logs). Clusters (a late troll kill within 10 s of the previous, counted once): model 1.08 per hunt, 106 of 108 starting within 20 s after a cyclops kill; live 1.2 per hunt, 11 of 12 after a cyclops loot. So both have the same mechanism and the same count: trolls queue while the knight fights a cyclops and die back to back after it. The difference is cluster size: live about 2.6 trolls per cluster (19 quick kills over 12 clusters), model about 2.1 (115 over 108), about 0.5 troll per cluster or 0.6 kills per hunt, most of the late gap. Cyclops fights ending after 150 s match in count and length: model 2.71 per hunt, engage to kill median 28.8 s, mean 29.3 s; live 2.7 per hunt (27 fights), median 27.8 s, mean 28.5 s, sd 6.5. CORRECTED 19:5x: a first reading gave live a 35.9 s mean, but approach and strike lines carry no @t stamp and the parser took the last stamped line before them, which in 7 fights was 18-40 s stale (one fight: last stamp 119.3 s, knight's first move toward the cyclops 154.5 s); timing each engage by the first stamp after its line gives the numbers above. So fight length does not explain the larger live clusters. Open: how many trolls are awake and heading for the knight when a late cyclops fight starts; next output is troll positions (live seen-at lines, model slots) at the start of each late cyclops fight.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_54 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 54u32, "2026-10-22T23:45Z", "tibia-lane", "Split of note 53 (19:4x; model job tibia-cyclops-steps, same 271 late cyclops fights; live from the same 27 fights, counted by lines between the engage line and the loot, so unaffected by the stamp correction of note 53): knight steps per late cyclops fight model mean 4.9, median 6; live mean 2.8, median 4, with a cyclops within 1 tile in 95 percent of the live seen-at lines during the fight. The model knight walks a little more during these fights; with fight lengths now matching (note 53, corrected) this is a side difference, not the cluster cause. Descriptive; no rule changed.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_55 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 55u32, "2026-10-22T23:47Z", "tibia-lane", "Live hit cadence in the 27 late cyclops fights (PR-23 logs, no compile; server text lines carry exact stamps): per fight 260 cyclops damage (its full health) in 11.5 landed hits, 0 damage on trolls (no unlogged target swaps), 8.4 potion or heal lines. Gaps between landed hits fall at 2.0 s (224 of 284) or whole multiples (4 s 51, 6 s 5, 8 s 3, 10 s 1), i.e. one swing every 2000 ms and about 79 percent landing (284 hits over 358 swing intervals); first to last landed hit 26.6 s mean. Together with the corrected fight lengths of note 53 (live 28.5 s, model 29.3 s), the fights match and this line of inquiry is closed. Source check kept for reference: potions do not delay swings (client.spi drink uses useInventoryItemWith -> game.cpp playerUseWithCreature; potions are ITEM_TYPE_POTION so ItemType::triggerExhaustion is true, items.cpp:127, and only the potion task is cleared, while Player::doAttacking's swing check sits in setNextActionTask with classicAttackSpeed = false; only a plain playerUseItem clears that, game.cpp:4605).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_56 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 56u32, "2026-10-22T23:54Z", "tibia-lane", "Troll queue at late cyclops fights (19:5x; model job tibia-queue-at-cyclops, solo, 100 held-out hunts, src with F34; live from the 10 PR-23 logs, seen-at lines). At the START of a late cyclops fight live trolls are almost never in the client view (first seen-at line after the engage: 0 trolls in 25 of 27, 1 in 2), so the queue forms during the fight. At the cyclops KILL (cyclops kills after 150 s; model: trolls within 1 tile in the tick before the kill; live: the last seen-at line before the loot): model 271 kills, mean 1.10 trolls adjacent, distribution 0:100 1:45 2:126, never 3; live 28 kills, mean 1.21, distribution 0:9 1:7 2:9 3:3 (some 0 rows are stale lines). The mean queue is close; the visible difference is the tail: live has three trolls on the knight at 3 of 28 cyclops kills, the model never in 271. With 10 live hunts this is a lead, not a finding. Next session: whether a third adjacent tile is free in the model at those moments (terrain around the late fight spots 1948-1952,1341-1349 plus the cyclops tile) or a third troll is awake and walking but held back (taken_by_others, path), before any candidate.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_57 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 57u32, "2026-10-22T23:57Z", "tibia-lane", "Model troll reach at late cyclops kills (job tibia-troll-reach, 19:5x, 271 kills after 150 s): trolls within 1 tile mean 1.10, within 3 1.15, within 11 1.24 (live, client view about 8 by 6: 1.32 in view, 1.21 within 1); at the 126 kills with two trolls adjacent a third troll is within 3 tiles in 0 and within 11 in 1. (The alive-anywhere count came out 4.00 at every kill because a slain foe waiting for RespawningAfter is buried_foe, a fresh_foe with full health at position 0,0,15 until returns_at (hunting_pack.spi:158, :939), so it counts as alive but is never within 11 tiles; the distance counts are unaffected.) Reading for today: the model's queue at a cyclops kill and the trolls around it match live within the live sample; the remaining late-kill difference sits in the cluster sizes of note 53 (live 2.6 vs model 2.1 trolls per cluster), which rest on only 12 live clusters in 10 hunts. Stamp check for note 49 (after the note 53 correction): its live late troll lines number 71 timed by the latest earlier stamp and 72 by the first later stamp, so its counts stand. Decision, mechanical: no more model tracing on the late gap until more live hunting-ground hunts exist; the next live block should add solo hunting-ground hunts (same settings as PR-23) so cluster size and late kills have about twice the live sample before any candidate (prepared as PR-23B in scratch block-next-session.ps1, TIBIA umbrella note).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_58 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 58u32, "2026-10-23T00:33Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P5, written 20:3x before PR-23B (10 solo hunting-ground hunts, PR-23 settings; the last part of tonight's live window, scratch night-block.ps1, job tibia-night-block3; BENCHMARK PR-23B). Pool PR-23 and PR-23B (20 hunts) and recompute with the note 51/53 definitions (troll kills from 200 s, cluster = a kill within 10 s of the previous, counted once; cluster size = kills in it). (a) If the pooled live late troll kills per hunt minus the model's 2.94 has a 95 percent interval (Welch, per-hunt values) that excludes 0 AND the pooled cluster size exceeds the model's 2.1 by more than its standard error, the gap is real: the next model output is the per-troll arrival trace (wake tick, first tile within 3 of the knight, death tick) during late cyclops fights. (b) If the late-kill interval includes 0, the late-troll thread closes as within live noise, and PARTY moves on to a duo rerun with candidate 2 (source target order), whose only failed P3 criterion was these kills; its pre-registration is drafted (scratch pr25-candidate2-draft.md, predictions in note 63). (c) Interval excludes 0 but cluster size does not differ: the gap is real but not in the clusters; next is the per-spawn late-kill attribution with server-side spawn logging (the hunting-ground probe of note 62). Baseline before PR-23B, PR-23 alone: late troll kills per hunt 4 4 5 5 2 2 6 2 5 5 (mean 4.0, sd 1.49, including the 301.1 s kill), difference to the model 1.06 with an approximate 95 percent interval -0.01 to 2.13 (t9, model error ignored), so today's 10 hunts do not settle it.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_59 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 59u32, "2026-10-23T00:37Z", "tibia-lane", "Baseline for rule P5's second condition (note 58), PR-23 alone, written before PR-23B: live late clusters 12, sizes 4 4 3 2 2 2 4 2 2 2 2 2, mean 2.58, standard error 0.26; model 2.06 (1 + 115 quick kills / 108 clusters, note 53). The difference 0.52 already exceeds one live standard error, so with PR-23B the first condition (late kills, note 58 baseline interval -0.01 to 2.13) is the deciding one.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_60 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 60u32, "2026-10-23T03:16Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-25 pre-registration for candidate 2 drafted (scratch pr25-candidate2-draft.md, 23:2x), for rule P5 outcome (b) only. It needs its four numbers (party potions, party kills, ally potion share, deaths) from one fresh prediction job on the tree with F34; the 15:31 candidate 2 numbers predate F34 and are not to be used. Tests as P1: party kills and party potions, one-sample t, critical 2.26; the ally share is descriptive. The older scratch pr25-draft.md (candidate 1) is superseded.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_61 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 61u32, "2026-10-23T03:25Z", "tibia-lane", "Method of record for rule P5's first condition (checked on PR-23 at 23:2x, before PR-23B): eoie table logs <hunting-ground logs> --count late_trolls='Loot of a troll.*@t\\+([2-9]\\d{5}|\\d{7,})' --out <tsv>, then eoie table test <tsv> --test one-sample --metric late_trolls --arms all --prediction 2.94. On the 10 PR-23 logs: mean 4.0, t 2.2486, df 9, p 0.051, 95 percent interval 2.93 to 5.07 (includes the model's 2.94, so not settled; this matches note 58's manual baseline). For PR-23B the same two commands run on the 20 pooled logs. The cluster-size condition needs sequences (EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES); until that verb exists it is computed with the note 59 definition by hand.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_62 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 62u32, "2026-10-23T03:31Z", "tibia-lane", "Hunting-ground server probe drafted for rule P5 outcomes (a) and (c) (scratch server-probe/ground_trace.lua, 23:3x; parses with luac and passes a stub run under plain Lua with a fake Canary API at 00:2x: appear, move, death, gone and a respawned troll's new id all come out as intended; not installed, not part of tonight's block): a 250 ms poll over 16 tiles around 1947,1345 writes every monster's appear, move and gone line, and a death CreatureEvent writes each death with tile, all on the server clock relative to the knight's arrival, to ground-trace.log in Canary's directory. That gives per-troll wake (first move), arrival near the knight and death times, and spawn attribution by appear tile, which the client logs cannot give (note 39: client view 8 by 6). It runs only after P5 says (a) or (c), installed and removed the same way as lead_trace.lua.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_63 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 63u32, "2026-10-23T03:42Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-25 predictions fixed before any PR-25 hunt (job tibia-pr25-predict, 23:4x, scratch duo-predict.ps1 -Targeting -SourceOrder on the tree with F34, 100 held-out hunts): candidate 2 party potions 5.90 (knight 2.67, ally 3.23), party kills 15.33 (cyclops 5.61, trolls 9.72), ally potion share 55 percent, deaths 0; hits taken knight 19.65, ally 21.85. The same job reproduced the duo baseline (15.37 kills, 7.62 potions, ally 6 percent) and solo (13.18, 14.79). F34 moved candidate 2 only slightly (15:31: 5.83 potions, 15.32 kills, 56 percent). Filled into scratch pr25-candidate2-draft.md; used only under rule P5 (b).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_64 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 64u32, "2026-10-23T04:01Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-23B pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md at 2026-10-23 00:00, before any PR-23B hunt: question, scenario, predictions (2.94 late troll kills per hunt; clusters 1.08 per hunt of 2.06 trolls), pooled test commands and the (a)/(b)/(c) decision of rule P5 (note 58). The hunts are the last part of tonight's live window (job tibia-night-block3 since the 00:16 re-queue).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_65 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 65u32, "2026-10-23T04:31Z", "tibia-lane", "Descriptive, outside rule P5 (00:3x, PR-24 duo logs, both knights' loot lines merged per hunt, no compile): late troll kills (from 200 s) 40 in 10 duo hunts, 4.0 per hunt, the same as solo PR-23's 4.0. Two knights kill no more late trolls than one, which points to troll availability (respawn and arrival) rather than kill speed as the limit, and it independently replicates the live level of about 4 (model solo 2.94; model duo late kills not computed). Duo clusters differ: 16 (1.6 per hunt), only 7 starting within 20 s after a cyclops loot, sizes mostly 2 (mean 2.12), as expected when two knights kill in parallel. P5 applies exactly as written (it is defined on solo hunts); any follow-up on troll availability needs its own rule first, whatever P5's outcome.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_66 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 66u32, "2026-10-23T04:33Z", "tibia-lane", "Validity condition for rule P5, written 00:3x before PR-23B. Source: spawn_monster.cpp:359-367 divides each spawn's interval by rateSpawn x boostedrate x eventschedule/100, where boostedrate is 2 for the day's boosted creature (g_game().getBoostedMonsterName()). Re-verified at the same time: the model's 94.2 s respawn (check every gcd(30000, 60000) = 30 s from the death, cleanup sets lastSpawn, spawn at the third check plus 3 x 1400 ms) holds for the conditions of PR-15/23/24: rateSpawn = 1 in config.lua; no spawned kind was boosted (the logs' boosted creature: 10-20 Monk, 10-21 Bandit, 10-22 Wasp); and no spawn event is active (data/json/eventscheduler/events.json holds only 2024-dated events, including Fast Respawn 11/01-11/15/2024 at spawnrate 200, and events_scheduler.cpp parses the full date with the year, so none applies in 2026; the 175 percent Test Event Scheduler lives only in tests/fixture). Condition: if PR-23B's logs show Troll or Cyclops as the boosted creature (or a spawn event becomes active), its respawns run twice as fast, PR-23B is not comparable with PR-23 or the model, P5 is not applied, and PR-23B is rerun on an unboosted day. The same check applies to every future hunting-ground block (spawn rates); arena blocks summon with /m and are unaffected.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_67 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 67u32, "2026-10-23T08:48Z", "tibia-lane", "P5 VERDICT (rule note 58, applied mechanically by scratch p5-eval.ps1 at 04:4x on PR-23's 10 logs plus PR-23B's 10, block job tibia-night-block3): valid, with Wasp boosted in PR-23 and Poison Spider in PR-23B, no spawned kind (note 66 condition). Late troll kills per hunt 3.85, 95% CI 3.22-4.48, against the model's 2.94 (eoie table test one-sample, t 3.02, df 19, p 0.007). Clusters by the note-59 definition: 23, sizes 4 4 3 2 2 2 4 2 2 2 2 2 3 2 2 3 3 3 2 4 4 4 2, mean 2.74 (se 0.18) against 2.06. Outcome (a): the late-troll gap is real and sits in the clusters. Next by the rule: the per-troll arrival trace, with the hunting-ground server probe drafted in scratch (server-probe/ground_trace.lua, stub-tested; logs appear, move, gone and death per monster on the server clock), in a future live window under its own pre-registration. Candidate 2's duo rerun (PR-25, outcome b) does not start. Descriptive, PR-23B against PR-23: late trolls 3.7 vs 4.0, potions 13.9 vs 14.8, kills 13.2 vs 14.2, 0 deaths both. BENCHMARK PR-23B RESULT row written.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_68 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 68u32, "2026-10-23T08:52Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P6, written 04:5x before any PR-26 run, for P5 outcome (a) (note 67). Draft pre-registration PR-26 in scratch pr26-arrival-draft.md; it lands in docs/BENCHMARK.md when a window runs it. PR-23's hunting ground runs 10 hunts with the server probe ground_trace.lua: appear, move, gone and death per monster within 16 tiles, knight out of view and back, on the server clock. Before writing this rule the probe was fixed so that the knight leaving view no longer restarts its clock (tonight's lead-trace lesson) and stub-tested again, and luac passes. No troll can come from outside: the spawn file is the server's only monster file, with 4 troll spawns. Respawn timing already follows the source (note 39). Each killed troll in a late cluster is classified R (respawned after the knight's first sighting and in view until death), F (out of view at least once, then back) or I (present at the first sighting, never out of view). (a) F is 30 percent or more of late-cluster kills: returners, so the first model read is how a troll that leaves and comes back is handled (escape reset to a fresh foe; F35 only kept health), before any candidate. (b) Else, R is 70 percent or more and in at least half the clusters the R appear times lie within 1500 ms: synchronised respawns from different spawns, compared with the model's per-spawn respawn phases before any candidate. (c) Else report only. Valid only without a boosted Troll or Cyclops.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_69 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 69u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 10 h left. PR-26 under P6 (10 hunts with the ground probe, about 1 h live); a model change from P6 outcome a or b, scored and verified live (10 hunts, about 1 h); then PR-25, the duo rerun with candidate 2 (10 duo hunts, about 1 h); close at 100.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_70 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 70u32, "2026-10-23T11:33Z", "tibia-lane", "P6 instrument ready (07:4x, before any PR-26 run): scratch server-probe/read-ground.ps1 -Traces <the 10 kept ground traces> applies rule P6 (note 68) as written. Classes per killed troll in a late cluster: I = present at the first sighting and never out of view; F = out of view at least once; R = first seen within its spawn radius after the sighting and in view until death; other = first seen elsewhere after the sighting (spawned out of view and walked in), which P6 does not name, so a large other share falls to (c). Late = server death at or after knight sighting + 202500 ms (policy start taken as sighting + 2.5 s), clusters by the note-59 gap of 10 s; then the F-share 30 percent test, the R-share 70 percent test and the 1500 ms appear-sync test. Tested on a synthetic trace with known classes (I, R, F and a synced R pair): R 3, F 1, I 1, outcome (c), as hand-computed. Cross-check after the block: late troll deaths per run against the client Loot of a troll count from P5 method. PR-26 is pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md (07:2x) and queued as job tibia-block-pr26 after PC-9.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_71 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 71u32, "2026-10-23T14:43Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-26 load overlap, recorded 10:4x before any PR-26 reading. eoie reported its heavy job eoie-preview-GE2 (tree copy, then the formatter, about 12 percent of the box) ran 10:26:15-10:40:00 beside the PR-26 block, admitted by an eoie admission backfill bug that eoie is filing. By the run end stamps (run 1 10:22:27, 2 10:28:24, 3 10:34:23, 4 10:40:22, about 6 min each), runs 2 (last 2 minutes), 3 and 4 overlap it. PR-26 is diagnostic (no numeric prediction), so the overlap does not void the block. P6 is read twice: on all 10 runs as pre-registered, and on the 7 runs with no overlap (1 and 5-10). Both readings are reported, and any difference between them is named; neither is chosen after a look. Checked 10:5x against eoie job list for 09:52-10:50 (eoie's root cause: the shared job host checked an empty window registry, so every detached job gate passed in that span; fixed 10:50): the only job that ran beside the block was eoie-preview-GE2, 10:26:15-10:40:00. main's jobs in that span printed nothing (queued behind tibia-block-pr26), and eoie's probes 10:44-10:50 lasted 1-2 s each. PC-9 (09:52-10:16) had nothing beside it.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_72 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 72u32, "2026-10-23T15:25Z", "tibia-lane", "P6 APPLIED (rule note 68; PR-26, 10 hunting-ground hunts 10:16-11:15, ground_trace.lua; boosted Poison Spider, so valid): (c), report only, in both readings fixed in note 71. All 10 runs: 96 troll deaths, 12 late clusters with 35 trolls, R 30, F 0, I 5 (R 86 percent, F 0 percent), respawns appearing within 1500 ms in 0 of 12 clusters. The 7 runs with no load beside them: 10 clusters, 30 trolls, R 25, F 0, I 5 (R 83 percent), synced 0 of 10. So the clusters are not returners (F 0) and not synchronised respawns. They are respawns from different spawns that appear seconds to tens of seconds apart and die within 10 s of each other, so they gather on the way to the knight. Method: read-ground.ps1 on the one kept block trace, split into its 10 run sections (canary restarts per run and ids repeat, so each section is read alone). Cross-check: trace troll deaths per run equal the client Loot of a troll count in 9 of 10 runs (run 9: 12 vs 11); late troll deaths 3.9 per hunt against P5's live 3.85. BENCHMARK PR-26 RESULT row written. By the rule no candidate follows. Pointer for the model side, no rule yet: compare model respawn appear times per spawn and the walk-in time to the knight with these live ones (late R trolls appear 190-290 s and die 7-48 s later).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_73 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 73u32, "2026-10-23T15:32Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P7, written 11:4x before any model run it judges (after P6 (c), note 72). Live reference from the 7 clean PR-26 runs (1, 5-10), respawned trolls killed (41), per spawn tile: near spawns 1943,1334 (n 16, appear about 99-104, 189-194 and 281-284 s, life median 10.2 s) and 1950,1345 (n 18, appear about 108-113, 198-203 and 289-293 s, life median 10.6 s), on a cycle of about 90 s; far spawns 1941,1354 (n 4, appear 256-259 s, life median 34.2 s) and 1953,1355 (n 3, appear 257-259 s, life median 47.9 s), once each. Life = server death minus first appear. The late clusters at about 290-305 s are the near spawns' third cycle meeting the far spawns' first respawn. Model check: the hunting-ground model on 7 seeds of its held-out block, the same table per spawn (respawn appear times relative to the first sighting, life median), computed from the simulator's own events in a work copy with src untouched. (a) A far spawn's first respawn in the model differs from live 256-259 s by more than 15 s, or a far spawn's model life median differs from live by more than 30 percent: that is the locus of the late-troll gap, and a candidate (spawn timing or walk-in) gets its own D40-criteria rule before any scoring. (b) Same tests for the near spawns' cycle (more than 15 s off at any of the three cycles) when (a) does not hold: the locus is the near cycle, candidate as in (a). (c) Neither: the model reproduces the respawn timing, the gap is in how the clusters are fought, and that is reported without a candidate. One work-copy compile job.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_74 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 74u32, "2026-10-23T15:39Z", "tibia-lane", "P7 APPLIED (rule note 73; model job tibia-p7-model2: arena_hunt mirroring the current arena, respawning_like_canary 60000, cavebot_with_heal 160, 7 held-out seeds; deaths and respawns read from each troll slot moving onto and off the grave floor z 15; scratch p7-model.ps1 and p7-judge.ps1): (a). Far spawn 1953,1355: model life median 20.1 s against live 47.9 s, more than 30 percent off (thin: model n 1, live n 3). Far spawn 1941,1354 is within bounds (appear 269 vs 257 s, life 28.8 vs 34.2 s). Not part of the verdict, but the sharper signal: the near spawns cycle about every 101 s in the model against about 90 s live (model 99/200 and 105/208 s, live 101/191/282 and 110/200/290 s), so the model's third near cycle falls after the 300 s hunt and live's does not; that alone removes about one troll kill per near spawn from the model's late window. Arithmetic: the model respawns 94.2 s after each death (canary_respawn_ms 60000 = first check 30 s after the death sets lastSpawn, spawn one 60 s interval later, plus 4200 ms). Live, a near troll dying at about 111 s reappears at about 191 s, 80 s later, which fits a check event already running on its own 30 s grid when the troll dies: the next grid point (0-30 s after the death) sets lastSpawn and the spawn comes 60 s later, so the delay is 64-94 s, mean about 79 s. This contradicts note 39's death-relative reading in one place: whether the check event is restarted at each death or keeps running. Next by the rule: the candidate (spawn check on a running grid) gets its own D40-criteria rule (P8) before any scoring, after the Canary source line that settles the event lifecycle (spawn_monster.cpp startSpawnMonsterCheck / checkSpawnMonster) is read.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_75 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 75u32, "2026-10-23T15:44Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P8, written 11:5x before any score (P7 outcome (a), note 74). Source (Canary spawn_monster.cpp): for a non-blockable monster the check that finds it missing schedules the spawn 3 x 1400 ms later (line 304), so the monster is still missing at the end of that check and the check event reschedules itself one more interval (lines 308-311); a death inside that interval finds the event running, and startSpawnMonsterCheck adds none (lines 165-170, called from monster.cpp:366 on the monster's own disappear). The next check runs cleanup, which sets lastSpawn (line 332), and the spawn follows spawntime later. So a troll that dies within about 30 s of respawning comes back at (its respawn check + 30 s) + 60 s + 4.2 s, not at death + 94.2 s: for a near troll dying at 111 s, 189.2 s instead of 205.2 s, matching live (189-191, then 281-284). Each hunting-ground spawn block holds one monster, so the event stops otherwise, and note 39's death-relative reading holds for longer-lived trolls. Candidate S1 (scratch d20-patch.ps1 -S1, work copy only): per foe check_until = the respawn check + 30 s, set when it respawns; a death before check_until returns at check_until + canary_respawn_ms - 30000, otherwise at death + canary_respawn_ms as now. Score: d21-score -W 0 -Flags S1 on today's bundle, 100 held-out runs, D40 criteria unchanged (arms, hunter clears 57-85, hunter hits 19.37-24.93, hunting-ground kills, retreat (b) against live 11.05, (c1) 32.26-53.43, (c2) 42-63, in-reach 21-35). Descriptive: the P7 table again (p7-model with S1) and late troll kills per hunt against P5's live 3.85 and model 2.94. Outcomes as D40: (A) every criterion passes: S1 joins the bundle once build.ps1 is green with moved exact pins refreshed under the D38B rules, and PARTY's next live step is PR-25; (B) every criterion the bundle passes today still passes: same as (A); (C) otherwise rejected, failing criterion named.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_76 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 76u32, "2026-10-23T16:09Z", "tibia-lane", "P8 APPLIED (rule note 75; job tibia-p8-s1-score 11:45-11:55, S1 on today's bundle, 100 held-out runs): (B). Every criterion the bundle passes today still passes, with identical numbers except the kills: cyclops arms t PR-11 -1.02, PR-12 0.80, PR-16 -0.63/1.61, PR-17 0.05/0.12 (a holds); hunter clears 76 and hits 20.93 (hold, as the bundle); retreat (b) 11.73 per clear vs live 11.05, Welch t 0.45 (holds); hunting-ground kills 13.61 per hunt against the bundle's 13.18 (d holds). (c1) 0 percent and (c2) 25 percent fail as they do for the bundle today, so they do not count against S1. By the rule S1 joins the bundle once build.ps1 is green, with moved exact pins refreshed under the D38B rules; then PARTY's next live step is PR-25. Descriptive, P7 table with S1 in src (job tibia-p8-s1-table3, 7 held-out seeds): the near spawns now cycle like live, 1950,1345 at 105/195/284 s (live 110/200/290) and 1943,1334 at 99/189 s (live 101/191; its third cycle falls just past the kills read here); far spawn 1953,1355 walk-in life 43.9 s (live 47.9; was 20.1 with n 1) and first respawn 275 s (live 258); far spawn 1941,1354 269 s and 27.9 s (live 257 and 34.2). Late troll kills (at or after 202.5 s) 3.57 per hunt with S1 against 2.86 before on the same 7 seeds (live 3.85; P5's 100-hunt model 2.94); all troll kills 9.14 against 8.29 (PR-23 live 9.4). So S1 closes most of the late-troll gap. D38B state 14:1x: Cells accept refreshed the route-timing pin and the random-control pin; the cyclops hunting-ground pin is a nested tuple, set by hand; s1-review finds all 8 changed lines numeric literals in place; the plain pack_sim rerun is queued as job tibia-s1-rerun2 behind main's matrix (review-2). Wording note, not an assert: with S1 the cyclops hunting-ground central shuttle (132200 experience, 2 deaths; was 91900 and 10) now beats sweeping (127200), so the test name's 'sweeping leads' holds only against standing.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_77 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 77u32, "2026-10-23T17:10Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE P9, written 13:0x before any run it judges (after P3's kills-only failure, note 36, and P8's (B), note 76). Question: with S1 in the bundle, does candidate 2 (P3's source-ordered targeting) now meet P3's calibration on PR-24? P3 failed only on party kills (15.32 against 15.70-17.50), a kill-rate shortfall shared with the solo arm, and S1 raised solo hunting-ground kills from 13.18 to 13.61 per hunt. Run: scratch duo-predict.ps1 -SourceOrder after S1 is adopted in src (so the work copy holds S1 plus candidate 2), 100 held-out hunts, plus its baseline duo and solo arms. Criteria are P3's, unchanged: party potions in 4.01-6.19, party kills in 15.70-17.50, ally potion share in 25-75 percent. (a) All three hold: PR-25 (the duo rerun with candidate 2) is pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md with these model numbers as its predictions before any live run, and runs in a live window. (b) Kills still below 15.70 while the other two hold: the shortfall remains, it is named with the new number, and no PR-25 runs. (c) Potions or share now outside their bands: S1 interacts with candidate 2; report, and no PR-25. Runs only if S1 is adopted; if the S1 rerun is red, this rule waits.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_78 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 78u32, "2026-10-23T19:07Z", "tibia-lane", "S1 ADOPTED under D38B (15:0x). Plain reruns with S1 in src: pack_sim 39 of 39 green (job tibia-s1-rerun2), hunt_sim 11 of 11 (job tibia-s1-rerun), hunting_pack has no runnable test cells. Pins refreshed in src/pack_sim.spi, every change a numeric model-output literal in place (scratch s1-review.ps1: 8 lines): the hunting-ground route timings, the random-control tuple, and the cyclops hunting-ground tactic table (nested, set by hand). src/hunting_pack.spi: foe check_until, set at each respawn to the respawn check + 30 s, and a death before it returns on the running check grid (spawn_monster.cpp 165-170, 304, 308-311). The two parenthesized match conditions in foe_turn keep their parentheses (SPIRAL-FORMATTER note 80: same meaning either way). Next by rule P9 (note 77): candidate 2's duo prediction with S1, job tibia-p9-duo.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_79 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 79u32, "2026-10-23T19:12Z", "tibia-lane", "P9 APPLIED (rule note 77; job tibia-p9-duo, 15:0x-15:1x, S1 in src plus candidate 2, 100 held-out hunts; the same job ran the baseline duo and solo arms): (a), all three hold. Candidate 2 duo: party kills 16.4 (band 15.70-17.50; P3 had 15.32), party potions 6.09 (band 4.01-6.19), ally potion share 54 percent (band 25-75; knight 2.83, ally 3.26), 0 deaths. By kind: cyclops 5.63 and trolls 10.77 per hunt, against PR-24 live 5.8 and 10.8. Hits taken: knight 20.26, ally 21.8 (live 18.8 and 20.3). Baseline duo arm with S1: kills 16.43, potions 7.97, share 5 percent, so the targeting candidate fixes the split and the potions while S1 fixes the kills. Solo arm with S1: 13.61 kills (PR-23 live 14.2). By the rule PR-25 is pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md (15:1x) with these predictions before any run. It runs in the next tibia live window, which comes after main's cells-pack (job-1792766412460-main-cells-pack, 19:00 at the earliest), so not in this session.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_80 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 80u32, "2026-10-23T19:16Z", "tibia-lane", "D38B addendum for S1, written 15:2x before the full build reports: gpu_sim pins the same hunting-ground hunt twice, as a C oracle literal (22, 1350, 19, 0) and as a WGSL equality chain on the same four numbers. Both use arena_hunt () with RespawningAfter 60000, so S1 can move them. The pair's property is that the GPU twin equals the oracle. If both cells fail, the oracle's new tuple is read from the C cell's assert line and the WGSL run is checked to give exactly that tuple, by setting its equality chain to the new numbers and rerunning. Both pins then carry the same new numbers, a literal change in place. If the WGSL run gives anything else, the twin property is broken: S1 is restored (scratch s1-accept.ps1 -Restore) and the divergence is named.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_81 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 81u32, "2026-10-23T19:33Z", "tibia-lane", "S1 build scope, 15:3x: tibia-s1-full-build was stopped by me after 7 of 32 files, all green (activity, arena_host, blessings, block_plan, catalog, character, coins), to free the compile windows for main's compiler install. A grep of src and apps for arena_hunt, cyclops_hunting_ground and respawning_like_canary finds gpu_sim as the only test file outside pack_sim and hunt_sim that runs the respawn path S1 changes (apps/predict is not a build.ps1 test file). So P8's build.ps1 condition reduces to gpu_sim under the twin-pin rule of note 80, which runs after main's install. The rest of build.ps1 runs as the next session's first full gate.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_82 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 82u32, "2026-10-23T20:52Z", "tibia-lane", "S1 build condition met (16:5x, P8): gpu_sim 6 of 6 green, no memo (job tibia-s1-gpu3). The hunting-ground GPU twin reproduces the oracle's new numbers (23 kills, 1370 experience, 24 potions, 0 deaths; were 22, 1350, 19, 0), so S1 holds on the CPU oracle and the WGSL twin alike. The earlier rerun's WGSL timeout at the 300 s per-cell cap was load (this run: 247 s). With pack_sim 39 of 39, hunt_sim 11 of 11 and gpu_sim 6 of 6, every test file on S1's code path is green (note 81). The other 25 build.ps1 files run as the next session's first full gate.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_83 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 83u32, "2026-10-24T00:32Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-25 RESULT (20:30; 10 duo hunts 19:28-20:29, all counted, no crash; boosted Poison Spider; the first duo block on the Rust launcher): both tests fail, so candidate 2 with S1 does not predict fresh duo hunts. Party kills 15.5 per hunt (95 percent CI 14.66-16.34) against 16.4, t -2.41; party potions 8.0 (CI 6.22-9.78) against 6.09, t 2.42 (df 9, critical 2.26; per-hunt sums kills 15 16 16 16 14 16 13 16 17 16, potions 7 6 5 9 9 8 6 6 12 12). By the rule the remaining gap is named here. GAP NAMED: the split. Live today the knight took the pressure: potions knight 6.9 against ally 1.1 per hunt (ally share about 14 percent), damage taken 37.1 against 13.3 (block secondary). The model predicted a 54 percent ally share, and PR-24 live had about 45 percent (knight 2.8, ally 2.3). Live also moved between the two duo days: PR-24 16.6 kills and 5.1 potions, PR-25 15.5 and 8.0. The harness since P3 enforces login order knight then ally, and per the source facts of note 34 the last login should be first in a pre-existing cyclops's targetList, so live favouring the knight contradicts candidate 2's assumption (a). Next, CPU-light: read PR-25's world.log login order and the first-hit times per knight (as note 32 did for PR-24) before any new rule; whether the login order actually held tonight decides between a harness problem and a model one. Login read, 20:3x: canary world.log keeps only the last run, and in hunt 10 the knight logged in at 20:24:10 and the ally at 20:24:29, 19 s later. So the order held, but candidate 2's case (a) assumes both knights come newly into view within the same think at the hunt start; with a 19 s gap the cyclopses take the knight before the ally exists, and target persistence keeps it, which fits the lopsided split (ally share 14 percent). In PR-24 the logins were racy and nearly simultaneous, and the split was even (45 percent). Leading reading: a harness timing artifact (the enforced knight-then-ally order waits for the knight's login line before starting the ally client). Next: per-hunt login gaps from the client logs (the world.log of earlier hunts is overwritten), then a rule choosing between modelling the gap and making the harness log both knights in within one think.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_84 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 84u32, "2026-10-24T03:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:25 Login gaps read (note 83's next step), CPU-light: per hunt, the ally's policy-start wall second minus the knight's (client logs, scratch pr25-logs and arena-logs): PR-25 13 11 19 19 15 12 12 12 13 19 s (mean 14.5); PR-24 2 -1 4 5 1 1 1 4 1 1 1 s (mean 1.8, 11 hunts). Cause in the harness: live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 174 starts the ally's run-probe with -AfterLine 'Knight Sample has logged in.', and live/run-probe.ps1 10-16 waits for that world-log line before it launches the client at all, so the ally's whole OTClient boot plus the probe's 3000 ms login delay (apps/arena/probe.spi 70) comes after the knight is already in game. PR-24 booted both clients in parallel (racy order, gap about 2 s, ally share 45 percent); PR-25 serialized them (gap about 15 s, ally share 14 percent). RULE P10, written now, before any harness change or duo run: candidate 2's case (a) and the arena's competitor protocol treat login as a scripted moment, not as client boot time, so the choice is (H) fix the harness, not (M) model the gap. (H): both clients boot in parallel; the ally's probe holds only its loginWorld until a gate file appears in its user dir (TIBIA_LOGIN_GATE, polled every 50 ms), and the harness writes that file as soon as the world log shows the knight's login line, then logs the ally's login-line delay in ms. Acceptance in the next duo block (PR-27, pre-registered in BENCHMARK before it runs): the ally's login line within 1000 ms after the knight's in at least 8 of 10 hunts; then PR-25's two tests are rerun unchanged on PR-27 (party kills against 16.4, party potions against 6.09, one-sample t, critical 2.26). If fewer than 8 of 10 hunts meet the gap, (H) failed and (M) gets its own rule. One duo window.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_85 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 85u32, "2026-10-24T05:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:05 tibia: rule P10 landed (job tibia-p10-land-2, exit 0). The patch has 4 sites: client file_exists, the probe gate helpers, the probe login waiting on the gate, and the harness booting both clients and writing the gate file at the knight login line. The formatter check is clean, and 9 of 9 fresh arena Lua files carry TIBIA_LOGIN_GATE. The first try failed before compiling because the land script sourced spiral lib.ps1 without polyglot core.ps1 (ResolveLink missing), and it restored itself. The full build on main's 22:27 compiler is 34 of 34 green after a rerun of pack_sim and views, which had crashed the compiler host at about 1.8 GiB free. PR-27 mechanics were added to BENCHMARK before any run. block.ps1 -Runs 11: run 1 is the smoke hunt (void the window if its knight log has no ally login delay), and runs 2-11 are counted. Acceptance is read from knight logs via table logs --count gated plus --last delay, then table query --where gated>0 --where delay<=1000 (an empty cell passes delay<=1000; reported to eoie). The runner is scratch block-pr27.ps1, and the read is a PR-27 section in scratch post-block.ps1, stub-checked. The window was asked of main at 01:0x.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_86 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 86u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:24 tibia: user feedback during the night: stop adding hand-written Lua. Rule P10 as landed (note 85) added a $'g_resources.fileExists' binding to apps/arena/client.spi, and its probe gate check used lua.joined. Both are replaced, without changing behaviour, through lib. (1) A lib slice gives file_system.file_exists a Lua arm (io.open; OTClient opens the standard libraries, luainterface.cpp:791) plus a lua test (main.lua true, a missing name false). It's an eoie do program with 2 anchored replaces and an undo, round-tripped byte-exact on a copy. Gate job tibia-lib-lua-file-exists-gate (heavy, after the block), then the kit land. (2) scratch gate-via-lib.ps1 removes the client binding, makes the probe call file_system.file_exists gate, and has the harness pass the absolute gate file path instead of the PhysFS-relative name. The dry run on the tree matched 1 of 1 for each of the 3 anchors. It runs after the lib land and after PR-27's data is in, so PR-27 keeps the harness it was registered with. Older hand-written Lua in the live harness (bot-lua-dofile.lua, the nexbot trace snippet in run-policy-scenario.ps1) predates tonight and isn't touched here.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_87 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 87u32, "2026-10-24T07:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:44 tibia: PR-27 RESULT (BENCHMARK). 11 duo hunts 02:34-03:39 in one live-gui window. The smoke hunt (run 1) was valid with an 812 ms gate delay; no crash, boosted Hunter. Acceptance (P10) FAILED by the pre-written rule: the ally's login line came within 1000 ms of the knight's in 4 of 10 counted hunts, against the 8 needed. So the kill and potion tests are not read (they were printed by the read script, and no decision uses them). Delays per counted hunt in ms: 746 745 618 934 1116 1193 1318 2346 1847 1520 (median 1155, mean 1238). PR-25 had 11-19 s and PR-24 -1 to 5 s, so the gate cut the gap about twelvefold, but the delay grew through the block (about 0.7 s in hunts 2-4, 1.1-2.3 s in hunts 6-11). By the pre-registration, modelling the remaining gap gets its own rule, which is the next step on this item: either move the model's ally-entry time to the measured delay distribution, or shrink the harness side further (the gate poll is 50 ms, so most of the 0.6-2.3 s is the ally client's loginWorld round trip after the gate opens). That choice is decided by a rule written before any new run.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_88 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 88u32, "2026-10-24T07:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:44 tibia: RULE P11, written before any new run or model change, per PR-27's pre-registration ('modelling the gap gets its own rule'). (1) Data hygiene: post-block.ps1 printed PR-27's kill and potion sums, so those outcomes are seen and excluded from every future test. Only PR-27's login delays (harness facts, not outcomes) may feed a model. (2) Side: the model, not the harness. The delay is the ally client's loginWorld round trip after the gate opens (the poll is 50 ms), so harness work can't push it reliably under 1000 ms, and it drifted upward through the block. The model's ally therefore enters after a delay drawn from PR-27's 10 measured values (618-2346 ms, sampled per held-out seed), with candidate 2, S1, camp.party 2 and the adopted bundle unchanged. (3) New predictions on the same 100 held-out seeds, recorded in BENCHMARK as PR-28's pre-registration before any hunt. (4) Test: PR-28 = 10 fresh counted duo hunts plus one smoke hunt, the same P10 harness and arguments, one-sample t against the new kill and potion predictions, critical 2.26 (9 df). Descriptive acceptance only: log the delays and compare their distribution with PR-27's, with no threshold, since the model now carries the delay. (5) Decision: both hold, candidate 2 with modelled entry becomes the duo default and the 10-14 saturation sweep reruns on it; either fails, the gap is named from the per-knight split on this item, with no adoption.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_89 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 89u32, "2026-10-24T08:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:43 tibia: P11 implementation design (src/hunting_pack.spi), written before code. (1) camp gains ally_enters : ally_entry = EntersWithTheKnight | EntersAfterMeasuredDelay, defaulting to EntersWithTheKnight in every existing camp constructor (lines 281, 435, 1591), so every pinned result stays identical. (2) pack gains ally_in_world : bool. start sets it true unless party > 1 with EntersAfterMeasuredDelay, in which case the ally starts at terrain.grave () with ally_in_world false, so foes can't target a knight that hasn't logged in and a dead ally is never confused with one not yet entered. (3) advance computes entry_ms from the seed, delays[rng.roll_between 0 9 (key seed 0 0 purpose_entry)] over PR-27's 10 counted values (746 745 618 934 1116 1193 1318 2346 1847 1520), and on the first tick with now >= entry_ms places the ally at knight_start camp 1 with ally_in_world true and next_decision_at = now. Until then turned (knight_turn ...) is skipped for the ally. (4) The C and WGSL mirrors take the same field (the pack layout changes, so the hunt_sim WGSL struct and its size pins move); those pins are refreshed under D38B with a numeric-only review. (5) PR-28's pre-registration takes its predictions from 100 held-out seeds with EntersAfterMeasuredDelay, written to BENCHMARK before any hunt.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_90 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 90u32, "2026-10-24T11:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:01 tibia: the file_system Lua file_exists slice (granular form) went RED in its kit gate at 06:5x (job tibia-lib-lua-file-exists-gate-2), so its chained land was skipped automatically and lib file_system.spi is unchanged. Candidate 16 of 21 with 0 type errors; baseline 20 of 20. Candidate-only reds: file_exists_on_lua_tells_files_from_missing_paths/lua, path_join_without_a_native_arm/lua, find_parent/fsharp, find_parent_2/rust, trace_file/fsharp. Leading hypothesis, from the gotcha log ('!!!!BackendSwitch partially evaluates every arm'): the new Lua arm's nominal lua_file and its io.open, ~= nil and :close() primitives reach the F# and Rust builds of every file_exists caller (find_parent, trace_file), and path_join's Lua path picks up the same text. Morning: test the hypothesis in a lab (one file_exists caller compiled for fsharp and rust with the arm), then guard the arm the way lib's per-backend keys do, and re-gate. Until then, gate-via-lib and the S1, S2 and S3a chain wait, because gate-via-lib needs this Lua arm. CORRECTION 2026-10-24 21:34: the hypothesis was wrong. The gate's raw notebook (spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-LUA-FILE-EXISTS) shows all 5 reds are unrelated to the arm. find_parent/fsharp, find_parent_2/rust and trace_file/fsharp search upward for 'workspace' and README.md, and the candidate's lib copy sat in %TEMP% while the baseline ran on the real tree. That was a location asymmetry in the kit gate, now fixed: the baseline runs on an unchanged copy beside the candidate. path_join_without_a_native_arm/lua timed out at 300 s under memory pressure (baseline 24 s). file_exists_on_lua's 'main.lua' assumed a working directory the spiral lua runner doesn't use. The redo is lib slice lua-file-io (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_91 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 91u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:19 tibia: grooming re-size. Progress 92 to 60 (estimate 600 min unchanged): about 240 min are left by the per-item sum in TIBIA note 42. P11's sim change with D38B pins is about 90, the 100-seed predictions job about 30, PR-28's pre-registration about 15, the live window about 80, the read about 15. The 92 predated PR-25's and PR-27's failed pre-registered checks; both moved work back into the model (notes 83, 87-89). The file_exists Lua arm RED (note 90) doesn't gate PARTY: PR-28 can run on the current harness, whose P10 macro is replaced only after that arm lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_92 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 92u32, "2026-10-25T10:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 06:44 P11 implemented per note 89 (scratch party-p11.ps1, 10 anchored edits in edition-3 src/hunting_pack.spi): ally_entry union (EntersWithTheKnight | EntersAfterMeasuredDelay), a camp field set to EntersWithTheKnight in all 3 constructors so every pinned result keeps today's entry, with_delayed_ally_entry, pack.ally_in_world, start parking a delayed ally at terrain.grave (), measured_entry_ms over PR-27's 10 counted delays (746 745 618 934 1116 1193 1318 2346 1847 1520), ally_entry_ms drawn per seed with purpose_entry 20, ally_entered run at the top of advance (places the ally at knight_start camp 1 with next_decision_at = now), and knights_act giving the ally its turn only once it's in the world. One simplification from the design: foe_turn only retargets toward the nearer knight and the grave is off-map, so a not-yet-entered ally can't be targeted without extra gating. Guarded land with hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim and gpu_sim tests, memo off, queued as heavy job-1792924982927 (restores on red). Expected red, per note 89: WGSL struct-size pins if pack's layout shows in them, which get a numeric-only refresh under D38B. Next: predictions from 100 held-out seeds with with_delayed_ally_entry, then PR-28's pre-registration in BENCHMARK.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_93 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 93u32, "2026-10-25T12:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:00 Candidate 2 lands with P11. Candidate 2 (the PR-25/PR-27 arm) had never landed in src: it lived as two words-spelled patch scripts (scratch party-target-patch.ps1 and party-target-patch2.ps1) applied to predict work copies, so it no longer applied after the edition-3 respell. It is already an off-by-default option (camp.party_targeting, targeting_like_canary), so it moves into src unchanged: patched on respell-repo's words copy and respelled, with a control check that the unpatched copy respells to today's tree byte for byte. P11 is applied on top (off by default), so both options are in src with every pin unchanged, and PR-28's predict arm is plain src: ground |> with_party 2 |> targeting_like_canary |> with_delayed_ally_entry. The first combined land (job-1792925890260) restored itself on a P11 type error: a record update can't take a field access as its base ({ p.ally ?| ... } mis-parses; gotcha in memory). Fixed with a local binding plus with_ally/now_in_world setters; scan compiles. Re-land queued, job-1792929363902 (hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim, gpu_sim with memo off; restores on red). Next: scratch duo-predict-pr28.ps1, whose control arm must reproduce PR-27's registered 16.4 kills / 6.09 potions before the delayed-entry arm's numbers go into PR-28's pre-registration (draft in scratch pr28-preregistration.md).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_94 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 94u32, "2026-10-25T13:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 09:29 combined land (candidate 2 + P11, both off) job-1792929363902: pack_sim 39/39, hunt_sim 11/11, gpu_sim 4/6 red on two 300 s cell timeouts only (wgsl full-hunt 361 s, routing 378 s; every gpu_sim test ran ~7x its 10-19 time under 5 windows + 2 heavy slots, the c oracles passed); tree restored by hash. Rerun gpu_sim alone on the same file: job-1792934707921-tibia-party-c2-p11-gpu.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_95 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 95u32, "2026-10-25T16:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 12:21 gpu_sim-alone rerun (job-1792934707921) red the same way: the two WGSL hunt tests at 310 s and 302 s against the 300 s cell budget, every gpu_sim test about 6x its 10-19 time (10-tick oracle 98 s vs 17 s), C oracles green. Rule written before acting: those tests take 83-121 s unloaded, so the 300 s default leaves 2.5x headroom and fails at today's load whatever the candidate; gpu_sim.spi gets '--timeout 900000' on its ///- line (the fiscal pattern), covering 7x load, and the budget stays even if the candidate is red for another reason. Combined land rerun with it: step 1 of job-1792945250381 (tibia-serial-b; party-gpu-land.ps1).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_96 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 96u32, "2026-10-25T19:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:31 Combined land DONE (serial-b step 1, 14:29-14:55): candidate 2 (party_targeting, targeting_like_canary) and P11 (ally_enters, with_delayed_ally_entry, measured_entry_ms) are in src/hunting_pack.spi with both options off by default; gpu_sim 6/6 with the new 900 s cell budget (pack_sim 39/39 and hunt_sim 11/11 were green on the same file earlier), formatter clean. Next per P11 (3): the predictions job on the same 100 held-out seeds is queued, job-1792956566092 (scratch duo-predict-pr28.ps1, its main respelled to edition 3): control = PR-27's arm without the delay must reproduce PR-27's registered 16.4 kills and 6.09 potions; the delayed-entry arm gives PR-28's predictions, which go into BENCHMARK as PR-28's pre-registration before any hunt.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_97 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 97u32, "2026-10-25T19:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:42 PR-28 PRE-REGISTERED in docs/BENCHMARK.md (line 310, before any hunt), per P11 (3). Predictions job job-1792956566092 (100 held-out hunts, 236 s): control (PR-27's arm without the delay) reproduced PR-27's registered numbers exactly, 16.4 party kills and 6.09 party potions (knight 2.83, ally 3.26, ally share 54%), 0 deaths; PR-28's arm (candidate 2 + camp.party 2 + with_delayed_ally_entry) predicts 16.73 kills and 6.68 potions per hunt (knight 4.88, ally 1.8, ally share 27%), 0 deaths: the modelled solo opening shifts potion cost onto the knight. Test and decision rows unchanged from the draft (one-sample t on sum_kills and sum_potions, critical 2.26). Asked main for one ~80 min live-gui window (PR-28 smoke + 10 counted, the first live run of the Spiral parity prelude, the LIB-MIGRATION smoke clear).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_98 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 98u32, "2026-10-26T01:09Z", "tibia-lane", "01:09Z PR-28 RESULT (BENCHMARK.md row under the pre-registration): BOTH HOLD.\n- Valid 9 hunts: 16.78 party kills against 16.73 (t 0.17) and 6.89 potions against 6.68 (t 0.59).\n- As run, with crashed hunt 2 included: 15.5 kills and 6.4 potions; it holds either way.\nDecision P11 (5): candidate 2 with with_delayed_ally_entry becomes the duo default, and the 10-14 saturation sweep reruns on it. Both are follow-ups for this item: flip the defaults in hunting_pack's duo camp, then the sweep job.\nGap named (descriptive): the per-knight potion split misses. Live gives knight 3.33 and ally 3.56 potions per hunt (ally share 52 percent); the model predicted knight 4.88 and ally 1.8 (27 percent). Kills split evenly (8.2 and 8.6). The totals land but the model under-taxes the ally, so the next modelling rule should look at the ally's incoming damage.\nHarness gap found: counted hunt 2 ended after about 60 s (ally `finish: offline`, the knight never hit) and run-arena logged it as a normal row, so the crash rule never fired a rerun. Proposed rule, pre-registered for PR-29 onwards: a hunt whose world stops before RunMs minus 10 s, or whose ally log ends `finish: offline` before RunMs, counts as a crash and is rerun once inside the window.\nLogin delays 803-1119 ms (mean 892), against PR-27's 618-2346.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_99 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 99u32, "2026-10-26T03:04Z", "tibia-lane", "03:04Z Note 98's crash rule is implemented in live/run-arena.ps1, with helpers in live/arena-check.ps1 (unstaged for the user). It keys on the probe's last 'finish:' reason, because seen-at lines carry no @t and the world-stop time can't be read from the logs. A hunt crashes when the knight's or the ally's finish is 'offline' or 'game login error', or is missing (a hard client crash, as with 0xC0000374). A world that stops early makes the probe write 'offline', so both of note 98's clauses reduce to this signal. A crashed attempt keeps its logs tagged crashN, writes no TSV row, and is rerun once inside the window; a second crash prints 'counted as missing'. 'timeout' and 'death' stay outcomes (BENCHMARK line 247). Checked against all 1,300+ arena logs on disk: every competitor body writes a finish line, and the rule flags only PR-28 hunt 2 (knight and ally offline; the positive control) and one nexbot log with no finish line, out of 80. Replaying it over PR-28 gives hunt 2 CRASH and the other 10 rows. block.ps1 and score.ps1 match logs by '-<competitor>-<run>.log', so crash-tagged logs never pair with a row. In arena-summary they group as '<competitor>-crashN', apart from the competitor's own stats.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_100 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 100u32, "2026-10-26T03:13Z", "tibia-lane", "03:13Z RULE P12, the 10-14 saturation sweep rerun on the duo default (P11 (5)), written before any code or run.\n(1) Camp: PR-28's predicted camp, arena_hunt () |> mirroring_the_current_arena |> respawning_like_canary 60000 |> duo_default (with_party 2, targeting_like_canary, with_delayed_ally_entry), simulated for scenario_ticks.\n(2) Baseline: the shared-heal cavebot, cavebot_heal_tuned_on Profit over training_block on that camp (one heal for both knights).\n(3) Candidate: rules evolved on training_block by note 4's search (seed 91, 12 generations by 12 offspring) under ProfitKeepingKills, with the floor at the baseline's training kills. Note 4's profit-at-cavebot-XP objective no longer exists; ProfitKeepingKills is its current form, the same profit-under-a-floor shape that ps-slice's room test uses.\n(4) Judged once on held_out_block: party kills, deaths, profit.\n(5) Decision, note 4's 5 percent bar: if the evolved rules beat the baseline's held-out profit by more than 5 percent with kills and deaths no worse, the duo lever is open again and gets its own pre-registered live check. Otherwise saturation stands on the live-checked duo model, TIBIA-PARTY's model work closes, and only the named ally-potion gap (note 98) stays open.\n(6) Implementation: the evolve slice's trial_on and search_plan, run by a heavy job after slices-c. It is a 'sweep' entry in the apps/predict family, not an <ID> script.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_101 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 101u32, "2026-10-26T03:17Z", "tibia-lane", "03:17Z P12 is implemented and queued. apps/predict/sweep.spi (in the tree, unstaged) holds exactly P12 (1)-(4): duo_ground, the Profit-tuned shared heal, evolved_rules (seed 91, 12 by 12, ProfitKeepingKills at the baseline's training kills, through the evolve slice's search_plan), then one held-out ledger row per arm plus the evolved genes. scripts/predict.ps1 gained -Module (default predict, so existing runs are unchanged): a non-default module joins the work copy's spiproj modules and becomes the compile entry, so the predict family has one runner. Job job-1792984577364-tibia-duo-sweep-p12 runs after slices-c, and it exits up front if hunting_pack has no trial_on, meaning the evolve slice did not land. The output goes to scratch duo-sweep-p12.tsv; P12 (5) is applied to it by hand, mechanically. sweep.spi is not yet type-checked: its first compile is that job's.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_102 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 102u32, "2026-10-26T03:18Z", "tibia-lane", "03:18Z RULE P13, written before computing: a post-hoc diagnosis of note 98's ally potion gap. It uses PR-28's seen outcomes, so it is descriptive only and nothing is adopted from it.\n(1) Per knight, PR-28's 9 valid live hunts (runs 3-11) against the model's 100 held-out PR-28 hunts (scratch duo-pr28-pr28-delayed-entry.tsv): hits taken per hunt (live: 'You lose N hitpoints due to an attack' lines in each knight's log; model: the first_hits and second_hits columns) and potions per hunt (live: 'Using one of N health potions' lines; model: the first and second columns).\n(2) Test: a one-sample t of the live per-hunt values against the model mean, as in PR-28 (critical 2.31, df 8). A lead is a knight metric with |t| > 2.31.\n(3) Reading: (a) the ally's hits lead (live above the model) means the next candidate is in which knight the monsters attack; (b) hits hold but the ally's potions lead means the candidate is in the ally's heal rule or potion accounting; (c) the knight's numbers lead in the mirror direction means the same split, from the knight's side. Any candidate then gets its own pre-registered fresh live check.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_103 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 103u32, "2026-10-26T03:19Z", "tibia-lane", "03:19Z P13 RESULT (scratch p13-read.ps1, kit table logs/test over PR-28's 9 valid hunts against the model's 100): no lead under P13 (2); every |t| is below 2.31. Knight: hits 22.0 live against 29.21 model (t -1.76), potions 3.33 against 4.88 (t -1.75). Ally: hits 22.67 against 14.62 (t 1.72), potions 3.56 against 1.8 (t 1.83). Descriptive, and not a rule outcome: the hits split has the same shape as the potion split. Live, the monsters hit both knights about equally (49/51 percent), while the model sends about two thirds of the hits to the knight (67/33), so the potion gap looks downstream of which knight the monsters attack (P13 (3a)'s direction, below threshold). A fresh check powered for a 7.5-hit shift at sd about 13 needs about 17 hunts per arm (t 2.31). Any targeting candidate (for example Canary's target choice among two players) needs its own pre-registered rule and that sample size. Nothing is adopted.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_104 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 104u32, "2026-10-26T03:21Z", "tibia-lane", "03:21Z Source reading for P13's descriptive hits split, with no rule outcome. Canary's data (data-otservbr-global/monster): cyclops changeTarget interval 4000, chance 10, strategiesTarget nearest 70, damage 30; troll changeTarget chance 0, nearest 100. The model already carries all of this: change_target_percent (Cyclops 10, Hunter 10, else 0), damage_strategy_percent (Cyclops 30), the 4000 ms next_retarget, nearest_of, and the entrant logic for which player entered view first. So no targeting mechanism is missing. The leading named difference is the ally's entry delay. Trolls never re-target, so each keeps the knight it acquired first, and a later ally entry hands the knight more first acquisitions. The model draws from PR-27's pool (mean 1238 ms); PR-28's live delays were shorter (803-1119, mean 892). P11 (1) allows login delays, as harness facts, to feed the model. Candidate for a future rule P14: the delay pool becomes PR-27 plus PR-28 (19 values), predictions are re-run, and the check is a fresh block of at least 17 hunts per P13's power estimate. Second candidate, named but unweighted: the ally's start tile relative to the 4 troll spawns (AllyAt), which decides 'nearest' at acquisition.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_105 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 105u32, "2026-10-26T03:26Z", "tibia-lane", "03:26Z P12 AMENDMENT A1, written before any P12 run (the job is still queued behind slices-c). Two corrections. (a) A misreading: note 3's 'shared sweep' is the patrol rule (both knights sweep the spawns together), not a shared heal, so P12 (2)'s 'shared-heal cavebot' was wrong. (b) Cost: at PR-28's measured 1.18 s per C hunt (236 s for 200; GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT note 3), 12 by 12 over 100 training seeds is about 14,500 hunts, about 4.8 h, plus about 1.2 h per Profit heal tuning. That is over the 235-minute job cap. Amended: (2) the baseline is the arm PR-28 validated live, cavebot_with_heal 160 standing, which is the duo default as checked; no tuning. (3) The search is pack_sim's standard budget, 6 generations by 6 offspring, seed 91, from { 160; 0; 4 }, under ProfitKeepingKills at the baseline's training kills: about 3,700 hunts, about 75 min, about 85 min with the floor and the held-out rows. (1), (4) and (5) are unchanged. A 12 by 12 search returns only if LIB-PAR-MAP's Rust par'.map makes it fit (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT (4)).")
inl note_of_tibia_party_106 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 106u32, "2026-10-27T23:02Z", "tibia-lane", "23:02Z P12 RUNNING on the worker node (job-1793142159161-tibia-duo-sweep-p12d). p12c had exited on its own guard ('the evolve slice has not landed'). The guard now passes: trial_on is at hunting_pack.spi:2201 (tree r9, 72B4E260B65B). apps/predict/sweep.spi (776082349656) is exactly A1: duo_ground, validated arm cavebot_with_heal 160, a 6 by 6 search with seed 91 from { 160; 0; 4 } under ProfitKeepingKills at the validated arm's training kills, then held-out ledger rows for arm 0 (validated) and arm 1 (evolved) plus the genes. Every name it uses resolves in the r9 tree. It runs through a new node runner, scratch node-kit/predict-node.ps1 with push-predict.ps1. That is the same steps as scripts/predict.ps1 (spiproj packageDir onto the node's lib mirror, C backend with the pinned compiler core 022C3338096B, gcc -O2, run), so the predict family can now go to the node. P12 (5) gets applied mechanically to the two held-out rows: the lever opens only if arm 1's profit is more than 1.05 times arm 0's, with kills no lower and deaths no higher; otherwise saturation stands and the model work closes.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_107 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 107u32, "2026-10-27T23:13Z", "tibia-lane", "23:13Z P12 DONE, read by rule (5) as pre-registered (notes on P12 and A1). Job job-1793142362955-tibia-duo-sweep-p12e on the worker node: tree r9 (hunting_pack 72B4E260B65B), sweep.spi 776082349656, compiler core 022C3338096B, compiled in 51 s, ran in 266 s, exit 0. Rows: evolved genes { first = 106; second = 0; third = 4 }. Held-out arm 0 (validated cavebot_with_heal 160): kills 1673, deaths 0, profit -17929. Held-out arm 1 (evolved): kills 1669, deaths 1, profit -29269. Rule (5): arm 1's profit doesn't beat arm 0's by more than 5 percent (it is 63 percent lower), and its kills (-4) and deaths (+1) are both worse. VERDICT: saturation stands on the live-checked duo model, TIBIA-PARTY's model work closes, and only the named ally potion-share gap (note 98) stays open. Positive control: arm 0's 1673 held-out kills are 16.73 per hunt, the same as PR-28's predicted 16.73 kills on this camp, so the node runner (scratch node-kit/predict-node.ps1) reproduces the local predict family. Descriptive only, no claim: the run took 266 s against A1's estimate of about 85 min from 1.18 s per local hunt; the per-hunt cost on the node wasn't measured separately.")
inl note_of_tibia_party_108 () = Note ("TIBIA-PARTY", 108u32, "2026-10-28T10:32Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: Active -> Paused. By P12 rule (5) (note 107, 10-27), saturation stands and the model work is closed. The only open part is the named ally potion-share gap (note 98: live 52 percent against 27 predicted). Resume condition: a pre-registered ally-share model candidate (P13's entry-delay idea, notes 103-104), or a user request. Under TIBIA-PARK's K2/K3 it parks with that condition.")
inl eoie_note_author () = Task ("EOIE-NOTE-AUTHOR", "agile note records author=user unless --as is passed, so an agent's note is attributed to the user (found 2026-10-14 10:06 by the Tibia lane: TIBIA-HUNTING-GROUND note 1). Default the author from the session (an env var such as EOIE_AUTHOR) or require --as", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,friction", "current", "current")
inl spiral_macro_arg_expressions () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-ARG-EXPRESSIONS", "Found 2026-10-14 10:13 by the Tibia lane (apps/tibia/terrain.spi route_between): a !!!! macro argument cannot be an application, an operator expression over one, or another macro; !!!!ArrayIndexSet(rows, row, arena_spot_low row ^^^ all_bits ()) and word_holds !!!!ArrayIndex(open_lows, row) bit are parse errors (Expected: ) / the block is left out of its module). Accept full expressions as macro arguments (parenthesized at least), or point the error at the argument with a hint to bind it first", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,parser,macros", "current", "current")
inl wgsl_tail_call_in_match () = Task ("WGSL-TAIL-CALL-IN-MATCH", "Found 2026-10-14 10:18 by the Tibia lane: on WGSL a let rec whose tail call sits inside a match arm (match c with | NoRoute => if .. elif cond then layers (k + 1) else .. | _ => c) is emitted as a recursive function, and naga rejects it (declaration of sp_layers_9 is recursive); moving the tail call into a plain if/else makes it a loop and the twin is bit-exact (apps/tibia/terrain.spi route_between, gpu_sim.spi a_hunting_ground_hunt_with_routing_on_gpu). Lower tail calls in match arms to loops too, or report it at compile time", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl wgsl_lanes_large_dispatch_hang () = Task ("WGSL-LANES-LARGE-DISPATCH-HANG", "Found 2026-10-14 14:40 by the Tibia lane: spiral-wgsl with SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES=16384 on a 2 MB simulator shader (one arena clear per lane) hangs over 10 minutes with no output (4096 lanes finish in 5.9 s, so ~9 s was expected); likely one dispatch outliving the Windows GPU timeout (TDR) and the runner waiting on a lost device. Chunk dispatches under the TDR budget and report device loss as an error. Repro: C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\ac4b4e98\\tmp\\typecheck\\lanes.spi --backend Wgsl", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,runner,gpu,tibia", "current", "current")
inl tibia_potion_fidelity () = Task ("TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY", "Potion use is the open fidelity gap (PR-4, 2026-10-14 14:53): the simulator predicts 13.23 potions per cyclops clear for the rules policy and 9.09 for the retreat policy, live is 11.53 and 12.90 (clear times match within noise). Candidates to measure: live potion cooldown and exhaust vs the model, heal amount distribution, how often a live retreat step costs an attack turn or draws extra hits (retreat damage 2217 vs rules 2029 live), block-charge regain while moving. Fix the model, then re-run the prediction check before designing more policies in the simulator", Story, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,fidelity,simulator", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_potion_fidelity () = Planning ("TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl tibia_nexbot_crash () = Task ("TIBIA-NEXBOT-CRASH", "nExBot 4.0.4 (19632d4) crashes OTClient Redemption 4.x (Aug 15 2026 build) with 0xC0000374 (heap corruption) about 10 s after login, in all 3 PR-3 check runs (2026-10-14 16:33); otclient.log shows no Lua error, and a [nexbot-load] trace before each loadScript (TIBIA_NEXBOT_TRACE=1 in run-policy-scenario.ps1) printed nothing, so the crash precedes module loading or g_logger is not reachable from the bot environment. Next: trace with the bot's own info()/warn() into its storage folder, bisect _Loader.lua phases (styles, sanitizeStorage, ACL init), try an OTClient debug build for the native stack. PR-3 waits for this", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,baselines,nexbot", "current", "current")
inl lib_rust_testing_globals () = Task ("LIB-RUST-TESTING-GLOBALS", "lib/spiral/rust/testing.spi run_tests' emits Rust globals on every backend: apps/spiral's F# output starts with 'thread_local! { static SPIRAL_TEST ... }' and '#[test] fn verify_app() ...' (installed pack-1g output, lines 1-2). Its two global calls are not behind a Rust backend switch. Fix: put them behind the Rust arm (like fsharp_global for F#), check apps/spiral F# and Rust outputs, run a suite review if any sample reaches run_tests'", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lib,backends", "current", "current")
inl package_defaults_per_package () = Task ("PACKAGE-DEFAULTS-PER-PACKAGE", "Package numeric defaults (spiproj default-int/default-float/edition) are honored only by the one-shot CLI (host applyPackageDefaults sets SPIRAL_DEFAULT_* before startup). The batch harness, --server-tcp (Kino) and the language server compile with the process-wide default_env (core 7281, 44800 literal parse, 51814/54140 Infer int), so a package declaring default-int: i64 silently gets i32 there. Found by the new regression sample package_default_int_i64 (held in the job tmp until fixed). Fix: thread the package's spiproj defaults into its parse and infer (natural to do with the Infer pair unification).", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,compiler,packages,bug", "current", "current")
inl kino_test_cell_i64_as_f64 () = Task ("KINO-TEST-CELL-I64-AS-F64", "Kino test cells render wide i64 values as f64 (found 19:10 2026-10-15 by lane B's Lua wide-integer Kino probe): a 9007199254740993i64 literal inside a test cell printed 9007199254740992.0 on Rust too, so the loss is in Kino's test-cell result path (value transport or rendering), not in a backend; plain cells and the harness are exact (Lua suite DISAGREE 0, 64k randomized ops 0 mismatches). Find where the cell value goes through a float and keep integers exact.", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,correctness", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_test_cell_i64_as_f64 () = Planning ("KINO-TEST-CELL-I64-AS-F64", Unestimated, LoadUnset, Assigned Agent)
inl swift_array_reference_semantics () = Task ("SWIFT-ARRAY-REFERENCE-SEMANTICS", "Swift backend copies Spiral arrays (found 19:42 2026-10-15 by the Swift list probe single-flight-20261015-193523): samples/dynamic_array_mutable_alias exits -7 on Swift vs 0 on C, i.e. set inside a join point (write_at) does not reach the caller's array, because Swift [T] is a value type and every join-point argument is a copy; dynamic_array_union_shared_alias and dynamic_array_union_nested_shared_alias fail the same way. Spiral arrays are shared references on every other backend. Fix direction: emit arrays as a final class box (reference) holding the storage, or pass them inout through join points; then re-probe those 3 samples onto the Swift list.", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "swift,correctness,codegen", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_swift_array_reference_semantics () = Planning ("SWIFT-ARRAY-REFERENCE-SEMANTICS", Unestimated, LoadUnset, Assigned Agent)
inl kino_lua_dropped_write () = Task ("KINO-LUA-DROPPED-WRITE", "Kino's Lua run drops a console.write line (found 19:44 2026-10-15 by lane B's i64 probe): Lua shows 4 lines vs Rust 5 for the same test cell; the same program, including Kino's show_value_main wrapper, prints all 5 lines on Lua 5.1 outside Kino. Kino keeps neither the generated main.lua nor a run-mode capture, so the next step is capturing the Lua program and its raw stdout in run mode.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,lua,correctness", "current", "current")
