inl note_of_pipelines_green_1 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: state 10-15: every repo of ours runs one gh-pages.yml through scripts/workflow.ps1 (spiral, eoie after today's change, polyglot, dice, alphabet, i574n.github); no push since 10-14 21:31, so none of today's packs ran in CI yet. Children: CI-AFTER-SPI-MIGRATION, CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE, WORKFLOW-END-TO-END, CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE, CI-NIX, CI-K3S, SUITE-MAP, NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE, PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_2 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 2u32, "2026-10-16T15:48Z", "main-thread", "11:48 main: CI state read with gh (i574n remotes): green on the last push for spiral (build windows+ubuntu), eoie, polyglot, alphabet, i574n.github, x. Red: (1) spiral's nightly scheduled compiler job (run 38893948663): No .NET 11 SDK found (workflow.ps1 -Compiler never installed it) and upload-artifact refused SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR=github.workspace/../spiral-bin (relative '..'); fixed in scripts/workflow.ps1 (install-dotnet.ps1 when Resolve-SpiralDotnet finds none) and gh-pages.yml (runner.temp/spiral-bin). (2) dice Windows job failing since 10-09 (every run): contract/build.ps1 runs dice_contract test cells whose NEAR wasm runner goes through invoke-block -Linux and workspace/target/release/spiral_wasm, which a Windows runner does not have (NEAR sandbox is Linux-only, decision NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE); fixed in dice scripts/build.ps1: the two contract steps run where nearSandboxRunsHere = IsLinux (the ubuntu job still builds and tests the contract). Playwright 1.44 chromium download 400s in those logs are non-fatal. Both need the user's push to verify.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_3 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 3u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: 6 of 7 repos green on their last push; spiral blocked only by the workflow-file error fixed in the tree; the scheduled compiler job also needed the .NET resolve fix (CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_4 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 4u32, "2026-10-18T11:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:46 main-thread triage of the red runs: spiral compiler job (run 39121783429 on adffe65a, which already has the macro lookahead fix) reached the native tier on Linux, then test.ps1 called kernel32 SetErrorMode (Value cannot be null, path1); fixed in the tree: the WerFault guard runs only when IsWindows (needs the user's push; the other native-tier paths already branch on IsWindows). dice windows job 39106582938: workflow step without a conclusion and no log blob (lost runner; ubuntu passed); rerun --failed at 07:46. x: e2e tutorial steps xc --help and --per-account expect the old usage without the xc thread line; the X lane's uncommitted README already adds it, so it clears with that commit. eoie: own source-topology gate (rust_file_max=982 over its limit), eoie lane. i574n.github: same run ids as x (shared workflow), recheck after x.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_5 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 5u32, "2026-10-18T11:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:47 main-thread correction to the previous note: i574n.github's repo is i574n/.github; its red run 39103223121 (06:12Z) failed apps/spiral compile on spiral main 1723e220 (the first macro fix), which adffe65a fixed; rerun --failed at 07:47. The x and i574n.github ids in the earlier note were a listing bug (one repo's JSON reused for the next).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_6 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 6u32, "2026-10-18T13:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:22 main-thread: dice run 39106582938 rerun --failed is success (the windows failure was a lost runner); spiral push 39127751148 (e1eef3bc) ubuntu success, windows running; i574n/.github 39103223121 rerun --failed again at 09:21 now that spiral main has the Gleam warm-slot fix (its 07:47 rerun cloned the old main and failed on the same stale-Gleam symptom). Still red: x (the X lane's README tutorial update is uncommitted) and eoie (its topology gate, eoie lane).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_7 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 7u32, "2026-10-18T13:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:32 main-thread: push run 39129835185 on 80b16019 (user commit 09:02) failed: build ubuntu, apps/spiral compile, lib/spiral/rust/near.spi:44:19 Expected: } (git-0e's 08:54 block-form conversion put ') : () -> ()' after an indented block closer inside a record, which the installed parser also rejects). Isolated (a lib copy with only near.spi reverted compiles) and reverted by git-0e at 09:31 to its e1eef3bc content; a lib-backed probe compiles again. Needs the user's next commit; the other 11 converted lib files compile. My bless now aborts first if a lib-backed sample does not compile.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_8 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 8u32, "2026-10-18T13:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:42 main-thread: spiral push 39127751148 on e1eef3bc is fully green (build ubuntu + windows, push to gh-pages): the first green spiral push of the day, carrying the macro lookahead fix, the cells warm-slot fix and today's backend work. i574n/.github rerun 39103223121 failed again, as expected: it cloned spiral main at 80b16019 (broken near.spi); goes green after the user commits git-0e's near.spi revert, then one more rerun.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_9 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: diagnosed the 06:48 push runs: spiral, dice, polyglot fail on Linux only, rustix 0.37.27/0.37.28 (near-workspaces 0.11 -> cargo-near -> near-cli-rs -> keyring -> zbus 3 -> async-io 1) uses rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range attributes that nightly-2026-10-18 rejects (the Windows nightly verification never compiles rustix); alphabet fails in dir-tree-html exit 3 (UTF-8 slice of its Korean README); x fails in apps/client/protocol.spi:80 and eoie in eoie-do-edit (their lanes); dice/polyglot/alphabet also lose html at mix spiral.html before mix deps.get Next: check the next push runs of spiral, dice, polyglot, alphabet after the user commits the fixes")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_10 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:53 lap 2 grain: push checklist (uncommitted fixes the user's next push must carry, git status at 09:5x): spiral 30 modified + 18 new (gh-pages.yml nightly pin, scripts/core.ps1 mix deps.get, workspace/.cargo/config.toml rustix cfg, scripts/publish-tree.ps1 failed-render guard from helper 2, test.ps1 per-backend native keys, sm'.spi fixes, Cells edits); dice 6 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, .cargo/ rustix cfg); polyglot 3 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, apps/chat/.cargo/); alphabet 3 + 2 (gh-pages.yml); dir-tree-html 3 (gh-pages.yml, dir_tree_html.spi UTF-8 runs + README test); blog 6 + 4; x 24 + 2 (ci.yml, protocol.spi fix); eoie 32 + 8 (gh-pages.yml nightly, missing generated crates per the eoie lane); i574n.github 3 Next: after the user pushes, read each repo's run; the first new failure per repo becomes this item's next grain")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_11 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 11u32, "2026-10-19T18:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:13 lap 4 grain: push checklist refreshed at 14:1x: uncommitted spiral 229 (22 new; includes the core v1 consumer migration, pack 7 compiler, 147 regenerated sample outputs from the accepted reviews), dice 12, alphabet 5, polyglot 6, dir-tree-html 3, i574n.github 4, plus the fork clone deps/The-Spiral-Language (core v1); the 10-17/10-18 gh-pages runs were green in dice, alphabet, polyglot and spiral, so the 06:48 reds are only the causes already fixed in these trees Next: after the user pushes: watch spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, then i574n.github (push-only workflow, last) with ci-runs.ps1")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_12 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 12u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 lap 5 grain: compiler install race fixed in build.ps1: other lanes compile continuously with the shared Release DLL (a Rust compile from another session started mid-build at 16:08), so the Release copy step failed twice with MSB3021/MSB3027 (FParsecCS.dll and others locked by .NET Host); Unlock-SpiralOutputs already ran before the 3-minute compile, which left the window open; now a build that fails only on locked-file copies (no error FS) unlocks again and reruns the incremental build up to 3 times, so the copy follows the unlock within seconds; the pack 9 install then succeeded and hashes equal the candidate Next: none; watch the next installs for the retry line")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_13 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 13u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 correction to note 12: the pack 9 install at 16:10 built in 67 s without contention, so the new unlock-and-recopy retry did not fire; it is parsed and in place but not yet exercised by a real lock race.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_14 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 14u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:45 lap 6 grain: second face of the shared-DLL lock race: the pack 12 install (20:33) built fine, but Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun could not swap 5 DLLs (FSharp.Core, FSharpx.Collections, Hopac.Core, Hopac.Platform, the host SpiralCompiler.dll: Move access denied while other lanes ran compilers), so they stay IL; same code, slower startup, and the host no longer hash-equals the R2R candidate (the core DLL does); the unlock-and-recopy retry covers only the msbuild copy Next: make the ReadyToRun swap use the same move-aside trick (rename the locked IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp>, then move the R2R one in), so a busy machine still gets precompiled outputs")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_15 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 15u32, "2026-10-20T00:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:46 main: R2R swap fixed in build.ps1 (Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun): when Move(r2r, dll, overwrite) is refused because a running compiler holds the dll, it renames the IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp> (Unlock-SpiralOutputs already deletes those later) and moves the R2R image in, moving the IL file back if that fails; proven with a simulated open-with-delete-share handle (overwrite refused, aside + move-in works); parse clean; first real exercise is the next Release build.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_16 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 16u32, "2026-10-21T22:17Z", "main", "18:17 CI survey (gh run list on each repo's i574n remote; the first remote of spiral, polyglot, dice and alphabet is the fc1943s backup, whose runs are from 2025): green: polyglot, dice, tibia; red: spiral gh-pages 10-21 07:49, alphabet gh-pages 10-20 00:04, x ci 10-20 00:04 (x lane), eoie gh-pages 10-19 22:10 (eoie lane), i574n/.github gh-pages 10-19 22:09. spiral's only failure was DISAGREE 1: samples/zig_exit_wide F# 'exit 305 (warm)' vs C 49 and every other backend 49. Linux truncates exit codes to 8 bits, but the warm F# tier reads the code from fsi output, and its 8-bit mask in test.ps1 (line 787) never ran: warm.ps1 returns Exit as [string], so '-isnot [int]' was always true. Fixed: the mask now applies to any numeric string off Windows (305 -> 49, -1 -> 255, 'timeout' and '' unchanged); unit-checked, parse clean. Windows was never affected (32-bit exit codes).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_17 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 17u32, "2026-10-21T22:20Z", "main", "18:20 alphabet gh-pages (run 39367599510, HEAD 01da480): apps/hangul/hangul.livemd's two Cells tests (hanja, hanjaconvert) fail on the Zig target only: hanja.spi:2:5 ETY-TermError, Unification failure, Got array (i32 * i32), Expected array_base (i32 * i32), in hanja_to_hangul_table. hanja.spi declares '() : array_base (i32 * i32) = ;[ ... ]'. The parser turns ';[' into an application of the term named array to the RawArray (compiler line 45226), and core base.spi:24 has 'nominal array t = array_base t', so the literal is the nominal array; other targets pass, so a backend-dependent path resolves array differently (not found by reading: no 'inl array' in core or lib). Next: one probe compile of the hanja cell for Zig and Rust when a compile window is free. Also removed 2 stray files of mine from alphabet/apps/hangul (hanja.spi.err/.out from the 08:21 format-timings job).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_18 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 18u32, "2026-10-21T22:22Z", "main", "18:22 i574n/.github gh-pages (run 39357416239): lib/spiral/parsing.spi test pipe2 failed on Zig only, at 1343:86 (got array, expected string) and 1343:92 (got array_base, expected unit). Cause: commit 002c71f6 (10-19 22:09) rewrote the test lambda 'fun a, b => a () ++# b ()' to 'fun a, b => array () ++# b ()', apparently a rename sweep that caught the parameter a; array () then means core's nominal constructor applied to unit. The other targets passed only through the CI memo (memo_hits 37), and Zig was the one target compiled fresh. Fixed back to a () ++# b () (test block only, so builds and other lanes are unaffected). That commit's lib diff contains no other such rewrite. Gate: Cells --gate on parsing.spi when a slot is free, after the night-pack review. Open question for CELLS: memo replays can keep a broken test green on old targets; the alphabet hanja case below may be the same.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_19 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 19u32, "2026-10-21T22:24Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:24 x: x ci status for this survey. Red on HEAD 61b8fff only from the windows-2025 CLIXML e2e failure (run 39367603402); ubuntu passed in 9.5 min against a 120-min timeout. The uncommitted tree fixes it, and it is green locally on CI's own scripts/build.ps1 (10-20 precommit; 10-21 x-split-fix3 for the post_id slice and the sim fixes). Precondition for x going green: x CI clones i574n/spiral's default branch, and pushed 33ee6412 lacks the postability profile reader, the 'x-compat report' line x's client build parses, and today's lib Gleam fixes, so spiral must be pushed with x (the user has always pushed both together; X-DONE note 8).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_20 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 20u32, "2026-10-21T22:24Z", "main", "18:24 correction to note 18, from x's memo analysis: Cells' memo key (memo.ex key/2) hashes the test program itself plus the package blocks it reaches, and pipe2's own text changed, so on the non-Zig targets pipe2 most likely ran fresh and passed. That makes the 'masked by the memo' explanation unlikely: array () genuinely type-checks on the other targets and fails only on Zig, the same Zig-only pattern as alphabet's hanja array_base literal. Both point at one question: what the term array resolves to when Cells compiles for Zig versus the other targets (a different package or prelude set for the Zig builder?). The pipe2 fix back to a () stands either way (the lambda's a was otherwise unused). Probe planned: compile the hanja and pipe2 cells for Zig and Rust with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 once a compile window is free.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_21 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 21u32, "2026-10-21T23:24Z", "main", "19:24 alphabet cause found by probe (installed compiler, a tiny package with core- and spiral-, 4 backends each): a let annotated : array_base (i32 * i32) whose body is a ;[ ... ] literal fails on EVERY backend (Rust, Zig, C, F#: ETY-TermError at 2:5), because ;[ is core's nominal array; annotated : array it compiles everywhere. So hanja.spi's annotation was always wrong, and the 'Zig only' CI failure means the other targets never compiled it fresh in that run (a memo replay or another path; not the compiler). The hangul test cell expects array_base (it wraps with fun x => array x), so the fix keeps the annotation and unwraps in place: hanja.spi now ends with '    |> fun (array x) => x' (1 line; probe: compiles on all 4 backends). Verifying on the real 465 KB table now (job main-hanja-probe). The same probe also re-reads parsing.spi's pipe2: array () is core's nominal constructor there too, so the a () fix was right on every backend.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_22 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 22u32, "2026-10-21T23:25Z", "main", "19:25 alphabet fix verified on the real 465 KB table (job main-hanja-probe, 57 s): hanja.spi with the in-place unwrap plus a main that wraps it like the hangul test cell (fun x => array x : array _ |> am'.length) compiles on Rust, Zig, C and F#. alphabet apps/hangul/hanja.spi is changed for the user to commit (+1 line); its gh-pages turns green on the next push if nothing else breaks.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_23 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 23u32, "2026-10-22T04:13Z", "main", "00:13 lab drain: fsharp-warm-exit.py landed in refined form: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 computes processExit (the Linux 8-bit mask of the warm F# exit; tonight's version also guards non-numeric exits), so the script's anchor is gone. Removed spiral/lab/PIPELINES-GREEN (1 file); the item stays open for its remaining work.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_24 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 24u32, "2026-10-22T07:28Z", "main", "03:28 main: why the spiral and alphabet pages are stale since 10-18 (read-only: gh run view --log-failed; ties to TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO note 25). spiral: the last push run (39367586923, 10-20 04:04, HEAD 33ee6412) fails in build (ubuntu) at lib/spiral/build.ps1. 346 of 347 lib tests pass; parsing.spi test pipe2 on zig hits ETY-TermError at 1343:86, 'Got array _ Expected string'. HEAD's line 1343 reads 'fun a, b => array () ++# b ()', a parameter 'a' rewritten to 'array' by a rename (the only such line in HEAD's lib). The working tree already has 'a () ++# b ()' (inside an uncommitted 38+/30- parsing.spi change), so committing tree lib unblocks it. (The scheduled 10-21 run fails in the compiler suite job, a separate track.) alphabet: run 39367599510 fails in apps/hangul. hanja.spi:2:5 hanja_to_hangul_table declares array_base (i32 * i32), but the ;[ ... ] literal is 'array', the core-v1 split. The user already STAGED the fix ('|> fun (array x) => x' after the literal). dice and polyglot last deployed 10-20 (green). So both red pages turn green with the user's next commits; no code change is needed from main.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_25 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 25u32, "2026-10-22T07:30Z", "main", "03:30 main: the scheduled spiral compiler-suite job (run 39594781370, 10-21) fails on one row: samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp] DISAGREE on the Linux runner (exit 305 while C, Rust, Zig, Python, Gleam and OpenQasm exit 49 = 305 mod 256). Cause: the warm F# native runner returns the program's int without a real process exit, and HEAD's test.ps1 masked it to 8 bits off Windows only when '\\.Exit -isnot [int]' was false. The warm result is not a .NET int, so it was never masked. The STAGED test.ps1 already fixes it: parse the exit as a number, then '-band 255' when not on Windows. So all three red signals (spiral build, alphabet build, spiral compiler suite) have fixes waiting in the user's next commits.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_26 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 26u32, "2026-10-22T07:31Z", "main", "03:31 main: the rest of the CI picture (read only, last runs). i574n/.github gh-pages (39357416239) fails the same way as spiral: it clones spiral and builds its lib, and dies on 'notebooks failed: parsing' (the pipe2 line), so its push job finds no gh-pages artifact. It is fixed by the same spiral commit. Green: tibia, blog, dir-tree-html (and dice and polyglot, 10-20). Red, owned by other lanes: x ci (10-20 04:04) and eoie gh-pages (10-20 02:10); not diagnosed here.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_27 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 27u32, "2026-10-22T19:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:37 x: x ci status update (details in X-DONE notes 41-51, not repeated here). The red on HEAD 61b8fff (windows-2025 e2e '#< CLIXML') now has a fix verified locally: '-OutputFormat Text' in e2e's step launcher, e2e 31+5 green on 10-22 15:00. CI parity: zig 0.17.0 checked (prelude passed, exsss smoke 5/5 incl. rust+gleam); rust was already pinned by Cells via the rustup proxy. The full notebook rerun on CI's zig is due ~17:50-18:55. Preconditions for green are unchanged: the user commits x's whole working tree and pushes spiral with it (still 33ee6412).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_28 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 28u32, "2026-10-23T11:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:53 main: CI survey (gh run list on the i574n remotes). No push since 10-20 04:14 in any repo. Green on the last run: dice, polyglot, x (dynamic), eoie (dynamic), tibia, blog, dir-tree-html. Red: alphabet push 39367599510 (hanja fix staged by the user), i574n/.github push 39357416239 (parsing.spi pipe2, fixed in the tree), and spiral's scheduled compiler job 39773873815 (10-22 11:52, HEAD 33ee6412) with DISAGREE 1, samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp]: the Linux 8-bit warm F# exit mask fixed in the tree on 10-21 (note 17). Spiral's 10-23 11:39 schedule is running on the same HEAD and will show the same row. All three reds clear with the user's next commits; nothing new to fix in code.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_29 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 29u32, "2026-10-24T03:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:11 main grooming estimate, corrected 03:00: the 07:53 survey already showed nothing left to fix in code. All 3 reds (spiral's schedule on HEAD 33ee6412, alphabet, i574n/.github) are fixed in the tree and clear with the user's next commits; the '240 m, reproduce and fix each red' reading was wrong. Agent estimate 0; blocked on the user's commits and pushes.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_30 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 30u32, "2026-10-24T07:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:00 main: survey refreshed with gh run list: unchanged since 07:53. No push in any repo since 10-20 04:14; red: spiral schedule run 39947580678 (10-23 11:39, HEAD 33ee6412), alphabet 39367599510, i574n/.github 39357416239; the other 7 green. Status Active -> Blocked: every red is fixed in the tree and waits on the user's commits.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_31 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 31u32, "2026-10-25T08:29Z", "main", "10-25 04:29 main: spiral CI red cause found (run 40161876207 on HEAD 47d1e562, build ubuntu-24.04, workflow.ps1): RUST-FAILED apps/spiral, 'ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: rust_type' in the lua/lua_function/lua_error/lua_value/lua_multi_value nominals (each body is rust.rust_type applied at type level). The CI compiler is built from HEAD's apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, which is 1,136+/153- lines behind the working tree (the uncommitted 5i/5j/5k pack layer, plus Program.fs 13 lines), while HEAD's lib and apps are edition 3 (lib rust.spi: ':= rust_type \\* fs rs. ...'). Locally the installed tree compiler builds apps/spiral, so the commit pairs new sources with the old compiler. Fix: the user commits apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs + tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs with the next commit (the 5k install lands in the tree first: job-1792916161438). Pages: spiral and alphabet still serve renders without the README section, so they refresh on the first green run. kit (eoie renamed) is served at i574n.github.io/kit/; pages-sweep.ps1 now lists kit instead of eoie.")
inl ci_single_pipeline () = Task ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", "One workflow file per repo delegating to scripts/workflow.ps1 (user 2026-10-15 21:05): spiral compiler.yml folded into gh-pages.yml, eoie.yml replaced by gh-pages.yml + scripts/init.ps1 + scripts/workflow.ps1", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,pipelines,eoie", "current", "current")
inl spiral_to_yaml () = Task ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", "Idea (user 2026-10-15 21:50): a Spiral-to-YAML backend for workflow files so CI definitions are typed and cannot drift", TaskKind, P1, D5, 66u32, Active, "CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", "ci,backend,idea,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_to_yaml () = Planning ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night input: with every job delegating to workflow.ps1, the 7 workflows are 42-117 lines whose bulk is toolchain setup (4-17 uses steps each), per-repo copies of version pins that drift (CI zig 0.17 vs local 0.18-dev, OTP 27 vs 29, old Rust nightlies). The first useful output is one typed toolchain-pin source shared by all repos.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured the drift note 1 described: today the 9 workflows agree on every shared pin (zig 0.17.0, OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2, Python 3.12.10, dotnet 9.0.202, rust nightly-2026-10-18; blog, x, eoie and dir-tree-html use subsets), i.e. the 10-17 drift (OTP 27 vs 29, old nightlies) was fixed by hand-syncing 9 copies, which is exactly what a single typed pin source would make unnecessary. One pin worth a look before typing it: setup-dotnet 9.0.202 in CI while the local compiler builds target net11.0 (bin/.../net11.0), so either the CI compiler job installs its own SDK through the scripts or the pin is stale. Gate CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: answered the dotnet question from lap 1: the compiler does not use the workflows' setup-dotnet 9.0.202 at all. Resolve-SpiralDotnet (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1) only accepts an SDK that has .NET 11 (Test-DotnetHasSdk11), looking in SPIRAL_DOTNET, the cache's toolchains/dotnet, the shared cache, then PATH; when none qualifies, scripts/install-dotnet.ps1 installs channel 11.0 preview into the cache with dotnet-install (called from spiral scripts/workflow.ps1:19, lib/spiral/lib.ps1:19 and Cells run_notebook.ps1:90). So the 9.0.202 pin in 6 workflows serves only older F# paths (lib/fsharp builds, polyglot), and a typed pin source has two .NET entries with different owners: workflow 9.0.202 and the compiler's 11.0 preview from install-dotnet.ps1 - a second, script-level pin that YAML generation alone would not see.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:23 helper-lane lap 3: full pin census as the typed pin source's input (lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.tsv: repo, file, line, pin over the tracked .ps1/.yml/.toml/.psd1/.json/.nix/Dockerfile files of the 9 repos, deps and samples excluded; 199 hits). YAML side: 100 action refs (uses: @vN, already locked by actions.lock), plus setup pins repeated per workflow - otp 29, elixir 1.20, gleam 1.19.2 and zig 0.17.0 in 9 workflows each, rebar3 3.27.3 in 9 (written with two quote styles, 6 single and 3 double: drift a typed source would remove), python 3.12.10 in 7, dotnet 9.0.202 in 6, rust nightly-2026-10-18 in 10. Script side, invisible to YAML generation: nightly-2026-10-18 hard-coded in 22 places across 14 scripts (test.ps1, five spiral app build.ps1, init.ps1 x2, polyglot math, dice lib and contract, alphabet documents, dir-tree-html), nightly-2024-07-14 in 7 (the NEAR contract pin: spiral and polyglot init, polyglot chat contract, dice contract), x's rust-toolchain.toml channel, and install-dotnet.ps1's Channel 11.0 Quality preview. So one typed pin record needs about 10 entries (rust main, rust near, dotnet workflow, dotnet compiler, otp, elixir, gleam, rebar3, zig, python), emitted both into YAML and into a generated pins.ps1 the scripts dot-source.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:41 helper-lane lap 4: the typed pin source as code, lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): union tool with the 10 entries from the lap-3 census (RustMain, RustNear, DotnetWorkflow, DotnetCompiler, Otp, Elixir, Gleam, Rebar3, Zig, Python), one pin list, and two exhaustive renderers - render_ps1 writes a pins.ps1 the scripts can dot-source (one line per pin, for example RustToolchain = nightly-2026-10-18), render_yaml writes the with: lines of the setup steps with one quote style (the rebar3 drift disappears), skipping the two script-only pins (RustNear, DotnetCompiler) by an empty key. Adding a tool without both renderings is a missing-case error. Probe main compares both outputs with the exact expected text (expect exit 11).")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: pins.spi exits 11 as predicted - both renderers (pins.ps1 lines and setup-step with: lines) are byte-equal to the expected text from the typed pin list.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): pins.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): pins PASS (11) -> next: render a full setup step block | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: pins.spi 11 -> next: next pin set")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 8u32, "2026-10-26T01:01Z", "main", "10-25 21:01 main: pin re-census of the 11 workflow files across the repos. Every shared pin agrees (otp 29 x12, elixir 1.20 x12, gleam 1.19.2 x12, zig 0.17.0 x11, python 3.12.10 x8, dotnet 9.0.202 x9, rust nightly-2026-10-18 x13) EXCEPT rebar3, which drifted again: 3.28.0 in spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet and dir-tree-html (6), 3.27.3 in x/ci.yml:50, eoie gh-pages.yml:36, tibia :47, blog :32, i574n.github :32 and fiscal :30 (6). 3.28.0 is rebar3's current stable (published 2026-10-21; 3.27.3 is 10-12), so someone bumped half the copies by hand: exactly the hand-sync drift this item's typed pin source removes. Healed main's two: blog and i574n.github set to 3.28.0, each file's own quote style kept, LF, no new uses: (no actions-lock change), unstaged for the user. Asked: kit for eoie (after its rename), tibia for tibia (after its PR-28 block). x (no live session) and fiscal (offline): their teams bump x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 to 3.28.0, the same one-line edit. That makes 2 drifts in 6 days with every copy hand-synced, which raises the case for pins.spi (lab, probe GREEN 10-19) rendering every workflow's with: lines.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 9u32, "2026-10-26T10:44Z", "main", "10-26 06:44 main: first working piece: a drift checker, %TEMP%\\spiral-to-yaml\\pins_check.spi (lib-first, edition 3, compiles to dependency-free Rust). A typed pin list (8 setup pins: rust channel, dotnet, otp, elixir, gleam, rebar3, zig, python), each owned by an action (moonrepo/setup-rust, actions/setup-dotnet, erlef/setup-beam, mlugg/setup-zig, actions/setup-python). It reads every workflow path given on the command line, tracks the current step's uses: line, compares each owned key's value ignoring the quote style (which itself drifted), and prints path:line: key found X, pins say Y, with the mismatch count as its exit code. Positive control (a probe with otp '28', zig 0.16.0 and an unrelated cache-apt 'version: 1.0'): exit 2, exactly the two planted drifts. Two bugs found by the controls and fixed. (1) A bare key matched other actions' keys (alphabet's cache-apt version: 1.0); pins are now scoped to their action. (2) lib's argument_offset already skips the program name, so index 1 silently skipped the first file; it now starts at 0. Run over all 11 tracked workflows: exit 2, the two real drifts left from n8, x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 (rebar3 3.27.3, should be 3.28.0); eoie and tibia are already fixed. The old lab pins.spi is stale (rebar3 3.27.3) and is superseded by this list. Next: wire pins_check into each repo's workflow.ps1 so CI fails on drift, then render the setup blocks from the same list.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 10u32, "2026-10-26T10:51Z", "main", "10-26 06:51 main: all 11 tracked workflows agree with the typed pins (pins_check exit 0, 06:4x). x fixed x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal fixed fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 (rebar3 3.28.0, both unstaged), each confirmed with pins_check by its own lane. The checker found the drift and it was fixed the same hour, without a manual census. Next step unchanged: run pins_check from each repo's workflow.ps1, so CI fails on drift instead of a session noticing it.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 11u32, "2026-10-26T11:13Z", "main", "10-26 07:13 main: pins_check wired into the org workflow (unstaged in i574n.github, the org-tooling home). scripts/pins/ holds pins_check.spi (the typed pin list, moved from the %TEMP% draft), package.spiproj (edition 3, packageDir ../../../spiral/lib) and pins.ps1. pins.ps1 compiles with lib BuildSpiral into dist/pins_check.rs, which the root .gitignore 'dist' already covers, builds it with rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, and runs it over every .github/workflows/*.yml of the 11 org repos: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, eoie, fiscal, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, tibia, x. It throws if it finds no workflow. scripts/workflow.livemd runs `pwsh pins/pins.ps1` as its first cell, before build.ps1. Proof on the consumer path (pwsh scripts/pins/pins.ps1): compile 43 s, then 'pins_check: 0 pin lines disagree', exit 0. Positive control through Invoke-Block on the probe: the 2 planted drifts are printed and Invoke-Block exits 2, so the workflow stops. Next: render the setup-* steps from the same typed list (the YAML-backend half of this item), starting with erlef/setup-beam, which carries 4 of the 8 pins.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 12u32, "2026-10-26T11:51Z", "main", "10-26 07:51 main: pins_check now also types the action refs. An action_pin list holds 14 actions at their newest releases, read from gh api releases/latest at 07:2x. Both pin kinds are bundled into one pinset record. Each `uses: name@ref` line is checked against it; an action with no pin (cache-apt @master) is skipped. The first run over the 11 org workflows found 60 lagging refs, outside the tool pins: checkout v6.1.0 (latest v7.0.1), upload-artifact v5.0.0 (v7.0.2), download-artifact v6.0.0 (v8.0.2), setup-dotnet v5.4.0 (v6.0.0), setup-python v6.3.0/v7.0.0 (v7.1.0, released 10-22), setup-go v5.6.0 (v7.0.0), setup-tectonic v3.1.5 (v4.0.6). Fiscal and tibia already ran the new majors in CI; the only breaking change named in the notes is the Node 24 runtime. Main bumped alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, i574n.github, polyglot and spiral (48 lines, unstaged) and regenerated each actions.lock with `gh actions-lock`. Tags are kept in the workflows; only the lock SHAs move. x (7), kit/eoie (4) and tibia (1) bumped their own. The pins run now reports 0 drift in every repo. New --fix mode: `pins_check --fix <files>` rewrites each drifting value in place (the ref after @, or the value after the key, quote style kept) and writes a file only when the unfixed rebuild equals the original bytes. Controls on %TEMP%\\pins-fix-control: an LF probe got exactly its 2 planted fixes, the recheck exits 0 and the line count is kept; a ref probe got exactly 1 fix; a CRLF probe was refused ('its lines do not round-trip'), so a fix never rewrites line endings. The next bump is therefore one edit to the typed list plus `pins_check --fix`, not a hand script. Open: the polyglot and spiral actions.lock regeneration timed out at 180 s (the other 5 took seconds; rate limit fine); a 900 s retry is running.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 13u32, "2026-10-26T12:03Z", "main", "10-26 08:03 main: n12's open point closed: the polyglot and spiral actions.lock regeneration finished on the 900 s retry (exit 0; both had timed out at 180 s with no output, likely the tool walking their large trees, while the rate limit stood at 4,939 left). The scratchpad relock.ps1 runs gh.exe by full path (bare gh resolved to a non-Win32 shim under Start-Process), with an empty file as stdin and a per-repo timeout. Final pins_check run over all 11 org workflows: 0 pin lines disagree, exit 0. Main's 7 repos carry unstaged diffs in .github/workflows only (gh-pages.yml refs + actions.lock), 16 to 36 lines each; x, tibia and kit/eoie report theirs done and verified with the same binary.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 14u32, "2026-10-26T12:39Z", "main", "10-26 08:39 main: first workflows generated from typed Spiral (the item's goal, not just a checker): i574n.github/scripts/pins is now one package of 4 modules.\n- pins: the facts. The tool union, version_of as a total match (no union equality needed), tool_pins derived from it, action_pins, action_of, yaml_key.\n- pins_check: the drift checker and --fix, now opening pins.\n- workflow: the typed model. trigger = Push | PullRequest | Dispatch; runner = Runs | Matrix {keep_going; os}; value = Plain | Quoted | ToolPin; setting; uses_step and run_step, each with an optional condition; job and workflow as multi-line records. Plus the renderer: action refs come from action_pins and pinned values from version_of, so a workflow cannot state a version.\n- workflows: the entry. Per-repo definitions plus `workflows <git-root> [--write]`: compare each rendered target with its file (first differing line, exit = files differing) or rewrite it; a missing file is reported, not a panic.\npins.ps1 builds both entries and runs both checks in the org workflow's first cell.\nResults: dir-tree-html/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml renders byte-identical (v1, 53 lines). blog's (v2: dispatch trigger, top-level permissions, fail-fast false, run and job conditions) differed in exactly 2 lines, both quote-only (rebar3 '3.28.0' and zig 0.17.0 unquoted -> \"...\"). Under the rule written before rendering (canonical pins are double-quoted; a quote-only difference is normalized by --write; any other difference is a model bug), blog was rewritten. It is now identical, and pins_check still gives 0.\nControls: a planted timeout 120 -> 90 on a copy is reported at line 10 (exit 1); --write restores it byte for byte; the recheck exits 0.\nSpiral gotchas found: `when` is reserved; the tool cases Gleam and Zig collide with lib's backend union, so callers outside pins write pins.Gleam and pins.Zig.\nLock lesson (from kit): gh actions-lock needs --no-interactive in agent shells, or it waits on a prompt with empty stdin. That was the cause of the 180 s polyglot/spiral timeouts.\nNext: the other 9 workflows, one per change, each held to the same rule. Then the setup-beam/setup-zig blocks shared across repos become functions, so a pin bump is one typed edit plus `workflows --write`.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 15u32, "2026-10-26T12:53Z", "main", "10-26 08:53 main: rule for adopting a workflow into the typed source, written before rendering the next ones (i574n.github, polyglot). The rendered file may differ from the live one only by (a) pin quote style (canonical: double quotes), (b) removed YAML comment lines (no-comments rule; a fact in such a comment goes into a name or is dropped), (c) indentation alone (canonical unit: 2 spaces, as in 10 of the 11 org workflows; polyglot alone uses 4). Anything else is a model bug and blocks the --write. Checked mechanically by scratchpad wf-rule-diff.ps1 -Repos <list>, which renders into a scratch copy and classifies every differing line as quote-only, indent-only, removed-comment or OTHER. Applied so far: dir-tree-html 0/0/0 and blog 2 quote-only (both normalized earlier); dice 2 quote-only + 3 comments; alphabet 1 quote-only + 3 comments; OTHER 0 in all four. The dropped dice/alphabet comment said the cells notebooks need Gleam >= 1.14 because kino's manifest pins gleam_stdlib 1.0.5; the pin itself (1.19.2) already satisfies it. Also: workflows.rs from :=-inlined helpers came out at 2,747 lines (over the 1,000-line gate). Every renderer and definition function is now a :~ join point (main stays :=); the line count after that rebuild is recorded next.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 16u32, "2026-10-26T13:35Z", "main", "10-26 09:35 main: 7 of 11 org workflows are now generated from the typed source and byte-identical: dir-tree-html, blog, dice, alphabet, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral (unstaged, .github/workflows only). Each passed the n15 rule (wf-rule-diff.ps1, 0 OTHER lines). dice and alphabet: quote-only + 3 comment lines; i574n.github: 1 quote-only + 3 comments; polyglot: 75 indent-only (4 to 2 spaces); spiral: 6 quote-only. The classifier caught one real model bug before any write: polyglot's snok/install-poetry has 3 `with` settings the first definition lacked (reported as 3 OTHER lines; fixed, then 0 OTHER).\nModel v5 for spiral: Schedule (cron) and DispatchInputs (typed inputs: name, description, required, default) triggers; a Lines value (key: | block); guard_first on run steps for the authored `- if: always()` then `run:` shape (blog keeps run-then-if); record update for the build job's condition ({ notebooks ?| condition = ... }).\nLayout: modules pins (facts), workflow (model + renderer), org_jobs (shared pages and notebook jobs: notebook_build, push_job, the verified deploy check), workflow_check (compare/write, missing file reported), plus entries pins_check and workflows. All renderer and definition functions are :~ join points (the :=-inlined form gave 2,747 Rust lines). dist/ is gitignored, so the generated .rs is outside the 1,000-line gate (which covers tracked, unignored files); an entry split was tried, didn't shrink the units (the model dominates, at 1,249 lines for 2 workflows), and was merged back into one entry.\nRemaining 4 belong to other lanes: eoie/kit, fiscal, tibia, x. Each was offered (08:4x): main drafts the definition and sends the classification, and writes nothing until the owner says yes.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 17u32, "2026-10-26T14:15Z", "main", "10-26 10:15 main: 10 of 11 org workflows are generated from the typed source. Since n16: fiscal (byte-identical as it stood, adopted on fiscal's yes; nothing written), tibia (1 quote-only line; written unstaged with the index untouched, gh actions-lock --no-interactive 'All 1 workflow valid', pins_check 0) and x (same: 1 quote-only line, lock valid, pins 0). eoie/kit's definition is approved (4 quote-only lines) and waits for kit's 'rename finalized'; then its target key moves from the eoie folder to the new one and it gets written. Model v6 for them: run steps with an id head (id, if, run), job env blocks, the verified push taking a gate and the deploy URL's owner (kit uses ${{ github.repository_owner }}, the rest i574n), a Lines value for actions/cache paths, shared notebook_uploads (failure notebooks, always ci-metrics, ubuntu pages). Owners see their targets in a pending list, checked only with --pending (which the classifier uses on its scratch copy), so a live check or --write can't touch a repo before its owner says yes. A target whose repo isn't checked out is skipped, so i574n.github's own CI (which checks out only itself) passes. Job and run-step records are built by job_of and plain_run plus record update, so a new field is one edit.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 18u32, "2026-10-26T15:03Z", "main", "10-26 11:03 main: evidence for n15's indentation clause: polyglot's own .editorconfig sets indent_size = 2 for *.{json,ts,yml,html} (the same block as spiral, i574n.github, dice, alphabet and tibia), so the 4-space gh-pages.yml was the outlier against its repo's declared style, and normalizing it to 2 brought it into line. x, fiscal, eoie, blog and dir-tree-html have no .editorconfig.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 19u32, "2026-10-26T15:19Z", "main", "10-26 11:19 main: .editorconfig joins the typed source: 6 repos (spiral, polyglot, i574n.github, dice, alphabet, tibia) carried a byte-identical 250-byte file, which is one fact in six copies. It is now rendered by i574n.github/scripts/pins/editorconfig.spi and checked in the same `workflows` pass. Correction from x's review: the 2-space glob was [*.{json,ts,yml,html}], which told editors to indent Elixir (mix format) and Gleam with 4, wrong for x and for spiral's own apps/cells. The model's glob is now [*.{json,ts,yml,html,ex,exs,gleam}], and every adopted copy is rewritten from it. Adoption rule, written before acting: adopt only where every tracked text file already conforms (final newline; LF in the index), so the file can't make editors rewrite other files on save. My first scan read worktree bytes, which core.autocrlf rewrites on checkout, so its CRLF counts were artifacts; the index check (git ls-files --eol) is the right one. Adopted: dir-tree-html (0 to fix); blog (main added a final newline to 8 hand-written files, bytes + \"\\n\" only); fiscal (fiscal fixed scripts/init.ps1); x (x fixed 11; its 8 build.ps1 lines over 120 don't block adoption, since max_line_length is advisory and the check enforces only the file's identity). Pending: eoie/kit (12 files listed to kit; kit fixes them after the rename, then the target is keyed on the new folder name). Written 11:2x: 10 repos (the 6 rewritten with the widened glob, and dir-tree-html, blog, fiscal and x created), all unstaged; 1 distinct hash across the 10; the recheck exits 0.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 20u32, "2026-10-26T15:50Z", "main", "10-26 11:50 main: rust-toolchain.toml joins the typed source (rust_toolchain.spi): 8 repos pinned the same channel as the workflows' RustMain (nightly-2026-10-18), the same fact in 8 files. They now render from version_of RustMain, with per-repo extra lines (tibia: components clippy/rust-src/rustfmt, target wasm32-unknown-unknown, profile minimal). All 8 render byte-identical, so adopting wrote nothing: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, polyglot and spiral directly, x and tibia on their owners' yes. Total under the check now: 10 workflows, the CI-NIX flake, 10 .editorconfig and 8 rust-toolchain.toml, 29 files, 0 differing (12:0x). A nightly bump is one edit to pins.spi followed by workflows --write, which moves the workflows' setup-rust channel, the flake's rust-bin date and every toolchain file together (x's note: its ci.yml channel and its toolchain file can no longer drift apart). .gitattributes was looked at and left alone: its 6 variants differ by real per-repo lockfile lists, and its only cross-repo gap (*.spi.ipynb linguist-generated, set only in tibia) affects nothing, because no repo tracks a notebook output.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 21u32, "2026-10-26T21:53Z", "main", "10-26 17:53 main: 10-26 17:5x main: progress was 20% while most of the item's stated goal ('CI definitions are typed and cannot drift') is working, so the record was stale. Milestones, registered now from the item's own words, before scoring: (1) one typed toolchain-pin source shared by all repos (n1's first useful output): DONE (i574n.github/scripts/pins/pins.spi). (2) Workflow files rendered from typed Spiral definitions and drift-checked: DONE for 10 of the 11 workflows. eoie's is pending its re-keyed folder name after kit's rename (along with its .editorconfig); 29 files are under the check with 0 differing. (3) The SHAPE the title names, a Spiral-to-YAML BACKEND: NOT DONE. What exists is a Spiral library (workflow.spi model + render) emitting YAML text, not a compiler backend. By gate-met-is-not-goal-met the item doesn't close on the cheaper shape. Whether a real backend is still wanted, now that the library already achieves typed, non-drifting CI, is the user's product call, and it's recorded here as the open question, not decided by main. Progress = 2 of 3 = 66%, status Active (eoie adoption follows the rename; milestone 3 awaits that call).")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 22u32, "2026-10-27T14:23Z", "main", "10-27 10:23 main: milestone 2's last step (kit's workflow, pending since the rename) is unblocked: the rename finalized 10-26 20:37 and the repo moved to C:\\home\\git\\kit at 04:30 (C:\\home\\git\\eoie is a junction). Re-keyed in i574n.github/scripts/pins (unstaged): workflows.spi's targets workflow_target \"eoie\" \"gh-pages.yml\" and repo \"eoie\" .editorconfig now name \"kit\" (the renderer kit () was already named so), and pins.ps1's $orgRepos lists 'kit' instead of 'eoie'; 29 targets in total. NOT run yet: pins.ps1 compiles pins_check and workflows to Rust and runs them over the repos, which may write rendered files into kit's tree, and kit-release-SLICES leases that tree until it finalizes. So the check runs after SLICES ends (with kit's yes before adopting any differing file, as x and tibia gave theirs). Milestone 2 is done once kit's workflow and .editorconfig render byte-identical, or are adopted. Milestone 3 (a real compiler backend instead of the library) stays the user's open product call.")
inl toolchain_latest () = Task ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", "Move every pinned toolchain to its newest release, rc, beta or nightly (user 2026-10-15 21:20: we are far behind, the dib era pinned 2024 Rust): Rust channels, .NET, OTP/Elixir/Gleam, Python, Zig, Lean, Swift, Kotlin, Node-free bun, actions versions", Epic, P1, D5, 30u32, Paused, "", "toolchains,ci,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_toolchain_latest () = Planning ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_1 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: pins in our workflows today: Rust nightly-2025-05-09 (gh-pages builds), nightly-2024-07-14 (NEAR contracts), nightly-2025-11-01 (app builds), 1.88.0 (eoie); setup-dotnet 9.0.202 while the compiler builds on .NET 11 rc; OTP 27, Elixir 1.18, Gleam 1.19.1, rebar3 3.24.0 (local OTP is 29); Python 3.12.10; zig 0.17.0 in CI vs 0.18-dev locally; Kotlin 2.4.21, Swift 6.4, Lean v4.35.0-rc4, playwright 1.44.0, trunk 0.21.14. Next grain: check release dates for each family, then bump one family per pack with a full suite.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_2 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: version matrix taken; .NET 11 rc.2 (11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105) installs in 63 s into a private cache and builds the compiler (145 s); a full review on that build (single-flight-20261017-034133) has 0 differing cells against bless 145428 on all 6,906 rows. install-dotnet.ps1 already takes the newest 11.0 preview, so only the shared local cache is behind (rc.1); move it as its own step after PACK-NIGHT-1017 is blessed, with a Tibia toolchain notice. Order: .NET rc.2, CI Rust nightlies to stable 1.99, CI OTP 27 to 29, scoop erl before choco on PATH.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_3 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (brzozowski alpha54, samples alpha705, spiral-bin alpha417; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 11): when moving toolchains, pin compiler identity as content fingerprints plus a mandatory capability probe, not one wrapper hash (identical spiral-compile wrappers sat on top of a good and a bad companion). Choose a new release by capability superset: an apparently latest lane was a functional subset of the stable one. Keep health per capability and history-aware (SDK ok / compiler attested / restore smoke failing as separate states). Use typed env profiles validated before launch (a malformed export produced SPIRAL_DOTNET=/dotnet). Preserve executable bits on rehydrate.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_4 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 4u32, "2026-10-18T09:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 05:05 main-thread local scoop status (manifests as of today): gleam 1.19.1 -> 1.19.2, elixir 1.19.4 -> 1.20.4, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0 (Kotlin native tier runs KotlinServer on JDK 25), pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.6, rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, uv 0.9.21 -> 0.13.0, go 1.25.4 -> 1.27.2. Web: OTP 29.0 shipped 2026-05-13 (CI still pins 27). Not installed mid-run: every tool version is part of the native-memo key (a bump replays nothing) and peers share the binaries, so bump one family per quiet window with a full review.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_5 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:26 lap 1 grain: Rust family done today (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE: nightly-2026-10-18 in every build script, the matrix, Cells and 8 CI workflows, every build verified, Linux rustix fixed by cfg); remaining families from the 10-18 scoop list: gleam 1.19.2, elixir 1.20.4, temurin 27, pwsh 7.6.6, rustup 1.29.1, uv 0.13, go 1.27.2, and OTP 29 in CI (still 27 in some workflows) Next: check which workflows still pin otp-version 27 vs 29 and gleam 1.19.1 vs 1.19.2; bump one family per quiet window with a full review")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_6 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: CI pins re-read across 10 repos: OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2 everywhere except tibia (OTP 27, Elixir 1.18, Gleam 1.19.1, tibia lane's tree, told); setup-dotnet pins 9.0.202 in spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, dir-tree-html, i574n.github while the compiler builds net11.0 (run_notebook installs .NET 11 itself), so the 9.0.202 pin is the next family to move Next: check what still needs the .NET 9 SDK in CI (fsi notebooks? dotnet tools) before bumping setup-dotnet to 11")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_7 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:03 lap 2 grain: found why CI still pins setup-dotnet 9.0.202: lib/spiral/lib.ps1 generates F# projects with TargetFramework net9.0 and runs the global dotnet publish (used by dice lib/build.ps1 and lib/fsharp/build.ps1), while the compiler, matrix and Cells already use their own .NET 11 SDK through Resolve-SpiralDotnet (env.ps1, install-dotnet.ps1); other direct dotnet calls are bench scripts (brzozowski bench.ps1, lib bench/parsing) and polyglot's temp cube; the only fsproj files left with net9.0 are vendored fable-library sources in polyglot Next: lib.ps1: generate net11.0 and publish with Resolve-SpiralDotnet, prove with dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 (HEAVY QUEUE), then drop setup-dotnet from the gh-pages workflows (alphabet mirrors dice)")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_8 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:03 lap 2 grain: .NET step applied in the tree: lib/spiral/lib.ps1 PublishFsharp now generates net11.0 and publishes with the .NET 11 SDK from Ensure-SpiralCompiler (Resolve-SpiralDotnet) instead of the global dotnet, so dice lib/build.ps1 and lib/fsharp/build.ps1 no longer need CI's setup-dotnet 9.0.202 Next: HEAVY QUEUE 2: dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 on net11; on green, remove setup-dotnet from the dice and alphabet workflows (and the other repos that only pin it for this)")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_9 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 9u32, "2026-10-19T14:51Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:51 proven: dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 with the patched lib.ps1 (net11.0, .NET 11 SDK from Resolve-SpiralDotnet) publishes and runs: FSHARP-OK dice/lib/fsharp / result 72096518 (main heavy queue 2, 10:50-10:51). Remaining: setup-dotnet 9.0.202 in the gh-pages workflows is now unused by our builds; left in place until the next push shows no other step needs a global .NET 9 (removing it is then a one-line workflow edit in each repo, alphabet mirroring dice).")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_10 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 10u32, "2026-10-19T18:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:36 lap 4 grain: scoop status 14:3x, matrix-relevant tools behind latest: zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 -> dev.349 (Zig is now the first oracle), wasmtime 49.0.2 -> 49.0.3, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0 (the Kotlin server runs on JDK 25), pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.7 (every script), rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, cmake 4.2.1 -> 4.4.4, uv 0.9.21 -> 0.13.1, git 2.52 -> 2.56, zed 1.23.2 -> 1.24.2; current: gleam 1.19.2, bun 1.4.3, erlang 29.1.2, elixir 1.20.4, .NET 11 rc.1; node 23.5.0 and python 3.12.10 are not scoop-managed and old (node 23 is an EOL odd line). Bumps change toolchain.tsv stamps, so they need one dedicated -AcceptToolchain pack, never mid-review Next: toolchain pack after pack 8b accepts: scoop update zig-dev wasmtime temurin-jdk pwsh rustup, rerun -Native (memo replays only the rows whose tool stamps changed), accept with -AcceptToolchain if DISAGREE 0")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_11 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 11u32, "2026-10-19T23:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:21 lap 6 grain: toolchain pack staged but moved to 10-20: scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1 updates wasmtime 49.0.2, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 and rustup 1.28.2 (scoop keeps the old versions, so scoop reset app@old rolls back) and reviews against the latest accept without accepting; zig-dev stays out of it (Zig is the first oracle, a dev-snapshot bump gets its own pack); tonight's matrix window went to packs 11 and 12; NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS needs the native Cells rustc (1.88.0-nightly) itself bumped to >= 1.93 for near-sys 0.2.14, a rustup self-update does not do that Next: run toolchain-job.ps1 first thing on 10-20, then a separate zig-dev pack, then the native rustc bump with the NEAR rerun")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_12 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 12u32, "2026-10-20T02:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:43 main: toolchain pack blocked by scoop itself: scoop config scoop_repo = https://github.com/i574n/Scoop-Core (branch main), which does not exist (also not under fc1943s), so every scoop update fails at Updating Scoop with Repository not found before touching an app; wasmtime, temurin-jdk and rustup stayed at 49.0.2, 25.0.1, 1.28.2; scoop status works only from local manifests (last core update 2023-03-08). Unblock (user decision, persistent tool config): point scoop_repo back at the upstream ScoopInstaller/Scoop or restore the i574n/Scoop-Core fork, then rerun scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1. The review it started is left to finish as a determinism check (stopping test.ps1 mid-compile can leave sample outputs empty).")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_13 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 13u32, "2026-10-20T03:10Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:10 main: scoop unblocked per the user: scoop_repo and the core clone now point at https://github.com/Ash258/Scoop-Core (main, archived but live; the original upstream of the missing i574n fork), scoop update succeeds again (core 52fe1646); Mold fixed to match in C:\\home\\git\\dotfiles\\dist\\windows\\install.ps1 (fresh clone of i574n/dotfiles for the user to commit): SCOOP_REPO -> Ash258/Scoop-Core, and the mold bucket URL i574n/mold.git (a rename redirect) -> i574n/dotfiles.git. The toolchain pack runs after pack 13 frees the matrix.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_14 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 14u32, "2026-10-21T03:26Z", "main", "23:26 scoop status (read-only) after last night's repo fix: matrix-relevant updates wasmtime 49.0.2 -> 49.0.4, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0-35, rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 -> dev.349 (the first oracle, so its own pack), uv 0.9.21 -> 0.13.2, go 1.25.4 -> 1.27.2, cmake 4.2.1 -> 4.4.4, git 2.52.0 -> 2.56.0.2, pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.7 (left to the user: the shell under every running session). Rewrote scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1: the scoop update of wasmtime, temurin-jdk and rustup takes the exclusive compiler-install window (other lanes may be running those tools), prints rollback commands (scoop reset app@old), then a -ReuseNative review that reruns only rows whose tool stamps changed. Runs after the evening pack install so its review has a fixed compiler.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_15 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 15u32, "2026-10-21T23:02Z", "main", "19:02 scoop status recheck (read-only): the matrix-relevant updates are unchanged since 10-21 03:26 (wasmtime 49.0.2 -> 49.0.4, temurin-jdk 25.0.1 -> 27.0.0-35, rustup 1.28.2 -> 1.29.1, zig-dev dev.146 -> dev.349 as its own pack since Zig is the first oracle, uv, go, cmake, git; pwsh 7.5.4 -> 7.6.7 stays with the user). Fixed the staged scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1 the same way as pack-night.ps1: its review baseline is now the newest run with >= 7,000 rows, not the newest dir (a -Filter rerun would have shown every row missing). Syntax-checked. Still queued behind the night-pack install (it needs the exclusive compiler-install window); not tonight, the window closes at 21:00.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_16 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 16u32, "2026-10-22T03:22Z", "main", "23:22 main (user 23:1x: update otp to latest, you have system permissions): upstream latest is OTP-29.1.2 (released 2026-10-14), already scoop's current; the drift was Chocolatey's erlang 26.2.5.5 plus rebar3 3.23.0, which resolved first on PATH (bare erl booted OTP 26 and crashed with load_failed: spiral/erl_crash.dump of 10-19, ERL-CRASH-DUMPS-IN-TREE). Done in an elevated shell: scoop install rebar3 (3.28.0, upstream release of 2026-10-21), choco uninstall rebar3 erlang, removed the orphan C:\\ProgramData\\chocolatey\\bin\\rebar3.cmd (15 bytes, pointed at the removed install). Now: erl = scoop OTP 29 erts 17.1.1, rebar3 = rebar 3.28.0 on OTP 29, elixir 1.20.4 (latest stable). Locally only CI workflows name rebar3 (setup-beam installs its own); running VMs were all scoop OTP 29, so none was affected.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_17 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 17u32, "2026-10-22T08:20Z", "main", "04:20 main: CI pin check against upstream (read only, gh api releases). rebar3 3.27.3 -> 3.28.0 (released 2026-10-21; local scoop already runs it) bumped in gh-pages.yml of spiral (3 jobs: build, compiler, cells-gate), polyglot, dice and alphabet; YAML parses; uncommitted for the user. Already current: gleam 1.19.2 (10-12), bun 1.4.3 (10-10), Elixir 1.20, OTP 29. Zig's 0.17.0 pin is newer than the GitHub mirror's 0.15.1, since Zig publishes on ziglang.org; zig-dev stays its own pack. The dotnet-version 9.0.202 pin (setup-dotnet) is not the compiler's SDK, because workflow.ps1 installs .NET 11 itself; 9.0.319 is the newest 9.0 patch, left for the user. The scratchpad toolchain-job.ps1 (wasmtime, temurin, rustup and zig-dev review job) now lives in %TEMP%\\toolchain-latest; it needs the compiler-install window.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_18 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 18u32, "2026-10-24T00:21Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:21 main: upstream re-read (gh api releases/latest). gleam v1.19.2 (10-12), OTP-29.1.2 (10-14), elixir v1.20.4 and rebar3 3.28.0 are current. New: bun v1.4.4 (10-22; local ~/.bun is 1.4.3, while CI's setup-bun@v2.2.0 has no bun-version pin and takes the latest on its own) and wasmtime v50.0.0 (10-20; the job's scoop app). %TEMP%\\toolchain-latest\\toolchain-job.ps1 gains -Bun: bun upgrade inside the same compiler-install window, before/after logged with a rollback note. Bend rows depend on bun's 18 MB main-thread stack (BUN_JSC_maxPerThreadStackUsage), so the job's matrix review is the check. It needs the exclusive compiler-install window, so it runs after tonight's chains, not inside them (scratch next-session.md). Status corrected to Active: OTP/rebar3 and the CI pins moved on 10-22.")
inl note_of_toolchain_latest_19 () = Note ("TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", 19u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl ci_nix () = Task ("CI-NIX", "Nix on GitHub Actions (user 2026-10-15 21:20): a flake that pins the toolchains and generates the files the workflows need, built and checked on the Linux runners only (no WSL on the dev machine)", Story, P1, D5, 66u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN,TOOLCHAIN-LATEST", "ci,nix,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_nix () = Planning ("CI-NIX", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_nix_1 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:12 helper-lane lap 1: pin inventory a flake must reproduce (read from the 8 repos' single workflows today): spiral, polyglot, dice, i574n.github use setup-dotnet 9.0.202, setup-python 3.12.10, erlef/setup-beam OTP 29 + Elixir 1.20 + Gleam 1.19.2 + rebar3 3.27.3, mlugg/setup-zig 0.17.0, moonrepo/setup-rust nightly-2026-10-18, oven-sh/setup-bun; alphabet adds setup-go, setup-tectonic and cache-apt-pkgs; polyglot adds snok/install-poetry; blog, x and dir-tree-html use subsets; all publish with peaceiris/actions-gh-pages and pass artifacts with upload/download-artifact. So the flake's devShell is about 9 toolchains; nixpkgs carries every one except a dated rust nightly (needs oxalica rust-overlay or fenix) and a pinned bun/zig minor (overlay or fetchurl). Still gated on PIPELINES-GREEN and TOOLCHAIN-LATEST.")
inl note_of_ci_nix_2 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:04 helper-lane lap 2: drafted lab/CI-NIX/flake.nix (unrun; Linux-only by design): a devShell for x86_64-linux with the 10-19 pin set - oxalica rust-overlay nightly 2026-10-18 plus wasm32-unknown-unknown, mitchellh zig-overlay 0.17.0, beam.packages.erlang_29 (erlang, elixir_1_20, rebar3), gleam, dotnet-sdk_9, python312, bun and powershell (every workflow is pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1). Unverified points, to settle on the first CI run: whether nixpkgs already has an elixir_1_20 attribute under erlang_29, and that gleam/bun/python minor versions match the workflow pins (nixpkgs follows its own versions, so exact Gleam 1.19.2 and Python 3.12.10 may need overrides). The flake does not yet generate workflow files (the item's second half).")
inl note_of_ci_nix_3 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:21 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the CI job sketch lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml (non-blocking: continue-on-error, ubuntu-24.04 only, checkout then cachix/install-nix-action@v31.11.2 with flakes enabled, then nix develop ./lab/CI-NIX --command pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1). Version check: cachix v31.11.2 tagged 2026-10-14 is the newest Nix installer action (DeterminateSystems v23 is 2026-09-09). Fixed a flake gap against gh-pages.yml: setup-rust installs clippy, rust-src and rustfmt, so the rust-overlay toolchain now lists those extensions. Adding the job needs gh actions-lock in spiral (actions.lock lists the 10 current uses; a new uses fails at startup until regenerated). Still gated on PIPELINES-GREEN.")
inl note_of_ci_nix_4 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:25 helper-lane lap 6: version recheck of the sketch's actions (gh api releases, read-only). cachix/install-nix-action v31.11.2 (2026-10-14) is still the newest, and DeterminateSystems/nix-installer-action is still at v23. actions/checkout has v7.0.1 (2026-07-20) above the sketch's v6.1.0, so lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml now pins checkout@v7.0.1. Adopting the job into a workflow needs gh actions-lock regenerated in that repo (actions.lock rule).")
inl note_of_ci_nix_5 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:29 (subagent): blocked: PIPELINES-GREEN (dice/polyglot/alphabet CI still red since 06:48) -> next: user push + green CI; then paste nix-job.yml + gh actions-lock | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): PIPELINES-GREEN pending (CI red since 06:48) -> next: user push + green CI | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: job sketch current (checkout v7.0.1, install-nix-action v31.11.2) -> next: adopt into a workflow on the user's go")
inl note_of_ci_nix_6 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 6u32, "2026-10-26T14:54Z", "main", "10-26 10:54 main: the flake is now generated from the typed pin list (the item's second half, 'a flake that pins the toolchains and generates the files'), so it can't drift from the workflows again. i574n.github/scripts/pins/nix_flake.spi renders spiral/lab/CI-NIX/flake.nix. The nightly date comes from RustMain (nightly-2026-10-18 -> \"2026-10-18\"), erlang_<OTP> from Otp (29), elixir_<major_minor> from Elixir (1.20 -> 1_20), python<major><minor> from Python (3.12.10 -> python312), dotnet-sdk_<major> from DotnetWorkflow (9.0.202 -> 9), and zig-overlay's \"<version>\" from Zig (0.17.0). It is one more target in the same workflows check, so the org workflow fails if the flake drifts. First render: byte-identical to the 10-19 hand-written flake (1,087 bytes, LF). Control: a planted zig 0.16.0 in a scratch copy is reported and --write restores it. Limits, stated: gleam, rebar3 and bun come from nixpkgs at whatever version the pinned nixpkgs rev carries, and the flake can't pin their exact versions without overrides; Python's patch (3.12.10) is also nixpkgs'. Exact parity needs overlays or fetchurl derivations, which are the next step. Also the job sketch: cachix/install-nix-action's newest release is 31.11.3 (2026-10-22). Its tags dropped the 'v' (v31.11.3 is a 404, 31.11.3 resolves to 75c75c31), so the typed list pins '31.11.3' and lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml now uses it; pins_check covers that file. Still gated on PIPELINES-GREEN and the user's go to add the nix job to a workflow (plus gh actions-lock --no-interactive in that repo). Also fixed while proving it: --write panicked when a target's parent folder was missing; it now creates the folder first (lib file_system.directory_get_parent + create_dir_all), and the control created spiral/.github/workflows in an empty scratch root.")
inl note_of_ci_nix_7 () = Note ("CI-NIX", 7u32, "2026-10-26T21:50Z", "main", "10-26 17:50 main: 10-26 17:5x main: progress was 0% while two of the item's three parts are done, so the record was stale. The item never defined milestones; registered now, from its own title, before scoring: (1) a flake that pins the toolchains: DONE (spiral/lab/CI-NIX/flake.nix); (2) generates the files the workflows need: DONE (n6, 10-26 10:54: rendered by i574n.github/scripts/pins/nix_flake.spi from the typed pin list, a drift-checked target of the org workflows check, byte-identical first render, planted-drift control); (3) built and checked on the Linux runners: NOT DONE. The job sketch lab/CI-NIX/nix-job.yml is current (install-nix-action 31.11.3); adoption waits on dep PIPELINES-GREEN and the user's push. Progress = 2 of 3 = 66%. Status stays Planned: the remaining milestone is blocked on its dep, not active work. Known limit, unchanged from n6: gleam, rebar3, bun and Python's patch come from the pinned nixpkgs rev, so exact-version parity needs overlays (part of milestone 3).")
inl ci_k3s () = Task ("CI-K3S", "Kubernetes compliance tested with k3s on GitHub Actions (user 2026-10-15 21:20): manifests for the services that can run headless, applied to a throwaway k3s cluster in CI", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,kubernetes,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_k3s () = Planning ("CI-K3S", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_k3s_1 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:44 helper-lane lap 1: inventory for the manifests (git ls-files over spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, eoie, blog, i574n.github, dir-tree-html): no Kubernetes manifests, compose files or service Dockerfiles exist; the only container files are five .devcontainer/Dockerfile (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, i574n.github), which are dev images, not services. Headless long-running candidates that a k3s smoke could host: the Spiral compiler host's --server mode (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host) and the Cells compiler daemon (apps/cells compiler_daemon.ex); everything else is a CLI or a static site. So step 1 is a service image (compiler --server with a readiness probe), and only then manifests + a k3s job. Gate PIPELINES-GREEN unchanged (Active 35%).")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_2 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: checked whether the compiler server can be the first k3s service: SpiralCompiler --server <socket> <stall> (host Program.fs:1183) and its TCP variant runTcpServer (:981-992) listen on 127.0.0.1 with port 0 (OS-picked), write the port and pid to <socket>.bridge / pid files, and print spiral-session-ready port=N pid=N. So in a container it is not reachable as is: a Kubernetes Service needs a fixed port on 0.0.0.0 and a readiness probe; the ready line is a good exec/log probe, but the bind address and port must become configurable first (e.g. an env var for address and port, defaulting to today's loopback/0 so local Cells clients are unaffected). That small host change is the actual first step of this item.")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_3 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:10 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the host change as lab/CI-K3S/server_bind_patch.py (one anchor in apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs runTcpServer line 984, --check OK): SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND=address:port picks the listener's endpoint, unset keeps today's 127.0.0.1:0, so local Cells clients and the .bridge port file are unchanged; an invalid value fails at startup with the expected form. Probed the parser first (lab/CI-K3S/bind_parse.fsx, bounded fsi): IPEndPoint.TryParse reads 0.0.0.0:7470, 127.0.0.1:0 and [::]:7470 as expected, rejects localhost:7470, but silently reads a bare 7470 as the IPv4 address 0.0.29.46 with port 0, so the patch only calls TryParse when the value has exactly one colon (or ]: for IPv6) and otherwise fails. Rides the next host build; a k3s Service then targets the fixed port with spiral-session-ready as the readiness signal.")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_4 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CI-K3S/server_bind_patch.py dry run matches apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs today. It rides the next host build: with SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND unset nothing changes, so it is offered to main alongside pack 11.")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_5 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 5u32, "2026-10-22T18:53Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:53 main: lab drained. server_bind_patch.py landed in apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs (its whole New block is there: SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND parsed as address:port, loopback:0 when unset). bind_parse.fsx was the IPEndPoint.TryParse probe for 0.0.0.0:7470, 127.0.0.1:0, [::]:7470, localhost:7470 and 7470. The landed guard sends only address:port forms to TryParse and fails with a usage message otherwise. The k3s manifests and the CI job are still the open scope (Planned).")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_6 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND patch rides the next host build -> next: after the build: manifest sketch with readiness on the ready line | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): host patch rides a host build -> next: after the build: manifest | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: server_bind_patch.py offered to pack 11's host build -> next: main")
inl note_of_ci_k3s_7 () = Note ("CI-K3S", 7u32, "2026-10-26T22:18Z", "main", "10-26 18:18 main: 10-26 main: design sketch for the first k3s target, recorded in the item (research lives in the backlog) rather than as files, because the files need a home that doesn't exist yet. Placement is a git-structure decision for the user: no repo has a deploy folder, new lab/ folders are retired (10-24 rule), and a new top-level directory is not main's call. Proposal: spiral/deploy/k3s/ for manifests, with the CI job as one more target in i574n.github/scripts/pins/workflows.spi, so its action pins stay typed like the other 10 workflows.\nService: the compiler host's --server mode, the only headless long-running service the 10-19 inventory found. SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND (address:port, loopback:0 when unset) landed 10-22.\n- Image: a two-stage Dockerfile. Build stage: dotnet SDK at the pinned DotnetWorkflow version, scripts/build.ps1 -Mode single-flight. Run stage: dotnet runtime with only the published single-flight output, ENTRYPOINT the host with --server, ENV SPIRAL_SERVER_BIND=0.0.0.0:7470.\n- Deployment (1 replica): containerPort 7470; readiness and liveness by tcpSocket on 7470 (the host prints a ready line, but a TCP probe needs no log parsing); resources requests 500m/1Gi, limits 2/4Gi (cold compile peaks measured on the box near 3 GB); a non-root securityContext with readOnlyRootFilesystem plus an emptyDir for the compiler's temp and cache dirs.\n- Service: ClusterIP on 7470.\nCI job, Linux runner only (matches CI-NIX's rule): install k3s pinned to an exact version via its install script with INSTALL_K3S_VERSION; docker build; k3s ctr images import (no registry needed for a throwaway cluster); kubectl apply -f deploy/k3s; kubectl rollout status deployment/spiral-compiler --timeout=300s; then a compliance smoke: kubectl port-forward and one compile request of a tiny sample through the server protocol, asserting the reply; finally kubectl delete. 'Compliance' in the title is read as: manifests that pass kubectl apply --dry-run=server plus a real rollout with probes green. A linter (kubeconform) pinned like the other tools can be added in the same job.\nOrder: blocked on dep PIPELINES-GREEN as before. Implementation needs the user's placement call above, so it waits on that decision and not on main.")
inl near_windows_native () = Task ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", "NEAR builds still need WSL (user 2026-10-15 21:20: avoid WSL on this machine, patching near would cost patches and compile times): track whether native Windows builds become possible; until then NEAR parts stay last and off the local machine", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "near,windows,wsl,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_windows_native () = Planning ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_near_windows_native_1 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: no WSL3 exists as far as known; WSL2's memory is bounded only by .wslconfig (memory=, autoMemoryReclaim). Decision kept: no WSL on the dev machine, NEAR and other Linux-only parts run on CI Linux runners.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_2 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T02:56Z", "main-thread", "22:56 main: correction of my previous note: WSL 3.0.3.0 exists and is installed (kernel 6.18.54, user updated it 10-15). Benchmark (job tmp/wslbench/bench.ps1, Ubuntu): cold boot 12.7 s, warm call 0.9 s, idle VM ~2.0-2.3 GB of host memory, a 2 GB allocation inside raised the VM to 4.3 GB and it returned to 2.3 GB within 20 s (reclaim works, unlike WSL2), ext4 write 643 MB/s vs /mnt/c 74 MB/s, listing 9,006 files over /mnt/c 5.9 s, shutdown releases everything. Verdict: NEAR tests can come back as bounded WSL jobs: clone into ext4, run through Invoke-Heavy -Wsl, wsl --shutdown afterwards, never during a Tibia live block. Risk: .wslconfig memory=12GB on a 16 GB machine lets one cargo build take the host down; recommended memory=6GB (user's file, not edited). NixOS and nixos2 distros are installed too (CI-NIX can be tried locally in WSL).")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_3 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:44 helper-lane lap 1: narrowed what actually needs WSL, from dice/contract's scripts: the contract build is already Windows-native (BuildSpiral to Rust, then cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown, export check, dist/dice.wasm); only tests/build.ps1 goes to Linux (Invoke-Block -Linux -> spiral scripts/core.ps1 Invoke-Linux -> wsl --exec bash, cargo run of the near-workspaces sandbox tests, which need the Linux/mac-only neard sandbox binary). So the WSL-free route on this machine is: keep the native wasm build, run the contract logic as mocked unit tests on Windows (NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS: testing_env!/VMContextBuilder), and leave the sandbox tests to CI Linux. Side find: the contract pins nightly-2024-07-14 while the workspace moved to nightly-2026-10-18 (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE).")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_4 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: confirmed the split already in dice's scripts: scripts/build.ps1 sets nearSandboxRunsHere = IsLinux and only then builds the contract (contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1) and runs the sandbox tests (contract/tests/build.ps1, 3 retries); the gh-pages workflow runs on ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025, so CI Linux covers the sandbox and Windows (local and CI) skips the whole contract, including the wasm build that needs no WSL. Concrete improvement within the no-WSL rule: on Windows run contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1 too, so the wasm build and its export check (new, contribute_seed, roll_within_bounds ...) are proven natively on every Windows run; only tests/build.ps1 needs Linux. Not edited: dice and alphabet must change together (mirror rule) and dice CI is red on other causes right now.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_5 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:12 helper-lane lap 3: staged the change as lab/NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE/dice_build_patch.py (anchor --check OK on dice scripts/build.ps1, LF): contract/build.ps1 -SkipTests 1 moves out of the IsLinux block, so the wasm build and its export check run on every Windows run, while contract/tests/build.ps1 (the NEAR sandbox) stays Linux-only. Checked the Windows prerequisites by reading: contract/build.ps1 is platform-neutral (Spiral compile, cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build for wasm32, a pure-PowerShell wasm export reader), and polyglot scripts/init.ps1 installs nightly-2024-07-14 with the wasm32 target on any OS; this machine has that toolchain. Mirror rule: alphabet has no NEAR contract (its scripts never mention contract or near), so the change has no alphabet counterpart. Not applied: dice CI is still red from the 06:48 run; apply after the user's push turns it green.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_6 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE/dice_build_patch.py dry run matches dice/scripts/build.ps1 today. It is applied together with the alphabet mirror.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_7 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 7u32, "2026-10-22T05:56Z", "main", "01:56 main: lab/NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE/dice_build_patch.py was reviewed and NOT applied. It moves dice's contract build out of the Linux-only block, so a Windows run would build the contract. Per NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES, dice_contract.spi no longer type-checks on the current tree (117:12, array vs array_base since core v1), so dice's Windows build would fail. It waits on that fix, then a native contract build proof, then dice plus the alphabet mirror together.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_8 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T08:09Z", "main", "04:09 main: the 10-19 blocker is gone. dice/contract/dice_contract.spi type-checks and compiles to Rust on today's installed compiler (180 KB, exit 0; compiled into TEMP, and dice's tracked dice_contract.rs is unchanged). The 117:12 array vs array_base error was fixed in dice by the '|> am'.base' after the ;[ ... ] literal (commit 4218032, 10-19). This corrects my 03:5x note on dice_build_patch.py: the type-check reason is gone, so the remaining question is only whether the NEAR wasm contract builds natively on Windows (cargo near / wasm32, near-sdk pin).")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_9 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: dice CI red since 06:48; patch staged -> next: after the user's push turns dice green: apply + Windows contract build | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice CI red; patch staged -> next: after green: apply | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice_build_patch.py dry run clean -> next: apply with the alphabet mirror")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_10 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 10u32, "2026-10-27T07:59Z", "main", "10-27 03:59 main: 10-27 03:59 main groom: fresh status (the last real note was 10-22; the item was on the stale list in EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS n5), from tonight's dice contract build (dice export migrate job-1793062386837, GREEN). The WASM build is NATIVE on Windows: dice/contract/build.ps1:53 runs 'cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown' with no WSL. The NEAR SANDBOX TESTS still go through WSL: dice/contract/tests/build.ps1:9 runs 'cargo run --release' via Invoke-Block -Linux -Retries 6. So the item's remaining work is exactly the sandbox test runner: either a native Windows near-sandbox binary for near-workspaces, or a documented reason it can't run natively. Also relevant: build.ps1:50 has a TODO (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE) keeping nightly-2024-07-14 pinned, because nearcore 1.79 rejects post-1.82 wasm features.")
inl fiscal_app () = Task ("FISCAL-APP", "New app (user 2026-10-15 21:50): a Spiral generator from all SEFAZ XSDs, Tibia style with test blocks and X-compatible from day one, Delphi backend, ACBr and Zeus as the benchmarks the way Tibia uses open-source bots, generating GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes able to carry ERP engines; open choice: generate at build time in Rust (build.rs + OUT_DIR caching, crate reuse) or in Spiral itself, since Zig comptime would re-parse the XSDs on every build", Epic, P0, D8, 45u32, Active, "SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER,SEFAZ-XSD-SCHEMA-MODEL,SEFAZ-XSD-PATTERNS,SEFAZ-XSD-GENERATOR,FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", "app,sefaz,delphi,spiral,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_app () = Planning ("FISCAL-APP", Minutes 210u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_app_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:22Z", "main-thread", "10:22 main-thread fork: decision input (user question 2026-10-17: keep advancing Tibia for dogfood, or start the XSD tasks?). Measured from the backlog: the Tibia lane surfaced ~20 tooling items on 2026-10-12..14 (compiler GADT-ONCE fixes, C self-tail/incref loops, WGSL tail-call/union/dispatch bugs, Kino memo scope and Rust/C flag parity, hopac Debug crash, private-cache leak, macro-argument limits, eoie note author), 2 tooling notes on 10-14, and 0 since: 10-15 had 68 tibia-lane notes all on TIBIA items (potion fidelity, hunting ground, retreat, policy), 10-16/17 none. Remaining TIBIA work is game fidelity and policy, i.e. business logic; its compiled modules stay valuable as a regression corpus (already in the pre-pack corpus check). SEFAZ-XSD would exercise surfaces Tibia never touched: generating thousands of types from XSDs (compile-time scale, a real load for hopacsota's parallel evaluation), reflection/prototype codegen (the CONVO-IN-SPIRAL schema machinery, both directions), XML parse/serialize, the Delphi backend (least used), X-compat from day one. Recommendation: make SEFAZ-XSD-APP the next dogfood once reprioritized; Tibia stays maintenance + corpus. Open choice in the title (Rust build.rs vs Spiral-side generation) is the first experiment, in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/ per LAB-IN-REPO.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:46Z", "main-thread", "10:46 main-thread fork: XSD census (research only, not a start; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/census.py, input = sparse XSD-only clone of the Zeus benchmark ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET, kept outside the repo as data): 580 .xsd (500 distinct contents, 5.1 MB, 1 parse error; NF-e/NFC-e, CT-e and its modals, MDF-e, events, DF-e distribution, several versions each), 12,076 elements, 2,238 complexTypes (572 named, 151 distinct names; the rest anonymous inline), 5,389 simpleTypes (1,445 named, 179 distinct names; TCOrgaoIBGE x35, TVerEvento x33 across versions), 11,360 enumeration facets, 2,055 regex patterns, 1,875 maxLength / 1,759 minLength, 393 choices, 819 attributes, 590 includes + 83 imports, max nesting depth 23. Mapping for the generator: enumerations -> unions (11k cases), choices -> unions, minOccurs/maxOccurs -> option/array, length/pattern facets -> validated nominal types whose regexes compile through the brzozowski derivatives work (lib dogfood), shared tiposBasico -> one module per (family, version) with content-hash dedupe (80 duplicate files). Build.rs-vs-Spiral question: parsing 5 MB is ~1 s, so the real cost is compiling the generated Spiral (12k elements of types and validators) - first experiment: generate nfe v4.00 + its tiposBasico only, measure single-flight and hopacsota compile time, which doubles as a large hopacsota load test.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:54Z", "main-thread", "10:54 main-thread fork: enumeration mapping measured (research, not a start; scripts spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/enum_gen.py and enums_gen.py, generated .spi written to the git-ignored lab/.runs/sefaz-generated/, XSD data = the DFe.NET sparse clone): TCodUfIBGE (tiposBasico_v1.03, 27 values) -> union + parse (option) + code_of: 94 lines, compiles and round-trips '35' (exit 27), 3 s with gcc. All 58 distinct named enumerated simpleTypes of the corpus = 2,179 cases -> 4,653 lines in one module: compiles in 5 s total (only one parse reachable from main, so this is front end + type check cost, peval of the rest is lazy), 1.25 MB of C for that one used parser chain. Most of the 11,360 enumeration facets are anonymous inline simpleTypes inside the leiaute files, so the generator must name them by element path. Next for the decision: generate the full NF-e v4.00 type set (named + inline) and compile it with every type reachable, single-flight vs hopacsota.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: inline enumerations measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/inline_enums.py): 1,405 enumerated simpleTypes (313 named, 1,092 inline: leiauteNFe 325, cteTiposBasico 186, prod 81, ...) collapse to only 266 DISTINCT value sets holding 3,312 values (vs 11,360 enumeration facets): dedupe by value set is a ~3.4x cut. Shape: 473 single-value enums (fixed constants such as versao 1.00 -> a literal/singleton type, not a union), 306 two-value (mostly 0/1 or 1/2 flags), 130 three, 132 four; the largest has 792 values. So the generator emits ~266 shared enumeration unions plus typed singleton constants, keyed by value set and referenced by element path; that is far below the 4,653-line / 2,179-case module that already compiles in 5 s. Still to measure for the decision: complexTypes (2,238, mostly anonymous) and the 2,055 patterns.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 5u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: patterns measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/patterns.py): 2,055 pattern facets are only 196 DISTINCT regexes (median length 20, max 650: the NFC-e QR-code URL); features over the distinct ones: char classes 182, bounded repeats {n,m} 166, alternation 98, groups 64, optional 51, star/plus 16, \\\\d\\\\s\\\\w 12, XSD class subtraction -[ 4, negated class 1, \\\\p properties 0; most reused [0-9]{8} x73, [0-9]{1,4} x67, [0-9]{6} x67, [0-9]{1,15} x64. Reading: a small regex dialect (classes, bounded repeats, alternation, groups) compiled once per distinct pattern into DFAs - exactly what the brzozowski-derivatives work does; only XSD class subtraction needs a dedicated rule. Decision inputs now complete on enumerations (266 value sets) and patterns (196); complexTypes are the remaining unmeasured part.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: complexTypes measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/complex_types.py, structure = element names/types/occurs and sequence/choice nesting): 2,238 complexTypes are 826 distinct structures (2.7x dedupe); 539 contain a choice (-> unions), 719 carry attributes; most are small records (1-10 descendant elements for ~1,650 of them) with a long tail (84 with 40+ descendants: the NF-e/CT-e document roots). Generator size estimate after dedupe: ~826 records/unions + ~266 enumeration unions + ~196 compiled patterns + typed singleton constants, organized per (family, version) with shared basics. All research scripts (census, enum_gen, enums_gen, inline_enums, patterns, complex_types) live in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app and read a DFe.NET sparse XSD clone (git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --no-checkout https://github.com/ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET.git; git sparse-checkout set --no-cone '*.xsd'). Still a research item: start only on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 7u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the six measurement .py scripts deleted; every number they produced (1,405 enums -> 266 value sets, 2,055 patterns -> 196 regexes, 2,238 complexTypes -> 826 structures) and the DFe.NET sparse clone command are in notes 1-6. The generator itself is to be written in Spiral on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:12Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:12 helper-lane lap 1: verified the lab folder spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app is gone (cleanup 10-17 18:05; numbers live in notes 1-6). Prerequisite found for the Spiral-side generator that note 7 settles on: lib/spiral has no XML reader at all (the only xml hits are xmlns attributes in 10 leptos svg files), so the first slice needs either a Rust-arm primitive over a crate (roxmltree, read-only DOM, fits XSD reading) or an XSD-subset tokenizer in Spiral; roxmltree keeps it to one $ primitive plus typed Spiral walking, but Kino test blocks cannot use crates yet (KINO-RUST-CRATES), so tests of that slice would be e2e only. Still research: start only on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:05 helper-lane lap 2: sized the first slice's input so the reader decision is concrete (research only, no generator started): the DFe.NET sparse clone still exists as data at C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\xsd-data (580 .xsd). tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd is 23 KB / 608 lines with only 13 element kinds (enumeration 116, annotation/documentation/restriction/simpleType 53 each, whiteSpace 49, pattern 43, maxLength 16, minLength 3, element 2, complexType 1, sequence 1, schema 1), 7 attribute names (name, type, base, value, targetNamespace, elementFormDefault, attributeFormDefault), 2 comments, no CDATA and no entity references. So a Spiral XSD-subset tokenizer (start/end/self-closing tags, double-quoted attributes, text, comments) covers this file without a crate, which favours the no-crate route over roxmltree for slice 1 and keeps Kino test blocks usable.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:21 helper-lane lap 3: sized the second input the same way (research only; lab/SEFAZ-XSD-APP/xsd_shape.py, python -I over the job-tmp DFe.NET clone): leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd is 352 KB / 7,718 lines, 21 element kinds (documentation 915, annotation 914, element 900, enumeration 424, simpleType/restriction 295, whiteSpace 176, sequence 175, complexType 121, maxLength 117, minLength 116, pattern 77, choice 22, attribute 15, length 5, include 2, unique/selector/field 2 each, import 1), 20 attribute names (incl. minOccurs 333, maxOccurs 26, use 15, ref 2, xpath 4, schemaLocation 3, xmlns:ds), indentation depth 24, 6 comments, no CDATA, only double-quoted attributes, 23 entity references of exactly two kinds (&quot; 14, &amp; 9) plus the xml declaration. So the slice-1 tokenizer from lap 2 grows by: a processing-instruction skip, the 5 predefined entities in attribute values and text, and identity constraints (unique/selector/field) parsed but ignored by the generator. Still no crate needed. Research only; the generator starts on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 11u32, "2026-10-22T04:33Z", "main", "00:33 app home decided (user 22:0x drain direction; epic says a new app Tibia style, and tibia moved to its own repo on 10-17; the 10-17 census kept the DFe.NET corpus outside the repo as data): the app now lives in a new sibling folder C:\\home\\git\\sefaz (not git-initialized; the user decides): src/ holds the reader and generator modules (xml, xsd, xsd_regex, corpus, resolve, generator, 13 generated_* modules, package.spiproj with packageDir ../deps/spiral/lib) plus their fixtures (tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd, patterns, pattern probes and expectations, corpus.txt); scripts/ holds the oracles, delphi-arms.ps1, init.ps1 (deps/spiral junction or clone; deps/corpus from a sparse DFe.NET checkout of the 4 schema folders) and gate-core.ps1; samples/ and generated/ as in the lab; .gitignore excludes deps/ and target/. Every lab path was rewritten (0 left; corpus.txt points into deps/corpus). Proof queued: job main-sefaz-move-gate runs lib_gate on the 5 core modules from the new src after the night install. spiral/lab/SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER stays until that passes.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 12u32, "2026-10-22T05:14Z", "main", "01:14 main: spiral/lab/SEFAZ-XSD-APP is drained. Its only file, xsd_shape.py (corpus shape survey: element/attribute kinds, depth, entities), moved to C:\\home\\git\\sefaz\\scripts\\xsd_shape.py. The app now lives in C:\\home\\git\\sefaz (src, scripts, samples, generated, deps/corpus).")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 13u32, "2026-10-22T11:37Z", "main", "2026-10-22 07:37 main: split done (user 07:1x: 'fiscal is cloned, keep what's lib in lib then what's fiscal move to fiscal'). LIB (spiral/lib/spiral, landed via lib_land, gated on a copy): xml.spi 501CDF289B7205BC (3 of 3), xsd.spi D827012118A640EE (5 of 5), xsd_resolve.spi DC0785BDB71ED3FD (was resolve.spi, renamed for the lib namespace; 0 tests, since all of its tests read the BR corpus, so an inline test is a follow-up), xsd_regex.spi F7BC90E4DD250D30 (2 of 2); package.spiproj lists them after testing. Every corpus-reading test and helper left the lib versions (census_item/items/corpus, resolve_corpus, the tiposBasico and pattern-corpus tests). FISCAL (C:\\home\\git\\fiscal, untracked, for the user's first commit): src/ corpus.spi (1 of 1), corpus_checks.spi (the cut corpus parts, 9 of 9; the DFA census test no longer writes a TSV into TEMP), generator.spi (4 of 4; resolve. -> xsd_resolve.), the 13 generated_* modules, corpus.txt and the pattern data, tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd; scripts/ (init, gates; delphi-arms now defaults to lib xml.spi), samples/, candidates/, generated/; deps/ from init.ps1 (spiral junction) plus the 580-schema corpus copied from sefaz. Paths C:/home/git/sefaz -> C:/home/git/fiscal. Follow-ups: candidates/dfa-table/xsd_regex.spi is now a candidate for a LIB module, so candidate-gate.ps1 must apply it to a lib copy; generated modules not re-gated (unchanged files, gated before). C:\\home\\git\\sefaz is kept as the only other copy until 'git -C fiscal ls-files src' lists the files (the user committed), then it is deleted by that condition.")
inl note_of_fiscal_app_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-APP", 14u32, "2026-10-27T07:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:30 fiscal: estimate set to 390m remaining = the sum of its open children: SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER, -SCHEMA-MODEL, -PATTERNS and -GENERATOR are Done, so the only open child is FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH (390m, see its note of this time).")
inl suite_map () = Task ("SUITE-MAP", "Map the whole test suite (user 2026-10-15 21:50): harness rows, lib and app test blocks, Kino suites, eoie tests, Tibia tests, by area, cost and overlap, like the TODO sweep does for comments, so it can be split and run in parallel deliberately", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "tests,harness,map", "current", "current")
inl spiral_path_syntax () = Task ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", "Member access spelled tight '::' (st::head, sm'::replace, (f x)::head, ::(+)), Rust-style, so posted Spiral never autolinks on X and needs no zero-width defusing (user 10-26: prioritize, then a full migration round; ACCESSOR-2 measured it: 0 real conflicts with cons/GADT ' :: ', notes 9-10): at a token start '::' + name or '::(op)' is the same TokSymbol as '.name', whatever is on its left; spaced ' :: ' stays cons/GADT; respell maps '.x' <-> '::x'; edition-3 packages report single-dot symbols; every repo migrates in the spiral.toml round", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,spiral,syntax,x-compat,edition3,migration", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_path_syntax () = Planning ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", Minutes 180u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night evidence (candidate --x-compat autolinks = member access X would link): lib/spiral 272 (.map 154, .run 22, .to 22, .box 16), apps/tibia 155 (.at 28, .ms 25, .target 18), blog 5, x client 0 after hand renames. Renaming cannot scale, so postable edition-3 sources need a separator change.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: measured the '::' conflict the title asks about: GADT case signatures (| Case :: ...) occur on 4,412 lines in 467 .spi/.spir files across the repos (eoie state alone: core 47, coverage 32, pipeline 24, differential 18; tibia 23; dice 2), so '::' as member access would only be separable by spacing (a::b vs Case :: t), a whitespace-sensitive lexer rule that the formatter and every reader must keep. Also sharpened the problem: X only autolinks when the field name after the dot is a real TLD (the 10-17 census hits are .map, .run, .to, .box, .at, .ms - all delegated TLDs), so members whose name is not a TLD (p.author) are safe while TLD names (.post is a sponsored TLD, hence posts.post) link; the separator change matters only for TLD-named members.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: sized the rename-only alternative against IANA's current TLD list (1,437 delegated TLDs, data.iana.org): across lib/spiral .spi, dotted member names that are TLDs number 17 distinct names in about 397 uses (regex count, macro text included): map 150, box 94, show 83, to 20, run 15, org 13, now 5, data 3, storage 3, stream 2, pin 2, day 2, open/do/city/select/zip 1 each. The top three (map, box, show) are 82 percent of all autolink-prone uses in lib, so renaming three functions (e.g. map -> each / map_to, box -> boxed, show -> shown) would remove most of lib's X links without a separator change; tibia's .at .ms .target and the long tail would still need the separator or per-repo renames. Input for the edition-3 decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES' renames could absorb the three.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: extended the rename-only sizing from lib to every repo with a reusable counter (lab/SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX/tld_members.py + tlds.txt, IANA list fetched today: 1,437 TLDs; regex over .spi, so file names inside strings such as eoie's .rs/.md also count). TLD-named members after a dot, distinct/uses/top-3 share: lib/spiral 16/381/81% (map 135, box 94, show 83); tibia/src 16/156/48% (at 34, ms 26, health 15, gold 13, band 13); eoie/src 19/257/55% (rs 58, zip 54, report 31, target 30); dice 10/195/69% (map 56, box 41, th/td 38 each: html tables); x 3/33 (id 31); blog/engine 3/7; apps/cells/spi 1/5 (one). So renaming three lib functions fixes lib and most of dice, but tibia and eoie spread over domain words (at, ms, health, report, target, id), and x's .id is its own record field; a rename-only route would mean per-repo vocabulary bans, which favours a separator change for edition 3. Input for the edition-3 lane's decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES carries the lib part.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:45 helper-lane lap 4: sized the other side of the '::' separator option: tight a::b already occurs 5,137 times in 437 .spi files under C:\\home\\git, but nearly all of it is Rust path text inside macros (std::fs::..., std::env::... in eoie, x, dice, lib), which the Spiral lexer never reads as Spiral tokens, while every Spiral use of '::' is the spaced GADT signature (Case :: type, 4,412 lines per lap 1). So '::' member access is lexically separable today by the rule tight = member access, spaced = GADT signature, with macro bodies unaffected; the cost is readability, since Spiral member paths would look like the Rust paths in the macros next to them, and the formatter must never insert spaces around a member '::'. X does not autolink a::b (no dot), which is the goal. Input for the edition-3 lane: '::' works mechanically; a non-Rust-looking separator avoids the visual collision.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): measurements handed over; waits for the edition-3 separator choice -> next: edition-3 decision | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: sizing complete (TLD members, '::' separability); the title assigns the call to the edition-3 syntax lane -> next: edition-3 lane decision | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: edition-3 lane decision -> next: edition-3 lane")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 7u32, "2026-10-26T05:09Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:07 x: DECISION RULE ACCESSOR-1 (user 10-26 ~01:0x: prioritize a compiler accessor change so posting needs no dot defusing; then a full migration round), written before any candidate is measured. Candidates: tight '::' (st::head), tight ':' (st:head), '..' (st..head), U+00B7 middle dot (st·head), '#', '@', '$', prime (st'head). Hard gates, each must pass or the candidate is out: G1 X-safe: no profile entity (x.json domain_pattern with tlds.txt, mention, hashtag, cashtag) matches '<lhs><sep><tld-named member>' for lhs in {ident, sm', ), ], }} at line start, after a space and after '('; also no Mastodon custom-emoji ':name:' or Bluesky link facet; G2 copy-paste: a posted cell pasted back compiles with no stripping (no zero-width chars); G3 ASCII, typed without IME; G4 no meaning change for any token the current tokenizer accepts in .spi/.spir outside string literals and macro bodies across x, spiral, eoie, tibia, dice, alphabet, fiscal, polyglot, blog (count of conflicting sites = 0, or every conflict is a currently-rejected token sequence). Score among survivors, lowest wins: S = 2 * (weight per access, X code_point_weight) + 3 * (whitespace-sensitive lexer rules added) + 1 if it collides visually with a token Spiral already has. Tie-break: Rust/ML-family precedent for member access, then fewer characters. Deadline: decided by 2026-10-26 03:00 local; if no candidate passes all gates, the fallback is keep '.' + lexer treats U+200B as whitespace + poster defuses TLD dots only. Outcome goes to the compiler lane as one spec (tokenizer change + migration tool + transition window), not a menu.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 8u32, "2026-10-26T05:29Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:28 x: ACCESSOR-1 OUTCOME (rule = note 7, applied as written; scripts %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\accessor_census.ps1 + accessor_sites.ps1). G1 X-safety against x.json (domain_pattern + tlds.txt, mention, hashtag, cashtag) plus Mastodon ':name:' emoji and the atproto link regex, 6 lhs (st, sm', f x), xs], { a }, x1) x 8 members (map post io to co ab head id) x 5 contexts: raw dot 60 links; '#', '@', '$' 160 each (hashtag/mention/cashtag after sm' or ')'): OUT. Defused dot 0 links but U+200B: OUT (G2/G3; probe: 'st<U+200B>.head' TokenizerErrors, 'st.<U+200B>head' ParserErrors, plain exit 0). U+00B7: OUT (G3). Prime: OUT (G4: ' is a var char, Tokenize.fs:149, 462 sites where x'y is one identifier). Survivors '::', ':', '..' all 0 links incl. chains. G4 over 9 repos with strings/macros/comments stripped: 9 / 6 / 19 raw sites, all inside macro or host text on inspection (Zig 'break :blk', Tailwind ']]:block', Rust '\\ ::new' and '0..n', an F# slice in a tmp sample, plus 3 line-offset artifacts) -> 0 real conflicts for each; note the tokenizer already rejects ':' right after a var (Tokenize.fs:175, tokens.spi:392), so tight a:b and a::b are errors today. Score S = 2*weight + 3*(in-use token that gains a spacing-dependent meaning) + 1 (visual collision with a Spiral token): '..' = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4; ':' = 2 + 3 (x : t annotation vs x :t symbol) + 1 (annotation; st:head reads as Rust/TS 'st: head') = 6; '::' = 4 + 3 (x :: xs cons vs x ::xs) + 1 (cons/GADT) = 8. WINNER '..': st..head, sm'..replace, (f x)..head, ..(+). Reading of the ws term: the pre-measurement framing (my 00:5x answer and note 2 of 10-19: 'a rule the formatter and every reader must keep') penalises an in-use token whose meaning splits by spacing; a pure rule count (each candidate replaces today's '.x' rule one for one) would give ':' 3, '..' 4, '::' 5, so the margin rests on that reading, recorded here rather than re-decided. Precedent: Dart cascades (a..b is member access). Cost: weight 2 per access, equal to today's defused dot, with no zero-width char, and the splitter's own defuse rule never fires on '..x' (no label char before the second dot). Corpus to migrate: 32,995 dot symbols outside strings/macros in 9 repos (x 2,233). Spec sent to the compiler team as one candidate (see the 01:3x message): lexer pair in all three tokenizer copies, respell pair, edition-3 finding, migration in the same round as EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 9u32, "2026-10-26T05:36Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:36 x: DECISION RULE ACCESSOR-2 (user 10-26 ~01:4x: 'i don't know if i like .., experiment with :: ... without conflicting with list cons ... the rust similarity might pay off'), written before measuring; it supersedes ACCESSOR-1's pick and is a feasibility gate, not a re-score. Proposed lexing: at a token start, '::' immediately followed by a var-start char or '(' is a symbol (same TokSymbol as '.x' / '.(op)'), and the var lexer's ': right after a var' error gets one exception for exactly that '::' shape (st::head lexes as st + symbol). Everything else about ':' and '::' stays: spaced ' :: ' is cons/GADT, '(::)' an operator section, 'x: t' still an error. Tight '::' is ADOPTED if, on the 9-repo corpus with every '.x' symbol respelled to '::x' by the model lexer: M1 0 sites where an existing cons/GADT '::' (as the current lexer reads it, strings/macros/comments excluded) lexes as a symbol after migration; M2 0 respelled symbols that fuse with a preceding operator char (would lex as one operator); M3 0 respelled symbols that lex as cons; M4 the X/Mastodon/Bluesky gate of ACCESSOR-1 G1 stays at 0 links on chains ('a::b::c', '(f x)::m', '::m' alone). Any M1-M3 site that only a formatter respace fixes counts as resolved if the formatter already emits ' :: ' spaced for cons. If one gate fails and a lexer-only tweak closes it, allow ONE tweak then re-measure once; if it still fails, '..' (ACCESSOR-1) stands and the reasons go to the user.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 10u32, "2026-10-26T06:01Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:01 x: ACCESSOR-2 OUTCOME: tight '::' ADOPTED, no tweak needed; supersedes ACCESSOR-1's '..'. Model lexer in C# (%TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\dcolon_model_fast.ps1; strings/macros/comments masked with offsets kept) over 2,520 .spi/.spir in 9 repos. Rule: at a token start, '::' immediately followed by a var-start char, or by '(' + operator chars + ')', is a symbol, whatever is on its left; the var lexer's ': right after a var' error gets one exception for that exact shape (st::head = st + symbol). Every '.x' symbol respelled to '::x' (32,870) and re-lexed: M1 existing cons/GADT '::' tokens (9,793) that would lex as a symbol: 6, all macro/host text on inspection (Rust '\\ ::new' / '\\ ::Regex' / '\\ ::Stdio' in eoie host macros, an F# ')::acc' in a tmp sample's macro), so 0 real; a further 4,048 '::' have a name char on the left, which today's lexer rejects, so they are Rust paths in macros my masking missed, not Spiral; M2 respelled symbols fused into a preceding operator: 0; M3 respelled symbols not lexing as a symbol: 0; M4 X/Mastodon/Bluesky: 0 links on 10 standalone and chain cases ('f ::map', '(::io)', '::post' at line start, 'st::head::map::to', '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)::map', 'g ::(+) 1', sm'::replace) plus ACCESSOR-1's grid. Variant measured and REJECTED: firing only when '::' is also tight on the left (the compiler team's first sketch) leaves 120 standalone symbols lexing as cons (e.g. '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)', 'vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 ::z'), so the spec says right-tight, any left side. What stays: spaced ' :: ' is cons/GADT; '(::)' is an operator section; 'x ::xs' (tight right only) becomes a symbol, and no Spiral source has it today (M1). Cost: weight 2 per access (= today's defused dot) and no zero-width chars. Spec sent to the compiler team 02:0x.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 11u32, "2026-10-26T06:32Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:32 x: X-COMPLIANCE COMPROMISE (user 10-26 ~02:1x: 'would we lose such concise modeling [get_args () .gleam, vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 .z]? try arriving at a sota compromise considering every code we have and how it would conflict with x, we need all repos x compliant even if that requires migration'). One-liners are KEPT: the adopted right-tight rule makes '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)' and 'vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 ::z' the same standalone symbol argument they are today, same token count; only the both-sides-tight variant (rejected, note 10) broke them. Precedent: GHC proposal 229 (whitespace-sensitive operators, GHC 9.0, https://github.com/ghc-proposals/ghc-proposals/blob/master/proposals/0229-whitespace-bang-patterns.rst) classifies an operator by its spacing as prefix / suffix / tight infix / loose infix; here prefix and tight infix '::name' are the symbol, loose infix ' :: ' is cons/GADT, suffix 'x:: ' stays an error. Full census of X entities by lexical context over 10 repos (profile semantics incl. the two-letter-TLD rule; %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\x_entities_by_context.ps1): links code 1,189 / string 314 / macro 69 / comment 141; mentions 28 / 146 / 50 / 0 (Gleam '@external(erlang,' strings, Zig '\\@intCast' macro text); hashtags 5 / 61 / 25 / 0 ('#include', '#if', '\\#app_'); cashtags 19 / 42 / 9 / 0 (PowerShell '$env:', JSON '$ref') ('code' still holds some multi-line macro text). Three layers, all repos compliant: L1 '::' accessor: Spiral's own syntax, 32,870 symbols, no defusing. L2 macro-text escapes for literal sigils doubled: '@@' and '##' replace '\\@' and '\\#' (83 entity-shaped sites); measured X-safe ('@@intCast', '##include', '$$env' none match; '\\@intCast' is a mention today); '$$' as a macro escape for '$' changes only 2 existing '$$' sequences. L3 U+200B-transparent source: the lexer drops U+200B everywhere, strings and macro text included (0 raw U+200B in any .spi/.spir today, so no program changes), and the poster defuses only real entities (link/mention/hashtag/cashtag) left in foreign text: about 700 sites in 13M chars instead of today's 39,175 dot defusals, and a pasted post compiles byte-identically. Foreign syntax (C '#include', Gleam '@external', file names 'main.rs') is never contorted; lib helpers for repeated boilerplate such as Gleam '@external(erlang, ...)' ride BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP as an optional shrink. Items: SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX (L1), SPIRAL-MACRO-SIGIL-ESCAPES (L2), SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT (L3), X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY (poster side of L3).")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 12u32, "2026-10-28T10:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 DONE: tight :: member access is in the installed core 9FF1B708 -- spiral_compiler.fs:43095 (symbol lexer :: branch) + :180678 (respell . <-> ::). Composed into 5n via pack-next5n/lexer-dcolon-apply.ps1 + 5n built and blessed (review-5n.ps1). Functional re-run delegated to x/fiscal/tibia against the installed compiler (authoritative real usage); reopen on a red row.")
