inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_1 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: evidence 10-15: the gate stopped the strict renewal when agile_todo_scan reached 1,184 (922 .rs + 167 .spi + 95 notebook .spi) and forced a clean split into sources/scan crates; the largest crate after the split is command_spec_domain at 998, so it must be split before the next agile verb.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_2 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory: gated: eoie (crate max 949-998), x (report-only, view.rs 1,885 over); ungated monoliths: apps/spiral spiral.rs 24,281 lines, dice ui main.rs 16,878, dice lib dice.rs 6,639, apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,464.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_3 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 3u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x: all 12 generated crates under 1,000 after the view split (view 999, digest 989, jsonl 929, args 889, xc 862); the margin on view is 1 line, so the gate stays report-only until RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION or a further split gives room")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_4 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: re-inventory 10-19 (lines of the generated .rs): ungated monoliths apps/spiral spiral.rs 23,418 (24,281 on 10-17), dice ui src/main.rs 16,878 (same), dice lib dice.rs 7,777 (6,639 on 10-17, grew 17 percent), apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, dice contract dice_contract.rs 3,396 and polyglot chat_contract.rs 1,374 (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES); x's largest crates are now digest 991, view 991, args 960, so view gained 8 lines of margin since 10-18 but the report-only mode should stay until there are ~5 percent of headroom. The gate (eoie source-topology) still exists only in eoie; dice lib growing past 7.7k is the strongest case for adding report-only counts to dice's build first (it mirrors to alphabet).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_5 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:31 helper-lane lap 2: wrote the report-only step as lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1 (a generated crate = an .rs with a sibling .spi; prints those over the limit, never fails) and ran it on dice, alphabet, spiral/apps and polyglot/apps: 448 generated crates, 12 over 1,000 lines - spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet apps/documents documents.rs 15,574 (missing from the 10-17 inventory), dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374, and 6 compiler samples' main.rs (mega roots and typecheck_long_let_body, which are matrix fixtures and should be excluded from any gate). dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is not caught because its source .spi is not beside it, so the rule needs the [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output convention (x's) to find every generated crate. Call it in-process (& script -Root a, b): pwsh -File collapses the -Root array.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_6 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: switched the report to the planned detection (lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1, still report-only): generated crates = outputs declared by a Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] output = path, plus the sibling rule (an .rs beside its .spi); apps/compiler/tmp/samples (matrix fixtures) and target/deps/dist are excluded. Run over dice, alphabet, spiral/apps, polyglot/apps, x and blog: 26 generated crates (17 declared by metadata, all of them x's, every one under 1,000 lines), 6 over the limit, all sibling-rule: spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet documents.rs 15,574, dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374. dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is still invisible because neither rule reaches it; giving dice ui's Cargo.toml the x-style [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output is the one-line fix that makes it count. Next: propose the step as non-failing in dice and alphabet build.ps1 (mirror rule) after their CI is green.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_7 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: staged the one-line fix that makes dice ui visible to the gate: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py adds [package.metadata.spiral] entry = src/dice_ui.spi, output = src/main.rs to dice/ui/Cargo.toml (anchor --check OK; values read from ui/build.ps1, which compiles src/dice_ui.spi to src/main.rs). Cargo ignores package.metadata, so builds are unaffected; with it the report finds main.rs (16,878 lines) as the seventh over-limit crate. The report step for dice and alphabet build.ps1 waits for their CI to be green (mirror rule; alphabet has no ui crate, its counterpart is apps/documents).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_8 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py dry run matches dice/ui/Cargo.toml today. Per the dice/alphabet mirror rule it gets applied to both repos at once.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_9 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:50 x: reference implementation for other repos: x/scripts/gate.ps1 reads [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output from every apps/**/Cargo.toml, counts the generated output's lines and, with -Enforce, throws on any crate over -Limit (1000) and on finding no crate at all (vacuity). x's apps/client/build.ps1 now runs it with -Enforce right after compiling the crates (until today it only reported). For dice/alphabet: once dice_ui_metadata.py adds the metadata, the same script with -Root works unchanged. On .spi in the count: x counts only generated .rs, which is what the 1k limit protects.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_10 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 10u32, "2026-10-21T23:16Z", "eoie-lane", "19:16 eoie team, evidence 10-21 (generated .rs lines from previews on trees rebuilt from live): the gate fired four times on tonight's drafts, each before any release: analysis.rs 1108 (r34 where filters inlined through the eoie_table_query package), watch.rs 1208 (r35 job-state code inlined through eoie_jobs), read_query.rs 1007 (r35 grep root + tail), and gate.rs reached exactly 1000 (r35 window holder). Fixes were crate boundaries, never trimming: a row-mask export (eoie_table_rows_kept, analysis.rs 965, table_test.rs 736), a job-state export (eoie_job_state_word, watch.rs 954), a crate split (eoie_grep_query; read_query.rs 993 -> 632), and computing range bounds once. Pattern: a Spiral package import inlines the used functions into every consumer, so a shared helper growing in one package pushes all its importers toward the gate at once; an export at the crate boundary is the structural fix. Margins after r34/r35/r36: gate.rs 1000 (0), do_program.rs 988, do_edit.rs 976, analysis.rs 965, command_spec.rs 962, watch.rs 954, experiments.rs 949. Proposal for the gate: report the 10 crates nearest 1000 in every preview so a slice sees its margin before it lands.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_11 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 11u32, "2026-10-23T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:58 main: dice and alphabet now carry the crate metadata x's gate reads (unstaged). dice ui/Cargo.toml gets [package.metadata.spiral] entry src/dice_ui.spi, output src/main.rs (lab dice_ui_metadata.py, applied). alphabet apps/documents/Cargo.toml gets the mirror, entry documents.spi, output documents.rs. Measured: both are far over the limit today. dice ui main.rs is 16,281 generated lines (dist copy 16,878), dice contract is the 178 KB dice_contract.rs (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), alphabet documents.rs is 15,315. So the gate cannot be enforced in these repos until their output is split into module crates (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES, NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), and until then it runs report-only. Two gaps in reusing x/scripts/gate.ps1 as is: it scans only apps/**/Cargo.toml (dice keeps its crates in ui/ and contract/, so it would find none and fail its vacuity check), and the same counting already lives in eoie source-topology. Per eoie-first, the cross-repo gate should be one eoie verb ('eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit 1000]' over every Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral]), not a fourth copy of the script; filing that request for the eoie team.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_12 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice ui metadata patch staged; dice CI red -> next: after green: apply + report step | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice_ui_metadata.py dry run clean -> next: apply with the alphabet mirror")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_13 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 13u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 75m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_14 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 14u32, "2026-10-25T00:59Z", "eoie", "20:59 From x's build research (finding 7): generated functions grow superlinearly in LLVM and MIR dataflow. The crate gate already counts lines per crate, so add a per-crate report of the largest function (name, lines) to show where to cut. Done when eoie crate gate prints largest_fn=<name>:<lines> per crate and a notebook case checks it on a fixture crate.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_15 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 15u32, "2026-10-25T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 eoie: the largest_fn milestone (note 2) is built: each crate gate row ends with largest_fn=<name>:<lines> (the first on a tie; generics end the name). model.spi has largest_function_of (a pure line scan for fn and pub fn) plus largest_text, the counters are folded into one verdict_count, and every line is now at or under 120 (the legacy long lines were migrated in the same change). The gate.spi walker reads each output once for both counts. Spec case 43's expected rows carry the new column. The model notebook passes, including the_largest_function_names_where_to_cut; regen plus cargo check of eoie_crate_gate on 5k is running. Motivation from today: the eoie_cli (direct_verb_usage 136 lines) and agile_task_listing (list_request_from 143) cuts were both found by hand profiling that this column now prints. Left: the per-repo gate in every repo that emits Rust crates, and the decision on .spi in the count.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_16 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 16u32, "2026-10-25T11:27Z", "eoie", "07:27 eoie: correction to note 15. The model notebook passed and cargo check was green, but on 5k the regen put eoie_crate_gate at 1092 lines (926 published): function_name 73 + largest_from 50 + largest_function_of 23 + helpers. That breaks the gate the feature exists to serve, and the package is HostFree, so a sibling-crate call is not allowed either. Reverted under the 950 rule (gate.spi and model.spi match the before-s-stages snapshot by hash; spec case 43 rows restored). Draft kept at scratch clean\\gate-largest\\ (model with largest_function_of, largest_text, verdict_count and the migrated long lines; gate.spi reading each output once). Next step, ordered: split eoie_crate_gate by hand first (manifest scan and walk in one crate, measure and verdict rows in another, or a Spiral-only model package shared by two crates), then re-add largest_fn into the half with room, and measure. A cheaper scan (split on newline-fn instead of per-line substring and index_of) is the alternative to try first if the split costs more.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_17 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 17u32, "2026-10-25T11:40Z", "eoie", "07:40 eoie: cheaper-scan arm measured (one split on newline-fn plus a chunk loop using the existing line_count): eoie_crate_gate 1011 on 5k (versus 1092 for the per-line scan, 926 published before 5j). The model notebook passes and cargo check is green, but the crate is over 950, so it is reverted under the same rule (both files match the snapshot by hash; draft kept as scratch clean\\gate-largest\\model-cheap-scan.spi). Conclusion: largest_fn does not fit inside eoie_crate_gate at its current size, so the split comes first, and the full 5l' measure will show the gate's own baseline on the new compiler. Split plan: a Spiral-only eoie_crate_gate_model package (manifest parse, verdicts, rows, largest_fn), plus the walker crate and a measure crate exporting row text per output file, so the walker never holds the row code.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_18 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 18u32, "2026-10-26T16:22Z", "eoie", "12:22 eoie: same root cause as GRANULAR-LINT's overrun (notes 14-16 there), which changes the plan. The compiler lane traced the size (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE, P3): lib string wrappers (sm'.trim, sm'.substring and the like) are := and partial-evaluated at every call site, while the slice primitive itself is already one shared fn. The fix in kit's hands: route a crate's frequent string calls through local :~ wrappers so each is emitted once as a method. Do not :~ tiny helpers (measured: 11 small ones grew lint.rs from 2,056 to 2,103), and check the output has fn definitions, not Rc::new(move |..|) closures. Before the split planned at 07:40, try that on the largest_fn draft: wrappers for the scan's substring/index_of/trim calls, regen kit_crate_gate, compare against 950. The split stays the fallback. The lint measurement (job kit-lint-wrappers) runs the same method first, and its result decides whether to repeat it here.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_19 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 19u32, "2026-10-27T15:58Z", "kit", "11:5x kit: largest_fn built and proven on a probe copy, staged (clean/kit-rename/crate-gate-largest plus crate-largest-member.do; next-train steps 4b and 5a). The 10-25 plan's split, done the way tonight's lint split worked: the scan (function_name, largest_from, largest_function_of; edition 3, ported from the clean/gate-largest draft) lives in a new crate, kit_crate_largest (largest.rs 255 lines), with a test block. It exports kit_crate_largest(text) -> ' largest <fn> <lines>' or '', so the gate never holds the scan or the number formatting. kit_crate_gate reads each output once, stores the rendered suffix in crate_measure.largest, and measure_line appends it with no branch. Its existing test strings are unchanged with largest='', and a new case checks the named suffix. gate.rs 944 (was 921; under the crate's 950 rule; the first cut was 964, then 954 with sm'.format in the gate). Behavior: the generated crate, run on three real outputs, names the same largest function and length as an independent PowerShell ranking: host_run.rs supervisor_requested_48 79, gate.rs gate_run_0 97, task_listing.rs row_selected_15 66. Left: the gate in every repo that emits Rust crates, and the .spi-in-count decision (item text).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_20 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 20u32, "2026-10-27T19:23Z", "kit", "kit 15:2x: every repo that emits Spiral crates is gated, in one call: i574n.github/scripts/crate-gate-all.ps1 (unstaged) runs 'kit crate gate <root> --enforce --limit 1000' over kit/src, x, spiral, tibia and fiscal, and treats the gate's own 'no crate with [package.metadata.spiral]' refusal as 'nothing to gate'. Today: kit/src 235 crates and x 28, 0 over; spiral, tibia and fiscal emit no Spiral crates. The .spi question is settled by the user's 10-21 rule (line-gate-all-languages: Spiral sources are exempt; the gate counts generated output only). Left: the largest-function suffix (staged, next-train 4b/5a) ships with TRAIN; after install the gate names each crate's largest fn and this item closes.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_21 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 21u32, "2026-10-28T09:34Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: verified on the installed TRAIN2c binary (A5B55974): 'kit crate gate kit/src --limit 1000' reports crates=242 over=0, and every row carries its largest function (for example 'largest agile_init_entry_0 149'). With crate-gate-all.ps1 gating every Spiral-crate repo, this item is done.")
inl x_repo_template () = Task ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", "i574n/x as the from-scratch Spiral repo template (create-react-app style): find the shape every new Spiral repo should start from; record each decision with its reason and measured cost", Story, P2, D3, 82u32, Blocked, "", "template,spiral,x,ci", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_repo_template () = Planning ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", Minutes 180u32, LoadUnset, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_repo_template_1 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:27Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:27 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Decisions so far: (1) spiral cloned beside the repo (eoie model), no symlinks (dice's deps/polyglot link is the older, slower model) and no SPIRAL_ROOT override, because package.spiproj fixes packageDir ../../../spiral/lib; scripts/common.ps1 resolves XRoot/SpiralRoot and fails with the init hint. (2) scripts/init.ps1 clones spiral from the same owner and runs spiral's init-app-build; scripts/build.ps1 builds every app; scripts/workflow.ps1 = init + build; one CI file .github/workflows/ci.yml (ubuntu + windows) runs workflow.ps1, nothing else standardized (yml standardization stays with the main lane). (3) Root Cargo workspace, stable Rust (generated Rust builds on cargo 1.99.0, no nightly pin), release profile lto+cg1. (4) Kino test blocks run from the sibling Kino with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER=process (no daemon left), outputs under target/. (5) .gitattributes LF + generated client.rs marked. Measured locally (spiral already beside, 16 GB box, heavy gate): full scripts/build.ps1 517 s (client: cargo cold 130 s, e2e ~60 s, 4 zig test files ~180 s; sim: mix test 30/30, corpus run ~60 s, kino executor run ~30 s); client-only rebuild 345 s. Not measured yet: init from scratch, Linux CI.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_2 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:31Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:31 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Open decision: the sibling clone tracks spiral's branch tip with no pin. Everything in x was proven against spiral's uncommitted working tree (tonight's compiler packs, other lanes' Kino edits), so CI (init.ps1 clones spiral HEAD) can differ until spiral is committed and pushed. Options: a ref in init.ps1, or a recorded validated spiral commit per x commit. Also measured: stable Rust via rust-toolchain.toml; the generated client builds on cargo 1.99.0.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_3 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 3u32, "2026-10-16T05:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-16 01:40 template shape now includes a Cargo workspace of apps/<app>/<crate>/ entries, each Cargo.toml carrying [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, a metadata-driven build loop, and scripts/gate.ps1 (report-only, -Enforce throws) for the 1000-line generated-file rule")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_4 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory of x/scripts (4.5 KB): generic as-is init.ps1 (clone spiral beside from the remote owner and domain, spiral init-app-build), gate.ps1 (1,000-line generated Rust gate over package.metadata.spiral crates) and workflow.ps1; common.ps1 is generic except the XRoot name; only build.ps1 lists the apps. The blog repo is the second consumer to prove the template on.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_5 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T05:20Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 01:20 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) User decision, supersedes the open pin question in note 2: no spiral pin. Consumer repos keep cloning spiral main for dogfooding; a red downstream run after a spiral bug is the intended signal (fix spiral, rerun). Stop suggesting a pin unless a concrete new friction outweighs dogfooding.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_6 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T06:41Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 02:41 closed, not open: the template clones spiral main with no pin (user 2026-10-18, memory slide-with-spiral-main). A spiral regression turning x red is the dogfooding signal: fix spiral and rerun x CI. The 2026-10-15 23:31 'open decision' note is superseded")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_7 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 measured since note 1: Linux CI runs (ci.yml: xc-windows 1.4-2.1 min, build ubuntu-24.04 7.8 min, build windows-2025 11.4 min on the green run 39075003305); CI pins OTP 27 / Elixir 1.18 while the box runs OTP 28. Still unmeasured: init.ps1 from an empty folder")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_8 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:47Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:47 CI toolchain bumped to the latest releases in x, spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, blog, polyglot, i574n.github, tapas2, vault: OTP 27 -> 29 (29.1.2, 2026-10-14), Elixir 1.18 -> 1.20 (1.20.4 supports OTP 27-29), Gleam -> 1.19.2, rebar3 3.24.0 -> 3.27.3, setup-beam@v1 -> v1.24.1 where floating. Checked locally on official Elixir 1.20.4/OTP 29: x/apps/sim compiles with --warnings-as-errors, 30/30; Cells had 4 new 1.20 type-checker warnings (3 unpinned size() variables in domain.ex, 1 unreachable warm/1 clause), fixed; Cells still builds on 1.19 and the sim drift guard plus a real-Cells run pass. fc1943s backups, mechahaze and tibia untouched")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_9 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 9u32, "2026-10-18T18:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:17 template gotcha: .github/workflows/actions.lock (gh actions-lock) is enforced by GitHub; a commit that adds an action to a workflow without regenerating the lock fails at startup in 0 s with no log (x run 39140732842: the gh-pages publish job's download-artifact and actions-gh-pages were missing from the committed lock). The template should regenerate the lock in the same commit as any workflow change")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_10 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T00:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:17 init from an empty folder, measured on clean CI runners instead of locally (run 39137498346, the last green ci run): ubuntu-24.04 job 8.4 min, of which scripts/workflow.ps1 (init.ps1 clones spiral beside + init-app-build, then the whole build: client crates, cargo, e2e, sim) 7.6 min; windows-2025 job 19.1 min: moonrepo/setup-rust 6.5 min (toolchain install dominates), setup-beam 0.6, workflow.ps1 11.5; xc-windows (xc only) 1.4 min. Locally the same takes 30-60 min on this shared machine, so CI is the reference for template cost. Left: fold the gh-pages publish job and the actions.lock rule into the template, and the yml standardization the main lane owns")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_11 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 11u32, "2026-10-20T03:51Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 23:51 CI red on x 07b119c (both build jobs): apps/sim/build.ps1 fixture guard, sim-small.world.json stale since the prelude grew (cut, delimited, digit helpers; p2 6 -> 9 posts). Fixed uncommitted: build.ps1 -Accept regenerated the 4 sim worlds/expected, prelude.events.jsonl and prelude.final.json (sim-stress and thread-oauth1 unchanged), e2e -Accept rewrote the one README walkthrough output (xc thread 3000001); sim build green incl. xsim.run invariants and thread_run, client e2e 31+5. Template lesson: any edit to a client .spi that a sim world is built from needs apps/sim/build.ps1 before the commit")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_12 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 12u32, "2026-10-20T06:34Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 02:34 actions.lock rule is now a check in the template, not just a note: scripts/actions-lock.ps1 compares every .github/workflows/*.yml uses: (skipping ./ and docker://) with the lock's per-workflow list and throws with missing/stale entries and the fix (gh actions-lock, commit with the workflow change); scripts/build.ps1 runs it first, so a local build catches the 0 s startup failure before a push. Proven both ways: real repo passes (8 actions), a bumped setup-python fails naming missing v6.4.0 and stale v6.3.0. Also e2e.ps1 steps now set ProgressPreference SilentlyContinue: windows-2025 serialized a progress record as #< CLIXML into a step's stderr (run 39367603402, xc refresh step)")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_13 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 13u32, "2026-10-20T23:06Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 19:06 template lesson: apps/sim/build.ps1 ran the byte-exact ExUnit suite before its fixture guard, so a client source edit that a sim world is built from (posts.spi -> sim-stress) could not be accepted through build.ps1 -Accept (ExUnit failed first and the accept step never ran). mix test now runs after the guard: without -Accept the guard fails first with 'rerun apps/sim/build.ps1 -Accept and review the diff', with -Accept the fixtures refresh and ExUnit then checks them byte for byte (x-chain23 green both ways)")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_14 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 14u32, "2026-10-21T03:15Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 23:15 pre-commit proof (eoie job x-precommit, 22:01-23:14): CI's own scripts/build.ps1 (no -fast) over the uncommitted tree exits 0 locally: actions-lock 8 actions locked, crate gate 16 crates 0 over 1000, x-compat 0 in 36 files, postable 1617 posts with 0 cells over one post, e2e tutorial and extra cases incl. delete-thread 34, Cells 35 of 35 notebook files with 0 failures, sim ExUnit 43/43 after the fixture guard, corpus commute on both schedules, real-Cells executor run, thread_run accepts the thread and rejects a tampered one. CI is red on HEAD 61b8fff only from the windows-2025 CLIXML e2e failure (run 39367603402) that the uncommitted e2e.ps1 ProgressPreference fix addresses, so the user's next commit of this tree is expected to turn ci green; D1 needs two consecutive green runs on main")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_15 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 15u32, "2026-10-21T21:22Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:22 x: grooming. Status Active -> Blocked so the stop gate's ready list stops offering it: x's part is verified (CI's own scripts/build.ps1 green locally on the uncommitted tree; 10-21 x-split-fix3 green after the post_id slice and the lib Gleam fixes). The remainder is the user's commit and push plus two consecutive green ci runs on main (X-DONE D1), which no agent can do. Unblock when the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_16 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T03:48Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:48 x: template pieces added tonight (all in x, uncommitted). scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <dll> regenerates every crate with a candidate compiler and reports identical/DIFFERS before an install. The gate also measures every non-Spiral file against 1,000 lines with a named data allowlist (report-only until the generated Gleam is split). apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder runs scale ladders. If tonight's sim check passes, scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 splits generated Gleam into <=1,000-line modules. Consumers of the template inherit all of them; the publish step should move to git checkout-index per TREE-PAGES-V2 note 1.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_17 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T12:22Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:22 x: the template's shape now lives in x itself, not only in these notes: README section 'Starting another Spiral repo from x' (4 bullets, checked against the files). (1) scripts/ as is, only build.ps1 names the apps; init.ps1 clones spiral beside from the remote's owner, unpinned (note 5). (2) ci.yml + actions.lock, with gh actions-lock in the same commit (notes 9, 12). (3) Root Cargo.toml workspace, rust-toolchain.toml, .cargo/config.toml, .gitattributes, .gitignore. (4) package.spiproj packageDir ../../../spiral/lib, and per-crate Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output read by apps/client/build.ps1's loop. Correction to note 1: the toolchain is pinned to nightly-2026-10-18 now, not stable. The blog repo (note 4) remains the second consumer that proves it.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_18 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 18u32, "2026-10-23T00:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:26 x: grooming check before the commit. Every file the README's 'Starting another Spiral repo from x' section names exists in the tree (scripts/init.ps1 and build.ps1, ci.yml + actions.lock, Cargo.toml, rust-toolchain.toml, .cargo/config.toml, .gitattributes, .gitignore, apps/client/package.spiproj with packageDir ../../../spiral/lib). The Rust pin agrees in all three places, so a repo made from the template inherits one nightly: rust-toolchain.toml channel, ci.yml's two setup-rust channels, and Cells' own +nightly-2026-10-18. Note 23:48's publish point is already met: spiral's publish-tree.ps1 copies with git checkout-index. Status stays Blocked on the user's commit and two green CI runs (X-DONE D1).")
inl x_client_token_refresh () = Task ("X-CLIENT-TOKEN-REFRESH", "xc refresh ENV_FILE: trade X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN for a new access and refresh token at POST /2/oauth2/token (confidential client Basic auth) and rewrite X_OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN/X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN in the env file in place without printing them", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,auth", "current", "current")
inl backend_switch_globals_leak () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", "BackendSwitch validation is not hermetic for globals (found 23:58 2026-10-15 while migrating lib is_zig guards): evaluating a non-selected arm for its type runs global calls it reaches, so their text lands in the selected backend's output", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,backend-switch,both-cores", "current", "current")
inl rust_union_impls () = Task ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", "Rust backend emits an enum plus 'impl tag' and 'impl Default' (about 18 lines) for every union instantiation, and one array-collect loop per element type: x/apps/client/view/view.rs (1786 lines) spends 302 on 17 enum impls and 182 on 8 from_* loops; repro: any crate using list post, list link, list medium, option x -- emit Default/tag only when used, or generically", Story, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,rust,code-shape", "current", "current")
inl rust_match_duplication () = Task ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", "Pattern-match lowering duplicates arm bodies on every backend (core peval, not codegenRust): or-patterns and wildcard fallbacks reach one shared continuation through the prepass EPatternMemo node, which peval evaluates once per path, so the body is emitted per alternative (or as a nested-match staircase); plus aliasing specialization (SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION) and Rust-only flattened-record moves through recursion (x posts.merge_newest 107 lines); measured in x/apps/client args (1243 before workarounds) and view", Story, P1, D4, 35u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,code-shape,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_match_duplication () = Planning ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_1 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement (6-line repeated windows, variable numbers normalized): x view.rs 26 percent of 1,884 lines, top window x16 = runs of Rc str clone moves (flattened record fields moved one by one), fetch.rs 18, args.rs 11. The text metric also counts program shape (a mega sample scores 98 from unrolled straight-line code), so the next measure is by AST: clone-move runs and duplicated arm bodies.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_2 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:47Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:47 helper-lane lap 1: first AST-level measurement, and it moves the item: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (union of 3 payload cases + Dot, one or-pattern arm | Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7i32 * x - 3i32, inside a let function) compiled with the installed compiler (Invoke-Heavy, try.ps1) emits the arm body 3 times on every backend checked: C (v3/v8/v13 = 7l * v...), Zig (@as(i32, 7) *% ...) and Rust (7i32.wrapping_mul ...), all exit 41. So the or-pattern copy is made by partial evaluation, shared by all backends, not by codegenRust; the fix belongs in the core's or-pattern lowering (bind the matched payload to one variable and emit the body once, or wrap it in a join point), and its gain shows on C/Zig too. The other shapes in the title (_ staircases, aliasing specialization, flattened-record moves) still need their own probes.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_3 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:32 helper-lane lap 2: second shape probed (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/wildcard_pair.spi: match a, b with | Red, Red | Green, Green | _ => 7 * 3 + 1, inside a let function; C and Rust exit 224): the wildcard arm becomes a staircase of nested matches with the fallback body written 3 times (once under each first-scrutinee case plus the outer _), on both C and Rust - here the body folded to 22, but a non-constant body is copied the same way, so like the or-pattern this is partial evaluation's multi-scrutinee lowering, not codegenRust. Same probe also shows the aliasing duplication from SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION: pair_score a b and pair_score b b produce two functions, pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v0), because the second call passes one variable twice. So 3 of the title's 4 shapes are core (peval) behaviours shared by every backend; only the flattened-record moves are Rust-specific.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_4 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:14 helper-lane lap 3: retitled to the core cause and located it. In the pattern prepass (spiral_compiler.fs, pattern compile around 53005-53040) PatOr(a, b) builds cp id a on_succ (cp id b on_succ on_fail) with on_succ wrapped as EPatternMemo, and every clause's failure path chains into the next, ending in EPatternMiss; EPatternMemo is only a memo of the prepassed term (Utils.memoize at 52386/52525/52706) and is gone before partial evaluation (157548/159834 fail if one survives), so peval sees the same E reached from several control paths and inlines it on each - hence one copy of the arm per or-alternative, and the wildcard staircase where the fallback is reached from under each first-scrutinee case. Fix direction (the standard ML or-pattern lowering): when a memoized continuation is reachable from more than one path and is not trivially small, lower it to a local join point whose parameters are the pattern's bound variables, and have each path call it; that turns N copies into one body plus N calls on every backend. Related: SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION covers the aliasing shape.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_5 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: pinned the elimination point and the tool for the fix. The shared continuation dies in the prepass rewrite at spiral_compiler.fs 52706 (EPatternMemo x -> Utils.memoize dict f x: every reference gets the same rewritten E object, so peval later walks it once per path), with the free-variable pass doing the same at 52386. The prepass already has the node the fix needs: EJoinPoint of Range * E * TPrepass option * backend option * name option (51957), whose body becomes one method that every call site invokes with its free variables (the pattern-bound variables included) as arguments. Sketch: at 52706, when the memo is hit for the second time and the continuation is not trivially small (a literal, a variable, or a short op), emit it once as a local EJoinPoint bound by an ELet and replace each reference by an application of that binding; the first visit cannot know yet, so the rewrite needs a reference count of EPatternMemo nodes first (one cheap prior walk over the clause tree). Cost is one call per path instead of one body copy per path, on every backend. Next: a lab patch with the count + join-point lowering, probed on the or-pattern and wildcard samples via a Debug candidate.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_6 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:48 helper-lane: correction to the lap-4 sketch and a concrete first patch. Lowering at 52706 cannot add join points: lower (52580) turns each EJoinPoint into EJoinPoint' using scope.[x], a free-variable table computed earlier per node, so a node created during lowering has no scope entry. The join point must exist before that pass, i.e. at pattern compilation. Staged lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py (anchor --check OK, shared prepass so both cores): PatOr now memoizes EJoinPoint(range, on_succ, None, None, None) instead of the bare continuation, unless the continuation is a literal, unit, variable or symbol; both alternatives then call one join-point method whose arguments are the pattern-bound variables. Scope: or-patterns only (the wildcard/next-clause chain stays as is for now, it touches every multi-clause match). run.ps1 compiles or_pattern.spi before and after on C and Rust and counts the arm body's copies (expect exit 41, 3 copies -> 1). HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_7 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:27 helper-lane: pack 8 bisect (lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/pack8-bisect): or_join_patch.py alone breaks samples/frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any on every backend (TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type. Got: %d, i32), while the positional-rename and Assign candidates compile both. lib's format code uses type-annotated or-patterns in real code (sm'_real.spir 660-689: | (_ : f32) | (_ : f64) => ...), and the prepass does not distinguish real code (RawReal(r,a) -> f a, 53272), so the join-point wrapping also hit compile-time, type-directed continuations. Dropped from pack 8. Redesign options: skip the wrapping when an alternative carries a type annotation or type test (the continuation is type-specialized anyway), or lower only in runtime code by threading a real flag through the prepass; whichever is chosen must pass the format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep before it rides a pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_8 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:35Z", "main", "03:35 main: v2 of the or-pattern join point drafted (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch_v2.py; dry run by default; refuses if already applied). It applies the 10-19 14:27 redesign: wrap the shared continuation in EJoinPoint only when NEITHER alternative is type-directed. typeDirected walks the pattern and is true for PatAnnot, PatExists, PatFilledDefaultValue, PatDefaultValue and PatNominal (all compile-time type tests), recursing through Dyn/Unbox/When/Pair/Or/And/record members/arrays. So sm'_real.spir's '| (_ : f32) | (_ : f64)' keeps today's lowering, the case that broke frontier_format_any_union in pack 8. Trivial continuations (ELit, EB, EV, ESymbol) stay unwrapped as in v1. Checked without a build: every constructor and arity exists (E.EJoinPoint has 5 fields at core 52023; PatDefaultValue and PatRecordMembers* match the raw Pattern at 43910-43930), and the anchor matches once on both the tree core and the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core. Gate (its own pack, not PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, because it changes peval for every or-pattern): Debug candidate; run.ps1 counts or_pattern.spi's arm body (3 copies -> 1 on C and Rust, exit 41); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; then a lib Cells sweep and -Suite all with DISAGREE 0, where only residuals with or-patterns change.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_9 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 9u32, "2026-10-22T13:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:38 main: design notes for the clause-chain half (after v2 lands in PACK-NEXT-2), read from the tree core, no build. The clause chain is lowered at 53141/53144: List.mapFoldBack loop clauses (EPatternMiss (EV id)), where each clause's on_fail is EPatternMemo on_fail (the remaining clauses). The memo shares the E node in the prepass, but peval evaluates it once per failure path, which produces the nested-match staircase. A v3 would wrap that on_fail as v2 does for or-patterns: EJoinPoint (p r, on_fail, None, None, None) when on_fail is non-trivial (not EPatternMiss/ELit/EV), skipped when any remaining clause is type-directed (the same typeDirected walk). Better than the heuristic would be an explicit real-code flag: PartEvalPrepassEnv (52933) has only term and ty, and RawReal(r,a) -> f a (53350) drops the information. A field set by RawReal would let both v2 and v3 skip join points in compile-time code, but whole .spir modules (sm'_real.spir) may not pass through RawReal, so their entry path has to be traced first. The order is fixed: v2 has to prove itself in PACK-NEXT-2 (format_any samples, lib Cells sweep, DISAGREE 0). Then v3 is measured on x/apps/client args (1,243 lines before workarounds) as its own pack, because it touches every multi-clause match.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_10 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:09 main: or-join v2 is RED in PACK-NEXT-3's review. On lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3) the candidate (core 95AF71D2FE8AE0E6) fails with FatalError: Index was outside the bounds of the array, exit 5, on C. The installed compiler compiles it and the program exits 41. v2 had only ever been anchor-checked, so this review is its first execution. Probable cause from the patch: the shared continuation is wrapped in EJoinPoint(p r, on_succ, None, None, None) inside the pattern compiler, but that body reads x, which each alternative binds on its own. A join point's captured environment is fixed where it is created, so the alternatives' different bindings index past it. Next design: the join point must take the alternatives' bound variables as parameters (bind once, call with each alternative's values), or wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable. Gate for v3: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, plus the matrix.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_11 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:20Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:20 main: correction to note 10's cause, from a code read (no build). Pattern variables come from one per-clause dictionary (cp's v x, core 53083: PatVar -> ELet(p r, v a, EV id, on_succ)), so x gets the SAME index in every alternative; the shared body does not see different bindings. A better fit for \"Index was outside the bounds of the array\": the lowering pass handles EJoinPoint(r,body,...) through scope env x (52735), and scope looks up per-node free-variable data computed by an earlier analysis (52466). v2 creates its EJoinPoint inside the pattern compiler (53130). If that analysis already ran, the new node has no scope entry, its var table is too small, and EV i -> env.term.var.[i] (52738) indexes past it. v3 options: (1) create the join point where the analysis sees it (wrap the or-pattern body before the free-variable pass, or run scope on the new node at creation); (2) reuse a construct the lowering already scopes, such as a let-bound closure called from each alternative. Next step, after the compile holds: a Debug candidate with v2 under SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=0 to get the stack, confirming 52738 before writing v3.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_12 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 12u32, "2026-10-22T21:44Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:44 main: coverage gap. A census of or-pattern arms (| A | B => with an inner | and no ||; scratch or_pattern_census.py) finds 0 of 1,240 sample .spi files with an or-pattern arm, and 10 of 212 lib/spiral files, 2 of them binding variables. So the -Suite all matrix could not have caught v2's crash; only the dedicated probe did. Before v3: add a regression sample with a variable-binding or-pattern (or_pattern.spi's Circle x | Square x | Triangle x, exit 41), one sample per fixed bug, and gate v3 on lib Cells gates of the 2 binding files, since those are its real users.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_13 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 13u32, "2026-10-22T21:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:52 main: v3's lib gate target, made concrete: the only variable-binding or-pattern in lib/spiral is state_core.spi:594, | Some value, _ | _, Some (Ok (Some value)) =>, which binds value in both alternatives (exactly v2's crash shape). stream.spi:249/257 are wildcard-only (StreamNil, _ | _, StreamNil), the non-binding case. v3 gates: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi green, and the new regression sample.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_14 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 14u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:57 main: regression sample added before next3b's matrix started: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives (or_pattern.spi: Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3, exits 41 from Square 4), the matrix's first variable-binding or-pattern. On next3b (no or-join) it is a plain new row expected to agree with exit 41; for v3 it is the guard that would have caught v2.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_15 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 15u32, "2026-10-22T22:08Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:08 main: v3 option (2) ruled out by a code read. \"Wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable\" cannot be decided where v2 inserts the join point: at cp time the arm body is still an EPatternRef that the clause loop fills only after every pattern is compiled (core 53141-53152: \"I need to deal with the patterns first before replacing the strings in the body\"). Any v3 must therefore create the shared join point after the refs are filled (a post-pass over the lowered clause), or teach propagate/lower to scope a join point created inside cp. Both start from the Debug stack trace (next-session step 4).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_16 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 16u32, "2026-10-23T13:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:45 main: v3 design from a code read (no build), replacing both v2 placements. Facts: in compile_pattern (core ~53054-53153) the arm body is an EPatternRef that is filled only after every clause is compiled. A join point created inside cp has no scope entry from the earlier free-variable analysis, so v2's crash came from creating EJoinPoint there. Every pass downstream, type inference included, already handles join points that come from the RAW tree: let functions become RawJoinPoint through join_point and adjust_join_point (core ~44497-44527), and 'let x = e in b' is RawMatch(r, e, [PatVar x, b]), which pattern_match turns into ELet. v3 is therefore a raw rewrite before type inference. A clause | P1 | P2 => body whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, whose alternatives bind the same variable set V and are not type-directed (v2's typeDirected walk: PatAnnot, PatExists, PatNominal, PatDefaultValue, PatFilledDefaultValue), whose body is not trivial (a literal, a variable or a symbol), and which sits outside real code becomes: let k = fun V => join body in match s with | P1 => k V | P2 => k V | other clauses. k is a fresh uninterned name like ' arg' in adjust_join_point, and V is a tuple when there are several variables. Type inference sees an ordinary let-bound join point, so scoping, free variables and codegen need no change, and each backend emits one body plus N calls. The rewrite belongs where the parser builds RawMatch (or one raw-tree pass after parsing that also gets the RawReal flag). Gates as before: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one body copy, frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi, then -Suite all with DISAGREE 0. The clause-chain (wildcard staircase) half is the same rewrite applied to the remaining-clauses continuation, as a later pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_17 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 17u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:49 main: v3 implemented as %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\or-pattern-join-v3.ps1 and composed alone into pack-next8 (core 19B0B1D5F8629846 on next7 9CC2E91F). Two anchors: module OrPatternJoin before inl_or_let_process, and the 'match ... with' parser calling OrPatternJoin.lift instead of RawMatch. It parses under both defines with pair-drift clean. Shape: each clause whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, not type-directed, with a non-trivial body becomes 'let <uninterned ' or N'> = fun V => join body' around the match, and the clause body becomes the call. V is the first alternative's bound variables, which equal the second's (DisjointOrPatternVar enforces it), with unit for none and a pair chain for several. It is deliberately not the let-join form: let would PatDyn the arguments and stop static folding, while a plain fun whose body is a RawJoinPoint keeps static arguments specializing, and a dynamic scrutinee gives one body. The uninterned name can't shadow a user variable. Known risk for the gate: the rewrite runs at parse time without a real-code flag, so an unannotated or-pattern inside real code (.spir) would get a join point. The format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep (sm'_real.spir) are the check, and if they fail, the next step is the RawReal flag of note 9. Review rule (pack-next8, after next7): only outputs of samples containing an or-pattern arm may change; or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one arm-body copy on C and Rust; frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi pass; DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_18 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 18u32, "2026-10-23T14:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:16 main: pack-next8 now also carries the inert hopacsota census (HOPACSOTA-P3), so its core is BF7EE0AD76C18036. The census is off without its environment variable, so the review rule for or-pattern v3 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_19 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 19u32, "2026-10-24T06:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:3x main: or-pattern join v3 reaches its first full gate. It came into pack 5g through the next8 base (5g = next9 stack + my fixes, core 1A297706E577BB03), so review-4 is its review. Its rule's parts the matrix cannot check are wired into review-4: %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\orjoin-check.ps1 runs with the reviewed candidate DLL (SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, which try.ps1 and Cells' toolchain.ex both honor), compiling lab or_pattern.spi to C and Rust and counting arm-body copies (rule: exit 41, 1 copy), then lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi in process mode (lib's only or-pattern users). The matrix covers or_pattern_bound_alternatives and frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any. review-4 is in the requeue manifest for tibia's block end.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_20 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 20u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 11:48 (subagent): or-pattern join-point patch at pattern compile (lowering cannot add join points: scope table) + run.ps1 queued -> next: read the candidate run (3 copies -> 1) | lap 6 14:27 (subagent): bisect: or_join_patch is pack 8's regression (format_any samples; real-code type-annotated or-patterns); dropped from the pack -> next: redesign: skip annotated alternatives or thread a real flag; prove on format_any + lib sweep | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: or_join_patch dropped (format_any regression) -> next: redesign without type-annotated or-patterns")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_21 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 helper patch record folded from the deleted history folder: patch lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py, state 'candidate queued (run.ps1)', proof: or_pattern body 3 copies -> 1")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_22 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 22u32, "2026-10-27T16:43Z", "main", "10-27 12:43 main: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION drained to its one live probe (untracked, so no git change). Archived by scratch-archive.ps1 (kit archive 20261027-124241, blob verify 0 failures; restore with -Restore that manifest.tsv) and removed: or_join_patch.py (v1, dropped from pack 8 on 10-19 for the format_any regression), or_join_patch_v2.py and run.ps1 (v1's candidate runner). The v3 design superseded both patches and landed as OrPatternJoin in the core through pack 5g: spiral_compiler.fs:44537 typeDirected, 44546 trivial, 44551 lifted, which lift a non-trivial, non-type-directed or-pattern body into a function of its bound variables. The patches' anchor line no longer exists in the core, so they can't apply anyway. Also removed: or_pattern.spi, byte-identical (271 B, over the census's 200-byte floor) to the landed regression sample samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives/main.spi. By-stem search (RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, or_join_patch, lab\\or_pattern) over 971 scripts in the spiral, compiler-tmp, packs and i574n.github script folders and TEMP to depth 1: the only references are spent pack scripts (pack-next2/3 compose.ps1, next3/next3b review.ps1, next5g orjoin-check.ps1, all superseded or installed; current packs are 5n and 5o) and two old backlog exports. The positive control hit inside the folder. Kept: wildcard_pair.spi, the item's open case (a wildcard arm after two pair arms on a 3-case union), not yet measured. Spiral lab folder count is unchanged at 49.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_23 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 23u32, "2026-10-27T16:50Z", "main", "10-27 12:50 main: Wildcard fallthrough duplication MEASURED (node job-1793119666554, compiler 4FD712E9, which already carries OrPatternJoin v3). Probe: lab wildcard_pair.spi, a match a, b over a 3-case union with arms Red, Red / Green, Green / _. As authored, its wildcard body is static (7 * 3 + 1) and folds to a literal, so it hides the copies. A dynamic twin (TEMP rmd-wildcard/wildcard_pair_dyn.spi, body k * 3 + 1 with k runtime) shows them: the body is emitted 3 times on BOTH backends. C: 'v2 * 3l' at lines 45, 59, 67 of pair_score. Rust: 'v2.wrapping_mul(3i32)' plus wrapping_add at lines 18/19, 30/31, 37/38. Cause, read from the output's shape: the pair match is lowered as a nested match on a, then on b, and the fallthrough continuation is copied into every failed inner branch (Green then not-Green, Red then not-Red) and into the outer default (Amber). OrPatternJoin does not apply because no clause is a PatOr. Design pointer for the core (compiler lane authors it): the same raw-level lift that OrPatternJoin does, applied to a clause that more than one failed path reaches. When compile_pattern would place a non-trivial, non-type-directed clause body on two or more failure paths, bind it once as 'let <uninterned> = fun V => join body' (V = the clause's bound variables; unit for a bare _), and make each path a call. The same trivial() and typeDirected() guards apply, so static bodies still fold. Regression sample when it lands: the dyn twin, rule 1 copy on C and Rust and the same exit (pair_score Red Green 7 + pair_score Green Green 7 = 22 + 202 = 224, exit 224 on C after the 255 mask, 224 on Rust). The measurement scripts and both outputs are in TEMP rmd-wildcard (node-measure.ps1, out/).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_24 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 24u32, "2026-10-27T17:06Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-27: wildcard/clause-chain half design v1 (build-independent; from reading cp/compile_pattern 53102-53191 + OrPatternJoin 44525-44574). MECHANISM confirmed: cp threads the SAME on_fail into every sub-test fail edge (EPairTest 53144 -> both a- and b- fail to on_fail), each clause's on_fail = the next clause wrapped in EPatternMemo (53188); EPatternMemo is eliminated before peval (158180) so peval re-inlines the fallthrough at every decision-tree leaf reaching it. A fully-discriminating clause lands at 1 leaf (body once); a NON-discriminating clause (catch-all / wildcard in a branched position) lands at >=2 leaves -> body copied (n23's `_` 3x). FIX = the SAME OrPatternJoin lift (raw rewrite, `let k = fun V => join body` + call, reusing boundVars/typeDirected/trivial/parameters), keyed on the clause BODY's multi-reachability instead of a PatOr pattern; peval then copies only the call, static scrutinees still fold. TARGETING (my call, decide-autonomously; objection welcome): rule (a) conservative syntactic -- only multi-scrutinee (tuple/PatPair) matches, non-first clause, non-trivial non-type-directed body, pattern non-discriminating at a branched position (bare catch-all or a wildcard tuple component). Sound (under-targeting only leaves some dup, never changes behavior); avoids the whole-match churn of lifting all bodies (keeps your \"only affected samples change\" review rule). Exact boundary validated on the build gate. GATE: dyn twin (TEMP rmd-wildcard, exit 224, 1 copy C+Rust) landed as a sample; frontier_format_any_union/_join compile; lib_gate state_core.spi + stream.spi (:249/257 wildcard-pair users); -Suite all DISAGREE 0, only wildcard-fallthrough residuals change. Same real-code risk as or-join (parse-time, no RawReal flag; typeDirected + format_any/lib sweeps are the check). Lands by extending OrPatternJoin (lifted gains a clause-body branch) at the same parser site; re-anchor only if that section moves (5n/5o don't). Build-bound; sequenced AFTER COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO. Full design: compiler scratchpad note-rust-match-dup-wildcard-design.txt.")
