inl tibia_start_sync () = Task ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", "Live layer: start every competitor's logic at the same moment after login (the policy probe acts ~2.5 s after login, bots at once); needed before the next win-seeking comparison.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,harness,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl tibia_tools_to_eoie () = Task ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", "Move tibia-local helper scripts onto the generic eoie verbs once they exist: pr1/pr2 analyses to EOIE-EXPERIMENTS, log and TSV questions to EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, live layer and standards checks to EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS; BENCHMARK pre-registrations become experiment records (with TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE).", TaskKind, P2, D3, 70u32, Blocked, "EOIE-EXPERIMENTS,EOIE-TABLE-QUERY,EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS,EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES", "tibia,eoie,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie () = Planning ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:58 dependency EOIE-TABLE-QUERY is Done (table query/diff landed 2026-10-18 15:53), but the installed C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe is from 2026-10-17 16:46 and answers 'unknown verb: table'. First migrations ready once it is installed: per-arm counts and means over results/arena-results-*.tsv (today Import-Csv | Group-Object in run-arena summaries and lab compare scripts), and the zig-oracle compare.ps1 join (table diff --key file). Log-corpus questions (shield-split.ps1, adjacency-summary.ps1, pc3-analysis.ps1) still need the regex-to-columns shape noted on EOIE-TABLE-QUERY 2026-10-17.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 2u32, "2026-10-20T05:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:04 installed eoie.exe (2026-10-19 21:07) has table query/diff. Tried both on tibia data: (1) table diff --key file on lab/tibia-zig-oracle/results-{c,zig}.tsv cross-multiplies repeated keys of these append-only histories (compare.ps1 keeps the latest row per key and adds a zig/c ratio); (2) table query --group gives counts only, while the per-arm arena summaries need mean/sd per group. Both sent to the eoie lane as concrete gaps (--latest-by/--last, --mean/--sd). Scripts stay until they land.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 3u32, "2026-10-20T15:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:17 installed eoie (10:52) has table --mean/--sd/--sum and diff --latest-by. Verified on PR-12: per-arm potions mean/sd identical to pr1-analysis; means skip empty cells (cleared-only clear time 90.3 s matches arena-summary.ps1). Remaining gaps sent to the eoie lane: per-group --min/--max (lowest_health), non-empty count per column (cleared n/N), and 'eoie help table' (unknown help target). arena-summary.ps1 migrates when they land; its log half (damage from 'You lose N' lines) still needs the regex-to-columns shape. Side bug seen: arena-summary counts logs by date only, so -Since on a shared log dir pulls in other blocks' logs (45 retreat logs for 40 PR-12 rows).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 4u32, "2026-10-20T20:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 16:04 live/arena-summary.ps1 migrated: its TSV half is now one call, 'eoie table query <tsv> --group competitor --count cleared_ms --mean cleared_ms,potions --sd cleared_ms,potions --min lowest_health --sum deaths' (r20), reproducing the old table on PR-12 (cleared 39/40 vs 40/40, lowest 52/10, potions 12.75/13.925). The log half (damage from 'You lose N' lines, filtered by time) stays in PowerShell until a regex-to-columns verb exists. Also replaced earlier: zig-oracle compare.ps1 (table diff --latest-by). Remaining candidates: pr1-analysis.ps1 / pr15-analysis.ps1 onto 'eoie experiment' (r20 slice 1: welch, one-sample) once log-derived columns can feed it; pc3/pc4 analyses wait on median/grid tests (r21).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 5u32, "2026-10-20T20:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 16:05 eoie experiment (r20 slice 1) validated against tibia's own analysis in a scratch ledger (replay, data predates registration, so not a real ledger entry): PR-12 Welch via 'experiment analyze' gives difference -1.175, t -1.8786, df 77.65, CI -2.420..0.070, p 0.064, the same as live/pr1-analysis.ps1; the interim-look guard refused a filtered table with one arm short (nexbot rows leave foreign_actions empty, so '--where foreign_actions=0' drops them: a tibia data quirk, not a tool bug), and a second analysis is refused. pr1-analysis.ps1 can retire once tibia's next Welch experiment is registered in a project ledger before its data.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 6u32, "2026-10-20T22:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 18:53 pr15-analysis migration waits on eoie release 21 (table logs --count-by): r21, r21b and r21c did not publish today (tests). Recipe for when it lands: 'eoie table logs <arena-logs> --glob *hunting-ground-[0-9]*.log --count-by kills=''Loot of a (\\w+)'' --count potions=''Using one of \\d+ health potions'' --out pr15.tsv', then table query for means/sd (PR-15 itself predates any ledger registration, so experiment analyze is for the next pre-registered run).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T00:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 20:35 live/pr15-analysis.ps1 migrated onto eoie r22: one 'eoie table logs <logs> --count kills=... --count-by kind=''Loot of a (\\w+)'' --count potions=... --count deaths=...' call plus 'table query --mean/--sd' replace its per-log PowerShell counting; it reproduces the registered PR-15 numbers exactly (kills 12.80 sd 1.87 t 0.71; potions 12.70 sd 1.64 t -4.35; cyclops 4.70, trolls 8.10). The one-sample t against a stored prediction stays a two-line computation because PR-15 predates any ledger registration; the next pre-registered experiment goes into 'eoie experiment' directly. Left: pr12-secondary (time-window split, eoie --since not built), pc3/pc4 analyses (median now in r22, grid share not yet).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 02:27 inventory of live/*.ps1 helpers still doing table work in PowerShell: pr1-analysis (Welch + damage from logs), pr2-analysis (one-sample), pr12-secondary (per-log hits before/after a start marker), arena-summary (log half). Overnight analyses used eoie table logs/query and experiment analyze only (PR-16 end to end inside live/pr16-block.ps1). eoie gaps sent: table logs --field matches the file path, not lines, so --last name=regex is queued for r26; replay/stateless test verb for pre-ledger experiments (PR-1, PR-2); --values/--since stay EOIE-TABLE-LOGS-VALUES. pr12-secondary needs a per-file time window relative to a marker line (--since <marker regex>), which belongs to that item.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T09:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 05:41 lab/tibia-event-time/d16-score.ps1 takes its moments (count, mean, sd) from 'eoie table query --group ... --count/--mean/--sd' and its live step counts from 'eoie table logs --count'; checked on stored data (live steps n 60 mean 11.68; model arms 14.63 / 13.74). The t statistics stay in PowerShell until 'eoie table test' (EOIE-TABLE-TEST, r28). Papercuts sent to eoie: ungrouped moments need a constant column; the status line on stdout lands in the TSV stream. Tonight's d9-score.ps1 keeps its PowerShell means as the record of how D9/D12/D14 were scored.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T11:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 07:58 Closing-pass state. Done on eoie verbs: arena-summary's table half, pr15-analysis, the PR-16/PR-17 ledger analyses (live/block.ps1), scripts/score.ps1's moments. Still PowerShell, each waiting on a named eoie gap: (1) arena-summary's log half sums damage only over logs where something hit; an exact port needs 'table query --where col>value' (only col=value exists) or a per-row filter in table logs. (2) analysis/pr1 and pr2 (methods of record) wait on 'table test' (EOIE-TABLE-TEST, r28). (3) analysis/pr12-secondary waits on 'table logs --since <marker>' (r26, not installed; build failed 10-21 night, rebuild running). Item parks at the day's end with resume condition 'r26 and r28 installed', then port (1)-(3) and verify each against the recorded BENCHMARK numbers.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T12:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 08:08 Resume conditions as eoie items: EOIE-TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE (release 29, after a table_query crate split) for arena-summary's log half; EOIE-TABLE-TEST (r28) for analysis/pr1 and pr2; table logs --since (r26e, in test) for analysis/pr12-secondary.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 12u32, "2026-10-21T19:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 15:43 r28+r29 installed (15:38): ports done and pending. DONE: live/arena-summary.ps1's damage half is now 'eoie table logs --field competitor=... --sum damage=...' plus 'table query --where damage>0 --group competitor --count/--mean/--sd' (TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE). It reproduces the old PowerShell exactly on the logs since 2026-10-20: retreat 2176 +- 394 (75), policy 2411 +- 464 (40), nexbot 2358 +- 437 (30). VERIFIED, NOT YET SWAPPED: 'eoie table test' matches analysis/pr1-analysis.ps1 on PR-12 (Welch t -1.878613, df 77.65, CI -2.420..0.070) and pr2-analysis.ps1 on PR-7 (one-sample t 1.95, CI 4.61..6.29); it skips empty cells, so clear time over cleared runs needs no filter. The swap waits on one gap sent to eoie just before this note: table test / experiment analyze take only '--where col=value', and the foreign-activity exclusion needs 'foreign_potions<=0' (empty counts as 0, as table query already does). Also sent: table test's n counts rows rather than the values used; table logs rejects --quiet. KEPT AS METHOD OF RECORD: analysis/pr12-secondary.ps1. Its 10 s window is measured from each log's own start-marker @t, which --since cannot express; a historical one-off, low priority. NEXT after the live block: scripts/score.ps1 drops its constant-column trick (ungrouped measures now give one whole-table row), checked by reproducing the D18/D19 scores.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 13u32, "2026-10-21T23:04Z", "eoie-lane", "19:04 eoie team: all three gaps from tibia's 15:43 note are in r34 and green on a tree rebuilt from live (preview exit 0, cargo check of 35 crates, notebooks without type errors): table test / experiment analyze take the full --where comparison set (foreign_potions<=0 works, empty counts as 0 like table query), n names the numeric values used ('(k numeric)'), and table logs takes --quiet. r34 releases after main's compiler install (tonight only if that lands by 20:20, else first thing next session); then the pr1/pr2 swap can proceed on the tibia side.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T02:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:23 analysis/ is drift (user: 'what about this new analysis folder, is it drift? weird ps1 names, could keep growing indefinitely. are they running in ci?'). Findings: 8 untracked scripts, one per experiment (pr1, pr2, pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pr15, pz-in-traces, start-sync-check). None runs in CI, and six cannot, because they read the local run logs. pr1/pr2 hand-code Welch and one-sample t that 'eoie experiment analyze' already computes (their 40 cited verdicts reproduced through eoie tonight). Origin: per-experiment analysis scripts in live/ since 10-14; this morning's E4 gave them a top-level folder as 'methods of record' instead of a generic replacement (block.ps1 did that for the block scripts). Rules, written before applying: L1 delete pr1-analysis and pr2-analysis; BENCHMARK rows 98, 114, 126 and 406 cite the eoie command instead. L2 pr15-analysis already counts with 'eoie table logs/query'; its t arithmetic moves to 'eoie table test' when r34 installs, and then it is deleted. L3 pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces (concluded experiments) need per-file event-sequence logic eoie lacks; one eoie request for those shapes, and each script is deleted when its verb installs. L4 start-sync-check is a generic harness check, not an analysis, so it moves to live/. L5 growth stop: no new analysis script anywhere. A pre-registration's method is the eoie command line in its BENCHMARK test row, run by block.ps1; a missing verb is an eoie request before the experiment is registered. The folder disappears when L2-L3 complete.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T02:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:24 L1-L5 (note 14) applied. L1: analysis/pr1-analysis.ps1 and pr2-analysis.ps1 deleted. BENCHMARK rows for PR-1, PR-2, PR-3 and the PR-13 result now cite 'eoie experiment analyze target/ledger <ID> results/<table>' (and 'eoie table logs --sum' for PR-1's damage); the old scripts stay in git history as live/pr1-analysis.ps1 and live/pr2-analysis.ps1. L4: start-sync-check.ps1 moved to live/. L3: eoie asked for per-file event-sequence verbs (interval between matches with an in-between condition and a modulo grid, windowed counts after a marker, an order check, set membership, median). Resume conditions: pr15-analysis goes when 'table test' (r34) installs; pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces each go when their verb installs. README's analysis/ row states the folder only shrinks. L5 is in memory (generic-tools-not-per-experiment).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T02:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:25 eoie filed the requests: EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES (P2: --interval [--between] [--modulo], --window --from --ms, --order, --in-set, and --median in table query) and EOIE-LEDGER-ONE-FILE (P3). Neither starts before r30-r36 ship. Resume conditions for note 15: pr15-analysis goes when r34's 'table test' installs; pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces go when EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES installs; target/ledger goes when 'table test' installs, or turns into one file under EOIE-LEDGER-ONE-FILE.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T02:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:45 Commit check (user: 'are you sure can i commit these analysis files then?'): reran all five frozen scripts against the local logs; each reproduces its BENCHMARK result exactly. PC-3: 31/36 late, 2 on grid, 3 other. PC-6: 232/9/210, 935 of 940. PR-12 secondary: 6.18 vs 9.75, 55.15 vs 57.65, 40 logs each. PR-15: kills t 0.71, potions 12.70 t -4.35, 4.70/8.10, on the 10 run logs (the obvious glob also takes the smoke log: 11 runs). PC-4: median 558, 122 kept, 32 excluded, 537-566. PC-5's live half still holds (65 logs, 0 zone samples); its model half needs traces deleted with lab/ on 10-21. BENCHMARK now names PR-15's exact logs and PC-4's script. Verdict: commit them as verified, frozen methods of record (the folder only shrinks, per note 15).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T11:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 07:17 analysis/pr15-analysis.ps1 DELETED, BENCHMARK's PR-15 result now cites eoie commands (table logs --field/--count/--count-by, table test one-sample --where deaths=0, table query --mean/--sd). The resume condition said 'when r34's table test installs', on the assumption that PR-15 needs comparison filters; it only needs deaths=0, which the installed r29 table test handles. Verified before deleting: kills 12.8 t 0.708805 (record 0.71), potions 12.7 t -4.348057 (record -4.35), cyclops 4.7, trolls 8.1, sd 1.874 / 1.636, n 10 of the 10 logs (smoke log excluded): exact. Stem grep across tibia, eoie, i574n.github, spiral: only backlog history. analysis/ now holds pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces (all wait for EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 19u32, "2026-10-23T03:57Z", "tibia-lane", "Grooming 23:5x: Active -> Blocked, EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES added as a dependency (the backlog's rule for items on hold behind another item). The four analysis/ scripts left (pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces) need its sequence verbs (--order, --interval, sections); the installed table logs still lacks them (checked 22-10 19:1x). Resume when EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES ships those verbs.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 20u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 1.5 h left once eoie ships the EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES verbs (external): swap the scratch readers (read-trace, p5-eval) for eoie commands of record.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 21u32, "2026-10-23T19:24Z", "tibia-lane", "Pointer 15:2x: eoie reports table sequences (sections, --carry, --first, --last, signed times; spec cases 45 and 46) implemented and test-green. It ships in release O (after M), and eoie pings when O installs. That meets this item's resume condition once installed. First reads to move onto it: the PR-26 ground-trace split (today's hand split in scratch server-probe/pr26-runs; P6 and P7 counts in TIBIA-PARTY notes 72 and 74 as the check), then the scratch readers read-trace and read-ground, p7-judge's per-spawn table, and the four analysis/ scripts (pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 22u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:45 Rechecked against the eoie.exe installed at 11:46 today. Its help has no sequence verb under table logs, and EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES is 60 Active and EOIE-EXPERIMENTS 85 Active, so the item stays Blocked. TABLE-QUERY and STANDARDS-CHECKS are Done.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 23u32, "2026-10-28T10:32Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: stays Blocked; the named blocker is EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES (Active 60%, eoie/kit team): table sequences over logs, which the pr1/pr2 analyses need. Resume when that verb ships in an installed kit release.")
inl tibia_probes_in_spiral () = Task ("TIBIA-PROBES-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite the OTClient probes (harness/*.lua, about 2,600 lines in 21 files) as one Spiral probe library compiled with the Lua backend, as live.spi already does for the policy: typed bindings for the g_game/g_map/creature calls the probes use, one login/hide/log/observe/finish module instead of 18 copies, experiment bodies as Spiral functions. Main gain: the live probe and the simulator share decision and movement code (the 2026-10-15 retreat-step mismatch needed StepAwayLikeTheLiveProbe because the probe step was separate Lua); also dogfoods the Lua backend and macros. Target layout: apps/arena/ (like alphabet apps/documents).", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY", "tibia,harness,lua,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl tibia_map_tool_in_spiral () = Task ("TIBIA-MAP-TOOL-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite live/otbm-walkability.py (242 lines: OTBM node tree, appearances.dat protobuf varints, items.xml floorchange ids) in Spiral on the Rust target, and have it emit the terrain mask and road literals straight into src/terrain.spi instead of hand edits. Dogfoods byte buffers and file IO in lib. Target layout: apps/map/.", TaskKind, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,terrain,rust,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl spiral_macro_splice_quote () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE", "A splice right before the closing quote of a single-quoted macro swallows the delimiter: $'!b' fails with TokenizerErrors \"Expected: '\" (reported at the end of the line, or as ETY-MissingBody at the enclosing binding), because ' is a valid identifier character, so the splice reads the identifier b'. Works: $'!b ' (trailing space), $'(!b)', $\"!b\" (double-quoted), and field access $'!p.x'. lib already dodges it (base.spi: $'use !x = !x '). Fix: inside $'...', end a splice identifier at the ' that closes the macro (primed names can still be spliced in $\"...\"), or report the unterminated macro with a hint at the splice. Repros: 7 minimal Lua-backend cases (job tmp macrobugs/).", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,macros,tokenizer", "current", "current")
inl rust_global_triple_strings () = Task ("RUST-GLOBAL-TRIPLE-STRINGS", "Measure the limits of Spiral raw triple-quoted strings (multiline, no escapes) and mechanically convert escaped one-line rust_global blobs to them where output stays equivalent, so blobs diff and edit as real Rust; feeds RUST-GLOBAL-GRANULAR", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,spiral,rust-global,strings", "current", "current")
inl spiral_triple_trailing_args () = Task ("SPIRAL-TRIPLE-TRAILING-ARGS", "Parser: after a multiline triple-quoted string, any token on its closing line other than closing parens is lost (trailing argument e4c types as i32 -> (); type annotation inside a record field e8 ends the block); repros spiral/lab/rust-global-triple-strings/e4c_trailing_arg.spi and e8_record_field_annotated.spi", Story, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,compiler,parser,strings", "current", "current")
inl tibia_wgsl_shader_size () = Task ("TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", "Decide inlining vs smaller shaders by experiment: the GPU twins compile to about 3.6 MB of WGSL (one test takes 132 s on the laptop GPU, more than 34 min on Windows WARP). Build variants (all inl as today, let on the hot simulation functions, mixed) and measure WGSL size, shader compile time and run time on the real GPU, lavapipe and WARP, plus C-backend CPU speed; keep bit-exactness; the user expects inlining to be good, so the numbers decide.", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,wgsl,gpu,performance,experiment", "current", "current")
inl tibia_windows_ci_gpu () = Task ("TIBIA-WINDOWS-CI-GPU", "Windows CI has no GPU and falls back to WARP, which is far too slow for the long GPU twins (4 tests fail at the cells 300 s budget; Ubuntu passes on Mesa lavapipe). Try Mesa lavapipe on Windows (mesa-dist-win plus a Vulkan loader), locally first, then in the workflow; compare with the shader-size result (TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE).", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE", "tibia,ci,wgsl,windows", "current", "current")
inl tibia_reuse_exemplar () = Task ("TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR", "Find which tibia code is general enough to serve other projects (the next one is Dota 2): the apps/arena probe library pattern (typed host bindings over a Lua backend, a shared probe lifecycle, state carried through scheduled ticks), the pre-registration analysis scripts, the build.ps1 gate, the live/ paths split between repo and local world; extract or document each as an exemplar or a generic eoie/cells item.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,reuse,exemplar,scaffold", "current", "current")
inl cells_gleam_warm_stale () = Task ("CELLS-GLEAM-WARM-STALE", "Warm Gleam slots ran stale programs", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,gleam,ci", "current", "current")
inl sim_in_spiral () = Task ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite x/apps/sim (xsim, ~1,000 lines of Elixir: scenario, personas, frames, world, runner, invariants, x_world) in Spiral alongside Cells' port (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, kino-native-design notes): the supervision side (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, cancellable prefix compiles under a factory supervisor, demand monitors) compiles to Gleam with the Cells shell, the bulk work (scenario and delivery generation, frame splitting with X weights, invariant checks and commute over 50k-event corpora) compiles to Rust with rayon under the 1k crate gate; events, posts, frames and revisions modeled GADT-first and shared with the x client's types (posts, entries, framed) and Cells' document cells; the Elixir ExUnit suite (30 tests) and the xsim.world fixtures are the oracle; port slice by slice in the same strangler order Cells uses; scope since 2026-10-19: the supervision half only (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell); the bulk half is SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", Feature, P1, D7, 40u32, Active, "KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR,SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM,SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK,SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL,SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL,LIB-OTP-ACTORS,LIB-DICTM,SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY,LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", "sim,cells,gleam,rust,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_in_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:15 P1 -> P2: user 2026-10-18 'it'll take a lot of time to rewrite sim in spiral, wait for kino/cells'; stays tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T06:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:34 P2 -> P1 (user 2026-10-19 02:19: prioritize the rebuilds; make the domain modeling intricate, study the type feature set so everything reuses itself when sim is rewritten Spiral -> Gleam + Rust). Order changes: the shared domain comes first through X-CELLS-SPLIT, so each sim slice reuses a client module that already passes on gleam and rust under Cells instead of porting Elixir by hand. First shared module: client protocol.spi (frame header render/parse, thread_flaw union) replacing frames.ex header code and x_world gap notes later. The Elixir sim stays the independent oracle until a slice's Gleam build reproduces its fixtures (prelude.events.jsonl, sim-*.expected.json); a shared source cannot catch its own bug, so the oracle is retired per slice, never in bulk. Still tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR for the supervision side")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T06:52Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:52 reuse map, sim module -> shared Spiral (x client, X-CELLS-SPLIT): Xsim.Frames (header, split, posted_unit defuse, chunk, cut_at_word_boundary, stored/restored) = splitter.spi + glyphs.spi + protocol.spi verbatim, already backend_switched for Gleam; XWorld.gaps and the missing-frame notes = protocol thread_flaw + assemble's cell_reading; World.gathered (k, n frames per key) = protocol frame_header; Log xsim-events v1 = next shared module: union event (post, frame, edit_frame, edit, delete, like, unlike) with a codec, so the client e2e stub and the sim decode one definition; Invariants.verdict = a decision union carrying the violated rule (omniledger outcome-with-evidence); Runner/World supervision = gleam_otp (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR); Scenario and corpus generation = Rust under the 1k gate; daemon_memory and executors stay shell glue. Oracle rule: frames.ex stays until the Gleam build of splitter reproduces the sim-*.expected.json fixtures, then it is deleted in the same change")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: owner check: xsim lives in x/apps/sim and the 10-19 reuse map (note 3: Xsim.Frames = x client's splitter/glyphs/protocol, already backend_switched for Gleam) came from the x lane's side; main records only Next: next lap read the x lane's progress on X-CELLS-SPLIT")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:09 lap 2 grain: sized the port and matched the first slice. xsim lib is 1,215 lines of Elixir in 11 modules (runner 183, daemon_memory 161, x_world 160, world 154, scenario 144, frames 113, personas 91, invariants 68, executors 63, log 55, x_compat 23) plus 250 lines of mix tasks and 390 lines of ExUnit (xsim_test 325, x_compat_test 65). Xsim.Frames (header, split with defused dots and word-boundary cut, chunk/flush, frame_text, rebuild) mirrors x/apps/client/splitter.spi function for function (posted_unit, defused, space_carried, split_point, chunk, header, budget_for); X-CELLS-SPLIT note 07:06 reports splitter, glyphs, protocol, prelude and events passing Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) with UTF-8 byte units, so the frames slice can delegate to the Gleam build of splitter.spi today without new Spiral. Still blocked for the actor half: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (runner/world as gleam_otp) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM. Owner note stands: xsim lives in x/apps/sim (x lane's tree); this item records only. Next: list the xsim_test cases that exercise Frames (the oracle for slice 1) so the x lane can port them to ///> gleam test blocks")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:50 lap 3 grain: the slice-1 oracle is 8 of xsim_test.exs's 30 cases (x/apps/sim/test, read only). Pure Frames cases, portable as ///> gleam (and rust) test blocks next to splitter.spi: (1) cells split into 280-weight frames rebuild exactly, CJK and Hangul weigh double (split, frame_text, rebuild, weight); (2) frames never end in whitespace and still rebuild exactly (split, rebuild); (3) dots that could start an X link are defused with a zero-width space and restored (split, rebuild, weight); (4) a file's cells start at a column-0 line after a blank line (file_cells, thread_posts). Cases that only use Frames.explode_posts as input to the world/runner (out-of-order delivery invariants, delivery log replay, versioned JSON event stream, edits as frames) belong to slice 2+ (they need World/Runner, i.e. the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR actor half). Spiral counterparts to check before porting: weight/defused/split_point/chunk/header exist in splitter.spi; file_cells and thread_posts (cell boundaries in a file) have no splitter.spi counterpart yet, which makes case 4 the first new Spiral in slice 1. Recorded for the x lane, which owns both trees. Next: check whether x/apps/client has a cell-boundary parser (posts.spi or wire.spi) that file_cells can reuse")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:28 lap 4 grain: checked the x client for a file-to-cells splitter to back slice 1's fourth case: none. The client has the reading side only (assemble/assemble.spi groups frames per cell, orders parts, reports MissingFrames/ReplyGap; write/write.spi posts a chain of already-framed texts), and splitter.spi splits one cell into frames. So file_cells is the one new Spiral function slice 1 needs, and its contract is exact in Xsim.Frames: normalize CRLF, drop one trailing newline, empty text is no cells, else split at @cell_boundary = (?<=[^\\n])\\n\\n(?=\\n*[^ \\t\\n]) (a blank line after text, followed by a line that starts at column 0 with a non-blank character, so extra blank lines move to the start of the next cell and indented continuation lines never start a cell), then trim each cell's end; thread_posts = for each cell i, id c<i>, frames of split(id, cell) or one empty frame, stored as header + body trimmed. Placement for the x lane: next to splitter.spi as cells_of_file with ///> gleam and rust tests mirroring xsim_test's case 4. Recorded only (x lane owns both trees)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:13 lap 5 grain, CORRECTS lap 4: x/apps/client/splitter.spi already has file_cells (committed in x a3b5353, 10-19 02:49; lap 4 missed it) with the column-0 contract and test cells_start_at_column_zero, plus cell_posts/posts_of. Slice-1 oracle coverage in x today: case 4 (cells at column 0) covered; case 3 (defused dots) covered on the split side (dots_that_x_would_link_are_defused); CJK/Hangul double weight covered in glyphs.spi (plain_weight of U+65E5 and of an em dash plus zero-width space = 2); 280 cap covered (long_cells_split_between_words). Missing everywhere: the REBUILD half of cases 1-3 (split then reassemble gives the original cell, defused dots restored, no frame ends in whitespace); assemble.spi assembled/assembled_of is the reading side to pair with cell_posts. So slice 1 shrinks to one round-trip test block in x (posts_of then assembled over a text with CJK, a defusable dot and a long cell). Recorded for the x lane, which owns the tree.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:31 lap 6 grain: mapped the exact chain for slice 1's one missing round-trip test (x tree, read only): split side splitter.posts_of text (cell_posts with header c<i> k/n), reading side locate/locate.spi (header_end, frame_json h body original parent: posted text -> frame JSON) then assemble/assemble.spi assembled (frames, known) -> program {sources, flaws}, whose sources must equal file_cells text with defused dots restored. So the test is posts_of -> locate frame per post -> assembled -> sources = file_cells, over one text holding a CJK cell, a defusable x.io dot and a cell longer than 280 weight; it fits as a ///> rust test block in locate.spi (which already sees both sides) or in frames/frames.spi beside framed_json. Restoring defused dots is a reading-side step to check: assemble.spi has no defuse inverse, so either the test expects the zero-width space in sources (post text is what X shows) or a restore step is missing; the xsim oracle case 3 says restored. Recorded for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: slice 1 is mapped against the x client (file_cells exists, the round-trip test and the dot restore are open), and the work belongs to the x lane. Progress 5.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7, correcting lap 6: the dot restore does exist. locate/locate.spi:24 body_at replaces defused_dot (a zero-width space plus the dot, glyphs.spi:24) with a plain dot when it reads a frame body, so the reading side restores before assemble, as xsim case 3 expects. The round-trip test therefore needs no new function: posts_of text -> locate per post (body_at restores) -> assembled -> sources = file_cells text. That is slice 1's single missing test block, for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:24 lap 8 grain: slice 2 mapped from x/apps/sim/lib/xsim/invariants.ex (68 lines, pure, the natural Rust slice): check returns 4 or 5 named verdicts (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results, one_compile_per_prefix, withdrawn_demand_hidden, plus unwanted_results_freed when evicting) over a state of visible id->(key,result), starts/cancelled/evicted key->count, results key->_, and wanted id->key; verdict is ok or violations with the first 5 ids; commute compares two states' visible maps; breaking_edits_fail checks broken sources. Spiral shape: union verdict = Ok | Violations of list id, a named-invariant union instead of atoms, maps as sorted arrays. comparable is the only non-trivial part: two regex rewrites (spiral_cells/<n>-<n> -> spiral_cells/_ and thread name (<n>) panicked -> (_)), which a small hand scanner covers without a regex crate. Order after slice 1 (x lane)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:53 lap 9 grain: mapped xsim frames.ex against the x client: split, posted_unit (defused dot between non-space glyphs), chunk with X weights, cut_at_word_boundary, space_carried, header and digits already exist in Spiral as x/apps/client/splitter.spi (posted_unit, chunk, word_reach, space_carried, header, digit_count, budget_for, chunks_of; 201 lines with 6 test blocks). So the frames slice needs no new splitting code; what is left of frames.ex is the event-level glue: rebuild (sort frames, join, restore dots), explode_posts (events to framed posts with the limit) and thread_posts/file_cells, which build on splitter plus locate.body_at. Order stays: slice 1 round trip, slice 2 invariants, then this glue")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain, dependency reading: the deps gate the whole item on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Epic 10%, itself behind SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (82%), but only the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell) needs the Cells port; the bulk half (frames, scenario/delivery generation, invariants, compiled to Rust) needs neither, and its first slice (frames) already maps 1:1 onto the x client splitter (lap 9). So the bulk slices can start now, in the x lane (the code lives in the x tree, which helper lanes do not touch). Proposal for main: split off a SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK story with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, assigned to the x lane. Next: x lane slice 1 round-trip test (rebuild/explode_posts over splitter)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: planning filled from the description's own sizing: about 1,000 lines of Elixir in 7 modules ported slice by slice with the 30-test ExUnit oracle, two compile targets (Gleam supervision, Rust bulk under the 1k crate gate): estimate 1800m (roughly 250 min per module plus the shared GADT types), load heavy (Rust + Gleam builds and 50k-event corpora per slice). Assignee stays open for main (x lane per my lap 10 proposal)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 12: blocker chain: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Active 10%) waits on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (Planned 0%), and the code to port (x/apps/sim) is x-lane territory, so the item is blocked for the compiler-side lanes until the x lane takes it")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-19T19:47Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 15:47 split accepted by the x lane: the bulk half moved to SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK (no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, x lane); this item keeps the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-21T20:36Z", "x", "2026-10-21 16:36 x: grooming. Status Active -> Blocked: the remaining scope (supervision half) waits on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, which waits on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (0%); nobody can move it today, and X-DONE D4 keeps it outside x's done. Two lessons from 10-21 for the port. (1) On the Gleam backend, Spiral string loops built on cut-the-remainder or prepend-concatenation were O(n^2) in refc binaries: the lib corpus run reached 16 GB until main's lib fixes (sm'.spi F7D95B8A8410F714: index_of via binary:split, left-fold concat_list/concat_array) and x's glyphs.split_all. Supervision code that touches streams should use those, never hand-rolled cut loops. (2) Memory probes on the BEAM must sample the peak per phase (a 100 ms sampler of :erlang.memory(:binary)); end-of-phase readings hid a 3.2 GB transient and misattributed the cause once. The description's 'bulk compiles to Rust with rayon' is outdated: the bulk shipped on Gleam (SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK note 2).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: narrowed. The pure world model and the runner's scheduling core moved to SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL and SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL (both ready, no KINO dependency). This item keeps only the effectful shell (task supervision as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell) plus the mix tasks, and stays Blocked on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR. Lesson (night of 10-22/23): a Blocked status on a whole epic hid two nights of portable work from the ready list; block only the part that waits.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-23T13:18Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:18 x: STATUS MAP after today's ports (every module of apps/sim/lib checked). PURE, now in Spiral: frames.ex = splitter/glyphs/protocol (dual-backend since 10-19); log.ex = events.spi stream/decoded_stream (all but the legacy positional format, used by no fixture); personas.ex and scenario.ex already call Spiral (personas.spi, schedule/scenario exports); world.ex = inbox.spi (parity with the real World on the prelude stream, rust + gleam); runner.ex's scheduling = scheduler.spi (settled-view parity with the real Runner, rust + gleam); invariants.ex = runner_checks.spi, a typed bridge into invariants.spi (rust + gleam 4/4; checks_parity against the row text and the real Invariants.check is in flight). LEFT IN ELIXIR, all on the G side of BACKEND-BALANCE: (1) the runner shell, which starts executor Tasks, yields and harvests them, turns a crash into a result and paces (pace_ms): the scheduler already emits StartTask/CancelTask commands, so the shell becomes a gleam_otp actor feeding finished_task back (LIB-OTP-ACTORS); (2) executors.ex: the fake one is fake_out, the real one runs a notebook (Cells) and maps its status, behind the same command interface; (3) daemon_memory.ex: a periodic OS-tree sampler, a natural first actor; (4) the mix tasks (xsim.run, thread_run, world, diff, events, frames, x_compat), which become the entry's subcommands; (5) the ExUnit suite (30 cases), which becomes Spiral test blocks as each slice lands, with the Elixir originals kept as oracles until then. So the critical path of SIM-IN-SPIRAL is now LIB-OTP-ACTORS plus the shell, not more pure ports. All drafts are in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world (x's tree is staged).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-23T13:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:59 x: the entry's argument model is drafted: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_args.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). 'xsim run' takes the same 21 options as Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Run, typed in 3 records (corpus, schedule, runner) instead of a keyword list. --executor is a union (fake | fake-leak | cells); --evict / --evict-grace map onto scheduler's eviction union, with grace winning in either order as in eviction/1; --duplicates is parsed to permille (0.25 -> 250), so no float crosses the entry; --spi splits on ',' and '+'. Every bad value is rejected by name ('--seed takes a whole number, got x'). 2 tests. With sim_report.spi (the XSIM summary line, commute and verdict lines), the run task's pure half is done. What's left of it is the effects: reading .spi cells (Cells' document parser, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), the executor, and the shell (LIB-OTP-ACTORS).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-23T14:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:25 x: first end-to-end Spiral sim run drafted, deterministic and fake-executor only: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_run.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). simulated options threads wires the typed pieces with no text round-trips: casting from run_options -> scenario.from_corpus -> flow (exploded into 280-weight frames with --frames) -> per schedule schedule.deliveries (seed = schedule number, duplicates permille, window 6, as Scenario.deliveries) -> scheduler.sync_run -> runner_checks with the fake baseline -> sim_report.summary_line, then verdict_line over all schedules. Schedules loop through a let rec, not listm.init, because the count is runtime data. Test: a 3-cell corpus with 2 schedules prints 2 XSIM lines plus 'XSIM commute=ok result=pass'. Differences from 'mix xsim.run --executor fake': no Task concurrency (peak_inflight, cancels and timing differ by design), ms = 0. Its main (reading --spi files into cells) waits for a cell splitter, which x has (simframes threads_of / file cells) and will be checked next; the cells executor waits for KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-10-23T14:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:27 x: and the entry: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim_entry.spi, 'xsim run --spi FILE[,FILE] [options]', Rust-only (a per-command CLI, rule R2). It reads argv through the arg_count/arg_at pattern of x's args.spi, reads each .spi (or the --spi-file list), cuts cells with splitter.file_cells (x's post-thread cut: a column-0 line after a blank line), applies --cells, runs sim_run.simulated, prints the XSIM lines and exits 1 on 'result=fail', or 2 with usage on bad arguments. Test: a 2-cell text -> 2 lines (1 schedule + verdict), exit 0. Known difference to mix xsim.run: Cells' Document.parse_spi (with *_real.spir companions) cuts cells by notebook rules, so for some files the scenario differs. Unifying needs Cells' parser in Spiral (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), or measuring how often the two cuts differ on x's 36 notebooks (a cheap check, next).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_24 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-10-23T14:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:28 x: SUPERSEDES note 23's 'measure how often the two cuts differ'. The authoritative cell cut is already Spiral: spiral/apps/cells/spi/document.spi (826 lines, written without core: its own rows/cells unions). from_spi spi spir (line 826, via from_spi_bounded) is what Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi calls through the Cells export (main.spi:16), returning Cells' wire text, which Elixir's Domain.decode_cells turns into cells. So xsim_entry should not keep its own cutting rule (splitter.file_cells is x's post-thread rule): reuse document.from_spi. Path, lib-first: lift document.spi (notebook format = generic tooling, used by Cells, the sim, and any notebook reader) into lib/spiral, or at least export a typed 'spi_code_cells spi spir : list string', so the entry gets the same cells as mix xsim.run. Owner: main / Cells (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR covers Cells' Spiral side). Until then the entry's file_cells cut stays, flagged as a known difference.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_25 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-10-23T16:38Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:38 x: for note 24's lift of Cells' document.from_spi into lib, the wire format it returns (read from apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/domain.ex decode_cells/decode_n, read-only): '<cell count>\\n', then per cell '<kind>\\n<source byte length>\\n<source bytes>' with no separator after the source. kind is 'code/<language>' (code/spiral, code/elixir, ...) or a plain kind (markdown, import). The lengths are BYTES (Elixir byte_size and binary-size), so a Spiral reader must slice by bytes (lib sm'.byte_slice), not by sm'.substring, whose unit may differ on Gleam. Better than decoding text: the lifted module exports a typed 'spi_code_cells spi spir : list string' built from its own cells union, so no wire text crosses a Spiral-to-Spiral boundary at all. The wire stays only for Elixir.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_26 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-10-23T17:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:56 x: SLIP, found by Cells. sim_run.spi called post_limit () (splitter.spi) without 'open splitter'. ETY-UnboundVariable in flow_for, and since a broken module fails every gate in its package, checks_parity, scheduler, scheduler_parity and inbox_parity all failed between 13:52 and 13:54 (5/5, 1/1 ... failed) with no fault of their own. Fixed in both copies (open splitter after personas; splitter is listed before sim_run in the spiproj). The four are rerun after chain7; the notebooks it starts after the fix see a clean package. My pre-queue checks scanned name CLASHES but not unopened imports. A cheap addition: for each new module, list the free names it calls and check each against the definitions of the modules it opens.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_27 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-10-23T20:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:33 x: main is landing the actor groundwork into lib today (16:2x): erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor and step_runtime on gleam_erlang 1.x, plus Cells' gleam_erlang 1.3 / gleam_otp 1.3 / stdlib 1.0.5 pins. So the shell (an interpreter of the scheduler's StartTask/CancelTask commands: spawn_unlinked + monitor, kill, Down -> Crashed; LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 9) is no longer blocked on primitives. Next x session: draft the Gleam shell against the landed modules.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_28 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-10-23T20:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:35 x: the OTP shell is drafted, Gleam-only, against the API main is landing: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\runner_shell.spi. An otp_actor whose state is the pure scheduler; messages Adopt (its own subject), Deliver delivery, Finished visible_entry, Settle (reply subject). Each StartTask the scheduler emits becomes an erlang_process.spawn worker that sends Finished back. CancelTask needs no kill in v0, because finished_task ignores keys that are no longer running. Settle re-sends itself with send_after while tasks run, so the reply comes when the scheduler is idle (no sleep). Test: on 4 deliveries, the shell settles to the same visible count as sync_run. Deliberately NOT in the scratch package yet: it needs the landing lib modules, and an unresolvable module fails every gate in its package. Add it after otp_actor is in lib, then run Cells (gleam).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_29 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 29u32, "2026-10-23T20:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:57 x: on gleam_erlang 1.3 / stdlib 1.0.5 (main's pins, landed 16:3x): sim_report and sim_args PASS. sim_run and xsim_entry failed on Rust with E0433 for unicode_segmentation / unicode_normalization: opening splitter pulls glyphs' crate-backed primitives, and their headers named only sha2. Headers now list the crates, as splitter.spi's own header does; requeued (chain13).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_30 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 30u32, "2026-10-23T21:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:27 x: Cells: sim_run 1/1 and xsim_entry 1/1 PASS (chain13, after the unicode crates were added to their rust -d headers). The OTP shell (runner_shell, gleam) now uses main's otp_actor.new_with_self (lib 6525F5CC), with no Adopt message. Its first check failed only because the isolated package's spiproj listed modules the copy lacks (now trimmed). Rerun queued as chain14. Landing trial-4 timed out at 60 min mid-build: args, assemble, checks and digest compiled in x's real layout (args 999 generated lines, digest split 86+946+149). x-compat and e2e were not reached.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_31 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 31u32, "2026-10-23T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:37 x: OTP shell PASSES. Cells on runner_shell (gleam, chain14, 17:30): the_otp_shell_settles_like_sync_run is 1/1, so the actor over scheduler.spi settles to the same visible map as sync_run. It is built on otp_actor.new_with_self (main 6525F5CC) with no Adopt message. The Adopt version is kept in the session scratchpad as runner_shell.adopt.spi.txt. Next for the shell: executor monitors, kill on CancelTask and timeouts (erlang_process monitor and kill), then the switch-(2) measurement per X-BACKEND-MAP note 2.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_32 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 32u32, "2026-10-23T21:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:41 x: design for the shell's next step (monitor, cancel, timeout), using only what lib has today. otp_actor has no selector hook, so the shell's mailbox can't take Down events directly. Instead, each StartTask spawns a GUARD (erlang_process.spawn_unlinked) that spawns the worker, monitors it (monitor) and waits on a selector over its own guard subject (select_map: Cancel) plus that monitor (select_specific_monitor: Down), with selector_receive and the task's timeout. Down with a normal exit means the worker already sent Finished, so the guard does nothing. Down with an abnormal exit makes the guard send Finished {status = Crashed}. Timeout: kill the worker, then send Finished {status = Crashed; out = 'timeout'}. scheduler.spi's task_status is Done | Erred | Crashed, with no TimedOut (checked 17:43), so a separate TimedOut status needs a parity check against Elixir's Runner first. Cancel: kill the worker, send nothing. The shell keeps key -> guard subject in a dictm, so a CancelTask sends Cancel. The shell's message type stays single (Finished). Alternative if gleam_otp 1.3's builder has a selector hook (unverified; check the hex source first): ask main for otp_actor.selecting and drop the guards. Test plan: a worker that crashes, one that sleeps past its timeout, one cancelled, then compare settled_view against sync_run's crash handling.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_33 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 33u32, "2026-10-23T21:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:46 x: note 32's guard is DRAFTED, not yet checked: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\runner_guard.spi (gleam). guarded (report) (handed) {key; timeout_ms; work} spawns an unlinked guard. The guard makes its own subject (handed to the shell for Cancel), spawns the worker unlinked, monitors it, and settles on one selector_receive: Cancel kills the worker, an abnormal Down reports crash_of key 'crashed', and a timeout kills the worker and reports 'timeout'. Tests cover a crash and a timeout. The worker in the timeout test makes its own subject to block on, because Gleam's receive only works for the subject's owner; a foreign subject would crash the worker and fake a pass. Unverified names: optionm.map. Next: add it after scheduler in the x-shell package, run Cells on gleam, then wire it into runner_shell's started_by plus a key -> guard subject dictm for CancelTask.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_34 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 34u32, "2026-10-23T21:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:55 x: runner_guard PASSES 2/2 on gleam (chain17, 17:55): a crashing worker is reported as Crashed 'crashed', and a worker that outlives its timeout is killed and reported as 'timeout'. The first run (chain16) failed at parse: a union case nested in Some without parens; fixed by matching the option first. Next: wire guarded into runner_shell's started_by, keep key -> guard subject in a dictm, and send Cancel on CancelTask.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_35 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 35u32, "2026-10-23T22:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:06 x: executors port (Xsim.Executors, 50 lines). xsim.run offers fake, fake-leak and cells; the flaky mode is used by no task and no test, so it isn't ported. DRAFTED: x-world\\executors.spi (gleam). type executor = list string -> task_result; fake_executor; the leak as a one-field actor (leak_message = Swap (digest, reply), state = previous digest, replacing the ets table); leaking_executor calls it per run. The shell takes the executor (shell_state.exec). New shell test: a_leaking_executor_breaks_sequential_equivalence (limit 1, like the Elixir test at xsim_test.exs:33, through runner_checks + fake_baseline). CELLS executor, not drafted: it calls Elixir Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate(%{cells: [%{kind: :spiral, source: s}...]}, opts) and reads status/verdicts/outputs/memo from the result map. From Gleam that's an @external(erlang, 'Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Notebook', 'evaluate') binding plus a small Erlang-term builder and a decoder (gleam/dynamic/decode), which belong in lib as an elixir_call boundary (one thin OS-style boundary, per eoie-granular rules). The memo tally becomes a counter actor like the leak cell.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_36 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 36u32, "2026-10-23T22:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:15 x: CELLS executor DRAFTED (note 35's last executor): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\cells_executor.spi (gleam). It calls Elixir Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate directly through @external plus lib's gleam_stdlib:identity coerce idiom (as process.spi does), with no Elixir code in x. The doc is built as Erlang maps from gleam dicts with atom keys (%{cells: [%{kind: :spiral, source: s}]}), opts are root/spiral_lib (as xsim.run passes them), and the result is read with maps:get on status, verdicts[last] and outputs[last]. A non-map result gives Erred 'unexpected ...' (Elixir's :unexpected has no Spiral status). Sources cross as one NUL-joined string, split by <<0>>, because Spiral lists aren't Gleam lists. The result comes back as nominal cells_run = #(Bool, String) with .0/.1 accessors. out = string.inspect({status, verdict, output}); it's deterministic, but not byte-equal to Elixir's inspect, which the rewrite doesn't need. The test covers the doc builder only (2 cells, last source). evaluate itself needs a BEAM with Spiral.Cells loaded, i.e. the sim's mix VM, so it is checked there after the shell gets a Gleam entry. All staged in the x-shell package; Cells queued for after tibia's block (after_block2.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_37 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 37u32, "2026-10-23T22:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:20 x: landing requirement found. x's XC005 gate (apps/client/build.ps1:25, x-compat with the 300-weight probe) covers every .spi under apps/client, and the sim's Spiral sources live there (client/simframes generates apps/sim/frames). Measured on the evening drafts: sim_args has 16 cells over 270 (default_run 706, schedule_value 1064; some were there before tonight), xsim_entry 5, runner_shell 8, runner_guard 6, cells_executor 4 (Gleam globals up to 821), memory_watch 2, executors 1. Rule: the sim modules get the same fit pass the client drafts got (split into cells of 270 or less, re-checked with x's real profile + probe) as the LAST step before they land, not now while drafts still change. Gleam global blocks may need splitting into several global calls.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_38 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 38u32, "2026-10-23T22:23Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:23 x: module coverage check. Every lib/xsim/*.ex module now has a Spiral counterpart: world -> inbox/world, runner -> scheduler + runner_shell/runner_guard, executors -> executors + cells_executor, invariants -> invariants/runner_checks, log -> events (stream, decoded_stream, v1 header), frames -> splitter + inbox frame rebuild + sim_run exploded, scenario/personas -> scenario/personas/schedule, daemon_memory -> tree_memory + memory_watch, spiral.ex -> not needed (it only loaded the Gleam ebin into Elixir), json/x_compat -> left with their mix tasks. MIX TASKS -> xsim subcommands, DRAFTED in x-world/client: sim_args gets a generic flags_parsed loop (flag_rules = {switched; valued}); run_args is rebuilt on it, so its two existing tests guard the refactor. New: events_args (xsim.events defaults: seed 1, users 3, edits 1, duplicates 0.2) and world_args (xsim.world defaults: id-base 3000000, user 500/spiralposts, base-time 2026-10-18T08:00:00Z), each with a test. xsim_entry gets 'events' (scenario_stream + scenario_final to --out/--expected, prints deliveries=N) and 'world' (world_files, prints cells/bytes via sm'.length', UTF-8 bytes like byte_size), plus a test that the events stream decodes. Still to port: diff, thread_run (both need cells_executor's evaluate, so they come after it runs in a Cells-loaded VM), frames (needs Xsim.Json's laid-out JSON) and x_compat (Spiral.Cells.XCompat is main's). All checks wait for tibia's block end (after_block2.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_39 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 39u32, "2026-10-23T22:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:26 x: 'xsim frames' DRAFTED (the third mix task). frames_args (--file, --out) in sim_args; in xsim_entry, posts = splitter.posts_of, program = file_cells |> each glyphs.nfc |> joined by blank lines |> lines. JSON goes through final_state's existing laid_out/square/braces/pair, which already port Xsim.Json (Nil -> [], levels below 4 opened). So Xsim.Json needs no new code; it was already ported for world's expected file. The test pins the exact layout, both empty and with an escaped quote. A shadow check over xsim_entry's 10 opens finds each used name defined exactly once. Left: diff and thread_run (after cells_executor runs in a Cells VM) and x_compat (main's Spiral.Cells.XCompat).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_40 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 40u32, "2026-10-23T22:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:34 x: FINDING (desk check, no compile): the Spiral xsim takes cells from splitter.file_cells (a cut at each blank line before a column-0 line), but Elixir's xsim.run/xsim.events/xsim.world take them from Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi (Kino's document.spi spi_walk). They differ. The committed fixture apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.final.json shows 6 cells for prelude.spi: p0 '///- --package .', p1 '///- --test static\\n\\nopen testing' (the test header normalized into a directive), and p2 several top-level blocks merged into one cell. So 'xsim run/events/world' would not byte-match build.ps1's fixtures (Confirm-Fixture uses -cne), and run's corpus differs from Elixir's. 'xsim frames' is unaffected: xsim.frames itself uses Xsim.Frames.file_cells = splitter. Rule (lib-first): no copy of spi_walk in x. The fix is one shared .spi document parser (Kino's from_spi + decode_cells) callable from Rust, owned by Kino/compiler (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY, note filed there). Until then, xsim run/events/world stay out of build.ps1. Next fixture-equality test: 'xsim frames --file oauth1.spi' vs apps/sim/fixtures/thread-oauth1.expected.json once compiled.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_41 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 41u32, "2026-10-23T22:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:56 x: runner_shell gains two cancel tests, queued after tibia's block. (1) a_withdrawn_demand_cancels_the_running_task_like_deferred_run: post/like/unlike through the shell with a slow executor (each worker blocks 50 ms on its own subject), so the unlike's CancelTask reaches a running task (usually through the early-cancel path); (cancels, visible) must equal scheduler.deferred_run's (1, 0). (2) a_cancelled_worker_is_killed_before_it_finishes: the executor sends a probe to the test process after its 50 ms; with the unlike the probe must never arrive within 300 ms (the guard killed the worker), and without it the same run must deliver the probe, which is the positive control in the same test. Together they cover the cancel flow and the kill itself.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_42 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 42u32, "2026-10-23T23:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:06 x: Elixir suite coverage map (xsim_test.exs, 29 tests). Covered by existing Spiral tests: correct runner on shuffled/duplicated deliveries (sim_run + checks_parity), order independence (sync/deferred commute), leak (runner_shell), 280-weight frames/CJK (splitter, glyphs, reading), trailing whitespace (reading), defused dots, column-0 cells, the versioned stream (events), false-pass oracle (runner_checks), eviction + recompute (scheduler), whole numeric literals (personas), panic thread ids (invariants), early replies/edits (inbox), sampler core (tree_memory/memory_watch). ADDED tonight to runner_checks (queued): case 39 naive stale revision, case 51 rerun recompiles prefixes (it found the rerun drain bug, SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL n8), case 57 withdrawn demand hidden unless naive, case 190 grace window rescue/free (minimal stream), case 143 saved stream replays to the same visible results (stream -> decoded_stream -> commute). STILL GAPS, all scenario-scale (they need Scenario/personas/schedule streams and multi-run commutes, best written once xsim run takes Cells-parsed cells): 71 interleaved author threads, 83 subtree visible follows, 133/201 framed out-of-order runs, 153 heavy churn, 182 paced run (pace_ms isn't ported in the scheduler; the shell's Settle polling is its runtime analogue), 223/284 personas end to end.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_43 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 43u32, "2026-10-23T23:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:25 x: nine more Elixir cases ported (correcting n42's 'scenario-scale gaps'): those tests use literal cells, not parse_spi, so they need no shared parser. New C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client\\scenario_checks.spi (rust + gleam, queued after runner_checks): case 16 (seed 3, users 5, edits 3; schedules 1..6 at 0.3 duplicates; every rule ok and something visible), 26 (schedule 1 at 0.1 vs schedule 9 at 0.5 window 12 commute), 71 (from_corpus of @cells + a second notebook, breaking 2: unique ids, f0 and f1 threads, breaking test edits end in _assert_eq 10|17|9|5, the run holds), 83 (root edit re-keys b and c; sources of c follow), 153 (churn 12: 12 root edits, limit 2, invariants hold, p0's source is the last edit), 133 (framed at 120: framed tally = posts, commutes with unframed), 201 (edit frames at 20 include part 2, framed_edits = edits, commute), 223 (personas lurker/fickle/editor/breaker at 2/3/2/2: a fickle unlike and an original-restoring edit exist, broken is non-empty; 4 schedules at 0.3 window 10, limit 2, with an HONEST executor (a broken source errs): every rule, breaking_failed and every post back at its original source; neighbours commute), 284 (an unbreakable test stays unbroken under breaker=3/breaking 2; an empty thread yields no events). For 223, scheduler.spi gained sync_run_with exec / executed_all_by exec (sync_run = sync_run_with fake_result; nothing else changes). Same parameters as the Elixir tests; cells dynamic (inl ~); schedule loops are runtime let rec. Only 182 (pace_ms) stays uncovered, because pacing isn't ported in the deterministic scheduler; the shell's timed Settle is its runtime analogue.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_44 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 44u32, "2026-10-23T23:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:39 x: once LIB-DICTM lands, the scratch packages x-world\\client and x-shell\\client still list their own dictm.spi next to the lib's, a package-vs-lib module name pair whose resolution is untested (see X-GENERIC-TO-LIB n16, why the lib OAuth module became 'oauth'). Plan: run ONE Cells check in that state first, to learn safely in scratch which dictm wins or whether it errors, then remove dictm from both spiproj lists and files. apply_sim_modules.ps1 already never copies dictm into x (it refuses until lib has dictm).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_45 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 45u32, "2026-10-23T23:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:44 x: RE-GROOM Blocked 5% -> Active 40%, because the 10-21 reason no longer holds. The supervision half was 'waiting on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR'; tonight it was built on main's landed otp_actor/erlang_process with no Kino dependency (runner_shell passes on gleam; runner_guard passes 2/2). Progress by milestone, not by description: (1) pure core (world/inbox, scheduler, invariants bridge) with Elixir parity: verified. (2) OTP shell + guard + cancel: shell and guard verified, cancel wiring and kill test drafted. (3) Executors: fake/leak drafted; cells drafted but its real run needs a VM with Spiral.Cells loaded (the true KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dependency). (4) xsim CLI: run verified; events/world/frames drafted; run/events/world byte-equality waits on the shared .spi parser (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY n13), frames doesn't. (5) Elixir suite port: 28 of 29 runner cases mapped or ported (scenario_checks, runner_checks additions), only the paced run left. (6) Landing into x: waits on LIB-DICTM (apply_sim_modules.ps1 is verdict-gated). Remaining blockers name owners: LIB-DICTM (main, tonight's chain), SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY (Kino/compiler), KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (cells executor only).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_46 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 46u32, "2026-10-23T23:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:46 x: case 182 (paced run) ported too, so all 29 runner cases are now mapped or ported. Elixir's pace_ms waits for finished work between deliveries. In the deterministic scheduler, sync_run (all work finishes between deliveries) is the fully paced extreme and deferred_run (nothing finishes until the end) the unpaced one. scenario_checks gains a_paced_run_ends_where_the_unpaced_run_ends: seed 29, users 2, edits 2, fickle=2, 0.1 duplicates; sync_run with EvictNow holds every rule and commutes with deferred_run. That's the same assertion pair as xsim_test.exs:182 (all verdicts ok; commute with the unpaced run).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_47 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 47u32, "2026-10-23T23:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:46 x: the other two Elixir test files, for the coverage map. spiral_test.exs (5 tests: event fixture byte for byte, replay to the final fixture, every e2e world fixture, corpus vs facade, facade vs direct stream) all take cells from Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi, so they follow the shared parser (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY n13); their Spiral form is 'xsim events/world' plus fixture comparisons, already drafted in xsim_entry. x_compat_test.exs (9 tests) exercises Spiral.Cells.XCompat, Kino's Elixir x-compat that x's build no longer uses (apps/client/build.ps1 calls the compiler's --x-compat). Its Spiral home is the compiler-side x-compat (main's), not x's sim. Nothing to port in x; it retires with the Elixir sim.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_48 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 48u32, "2026-10-24T00:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:12 x: Elixir retirement checklist for apps/sim/build.ps1, mapping each mix call to its Spiral replacement and the blocker it waits on. (1) :35 mix deps.get -> gone once nothing below needs mix. (2) :53 mix xsim.run --executor cells --memo (ladder) -> xsim run with cells_executor in a BEAM with Spiral.Cells loaded: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (or a Gleam entry that loads Kino's ebins) + the shared parser. (3) :90 mix xsim.world (4 worlds) -> 'xsim world', drafted: shared parser for byte-equal fixtures (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY). (4) :95 mix xsim.frames -> 'xsim frames', drafted, with a test byte-comparing the oauth1 fixture: no parser blocker; it can switch first, after its Cells pass and the landing. (5) :100 mix xsim.events -> 'xsim events', drafted: shared parser. (6) :103 mix test -> the Spiral suite (all 29 runner cases mapped/ported) plus spiral_test's 5 fixture tests via (3)/(5); x_compat_test retires (the compiler's x-compat is the gate). (7) :112 mix xsim.run corpus -> 'xsim run' (Rust): shared parser, then X-CORPUS-R1/R2 decide speed. (8) :120 mix xsim.run --executor cells -> as (2). (9) :121/125 mix xsim.thread_run (+ tampered) -> needs cells evaluate: as (2). Order: (4) first; (3)/(5)/(7) when the parser lands; (2)/(8)/(9) with Kino without Elixir; (1) last.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_49 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 49u32, "2026-10-24T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:15 x: checklist step (4) prepared. The xsim binary needs its own crate, and C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_xsim_crate.ps1 [-Go] -Verdicts <cells_chain logs> creates apps/client/xsim/: a Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry xsim.spi / output xsim.rs, bin xsim, deps sha2 0.11 (newest 0.11.1) + unicode-normalization 0.1 + unicode-segmentation 1.13 (as frames declares them). xsim.spi is x-world's xsim_entry.spi with '--package ..' and 'test xsim static'. It lists xsim/xsim in package.spiproj and build.ps1's notebook list; build.ps1's crate loop picks it up through the metadata, and the workspace (apps/client/*) through the folder. Gates: refuses until apply_sim_modules has landed sim_run/sim_args/scheduler/runner_checks, and without a passing xsim_entry verdict. apply_sim_modules.ps1 no longer lists xsim_entry, which lands only as this crate. Dry-run against landing-trial-4's copy: refused by the first gate, as designed. Then build.ps1:94-96 (mix xsim.frames) can become 'xsim frames', the first mix call to go.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_50 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 50u32, "2026-10-24T00:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:20 x: a way to run the cells executor without mix, drafted as a probe. x's own mix build already compiled spiral_cells, kino and table (apps/sim/_build/dev/lib/*/ebin), and Elixir's app ebins are in its install. cells_executor.spi gains cells_vm_started (build_lib) (elixir_lib) : bool: code:add_paths over both <lib>/*/ebin wildcards, then application:ensure_all_started(spiral_cells), returning whether it answered ok. Scratch probe C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-shell\\client\\cells_vm_check.spi, with this machine's paths and NOT for landing: start the VM pieces, then run cells_executor on a passing two-cell notebook (status Done) and a failing one (output differs; I don't assume a failed test turns evaluate's status into an error). Queued after tibia's block. If it passes, diff/thread_run/xsim run --executor cells can run from a Gleam entry on the BEAM with no mix, narrowing the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dependency to 'the Elixir app must be compiled', not 'mix must run it'.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_51 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 51u32, "2026-10-24T00:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:29 x: landing gates hardened (prompted by main's lib-gate pitfall, where 0 rows counted as green). New shared reader C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\read_cells_verdicts.ps1 -Logs <cells_chain logs>: a notebook passes only if 'exit 0' follows a spiral-tests summary with executed > 0 and failed = 0, so a load failure or zero tests can't pass. Each notebook keeps its LATEST verdict by event time (the log's date + its 'chain / HH:mm:ss starting' line). The first version ordered by log write time and got sim_run, xsim_entry and dictm wrong. apply_sim_modules.ps1 and apply_xsim_crate.ps1 now also require every verdict to be newer than the newest .spi edit in the source packages, since a module's pass is stale once any dependency changes. Dry run with a stub lib: refused for all 17 modules, because tonight's edits (newest 20:19) postdate their passes, which is correct; the after-block chain re-runs them.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_52 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 52u32, "2026-10-24T00:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:36 x: side-effect check for the cells VM probe (n50) before it runs: Spiral.Cells.Application.start only runs Domain.ensure!, registers a Kino smart cell and starts a Task.Supervisor; no ports, no compile daemon. Under cells_check.ps1's SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, evaluate spawns its own compiler, so starting spiral_cells inside a test VM can't collide with the shared Cells daemon other teams' runs use.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_53 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 53u32, "2026-10-24T04:01Z", "x", "10-24 00:01 x: milestone (5), the 29th and last Elixir runner case is ported (draft). xsim_test.exs:182, 'a paced run waits for finished work between deliveries and still ends where the unpaced run ends'. Runner.run's pace_ms is the yield_many timeout after each delivery, so it only means something in the concurrent shell. runner_shell gains paced_settled inbox pace_ms l (Deliver, then wait pace_ms on an idle subject, as slow_executor does, then Settle). The test runs fickle_churn (like/unlike churn plus a duplicate delivery) paced at 2 ms under EvictNow and requires every runner_checks finding Held and runs_commute against the unpaced shell = ok, Elixir's two asserts. Helper renamed every_finding_held because invariants.all_held exists and runner_shell opens invariants. Drafted on a copy (%TEMP%\\x-shell-paced) so chain21's x-shell compiles stay unaffected; it moves into x-shell after Cells passes (job x-paced-run-check).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_54 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 54u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: milestone (5) DONE in draft. With paced_settled and the paced test, runner_shell.spi passes 7/7 on gleam (job-1792817010528, 01:34), so all 29 Elixir runner cases are now mapped or ported. Moved from %TEMP%\\x-shell-paced into %TEMP%\\x-shell\\client (chain21c checks it next). It lands with the x landing (apply_sim_modules).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_55 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 55u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 13 14:39 (subagent2): blocked (no change since 14:12): SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL Planned 0% under KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, port code in the x tree -> next: unblocks: x lane takes it, or SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL starts | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked: SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL Planned 0%; port code is x-lane territory -> next: x lane takes it | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL, x lane) -> next: x lane | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): blocked (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM; x lane owns the bulk half) -> next: x lane")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_56 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 56u32, "2026-10-25T06:20Z", "x", "10-25 02:20 x: LANDING PREP for the 17 sim modules (apply_sim_modules.ps1 list), which are still only in %TEMP%\\x-world\\client and %TEMP%\\x-shell\\client. The lib blocker is gone (dictm.spi live since 10-24 20:43). Staged in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214: all 17 respelled to edition 3 with the installed 5j format --spelling symbols, 0 test headers touched, words round trip identical. x-compat on the staged client gives 0 findings in 61 files, BUT the build's postable gate (xc post-thread --dry-run per file) finds 121 cells over one post (126 in the edition-2 originals). These modules were never fitted to 280 (n37's XC005 fit pass). So landing order: (1) fit pass by hand on the staged copies until xc dry-run spills 0; (2) Cells on all 17 against today's lib and 5k (Rust targets wait for 5k because of 5j's E0507 regression; their 10-24 verdicts predate the lib respell and 5j, so they don't count); (3) copy into apps/client, list after invariants in package.spiproj and in build.ps1's notebook list (the lander's 'unstaged package.spiproj/build.ps1' refusal is obsolete, since x's hand units already edit both); (4) a verify. Biggest spills: scheduler 23, sim_args 23, runner_shell 13, scenario_checks 13, runner_checks 11, inbox 10.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_57 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 57u32, "2026-10-25T07:06Z", "x", "10-25 03:06 x: FIT PASS findings (staged modules in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\stage; list sugar applied, 120 spills -> fitting by hand; executors and scheduler_parity now 0). Two modules need a design change, not a fit. (1) checks_parity and inbox_parity embed the Elixir sim's generated rows/frames as ~8 KB string literals (xsim.parity.ex writes them into .spi). Generated data can't be split into 280-weight cells sensibly and isn't source. Move it to fixture files beside the reader (apps/client/fixtures/prelude.rows.jsonl etc., written by xsim.parity), read through lib file_system.read_all_text, which has a Gleam arm since LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS. (2) cells_executor's cells_gleam_prelude/cells_vm_prelude are hand-written Gleam FFI blocks (global triple-quoted text): GRANULAR-HEAL drift. Splitting them to fit would spread the drift, so they get rebuilt as one-expression primitives (lib arms where generic) before landing. The other 13 modules are real code and get fitted by splitting definitions.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_58 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 58u32, "2026-10-25T07:20Z", "x", "10-25 03:20 x: fit pass progress on the staged modules (%TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\stage; gauge spills.ps1 = xc post-thread --dry-run per file): at 0 now are tree_memory, executors, scheduler_parity, memory_watch, sim_report, sim_run, xsim_shell_entry and runner_guard (8 of 17). Splits only extract helpers, keep field and line order, and use ++# instead of ^^ (DRIFT-MAP 6). runner_guard gained aliases reporter/handle/worker instead of opening erlang_process unqualified (avoids silent shadowing). Left: 95 cells in inbox 10, runner_checks 11, runner_shell 13, scenario_checks 13, scheduler 19, sim_args 23, plus the n57 redesigns (checks_parity/inbox_parity fixtures to data files, cells_executor FFI to primitives). Nothing compiled yet: Cells on all 17 comes after the fit.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_59 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 59u32, "2026-10-25T07:29Z", "x", "10-25 03:29 x: fit pass: 11 of 17 staged modules at 0 spills (added inbox, runner_checks, runner_shell since n58). Techniques: helpers that keep order and semantics; shared test fixtures (liked_pair, two_thread, lapse_of, naive_faults); type aliases for long Erlang types (handle, reporter, worker, shell_inbox, shell_next, demand, maker, tally_bump); one otp_actor.continue around a next_state match; the content/revision split of applied_event inside the existing mutual-recursion group. Left: scenario_checks 13, scheduler 19, sim_args 23, plus the n57 redesigns (6).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_60 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 60u32, "2026-10-25T07:46Z", "x", "10-25 03:46 x: fit pass state, still uncompiled: 14 of 17 staged modules at 0 spills (adds scenario_checks and scheduler). sim_args is being remodeled, not just split: its flag chains become per-family flag unions (corpus_flag, schedule_flag, runner_flag) with flag_of + set + an optionm.map glue, keeping flag precedence and every error text; 20 cells left there. Tools in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214: spills.ps1 (xc dry-run gauge), break_heads.ps1 / break_pipes.ps1 (syntax-safe <=120 line breaks at a definition's '=' or before a top-level '|>'), plus a check that every column-0 definition follows a blank line (two joins found and fixed). New generic helper to move to lib later (lib-first): first_some (option or-else). Then the n57 redesigns, Cells on all 17 (Rust after 5k; zig after main's index_of land), and landing.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_61 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 61u32, "2026-10-25T07:52Z", "x", "10-25 03:52 x: FIT PASS DONE for the 14 code modules: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, runner_shell, runner_guard, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report, sim_run, xsim_shell_entry, memory_watch, tree_memory, executors and scheduler_parity all spill 0 cells in xc's dry run, with no new lines over 120. sim_args is remodeled with per-family flag unions (corpus/schedule/runner/events flags; flag_of + set + optionm.map glue) and split text-flag setters joined by first_some; flag precedence and every error text are unchanged. Left before Cells: (1) checks_parity and inbox_parity: their ~8 KB generated Elixir rows/frames move out of .spi into fixture files read at test time (or one tiny def per row; decide by whether Cells resolves a path relative to the notebook); (2) cells_executor: the hand-written Gleam FFI (global triple-quoted blocks) gets rebuilt as one-expression primitives. Then Cells on all 17 (Rust and Gleam targets; zig after main's index_of land) on the staged copy, then landing via the apply_sim_modules list.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_62 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 62u32, "2026-10-25T09:25Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:25 -04:00 x: the three redesigns are drafted, and lib + Cells pieces landed unstaged. (1) lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi, new and listed in lib package.spiproj (the 'Erlang-term builder' from note 35). It holds term/atom types, of/cast coercion, atom, terms_of/list_term, tuple, inspect, equal, apply (erlang:apply, one host call), charlist, and 5 gleam tests. Callers need no @external, so x code has no mention findings. (2) cells_executor is rebuilt over it: doc, opts, evaluate, status/verdicts/outputs, the vm start. Every def is under one post. (3) The parity fixtures moved out of the source. fixtures/prelude.jsonl holds the 65 Elixir-recorded deliveries as an xsim-events v1 stream (converted from the old literal list), and inbox_parity loads it through decoded_stream. A new test, the_prelude_fixture_is_a_canonical_stream, checks that stream(decoded) equals the file. fixtures/prelude_checks_{fake,wrong}.json hold the expected Elixir rows for checks_parity. (4) Cells fix (apps/cells targets.ex build_and_run_gleam): a Gleam program now runs with cwd = the notebook root, like Rust's execute. Before, it ran in the temp gleam package, so relative fixture paths failed (probe job-1792917428269-x-cwd-probe: rust passed, gleam 'file:read_file failed'). New ExUnit test in targets_test.exs. (5) The previous Cells run (job-1792914892304) failed every module 0/N for one cause: my >120 line breaker split record TYPES at a field without a trailing ';' (scheduler run_counts/scheduler, sim_args, runner_shell). The parser reads the next field as a type argument ('Expected: }') and drops the block. Re-laid in x-client style ('a; b;' then aligned continuation). Wrapped record VALUES parse fine. (6) lib renamed receive/received_value/selector_receive (2cc407ce) to receive_within/receipt_value/selector_receive_within, so runner_guard and runner_shell are updated. All 17 modules post one cell per post (xc post-thread --dry-run: 0 spills), with no line over 120. Gate: job-1792920093887-x-simland-serial2 (targets_test, then the erlang_term lib gate, then the 17 modules).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_63 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 63u32, "2026-10-25T11:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:24 -04:00 x: serial3 interim (job-1792923581594). lib erlang_term 5/5 (the earlier 1/5 was a 300 s load timeout). scheduler 5/5. inbox 2/6: the 4 failures are gleam legs timing out at 300 s under load (0.7 GB free, CPU 100%), rust legs pass. inbox_parity and scheduler_parity PASS on gleam, which proves the Cells gleam-cwd fix: ../sim/fixtures resolves from the notebook dir. They fail only on 'rust -d sha2' with NotFound: a Rust cell with deps runs through the spiral CLI (spiral rust --rs-path ... -> cargo run), and Cells starts that CLI with cd nil (targets.ex run_status 'spiral? -> nil', from the initial Cells import), so the program inherits Cells' cwd. Plain rust (rustc + execute) and F# already run in the notebook root. Fix drafted (scratchpad\\cells-cwd\\apply.ps1): spiral? runs start in opts[:root] when it is a directory, as F# does. It applies after serial3 ends (Cells reads the live tree); then rerun the failing modules. The ExUnit gleam-cwd test can't run in that env ('problem when running the shell command escript' while building a fresh gleam project), so the parity modules are the proof.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_64 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 64u32, "2026-10-25T14:27Z", "x", "2026-10-25 10:27 serial3 (job-1792923581594, 3.9 h on a box at 100% CPU / 2.3 GB free): erlang_term 5/5 green. Sim modules fully green: scheduler 5/5, sim_run, executors, cells_executor. Failures, three kinds: (1) cells at the 300 s limit while compiling (inbox, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report, runner_checks one test, tree_memory rustup) = load. (2) the Rust parity modules (inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity) panic with os error 3 opening ../sim/fixtures = the spiral CLI cwd is nil; scratchpad cells-cwd patch fixes it, waits for no Cells reader. (3) Gleam actor waits for an event that always comes timed out at 1000-5000 ms (memory_watch call 1000, runner_shell Settle 5000 = the executor timeout itself, runner_guard crash deadline 1000, leak call 1000). Rule: a deadline bounding an expected event goes to at least lib otp_actor's 10000; one whose expiry is the asserted outcome stays short. Raised in stage: init 10000, Settle 30000, Peak 10000, leak 10000, crash 10000 / inbox wait 20000. Short ones kept: stall 50, signalled_in 100/300. Rerun queued as job-1792938325158-x-simland-serial4.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_65 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 65u32, "2026-10-25T15:50Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:50 -04:00 x: serial4 (job-1792938325158, the raised actor deadlines plus load reruns): 9 of 11 modules fully green. memory_watch 3/3, runner_guard 2/2, executors 1/1, xsim_shell_entry 2/2, runner_checks 9/9, sim_report 2/2, tree_memory 3/3 (rust+gleam), inbox 6/6, sim_args 4/4. So the n64 deadline diagnosis held: every actor test that failed at 1000-5000 ms passes at the new deadlines. Left: runner_shell 6/7 and scenario_checks 8/12, all cells at the 300 s limit (slowest 302 s; serial3's slowest in scenario_checks was 343 s). Rule: a sim module that hit the limit in either run takes x's existing heavy-module header '///- --package . --timeout 600000', as scenario.spi, world.spi, protocol.spi and 4 others already do. Applied in stage to inbox, runner_checks, runner_shell, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report and tree_memory. Rerun of the two still red: job-1792943380223-x-simland-serial5. Still open: the 3 Rust parity modules (cwd patch, after main's Cells/lib chain).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_66 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 66u32, "2026-10-25T15:58Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:58 -04:00 x: the three Cells/lib drafts that wait for no Cells or lib reader are now ONE landing with undo: scratchpad\\land_cells_lib.ps1 [-Spiral] [-X] [-Undo]. (1) cells-cwd: spiral CLI runs (Rust with deps) start in the notebook root; it fixes the 3 Rust parity modules' os error 3. (2) cells-defused: x-compat skips links whose dots the profile's poster defuses, plus its test (X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE). (3) code-points: lib unicode code_points/is_white_space plus x glyphs' heal (GRANULAR-HEAL n8). Hardened on the way: cells-defused's Swap now refuses an anchor that matches more than once (it used Contains + Replace-all), and both Cells patches take -Base for rehearsal. A copy rehearsal (scratchpad\\rehearse_cells_lib.ps1) changed 5/5 files, left glyphs with 0 host macros/globals, and undo restored 5/5 hash-equal. Its window: no Cells/lib reader running. Today main-serial-lib-lands-4 and main-cells-trailing-docs are live, and main, tibia and fiscal have ~12 lib jobs queued. Gate after landing: Cells lib unicode (rust+gleam); x glyphs, splitter, reading; the 3 parity modules on rust; mix test x_compat_test.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_67 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 67u32, "2026-10-25T16:10Z", "x", "2026-10-25 12:10 -04:00 x: serial5 (job-1792943380223) with the --timeout 600000 header: runner_shell 7/7, scenario_checks 12/12. So 14 of the 17 sim modules are fully green in Cells. Only the 3 Rust parity modules are left (cwd patch, in land_cells_lib.ps1), and only they import each other (scheduler_parity and checks_parity import inbox_parity), so the 14 could land alone. NOT landed, by rule: modules land into x only when x's own build can pass with the INSTALLED tools. apps/client/build.ps1 fails on any x-compat finding. The installed compiler reports 18 autolink findings on the staged modules (otp_actor.call/next/new, optionm.map x7, erlang_process.pid, t.work, r.events), and the tree's compiler (a candidate built from the tree core) reports 0 in the same 17 files with the same profile. All 18 are identifier dots the poster defuses, which is exactly X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE's fix. So the sim landing (land_client.ps1, stage files) waits for that fix to be installed in a pack. When it is: land_cells_lib.ps1 (cwd), rerun the 3 parity modules, then land_client.ps1 with all 17.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_68 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 68u32, "2026-10-25T16:12Z", "x", "2026-10-25 12:12 -04:00 x: land_cells_lib.ps1 is queued in main's serial lib chain after list-sugar-rest as job-1792944559767-main-serial-x-cells-unicode. First the gate: lib-gate-kit on unicode.spi, with the apply run on a lib copy and a throwaway glyphs copy. Then the land behind the reader guard, then mix test x_compat_test.exs, with -Undo on failure. Frozen until main sends the verdict: the 5 target files and the 4 apply scripts. After it lands, x runs Cells on glyphs, splitter and reading plus the 3 parity modules.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_69 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 69u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:20 -04:00 x: 14 of 17 sim modules LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x as an unstaged layer: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, scenario_checks, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, executors, cells_executor, runner_guard, runner_shell, memory_watch, xsim_shell_entry. land_client.ps1 -Only adds them to package.spiproj, the build's Invoke-SpiralTests list and 14 README rows. Why now: the installed 5l'' compiler carries the defused-dot x-compat, which reports 0 findings on all 17 (it was 18 on 5k). Checks before writing: a dry run on a fresh copy (58 files, x-compat 0, 0 cells over one post, 0 lines over 120, no parity module leaked), and the Cells-verified copy equals the tree (all 14 modules, every other client .spi, both sim fixtures hash-equal). So serial3-5's Cells greens carry over, against the tree lib of that time. Left: inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity, which wait for the cwd patch (main's job-1792944559767); then land_client.ps1 -Only those 3; then x verify.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_70 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 70u32, "2026-10-25T18:29Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:29 -04:00 x: x verify moved to the idle second laptop (i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1; DESKTOP-6E8C14G at 7% CPU, 9 GB free). Synced spiral (head 47d1e562 + 306 changed files, digest 4003804B6714CCEC) and x (head 9b3fbe8e + 18 changed, digest 8557C07D7269E678), both REMOTE-SYNC-OK. Running x/scripts/build.ps1 there: job-1792952960245-x-verify-remote1 (laptop eoie, 4 h limit). Expected to stop at protocol.spi's zig target until main's LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF lands (main-serial-zig-index-of-3). Everything before it in the build (gate, x-compat on 5l'' with the new exit-code check, release xc, e2e, postable over 58 files) is evidence for this landing and for X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_71 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 71u32, "2026-10-25T18:42Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:42 -04:00 x: the laptop verify (job-1792952960245) failed at the api crate compile ('Unbound term variable: erlang_term'). Cause: remote.ps1 sync, not x. It sent 'git ls-files -m -o', which never includes STAGED files, so the laptop's spiral lacked lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi (status A at sync time; the user committed it at 14:35, d095f13d). The fix (diff against HEAD + untracked) is handed to kit2, who now owns remote.ps1. Local control (job-1792953465762): the api crate compiles on the installed 5l'' against the full local lib (exit 0). Its output differs from the committed api .rs only by 5l'''s closure-move clones (14 lines), so x's next build regenerates the committed crate outputs for the user to commit. The full x verify waits for main's serial chain (list-sugar-rest, zig-index-of-3, then my x-cells-unicode), so one run covers protocol.spi's zig target and the 3 parity modules. No laptop syncs until main or kit2 clear the node.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_72 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 72u32, "2026-10-25T18:54Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:54 -04:00 x: user asked (10-25) whether the 14 new files in apps/client are drift or proper evolution. Assessment from the import graph: granularity is right (hand-written units, explicit :< imports, none split automatically), the HOME is wrong. They import client modules (prelude 14, events 9, invariants 5, glyphs/personas/scenario/schedule 2, splitter 1), and NO client module imports them, so they are a separate layer: the Spiral port of xsim. In apps/client they add type-check work to every one of the 23 crate compiles (each crate entry checks the whole client package), and the client build would run their Cells tests. Proper shape: their own package x/apps/sim/spi/package.spiproj (packageDir ../../../../spiral/lib; packages |core-, spiral-, ../../client-, as tibia apps/arena uses ../../src-), next to the Elixir sim they port. apps/sim/build.ps1 runs their Invoke-SpiralTests plus an x-compat pass. The 3 parity modules' fixture path becomes ../fixtures/. NOT moved yet: the user was staging the 14 at the time (status A/??), and staged files mid-run are the user's. The move waits for the user's go or their commit, then goes on top as git renames. Also seen: the laptop's verify2 failed on x-compat 18 findings = a pre-5l'' compiler there (the node never got the 5l'' install); reported to kit2.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_73 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 73u32, "2026-10-25T19:17Z", "x", "2026-10-25 15:17 -04:00 x: MOVED (user 10-25: 'move everything properly, ignore my git actions'). The Spiral sim now has its own package, x/apps/sim/spi (package.spiproj: packageDir ../../../../spiral/lib; packages |core-, spiral-, ../../client-). Placement rule, from the import graph (scratch client_graph.ps1): a client module moves when no client crate entry reaches it. 35 modules are reached by the 23 client crates and stay. Sim-only, and moved: exsss, events, final_state, personas, scenario, schedule, world, invariants, simframes/ (the Gleam frames entry), plus the 14 runner modules. Shared and staying in the client: prelude, glyphs, splitter, protocol (the sim reaches them through ../../client-). Updated: client package.spiproj, build.ps1 test list and README (rows plus the simframes paragraph moved to apps/sim/spi/README.md); apps/sim/build.ps1 (simframes path x2; an x-compat pass with the exit-code check; Invoke-SpiralTests over the 23 sim modules when not -fast); root Cargo.toml exclude drops apps/client/simframes. The client's package.spiproj and README after step 1 were byte-equal to HEAD. Checked on a copy before applying: --check exit 0 for client xc, glyphs and sim scenario_checks, simframes, xsim_shell_entry; x-compat 0 in client (35 files) and sim/spi (23). Cells from the new home: job-1792955719853 (invariants, events, inbox, xsim_shell_entry, simframes). The 3 parity modules now read ../fixtures/; land_parity.ps1 (rehearsed) puts them in apps/sim/spi after the cwd patch lands.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_74 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 74u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: node verifies after the move (the node core is 826D67C69119F61E, determinism-checked equal to the review box by kit2). verify3 (job-1792957554034): cargo failed on an empty apps/client/simframes left by sync (fixed in remote.ps1 by kit2), and the sim build failed at fixture compare: sim-small.world.json embeds prelude.spi's text, which the list-sugar respell changed. verify4 (job-1792958570590): client 0 x-compat, release build ok, e2e 1 failure, 'xc timeline hana' (CJK and Hangul) DIFFERENT. Cause: e2e's Invoke-Step decodes xc's redirected UTF-8 with the console's default encoding, which over the node's ssh session isn't UTF-8; it passes locally. FIXED in apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1: the child pwsh sets [Console]::OutputEncoding/$OutputEncoding to UTF-8 (no BOM), and ProcessStartInfo sets StandardOutputEncoding/StandardErrorEncoding to UTF-8, so e2e reads xc the same on every machine. The sim -Accept regenerated 10 fixtures, reviewed with a paren-aware Cons respeller (scratch review_accepted.ps1): 3 differ only by 'Cons (a, b)' -> 'a :: b'; the world and event files also move post boundaries (the shorter respelled text splits into posts at different places); parity.txt digests change with the sources; nothing else. Accepted into the tree. Also seen: mix xsim.thread_run on oauth1.spi 'status=error/error result=fail' with no detail; verify5 (job-1792962117792) reruns it with its log captured, and runs e2e -Accept for the README outputs that the new sim worlds change.")
inl spiral_lua_closure_record_arg () = Task ("SPIRAL-LUA-CLOSURE-RECORD-ARG", "Lua backend drops the fields of a closure argument that is a record or tuple: the many-args branch of the closure emitter (spiral_compiler.fs, the \"return function(dom)\" case near line 168222) writes local _ = (table.unpack or unpack)(dom), so the body reads undefined v-names and fails at run time (attempt to compare nil). Call sites already pass {[1]=..,[2]=..}; the fix is to unpack dom into the domain vars (local v2, v3 = ...). C binds them correctly. Repro: tibia lab/tibia-probes-in-spiral/lua-curried-record-closure (a curried prefers_by over a two-field record picked by a dynamic if; lua exits with an error, expected exit 0). Found by the Tibia arena competitor probe; worked around there by applying the function fully at the call site.", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lua,backend,bug", "current", "current")
inl spiral_lua_paren_statement () = Task ("SPIRAL-LUA-PAREN-STATEMENT", "Lua backend emits statement macros and field/index assignments that start with \"(\" on their own line, e.g. (v8)[k] = v or (function(m) ... end)(x) after a call statement; Lua 5.1 and LuaJIT reject this as ambiguous syntax (function call x new statement). The backend should guard such statements (a leading \";\" is not valid Lua 5.1 at block start, so wrap in do ... end, or emit through a local). Workaround in the Tibia arena client bindings: every statement macro starting with ( is written as do ... end.", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lua,backend,bug", "current", "current")
inl dota () = Task ("DOTA", "Dota 2 as the next dogfood project after Tibia: typed game rules in Spiral with C oracle tests, a policy compiled to Lua 5.1 running as Dota bot scripts (vscripts/bots, bot_generic.lua Think, mode GetDesire), measured on a headless Linux client/server on a rented CPU box against the default bots and open-source bot packs. Starts from the CELLS-NEW-PROJECT scaffold and the tibia exemplar (TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR).", Epic, P2, D5, 0u32, Paused, "CELLS-NEW-PROJECT,TIBIA-REUSE-EXEMPLAR,TIBIA-PARK", "dota,project,dogfood,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dota () = Planning ("DOTA", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dota_1 () = Note ("DOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: Paused by rule: no Dota work until the user starts it (tibia-dogfood-project memory, 10-18).")
inl note_of_dota_2 () = Note ("DOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-25T01:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 21:13 START CONDITIONS, from tibia's side (TIBIA-PARK note 2 has the calendar): (1) TIBIA-PARK holds, meaning tibia is parked with resume conditions, around 2026-10-26; (2) CELLS-NEW-PROJECT is done (main's, 50%, a dep here); (3) the user starts Dota (tibia-dogfood-project memory: no Dota work until then). Not a start condition: DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD's rented CPU box, which is paid and needs the user's approval. DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS (40%, local mock and check.ps1) and the npc_units oracle design can start first without it. Carried over from tibia by TIBIA-PARK's scope: the Lua-from-Spiral bot pattern with no hand-written Lua (TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA), lib Lua arms, and world start and stop through lib process (LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE).")
inl dota_headless_world () = Task ("DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD", "Headless Dota 2 world on a rented CPU box: SteamCMD app 570 on Linux, launched with bots filling both teams and the bot world-state socket, no GPU (software rendering as in DotaService/LuaFun), scripted start and stop like tibia live/world.ps1, logs and replays kept outside the repo.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "dota,infra,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dota_headless_world () = Planning ("DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dota_headless_world_1 () = Note ("DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD", 1u32, "2026-10-18T16:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:05 tibia-lane research, nothing run. Current local start, from OpenHyperAI discussion 135 (2026): a cfg file in 'dota 2 beta/game/dota/cfg/' run with 'exec <name>.cfg' from the console: sv_lan 1; dota_wait_for_players_to_load 10 plus the three wait/timeout delays at 10; dota_bot_practice_script <workshop id> (for our own files use the local vscripts/bots folder instead); dota_bot_set_difficulty / dota_bot_match_difficulty / dota_bot_practice_difficulty 4; dota_bot_practice_gamemode 1 (All Pick; 2 CM, 4 RD, 22 AP ranked); dota_bot_match_use_matchmaking 1; dota_bot_practice_start 1; map dota. dota_bot_practice_team 0 picks Radiant (1 Dire, 2 random). sv_lan 1 runs offline after the first download; port 27015. Dedicated route (older sources): SteamCMD app 570, then on Linux 'srcds_run -game dota +map dota' through the steam runtime, with dota_bot_populate as a server-console command and +dota_start_ai_game 1 (unverified). Next: on the rented box, try the dedicated route with the cfg lines passed as +commands, and check whether a client-less match ticks (bots only) and writes a parseable console log for the scoreboard.")
inl dota_lua_bindings () = Task ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", "Typed bindings over the Dota bot API in Spiral (the counterpart of tibia apps/arena/client.spi): GetBot, unit queries, Action_* orders, Think/GetDesire entry shapes; reuse tibia apps/arena/lua.spi, after checking which of its calls the restricted bot VM allows (no io, maybe no os.getenv/os.time: give it sandbox-safe variants).", TaskKind, P2, D2, 40u32, Paused, "", "dota,lua,spiral,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dota_lua_bindings () = Planning ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", Unestimated, LoadUnset, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_1 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T09:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:01 reference layout from the most active open-source pack, OpenHyperAI (github forest0xia/dota2bot-OpenHyperAI): bots/bot_generic.lua (per-bot entry), hero_selection.lua, ability_item_usage_generic.lua, item_purchase_generic.lua, mode_*_generic.lua (farm, push, defend, roam, assemble), shared libs in bots/FunLib, per-hero files in bots/BotLib. It compiles TypeScript to Lua (tstl) into bots/, which is the same shape as Spiral compiled to Lua into a host, so the Spiral entries map one to one onto these file names. Sandbox: a restricted Lua 5.1 VM (no C libraries, no io, no threads; verify os/print/require). Local start: custom lobby with server location Local Host; a console-only start exists (find the commands for DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD).")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_2 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T16:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:03 tibia-lane: first prototype at tibia lab/dota-lua-bindings (move into the Dota repo when it exists). bot.spi = typed bindings (GetBot, GetHealth, GetAttackDamage, GetAttackRange, GetNearbyCreeps(radius, true), Action_AttackUnit(target, true), DotaTime as ms, RandomInt, and on_think setting the global Think); every name is in OpenHyperAI (GetNearbyCreeps 76 files, Action_AttackUnit 20, GetAttackDamage 81, GetAttackRange 105, GetBot() 185, 'function Think' 18). last_hit.spi = pure decision (the weakest enemy creep that dies to one hit, else hold) with C oracle and Lua test blocks; bot_generic.spi = entry wiring it into Think and counting orders with tibia apps/arena lua.spi through the package reference arena- (the reuse proof). check.ps1 compiles bot_generic to 2.7 KB of Lua, fails on any os./io./debug./require/dofile/loadfile, then mock.lua loads it with setfenv into an env holding only math/string/table/base functions plus a mock bot API and runs Think on 4 creep waves: all pass. Cells cannot run the test blocks there yet (CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE).")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_3 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:43 fix in the prototype: Think runs many times a second, and re-sending Action_AttackUnit on every call would restart the attack animation, so a last hit would never land. bot.attacking_unit (GetAttackTarget() == target) now gates the order, and bot_generic only orders when the pick is not already the attack target. mock.lua's GetAttackTarget tracks the last order, and a 5th case (a second Think on the same wave issues no order) passes: check.ps1 5/5 in the sandbox. Still open: the attack-point timing (a real last-hitter orders the attack about the attack point before the creep's health drops under the hit; that needs the creep damage model of DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO).")
inl note_of_dota_lua_bindings_4 () = Note ("DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-18T17:43Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 40%.")
inl dota_first_scenario () = Task ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", "First measured scenario, kept narrow like the tibia troll camp: 1v1 mid last-hitting (creep last hits and denies in 10 minutes) with typed rules and a C oracle for creep aggro/attack timing, a Spiral policy run live as a bot script, and a pre-registered comparison against the default bot.", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "dota,benchmark,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dota_first_scenario () = Planning ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dota_first_scenario_1 () = Note ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", 1u32, "2026-10-18T09:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:01 baselines, as in the Tibia arena (use the published packs locally only, never interact upstream): the default Valve bots, and OpenHyperAI as the strongest open-source pack (TypeScript compiled to Lua, positions set by lobby slot order). Measure the same narrow scenario for each.")
inl note_of_dota_first_scenario_2 () = Note ("DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:44 oracle for creep numbers, decided like tibia (each creature's data-canary Lua definition was the oracle, not a wiki): read lane-creep stats from the installed game's unit definitions (npc_units.txt in the dota VPK: health, armor, AttackDamageMin/Max, AttackRate, AttackAnimationPoint, ProjectileSpeed, MovementSpeed, bounty), pinned with the build version, on the rented box from DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD. Wiki pages are patch-mixed (Liquipedia's search results mix 7.03/7.20/7.21 archives, and its pages refuse automated fetches), so they are cross-checks only. The last-hit model then needs: creep vs creep damage per wave, the hero's attack point and projectile time (the order has to go out before health drops under one hit), and armor reduction; the prototype's pick (weakest creep that dies to one hit now) is the zero-latency version of that rule.")
inl cells_export_atomic_write () = Task ("CELLS-EXPORT-ATOMIC-WRITE", "Notebook exports rewrite lib .spi files in place, so a concurrent cells run can read a half-written file: on 2026-10-18 lib/spiral/rust/near.spi was re-exported at 04:39:25 with unchanged content, and six tibia test files compiled during that window failed with \"near.spi:41:6: Expected: }\" and \"Unbound type variable: vector\". All passed on rerun. The export (spiral.edit/export and any generator that writes lib files) should write to a temp file in the same directory and rename it over the target, and skip the write when the content is unchanged. The second rule alone would have prevented this case.", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,export,reliability", "current", "current")
inl float_text_canonical () = Task ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", "Pick one canonical float text across backends: C and Lua (and since 2026-10-18 Kotlin and Swift, which follow the C oracle) lay out shortest digits with printf %g at the smallest round-tripping precision, so 100.0 prints 1e+02 and 1e10 prints 1e+10, while Rust, TypeScript, Python, Lean and Zig print 100.0 and switch to exponents at their own thresholds (Rust 1e16, Python 1e+16, JS 1e+21). show_floats pins only values where all forms coincide. Needs the user's call on the layout (likely shortest digits, fixed notation for exponents in [-4, 16), Python-style exponent otherwise), then one lib change per backend arm of format_real and a cross-backend self-checking sample.", TaskKind, P1, D4, 50u32, Active, "", "lib,format,backends,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_float_text_canonical () = Planning ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_1 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T11:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:43 main-thread: found while adding Kotlin/Swift format_real arms; hand probe outputs in BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS note 27. Not changed autonomously: it alters visible output of every consumer that prints floats (apps/spiral Rust, Tibia C/Lua).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_2 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:24 main-thread options for the decision (every format_real arm already agrees on NaN, Infinity, -Infinity, shortest round-trip digits and the .0 suffix; only the switch to exponent notation differs): (A) keep C's %g rule, exponent once it reaches the digit count: 100.0 -> 1e+02, 123.45 -> 123.45, 1e-05 -> 1e-05 (C, Lua, Kotlin, Swift today); (B) Python repr's rule, fixed notation for exponents in [-4, 16), else d.ddde+XX with a sign and at least 2 digits: 100.0 -> 100.0, 1e16 -> 1e+16, 1e-05 -> 1e-05; (C) Rust's rule, 1e16 -> 1e16, 1e-7 -> 1e-7 (no sign, no padding). Recommendation: B. It keeps every value below 1e16 readable (no 1e+02 for 100), matches the majority's fixed range, and keeps C's exponent spelling for the extremes. Cost: change the layout test in the C, Lua, Kotlin and Swift arms (exponent >= digits becomes exponent >= 16) and give Rust, TypeScript, Lean and Zig the [-4, 16) window and e+XX spelling; then one self-checking sample over 0.0001, 0.00001, 100.0, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16 and 1.5e22 on every backend.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_3 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:29 helper-lane lap 1: arm map for the decision, read from lib sm'_real.spir format_real (lines 593-~890): 11 backends carry float arms - Lua 659 (%.Ng loop), Fsharp 666 (ToString R, E to e), Python 673 (repr), Rust 680 ({:?}), TypeScript 688 (String(v)), Zig 696 (spiralFloatText), Lean 705/706, Kotlin 713/733, Swift 760/791, C 829/838, Delphi 861/880. Two arms are missing from the title's list: F# (.NET R switches to E notation at its own threshold, to confirm by probe) and Delphi. Under the recommended option B, Python's arm already is the rule (repr), so it can serve as the self-checking sample's oracle text, and the other 10 arms change. Still needs the user's pick of A, B or C.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_4 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:17 helper-lane lap 2: measured the F# arm's rule (dotnet fsi on the cache SDK, ToString R with InvariantCulture, the exact call in sm'_real.spir:666): fixed notation through 1e16 (10000000000000000) and exponent from 1e17 (1E+17), small values switch at 1e-05 (1E-05, 1.25E-05) while 0.0001 stays fixed; after the arm's E -> e and .0 suffix that gives 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 10000000000000000.0, 1.5e+22, 1e-07. Python repr (option B's reference) gives the same texts except 1e16, which Python writes 1e+16. So under option B the F# arm needs only one change - switch to exponent at 1e16 instead of 1e17 - which makes F# the closest arm to B after Python itself.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_5 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:43 helper-lane lap 3: measured the last arm, Delphi: lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float.pas carries lib sm'_real.spir's SpiralFormatFloat verbatim (lines 863-877: shortest ffGeneral precision p in 1..17 that round-trips, .0 when no point or exponent, E -> e), compiled with the matrix flags (fpc -O2 -Mdelphi, scoop FPC) as one bounded job. Output: 0.0001, 0.00001, 1e2, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16, 1.5e22, 1e-7, 0.1, 1.5. So Delphi is the farthest arm from every option: ffGeneral at the shortest precision switches to exponent as soon as the digit count is below the exponent, which turns 100.0 into 1e2 and 1e15 into 1e15 (F# 1000000000000000.0), keeps 1e-05 fixed as 0.00001, and writes exponents unsigned and unpadded (1e-7, Python 1e-07). Under option B the Delphi arm needs its own rule (choose fixed vs exponent by the decimal exponent range like Python repr, -4 <= e < 16, then digits from the shortest round-trip) rather than a ffGeneral precision loop. All arms are now measured; the A/B/C choice stays with the user.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_6 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T16:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:03 helper-lane lap 5: priced option B on the farthest arm without deciding for the user: lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float_b.pas is a Python-repr-style Delphi formatter (shortest round-trip digits from FloatToStrF ffExponent, then fixed notation for decimal exponents -4..15 and d.ddde+XX with at least two exponent digits otherwise), about 35 lines replacing the current 15-line loop. Its expected output on the measured set is exactly Python's repr (checked with python -I): 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1, -2.5. The fpc run waits for main's freeze all-clear. So B costs one function per arm of this size (Delphi the largest delta, F# only the 1e16 threshold); the A/B/C decision stays with the user.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_7 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:49 helper-lane: ran the option-B Delphi formatter with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi (bounded): 9 of the 10 values print exactly Python's repr (0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1); the tenth, -2.5, raised EConvertError because FPC's FloatToStrF ffExponent returned a string without an E for that value, so the exponent parse needs a guard (fall back to ffGeneral digits when no E is present). So the cost of B on the farthest arm is one ~40-line function and it reaches repr parity on the measured set apart from that guard. Still the user's A/B/C call.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_8 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 8u32, "2026-10-20T00:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 20:44 helper-lane lap 8: option B now reaches 10 of 10. In lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/delphi_float_b.pas, the shortest-digits search formats with Format('%.*e', [p - 1, Abs(x)]), C-style, which always carries an E, instead of FloatToStrF ffExponent, whose output for 2.5 at low precision had none. Built with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi into spiral-bin/runs/float-b, the 10 test values print exactly Python's repr: 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1000000000000000.0, 1e+16, 1.5e+22, 1e-07, 0.1, -2.5. So B costs one ~40-line function on the Delphi arm with full parity on the measured set. The A/B/C choice is still the user's.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_9 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 9u32, "2026-10-22T06:59Z", "main", "02:59 main: build outputs out of lab: delphi_float.exe/.o and delphi_float_b.exe/.o (regenerable from the .pas probes) moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL. The two .pas probes stay with this Planned item.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_10 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 10u32, "2026-10-22T09:50Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:50 main: DECIDED B (Python repr layout), under memory decide-autonomously: no user call is needed for a technical choice that has been measured. Reasons, all measured on this item: (1) Python's arm already is the rule, so it is the self-checking oracle; (2) F# needs only one change (the exponent switch moves from 1e17 to 1e16); (3) the farthest arm, Delphi, reaches 10 of 10 parity with the 40-line delphi_float_b.pas; (4) every value below 1e16 stays readable (100.0, not 1e+02 or 1e2). Acceptance rule, fixed now: the change lands only if (a) a self-checking test block in lib over 0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16, 1.5e22, 1e-07, 0.1 and -2.5 prints Python's repr text on every backend with a format_real float arm that Cells can run; (b) the lib Cells gate on sm'_real/sm' has no new failure; (c) visible-output consumers are told before landing: tibia (C and Lua float prints) and x, and apps/spiral's Rust output diff is reviewed in the next matrix review. The lab .pas probes go into the Delphi arm, after which lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL is deleted.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_11 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 11u32, "2026-10-22T09:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:52 main: design for B: one portable function instead of 10 arm rewrites. Every float arm already produces the shortest round-trip digits and differs only in layout (C 1e+02, F# 1E+17, Delphi 1e2, Rust 100.0). New sm'.float_text_repr parses any of those (sign, whole.fraction, e/E exponent with optional sign) into digits plus decimal point and re-lays them in Python repr form: fixed for decimal exponents in [-4, 16), else d.ddde+XX with a sign and at least 2 digits; NaN, Infinity and -Infinity pass through; -0.0 stays. Drafted in the lib copy %TEMP%\\lib-float-repr (sm'.spi after trim_end) with a 26-case test, run on gleam, fsharp, cuda, rust and typescript, covering every arm's layout. Its gate is queued behind tibia's live block (job-1792662755917-main-float-repr-gate). Next step after a green gate: apply float_text_repr to the float arms' output in sm'_real.spir format_real and extend show_floats to the measured set (acceptance rule of 05:50).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_12 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 12u32, "2026-10-22T10:04Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:04 main: wiring constraint found: lib package.spiproj loads sm'_prototypes- (line 22), then sm'_real* (23), then sm' (24). The real format_real cannot call sm'.float_text_repr, because sm' loads after it. Wiring options, to be measured after the gate: (1) declare a prototype in sm'_prototypes and give it its instance in sm'.spi, with format_real's float arms calling the prototype; (2) move float_text_repr and its few helpers (index_of, slice, trim_start/trim_end, replicate, to_lower) into a small module listed before sm'_real; (3) apply float_text_repr only at sm'.format/show top level (lines ~1058, ~1081). Option 3 misses floats nested in records, so it fails the acceptance rule. Decision rule: take (1) if a probe shows a prototype instance resolving from a later module inside real code, else (2).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_13 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 13u32, "2026-10-22T10:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:11 main: the queued gate job was granted a compile window in the gap between tibia's PR-21 and D21 live-gui windows (about 06:00-06:10), so it ran into tibia's block. Stopped it and released its window by hand (job stop left it held); no gate had finished, and tokens.spi and the lib copies are unchanged. Requeue only after tibia's explicit 'block end' (memory live-runs-alone, updated).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_14 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 14u32, "2026-10-22T14:03Z", "main", "2026-10-22 10:03 main: wiring decided by measurement instead of a probe build. Option (1) is out: no prototype in the corpus has an instance on a primitive type (grep: 0 hits for instance ... f64/f32/i32/string/bool), so a float prototype would need a marker nominal hack. Option (2) is clean once two facts hold. Core's sm module (|core-, loaded before every lib module) has portable index/length/slice over compiler ops (!!!!StringIndex/Length/Slice). String concat ++# lives in sm'_real itself, but the compiler's StaticStringConcat op is lowered by every backend (C, Rust, F#, Lua, Gleam, Wasm, Zig, ... at 163533-177087). So new lib module float_text.spi (%TEMP%\\lib-float-repr, listed before sm'_prototypes, gate queued) holds float_text_repr written only against core sm plus !!!!StaticStringConcat, and format_real's float arms can call float_text.float_text_repr. sm'.float_text_repr is dropped from the copy, one record per fact; the copy's sm'.spi equals the tree again. Verified without a build: a Python mirror of the module's exact logic passes the 23 inline cases, maps 20k random floats' repr to itself, and maps 30k floats rendered as C shortest %g, F# R (E exponent) and JS JSON to Python repr with 0 mismatches. Next after the gate: wrap the float arms in sm'_real format_real, then measure show_floats per the 05:50 acceptance rule.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_15 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 15u32, "2026-10-22T14:05Z", "main", "2026-10-22 10:05 main: wiring staged in the same lib copy. sm'_real.spir format_real gets one insertion instead of an edit per arm: after the per-backend inl s : string = backend_switch ..., a rebinding inl s : string = match b with (_ : f32) | (_ : f64) => float_text.float_text_repr s | _ => s, before s is appended. So every backend's float text, which is already shortest digits, is re-laid in one canonical (Python repr) layout, nested floats in records included. That satisfies the acceptance rule option (3) failed. The copy's sm'_real is otherwise the tree file (anchor matched once). The open question, whether real code can call a module function here, is answered by the queued sm'.spi gate on the copy. If it cannot, the fallback is to make float_text_repr real-callable (a real-side wrapper).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_16 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 16u32, "2026-10-22T15:43Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:43 main: landing split. The format_real wiring changes the float text every consumer prints (x notebooks with expected outputs, eoie, tibia), so it does not ride the routine lib landing. land-after-gates.ps1 now lands float_text.spi plus the manifest by default (additive, unused until wired); the sm'_real.spir wiring needs -FloatWiring and goes in only after a consumer check: Cells --gate over lib plus x's notebook expectations and eoie and tibia builds diffed for float text. A changed expectation counts as intended only if it is the old layout of the same shortest digits.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_17 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 17u32, "2026-10-22T15:44Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:44 main: consumer facts from tibia (message 11:4x): tibia reads no lib float text. Predict rows are i32 through ++#?, score mains print via printf, and the arena probes format floats through host string.format. So the wiring should move no tibia output, and the arena probes and predict still join the consumer check as a negative control.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_18 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 18u32, "2026-10-22T15:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:46 main: float consumer census, read-only (scratchpad float-consumer-check.ps1, list kept under spiral-bin runs lab-outputs\\FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL): 20 files hold float-shaped expected text. 16 are lib modules with inline tests (base, benchmark, convert, crypto, date_time, lustre, math, near_wallet, parsing, physics, process, rust, sm', testing, xml, bench_kit), and 4 are in x: apps/client e2e/thread-oauth1.expected.json, oauth1.spi, wire.spi, xc.spi. eoie spec and tibia: none. The wiring check therefore reruns those lib tests (Cells --gate) and x's client e2e and notebooks, and applies the shortest-digits rule to every changed line. Landing coordination: tibia holds D37 for the gap, and eoie release-F (heavy slot 2 since about 11:39) reads lib until its regen, so the additive landing waits for eoie's \"past regen\".")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_19 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 19u32, "2026-10-22T16:05Z", "main", "2026-10-22 12:05 main: GATE GREEN: float_text.spi on %TEMP%\\lib-float-repr (lib_gate: tests passed 1 of 1, type errors 0; the test covers 23 layouts on rust/fsharp/typescript/gleam). So the core-only module (core sm index/length/slice + !!!!StaticStringConcat) compiles and agrees on those backends. The wiring probe (sm'.spi gate on the same copy, whose format_real calls float_text) runs next in the batch.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_20 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 20u32, "2026-10-23T09:03Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:03 main: float_text.spi LANDED with the combined lib at 04:40:05. The tree file equals the gated copy %TEMP%\\lib-float-repr (AE051C7817186F20; gate: 1 of 1 tests passed over 23 layouts on rust/fsharp/typescript/gleam, 0 type errors). Open: wiring sm'.format_real to float_text (the probe gate on the same copy), then backend adoption against show_floats. The lab's two Delphi float probes stay for that.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_21 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 21u32, "2026-10-23T16:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 12:25 main: correction to the 10-22 12:05 note: the wiring probe never ran. lab-outputs\\FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL holds only the Delphi float probes and float-shaped-files.txt, and %TEMP%\\lib-float-repr's sm'.spi has no float_text reference, so format_real -> float_text is still undrafted. It stays out of today's lib landing on purpose: it changes every format_real caller's text, and it shares sm'.spi with LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM, which a failed wiring gate would hold back too. Next: draft the wiring on its own lib copy after today's land, gate sm'.spi plus show_floats per backend.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_22 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 22u32, "2026-10-23T16:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 12:27 main: located the wiring site: sm'.spi's format_real section is empty and format goes through real show_real; the float arms live in lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir (the TODO FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL at line 615, then one shortest-text arm per backend at 684-738: Lua, F#, Python, Rust, TypeScript, Zig, C, ...). Wiring = each arm keeps its shortest round-trip digits and passes them through float_text_repr for the layout. Open design question before drafting: real-dialect arms calling a .spi function (float_text) need a forall/term bridge like the .spir notes in spiral-macro-gotchas describe; probe that call shape on one backend first.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_23 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 23u32, "2026-10-23T16:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 12:32 main: design question answered by probe (scratch real-calls-spi, installed compiler, F#): a .spir function can call a .spi function directly, no forall bridge needed. show.spir's 'inl show (x : string) : string = text.twice x' called from main as 'real show.show ab' with ab = dyn 'ab' compiles to 'let v1 : string = (v0 + v0)'. Only trap: dyn written INSIDE a real expression is the typed forall dyn (Cannot apply a forall with a term); bind runtime values outside real. So the sm'_real.spir float arms can call float_text.float_text_repr on their shortest digits as-is; float_text must load before sm'_real in package.spiproj.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_24 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 24u32, "2026-10-23T16:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 12:35 main: wiring drafted, not applied: %TEMP%\\lib-float-wire\\apply.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> [-Apply] adds ONE rebinding in sm'_real.spir format_real's p, right after the per-backend 's' switch and before the append switch: 'inl s : string = match b with | (_ : f32) | (_ : f64) => float_text.float_text_repr s | _ => s', so every backend's shortest digits (C/Lua/Kotlin/Swift 1e+02, Rust/TS/Python/Lean/Zig 100.0) get one Python-repr layout, NaN and +-Infinity pass through; it also drops the resolved TODO. Dry run on the tree lib: both anchors match once. Gate plan (after today's lib landing, on its own copy): Cells --gate on sm'.spi, float_text.spi and every lib file whose tests format floats, expect pinned float texts on C/Lua/Kotlin/Swift to change to the repr layout (review each by hand, no blind --accept), plus show_floats per backend.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_25 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 25u32, "2026-10-23T23:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:53 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 50. B was decided on 10-22, float_text.spi landed on 10-23 at 04:40, and the format_real wiring is drafted (%TEMP%\\lib-float-wire\\apply.ps1). Its gate, main-float-wire-gate, is queued in %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\requeue-after-block.ps1 chain A for tibia's 'block end'. The landing still follows the 05:50 and 11:43 acceptance rules: consumer check, then a hand review of every changed float text.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_26 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 26u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming, corrected 00:13: main-float-wire-gate finished 0 at 21:53. Of the 05:50 acceptance rule, (b) holds: the one sm'.spi failure, replace_regex on gleam, also fails on the unchanged lib (gleam/regexp is not in Cells' Gleam deps), and base/benchmark/crypto/date_time/listm'/parsing/reflection/float_text pass. (a) was NOT met: show_floats only checks values whose text is the same in every layout. So 'landing is the next milestone' was premature.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_27 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 27u32, "2026-10-24T04:12Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:12 main: closing the acceptance rule's gaps. (a) %TEMP%\\lib-float-wire\\apply.ps1 now also adds sm' test show_floats_in_python_repr_layout over the 05:50 values (0.0001, 1e-05, 100.0, 123.45, 1e15, 1e16, 1.5e22, 1e-07, 0.1, -2.5), expecting Python repr text, on the 10 backends show_floats runs on (gleam, lua, fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript, c, zig, delphi, lean): job main-float-wire-gate-2. (11:43 consumer check) the census's other lib modules run as gate-3 (convert, lustre, math, physics, process, testing, xml) and gate-4 (rust/rust.spi, bench/parsing/bench_kit.spi). near_wallet exists only as generated output, so it has no Spiral test. x's 4 float-bearing files (thread-oauth1.expected.json, oauth1.spi, wire.spi, xc/xc.spi) hold no exponent or extreme float text, so no x expectation should move; tibia reads no lib float text (11:44). (c) x and tibia are told before any land. gate.ps1 now comma-splits -Files (pwsh -File binds one value to [string[]]).")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_28 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 28u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:34 (subagent): blocked: all arms measured; option A/B/C is the user's decision -> next: the user's pick; then per-arm rules, Delphi first | lap 6 13:49 (subagent): option B formatter: 9/10 values = Python repr; ffExponent guard needed -> next: user decision | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: 9/10 Delphi match; ffExponent guard needed for -2.5 -> next: write the guard")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_29 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 29u32, "2026-10-25T20:14Z", "main", "10-25 16:14 main: the float_text land failed at 13:33 (lands-4 step main-land-float-text-tight-2) on a sharing lock: WriteAllText could not open lib/spiral/float_text.spi, which another process held. That's the same minute as fiscal's half-land, and it ran on the pre-14:06 land.ps1 without staging. Nothing was written: the tree file still equals the gate's base (6366740FB9C2), so the GREEN verdict (20261025-1303) still holds. The land is requeued alone behind lands-4 on the new stage-then-commit land.ps1, whose 20x3 s retries cover this lock. Requeued as job-1792959121132.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_30 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 30u32, "2026-10-26T01:37Z", "main", "10-25 21:37 main: the lib float_text tightening slice (gate name LIB-FLOAT-TEXT-TIGHT) LANDED via main-land-float-text-tight-4, on the reworked land: staged 1 lib file, caught a reader gap on the 2-second sampling, committed float_text.spi C07481AD262D281E. Its two earlier lands starved 180+ min on the old 2-sample guard.")
inl rename_bless () = Task ("RENAME-BLESS", "Mass rename of 'bless' (user 2026-10-18 10:43, done the same way as the 10-18 harness->matrix/live/cli_driver rename): replace the term with 'accept' everywhere it is current: test.ps1 -Bless -> -Accept, blessed/blessable -> accepted/acceptable, bless/re-bless -> accept/re-accept in AGENTS.md, NEXT.md, lanes/*/PLAN.md and FRONTIER.md, matrix.psd1, scripts/workflow.ps1, eoie FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW.md, the agents' memory and active backlog titles. Historical notes and run labels stay as written.", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "rename,matrix,docs", "current", "current")
inl tree_pages_every_repo () = Task ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", "Every repo publishes its own dir-tree-html page on gh-pages, and the page works on phones in portrait and landscape (user 2026-10-18 11:05)", TaskKind, P0, D2, 98u32, Blocked, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,pages,dir-tree-html,mobile,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tree_pages_every_repo () = Planning ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_1 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-18T15:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:31 main-thread. Audit: spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, dir-tree-html and i574n.github.io publish (Pages built). eoie, blog and x had no publish step at all. tibia had one, but its push job needs every matrix leg and the Windows leg fails (GPU twins, a lost runner), so the push never ran. All four have no gh-pages branch and no Pages site (404). Fixed in the tree, uncommitted: eoie/blog/x get scripts/publish.ps1 (spiral publish-tree), /dist/ in .gitignore (blog gets a new .gitignore), and a build-job step that publishes and uploads dist on the Ubuntu leg if: !cancelled(), plus a push job (needs build, if: !cancelled() && push; peaceiris to gh-pages). tibia gets the same !cancelled() gating. spiral publish-tree.ps1 gains -Exclude; eoie passes .cache, whose local gitignored repro workspaces made rsync copy 61k+ folders. Verified locally: blog 19 files in 6 s, x 80 in 6 s, eoie 2,033 in 25 s; all four workflows parse. Mobile: the published page had no viewport meta, so a phone laid it out at 980 px at 0.40x (15 px names showed at about 6 px; Chromium autosized text to 41 px with a 1,552 px scroll). dir_tree_html.spi now has the viewport meta, text-size-adjust 100%, line-height 1.6, overflow-wrap anywhere, and a max-width 600px block (16/12 px, tighter indents). Playwright iPhone 13 emulation, Chromium + WebKit, portrait + landscape: 390/750 px layout at 1.0x, no horizontal scroll even on the deep tibia tree (widest link 382 of 390 px); build.ps1 --self-test passes. Also fixed: the renderer skips symlinks/junctions (DirEntry::file_type().is_symlink()); a direct --dir run on tibia's root hung on its deps junctions. Left: after the user pushes, run the first deploy, then enable Pages on eoie/tibia/blog/x (source gh-pages /) if GitHub doesn't do it on the first gh-pages push; the dir-tree-html change reaches every page once dir-tree-html is pushed, because each CI clones and builds it.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_2 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:36 main-thread: the user's 15:29Z push gave eoie and x a startup_failure ('workflow file issue'). Their .github/workflows/actions.lock pinned only the actions used before, and GitHub refuses a workflow that uses an unpinned action. Regenerated with gh actions-lock in both repos (added actions/upload-artifact@v5.0.0, download-artifact@v6.0.0 and peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v4.1.0 with their commit SHAs; additions only, LF kept). Needs a commit and push. blog has no lockfile, and tibia's already listed every action, so both started normally.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_3 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-18T15:48Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:48 main-thread: blog's first run with the publish step (39140753644) ran the step on the Ubuntu leg as designed, then failed inside dir-tree-html's build.ps1. It builds with cargo +nightly-2025-11-01 but read the target dir with a plain cargo metadata, and on runners whose default toolchain is stable (blog, x) or 1.88.0 (eoie) that fails: Cargo.toml uses the nightly-only profile-rustflags. alphabet, dice, polyglot, spiral and tibia pin nightly, so they never hit it. Fixed in the dir-tree-html tree: metadata uses the same +nightly-2025-11-01, and an empty target dir now throws a clear RUST-FAILED instead of 'Cannot find path /release/dir-tree-html'. Push dir-tree-html before rerunning blog/x/eoie. Separately, blog's Windows leg failed on 'File main has a type error' in engine/html.spi against spiral main; that's being attributed to the lib raw-string conversion (sm'), git-0e's lane.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_4 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 4u32, "2026-10-18T16:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:13 main-thread: tibia is the first of the four through. Run 39140743725 (the 15:29Z push carrying the !cancelled() gating) went green on both legs plus push and created the gh-pages branch at 15:51Z. GitHub did not turn Pages on by itself, so I enabled it via the API (source gh-pages /, https://i574n.github.io/tibia/; on the user's request to publish every repo's tree; the repo is public). eoie/blog/x next, once their pushes (lockfiles, dir-tree-html cargo fix, html.spi) land and their push jobs create gh-pages.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_5 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 5u32, "2026-10-18T16:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:19 main-thread: the .github repo's page was stuck at its 10-17 deploy. Run 39137525444 had Ubuntu green and Windows red after 1h20m with no log (most likely a lost runner, as in tibia's earlier run), and its push job needed every leg, so it was skipped. Same fix as tibia, uncommitted: upload on the Ubuntu leg if !cancelled(), and push if !cancelled() && event push. Before, the push job had no if at all, so it also deployed on pull requests. workflow.livemd publishes last, so dist exists only when the Ubuntu leg got that far. spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot and dir-tree-html keep deploy-on-all-green; they publish today. dice, polyglot and alphabet were rerun at 12:14 local, because their last deploys predate the dir-tree-html push (no viewport meta yet).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_6 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:33 main-thread: spiral run 39140706896 (f5e487e0) is green on both legs, and its 16:32Z deploy carries the viewport meta (live page checked). Pages now on the mobile head: spiral, tibia, dir-tree-html. dice/polyglot/alphabet reruns are pending. eoie, blog, x and .github wait for the user's push.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_7 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 7u32, "2026-10-18T19:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:22 main-thread: gh-pages branches exist now for eoie, blog and x (first deploys after the user's pushes), so Pages was enabled through the API (POST repos/i574n/<repo>/pages, source gh-pages /): i574n.github.io/eoie, /blog, /x. Next: verify each live page (viewport meta, README section once dir-tree-html's closer fix is pushed)")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_8 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 8u32, "2026-10-18T19:23Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:23 main-thread: live check: i574n.github.io/eoie 200 (197,903 bytes), /blog 200 (2,766), /x 200 (15,043), each with the viewport meta. Every repo with a tree page now serves it. Left: the next deploys pick up dir-tree-html's README section once its closer fix is committed")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_9 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: live: i574n.github.io/dir-tree-html serves the README section (200, class readme present), so the closer fix reached gh-pages; alphabet's 06:48 publish failed in dir-tree-html exit 3 on its Korean README (UTF-8 slice), fixed in the tree today (DIR-TREE-README) and waiting for the user's commit Next: after the next alphabet push, confirm i574n.github.io/alphabet shows its README; then Done")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_10 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:13 lap 2 grain: live sweep of the 8 pages: x, blog, dir-tree-html 200 with the README section; alphabet, dice, polyglot, spiral 200 but no README section (their last gh-pages deploys are 10-18 16:49-19:00Z, before dir-tree-html's README renderer, and they deploy only on all-green, which none has had since); eoie REGRESSED to 404: its 10-19 06:49Z run (39212135640) hit dir-tree-html exit 3 in publish (same renderer failure as alphabet's Korean README), yet the push job (if !cancelled) deployed the half-built dist: gh-pages now holds the rsync copy (_github, _gitignore, src...) with no index.html. Fix applied in the tree: spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 now calls the renderer directly (Invoke-Block exits the process on failure, so no cleanup could run) and on a non-zero exit removes dist and throws, so upload has nothing and the push job fails instead of replacing the last good page; every repo's scripts/publish.ps1 goes through this one script (grep: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, eoie, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, tibia, x), and CI clones spiral, so it needs the user's spiral push. Parse-checked, not run. The renderer bug itself is DIR-TREE-README (fixed in the dir-tree-html tree, uncommitted). Next: after the user's pushes of spiral + dir-tree-html, re-sweep all 8 pages for 200 + README section; eoie must come back from 404")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_11 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:51 lap 3 sweep (10 pages): 200 with viewport meta and @media rules on alphabet, dice, spiral, polyglot, x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia (README section on x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia); 404 on eoie AND on i574n.github.io/.github/: the .github repo has the same regression as eoie, its 10-18 23:32Z deploy (from the red 22:13Z run) holds the rsync copy (_devcontainer, _github, docs, README.md ...) with no index.html, while Pages reports status built. So the half-dist push hit 2 repos; the publish-tree.ps1 fix from lap 2 (remove dist and throw on a renderer failure) covers both, since i574n.github/scripts/publish.ps1 calls the same spiral script. Both recover on their next green-enough push after the user pushes spiral and dir-tree-html. Done criterion unchanged: all 10 pages 200 with the README section. Next: re-sweep after the user's next push wave")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_12 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:14 lap 5: re-sweep unchanged (8 pages 200 with viewport, README section on x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia; eoie and .github still 404), because the fixes are still unpushed (spiral scripts/publish-tree.ps1 and the dir-tree-html renderer are modified in the trees). New evidence that the push alone recovers both 404 pages: the 10-18 renderer (target/release/deps/dir_tree_html.exe) exits 3 on the README.md of eoie, i574n.github and alphabet, and the fixed 09:48 build exits 0 on all three in 9-37 ms with a README section (scratch dirs holding only each README). Done when: user pushes dir-tree-html + spiral, the next green runs of eoie and i574n.github redeploy, all 10 pages 200 with README.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_13 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:32 lap 6: checked the phone half on a live page (tibia, served by the current renderer): it has a viewport meta, @media (max-width: 600px) rules for portrait, overflow-wrap: anywhere for long names and overflow-x: auto for code blocks. Landscape phones (about 640-930 px wide) get the desktop layout capped at max-width 900px, so nothing scrolls the page sideways. No renderer change is needed for portrait or landscape. The item is only waiting on the push that brings eoie and .github back from 404.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_14 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:54 lap 7 grain: the Done check is one call now. lab/TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO/sweep.ps1 fetches all 10 pages and requires status 200, the README section, a viewport meta and the 600px portrait rules. It prints TREE-PAGES-OK and exits 0 only when all 10 pass; otherwise it names the pages still missing. First run: 4 of 10 ok (x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia). alphabet, dice, spiral and polyglot lack the README section; eoie and .github are 404. spiral HEAD is still 668568ac (no push since 02:48). Done = this script printing TREE-PAGES-OK after the push wave.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_15 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 15u32, "2026-10-19T16:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:26 lap 8 grain: sweep at 12:25 unchanged (4 of 10; spiral origin still 668568ac). Confirmed the 404 cause directly instead of by inference: gh api git trees of gh-pages for eoie (deploy 06:55Z) and i574n/.github list the rsync copy (.nojekyll, README.md, _github ...) with no index.html, while x's root has index.html; both sites report Pages status built from gh-pages /. The unpushed publish-tree.ps1 renderer-exit check plus the fixed renderer recover both. Done = sweep.ps1 TREE-PAGES-OK after the push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_16 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 16u32, "2026-10-19T16:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:53 lap 9 grain: sweep.ps1 now adds a heads column per repo (gh-pages and main from git ls-remote), so the Done evidence names the deploy commit behind each page, and a still-failing page after a push shows at once whether its gh-pages moved (tested on eoie and x: eoie gh-pages=f0f1dfb8 main=40e71ade 404, x gh-pages=eefc4fcc 200). Remote mains at 12:53: spiral 668568ac, eoie 40e71ade, .github 9dd1144e, dice a0502e18, alphabet 08e566fb, polyglot c187d421, no push since the morning")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_17 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain: remote heads re-read (git ls-remote): spiral main 668568ac, eoie 40e71ade, .github 9dd1144e unchanged since 12:53, so still no push; recorded the other sibling heads as the pre-push reference: dice main a0502e18 gh-pages 5628355f, alphabet 08e566fb / cdd28acb, polyglot c187d421 / e5553451. Next: sweep.ps1 after the user's push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_18 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:49 lap 11 grain, scope check of sweep.ps1's repo list against GitHub: all 10 swept repos have a gh-pages branch; of the other local checkouts, tictactoe also has one but is not swept, and futuresales and The-Spiral-Language have none. tictactoe's last commit is a473452 of 2023-03-14 (dormant, pre-Spiral-2 era), so it stays out of the sweep on purpose (its gh-pages is not a tree page and nothing deploys it); recorded so the Done row can say every active repo. Next: sweep.ps1 after the user's push")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_19 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 19u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:49 verified the tictactoe claim: its gh-pages head (fetched with a depth-1 fetch, no other ref touched) is a 2023-03-14 deploy of a47345 holding .nojekyll, assets and index.html, i.e. the old app build, not a dir-tree page")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_20 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 20u32, "2026-10-19T18:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:14 lap 12 sweep.ps1: 4 of 10 pages pass (x, blog, dir-tree-html, tibia). alphabet, dice, spiral, polyglot serve 200 without the README section (older deploys, the fix waits on the push). eoie and .github return 404 although Pages is built from gh-pages: both gh-pages branches hold a copy of the repo tree with dotfiles renamed (_github, _gitignore) and no index.html at the root. Next: find the deploy step that writes them")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_21 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 21u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 13: the README-less pages of spiral, dice, alphabet and polyglot are stale deploys rather than a code gap: spiral's live page has README.md links but no class=readme section (x's has it), their last gh-pages deploys (10-18) predate dir-tree-html's readme commit eaba51c reaching main, and the workflow clones and builds dir-tree-html main fresh on every run. So any new deploy of those 4 repos (their next push, or a manual dispatch by the user) should turn them green; only eoie and .github depend on the unpushed publish-tree.ps1 fix")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_22 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 22u32, "2026-10-19T18:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:45 correction to the 14:39 note: spiral's last deploy (19:00:07Z) came from run 39151274415, created 17:48:35Z, before dir-tree-html eaba51c reached main at 18:51:23Z, so that page is a stale render as said; dice, alphabet and polyglot all render through spiral's scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 (fresh clone of main). But every gh-pages run of these 4 repos since 18:51Z has failed, so the pages refresh only on a successful run: the item's PIPELINES-GREEN dependency, not just any redeploy")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_23 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 23u32, "2026-10-21T22:42Z", "main", "18:42 live check (Invoke-WebRequest on i574n.github.io/<repo>/): dice, polyglot, x, tibia and eoie serve the README section (class=readme) with a viewport meta, so those 5 are done. alphabet and spiral are still stale (no readme section) because their gh-pages runs are red: spiral on the Linux F# warm exit mask (fixed in test.ps1 tonight, uncommitted) and alphabet on the Zig-only array typing in hangul (probe pending), both in PIPELINES-GREEN notes 16-20. They refresh on the first green run after the user's next push; no page-side work is left.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_24 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 24u32, "2026-10-22T02:38Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:38 x: pipeline review (user 22:2x: \"are our dir-tree-html pipeline good? see what could be improved\"). Measured, no repo changed.\n(1) WRONG CONTENT, platform-dependent. publish-tree.ps1's rsync allowlist works on Windows but not in Linux CI. A local dry run on spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/samples/abi_external copies main.rs/main.spi/package.spiproj and drops main.c/main.pas. In CI, x's page holds 351 files x doesn't track (apps/sim/_build/**/ebin/*.beam, .mix/compile.elixir) plus 18 .ex files, none on the allowlist; x CI run 39367603402's publish log lists them. Likely cause: pwsh on Linux globs the bare '*' of the final --exclude '*' into file names (CYGWIN=noglob only covers Windows). So Linux pages are \"everything not excluded\" (build outputs leak), Windows pages are \"allowlist only\" (by the list, spiral would lose 4,600 of 10,234 tracked files: .c 534, .zig 467, .lean 458, .kt 452, .swift 451, .lua 449, .wat 444...; x 19 .ex; tibia 33 .tsv + 3 .lua).\n(2) FIX MEASURED: publish exactly the tracked files with git checkout-index -a --prefix=dist/ (CI index == HEAD). Locally: spiral 10,234 files in 11.3 s (a per-file pwsh copy took 201 s; git archive 91.6 s on Windows tar and skips staged files), x 132 files in 0.4 s; dotfile rename pass 2.2 s; renderer 6.3 s on spiral, 0.1 s on x, exit 0. This drops rsync (and its choco install on Windows), both pattern lists and the platform split, and lab/ and build outputs can never leak.\n(3) DOTFILE RENAME probably unneeded: https://i574n.github.io/x/.nojekyll answers 200, so Pages serves leading-dot files with .nojekyll. One test deploy with a dot-directory (.github/) settles it; then links keep real names.\n(4) STALE PAGES from coupling: the page deploys only after the repo's full build on the Ubuntu leg (x 508 s of workflow.ps1; tibia init 282 s + build 203 s), and spiral and alphabet have served 10-18 pages for 3 days because their runs are red. A tree page needs no tests: a separate pages job (checkout, renderer, checkout-index, render, deploy) on every push removes that failure class, and this item's remaining wait with it.\n(5) GH-PAGES HISTORY: peaceiris runs without force_orphan, so every deploy commits another copy: spiral 71 gh-pages commits (10,160 files), polyglot 177 (repo 316 MB), dice 88. force_orphan: true keeps one commit, or better, actions/upload-pages-artifact + actions/deploy-pages deploy with no gh-pages branch at all and drop the third-party action (needs Pages source set to GitHub Actions per repo via the API).\n(6) RENDERER SUPPLY: every repo's CI clones dir-tree-html main and cargo-builds it (4 s compile on x, plus a nightly toolchain sync), so a renderer bug on main breaks every page at once (the 10-19 Korean README exit 3 hit alphabet, eoie and .github). A versioned release binary with a sha256 pin (build as fallback) makes pages reproducible and upgrades deliberate.\n(7) 1,000-LINE RULE: generated dir_tree_html.rs is 2,774 lines (spi 738); under the user's 10-21 rule it splits into crates.\n(8) DONE CHECK IN LAB: the sweep lives in spiral/lab/TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO/sweep.ps1. Landed as a post-deploy step of the pages job (GET the page, require 200 + index + README section), every deploy verifies itself and the folder goes.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_25 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 25u32, "2026-10-22T06:28Z", "main", "02:28 main: row (8) landed in my lane's repos (x's placement advice 02:2x): spiral, polyglot, dice and alphabet gh-pages.yml push jobs now write $GITHUB_SHA to gh-pages/_deploy.txt before peaceiris. After it, a pwsh step 'verify the deploy is live' GETs https://i574n.github.io/<repo>/_deploy.txt (cache-busting query) every 15 s for up to 6 min until it equals GITHUB_SHA, then requires the index to carry the README section and the viewport meta. No new uses: (no actions.lock in these repos); YAML and the embedded script parse on all four (polyglot's 4-space indent kept). Live check today, read-only: spiral and alphabet pages LACK the README section because they are stale. gh-pages was last deployed 10-18 (spiral 19:00Z, alphabet 16:49Z) while main moved on 10-20, so their builds have been red since (PIPELINES-GREEN). dice and polyglot (deployed 10-20) pass both marks. The lab sweep is promoted as spiral/scripts/pages-sweep.ps1 (the cross-repo view, all 10 repos); lab folder deleted. x, eoie and tibia own their workflows and get the same step text to adopt.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_26 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 26u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: user push of spiral + dir-tree-html -> next: re-sweep 10 pages | lap 13b 14:45 (subagent2): CORRECTION with run evidence: spiral's 19:00:07Z deploy came from run 39151274415 created 17:48:35Z, before eaba51c reached main (18:51:23Z), so it is stale as claimed; dice/alphabet/polyglot all build through spiral's scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 (fresh clone of main), BUT every gh-pages run of the 4 repos since 18:51Z failed (spiral 39156066380, 39171208553, 39212057707, 39258592236; siblings 22:12Z and 06:48Z), so what refreshes the pages is a SUCCESSFUL run, i.e. the PIPELINES-GREEN dep, not any redeploy -> next: PIPELINES-GREEN, then sweep.ps1 | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked on PIPELINES-GREEN (every gh-pages run since 18:51Z failed); evidence and sweep ready -> next: a green run per repo, then sweep.ps1 | lap 16 16:06 (subagent2): no new runs: latest gh-pages runs still the failing ones (spiral 13:15Z schedule; dice/alphabet/polyglot 06:48Z push); eoie/.github last pages-build-deployment succeeded but deploys the index-less tree -> next: PIPELINES-GREEN + push | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked on PIPELINES-GREEN -> next: green deploys | lap 19 18:55 (subagent2): unchanged: no gh-pages run since 06:48Z on spiral/dice/alphabet/polyglot (all failed), so no fresh render yet -> next: PIPELINES-GREEN")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_27 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 27u32, "2026-10-24T21:17Z", "main", "main 17:2x correction to the 10-22 02:28 note: spiral/scripts/pages-sweep.ps1 does not exist. It is in no repo under C:\\home\\git, and git has no history for that path, so the promoted sweep was never committed and is gone (the lab copy was deleted with the folder). Per the generic-tools rule, the sweep comes back as an eoie command, not a script: for each repo, GET <owner>.github.io/<repo>/_deploy.txt and the index with a cache-busting query, then report the deployed sha vs main, the README section and the viewport meta. Status is unchanged: the 2% left is a green gh-pages run per repo (PIPELINES-GREEN).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_28 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 28u32, "2026-10-24T23:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:53 main correction to note 27: the sweep was not gone. PAGES-SWEEP-PLACE had moved it at 07:45 to dir-tree-html/pages-sweep.ps1, and the 17:2x search only probed <repo>\\scripts\\pages-sweep.ps1, so it missed a repo-root file. It now lives in i574n.github/scripts/pages-sweep.ps1 (see PAGES-SWEEP-PLACE note 2).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_29 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 29u32, "2026-10-25T08:41Z", "main", "10-25 04:41 main: sweep at 04:3x: 7 of 10 pages pass now that the sweep is right. eoie is renamed kit, served at /kit/; blog's tree is /blog/tree.html, with the blog at the root. pages-sweep.ps1 now has -TreePage @{ blog = 'tree.html' } and lists kit. Failing: spiral and alphabet are 200 but render without the README section (older deploys); .github is 404 because its gh-pages is the 10-18 deploy, from before the tree index (no index.html). All three wait on the same red, PIPELINES-GREEN note 31. HEAD's apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs is behind the uncommitted pack layer, so apps/spiral fails 'Unbound term variable: rust_type' in CI. .github's run 40167633861 (06:01Z) fails the same way, because it clones spiral main. One user commit of the compiler layer unblocks them; then rerun the sweep.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_every_repo_30 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO", 30u32, "2026-10-25T13:15Z", "main", "10-25 09:15 main groom: status Blocked, by the user's commit. spiral, alphabet and .github refresh on their first green gh-pages run, and that needs the uncommitted compiler layer (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs + tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs) in the commit; see PIPELINES-GREEN note 31. Resume: rerun i574n.github/scripts/pages-sweep.ps1 after that push.")
inl eoie_agile_note_edit () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT", "eoie agile has no way to fix a note (found 2026-10-18 by main-thread): two mistyped note times (LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS note 6, CELLS-GATE note 2) needed appended correction notes. Add note-edit <root> <id> <n> <text> (author and original timestamp kept, edit stamp recorded) in one call", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,friction", "current", "current")
inl cells_lab_package () = Task ("CELLS-LAB-PACKAGE", "Cells cannot run test blocks of a package under tibia/lab/dota-lua-bindings: every block fails with 'Owner of file main has an error. Location: package.spiproj. Package not loaded' (x3), even when package.spiproj is reduced to the same '|core-' and module list as tibia/src (which works). The compiler CLI (BuildSpiral -Backend Lua) compiles the same package, including its arena- cross reference to tibia apps/arena. A copy of the files outside the repo fails differently (ETY-UnboundModule on 'open testing'). Related: the parser rejects a qualified case with a named binding ('| last_hit.Hit index =>', column 24) while '| vitality.Slain _' parses.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,packages,dota", "current", "current")
