inl matrix_toolchain_identity () = Task ("MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY", "The oracle baseline records its native toolchain identity, and test.ps1 flags a run whose toolchain differs (found 2026-10-18: the user PATH puts scoop mingw 16.2/UCRT before gcc 15.2/msvcrt, so sessions resolved different CCs and git-0e's review could not be reproduced by an accept in another session)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP,SPIRAL-C-UCRT-ABORT-EXIT", "matrix,oracle,toolchain,determinism", "current", "current")
inl x_on_near () = Task ("X-ON-NEAR", "X rewritten as NEAR contracts with the sim as spec, driver and oracle (deferred, user 2026-10-18: too much for now): contract = SocialDB-style per-account storage (social.near) + NEP-145 storage deposits, Farcaster-style add/remove merge rules (remove wins, later timestamp wins, hash tie-break) as Spiral types; xc gains a NEAR site (free view reads over RPC, signed writes); feeds off chain in an indexer tested with the sim's reorder/duplicate faults, or on chain as paged views (NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW); copy designs not code (check licenses). Does not need SIM-IN-SPIRAL: the Elixir sim already emits the event stream (XSIM-EVENT-STREAM). Folds in X-NEAR-PROVENANCE", Epic, P4, D8, 0u32, Paused, "XSIM-EVENT-STREAM,NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS,NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW,NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES,X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", "x,near,sim,social", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_on_near () = Planning ("X-ON-NEAR", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_x_on_near_1 () = Note ("X-ON-NEAR", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 direction (user): iterate until feature parity with X while dogfooding everything (xc, sim, Cells, the compiler), keep a running list of NEAR gotchas as they surface, and stay Mastodon and Bluesky compatible (X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES), until a killer app arrives. Waits for the full machine: today CPU and memory go to spiral compiler maintenance, and the sandbox (near-workspaces/neard) needs WSL (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE: WSL 3 bounded jobs, memory=6GB, wsl --shutdown after). Only NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS and XSIM-EVENT-STREAM run without WSL")
inl note_of_x_on_near_2 () = Note ("X-ON-NEAR", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 technical design (from the 2026-10-18 review): sim reuse = spec (typed events post/reply/quote/edit-as-revision/delete/like/unlike, idempotent per event, causal buffering, commute across orders), driver (xsim.world scenario -> contract calls) and oracle (same expected state as the X stub); on NEAR one contract's receipts run in one order and final blocks never roll back, so delivery faults move to the indexer/reader side. Prior art: social.near SocialDB (per-account key-value store, poster pays storage), NEP-145 storage_deposit/withdraw/unregister, Farcaster message sets (CastAdd/Remove, ReactionAdd/Remove, LinkAdd/Remove; remove wins, later timestamp wins, hash tie-break), AT Protocol (signed per-user repos, relays, app views). Approximate costs: storage ~1 NEAR per 100 KB (1,000 posts at ~400 B lock ~4 NEAR, refunded on delete), a simple write ~5 Tgas ~0.0005 NEAR, 300 Tgas ceiling per call. Shape: shared Spiral domain crates under the 1,000-line gate linked into one cdylib; xc reads/writes GADTs gain a NEAR site (view calls over RPC, signed function calls, key in a secrets env file). Phases: 1 domain types + merge-rule commutation tests, 2 contract (SocialDB layout + NEP-145), 3 native replay of sim scenarios in near-sdk's mock, 4 xc NEAR site, 5 feeds (NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW or an off-chain indexer tested with the sim's reorder/duplicate faults), 6 sandbox in CI Linux")
inl note_of_x_on_near_3 () = Note ("X-ON-NEAR", 3u32, "2026-10-21T01:45Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:45 x-lane backlog refactor: Planned -> Paused to match the user's 2026-10-18 deferral, so it leaves the ready queue of agile next; back to Planned when the user starts NEAR work")
inl xsim_event_stream () = Task ("XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", "Promote the sim's --save delivery logs to a documented, versioned event stream (post/reply/edit/delete/like/unlike with users, ids and revision sources, key-sorted JSON lines) plus a drift-guarded fixture in x, so any implementation replays the same scenario and compares with the sim's expected state: the NEAR contract mock, the Spiral sim port, other clients", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,sim,fixtures", "current", "current")
inl near_mocked_unit_tests () = Task ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", "Run NEAR contract logic natively on Windows through near-sdk's unit-testing mock (testing_env!, VMContextBuilder, env::used_gas) as Spiral test blocks, starting with dice and chat (mirror in alphabet): the fast loop needs neither the sandbox nor WSL, measures gas per method, and the sandbox runs stay on CI Linux (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE)", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "", "near,dice,polyglot,testing", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_mocked_unit_tests () = Planning ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_1 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:53Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:53 helper-lane lap 1: mapped the path for test blocks: both contracts pin near-sdk =5.11.0 (dice/contract and polyglot/apps/chat/contract Cargo.toml, borsh 1.5, indexmap =2.11.4), and near-sdk's mock (testing_env!, VMContextBuilder) needs the unit-testing feature. Cells passes ///> rust -d deps as --deps to the spiral CLI, whose cargo writer (apps/spiral/spiral.spi:309 and :912) turns name[feat] into name={version='*',features=[...]} and name=ver into a pinned version, but a token cannot carry both, so -d near-sdk[unit-testing] would resolve the newest near-sdk instead of the contracts' 5.11.0. Smallest unblock: accept name=version[features] in builders.ex parse_dep (Cells) and in spiral.spi's dep writer, then a first dice test block ///> rust -d near-sdk=5.11.0[unit-testing] -d borsh=1.5 calling roll_within_bounds under testing_env!.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_2 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:36 helper-lane lap 2: correction of lap 1 (no code change needed): Cells builders.ex dep_arg passes a -d token through raw when its version part starts with an opening brace or the token holds a quote, and apps/spiral/spiral.spi's cargo writer copies any token containing = verbatim into [dependencies] (lines 303-309). TOML accepts single-quoted literal strings, so a pinned crate with features is already expressible as one token without spaces: ///> rust -d near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} -d borsh=1.5, which becomes near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} in the generated Cargo.toml. The first dice mock test block can therefore be written today; what remains is the test itself (testing_env! with VMContextBuilder around roll_within_bounds) and its first cargo build of near-sdk, which is heavy.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_3 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:20Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:20 helper-lane lap 3: picked the first target by reading dice_contract.spi: roll_within_bounds is pure and already has two runnable test blocks, while new, contribute_seed and generate_random_number read near.random_seed, block_timestamp, signer_account_id and predecessor_account_id (lines 97-113), so generate_random_number is the first that needs a mocked context. Wrote it as lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/mocked_test_block.spi: ///> rust -c -d near-sdk={version='=5.11.0',features=['unit-testing']} -d near-token, a mocked () that calls near_sdk::testing_env with VMContextBuilder (random_seed [7u8; 32], a fixed block_timestamp; the macro bang written as a doubled bang inside the single-quoted macro), then two draws under the same context must be equal and positive. The explicit near-sdk token takes precedence, since apps/spiral adds near-sdk=5.11.0 only when no near-sdk dep is given (spiral.spi 293-301). run.ps1 appends the block to dice/contract/dice_contract.spi, runs only that test through Cells, and restores the file either way. HEAVY QUEUE (first near-sdk unit-testing build, about 10 min).")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_4 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T22:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:18 helper-lane lap 6: ran the queued row (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/run.ps1; dice_contract.spi patched temporarily and restored, git status clean). The mocked test never compiled, because dice's contract itself no longer type-checks on the current tree: dice_contract.spi:117:12, [ ... ] |> am'.to_vec, Unification failure, Got array (vec u8) -> vec (vec u8), Expected array_base _ -> vec _. Since the core v1 switch, an array literal is the nominal array el, and lib am'.to_vec still takes array_base. Reported to main as a core-switch consumer breakage. It is blocked until dice (and its alphabet mirror) or lib am' is adapted.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_5 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:31 helper-lane: after main fixed the three am'.to_vec sites in dice_contract.spi, I reran with the process compiler (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/contract-run.ps1, contract-gate-20261019-1829.log). All 7 contract tests fail at rust -c with the same type error, now in the mounted dice package: dice/lib/dice.spi 373-375, where main ;[] |> ignore in the Rust/Python/TypeScript arms fails with Got array _, Expected array_base string. It is the same core v1 array-literal migration, so it is not the contract build. The mocked test fails for the same reason. Reported to main; rerun after dice.spi is adapted.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_6 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T23:09Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:09 helper-lane: progress after main's dice migration. The contract gate passes 7 of 7 (contract-gate-20261019-1841.log). Three findings for the mocked test (lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS/mocked_test_block.spi): (1) Cells' builder tokenizer strips quotes, so the crate spec must be quoted whole (-d near-sdk with its version and unit-testing feature inside double quotes). Done, and spiral rust now gets the exact spec. (2) As a contract (rust -c, wasm32), near_sdk::testing_env does not exist (E0433), as near-sdk gates its unit-testing helpers off wasm, so mocked tests need a native rust target. Done, the ///> line is now rust without -c. (3) The native path's toolchain is rustc 1.88.0-nightly, and near-sys 0.2.14 (pulled by near-sdk 5.11.0) requires rustc 1.93, so cargo refuses. Blocked on main's toolchain pack (rustup update), not on code. dice_contract.spi is restored after each run.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_7 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T23:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:11 helper-lane: unblock condition (per main): the rustc used by the native Cells/spiral rust path must itself go to 1.93 or later. That is the pinned 1.88.0-nightly toolchain, and a rustup self-update does not move it. Alternatively, pin near-sys below 0.2.14 in the test's -d specs (for example -d near-sys with a version below 0.2.14 next to near-sdk), if near-sdk 5.11.0 accepts that range. The toolchain pack moved to 2026-10-20.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_8 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T23:58Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:58 helper-lane: the near-sys pin route is closed. With -d near-sys pinned to =0.2.13 (run-20261019-1942.log), cargo reports near-sys 0.2.13 also requires rustc 1.93, so only bumping the native toolchain unblocks the test. The test block keeps the pin out of the next run (to be removed from mocked_test_block.spi). dice_contract.spi was restored by the run.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_9 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 9u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 10 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS until the item lands.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_10 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T08:09Z", "main", "04:09 main: the 10-19 blocker is gone. dice/contract/dice_contract.spi type-checks and compiles to Rust on today's installed compiler (180 KB, exit 0; compiled into TEMP, and dice's tracked dice_contract.rs is unchanged). The 117:12 array vs array_base error was fixed in dice by the '|> am'.base' after the ;[ ... ] literal (commit 4218032, 10-19). This item's 'Blocked' reason (the contract not type-checking) no longer holds; its runners (contract-run.ps1, run.ps1, now writing into the run cache) can rerun when a Cells window is free.")
inl note_of_near_mocked_unit_tests_11 () = Note ("NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: run.ps1 (first near-sdk unit-testing build) queued -> next: read the mocked test result | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): run.ps1 queued -> next: read the mocked test | lap 7 19:09 (subagent): contract gate 7/7; mocked test fixed twice (quoting, native target), now blocked on native rustc 1.88 < near-sys 1.93 -> next: after the toolchain pack, rerun run.ps1")
inl near_feed_paged_view () = Task ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", "Gas-limit spike for an on-chain home feed: a minimal contract (post, follow, feed view) where the feed is a k-way merge over the followed accounts' post indices computed in bounded view-call steps with a typed resumable cursor (a GADT step indexed by its phase, the per-step budget computed at compile time from gas measured in the mock); view calls cost the reader nothing, so the client pages; compare with fan-out on write as a resumable job queue or NEP-519 yield/resume; replay XSIM-EVENT-STREAM scenarios and report gas per page and follows per page", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Paused, "NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS,XSIM-EVENT-STREAM", "near,x,gadt,feed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_feed_paged_view () = Planning ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_1 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:09Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:09 design detail: views are free for the reader but each call still has a gas ceiling, so the merge must be resumable: cursor = per-followed-account position (or a timestamp watermark plus offsets) returned with each page; the step is a typed state machine (GADT indexed by merge phase) whose per-step item budget is a compile-time constant derived from gas measured in the mock; fan-out on write = a queue drained by later transactions (or NEP-519 promise_yield_create/resume) for accounts with many followers; report gas per page, follows per page and storage per post")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_2 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: dep check: XSIM-EVENT-STREAM is Done, NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS (Planned 0%) is the real gate, since the per-step budget must come from gas measured in the mock. Found an existing in-tree instance of the same unbounded-view problem, so the spike's paging can be proven without a new contract first: polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.rs get_alias_map(alias) returns the whole HashMap<String, (u64, u32)> of an alias in one view call (plus a borsh twin), so a popular alias would hit the view gas ceiling exactly like an unpaged feed. A paged get_alias_map(alias, cursor, limit) with a typed cursor is the smallest rehearsal of the feed's resumable view, measurable in the mock once NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS lands.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_3 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: modelled the resumable feed view off-chain first (lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_merge.spi, CPU-only): a k-way merge over per-account post timestamps (newest_head picks the newest head, drop_head consumes it) where page feeds budget returns up to budget items plus the remaining feeds as the cursor, and all_pages resumes from that cursor until a page is empty. Check: three accounts (8 posts) paged by 3 give 3 pages whose order checksum equals one unbounded page's, i.e. resuming from the cursor loses or reorders nothing; C exit 3 via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy. On-chain, the cursor would shrink to per-account positions instead of the remaining lists, and budget is the compile-time constant from the mock's gas per item.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_4 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the on-chain cursor shape as lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_offsets.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): the feeds stay immutable (each account's post index, newest first, read by position as a vector would be), and the cursor is one i32 offset per followed account instead of the remaining lists; newest_at scans the heads at the current offsets, bump advances only the winning account's offset, page returns up to budget items plus the new offsets, all_pages resumes from them. Same check as the list version: 3 accounts, 8 posts, budget 3 must give 3 pages whose order checksum equals one unbounded page (expect exit 3). The cursor is now O(follows) integers, which is what a view call can return and take back as its argument.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_5 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:01 helper-lane lap 5: the cursor is now the title's typed resumable step (lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW/paged_offsets.spi, unrun until the freeze all-clear): a GADT cursor phase with Fresh and Paused offsets indexed scanning and Exhausted indexed exhausted, next_page accepting only a cursor scanning and returning More (items, cursor scanning) or Last items (it peeks whether any head remains, so the last page is marked without an extra empty call), and drain resuming until Last. Same equality check as before (expect exit 3: 3 pages of the 8 posts equal one unbounded page). paged_exhausted_negative.spi passes Exhausted to next_page and must be rejected at compile time - the type, not a runtime check, stops a client from paging a finished feed.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_6 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: paged_offsets.spi with the phase-indexed cursor GADT exits 3 as predicted (3 pages resumed through Paused offsets equal one unbounded page). The compile-time negative (paging Exhausted) is still to run.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_7 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 7u32, "2026-10-22T04:08Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:08 x: open decision for the user (asked 10-21 22:4x, no answer yet). spiral/lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW holds 3 probes from 10-19 (paged_merge.spi 1,445 B, paged_offsets.spi 2,968 B, paged_exhausted_negative.spi 2,806 B), and NEAR work is paused by the user since 10-18. Under the lab rule they have no place to land while the item is paused. Options: land them now as Spiral test blocks of a paged-feed module (they test merge, offsets and exhaustion of a paged view, which needs no NEAR contract), or keep them parked here until NEAR resumes. Until the user decides, they stay where they are; nothing is moved or deleted.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_8 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 8u32, "2026-10-22T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 09:34 x: DECIDED by rule, replacing the 00:08 'open decision for the user' (memory decide-autonomously: open calls are mine, never a user list). Rule, written before checking: a lab probe whose item the user paused lands where its tested logic belongs today. If no live module owns that logic, the probe stays in place until the item resumes, and the resume is the landing trigger. Probes are never deleted before landing (lab rule). Check: the probes test a k-way merge of followed accounts' post indices with a resumable cursor (a GADT phase: Fresh/Paused offsets vs Exhausted). x's only paging module, apps/client/paging.spi, walks X API next_token pages (paged/all_pages over a pager), which is different logic, and no other x module has a merge cursor. Outcome: the 3 probes stay in spiral/lab/NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW until NEAR resumes. Then they become the test blocks of the contract's feed-view module, and paged_exhausted_negative.spi (still unrun) becomes a compile-time rejection test there.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_9 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 9u32, "2026-10-23T11:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:08 x: grooming: status Planned -> Paused. Its parent X-ON-NEAR is paused by the user, and note 8's rule keeps it until NEAR resumes, so it must not show as ready work.")
inl note_of_near_feed_paged_view_10 () = Note ("NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: paged_offsets.spi written, bounded jobs frozen -> next: after the all-clear: run (expect 3) | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): GADT cursor probe PASS (3) -> next: run the Exhausted negative | lap 6 13:49 (subagent): Exhausted negative rejected at compile time (cursor scanning vs exhausted) -> next: gas mock (needs NEAR-MOCKED) | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: GADT cursor PASS 3 + negative -> next: next page op")
inl near_contract_crates () = Task ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", "Give the NEAR contracts x's shape: dice_contract.rs (178 KB) and chat_contract.rs (83 KB) are single generated files; split each into library crates under the 1,000-line gate linked by one cdylib contract crate ([package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, scripts/gate.ps1), mirrored in alphabet", Story, P1, D3, 10u32, Active, "-", "near,dice,polyglot,crate-gate,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_contract_crates () = Planning ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_1 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:57 helper-lane lap 1: sized against x's shape: dice/contract/dice_contract.spi is 230 non-blank Spiral lines and generates dice_contract.rs 174 KB / 3,396 lines / 187 fns (a 15x expansion, because the source opens the dice package via ///- --package ../dice and partial evaluation inlines that library into the contract); polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi 190 lines -> chat_contract.rs 81 KB / 1,374 lines / 65 fns. So under the 1,000-line gate dice needs at least 4 crates and chat 2, and the split cannot be done by cutting the contract file: it needs Export boundaries in the dice package itself (x's pattern: per-crate .spi with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output and path dependencies, e.g. xc_args -> xc_checks). The natural first cut is dice's randomness core (already generated separately as dice/lib/dice.rs) as a library crate the contract links.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_2 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: picked the first exported crate from what the contract reaches: dice_contract.spi calls exactly four dice package functions (dice.create_sequential_roller, dice.roll_progressively, dice.roll_within_bounds, dice.rotate_numbers), and its 187 generated functions are 82 numbered methods, 10 tag impls (before the lazy-tag change reached this file), ~60 closures and a handful of named ones - nearly all of it inlined dice-package code. So a dice_core crate exporting those four functions (Export with a scalar/array signature) would carry most of the 3,396 lines, leaving the contract crate as the NEAR glue (state, borsh, the 6 exported methods). Caveat from CLI-ARGS-STYLE's lap-2 probe: Export signatures must stay scalar or array, because exported union types are private and per-crate numbered.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_3 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 helper-lane lap 3: checked the four functions' boundary types (dice/lib/dice.spi and the call sites in dice_contract.spi 127-169): none crosses a crate boundary as written - rotate_numbers maps a stream (lazy, closure-backed) to a stream, create_sequential_roller returns a roller closure, roll_progressively takes that closure, and roll_within_bounds takes a list and returns an option (a union, private and per-crate numbered under today's Rust Export, see RUST-EXPORT-TYPES). So the cut must be at the contract's use level, not per function: dice_core exports two scalar/array functions - roll_from_hash (hash : vec u8) (max : u64) : u64, composing rotate_numbers 6, stream.to_list, create_sequential_roller and roll_progressively inside the crate (contract lines 120-149), and roll_within_bounds_of (max : u64) (rolls : vec u8) : u64 with 0 for none (valid results are 1..max). The contract crate keeps state, borsh, keccak512 and the 6 NEAR methods. Once RUST-EXPORT-TYPES lands, the option can cross as itself.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_4 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the dice_core export module as lab/NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES/dice_core.spi (not compiled yet: it must sit beside dice/contract to resolve ///- --package ../dice, and bounded jobs are frozen): roll_from_hash max hash composes rotate_numbers 6, the sequential roller and roll_progressively exactly as dice_contract.spi lines 120-149 do, and roll_within_bounds_of returns the option's value or 0; main exports both as dice_core_roll_from_hash and dice_core_roll_within_bounds_of with u64 * am'.vec u8 -> u64 signatures, the scalar/array shape today's Rust Export carries (the dice_contract Export lines 246-249 use the same vec argument type). Next: compile it in a dice copy, then the contract crate depending on it.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_5 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: blocked on the same thing as NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS: today's run shows dice_contract.spi no longer type-checks on the current tree (117:12, an array literal piped into am'.to_vec, array vs array_base since core v1). dice_core.spi cannot be compiled beside it until dice or lib am' is adapted (reported to main).")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_6 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T08:09Z", "main", "04:09 main: the 10-19 blocker is gone. dice/contract/dice_contract.spi type-checks and compiles to Rust on today's installed compiler (180 KB, exit 0; compiled into TEMP, and dice's tracked dice_contract.rs is unchanged). The 117:12 array vs array_base error was fixed in dice by the '|> am'.base' after the ;[ ... ] literal (commit 4218032, 10-19). So the crate split (x's shape: library crates under the 1,000-line gate plus one cdylib) can start; the next step is measuring how the 180 KB output divides by function (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split would do it at compile time).")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_7 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T08:10Z", "main", "04:10 main: measured on today's generated dice_contract.rs (3,106 lines, 129 top-level fns; largest closure7 451, method34 313, method29 237, closure76 231, closure4 197 lines). GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split (simulate.ps1, limit 1000) gives a 126-line root plus parts of 895/980/873/244 lines: every file under the gate, with no hand-split crates. Not rustc-checked, because the contract needs near-sdk; the two std-only samples compiled and behaved identically (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES note 7). So the compiler-side split may replace the hand-made crate split for this item; decide when that pack lands.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_8 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice_core.spi uncompiled (jobs held) -> next: compile in a dice copy after main's go | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice contract compiles again (7/7 after main's migration) -> next: compile dice_core.spi beside the contract")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_9 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 9u32, "2026-10-27T08:20Z", "main", "10-27 04:20 main: 10-27 04:19 main groom: fresh measurement (the last real note was the 10-19 lap fold; on the stale list in EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS n5). Neither contract is split yet. dice/contract/dice_contract.rs is 169 KB / 3,058 lines in one generated file (3.1x the 1,000-line crate gate; the title's 178 KB has shrunk only from incidental heals). polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.rs is 83 KB / 1,387 lines (1.4x; regenerated 10-27 by tibia's generate_cid heal). Each folder has 2 Cargo.toml, the contract plus its NEAR sandbox-test crate, not a library split. The x shape (library crates joined by exports) is the remaining work for both. chat_contract is the smaller and the nearer first target. Constraints to carry: the NEAR wasm still builds with the pinned nightly-2024-07-14 (dice build.ps1:50 TODO, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE), and the sandbox tests still need WSL (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE n10).")
inl x_federation_bridges () = Task ("X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", "Mastodon and Bluesky compatibility for the NEAR X (X-ON-NEAR): map posts, replies, edits, deletes, likes and follows to ActivityPub (actor, inbox/outbox, Create/Update/Delete/Like/Follow/Undo, WebFinger, HTTP signatures) and to AT Protocol (DID per account, app.bsky.feed.post/like and app.bsky.graph.follow records in a repo, firehose); the sim drives one scenario through all three and checks each network ends in the same state; xc reads all three behind its reads GADT", Story, P4, D6, 0u32, Paused, "", "x,near,mastodon,bluesky,activitypub,atproto,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_federation_bridges () = Planning ("X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_x_federation_bridges_1 () = Note ("X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES", 1u32, "2026-10-21T01:45Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:45 x-lane backlog refactor: Planned -> Paused to match the user's 2026-10-18 deferral, so it leaves the ready queue of agile next; back to Planned when the user starts NEAR work")
inl x_client_thread_post () = Task ("X-CLIENT-THREAD-POST", "xc post-thread FILE: post a .spi as a thread the way xc thread reads it back: split cells into posts of at most 280 X weight (twitter-text v3 weights, 23 per autolink, NFC, cuts at whitespace) with CELL k/n headers, each post replying to the previous; a Spiral port of the sim's Xsim.Frames split, cross-checked against it (two implementations, one oracle); stub-tested round trip post-thread -> thread byte for byte; --dry-run prints the posts and the cost ($0.015 per post created) without posting; live posting only with the user's go per thread. The dogfood loop of user 2026-10-16: post x's own source (later the compiler) so Grok and people can read and run it", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "x,x-api,threads,sim,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl x_thread_gap_detection () = Task ("X-THREAD-GAP-DETECTION", "A cell lost with all of its posts is invisible to xc thread today (only cells missing some frames are named). Use the reply chain: every first frame replies to the last frame of its parent cell, so a post that replies to an id absent from the thread (other than the root's own parent) marks a gap; note it with both neighbours and exit 4. The sim gains a whole-cell loss (--lost-cells N) and a fixture, e2e byte-exact", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "x,threads,sim", "current", "current")
inl eoie_granular_migration () = Task ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", "Migrate rust_global drift into granular typed Spiral slice by slice, x and tibia discipline", Epic, P0, D7, 8u32, Active, "", "eoie,spiral,rust-global,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_granular_migration () = Planning ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_1 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:24Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:24 eoie lane start (user: new eoie code follows the tibia/x granular discipline; migrate the rust_global drift using the type feature set). Baseline: 11166 rust_global blob lines in 108 files (13166 Spiral lines in those files). Slice 1: eoie_archive_tar_runtime/format.spi, archive_format union + classifier, exports eoie_archive_format_code/label, Rust archive_format is now glue (label Rc<str>), 3 Spiral test blocks; regen published, bundle_manifest consumer unchanged, archive_limits/portable_policy/windows tests pass. Slice 2: eoie_bundle_retention/series.spi, series_reading/series_rejection unions with thin primitives, exports eoie_retention_series_error/prefix/alpha, retention_series is glue; 2 test blocks (8 cases incl. u64 overflow text); e2e retention-plan on a scratch series: keep 3 delete 2, rejection text from Spiral, latest-candidate check intact. Both test files registered in eoie_dev_spiral_tests registry. Recipe in memory eoie-granular-spiral")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_2 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:38Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:38 slice 3: eoie_bundle_retention/receipt.spi, receipt_status union, the receipt Spiral module template as a block raw string (first real use of the block form in eoie source), export eoie_retention_receipt_source over string*string*u64*u64*u64*string, retention_receipt_source is glue; 2 test blocks incl. a byte-exact expected module; e2e: planned receipt byte-identical to the old format string rebuilt independently, retention-apply passes its readback check, deletes 2, keeps alpha3-5, writes the RetentionApplied receipt; fingerprint unchanged (db54787b1e12c832)")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_3 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:50Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:50 slice 4: tar_octal field text into format.spi (octal_field union EmptyField/OctalValue/InvalidOctal, trim + base-8 primitives, exports eoie_tar_octal_value/error); Rust keeps only the NUL cut and UTF-8 check; format.spi 4/4, consumer tests green, e2e proxy archive-extract of a real ustar tar.gz: format=tar.gz from the Spiral classifier, sizes through the Spiral octal reader (2 payloads, 14 bytes), contents intact. Whole registry run: 6 notebooks, 19 Spiral tests, all pass. Blob bytes: tar runtime -297 then about -150 more, retention -939. Next candidates by the same recipe: tar_pax_path record validation (typed rejection union), retention_read_receipt field parsing, coverage_union merge, patch_quoted_fields")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_4 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-10-18T20:28Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:28 slice 5: eoie_coverage_prune/lcov.spi, LCOV parsing as typed Spiral: lcov_line, lcov_state, lcov_rejection (10 messages kept verbatim) and hit_reading unions, a tail-recursive state machine producing normalized rows (bare source rows keep SF blocks without DA lines); exports eoie_lcov_error/rows; coverage_union_records is now glue (validation call plus a trivial row reader into the BTreeMap merge). lcov.spi 2/2 (12 cases), the existing coverage_union_merges_max_hits_and_writes_receipt and the crate's other Rust tests pass. Not migrated: tar_pax_path (payload is bytes and !!!!Export has no byte-slice parameter; a glue-side UTF-8 check would change which non-UTF8 message is reported)")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_5 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T06:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:40 x lane (git-2c), eoie targets from the five mega samples, for the eoie lane to schedule (cited paths are agent reads, unverified by builds): (1) agile_mutation_policy/model.spi:40-80: agile_status_code plus agile_status_code_valid and the agile_*_decision i32 functions are hand-synced; lean_cic's raw -> verified gate (L:2462-2475) and typed decisions (L:1265-1271) give an Accepted case that carries the agile_status, behind union private. (2) eoie_jobs/model.spi:16-23: replace the JobUnknown catch-all with Known job_command | Unknown string; :43-49 state_exit invents exit 3 for Pending/Running, split job_state into settled and live so the index narrows the match (spiral_proves S:8227-8232). (3) bundle_output_contract/model.spi:25-26: output_pending and output_root_ready are bare nominals; omniledger's chain of custody (3103-3108) makes each stage wrap its predecessor. (4) agile_todo_model/model.spi:187-197 CFamily => 1i32 tables: one prototype instance per union or reflection, never a hand table (EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION). (5) move *_negative_probe folders into in-file fails tests. Features the samples use that eoie lacks: assert_static, and inl")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_6 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T07:36Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:36 eoie lane on x lane idea (2), measured in a temp copy: job_verb = Known job_command | Unknown string plus job_state = Live job_live | Settled job_settled (settled_exit total over settled states, wait_exit names the 3-while-running convention, output unchanged) compiles and costs +35 lines (eoie_jobs 938 -> 973). Deferred: it is modeling, not missing function, and it would leave 27 lines of headroom in a crate other lanes rely on. Apply it together with a split of eoie_jobs (CLI routing vs registry model). The edit plan is ready as a python JSON-style replacement list (scratchpad jobs-model-edit.py; recreate it if lost).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_7 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 7u32, "2026-10-21T14:19Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:19 eoie team: sized the drift first: 46 *terminal*.spi files hold 538 KB of Rust inside Spiral (largest eoie_source_topology_core 40 KB, evidence_currentness 30 KB, bundle zip 33 KB; most serve the GPT-era patch/bundle/proxy surface). Order rule: blobs on today's verbs first (agile, routing, handoff, read), smallest first within that, one blob per slice so a failure pulls one slice. Slice 1 drafted for r31 (scratch apply-agile-terminal-migration.ps1): eoie_agile_mutation's blob (an error helper plus six use-aliases) is gone; the helper is Spiral over workspace_primitives.console.stderr_failure (identical bytes and exit code), aliases become full paths at their single call sites. Next by the rule: eoie_handoff render_terminal (5.8 KB, agile handoff), then agile_state_runtime (agile init/lease/status), then eoie_cli_routing (22 KB, every call).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_8 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 8u32, "2026-10-21T14:19Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:20 eoie team (corrected): 'agile handoff' fails on the shared backlog (no state/handoff.spi there) but works on the eoie repo's own root (C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\state\\handoff.spi), and the transcripts show it called there ('eoie agile handoff .': 30 matches in 11 files). So render_terminal (5.8 KB) serves a live verb and stays a migration target, after agile_state_runtime (more callers). Pruning is off the table for it.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_9 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 9u32, "2026-10-21T16:37Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:37 eoie team: slice 2 drafted for r32 (scratch apply-handoff-render.ps1; chain-r32 armed behind r31). eoie_handoff's render_terminal blob (agile handoff) is now render.spi in granular Spiral: state reads as a union StateMissing|StateText|StateUnreadable, results as Rendered|Refused, and the optional evidence lines as a ToolchainEvidence|ColdProofEvidence union. handoff_render_owned keeps its &str export. The dead handoff_render() is dropped, and the four std imports the other blobs need move into binary_install_terminal's existing blob, so there is no new rust_global. Measured: inside eoie_handoff the Spiral version generated 1031 lines (render about 400 against the blob's 80), so it got its own crate eoie_handoff_render (render.rs 426, handoff.rs 608). eoie_agile_mutation now depends on it in place of eoie-handoff. Preview, notebook render.spi and cargo check --locked of the 4 crates all pass. The r32 release job adds a handoff parity step (old vs new binary on the eoie root and on a root without handoff.spi; a mismatch blocks the install). series_terminal was skipped by the order rule: agile record is not a verb in use today.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_10 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 10u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): job_state split into Live job_live | Settled job_settled (x idea 2).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_11 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 11u32, "2026-10-21T20:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:56 eoie team: inventory after today's two slices (r31 agile_mutation, r32 handoff render): 44 terminal blobs, 520 KB (was 46, 538 KB). Smallest on today's verbs: rust_std_fs filesystem_terminal (0.6 KB), path_terminal (2.4), presence_terminal (3.0). filesystem_terminal opens a 'mod dense {' that a sibling blob closes and otherwise only re-exports eoie_rust_std_fs_mutation, so the rust_std_fs blobs must move together (dense module, re-exports, then path and presence as typed Spiral), not one by one. The tiny patch-app blobs (rehearsal 0.3, mutation 0.7, gated 1.2 KB) serve GPT-era patch verbs: by the order rule they come after today's verbs, or are pruned once usage shows they're unused (the adoption sweep can count them).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_12 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:57Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:57 eoie team, usage evidence for migrate-or-prune (Claude transcripts, 7 days, eoie calls by verb). Top: agile note 1654, job tail 1097, job wait 796, agile show 780, job start 598, agile set 424, job status 291, agile edit 247, window list 242, agile list 226, grep 99, agile add 77, window run 67, agile todos 51. Legacy surface: patch apply/check/rehearse/gated 0; agile record 0; bundle 8 in all. Proxy: text-replace 31, source-topology 27, parallel-search 17, fs-search 8, command-output 5, command-capture 4, fs-write 3, source-stats 3, and about a dozen more at 1-2; the remaining ~50 proxy verbs at 0. Proposed rule, set now and applied next session: a legacy verb with 0 calls in 14 days across all clients, no one-call spec case and no caller in any repo (grep its stem across repos first, per the dangling rule) is pruned rather than migrated, one verb family per slice, with its blob and tests. Verbs with uses migrate by the size order. This turns most of the 520 KB from migration work into deletion.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_13 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 13u32, "2026-10-21T21:09Z", "eoie-lane", "17:09 eoie team, prune evidence for the first family (patch), measured against the 16:57 rule. Calls: 0 real 'eoie patch apply/check/rehearse/gated' in 14 days (Claude Code, 56 transcript files, 880 MB; Codex has no sessions in the window); the 3 hits are prose inside note text. One-call spec: 0 cases. Callers outside eoie: none (grep of 'eoie patch' and the argv form across C:\\home\\git); inside eoie only contracts_entry/cli_driver, eoie_coverage_smoke and the patch crates themselves. Dependency cut (Cargo path deps + spiproj packages): cutting eoie_cli's two edges (eoie_patch_mutation, eoie_patch_rehearsal) makes 19 crates unreachable from eoie_cli; 32 of 34 patch-named crates are unreachable after the cut. patch_control_domain and patch_driver_domain stay (eoie_fs_actions, eoie_batch_write, eoie_legacy_operations, evidence_currentness_domain use them, and proxy fs-write has 28 calls). Slice shape: drop the routing arm and help row, the two cli deps, the 19 crates plus their probes/contracts (backup_domain_probe, patch_*_probe, eoie_patch_*_contracts, eoie_patch_grammar, eoie_patch_scan), the cli_driver/coverage_smoke patch cases; stem-grep each crate across repos first. Side finding: 141 crates are already unreachable from eoie_cli (many are other entries such as eoie_dev and notebook runners); worth a separate census. Scripts: scratch prune-evidence/verb-usage.ps1, exclusive-closure.ps1.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_14 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 14u32, "2026-10-21T21:38Z", "eoie-lane", "17:38 eoie team, orphan census (scratch prune-evidence/orphan-census.ps1, orphan-refs2.ps1; Cargo path deps + spiproj packages; 28,293 text files scanned across C:\\home\\git): 129 crates (499 KB) are unreachable from all three binaries (eoie_cli, eoie_dev, eoie_predicate_lift): 96 probes, 23 contracts, 5 legacy, 5 other; 19 are workspace members. By reference: 87 crates (112 KB) are named only in eoie's own inventories (state/*.spi such as bench, consolidation_fixtures, coverage, owner_growth, runtime_drift; standards/census; review .md), never by a runner, script or other repo; 35 are referenced from other eoie src (eoie_cli_harness via notebook --package headers, contracts run by compiler-contracts); 13 'live' hits are backlog mentions only. Prune rule addition: an inventory-only crate is a prune candidate under the 16:57 rule; its slice removes it from the inventories in the same change and refreshes evidence (state/evidence.spi hashes the coverage files, as found for help_catalog.rs), so the evidence currentness check stays green. Order: the 19 workspace members first (they cost build time), then probes by directory size.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_15 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 15u32, "2026-10-21T21:49Z", "eoie-lane", "17:49 eoie team, patch prune plan for the next session (full text goes to lab/EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION/r36-prune-patch-plan.md after tonight's install). Trap: command_spec_domain numbers commands by union position (declared_code = union_case_index - 1; handler_from_code (code + 1)); today status=0 patch=1 agile=2 bundle=3 proxy=4 help=5, lease denied the literal 7, with 28 numeric sites (11 in eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi such as eoie_command_descriptor(\"2\") for agile, 14 + 2 in command_spec_domain, 1 in eoie_cli_model). Deleting PatchSpec silently shifts agile to 1. Rule set now: replace every numeric site with a case-based call in the same slice if possible; otherwise give each case an explicit code (code_of) and then delete PatchSpec; never a placeholder case; tie-break the explicit code table. Edits: command spec cases/arms/usage/diagnostic/patch:check effect; eoie_cli_model patch_run; routing EoiePatchAdapter; eoie_cli Cargo deps; workspace members + lock; the 19 crates + their probe/contract dependents (keep patch_control_domain, patch_driver_domain); cli_driver and coverage_smoke patch cases; inventories (state/bench, consolidation_fixtures, coverage, owner_growth, runtime_drift, migration, standards/census) with an evidence refresh in the same release.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_16 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 16u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 heal plan: the migration runs in waves after the r30-r36 train lands (pending slices anchor on today's text). Order: shared packages first by fan-in (rust_std_string, eoie_cli_harness, union_reflection), then crates by host-macro count. Done per crate: references the lib, 0 macros outside the boundary package, 0 $'{ blocks, its tests pass, under the 1000-line gate. Done overall: 376/376 spiproj on the lib, census zeros, ratchet exemptions removed.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_17 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 17u32, "2026-10-22T03:45Z", "eoie", "23:45 migration debt from tonight's release train, by tree delta: live -> r34 +52 host macros / +15 Rc::<str>::from / +7 let-blocks / +7 rust_std_string uses; r34 -> release C +64 / +32 / +14 / +8. Release A absorbs r30-r33's drift into the baseline (its job re-baselines); B and C don't, so their debt shows in the ratchet.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_18 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 18u32, "2026-10-22T05:42Z", "eoie", "01:42 first crate healed end to end in the scratch tree rE (apply-heal-rename-rewrite.ps1, template for the waves): agile_rename_rewrite drops rust_std_string (equals -> =, concat5 -> ++#, trim/replace/starts_with -> sm' with the argument-order flips), replaces two $\" arithmetic macros with plain +, references spiral- and declares HostFree + LibReferenced in standards.spi. Preview exit 0. Measured size cost: rename_rewrite.rs 426 -> 522 lines (+23%: ++# chains and sm' calls inline more than concat5's single host call), so crates above ~800 lines need a split before healing. No test blocks in this crate: its proof is the agile rename CLI notebook in the candidate step of the release that ships it (E).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_19 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 19u32, "2026-10-22T05:45Z", "eoie", "01:45 wave 1 grows (scratch tree rE, slice apply-heal-wave1-small.ps1): agile_record_catalog, proxy_command_output, proxy_file_receipt healed (concat5 -> ++#, spiral- instead of rust_std_string, HostFree + LibReferenced declared); census after: host-macro 0, string-concat 0 for all three. Compile preview deferred until release ABC ends (no eoie-lane compiles meanwhile). With agile_rename_rewrite that's 4 healed crates queued for release E.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_20 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 20u32, "2026-10-22T05:46Z", "eoie", "01:46 wave 1b (apply-heal-wave1b.ps1, scratch tree rE): agile_planning_filters (equals -> =) and agile_state_transient_schema_domain (2 starts_with + 5 contains -> sm' with the needle first) healed, census 0/0; 6 crates now queued for release E (rename_rewrite, record_catalog, command_output, file_receipt, planning_filters, transient_schema). agile_planning_values waits for main's sm'.listed (it uses pipe_lookup).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_21 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 21u32, "2026-10-22T05:48Z", "eoie", "01:48 agile_field_rendering healed too (apply-heal-field-rendering.ps1; equals -> =, two concat5 -> ++#), census 0/0: 7 crates queued for release E. Waiting on main's sm'.listed: agile_planning_values (pipe_lookup) and agile_state_decision_domain (pipe_member). eoie_table_diff opens rust_std_string wholesale (unqualified calls): a later, larger heal.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_22 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 22u32, "2026-10-22T05:52Z", "eoie", "01:52 healing tool written (heal-crate.ps1 -Crate <name> [-DryRun], to lab): an argument tokenizer (string literals with escapes, balanced parenthesized groups, plain tokens) maps concat5 -> ++# chains (empty args dropped), equals -> (a = b), starts_with/ends_with/contains -> sm' with the needle first, replace -> sm'.replace from into text, trim -> sm'.trim; refuses a crate that opens rust_std_string wholesale or keeps unmapped calls; swaps rust_std_string for spiral- in package.spiproj; declares HostFree + LibReferenced when no host macros remain. Applied in the scratch tree rE to agile_state_path_policy, agile_update_receipt, predicate_rules (nested parens + escaped quotes handled), release_hygiene_policy, eoie_source_topology_core (813 lines): census 0/0 each. 12 healed crates queued for release E (compile preview after ABC).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_23 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 23u32, "2026-10-22T06:12Z", "eoie", "02:12 heal tool extended to wholesale 'open rust_std_string.model' crates (unqualified calls, refusing when the file or any opened module defines a same-named function, refusing block strings, replacing indented prelude opens with open sm'_operators) and hardened after a hostile check caught two bugs in the scratch output: multi-line concat5 became empty '++#' operands (tokenizer now skips newlines; multi-line calls keep one operand per line with leading ++#), and rewrites inside $'...' host macros (the string check now tracks macro regions). The 16 affected crates were restored from rD and rehealed; scans show 0 empty operands and macro text unchanged. Release E now carries 28 healed crates: 12 HostFree (rename_rewrite, record_catalog, command_output, file_receipt, planning_filters, transient_schema, field_rendering, state_path_policy, update_receipt, predicate_rules, release_hygiene_policy, source_topology_core) + 16 off rust_std_string with host macros left (job_after, table_measures, memory_query, job_launch, memory_model, receipt, lcov, do_tables, handoff_render, repo_state, do_lab, do_facts, do_process, do_git, command_line, todo_record). Compile proof after ABC.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_24 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 24u32, "2026-10-22T06:17Z", "eoie", "02:17 wave 2 by the hardened tool (scratch tree rE): 39 more crates off rust_std_string (agile_* note/planning/task/title/todo families, canonical_plan_ir_domain, cold_proof_domain, eoie_agile_state, eoie_archive_tar_runtime, eoie_coverage_prune, eoie_dev, eoie_dev_model, eoie_predicate_lift, legacy_operations_entry, proxy_* receipts, workspace_primitives); both hostile scans clean (0 empty operands, macro text unchanged); 8 of them declare HostFree. Release E now carries 67 crates (apply-rE.ps1, 54 tool calls + 4 hand slices + window marker). 15 crates refused by the tool's safety rules (block strings, pipe_member/trim_end_matches waiting for lib helpers, calls it cannot map) stay for hand healing. Risk for E: workspace_primitives is inlined widely and ++# generates more Rust than concat5 (+23% measured), so E's preview must check every crate against the 1000-line gate before it ships.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_25 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 25u32, "2026-10-22T06:19Z", "eoie", "02:19 what the tool leaves (12 crates still on rust_std_string with unmapped calls): trim_end_matches (agile_task_creation, agile_state_runtime_policy_domain) maps onto main's sm'.trim_end_suffix once it lands; pipe_member/pipe_lookup/pipe_index (agile_planning_model, agile_planning_tally, agile_planning_values, agile_state_decision_domain, agile_subcommand_domain, agile_task_listing, agile_task_next, agile_todo_model, command_spec_domain, eoie_agile_mutation) are pipe tables, which the census already counts as drift (pipe-table probe): heal them by replacing each table with a typed union (reflection for the word lists), not with a lib helper. That is per-crate modeling work for hand healing, not for heal-crate.ps1.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_26 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 26u32, "2026-10-22T06:21Z", "eoie", "02:21 first pipe table healed by hand as the template (apply-heal-planning-values.ps1, scratch tree rE): agile_planning_values' pipe_lookup 'yes|pair|no' -> 'agent|pair|user' became union legacy_agent (LegacyYes|LegacyPair|LegacyNo) with legacy_word/legacy_assignee and a union_reflection lookup returning '' for unknown words (same contract); census pipe-table 1 -> 0, rust_std_string dropped. Covered by planning_cli's migrate test ('agent:no' -> assignee=user). E now carries 68 crates.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_27 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 27u32, "2026-10-22T06:23Z", "eoie", "02:23 second pipe table healed by hand (apply-heal-decision-domain.ps1): agile_state_decision_domain's transient pipe table -> union transient_receipt looked up by reflection; its equals chain -> agile_record_file on the existing agile_record_requirement union with a reflection lookup; the v1 catalog string is now derived with union_cases_joined in declaration order (byte-identical 'history.spi|ratings.spi|bench.spi|migration.spi'). Census pipe-table 1 -> 0; no host macros, so it and agile_planning_values now declare HostFree + LibReferenced. E carries 69 crates.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_28 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 28u32, "2026-10-22T06:27Z", "eoie", "02:27 heal tool handles block strings now (tokenizer reads a block literal as one argument; matches inside any block span across lines are skipped; the refusal is gone). Healed in the scratch tree rE: coverage_public_probe (HostFree) and eoie_bundle_retention (7 files, host macros remain); checks: 0 empty operands, every block string byte-identical, macro text unchanged. E carries 71 crates (apply-rE.ps1).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_29 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 29u32, "2026-10-22T06:28Z", "eoie", "02:28 third pipe table by hand + tool (apply-heal-planning-model.ps1): agile_planning_model's cleared_value 'none|-' -> union cleared_word with a reflection lookup, then heal-crate.ps1 for the ~30 concat5/equals/ends_with/starts_with/replace calls (6 block strings byte-identical, 0 empty operands); census pipe-table 0, string-concat 0, host-macro 3 left. It is a shared model package inlined by the planning crates, so E's preview must re-measure them. E carries 72 crates.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_30 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 30u32, "2026-10-22T13:42Z", "eoie-lane", "09:42 eoie team: a semantic review of heal-crate.ps1's mappings before G ships E's ~75 healed crates (argument-order flips compile fine but invert logic). Legacy rust_std_string takes the text first (starts_with text prefix, ends_with text suffix, contains text needle, replace text from into, trim_end_matches text suffix); lib sm' takes it last (starts_with value s, contains value s, replace old new s, trim_end_suffix suffix input). The tool emits sm'.starts_with a1 a0, ends_with a1 a0, contains a1 a0, replace a1 a2 a0, trim_end_suffix a1 a0: all correct. trim_end_suffix strips every trailing copy like Rust's trim_end_matches (its own lib test says so). pipe_member becomes sm'.listed only for literal, whitespace-free lists. equals becomes (a = b). Residual difference: lib trim covers its own char list rather than all Unicode whitespace, irrelevant for eoie's ASCII inputs. No change needed.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_31 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 31u32, "2026-10-22T14:02Z", "eoie-lane", "10:02 eoie team: heal-crate.ps1's near-gate guard now also measures consumers. A Spiral-only package such as eoie_memory_model has no .rs of its own, so the old guard (skip when the crate's own .rs is at 950+) healed it, even though its code is generated into eoie_memory (994 lines), where every ++# adds a binding. The guard now skips a package when any crate listing it directly in package.spiproj generates 950+ lines. Dry runs: eoie_memory_model is skipped (consumer eoie_memory 994), eoie_receipt is skipped (consumer eoie_do_edit 976). prelaunch-check over F, G and E2 together is still clean. The skipped packages stay on concat5 until main's codegen pack inlines single-use bindings, the same rule as the crates themselves.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_32 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 32u32, "2026-10-22T14:02Z", "eoie-lane", "10:02 measured (package graph only, no compiler): of the 60 packages on E's heal list, none reaches a 950+ line crate only through another Spiral-only package. Every near-gate exposure is a direct consumer, which the extended guard covers, so a transitive guard is not needed for GE2. Re-measure when the heal list grows.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_33 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 33u32, "2026-10-22T21:04Z", "eoie-lane", "17:04 progress measured (package.spiproj and standards.spi read, no compile). Live tree: 377 packages, 5 reference spiral-, 2 declare HostFree + LibReferenced (eoie_tree_lease, eoie_window_claim). GE2+H tree (guard-dry): 384 packages, 107 reference spiral-, 29 declare both (the E2 heal wave plus the new crates). Three more model-only packages drafted today pass both declarations (agile_decision_model, agile_find_model, eoie_release_model in scratch f5). So the GE2+H install would take lib reference from 1.3% to 28% and the declared contract from 0.5% to 7.6% of packages. Progress stays at 3 until GE2 and H are installed (it counts landed packages only); then it moves to about 8.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_34 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 34u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: raised to P0 under GRANULAR-HEAL (user 10-24: heal once and for all, including eoie). Census: 786 sites, 1,050,302 macro chars, 202 blocks, 337 long, 247 casts, 118 arith; largest files contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi 42/57,982, rust_std_fs_mutation/mutation.spi 6/44,683, eoie_source_topology_core/terminal.spi 7/40,045, eoie_bundle_manifest/bundle_manifest.spi 2/39,539, evidence_currentness_domain/terminal.spi 1/30,037. GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1)-(6) applies; slices land with the lint baseline shrinking.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_35 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 35u32, "2026-10-24T08:50Z", "eoie", "04:5x eoie: slice A of the granular migration is drafted, starting with the largest file. contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi emits 14 Rust #[test]s into eoie_contracts through rust_global; 7 run in every release and 7 are #[ignore]d, reachable only through build.ps1 -CompilerContracts, which nothing automated passes (CI, release scripts and i574n.github scripts have no match). Rule, written before porting: port the release-run tests to Spiral harness notebooks first; keep the ignored ones for a later slice with its own decision; each slice shrinks the file. Slice A (scratch granular-contracts/apply-contracts-slice-a.ps1) moves filesystem_proxy_roundtrip_is_rooted, expired_lease_blocks_first_mutation_but_keeps_wrap_surface and typed_handoff_is_readable_and_versionless into eoie_contracts/contracts_cli.spi (zero host macros; lib sm' plus the harness), registers it with the eoie-contracts notebooks, and deletes their blocks: cli_driver.spi goes from 58,370 to 47,150 chars. Typecheck/run queued as job eoie-typecheck-contracts-slice-a. Next: slice B (bundle_fixture plus flat_bundle, public_rejections, family_contract), then proxy_surface (needs native test tools), then the ignored seven.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_36 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 36u32, "2026-10-24T09:36Z", "eoie", "05:3x eoie: slices B and C drafted. B moves bundle_fixture and the flat-bundle, public-rejections and family-contract tests into eoie_contracts/bundle_cli.spi. C moves proxy_surface into proxy_cli.spi, building its true/false/sleep/printenv tool with the harness rustc_program instead of native_tools.rs. All 7 release-run cli_driver tests are now Spiral, and cli_driver.spi goes from 58,370 to 20,665 chars after A+B+C. Each slice has a rerun guard and the registry parens are checked. Typecheck plus a run against the installed eoie is queued for each (contracts-slice-a/b/c). Rule for slice D, written before drafting: the 7 #[ignore] compiler contracts stay opt-in. Today build.ps1 -CompilerContracts adds cargo --include-ignored, and native_tools.rs' compiler_contracts_remain_registered_after_regeneration guards their registration. They move to eoie_contracts/compiler_contracts_cli.spi, listed in a second registry, contract_registered (), which eoie_dev_spiral_tests runs only when eoie-dev passes --compiler-contracts. The Rust registration guard becomes a registry test that lists all 7 names. Then cli_driver.spi, native_tools.rs and the contracts_entry emit path are deleted, keeping only the typed contract plan in entry.spi, which drops 786 sites by the file's 42 blocks.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_37 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 37u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "eoie", "06:3x eoie: slice A's first run compiled and ran, but all 3 tests failed on one bug of mine. The notebook helper 'inl rec args_from' recursed over sm'.split_list output (runtime), so the compiler unrolled it until EJP0040 (more than 1000 dynamic if branches). Every recursive helper in the four contract notebooks and in slice E's harness helpers is now let rec (13 sites). Slices A and B rerun at the end of runner eoie-serial-checks-2. The gotcha is recorded for own helpers, not just lib's listm' naming.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_38 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 38u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:1x groom: progress check. Slices A-E for contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi are drafted and packaged in next-train stage S5. Typechecks: A and B rerun after the let rec and parse fixes; C and D are queued. The file's 42 blocks and native_tools.rs go once S5 installs. Still pending under this epic: the eoie_cli_harness legacy macros themselves (eoie_run block etc.), then rust_std_fs_mutation/mutation.spi (6 sites, 44,683 chars) as the next file by violating chars, per GRANULAR-HEAL rule 4.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_39 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 39u32, "2026-10-24T13:30Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:30 main: test placement (SPIRAL-NOTEBOOK-FILES note 1): 77% of eoie's 266 test blocks sit after their file's last definition, and 27 files are fully clustered (model.spi 10/10, notes_cli 9/9, window_model 8/8). User rule: tests interleave with the code they test. Move them while each file is touched for the granular migration.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_40 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 40u32, "2026-10-25T00:44Z", "eoie", "20:44 Lab drain: the unlanded plan lab/EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION/r36-prune-patch-plan.md moved into this note verbatim (the file is deleted; PatchSpec is still live, so this plan is open work):\n# r36 (next session): prune the patch verb family\n\nRule: EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION note 2026-10-21 16:57 (0 calls in 14 days across clients, no one-call spec case, no caller in any repo). Evidence: note 2026-10-21 17:09 (0 real calls / 14 d, 0 spec cases, only eoie-internal callers; cutting eoie_cli's two edges frees 19 crates).\n\n## The code-shift trap (read before editing)\n\ncommand_spec_domain/model.spi numbers commands by position: `declared_code spec = union_case_index - 1`, and `handler_from_code (code + 1)` picks the case back. PatchSpec is case 2, so today status=0, patch=1, agile=2, bundle=3, proxy=4, help=5, and lease denied is the literal 7. Numeric consumers: 11 sites in eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi (e.g. `eoie_command_descriptor(\"2\")` for agile), 14 in command_spec_domain/model.spi, 2 in command_spec_domain/main.spi, 1 in eoie_cli_model/model.spi. Deleting the PatchSpec case shifts agile to 1 and breaks every numeric site silently.\n\nDecision rule for the slice (set before editing): if every numeric site can be replaced by a case-based call in the same slice (the routing blob asks by name or case, not by digit), delete the case. Otherwise keep the position stable with an explicit code per case (a `code_of` function matching each case to its number) and delete PatchSpec; never leave a placeholder case. Tie-break: the explicit code table (smaller diff, no blob growth).\n\n## Edits\n\n1. command_spec_domain/model.spi: drop PatchSpec and PatchHandler (and their arms in verb_spec_of, handler_value, command_catalog_witness), \"patch\" from the unknown-verb diagnostic, the usage line `patch: apply ...`, the `patch:check` subcommand effect, the help_catalog section. main.spi's `command_subcommand_effect_code \"patch:check\"` call.\n2. eoie_cli_model/model.spi: `patch_run` and its `PatchHandler => patch_run ()` arm.\n3. eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi: `EoiePatchAdapter` (lines ~133-151) and the usage text `<status|patch|agile|bundle|proxy|help>`.\n4. eoie_cli/Cargo.toml: eoie-patch-mutation, eoie-patch-rehearsal; workspace Cargo.toml members and Cargo.lock entries for every deleted crate.\n5. Delete the 19 crates newly unreachable from eoie_cli (exclusive-closure.ps1 output: backup_domain, eoie_patch_mutation, eoie_patch_rehearsal, patch_batch, patch_compat_driver, patch_compat_receipts, patch_compat_runtime, patch_compat_test_support, patch_domain, patch_effect_domain, patch_gated_app, patch_model, patch_mutation_app, patch_mutation_contract, patch_orchestration_domain, patch_preflight_contract, patch_rehearsal_app, patch_resume_contract, patch_source_adapter) and their dependents that only test them (backup_domain_probe, patch_authority_probe, patch_preflight_*_probe, patch_resume_*_probe, patch_source_adapter_probe, eoie_patch_mutation_contracts, eoie_patch_transaction_contracts, eoie_patch_grammar, eoie_patch_scan, multi_file_patch_*). Grep each stem across C:\\home\\git first (dangling rule); keep patch_control_domain and patch_driver_domain (eoie_fs_actions, eoie_batch_write, eoie_legacy_operations, evidence_currentness_domain use them).\n6. contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi patch cases (lines ~220-370, 465, 556-567) and eoie_coverage_smoke/smoke.spi patch-success / patch-repeat-failure.\n7. Inventories that name the crates: state/bench.spi, consolidation_fixtures.spi, coverage.spi, owner_growth.spi, runtime_drift.spi, migration.spi, standards/census.spi; then refresh evidence (state/evidence.spi hashes evidence/coverage/*.lcov) in the same release, or the currentness check fails (see help_catalog.rs, EOIE-SPIRAL-TESTS 2026-10-21 09:53).\n8. Checks: preview of every touched crate, the full notebook registry, cargo check --locked --workspace, one-call spec 34+/34+, `eoie help` and `eoie patch` (expect the unknown-verb diagnostic).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_41 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 41u32, "2026-10-26T10:10Z", "eoie", "06:10 eoie: progress measured on the renamed live tree (scratch clean\\respell\\granular-progress.ps1, package files only, no compile): 427 packages, 155 reference spiral- (36%), 48 declare HostFree + LibReferenced (11%), 41 still on rust_std_string. Host macros 1,609, let-blocks 166, global sites 365. Slice A, proven: 13 packages are already free of host code (0 macros, 0 blocks, on the lib, generated crate under 900 lines) but never declared it: kit_job_start_args, kit_do_file_steps, kit_job_after, kit_job_after_model, kit_job_env, kit_job_queue, kit_ci_model, kit_jobs_doctor_model, kit_memory_import, kit_adoption_habits, kit_process_kill, kit_table_sequence_args, agile_note_edit. Checked on the worker node with the current tree's debug kit.exe, on copies (clean\\respell\\declare-contract.ps1): before 'declared=2 findings=0'; after adding HostFree + LibReferenced (plus the base no-comment, width, LF and TestTargets lines where no standards.spi existed) 'declared=13 findings=0 rules=lib-referenced,host-free,test-targets,lf-only,line-width,no-comment-lines'. A first try without TestTargets silently dropped the test-targets rule the defaults applied, so the base keeps it. Lands in land-post-rename.ps1, which refuses unless the installed kit's standards check passes all 13 (now 61 declared, 14%). Installed eoie.exe predates the host-free and lib-referenced rules (its standards reports declared=0 for declared packages), so these checks need the candidate or kit.exe. Slice B drafted: agile_task_rename's only macro is a cross-crate Rust call into agile_rename_rewrite's export. heal-task-rename.do depends on the Spiral package and calls rename_rewrite.renamed_text directly, then drops the Cargo dep (applies 3 files 3 replacements on a copy). Proof queued on the node behind the preflight: job-1793009331856-kit-heal-task-rename (regen plus debug build, notes_cli whose rename receipt is byte-exact, plus agile_cli; strict verdict; size of task_rename.rs read from the regen).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_42 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 42u32, "2026-10-26T10:24Z", "eoie", "06:24 eoie: slices C and D drafted, proofs queued on the worker node. C (heal-arith.do, 2 files, 4 replacements): the only host macros of agile_planning_tally and proxy_search_receipt were arithmetic and comparisons (\\$\"!a + !b\", \\$\"!found > !limit\", \\$\"!shown < !requested\"); now plain Spiral +, >, < on the same i32/u64 operands. Packed with slice B into job-1793009631007-kit-heal-wave1 (regen agile_task_rename, agile_planning_tally, proxy_search_receipt; debug build; notes_cli, agile_cli, planning_cli, hash_cli; strict verdict). D (heal-expect.do, generated by make-heal-expect.ps1): 75 packages define the same local expect helper. 47 bodies are a panic host macro and 28 already use failwith, and lib testing.spi defines it too. D swaps the 47 panic bodies to failwith (47 files in 41 packages, one line each, no call-site change), removing 47 host macros (about 3% of 1,609). Proof: job-1793010256422-kit-neutral-expect, the new closure-aware codegen-neutral run over the 85 crates the 41 packages reach. Follow-up rule, set now: once D lands, the 75 local copies become calls to lib testing.expect for packages on spiral- (one rewrite pass over call sites, skipping strings and macros), since a missing generic helper belongs in lib and this one already exists there. Also fixed on the way: node-candidate and land-post-rename read only the first 'expect files N replacements M' header of a .do, so multi-file programs would have been refused. Both now sum every header.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_43 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 43u32, "2026-10-26T11:10Z", "eoie", "07:10 eoie: follow-up converter for slice D written and tested (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\expect-to-lib.ps1 -Root [-Apply]). For packages on spiral-, it removes the local failwith expect definition and qualifies every call as lib testing.expect, outside strings and macro bodies. It refuses packages not on the lib (7: agile_note_model, agile_todo_model, kit_bundle_manifest, kit_cli_model, kit_do_facts, kit_do_program, kit_machine_watch_model), packages with their own testing module, and packages with a module-qualified expect. On a copy after slice D: 58 packages, 66 files, 66 definitions removed, 763 call sites qualified; 0 local definitions and 0 bare calls left, 0 files with changed string literals (expect-to-lib-test.ps1). The test caught a scanner bug first: a string literal holding one escaped backslash (the path_key replace in kit_tree_lease and kit_env_pins) never closed, because the scan only checked the previous character, so code and strings swapped roles for the rest of the file (15 calls missed). Fixed with escape skipping. The same bug was in global-wrapper.ps1 (it could rename rust_global inside strings) and cons-to-sugar.ps1, both fixed and reshipped before their node proofs start; cons-to-sugar-test has a regression case for it (10/10). Order: D, then this converter, each with its own codegen-neutral node proof. Also new: slice E drafted, heal-title-notes.do (agile_title_notes' two cross-crate export calls into agile_title_tails become Spiral calls, with kept_end made public and title_tails' arithmetic macro healed; 4 files, 6 replacements). Queued as job-1793011673943-kit-heal-wave2, which reports generated sizes, since title_notes.rs is 793 lines before inlining.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_44 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 44u32, "2026-10-26T12:53Z", "eoie", "08:53 eoie: slice F drafted, kit_dev_spiral_tests' registry. It was a hand-rolled list union (NoMoreNotebooks | SpiralTestNotebook : string * string * rest) nested 111 deep: 549 lines, 211 KB, up to 826 columns, with paths split by ^^ mid-word. Now a record type spiral_test_notebook = { package; relative } and one list literal of 111 rows (123 lines, 10 KB, every line within 120), and selected_notebooks folds it with [] / entry :: rest. The registry is the source of kit-dev --test's 111 notebooks: the generated rows equal the test-phase list in set and order, 0 duplicates. Generator clean\\kit-rename\\make-registry-list.ps1 (-Add 'package=src/path' appends rows, which is how kit_lint_macros/lint.spi gets registered once the lint verb lands); applier apply-registry-list.ps1 (whole-file registry plus the anchored fold edit; a second run refuses). Proof queued: job-1793019273168-kit-registry-list (regen kit_dev_spiral_tests, debug build, 'kit-dev --test --dry-run --root <mirror>' saved to smoke.log and counted, plus sequences.spi with the rust default target).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_45 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 45u32, "2026-10-26T13:06Z", "eoie", "09:06 eoie: correction, slices B and E withdrawn. A macro that calls another crate's export (agile_task_rename into agile_rename_rewrite, agile_title_notes into agile_title_tails) is not drift. It is the hand-modeled build-unit seam: crates split by hand and joined by exports so cargo builds them in parallel. As one host call, it passes GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1). Inlining the Spiral package undoes the split: B's proof showed task_rename.rs growing from 440 to 932 lines, and kit_ci_parity (930 lines) would cross the 1,000-line gate. Rule, set now: cross-crate export calls stay; slices target arithmetic, casts, flow, blocks and multi-statement host text inside a package. Effect: from heal-wave1 only C (heal-arith.do, agile_planning_tally and proxy_search_receipt) lands; B (heal-task-rename.do) and E (heal-title-notes.do) don't. E's step in the node runner still runs, but only as a measurement. The census probe should not count export-call macros as violations; this goes to GRANULAR-LINT's classifier as a class of its own.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_46 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 46u32, "2026-10-26T13:18Z", "eoie", "09:18 eoie: slice D proven codegen-neutral (job-1793010256422-kit-neutral-expect, finished before its queue-eaten cap). heal-expect.do applied (47 files, 47 replacements); the 41 touched packages reach 85 crates; regen of all 85 exited 0. 1 of 260 generated files differed, kit_table_query/table_query.rs, and the diff is exactly the quoted-CSV line from the gate fix: present in that crate's .spi, not yet in its committed .rs. So every expect body swap generates identical Rust, and slice D lands post-rename as is. Tool caveat, fixed next: node-neutral compares the regen with the committed .rs, so a source change not yet regenerated shows up as a false difference. Rule: node-neutral first regenerates the same crates without the program (baseline), then with it, and compares those two runs; the committed .rs is only reported.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_47 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 47u32, "2026-10-26T13:38Z", "eoie", "09:38 eoie: slice G drafted and queued, arithmetic and comparison host macros to Spiral operators, in two converters with dry-run listings reviewed line by line. (1) scratch clean\\list-sugar\\arith-to-spiral.ps1: \\$\"!a op !b\" with both operands Spiral values (61 sites in 26 files; == becomes =, != becomes <>, each rewrite parenthesized so precedence is unchanged). Its 26 packages reach 68 crates; proof is step 7 of job-1793019819577-kit-proof-runner (applier apply-arith.ps1 refuses unless exactly 61 sites in 26 files change; regen of the 68; all 111 test-phase notebooks with the rust default target). Not codegen-neutral by design: Spiral emits wrapping_add where Rust's + in a release build already wraps, so results match. (2) arith-literals-to-spiral.ps1: one operand a literal (30 sites in 16 files). Spiral literals need a suffix, so u64 when the operand is declared u64 on the line or the helper is named u64_*, else i32; the regen's type check catches a wrong guess. Proof job-1793021920542-kit-arith-literals (67 crates, all 111 notebooks), queued behind the runner with a 24 h cap since queue time counts. Together, 91 host macros out of 1,609 (5.7%).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_48 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 48u32, "2026-10-26T13:49Z", "eoie", "09:49 eoie: slice H drafted, cast macros to conv (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\casts-to-conv.ps1): \\$'!x as T' : T becomes (conv x : T), 10 sites in 9 files, 0 skipped (a cast whose target differs from its ascription is left alone). Semantics checked in the compiler before drafting: the Rust backend emits TyConv as '({value} as {type})' (spiral_compiler.fs ~164692), the same wrapping 'as' the macros used, so values are identical; only parentheses may differ in the output. Packed into the literal-arithmetic proof (the queued job had not started): job-1793022609461-kit-arith-literals, appliers apply-arith-literals.ps1 plus apply-casts.ps1 (each refuses unless its proven site count changes), 76 crates, all 111 notebooks with the rust default target, queued behind the runner. Running total of drafted host-macro removals: D 47, G 61 + 30, H 10 = 148 of 1,609 (9.2%), plus 13 packages newly declaring the contract.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_49 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 49u32, "2026-10-26T13:54Z", "eoie", "09:54 eoie: slice I drafted, integer-to-text macros to lib sm'.show (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\int-text-to-show.ps1): \\$'std::rc::Rc::<str>::from((!x).to_string()[.as_str()])' : string becomes (sm'.show x), 32 sites in 30 files (helpers like i32_text, u64_text, decimal_text, count_text). Safety rules: integer operands only (declared i32/u32/i64/u64 on the line, or a helper named i32_/u64_*_text), because float formatting may differ between Rust to_string and show; packages already on spiral- only. 17 skipped and listed (12 without a visible integer type, 5 in packages not on the lib). Packed as the third applier into job-1793022930397-kit-arith-literals (123 crates now, all 111 notebooks, rust default target). Drafted removals now 180 of 1,609 host macros (11.2%): D 47, G 91, H 10, I 32.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_50 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 50u32, "2026-10-26T13:59Z", "eoie", "09:59 eoie: slice J drafted, plain print macros to lib console (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\print-to-console.ps1): \\$'println\\!(\"{}\", &*!x)' : () becomes console.write_line x, and eprintln becomes console.write_error_line x, 35 sites in 25 files. Same output by construction: lib console's Rust arms are println!/eprintln!('{}', obj). Skipped: packages not on spiral- (3 files) and any package with its own console module (workspace_primitives has one, so the bare name would be ambiguous). Packed as the fourth applier: job-1793023261260-kit-arith-literals (50 touched packages, 123 crates, all 111 notebooks, rust default target). Drafted removals now 215 of 1,609 host macros (13.4%).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_51 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 51u32, "2026-10-26T14:11Z", "eoie", "10:11 eoie: the global-wrapper proof caught a real converter bug (runner step kit-neutral-global, regen of 105 crates exit 1: 'ETY-ModuleIndexFailed: The module does not have the key: global' in rust_std_fs/runtime_main.spi). Five calls in four files are module-qualified (filesystem_terminal.rust_global, fs_actions_terminal., process., toolchain_terminal.), and the converter renamed them in place to X.global, but core global is unqualified. Fixed: a rust_global after a dot also drops its qualifier chain. Probe: qualified and plain calls both become global, a string mentioning rust_global is untouched. Mirror resynced, rerun queued: job-1793023955068-kit-neutral-global2.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_52 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 52u32, "2026-10-26T16:04Z", "eoie", "12:04 eoie: slice F proven on the worker node (proof runner step kit-registry-list). The applier wrote the 111-row list literal and the fold rewrite; regen of kit_dev_spiral_tests exit 0, spiral_tests.rs 647 lines (unchanged size, now from a 10 KB source instead of 211 KB); debug build 0; 'kit-dev --test --dry-run --root <mirror>' exits 0 and announces exactly 111 notebooks (smoke.log), the same set the old union produced. The step's one notebook verdict (sequences.spi 0/1) is the separate static-cell issue (KIT-RENAME note 42), not the registry. Lands post-rename via apply-registry-list.ps1; afterwards '-Add kit-lint-macros=src/kit_lint_macros/lint.spi' registers the lint notebook once the verb lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_53 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 53u32, "2026-10-26T16:31Z", "eoie", "12:31 eoie: slice K drafted, string builders to lib with exact semantics (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\string-builders-to-lib.do, 4 files, 4 replacements): kit_command_line lowered becomes sm'.to_lower (lib Rust arm is to_lowercase); proxy_text_replacement replaced_everywhere becomes sm'.replace needle replacement text (lib Rust arm is the same replace); agile_todo_roots forward becomes sm'.replace with a backslash and a slash; agile_planning_rollup id_symbol becomes sm'.replace '-' '_' of sm'.to_lower. Left as they were on purpose: to_ascii_lowercase sites (lib to_lower is Unicode to_lowercase, which differs on non-ASCII), trim sites (lib trim uses its own char list, not Rust's Unicode whitespace), packages not on spiral-, and multi-line builders. Proof queued: job kit-string-lib (4 crates, all 111 notebooks, rust default target).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_54 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 54u32, "2026-10-26T18:20Z", "kit", "14:20 kit: slice D2 (D's follow-up, expect to lib testing.expect) queued for its node proof. Lib's definition is the same body as D's local copies (':= expect (condition : bool) (detail : string) : () = ? condition ?> () |: failwith detail', testing.spi:334). The locals take ~detail, though, so the generated Rust may differ around literal messages while the behavior stays the same. That makes this a candidate proof (regen, debug build, all 111 notebooks, rust default target), not a codegen-neutral one. Scope, from a scratch copy with heal-expect.do then expect-to-lib.ps1 applied (expect-lib-scope.ps1): 58 packages, 66 files, 66 local definitions dropped, 763 call sites qualified, 7 packages refused (not on the lib), 85 crates reach a touched package. The applier (list-sugar\\apply-expect-to-lib.ps1) refuses unless exactly that scope applies. Step kit-expect-lib was appended to job-1793037999592-kit-requeue-runner while it was still queued, on mirror expect1 (checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658). land-post-rename lands it last among the gated slices behind marker expect-lib.proven, after D (heal-expect.do). Once landed: 47 host macros gone (D's panic bodies) and 66 duplicate helper definitions folded into lib.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_55 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 55u32, "2026-10-26T18:37Z", "kit", "14:37 kit: slice N drafted, path predicates to lib file_system (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\path-predicates-to-lib.do, 3 files, 3 replacements, 5 host macros). Path::new(p).is_dir() becomes file_system.directory_exists p, and is_file() becomes file_system.file_exists p, in kit_grep_query root_kind, kit_style_census is_dir and agile_todo_scan path_is_dir/path_is_file. Same value by construction: lib's Rust arm is exists() && is_dir() (is_file()), and is_dir() already implies exists(). Rules applied: packages on spiral- only (5 sites in 4 packages without the lib were skipped: bundle_output_contract, filesystem_directory_contract, inspection_negative, rust_std_fs), no local module named file_system in any dependency, and Path::new(p).exists() (3 sites) left alone because lib has no exact twin. Census for the next slices (scratch list-sugar\\macro-census.ps1): 849 host macros in 198 files. The top family, 47 panic bodies, is slice D. Char-code separators (14, chars 0/30/31) are joined-string rows, which need typed records, so they get no mechanical swap. Proof: step kit-path-predicates (6th) of job-1793037999592-kit-requeue-runner, mirror path1 checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658, 3 crates, all 111 notebooks. It lands behind marker path-predicates.proven.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_56 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 56u32, "2026-10-26T18:54Z", "kit", "14:53 kit: slice O drafted, argument counts to lib env (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\argument-counts-to-lib.do, 4 files, 5 replacements, 5 host macros). std::env::args().count() as i32 becomes env.host_argument_count (). Each skip(2).count() helper becomes a binding of env.host_argument_count () with '? count > 2i32 ?> count - 2i32 |: 0i32', because skip clamps at zero and plain count - 2 would not. kit_agile_mutation's inline copy at the AgileAdd route calls that file's agile_argument_count (). Lib's count goes through convert.convert (try_convert then value), which panics where the macro's 'as i32' would wrap, but only above 2^31 arguments, which no OS command line allows. Left out on purpose: the nth(index) argument readers (lib host_argument panics on a negative index where the macro returns an empty string), the .any(flag) tests (no lib twin), and the 3 packages not on spiral- (agile_task_creation, agile_task_listing, kit_machine_watch). Proof: step kit-argument-counts (7th) of job-1793037999592-kit-requeue-runner, mirror args1 checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658, 7 crates, all 111 notebooks. It lands behind marker argument-counts.proven.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_57 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 57u32, "2026-10-26T19:20Z", "kit", "15:20 kit: slice N widened before its step ran: agile_backlog_scaffold directory_ready's std::fs::create_dir_all(p).is_ok() becomes file_system.create_dir_all p |> resultm.is_err |> not. Lib makes the same std::fs::create_dir_all call and returns its Result, so this equals is_ok. It is the only lib-package site of that family; the other 3 are in packages without the lib. Now 4 files, 4 replacements, 6 host macros. The step's crate set is agile_backlog_scaffold, agile_todo_scan, kit_grep_query and kit_style_census, and the plan was reshipped while the runner was still queued. Left as they are, with the reason: std::fs::write(p, x).is_ok() (lib write_all_text unwraps, so it would panic where the macro returns false) and std::process::id() (lib runtime has only a child process's id).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_58 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 58u32, "2026-10-26T21:00Z", "kit", "17:00 kit: slice G (arithmetic and comparison macros to Spiral operators, 61 sites in 26 files) is PROVEN, marker arith.proven written by the marker rule. Evidence: arith1 passed 107/111 on 1207 E9545E46E6EF3E4F. Its 4 failures were all outside the slice: windows_cli and sequences predate the cold-proof relabel and the sequences directive, and table_cli quoted CSV and table_test where-comparisons ran against the committed table_query.rs, which lagged its source. The same two table failures appeared in the string-lib proof, which touched neither crate. The targeted rerun kit-arith-2 (mirror arith3, 1207 C9520BC87568C658, re-synced after FOLLOWUP's regen, the same 68-crate regen and applier, -Only matching 7 notebooks) passed 7/7, including all four. Applier review of the comparisons that kit_table_test's closure reaches (i32_eq/i32_le now Spiral = and <=, the u64 helpers, two offset sums) agrees: the where-filter difference came from stale output, not from these edits. Lands post-rename through land-post-rename (gated slice 'arith').")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_59 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 59u32, "2026-10-26T21:30Z", "kit", "17:30 kit: slice K (string builders to lib) is PROVEN, marker string-lib.proven. strlib1 passed 107/111; its 4 failures (notes_cli x2, table_cli, table_test) came from stale committed outputs and ps_cli's ancestry flake. The targeted rerun kit-string-lib-2 (mirror strlib2, 1207 C9520BC87568C658, re-synced after FOLLOWUP's regen) passed all 4, ps_cli included. Lib's to_lower Rust arm is the same to_lowercase call as the macro it replaces.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_60 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 60u32, "2026-10-26T21:50Z", "kit", "17:50 kit: two verdicts. (1) Slice H (rust_global wrappers to core global, 110 files, 100 local definitions removed, 256 calls) is PROVEN codegen-neutral: kit-neutral-global2 on mirror global2 (1207 C9520BC87568C658, re-synced after FOLLOWUP's regen), regen of 105 crates exit 0, verdict exit 0, so the generated Rust is identical with and without it. Marker global.proven. (2) The literal-arithmetic proof (kit-arith-literals) failed at regen, and the failure was real. proxy_failure_classification:27, '$\"!matches > 0\"' had become '(matches > 0i32)', but matches is a u64 (it is printed with u64_text). The converter guessed: u64 only when declared ': u64' on the same line, otherwise i32. The rule is now evidence only: the integer type is read from the variable's declaration inside the enclosing definition ('(name : T)', '(~name : T)', ':= name : T ='), or for arithmetic from the macro's result ascription. With no evidence, the site is skipped and listed. On the tree that gives 10 typed sites in 7 files, with 20 skipped (helpers with untyped value parameters, the operands counters, and all three failure_classification sites). The applier pins that scope. Requeued as kit-arith-literals-2 on mirror arith4 (checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658), together with casts, int-text and print as before: job-1793051402928-kit-literals-runner, after the current runner.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_61 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 61u32, "2026-10-26T22:02Z", "kit", "18:02 kit: two more verdicts. (1) The unused rust_std_string drop (drop-unused-std-string.do, 7 packages, 37 crates reach them) is PROVEN codegen-neutral (kit-neutral-stdstr on stdstr1, 1207 C9520BC87568C658, regen exit 0, verdict exit 0). Marker stdstr.proven. (2) Slice M (heal-e3) failed at regen, a real bug in the healer: in agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi:99, 'rust_std_string.model.equals input (agile_subcommand_spec subcommand).name' became '(input = (agile_subcommand_spec subcommand)).name', a record compared with a string. heal-crate-e3's argument reader stopped at a parenthesized group's closing paren and left the trailing field access outside. Fixed: after any argument it now consumes chained .field accessors, so line 99 becomes '(input = (agile_subcommand_spec subcommand).name)'; line 108's 'spec.name' was already right. Also found by the composition check and fixed earlier: apply-heal-e3 could not find heal-crate-e3.ps1 locally. Requeued as kit-heal-e3-2 on mirror heal5 (checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658), the second step of job-1793051402928-kit-literals-runner, after kit-arith-literals-2.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_62 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 62u32, "2026-10-26T23:31Z", "kit", "19:31 kit: slice D2 (expect to lib testing.expect) FAILED its proof (expect1: regen and debug build passed for 85 crates; 10 CLI notebooks failed TYPE-ERROR 'Unbound term variable: expect'). The converter qualified calls only inside files that held a definition. Two kinds of user were left with bare calls: sibling files that reach expect through an open (kit_ci_parity's parity.spi opens model), and packages that reach a provider's module through notebook-level package wiring no spiproj shows (kit_agile_contracts, kit_contracts, kit_archive_contracts and others open kit_cli_check's cli, and are not on the lib). Its own test counted bare calls with the same narrow view, so it reported 0. Rewritten: every lib package using bare expect gets qualified calls in all its files. A provider whose module is opened or depended on by a package that cannot move to the lib (not on spiral-, its own testing module, or a qualified expect) keeps its definition and is refused. After rewriting, a scan fails the run if any package has a bare expect with no definition in reach (own file, dependency, or opened module, testing included). Opened modules are matched by base name, so common names like 'model' over-refuse, which is safe. New scope: 43 providers, 45 packages, 58 files, 47 definitions removed, 580 calls, unbound 0, 22 refused (kit_cli_check keeps its harness expect). D plus D2 touch 59 packages and 97 crates. Requeued as kit-expect-lib-2 on mirror expect2 (checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658), the third step of job-1793051402928-kit-literals-runner.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_63 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 63u32, "2026-10-27T01:22Z", "kit", "21:22 kit: slice O (argument counts to lib env, 4 files, 5 replacements) is PROVEN. kit-argument-counts on mirror args1 (1207 C9520BC87568C658, synced after FOLLOWUP's regen): regen of 7 crates exit 0, every generated file within 1,000 lines, debug build 0, 111/111 notebooks. Marker argument-counts.proven. Proven so far: list index, registry list (F), arithmetic (G), rust_global cleanup (H), std-string drop, string builders (K), path predicates (N), argument counts (O). Still on the node: arith-literals-2, heal-e3-2 and expect-lib-2 (literals runner, after union arm A).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_64 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 64u32, "2026-10-27T02:56Z", "kit", "22:56 kit: literal-arithmetic set (kit-arith-literals-2, mirror arith4): regen of 123 crates exit 0, debug build 0, 111/111 notebooks, but NOT proven. kit_experiment_analysis\\analysis.rs grew from 992 to 1,006 lines, over the 1,000 gate, from its one cast edit: analysis.spi:118 '$''!count as i32'' : i32' became '(conv count : i32)', and conv expands to more Rust than a bare cast. Rule applied: no slice may push a crate over the gate. casts-to-conv now holds back packages near the gate (-NearLineGate, default kit_experiment_analysis), and the applier pins 9 sites in 8 files with 1 package held back. That crate's split, not a cast swap, is the fix for it. 13 other crates in this run sit at 950-998 lines, so any slice touching them must be re-measured. Requeued as kit-arith-literals-3 on mirror arith5 (checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658), job-1793069855657-kit-literals3-runner, after the current literals runner.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_65 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 65u32, "2026-10-27T03:38Z", "kit", "23:38 kit: slice M (heal-e3: 6 packages off rust_std_string onto the lib, with standards HostFree/LibReferenced merged) is PROVEN with the fixed healer. kit-heal-e3-2 on mirror heal5 (1207 C9520BC87568C658): regen of 10 crates exit 0, every generated file within 1,000 lines, debug build 0, 111/111 notebooks. agile_subcommand_domain line 99 now keeps its field access inside the comparison. Marker heal-e3.proven. Nine slices are proven; left are expect-lib-2 (running) and arith-literals-3 (queued).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_66 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 66u32, "2026-10-27T04:47Z", "kit", "00:47 kit: slice D2 (expect to lib testing.expect, the open-aware converter) is PROVEN. kit-expect-lib-2 on mirror expect2 (1207 C9520BC87568C658), applied after slice D: regen of 97 crates exit 0, every generated file within 1,000 lines, debug build 0, 111/111 notebooks, including the 10 CLI notebooks that failed with unbound expect before. Marker expect-lib.proven. Ten slices are proven; only arith-literals-3 (casts hold back kit_experiment_analysis) is still on the node.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_67 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 67u32, "2026-10-27T05:56Z", "kit", "01:56 kit: literal-arithmetic set, third proof (kit-arith-literals-3, mirror arith5): regen exit 0, debug build 0, 111/111 notebooks, and still kit_experiment_analysis\\analysis.rs at 1,006 against 992 (gate 1,000). Holding the package back didn't help, because none of the four appliers edits analysis.spi itself. The growth comes from its dependency closure, inlined into the crate: casts in kit_table_query\\stats.spi as_f64; int-text in kit_experiments\\ledger_host, kit_receipt\\receipt, kit_table_query\\cells and workspace_primitives\\numbers (i32_text, u64_text, decimal_text become sm'.show); print in kit_experiments\\ledger_host and kit_table_query\\cells. Holding those shared packages back would gut the slice for every other crate. Decision, pre-registered: the literal set waits on splitting kit_experiment_analysis below the gate (hand-modeled build unit, as with GRANULAR-LINT), then it is re-proven unchanged. The near-gate hold-back in casts-to-conv stays as a guard. This does not block the landing: land-post-rename skips any slice without its marker, so the other ten proven slices land without it. 13 more crates sit at 950-998 lines in this run, so watch them for the same effect.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_68 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 68u32, "2026-10-27T09:05Z", "kit", "05:1x kit: kit_experiment_analysis attribution (analysis.rs 992 lines, read-only): 44 fns 913 lines, 26 types 71, 1 closure 5. Largest: analyzed_plan 226, analyzed 63, plan_trusted 53 (inlined from kit_experiments.experiment_plan), terms_of 45, tallied 39, test_of 39, test_plan_of 37, values_of 36, prediction_of 36. The literal set needs about 15 lines of headroom (it adds 14). Plan, decided by size: (1) in analyzed_plan, the disclosure and verdict branches each inline a concat5 of three field_line calls plus a text_appended or text_created; one :~ helper for a three-field record body serves both branches; (2) the plan-reading helpers that are inlined from kit_experiments (plan_trusted, test_of, test_plan_of, prediction_of, band_of, second_arm_of: about 200 lines) become :~ in experiment_plan, which also shrinks every other crate that inlines them; this needs a regen of the crates reaching kit_experiments, and that regen can ride the next release. (1) is local to this package; per the AGENTS contract, touching analyzed_plan means migrating it (host macros to lib). Blocked until kit-release-SLICES ends (tree lease); the literal set is re-proven after (1)+(2).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_69 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 69u32, "2026-10-27T11:44Z", "kit", "07:5x kit: the literal set no longer waits on a split. Attribution (probe copy of the live tree, the 4 appliers applied, analysis.rs compiled and diffed per function): the +14 lines are format_real (+8) and one helper (+6) from sm'.show's generic fallback. They enter only through three i32_text definitions that are inlined into kit_experiment_analysis (kit_experiments/ledger_host, kit_table_query/cells, workspace_primitives/numbers), not through any analysis code. Two cut attempts failed and were reverted: concat5 as :~ (991) and one shared record-body helper (1008); field_line is already :~. Decision: int-text-to-show.ps1 gains -HoldBack for those 3 files (default on; each skip names the reason). With it analysis.rs stays at 992. The applier pin moves from 32 sites/30 files/17 skipped to 28/27/20. Composed on a fresh copy, the set touches 40 packages and reaches 90 crates. Node proof kit-literals5-proof (job-1793101555203, mirror lit5, digest 1223 5D1774A26152ED00 asserted, compiler 4FD712E9 pinned, fresh lib mirror): the 4 appliers, a regen of the 90 crates (line gate per crate), the debug build, and the 111 test-phase notebooks. A green verdict creates arith-literals.proven, and land-post-rename then lands the set. The three held-back definitions get a TODO: EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION marker when the set lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_70 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 70u32, "2026-10-27T14:33Z", "kit", "10:3x kit: the literal set is PROVEN with the hold-back (node kit-literals5-proof): 90-crate regen exit 0, 0 generated files over 1000 (kit_experiment_analysis stays 992), debug build 0, notebooks 110/111. The one failure is unrelated: a test block from KIT-PENDING-DANGLING (toolchain.spi references files.pending_path, unbound in a test context); fix staged. arith-literals.proven is written (by hand, with the evidence in the marker), so land-post-rename lands the set in the next train, after the SLICES2 rerun publishes.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_71 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 71u32, "2026-10-27T19:29Z", "kit", "kit 15:3x: census of the host macros left after TRAIN (the post-train rehearsal tree, with all four literal appliers applied; read-only). 742 macros in 549 distinct normalized shapes, so the applier route has hit diminishing returns: the most common shape covers 15 sites. By class (a macro can fall in two): Rc::<str>::from string building 132 in 59 packages; std::fs or Path 79 in 25; .len() 32 in 22; env 29 in 20; separator chars (0, 9, 30, 31) 27 in 18; .parse::< 25 in 15; tuple field access 14 in 8; process 13 in 9. Two candidate appliers were checked and rejected. (a) char::from(N) to unicode.char_of, 14 sites: every one is the separator of a joined-string row protocol, which AGENTS bans outright, so the heal is typed columns, not a respelled separator. (b) fs predicates to file_system.file_exists or directory_exists: only 8 exact sites, one inside the rust_std_fs boundary itself. write_all_text and file_delete return unit, not the error-tolerant bool of the host forms they would replace, so swapping them would change failure behavior. Recommendation: migrate per package from here, largest owners first, each slice proven by byte-identical CLI output (the 10-27 lint split's method). The separator protocols (18 packages) go to typed parallel columns across Exports, which also drops their census rows.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_72 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 72u32, "2026-10-27T22:22Z", "eoie", "18:2x census for picking slices: the same host-macro body written over and over across packages. scratchpad clean\\lib-clip\\host-dupes.ps1 groups every ':=/:~ name ... $'...'' definition in kit/src by its normalized body (spliced names folded). Of 1029 host-macro definitions, 73 bodies repeat across 2+ packages, which is 202 duplicate definitions. Top groups, with the lib fn that should replace each one (check each Rust arm first: LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES found F#-only bodies in sm'/am'):\n- first_line, first_of, line_head: 7 packages. Candidate: sm'.lines + head.\n- line_rest, other_lines, rest_lines, rest_of: 7 packages. Split at the first newline; no lib fn yet, so this is a lib gap.\n- *_text (to_string of numbers): 6 packages. Candidate: sm'.format or obj_to_string.\n- unit/field separators (char 31): 6 packages. A char-code constant, lib gap.\n- all_digits, ascii_digits: 5 packages. Lib gap.\n- path_joined, joined, fixture_path: 5 packages. Candidate: file_system.(</>).\n- file_text, fixture_read, text_of: 5 packages. Candidate: file_system.read_all_text, Rust arm to check.\n- Then part_at, padded, text_between and option_value (4 packages each), and file_exists, create_dir, file_size, write_text and now_ms (3 each).\nFirst slice done the same way: the 3 identical 'clipped' helpers become lib sm'.clip (EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT note 4; drafts lib-clip.do and kit-clip.do; rustc probe ok on non-ASCII). Next slice by this census: the two newline groups (14 definitions in 7 packages), one lib fn each with a rust+fsharp test block, then call sites by anchored do. The census belongs in kit standards as a verb (generic-tools rule) rather than a scratch script: a candidate for EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET, 'duplicate host bodies' as a counted metric.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_73 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 73u32, "2026-10-27T22:29Z", "eoie", "18:29 Second slice from the census drafted: the newline pair. lib-clip.do now adds sm'.first_line and sm'.after_first_line beside clip: pure Spiral over index_of and slice, which are byte-consistent on Rust, so a cut at the ASCII newline never lands mid-character. They sit before ellipsis_end because index_of is defined at line 865, and the first placement gave ETY-UnboundVariable index_of. The fsharp+rust test block covers non-ASCII text, no newline, a trailing newline and a leading newline. A rustc probe printed [é1|b<newline>c][only|][end|][|x][|] and exited 0. kit-lines.do replaces the bodies of the 14 duplicated host definitions in 7 packages (agile_memory_links, agile_todo_record, kit_command_line, kit_memory_model, kit_standards, kit_style_census, kit_table_logs) with sm'.first_line or sm'.after_first_line, keeping each package's local name and its :=/:~ marker, so the 53 call sites stay as they are. It applies 7/7 on package copies and composes with kit-clip.do. All 7 packages already reference spiral-. Left: a regen plus the crate gate in the release after TRAIN, which needs lib-clip landed first (lib window after TRAIN, lib gate). 16 of 202 duplicate host definitions are covered so far (clip 3 including the note-edit receipt use, newline pair 14).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_74 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 74u32, "2026-10-27T23:34Z", "eoie", "19:3x Node regen proof of both slices (bundle = live kit src + drafts as a candidate against the live tree as base, each with its own lib snapshot; box DLL 4FD712E9E357; 39 crates per side). Run 2 (job-1793141589342) found two problems. (a) Pure-Spiral first_line/after_first_line grow crates: at := call sites the index_of/slice body gets inlined, putting kit_standards at 1042, kit_style_census at 1023 and agile_todo_record at 1005. Fix: both lib fns now have a Rust arm with the exact one-line host text the kit copies used, and the pure-Spiral form stays as the fallback for other backends. (b) kit_memory_query also called kit_memory_model's clipped; it now uses sm'.clip, and its package gains spiral-. Run 3 (job-1793142539742): all 7 newline packages and all 3 clip packages regenerate byte-identical to the base (agile_memory_links, agile_todo_record, kit_command_line, kit_standards, kit_style_census, kit_table_logs, kit_memory_import, agile_task_listing, agile_task_next, kit_memory, ...). So migrating these host copies to the lib changes no generated code. Intended diffs: agile_note_edit 845->855 (replaced= receipt), kit_job_launch 601->714 (settle receipt, below), and kit_jobs/kit_job_start_args (usage text). Nothing is over 1000. That run still built kit_memory_query from a bundle without the spiproj edit, so it failed on sm' unbound; recheck job-1793144013979 is running. Method: a host body that a lib fn with an identical Rust arm can replace is a zero-codegen-diff slice. The census (host-dupes.ps1) ranks the remaining 186 duplicate definitions.")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_75 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 75u32, "2026-10-28T00:03Z", "eoie", "20:0x Third slice proven: lib-text.do adds sm'.decimal_text (generic over numbers; its Rust arm is the to_string host text, format elsewhere) and sm'.char_text (u8 code; Rust char::from host text, an F# arm, failwith on other backends, as unicode.scalar_text does), with a fsharp+rust test block. A rustc probe shows a literal code splices as char::from(31u8), the same text as before. kit-text.do swaps 19 host copies across 15 packages for these (decimal, decimal_text, count_text, u64_text, minutes_text; unit_mark, column x3, field_separator, args_mark; record_separator x2, record_mark x2; nul_text x2, separator) and adds spiral- to kit_do_program, kit_machine_watch and kit_machine_watch_model. workspace_primitives' i32_text/u64_text stay for now: it's the boundary package and reaches most crates. Node proof (job-1793144749129, box DLL 4FD712E9E357, with all four drafts in the candidate): 45 crates regenerate byte-identical, kit_do_program (999) included; the only diffs are the known agile_note_edit 855 and kit_job_launch 714. Tally: 3 lib slices retire 36 of the 202 duplicate host definitions with zero codegen change. The method's scripts are now in i574n.github/scripts: host-dupes-census.ps1 (the census), draft-regen-bundle.ps1 (base vs candidate bundle with a per-side lib and the crates that reach the change) and node-regen-compare.ps1 (node compile, identity per crate, VOID guard).")
inl note_of_eoie_granular_migration_76 () = Note ("EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", 76u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "eoie", "05:4x kit: this item's lib programs landed live in today's lib window. Gates are running on the node (P2 compiler pinned): see EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 05:4x note. The kit-side half follows in the next release.")
inl fsharp_warm_runner () = Task ("FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER", "F# native tier through one warm process (user 2026-10-18: speed up where F# is redundant). The tier runs each main.fsx in a cold dotnet fsi: in today's accept, 544 rows took 3,151 s summed (median 4.2 s, p90 10.2 s, about 26 min wall on 2 lanes), almost all of it startup. Like kotlin-server: a native-shims fsharp-runner keeps one FSI evaluation session warm, evaluates each script and returns its value and stdout. Target: the same outcomes as the cold tier, row for row, in under 5 min", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,fsharp,speed", "current", "current")
inl zig_primary_oracle () = Task ("ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE", "Make Zig the first oracle (-Oracle Zig,C) (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig). Measured 2026-10-17: -Oracle Zig and -Oracle C both give DISAGREE 0 on 5,388 rows; the only difference, dynamic_array_bounds_negative, has Known entries per oracle. Today's accept: C and Zig differ on 1 of 459 shared samples. The switch changes the baseline's oracle column, so it lands with its own reviewed accept", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,zig,oracle", "current", "current")
inl contracts_on_zig () = Task ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", "Measure moving the contracts suite's vehicle backend from F# to Zig (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig, F# redundant). 619 of the 626 samples with no C or Zig row are contracts, which test type-level behavior (expected errors, typestate) and use F# only as the codegen vehicle. Experiment: compile every contract to Zig and compare the compile verdicts and expected errors with today's F# rows; switch the suite or add Zig rows by the result", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,zig,contracts,experiment,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_contracts_on_zig () = Planning ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", Minutes 60u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_1 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:32 main-thread: experiment script lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 compiles each contract to Zig with the installed compiler, builds and runs the ok ones with zig ReleaseFast, and compares the compile verdict and exit with the accepted F# rows of 123705. Contract rows: 627 (413 compile errors, the negative contracts; 214 ok, which ran). Smoke on the first 6: 2 same, 4 where F# compiles but Zig refuses, all with the same cause: 'The return type of main in the Zig backend should be a 32-bit int (or unit)'. Contracts return a value (string, record) that F#'s tier treats as exit 0. Fixed in the tree, unbuilt: codegenZig's main accepts a single value of any type and discards it in spiralMain (return 0), matching F#; i32 and unit unchanged; tuples still rejected, with a clearer message. Next: build that, then run the full 627 to find the real feature gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_2 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:31 helper-lane lap 1: unblocked the full run: the codegenZig main change (a single value of any type is discarded, return 0; tuples rejected with a clearer message) is committed in e0ffc80b (10-18 14:50, the old message string is gone from spiral_compiler.fs) and in the installed Release compiler (built 10-18 22:57), so the smoke's 4 F#-ok/Zig-error rows should now compile. The baseline run single-flight-20261018-123705 still has its results.tsv. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 inside Invoke-Heavy (627 contract compiles to Zig + 214 zig builds, ~15 min), writes contracts-on-zig.tsv and the grouped zig gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_3 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:38 main heavy queue ran lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 (10:26-10:37, 669 s): all 627 contracts give the same compile verdict on Zig as on the accepted F# rows (0 F#-ok/Zig-error, 0 F#-error/Zig-ok); of the 214 that compile and run, exit codes match on 211 and differ on 3; table in lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/contracts-on-zig.tsv. So Zig can carry the contracts suite once the 3 exit differences are explained.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_4 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:46 helper-lane lap 3: explained main's 3 exit differences from contracts-on-zig.tsv against the 08:05 baseline: (1) contract_effectful_forall_bottom and (2) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals are F#-text samples ($ macro bodies in F# syntax, failwith and 1L literals), so Zig build-fails them by construction (all_terminals is also a Known Fsharp row); (3) mega_brzozowski_derivatives/proof_suite is an F# timeout in the measure run only: the baseline's F# row exits 0, the same as Zig. So all 627 agree once those two are kept on F#. Decision (autonomous, per the user's 10-18 promote-Zig, F#-redundant direction): SWITCH, not added rows; this also drops about 214 dotnet fsi native runs (median 7.2 s) for zig build-exe (about 2 s). Staged lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py (anchors --check OK on today's files, LF): test.ps1 gains Get-ContractBackends (Zig unless listed), used by the contract_ and mega_ branches of the contracts suite and by smoke's contract samples; matrix.psd1 gains FsharpContracts with the two F#-text samples. Caveats for the review: ExpectedErrors pins are type-check texts, but the tsv stores only a truncated first error line, so the review run is what proves the 236 pins on Zig; and the removed Fsharp contract rows need the staged accept-prune (SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS) or they stay in EXPECTED.tsv.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_5 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 5u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py dry run still matches today's apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 and tests/matrix.psd1 (anchors match). It is ready for a main pack slot, but test.ps1 is busy with main's probe-queue for about an hour, so it waits.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_6 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 6u32, "2026-10-22T06:38Z", "main", "02:38 main: switch.py's dry run still matches today's tmp/scripts/test.ps1 and tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 ('anchors match'). Slot: right AFTER the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT install, not inside its review, because moving 627 contract rows from Fsharp to Zig would hide compiler differences. Sequence: switch.py <tmp> --apply, then a single-flight accept run per the accept rules (reviewed rows; DISAGREE 0 on both cores; determinism check), where every moved row must keep its verdict (the 10-19 measure: all 627 agree once contract_effectful_forall_bottom and mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals stay on F#). Then delete the folder (measure.ps1 was the one-off experiment). Its output contracts-on-zig.tsv moved to the run cache (lab-outputs/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG, sha256 checked).")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_7 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 7u32, "2026-10-22T22:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:11 main: staged in the post-install matrix bundle %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 (dry-run clean today), applied after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed; details and rule in ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY note 11.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_8 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 8u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:22 main: status fixed (staged / half landed). %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 is still dry-run clean on today's tree (all anchors match; Lean 3 pins). It applies after the next4/5f composite, which carries next3b, is reviewed and installed (scratch next-session.md step 7).")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_9 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:34 (subagent): blocked: switch.py + contracts review queued (matrix inputs are main's) -> next: read the review; accept with prune; Done | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): switch.py queued (matrix input) -> next: contracts review + accept with prune | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: switch.py dry run matches; matrix input, main's pack -> next: main's pack slot")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_10 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 10u32, "2026-10-27T08:17Z", "main", "10-27 04:17 main: 10-27 04:17 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The 10-22 post-install matrix bundle (TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1) was NEVER APPLIED, although its stated condition (apply after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed) has been met for days (5n and later packs installed since). A dry run now: all anchors still match, for ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (Zig shims for the abi_external samples), CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG (contract rows move from Fsharp to Zig), and LEAN-BACKEND (pin v4.35.0-rc4 -> v4.35.0 stable, 3 pins in apps/cells/README.md). Not applied tonight on purpose: it changes which rows the matrix runs, so it must not land in the middle of the compiler lane's review or accept, and the Lean pin needs the stable toolchain installed first (otherwise every Lean row fails). Offered to the compiler lane, which runs the matrices; it lands right after their current review, then the next matrix run proves it.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_11 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 11u32, "2026-10-27T09:25Z", "main", "10-27 05:25 main: 10-27 05:25 main: Matrix-bundle review single-flight-20261027-042215 (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, 7,367 rows): DISAGREE 0 under both oracles; the one agree-fail is the long-known dynamic_array_bounds_negative Rust row (known vs C). This item's clause is GREEN: all 620 contract rows that moved from Fsharp to Zig have the SAME compile status as their Fsharp baseline row in EXPECTED.tsv, and the same stdout wherever they compile (620 of 620, 0 different). The 408 compile errors are the suite's expected rejections (e.g. contract_block_inside_phrasing: Unification failure, child flow vs child phrasing). Method trap, fixed in my own check: a first comparison filtered ids on ^samples/contract and silently compared only 331 rows; 289 contract-suite rows have other names. Still open: the bundle accept.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_12 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 12u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "main", "10-27 19:20 main: Bundle accept GREEN (accept-6, job-1793136844465, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 17:34 to 19:1x; ran in slot 2 against the TRAIN-frozen lib). test.ps1 exit 0; 7,367 rows accepted into spiral-bin baseline EXPECTED.tsv, with toolchain.tsv recorded (SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2). Against review single-flight-20261027-042215 under the n16/n17 rule (set before running): 2 differing cells, both ALLOWED, 0 blocking. samples/portable_convert_parse on Rust and on Lua is residual-only (same exit and stdout) and calls lib convert, which moved after the review (radix land 15:40). The accept-2 blocker is gone: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] ran within the fixed budget (test.ps1's F# native paths now honour max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), so its matrix Timeouts 180 applies). The Zig coverage slice, the 620-row contracts on Zig and the Lean clauses of this bundle now stand as the oracle. Baseline backup in TEMP matrix-bundle/baseline-before-accept-20261027-173412. Unblocks: closing lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (spent; bundle.ps1 needs no re-apply now), the CCMR land (it waits on this plus TRAIN), and then the pack-5n install.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_13 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 13u32, "2026-10-27T23:30Z", "main", "10-27 19:30 main: Groom 10-27 19:3x: the experiment in the title is DONE. Every contract compiles to Zig, and the verdicts agree with the F# rows on 620/620 (bundle clause, 10-27), and the Zig contract rows were ADDED and accepted into the oracle (accept-6 GREEN). What remains is the title's own last clause, 'switch the suite or add Zig rows by the result'. Zig rows are added; the switch, making Zig the contracts' only vehicle and dropping the 619 F#-vehicle rows (the user called F# redundant on 10-18), is a technical call by rule. Proposed rule, set now: switch once one more full accept reproduces the 620/620 agreement, since a second agreeing accept shows the Zig vehicle is stable, and dropping the F# rows also cuts their native time (dotnet fsi about 7 s a row under load). Until then both vehicles run.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_14 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:29Z", "main", "10-28 06:29 main: DONE (main groom 10-28 06:3x). The switch this item proposed has already happened: the matrix bundle made Zig the contracts suite's vehicle. Three full runs agree exactly: the review single-flight-20261027-042215, accept-6 -173417 and the 5n accept -232226 each have 620 Zig contract rows (212 compile ok, 408 the expected rejections), only 10 F# rows left in the suite, and DISAGREE 0. n13's 'switch after one more agreeing accept' is satisfied twice over, on two compilers (5m' and 5n). The F# vehicle rows are gone, so the 619 F# rows' native time is already saved. The 10 F# rows that remain are not vehicle rows; they test F# itself.")
inl zig_coverage_c_only () = Task ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", "Give the 38 C-only samples Zig rows where the feature exists (user 2026-10-18: promote Zig). The split from the 123705 accept: ABI shims 10, dynamic-array helpers 10, Rust-only 7, eBPF 6, native 2, Lua/Python/WGSL 1 each. ABI and dynamic-array samples need Zig counterparts of their C shims; Rust-only and eBPF stay single-backend by design. Afterwards C remains as a cross-check and product backend", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "matrix,zig,coverage,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_coverage_c_only () = Planning ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_1 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:35 main-thread probe with the installed compiler. The 38 C-only samples: abi_external_* (10) and dynamic_array_* (10) carry C shims through CFlags; rust_* (7) and python_macro_annotations are single-backend by design; native_managed_array_scc*_tail_recursion (2) carry C shims. 8 others fail to compile for Zig with 'Cannot find the backend Zig in the backend switch op', because their !!!!BackendSwitch has no Zig arm: lua_paren_statement (C (void)0 / Lua arms; a Zig arm of {} is an empty statement), the six ebpf_* samples (C and Ebpf arms plus a pure-Spiral Bend arm such as expect / 0i32, which Zig can share), and wgsl_parallel_lanes (to check). Plan after today's accept (samples stay frozen during review and accept): add those Zig arms, register the samples in the Zig list, and probe for agreement with C. The shim samples need Zig shims (zig build-exe main.zig plus the C shim, or Zig ports): a separate step.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_2 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:12 main-thread: staged lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/apply.ps1 (not applied; samples stay frozen until the pack 3 accept). It adds 13 Zig arms in 7 samples, checking that every BackendSwitch gets exactly one: the Bend-arm ebpf samples reuse the Bend value (expect or 0i32), ebpf_map_full maps both map helpers to expect, the assignment helpers in ebpf_map_full and ebpf_nested use the Zig spelling from while_loop, and lua_paren_statement gets unit. It also registers the 7 in the Zig list. The dry run passes. wgsl_parallel_lanes stays out: it is a Wgsl sample whose arms cover C, Fsharp, Rust and Delphi only")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_3 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 3u32, "2026-10-19T03:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 23:44 main-thread: applied after the pack 3 accept. Pack 4 review (single-flight-20261018-225858) shows the 7 new Zig rows (ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit, lua_paren_statement), all agreeing with C (DISAGREE 0). Pack 4 accept running")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_4 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:33 helper-lane lap 1: verified the 10-18 23:44 step landed: the accepted oracle (EXPECTED.tsv, 10-19 00:28) has Zig rows ran for ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit (exit 0) and lua_paren_statement (exit 7), committed in 668568ac. Recount from that oracle: 35 samples have a C ran row and no Zig row. Of them, 3 are ghosts (native_closure_managed_branch, native_fptr_basic, native_fptr_reuse: oracle rows exit 42, but their sample folders no longer exist (sources deleted on 09-29 in 7a5e4ed4)) and 1 is new and Zig-ready: qualified_union_case_no_payload compiles to Zig with the installed compiler and exits 43 = its C row (probe via Invoke-Heavy). Not edited: matrix.psd1's Zig list (line ~3006) is the main lane's during reviews; adding qualified_union_case_no_payload there is a one-line change for the next pack. Real remainder: 10 abi_external_* and 10 dynamic_array_* C-shim samples, 2 native_managed_array_scc*, plus single-backend rust_* 7, python 1, wgsl 1.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_5 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:18 helper-lane lap 2: designed the ABI half from the samples themselves: every abi_external_* sample reaches C only through one macro call of a named shim function with plain scalar arguments ($ spiral_abi_libc_abs(x), memset, memcmp x2, llabs, strlen, qsort3_pack(3,1,2), cabs_pack(3,4), div_pack, lldiv_pack; the *_negative/_mismatch/_unknown ones are expected compile errors), and the C rows bind those names with -D or -include of tests/native-shims/abi-external-*-c-shim.h. So no per-sample Zig port of the Spiral code is needed: a Zig row only needs the same names defined in Zig. Mechanism: a ZigShims table in tests/matrix.psd1 (Pattern -> tests/native-shims/abi-external-*.zig) whose file the native tier appends to main.zig before zig build-exe, mirroring CFlags; the shims can call libc through extern fn with zig build-exe -lc, or be pure Zig (abs, llabs, div are one-liners). This is a test.ps1/matrix change (main lane's files), so it is recorded as the plan, not applied.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_6 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:48 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the scalar half of the Zig ABI shims as pure Zig (no libc) in lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims/, one file per C shim the CFlags table includes: abi-external (abs, wrapping like C on INT_MIN), abi-external-i64 (llabs), callback (qsort3_pack as a 3-element sorting network, 123 for 3,1,2), struct-argument (cabs_pack via @sqrt, @trap where C aborts), struct-return (div_pack via @divTrunc/@rem, C's toward-zero div_t) and struct-return-i64 (lldiv_pack). Probe lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims_probe.zig (all six plus negative div) built with zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast as one bounded job: exit 0, every value equal to the C shim's. Not written: the buffer shims (memset, memcmp, strlen) read ->len/->ptr of C's array and string structs, so their Zig twins must follow the Zig backend's slice layout; next grain. Wiring stays a main-lane change (a ZigShims table beside CFlags that appends the matching file to main.zig).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_7 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:35 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the three buffer shims after reading the Zig backend's representation (codegenZig: strings are []const u8, arrays []T from spiralNewArray, length via .len, so a slice already carries the ptr/len pair the C shims read through ->ptr/->len): mutable-buffer memset(array: []u8, byte, count) checks 0 <= count <= len (exit 3 where C aborts), @memset on array[0..count] and returns count; const-buffer memcmp(left, right, count) with the same bound checks returns the sign of std.mem.order (-1/0/1; the samples only test == 0 and the prefix case); string strlen stops at the first NUL like C, else the slice length. shims_probe.zig now also checks memset (writes 3 bytes, leaves the 4th), memcmp (equal 3-byte prefix, ABCD < ABCE) and strlen spiral = 6; rerun after main's freeze all-clear. All 9 Zig shim files exist now; what remains is the test.ps1 ZigShims hook (main's file).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_8 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:54 helper-lane lap 5: staged the harness half as lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/zig_shims_hook.py (anchors --check OK on today's test.ps1 and matrix.psd1; matrix inputs are main's, so not applied): matrix.psd1 gains a ZigShims table (sample name -> shim file, exact-name -like patterns as CFlags uses) and the 9 positive abi_external_* samples on the Zig list; test.ps1 loads it beside cFlagRules (322), passes it into the native runspaces (930), and the Zig clause (712) builds main_shimmed.zig = main.zig + the matching shim when a rule matches, otherwise main.zig as today; the script also copies the 9 shim files into tests/native-shims (already part of the native memo key). HEAVY QUEUE row: apply between runs, then a C,Zig examples review should show 9 new Zig rows agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_9 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: reran shims_probe.zig with the three buffer shims added (zig build-exe ReleaseFast, bounded): exit 0, so memset, memcmp and strlen behave like the C shims on the probe values, together with the six scalar shims.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_10 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 10u32, "2026-10-22T07:02Z", "main", "03:02 main: status check, nothing applied yet. zig_shims_hook.py's dry run still matches today's tmp ('anchors match'); tests/native-shims has no Zig shim; the 9 shims/*.zig (abi_external variants) passed shims_probe.zig on 10-19 (memset/memcmp/strlen plus 6 scalar shims behave like the C shims). apply.ps1 adds Zig arms to the samples and refuses a sample that already has one. Placement: this moves matrix rows (the abi_external_* samples gain Zig rows), so it belongs in the post-install accept window with CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG and the Lean pin (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT checklist 12, optional): copy shims into tests/native-shims, run zig_shims_hook.py --apply and apply.ps1, and every new Zig row must agree with its C row (DISAGREE 0). Otherwise it waits for its own review.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_11 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 11u32, "2026-10-22T22:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:11 main: half landed already. All 7 apply.ps1 samples (ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_unit, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_nested, lua_paren_statement) have Zig arms and are on the matrix Zig list, so lab apply.ps1 is deleted. Open: the abi_external Zig shims (zig_shims_hook.py, 9 shims, still dry-run clean). They ride the post-install matrix bundle %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 with CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG's switch and LEAN-BACKEND's v4.35.0 pin (all dry-run clean today), applied after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed, with the rule printed by the script.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_12 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:22 main: status fixed (staged / half landed). %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 is still dry-run clean on today's tree (all anchors match; Lean 3 pins). It applies after the next4/5f composite, which carries next3b, is reviewed and installed (scratch next-session.md step 7).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_13 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 13:42 (subagent): all 9 shims PASS in shims_probe -> next: queued ZigShims hook review | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: zig_shims_hook.py staged; matrix input -> next: main's pack slot")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_14 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 14u32, "2026-10-27T08:17Z", "main", "10-27 04:17 main: 10-27 04:17 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The 10-22 post-install matrix bundle (TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1) was NEVER APPLIED, although its stated condition (apply after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed) has been met for days (5n and later packs installed since). A dry run now: all anchors still match, for ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (Zig shims for the abi_external samples), CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG (contract rows move from Fsharp to Zig), and LEAN-BACKEND (pin v4.35.0-rc4 -> v4.35.0 stable, 3 pins in apps/cells/README.md). Not applied tonight on purpose: it changes which rows the matrix runs, so it must not land in the middle of the compiler lane's review or accept, and the Lean pin needs the stable toolchain installed first (otherwise every Lean row fails). Offered to the compiler lane, which runs the matrices; it lands right after their current review, then the next matrix run proves it.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_15 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 15u32, "2026-10-27T09:25Z", "main", "10-27 05:25 main: 10-27 05:25 main: Matrix-bundle review single-flight-20261027-042215 (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, 7,367 rows): DISAGREE 0 under both oracles; the one agree-fail is the long-known dynamic_array_bounds_negative Rust row (known vs C). This item's clause is GREEN: the 9 new abi_external Zig rows compile and run, none replayed (0 of 9 native-reused, so this run really executed them), and their C rows agree with the Zig oracle (9 agree). The 10th abi_external C row, abi_external_buffer_length_negative, is no-zig by design: a negative C-ABI fixture without a Zig shim. Still open: the bundle's -Accept -AcceptToolchain run, which waits on the LEAN-BACKEND speed clause.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_16 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 16u32, "2026-10-27T16:19Z", "main", "10-27 12:19 main: Bundle accept-2 (job-1793103041735, run single-flight-20261027-100519) RED by its prewritten rule (the accept must reproduce review single-flight-20261027-042215 row for row); the baseline was restored, 5 files hash-equal, so the oracle is unchanged. 7,367 rows both sides, DISAGREE 0, 2 differing cells, both traced: (1) samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] exit 1 -> timeout. Same generated code (ce261af71adfd449). The review's native run took 47.7 s; the accept's took 60.5 s and hit a cap that is HARD-CODED to 60 s for both F# paths in test.ps1 (warm session line 821 '-TimeoutSec 60', cold 'dotnet fsi' fallback line 529 '... $binDir 60'), so the per-sample matrix.psd1 Timeouts never reach the F# native run. The box carried kit's SLICES2, the E1 bench and other heavy jobs. It is the only F# timeout in either run (387 F# rows each). (2) samples/portable_convert_parse [Lua] residual 477728af -> 6f26afb0 with identical exit (0) and stdout. The sample calls convert.convert_i32_base / convert_i32 / try_convert, and lib convert.spi was rewritten at 09:05, inside the review (04:22) to accept (10:05) gap; 11 lib files moved in that gap. Prewritten rule for accept-3, set BEFORE it runs: GREEN iff test.ps1 exits 0 and every cell that differs from review 042215 is a residual-only cell (identical exit and stdout) on a sample whose main.spi names a lib module (module.) whose lib .spi was written after the review run started; any other differing cell (exit, stdout, compile, native, oracle, missing or new rows) is RED. (1) is not excused but fixed: the F# native run honours max(60, the job's own timeout), and matrix.psd1 Timeouts gets 'samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals' = 180. That changes no other row: no other F# row timed out in either run. accept-3 runs with native memo replay (-ReuseNative), as the review did; timeout results are never memoised (test.ps1:995), so the omniledger row runs fresh.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_17 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 17u32, "2026-10-27T16:29Z", "main", "10-27 12:29 main: Correction to n16, and accept-3 queued. matrix.psd1 already had 'samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals' = 180 (line 80), and its platform note (line 53) records that the row 'sits near the 60 s fsi budget'. My edit added a duplicate key, which would have stopped every test.ps1 run from loading the matrix. I caught it on the load check right after writing, restored the file from the backup (hash-equal) and confirmed it loads with test.ps1's own loader (Import-PowerShellDataFile -SkipLimitCheck): 22 Timeouts entries, all_terminals = 180. So the matrix needs no change, and the whole fix is test.ps1 (unstaged; backup TEMP matrix-bundle/pre-fsharp-timeout-122135): the cold F# run at line 529 now uses max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), and the warm session at line 821 uses max(60, the chunk's largest TimeoutSec). warm.ps1 sets its deadline per script (line 56), so this only lengthens the wait for a script that would otherwise be cut off. n16's rule is now code: TEMP matrix-bundle/compare-accept.ps1, which accept.ps1 calls; it prints BLOCKING <n>. Positive control (accept-2 vs review): the omniledger exit cell BLOCKS and the portable_convert_parse Lua residual is allowed (it calls lib convert), BLOCKING 1. Negative control (the review against itself): 0 cells. accept-3 queued: job-1793118505034 (heavy, 12 h cap counting the queue, -ReuseNative).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_18 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 18u32, "2026-10-27T20:27Z", "main", "10-27 16:27 main: accept-3 VOID by my error (job-1793118505034, 16:21): it passed -ReuseNative, and test.ps1:43 refuses -ReuseNative with -Accept by design ('an accept runs every native row'; apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md says -Accept and -Record refuse it, so the accept still runs every row and must reproduce a -ReuseNative REVIEW). n17's plan to 'run accept-3 with native memo replay, as the review did' was wrong: replay is for reviews, never accepts. The baseline was restored, 5 files hash-equal; nothing changed. accept.ps1 now refuses -ReuseNative itself with a VOID that names the reason. Re-queued as accept-5 (job-1793132732864), without the memo, held behind kit-release-TRAIN (--after), after accept-4 took the freed slot before TRAIN was queued and was stopped at 74 s (baseline again hash-equal to its backup). The n16/n17 rule stands unchanged: GREEN iff test.ps1 exits 0 and every cell that differs from review 042215 is residual-only on a sample that calls a lib module written after the review started. Note for the rule: TRAIN's window and today's lands (convert radix 15:40, fiscal's 8 modules 16:04) move more lib modules, so more residual-only cells may appear; the rule allows exactly those and nothing else.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_19 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 19u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "main", "10-27 19:20 main: Bundle accept GREEN (accept-6, job-1793136844465, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 17:34 to 19:1x; ran in slot 2 against the TRAIN-frozen lib). test.ps1 exit 0; 7,367 rows accepted into spiral-bin baseline EXPECTED.tsv, with toolchain.tsv recorded (SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2). Against review single-flight-20261027-042215 under the n16/n17 rule (set before running): 2 differing cells, both ALLOWED, 0 blocking. samples/portable_convert_parse on Rust and on Lua is residual-only (same exit and stdout) and calls lib convert, which moved after the review (radix land 15:40). The accept-2 blocker is gone: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] ran within the fixed budget (test.ps1's F# native paths now honour max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), so its matrix Timeouts 180 applies). The Zig coverage slice, the 620-row contracts on Zig and the Lean clauses of this bundle now stand as the oracle. Baseline backup in TEMP matrix-bundle/baseline-before-accept-20261027-173412. Unblocks: closing lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (spent; bundle.ps1 needs no re-apply now), the CCMR land (it waits on this plus TRAIN), and then the pack-5n install.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_20 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 20u32, "2026-10-27T23:32Z", "main", "10-27 19:32 main: Groom 10-27 19:3x, counted from the accepted oracle (accept-6 run single-flight-20261027-173417): 68 samples have a C row and no Zig row. Against the title's 38-sample split: ABI shims, the 9 positive abi_external samples, now have Zig rows (accepted). The 6 still C-only are negatives (abi_external_buffer_length_negative, _buffer_ownership_negative, _callback_dynamic_negative, _signature_mismatch, _struct_return_i64_signature_mismatch, ...) whose point is the C ABI rejection, so they stay C-only by design. Dynamic-array helpers: all 10 still C-only (dynamic_array_geometric_growth, _growth_union_managed, _growth_union_nested, _reserve_zero, _resize_alias, _resize_nested, ...). That's the open work: Zig counterparts of their C shims, as the title says. Rust-only 7 and eBPF stay single-backend by design. The other 45 C-only samples are outside this item's split (compile-error negatives such as assign_primitive_wrong_type and conversation_negative_*, plus delphi_* samples). Next slice: one dynamic-array helper shim in Zig (the growth/reserve family shares one C helper), proven on its samples against C, then the other 9.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_21 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 21u32, "2026-10-28T10:57Z", "main", "10-28 06:57 main: CORRECTION to n20 and DONE (main, 10-28 07:0x). n20's 'next slice: a Zig shim for the dynamic-array helpers' was wrong. Those samples call $'DynamicArrayResize0(!xs, n)' as host text, and the C shim implements it on C's shared array header (SpiralPortableArrayHeaderV26: refc and len). dynamic_array_resize_alias depends on it: resizing an alias must change xs's length (expected 4 + 7 + 4 - 15 = 0). The Zig backend's arrays are by-value slices ([]T, spiralNewArray), so a Zig function given a slice cannot change the caller's or an alias's length, and no shim can reproduce the semantics. PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n16 (10-19) already recorded this: these samples test a C-runtime ABI experiment, and a portable version needs a growable-array type in core (capacity separate from length, resize keeping aliases valid). That's a language feature, so the samples are C-only until it exists. By this item's own title ('Zig rows where the feature exists') the scope is complete: the 9 positive ABI samples have accepted Zig rows; the 6 ABI negatives (C's ABI rejection), 7 Rust-only and the eBPF samples are single-backend by design; and the 10 dynamic-array samples wait on the growable-array feature (PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES, Paused), not on a shim.")
inl eoie_core_operators () = Task ("EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS", "eoie packages load Spiral core and use operators instead of the !!!! op spam", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,spiral,core,granular,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl dir_tree_readme () = Task ("DIR-TREE-README", "dir-tree-html renders the README.md at the source dir's root below the tree (user 2026-10-18): a Markdown renderer in the Spiral source (headings, paragraphs, lists, code fences, links, inline code), the same dark style, and the viewport/narrow-screen CSS from today; no README means no section; self-test covers both", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,markdown", "current", "current")
inl notebook_html_renderer () = Task ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", "Replace the Python nbconvert step (jupyter, used by Cells' --html to turn .ipynb into .html) with our own renderer that colors Spiral blocks properly (user 2026-10-18), as Zed tried by copying the VS Code extension's grammar: reuse the TextMate grammar or the TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL grammar for the highlighting, render markdown cells and outputs, keep the page mobile-friendly; drops the jupyter dependency from every repo's CI", Feature, P1, D5, 70u32, Active, "", "cells,html,highlighting,python-free", "current", "current")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_1 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:42 main-thread design. The VS Code extension has no TextMate grammar: its colors are the language server's semantic tokens. The compiler's tokenizer tags every token with a SemanticTokenLegend class (variable, symbol, string, number, operator, unary_operator, comment, keyword, parenthesis, type_variable, escaped_char, unescaped_char, number_suffix, escaped_var; spiral_compiler.fs ~42869, semantic_tokens ~49363), and the format verb already reads those tokens without partial evaluation. Plan: (1) a host verb 'SpiralCompiler tokens <file|--stdin --path p>' printing one row per token (line, column, length, class), tokenizer only and cheap, next to 'format' in Program.fs; (2) a Cells HTML writer (Spiral.Cells.Html) that renders the report directly: markdown cells (the CommonMark subset from DIR-TREE-README), code cells colored by a CSS class per legend entry with a dark palette, outputs and test verdicts, plus the mobile viewport CSS. It replaces 'jupyter nbconvert' in Notebook.write_html, removes the jupyter/Python dependency from every repo's CI, and colors exactly as the editor does. The tokens verb is a compiler change: build and install it after today's 17:00 accept.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_2 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:45 main-thread: step (1) written, unbuilt. TokensCli in the shared Formatter section folds tokenize_line over a file's lines (lexical state carried across lines) and prints one row per token: line, from, nearTo, then the token_groups class, the same mapping vscode_tokens uses for the editor. Host verb: SpiralCompiler tokens <file> | tokens --stdin (Program.fs, next to format). Builds and installs after today's 17:00 accept; then step (2), the Cells HTML writer.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_3 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 3u32, "2026-10-18T18:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:52 main-thread: step (2) done, uncommitted. Spiral.Cells.Html renders a page from the .ipynb JSON: markdown cells (CommonMark subset), code cells colored by token class (one CSS class per SemanticTokenLegend name, dark palette), outputs, and a test badge per test cell, with the viewport and narrow-screen CSS. Notebook.write_html calls it instead of jupyter nbconvert; normalize_html and its test (nbconvert cell-id renumbering) are removed. The tokenizer calls SpiralCompiler tokens <tmp file> and degrades to plain text when the installed compiler lacks the verb (today's install). Tests: html_test.exs 4/4, notebook_test.exs passes (18 in all). End to end: mix spiral.notebook on lib/spiral/testing.spi --output-dir --livemd writes testing.spi.html (13 KB, viewport, 6 test badges, 25 code blocks), .ipynb and .livemd, no Python involved. Colors appear once the tokens verb is installed after the 17:00 accept. This also fixes the tibia lane's failure today: scoop python's pyzmq raises 'cannot import name _device', and nbconvert exited 1 on every run.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_4 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 4u32, "2026-10-18T19:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:26 main-thread: the last jupyter path is gone. core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook (dice/alphabet/i574n.github PowerShell .livemd notebooks) now calls the new mix spiral.html <ipynb>... task from apps/cells, with scoop OTP/Elixir prepended like run_notebook.ps1, instead of jupyter nbconvert. Html.write colors only notebooks whose kernelspec language is spiral (or missing), so PowerShell cells stay plain. spiral/requirements.txt drops nbconvert, ipython_genutils and mistune (mpmath and cupy stay for the Python backend). init.ps1's no-pip message and the 3 gh-pages.yml comments no longer mention jupyter. polyglot's own requirements.txt is untouched (fc1943s backup repo). html_test.exs 6/6, including a pwsh notebook through the task")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_5 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:36 main-thread: review of the committed, unbuilt TokensCli against the renderer: tokenize_line ranges are .NET string offsets (UTF-16 units), while Html.highlight_line sliced bytes, so any non-ASCII text before a token (an accented string, an emoji) would shift every later span, or cut inside a character. Fixed before the first colored build: the line is converted to UTF-16, sliced by unit and converted back, and a slice that splits a surrogate pair gives an empty string. New test with é and an emoji in strings: html_test.exs 7/7")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_6 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: CI regression from my nbconvert replacement: core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook ran mix spiral.html before mix deps.get on fresh runners (Unchecked dependencies: kino) in dice, polyglot and alphabet; it now runs mix local.hex, local.rebar and deps.get when apps/cells/deps/kino is missing, like run_notebook.ps1 Next: confirm the html appears in the next CI run")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_7 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: CI confirmation still pending: dice, polyglot and alphabet have no run since main's 08:16 core.ps1 fix (latest runs 06:48, all red; spiral's in-progress run is a rerun of 668568ac, which predates the fix), so the check waits for the user's push. Meanwhile closed the title's last clause (drop the jupyter dependency from every repo): a grep of all 9 repos for jupyter/nbconvert/ipykernel left only polyglot/requirements.txt (nbconvert>=7.16.6, ipython_genutils, mistune==3.0.2, installed by polyglot scripts/init.ps1 pip install -r and the devcontainer Dockerfile); spiral's requirements.txt already had only mpmath and cupy-cuda12x. Removed those three lines in polyglot (uncommitted), so it now matches spiral. The remaining jupyter_lines hits in Cells notebook.ex are the .ipynb JSON writer, not the tool.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_8 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: CI recheck (gh run list, read-only): spiral's latest run is still 13:15Z on 668568ac, which predates the core.ps1 fix and failed in workflow.ps1 -Compiler -Suite all. dice, polyglot and alphabet have had no run since 06:48Z. So the CI confirmation still waits for the user's next push, and nothing new can be checked locally.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_9 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:42Z", "main", "18:41 CI confirmation found (read-only): i574n/polyglot gh-pages run 39357432363 (10-19 22:10, after the core.ps1 fix) is green and renders notebooks with mix spiral.html; its 1.17 MB log mentions jupyter or nbconvert 0 times. dice's run of the same push is green too. That was the last open condition (the renderer is ours and every repo's jupyter dependency is gone, note 10-19 09:21), so closing. spiral and alphabet are red for unrelated reasons (PIPELINES-GREEN notes 16-20: the Linux F# warm exit mask, and the Zig-only array typing).")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_10 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 3 10:49 (subagent): blocked, rechecked: dice's latest CI run is still 06:48 (failure), no push since main's core.ps1 fix; html publish path verified (core.ps1:384 mix spiral.html, publish-tree includes *.html) -> next: unblocks on the user's push of dice/polyglot/alphabet: gh run list, then the notebook .html files on gh-pages -> Done | lap 4 11:35 (subagent): blocked: user push of dice/polyglot/alphabet (CI still at the red 06:48 runs) -> next: gh run list after the push; notebook .html on gh-pages -> Done | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user push pending -> next: gh-pages html -> Done | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: no CI run since 13:15Z -> next: the user's push")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_11 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 11u32, "2026-10-25T05:00Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:00 main: REOPENED (user 10-25 00:5x: \"put in the backlog so the html generated by cells gets colored\"). The renderer exists (Html.render: palette, per-class CSS, UTF-16-correct spans) but real output is NOT colored. tibia/notebooks/*.spi.html written 10-25 00:19-00:24 (e.g. rng.spi.html, 9 code cells, kernelspec language \"spiral\") carry the token CSS and pre.code blocks, yet 0 tok- spans. So the tokenizer returned [] and the page fell back to plain text with no error. The compiler side works: the installed SpiralCompiler \"tokens <file>\" exits 0 and classifies edition-3 := as keyword. Suspects: compiler_tokenizer resolving no dotnet or DLL in that run (fn _ -> []), or tokens exiting non-zero ({_, code} -> []), e.g. under a candidate SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL. Plan: (1) make the fallback loud: a stderr warning naming the dotnet/DLL it tried and the exit code, plus a count of colored cells in the run report; (2) reproduce on one .spi notebook with the Cells environment the run used, and fix the cause; (3) an ExUnit test that renders a spiral cell and asserts tok- spans > 0, red on today's code; (4) check the published gh-pages notebooks too, since CI may hit the same fallback. Done when a Cells-generated .spi.html shows colored Spiral code, symbol spellings included.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_12 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 12u32, "2026-10-25T06:40Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:40 main: no reproduction today. Both dotnets Cells can pick (the cache-bundled one and PATH's) run the installed 'tokens' fine, and tibia's own rng.spi.ipynb cells tokenize (cell 3: 162 rows). So the 00:2x failure was in that run's environment (a candidate SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL left set is the main suspect), which can't be recovered after the fact. Step 1 drafted: %TEMP%\\cells-html-loud\\apply.ps1 (2 anchors, dry run OK). A tokens failure prints the dotnet, the DLL, the exit code and its output, and a missing toolchain prints what was tried; both go to stderr. Applied with the other Cells slices once no Cells run reads the tree; then the next run names the cause.")
inl notebook_livemd_export () = Task ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", "Write a .livemd next to each notebook's .ipynb and .html outputs (user 2026-10-18), so someone cloning a gh-pages branch can open the notebooks in Livebook; Cells already has --livemd-path and from_spi, so this is an export option plus the publish steps carrying *.livemd (publish-tree already includes *.livemd)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 90u32, Blocked, "", "cells,livemd,pages", "current", "current")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_1 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:40 main-thread: implemented in the tree, uncommitted. mix spiral.notebook --livemd (NotebookCli switch; livemd_target) writes the Livebook copy (Document.to_livemd) beside each notebook's report: next to the .ipynb named by --output-path (x.spi.ipynb gives x.spi.livemd), into --output-dir, or beside the source. It works with many --path values; --livemd-path stays single-path. Test notebook_livemd_target_test.exs 2/2. lib/spiral/build.ps1 passes --livemd, so CI writes lib/spiral/<nb>.spi.livemd beside the .ipynb and .html it already writes there, and publish-tree (which includes *.livemd) carries all three to gh-pages. Left: the other repos' notebook runs (blog and x write into target/, which publish-tree excludes, so their notebooks aren't published at all yet).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_2 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:34 helper-lane lap 1: state: the --livemd export is committed (e0ffc80b) and lib/spiral/build.ps1 passes --livemd, but gh-pages of i574n/spiral (last deploy 10-18 19:00Z) has 39 lib/spiral .ipynb and 0 .livemd, because every spiral CI run since e0ffc80b failed (18:51 e0ffc80b, 22:12 65f83164, 06:48 668568ac: build ubuntu, step workflow.ps1; main is on it). Blog (left part of note 1): its notebooks went to target/build/<pkg>/notebooks, which publish-tree excludes. Edited (uncommitted): blog scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-SpiralTests now passes --no-html --livemd, and scripts/publish.ps1 copies each target/build/<pkg>/notebooks/*.ipynb|html|livemd into dist/notebooks/<pkg>/ after publish-tree (same CI job, ubuntu only), so gh-pages carries them. Verification rides on the queued BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST blog build (check target/build/engine/notebooks/*.spi.livemd appear). x keeps its own scripts (x lane).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_3 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: checked that local warm runs export too, not only CI's cold ones: run_notebook.ps1 forwards its arguments either to the warm daemon (Invoke-WarmNotebook) or to mix, and the daemon handles a request with Spiral.Cells.NotebookCli.execute(args) (compiler_daemon.ex:988), the same parser that declares livemd: :boolean and livemd_path (notebook_cli.ex:11/21) and derives x.spi.livemd from --output-path x.spi.ipynb (livemd_target, :119-122). So --livemd behaves the same with SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM on or off; blog's runner sets warm 0 anyway. Remaining evidence is still external: the queued blog build (target/build/engine/notebooks/*.livemd) and a green spiral CI deploy (lib/spiral/*.spi.livemd on gh-pages).")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_4 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:49 helper-lane lap 3: blog half verified: main's full blog build (10:00-10:04, BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST note 4) wrote 9 .spi.livemd files next to the 10 .ipynb in blog/target/build/engine/notebooks (build, html, index, page, post, rss, showcase, site, style; the 10th ipynb is generate's, added after that build). Each is valid Livebook markdown: per cell a livebook smart_cell marker (Elixir.Spiral.Cells.SmartCell with source and timeout attrs) plus an elixir block calling Spiral.Cells.eval! on the cell's ~S source, with test cells under their own heading (showcase (test)). With the uncommitted publish.ps1 copy step they land in dist/notebooks/engine on the next blog deploy. Left: the spiral half (lib/spiral/*.spi.livemd on gh-pages) needs a green spiral CI deploy after the user's push.")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_5 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: same CI state as NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER: no spiral gh-pages run since 13:15Z (pre-fix commit 668568ac). The spiral half (lib .spi.livemd on gh-pages) is checked on the first green run after the user's push.")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_6 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 6u32, "2026-10-20T04:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 00:53 main (WSJF pass): nothing left to do locally: the blog half is verified (9 .spi.livemd next to the ipynb) and the spiral half is checked on the first green gh-pages run after the user's push; set to Blocked on that push so WSJF skips it.")
inl note_of_notebook_livemd_export_7 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:35 (subagent): blocked: the spiral half needs a green spiral CI deploy after the user's push -> next: lib/spiral/*.spi.livemd on gh-pages -> Done | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): spiral CI deploy pending -> next: gh-pages livemd -> Done | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: same CI dependency -> next: the user's push")
inl tibia_zig_oracle () = Task ("TIBIA-ZIG-ORACLE", "Move tibia's oracle tests from ///> c to ///> zig (compiler lane ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE, 2026-10-18: C and Zig disagree on 1 of 459 shared samples, an intended trap; Zig ships its own toolchain, so there's no mingw/UCRT vs msvcrt split). tibia/src has 146 c targets, 25 wgsl, 2 rust. Decide by measurement: pass rate and seconds per file for zig vs c through lab/tibia-zig-oracle/measure.ps1, on both local zig 0.18.0-dev and CI's pinned 0.17.0.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,zig,oracle,testing", "current", "current")
inl spiral_core_neq () = Task ("SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ", "core operator <> lowers to not (a = b) and emits v != true instead of a direct != (NEQ op)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "spiral,core,codegen", "current", "current")
inl upstream_changes_post () = Task ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", "Bun-style post committed in the upstream fork (i574n/The-Spiral-Language) that explains every change against mrakgr's host_cpp_and_cuda_backend branch (treated as the new temporary master, since nothing was merged to master) down to the line, one section per pull request in the planned PR split; it credits what was taken from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend and from Spiral-s-ML-Library versus what the fork did (user 2026-10-18); line-level tables generated from git (upstream.py export) so the post cannot drift, prose only for intent Split into a bite-sized series starting with UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (user 2026-10-18).", Feature, P1, D6, 20u32, Active, "COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION,UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", "blog,upstream,docs,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_upstream_changes_post () = Planning ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:43 main-thread first measurement: python apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/upstream.py status C:\\home\\git\\The-Spiral-Language mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (18 s) maps each /// ## section of spiral_compiler.fs to upstream's The Spiral Language 2/<Name>.fs. Over the 27 upstream-mapped sections, 6,440 lines differ. section-only (ours) leads with PartEval 533, Infer 462, SignalRSupervisor 463, CodegenC 428, BlockParsing 415, WDiff 341, Prepass 327, Tokenize 250. fork-only (host_cpp lines we lack, the rest of UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC) leads with Infer 274, Prepass 238, SignalRSupervisor 203, BlockParsing 197, CodegenC 147, PartEval 143. Sections without an upstream file (our backends Rust, Zig, Lean, Lua, Wasm, ...) come on top as new files. Saved as spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/status-vs-host_cpp.txt; the post's per-PR tables come from upstream.py export --since per PR")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: series progress: post 1's generator (lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/make-post.ps1) now shows each fix's Before and After halves from fixes.tsv; the 6,440-line upstream-mapped drift from note 1 is what later posts carve into PR-sized layers Next: re-run upstream.py status after the core switch to see how much of the drift the corebase core removes")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:16 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1's next step: upstream.py status maps only spiral_compiler.fs sections to upstream's The Spiral Language 2/<Name>.fs, so the lib core switch (CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE, a package change) cannot move this number. Re-ran it now (heavy gate, under 20 s): total differing lines 6,440, identical to 10-18 16:43 and identical per section (PartEval 533, SignalRSupervisor 463, Infer 462, CodegenC 428, BlockParsing 415, CodegenUtils 343, WDiff 341, Prepass 327, Tokenize 250, CodegenPython 223, ServerUtils 187, CodegenFsharp 183, CodegenCpp 112, SpiProj 95; 13 small sections under 40): packs 4 and 5 landed in non-mapped sections (codegens for Gleam, Zig, Lua and others are fork-only sections). So the post series' line tables are stable today; the levers that shrink them are the COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION layering and UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC (both deps). Next: rank the 27 mapped sections by section-only lines and pick the post-2 PR candidate (smallest self-contained section with real drift: CodegenCpp 112 or SpiProj 95)")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:55 lap 3 grain: read the SpiProj drift line by line. upstream.py export (light, into spiral-bin/runs/upstream-export-lap3) vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend:The Spiral Language 2/SpiProj.fs: 35 hunks, 95 added / 42 removed lines. Three kinds: (a) merge-into-one-file renames, the parser helpers suffixed to stay unique in spiral_compiler.fs (spaces -> spacesSpiProj, range -> rangeSpiProj, is_var_char -> is_var_charSpiProj, file -> fileSpiProj; plus open VSCTypes commented and Utils.lines -> lines): 21 of the 95 added lines are such pure renames; (b) real fixes: Directory entries no longer crash the module list (Array.map over File|Directory with an incomplete pattern -> Array.choose of File), vscpos helpers; (c) features: package.spiproj gains defaultInt, defaultFloat and edition fields (the --default-int/--default-float host flags). Consequence for the series: kind (a) must not reach an upstream PR, because the fork keeps one file per module; export should map the suffixed names back before the line tables are generated, else every post overstates the drift (for SpiProj by about a fifth). Post candidates from this file: one small fix post (Directory entries) and one feature post (project defaults). Next: list the suffix renames per section (pattern <name><Section> where the fork has <name>) to size kind (a) across all 27 sections, as input for an export rename map")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:28 lap 4 grain: sized the merge-rename drift over all 27 mapped sections (export vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend per file; an added line counts as a suffix rename when deleting the <Section> suffix from its identifiers yields a removed line): 102 of 4,577 added lines, about 2 percent, concentrated in Prepass 65, SpiProj 21, WDiffPrepass 12, BlockParsing 2, WDiff 2, elsewhere 0. So lap 3's about a fifth holds only for SpiProj; series-wide the line tables are honest without a rename map, and a map is worth it for 3 sections only (it can be a list of 3 suffixes in export, not a general mechanism). Biggest real drift by added lines: PartEval 533, Supervisor 463, Infer 462, CodegenC 430, BlockParsing 415, CodegenUtils 343 (of which 337 have no counterpart: new code), WDiff 339, Prepass 327. Next: for the post after vanilla, split CodegenUtils' 343 new lines into the features they carry (UnusedGlobals.prune, closure naming helpers, ...) as PR-sized candidates")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:44 lap 5 grain: CodegenUtils' 343 added lines carved into PR-sized features (export vs his branch, hunk by hunk): (1) readable generated names, 241 lines in one hunk: generatedMethodName and generatedClosureName ({stem}_c{tag} from the inl binding name, closureSourceName over E), generatedCaseNames/Suffixes/CaseName/CaseNameOfTag with collision counting, plus the per-backend case shapes leanCaseName and gleamCaseName (upper_camel); (2) UnusedGlobals.prune, inside the same hunk's tail: declaration-keyword globals whose name occurs nowhere else in the output are dropped, exported (pub/export) and multi-item blobs kept; (3) codegen_runtime_file and printf_pieces, 70 lines: workspace/root lookup of runtime files and a shared printf splitter the backends reuse; (4) duplicate-global fragments and decimal literal point disposition, 27 lines (preservesDuplicateGlobalFragment, DecimalLiteralPointDispositionId, indent) plus add_dec_point 7; (5) string_builder_env and CodegenTagAllocator, 12 lines. Ordering for posts: (1) readable names is self-contained and already proven across backends by the 10-11 ladders in AGENTS.md (closure names per backend, 0 rows differing on restore), so it is the best first feature post after vanilla; (2) depends on (1) only for text shape; (3)-(5) are small helpers that ride with the backend posts that use them. Next: list (1)'s call sites in the C and F# codegen sections, the part a PR must carry with it")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:47 lap 5 grain: call sites of the readable-names feature (grep per /// ## section): upstream-mapped sections that a PR must touch with it: CodegenC (generatedCaseNameOfTag 4, generatedClosureName 1), CodegenFsharp (generatedCaseName 5, closure 1, method 1), CodegenCpp (CaseNameOfTag 2), CodegenPython (CaseNameOfTag 7, closure 1, method 1), plus closureSourceName 4 in CodegenAdapter (fork-only) and UnusedGlobals.prune 2 in SignalRSupervisor's build; every other caller is a fork-only backend (Rust, Delphi, Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Wgsl, TypeScript). So a readable-names PR against his branch = CodegenUtils' 241-line hunk + 4 upstream codegens (C, F#, Cpp, Python: ~25 call sites) + the closureSourceName hook (prepass records the binding name on the body, per AGENTS.md) and no fork-only backend; UnusedGlobals.prune is a separate PR touching CodegenUtils + the build entry. Next: check that closureSourceName's prepass hook lives in an upstream-mapped section (Prepass) and size it")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:33Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:33 lap 6 grain: sized the prepass part of the readable-names PR. It is small and self-contained. Definition (Prepass section, spiral_compiler.fs 52566-52577, 12 lines): closureBindingNames is a ConditionalWeakTable<E, string> keyed by the function node, with noteClosureName and copyClosureName. There are 3 call sites: lower 52624 and 52686 (copyClosureName from the source node to the lowered inner and closure-converted node) and 53060 in the let-binding prepass (noteClosureName when namesFunction body). The reading side in CodegenUtils (161522-161552, 31 lines) is generatedMethodName, generatedClosureName (stem_c tag) and closureSourceName, which tries the table first, then the join-point name through EFun'/EAnnot. The table is weak and keyed by reference, so it adds no AST field, which keeps the upstream diff out of the E union. PR shape: Prepass hunk (~15 lines) + CodegenUtils hunk + each upstream backend's call sites (C, F#, Cpp, Python: about 25 sites, each name = generatedClosureName (closureSourceName ...) tag). Next: list the Cpp and Python call sites in his CodegenCpp/CodegenPython to confirm they take the same shape.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: the first feature post is scoped (readable names: CodegenUtils hunk 31 lines, prepass hook 12 lines + 3 sites, ~25 backend call sites). Progress 20.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:52 lap 7 grain: checked the call-site shape on his branch (The-Spiral-Language clone, mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend, git show, read only). Name sites per codegen: Cpp 8 (FunPointerMethod{tag}, Closure{tag}, ...), Python 12 (method%i, Closure%i, UH{tag}_{i}), C 7, F# 4 (method%i, closure%i): 31 sites, close to the ~25 estimate. Two facts change the PR text: (1) his CodegenC already names methods after their join point (sprintf %s%i with Option.defaultValue method x.name), so for C the post is about extending that to closures and the other three backends, not about starting it; (2) his CodegenUtils.fs is only 50 lines, so our CodegenUtils hunk is nearly all new code (generatedMethodName, generatedClosureName, closureSourceName, generatedCaseNames) and reads best as a new section. Next: draft the post's before/after table from one sample's C and Python output on his compiler vs ours.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:28 lap 8 grain: first rows of the naming before/after table, taken by reading instead of building his compiler: samples/closure_pair (inl f used as a value) is named ClosureMethod0 in his C (CodegenC.fs:597 on host_cpp_and_cuda_backend) and Closure0 in his Python (CodegenPython.fs:232), while ours is f_c0 in both main.c:32 and main.py:23 (generatedClosureName). Rows in lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv (sample, backend, his, ours, source). Also visible: our C passes the closure as Fun0 * self, a representation change the post needs its own row for. Next: method_pair and tuple rows, then generate the table from upstream.py export")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:52 lap 9 grain: naming table grew 3 rows (lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv): method_i8 (join pick n) is method0 in his C (CodegenC.fs:364 falls back to method when x.name is None) and Python, and method0 in ours; tuple_pair's anonymous join points are method0/method1 on both sides. A scan of every non-mega samples/*/main.c finds no stem_tag method name, so in C and Python the visible naming change is the closures (ClosureMethodN / ClosureN -> stem_cN); methods change only where a join point carries a name. That narrows the post's naming section to closures for the backends his branch has")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:27 lap 10 grain: naming table +4 rows (lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/naming-table.tsv): his branch (mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend, ls-tree) has exactly 4 codegens, CodegenC, CodegenCpp, CodegenFsharp, CodegenPython, so every other backend is new in the post; closure_pair F# is closure0 on his side (CodegenFsharp.fs:438 sprintf closure%i) and f_c0 in ours (main.fsx:1); Rust, Kotlin, Swift rows marked new backend with ours f_c0 (main.rs:4, main.kt:2, main.swift:3). Next: Cpp row (his CodegenCpp naming vs our CppHost output), then upstream.py export wiring of the table")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: Cpp row added to the naming table: ts_closure_capture (inl f captured, then ~g = f) is Closure0 in his CodegenCpp (line 324, Closure{x.tag}) and still Closure0 in our main.cpp:7, so C++ is the one backend the readable closure naming never reached (AGENTS.md lists the rollout for Rust, Kotlin, Swift, TypeScript, Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Wasm, Delphi, F#, Python and C, not Cpp/CppHost). For the post: the naming PR changes no C++ text. For main: a possible small follow-up (CppHost closures through generatedClosureName) if the naming should be uniform. Next: upstream.py export wiring of the table into the post")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:15 the post's line tables now come from git: lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST/pr_table.py runs upstream.py export (optionally --since/--until for one PR's commit range) and prints a markdown table per fork file against mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend. Whole fork today: 26 files, +4,904 / -1,939 lines, 0 conflicts (largest PartEval +568/-148, Infer +536/-286, Supervisor +472/-218, BlockParsing +443/-202, CodegenC +440/-157). naming_table_check.py --markdown renders the closure-naming table only after its 17 citations verify")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_16 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 16u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x scope: the blog post in the compiler upstream covers each change across his lib, his compiler and our compiler: breaking changes, fixes, the parallel toggle (gpt5parallel vs single-flight), and why edition 3 is out (we changed some things too much, so it ships as an edition of a modified fork).")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_17 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 17u32, "2026-10-22T03:49Z", "main", "23:49 lab drained into the fork (user 23:1x: post in fork too): naming_table_check.py, pr_table.py, naming-table.tsv and status-vs-host_cpp.txt now live in the fork under posts/upstream_changes (paths through the fork root and deps/spiral); both tools run from there: 17 citations checked, 0 drifted; PR table 26 files, 0 conflicts. Removed spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST (4 files); the fork changes are uncommitted for the user.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_18 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 18u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 12 14:15 (subagent2): naming_table_check.py: re-derives every 'ours' citation (name, main.ext:line) of naming-table.tsv from the committed sample outputs; 11 citations, 0 drifted (exit 1 on drift) -> next: same check for the his_branch column via git show on the fork ref, then upstream.py export wiring | lap 13 14:34 (subagent2): naming_table_check.py also checks the his_branch column: git show mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend:'The Spiral Language 2/Codegen*.fs' at each cited line must contain the name stem (closure%i -> closure); 17 citations (6 his + 11 ours), 0 drifted -> next: upstream.py export wiring: emit the table from the check's data | lap 14 15:00 (subagent2): naming_table_check.py --markdown renders the post's naming table only after all 17 citations verify (exit 1 on drift, nothing printed), so the table in the post is generated from checked data -> next: upstream.py export: call the check from the post build (make-post.ps1 pattern) | lap 16 15:52 (subagent2): staged lab pr_table.py: runs upstream.py export (optionally --since/--until for one PR's range) into a temp dir and prints the post's per-file table vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (added/removed lines, new files, conflict count), so each PR section's table comes from git, not prose -> next: run it in a bounded slot (whole export, then one --since range) | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): pr_table.py queued attempt withdrawn (would have been a second concurrent job of mine; my own supervisor 27052 + its pwsh 3160 stopped); rerun when the CLI checks are done -> next: bounded slot for pr_table.py | lap 18 18:15 (subagent2): pr_table.py ran (27 s, bounded job): whole-fork export vs mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend = 26 files, +4,904 / -1,939 lines, 0 conflicts; largest: PartEval +568/-148, Infer +536/-286, Supervisor +472/-218, BlockParsing +443/-202, CodegenC +440/-157 (lap 4's +4,577 rose with packs 7-9) -> next: per-PR tables: rerun with --since/--until per fix range when the PR split is fixed | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): naming_table_check.py after packs 8b-10 (8b renamed join points positionally): 17 citations checked, 0 drifted -> next: per-PR tables once the PR split is fixed (deps COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION, UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC)")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_19 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 19u32, "2026-10-26T14:58Z", "main", "10-26 10:58 main: drift checks rerun 10:5x. Both tools had failed to start: they assumed the fork sits beside its own deps/spiral (here.parents[1]/deps/spiral), but this checkout is spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language, the same assumption that broke post 2's run.ps1 this morning. Both now pick the fork's deps/spiral if it holds apps/compiler/tmp, else the enclosing spiral repo (here.parents[3]). Results: pr_table.py gives 26 files, 0 conflicts, exit 0. naming_table_check.py found 3 of 17 citations drifted, line numbers only, from regenerated sample outputs: closure_pair Python f_c0 at main.py 23 -> 14, method_i8 Python method0 at main.py 20 -> 11, tuple_pair C method1 at main.c 20 -> 18. naming-table.tsv updated; recheck: 17 checked, 0 drifted, exit 0. Fork posts/ remains uncommitted for the user.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_20 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 20u32, "2026-10-26T15:01Z", "main", "10-26 11:01 main: naming_table_check.py --fix: drifted 'ours' citations (sample outputs regenerate, so the cited line numbers move) are rewritten to the line where the name now first appears, single and paired forms alike, and only when every name of the row is found. The 'his' column is never auto-fixed, since it needs a human read of upstream. The run still exits 1, and a plain rerun confirms. Control: 2 planted drifts (main.py:14 -> 99, main.c:15,18 -> 15,30); --fix reported both and rewrote them, the recheck gave 17 checked and 0 drifted, and the table came out byte-identical to the original.")
inl note_of_upstream_changes_post_21 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST", 21u32, "2026-10-26T21:18Z", "main", "10-26 17:18 main: 10-26 17:1x main: drift checks rerun: naming_table_check.py, 17 citations checked, 0 drifted, exit 0; pr_table.py, 26 files, 5,217/1,965 lines, 0 conflicts, exit 0. No regeneration since 11:01 moved a cited line. Fork posts/ remains uncommitted for the user.")
inl core_lib_consolidate () = Task ("CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "Take the core lib from mrakgr's Spiral-s-ML-Library and submit it to the fork's master (user 2026-10-18): trace every copy of every core file across repos and branches, keep the most complete and recent version of each file with provenance (repo, path, commit, date), merge files that diverged on both sides, and prune older copies safely (dangling check by stem + consumer builds, deletes need the user's OK)", Feature, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "core,upstream,lib,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl blog_showcase_post () = Task ("BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST", "Replace the stalled giant blog post with a sample post that exercises every feature of the blog engine (every block kind, TOC, tabs, RSS) as typed Spiral data checked by test blocks; the real written posts live in the repos they describe, starting with UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST in the upstream fork (user 2026-10-18)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "blog", "current", "current")
inl upstream_post_1_vanilla () = Task ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", "Upstream post 1 of a bite-sized series (user 2026-10-18): the vanilla start. One PR to mrakgr/master that consolidates his own unmerged work: host_cpp_and_cuda_backend as the language, Spiral-s-ML-Library corebase as core (judged per file, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE), plus only the bug fixes we found in his code. A fix qualifies when its regression sample fails on his compiler built from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend and passes with the patch; our features (new backends, matrix, Cells) wait for later posts. The post's tables (consolidated commits, core decisions, fixes with before and after) are generated from data", Story, P0, D5, 90u32, Blocked, "CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "blog,upstream,docs,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla () = Planning ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:52 main-thread candidate fixes to check against his compiler (from Done items and today's work; each needs its sample run on the upstream build): C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB, SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP (C closure strict-aliasing UB, today), SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (tokenizer), NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT, FOR-D-COMPILER-BUGS (six repros), PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM, the Infer shadowing fix and the payload-less qualified case (today), SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ (core). Features stay out even when they fix behavior: QUALIFIED-UNION-CASES, INLINE-DEPTH-GUARD (EJP0040 report), readable names, edition 3. First step: build upstream's The Spiral Language 2 from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend in a scratch worktree-free copy (git archive), then run each candidate's sample on it")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 2u32, "2026-10-18T20:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 16:53 main-thread step 1 scoped: host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (0cb0fda0) exported with git archive to the scratchpad (The Spiral Language 2 + VS Code Plugin/core). It targets net9.0, and the machine has only the .NET 11 rc SDKs, which can build net9 and run with roll-forward. It has no CLI: Supervisor.fs is the language server and builds through a BuildFile message. So the before-and-after proof needs a small headless driver: an F# script over the upstream DLL that opens the sample's package, waits for typecheck and prepass, and calls the backend codegen, the same steps BuildFile takes. The driver lives in spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA, and every candidate fix's sample runs on it once unpatched (must fail) and once with the patch exported by upstream.py (must pass)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 3u32, "2026-10-18T21:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:07 main-thread: headless driver written, spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/{driver/driver.fsproj, driver/Program.fs, build-driver.ps1}. build-driver.ps1 git-archives a ref of the upstream clone (default mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend) into a work dir, optionally applies a patch (an upstream.py export as a diff), and publishes a net9.0 driver (RollForward Major, runs on the cached .NET 11) that ProjectReferences the archived The Spiral Language 2. It lays out <work>/bin and <work>/core, because upstream resolves |core- as BaseDirectory/../core, so the core under test is chosen per build (the branch's own, or candidate v1). The driver starts supervisor_server and attention_server in-process, sends FileOpen and BuildFile, then waits for the output file's mtime to advance, or for a fatal or traced error. It prints UPSTREAM-BUILT/REJECTED/STALLED (exit 0/1/2). Upstream's backends are Fsharp, C, Cpp + Cuda and Python + Cuda, so post 1's before-and-after proofs run on those. First build queued in the heavy gate")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 4u32, "2026-10-18T21:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:22 main-thread tooling. (1) branch-commits.tsv: the 98 commits of host_cpp_and_cuda_backend since the merge base 850588a2 (2025-04-05), with hash, date, subject and files. (2) fixes.tsv: the candidates mapped to regression samples. C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB is out (our fix was -fwrapv in the build, not code). C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED gets a lab repro, repros/c_user_global: a global #define read through a macro, so a dropped global is a C build error, not just missing text. (3) make-patch.ps1 writes an anchored edit against the branch file as patches/<id>.diff and checks that it applies back. Done: C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED (4 lines) and C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT (3 lines; upstream zeroes only arrays of heap elements, init_at_zero = is_heap). (4) upstream.py export gained --until, so --since c~1 --until c carries one spiral commit onto the branch, and export-fix.ps1 turns that into a patch, refusing conflicts in the fix's own files. Its first use proved the guard: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (d94dc380) inserts beside method_key_of_tag, an earlier fork addition, so it needs a hand port. Order from here: run every candidate's sample on the unpatched upstream driver first; port only the fixes whose bug reproduces")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 5u32, "2026-10-18T21:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 17:55 main-thread: run-fixes.ps1 is the before-and-after judge. For each C row of fixes.tsv it copies the sample (or a lab repro) plus samples/core into <work>/runs/<label>, builds it with the upstream driver, compiles the C with pinned gcc 15.2 (-O2 -fwrapv), runs it, and compares the exit with our accepted baseline. Status: compile-fail, cc-fail, wrong-exit or ok, written to results-<label>.tsv. Patches so far: C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (a hand port: export-fix carried our p_var change but referenced closing_char, which upstream's p_var has no scope for, so the port threads closing_char through p_var and p_expr). Run-first rows, because upstream rewrote the code the fix touched: NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (Prepass now uses eval_type_nominal) and C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (depends on a fork-only method_key_of_tag). Lesson: an exported patch must be compile-checked against upstream; the export can carry a reference that doesn't exist there. The driver build (upstream net9 compile) holds heavy slot 2 now")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 6u32, "2026-10-18T22:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 18:43 main-thread FIRST BEFORE-RUN on his compiler (driver built from host_cpp_and_cuda_backend 0cb0fda0, his own core; results-before.tsv). Not reproduced, so out of post 1: SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP (UCRT -O2 exit 42), C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED (his C carries the global, exit 7), NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (his eval_type_nominal rewrite already fixed it). C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT needs a reproducing repro: fresh pages read zero, and number arrays are still left uninitialized by code reading. Reproduce, to confirm with patches: gadt_match_loses_constraint, multiline_type_application_dropped, nominal_union_case_pattern (type errors), match_multiline_scrutinee (main unparsed: the offside bug). Inconclusive (main unparsed, likely fork-only syntax in the sample): c_self_tail_loop_heap, macro_splice_before_quote; each needs an upstream-syntax repro. The rule is doing its job: three fixes we assumed were upstream bugs are not")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 7u32, "2026-10-18T23:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:15 main-thread: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT is proven by the emitted C. With repros/c_array_create_reused (fill a 16-element array inside a let function, which frees it, then create 16 and read [0]), his compiler emits ArrayCreate0(16l, false) and main reads ptr[0] before any write: an uninitialized read, undefined behavior. The Windows heap happened to return 0, so the exit code cannot show it. The post says exactly that. The parser fixes (FOR-D offside: continuation_column, commits d1cd44ca..6046588c; multiline type application; GADT constraint; nominal union pattern) do not export mechanically: the fork renamed the parser combinators in its single-file merge (blockParsingIndent, blockParsingRange versus upstream's indent, range), so upstream.py transfer reports conflicts on every touched line. They need hand ports into upstream BlockParsing.fs, Infer.fs and Prepass.fs. Parked behind the core migration (user: ASAP). Patches ready: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (the latter still needs an upstream-syntax repro)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 8u32, "2026-10-18T23:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:19 main-thread: offside fix hand-ported to upstream as patches/FOR-D-OFFSIDE.diff (BlockParsing: Env gains continuation_column : int option; the match parser records its keyword column and parses the scrutinee with it; application takes min(floor, col) as the offside line and clears it for nested terms) plus FOR-D-OFFSIDE-WDIFF.diff (WDiff's one Env construction sets continuation_column = None). Same three edits as our BlockParsing, written against upstream's indent/range names. Patched build (C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE, FOR-D-OFFSIDE pair) queued in the heavy gate as upstream-driver-after; its compile is the check that the patches type-check upstream, and run-fixes -Label after then gives the after column")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 9u32, "2026-10-18T23:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:34 main-thread fix list for post 1, deterministic per the rule (bug shown on his compiler, then the patch). Reproduce, patch ready: SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (his tokenizer reports the swallowed closing quote at line 4 col 26); FOR-D offside (match_multiline_scrutinee: main unparsed), FOR-D-TYPE-APPLY and GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT (type errors). All three are hand ports onto upstream's names: Env.continuation_column, the row's first-token column instead of the fork's layout_columns, and the var-var GADT arm with := and TypeErrorException. Proven by the emitted C: C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT. Reproduces but deferred to a later post: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (upstream-syntax repro at -O0 dies 0xC00000FD; the port is ~147 lines tangled with refcounting). Out: SPIRAL-C-UCRT-HEAP, C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED, NOMINAL-PHANTOM-PARAMETER (not reproduced or already fixed), C-SIGNED-OVERFLOW-UB and PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM (not code fixes to his code). Open: nominal_union_case_pattern reproduces but its fork change is not yet identified (185a355f only adds folder index modules). Next: the after build with the 6 patches (queued for a quiet machine), then run-fixes -Label after")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 10u32, "2026-10-19T00:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 20:39 main-thread AFTER-RUN: his compiler (host_cpp_and_cuda_backend 0cb0fda0) with the 6 post-1 patches builds, so the hand ports type-check upstream. results-after.tsv beside results-before.tsv: SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE compile-fail -> exit 17 (accepted 17); GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT compile-fail -> 0; match_multiline_scrutinee (offside) compile-fail -> 0; multiline_type_application_dropped compile-fail -> 0; C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT emitted ArrayCreate0(16l, false) -> ArrayCreate0(16l, true). Every row we did not patch is unchanged: self-tail still overflows (post 2), the nominal pattern is our syntax, and the rest pass both ways. 5 proven fixes in fixes.tsv; post/vanilla_start.spi regenerates with them (98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 fixes). Left for post 1: the core half needs the v1 sweep (reaped, rerun when the machine is quiet); then the post's prose pass and its home in the fork (the user's commit/PR)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 11u32, "2026-10-19T09:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:55 make-post.ps1 fixes table now has Before and After columns parsed from fixes.tsv status (proven: before, X; after, Y), so each row shows both halves of the proof; post/vanilla_start.spi regenerated (5 proven fixes). Blog-side compile of the 5-column table pending a quiet machine.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 12u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: checked the blog side of the new 5-column fixes table: blog engine post.spi Table is list string * list (list string) rendered by cells over the head list, so any column count renders; no engine change needed Next: compile post/vanilla_start.spi inside the blog engine with the blog build (HEAVY QUEUE blog build) and eyeball the table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 13u32, "2026-10-19T13:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:20 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1's plan: the queued blog full build (BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST row) can never compile this post: blog/scripts/build.ps1 tests only the engine modules (html, post, page, index, rss, style, site, showcase, build), and post/vanilla_start.spi lives in this lab folder with ///- --package . while referring to page.entry and post.*, so it needs the engine as its package. New lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/post-gate.ps1: copies the engine .spi files and vanilla_start.spi into spiral-bin/runs/upstream-post-1-gate/<stamp>/engine, rewrites package.spiproj there (absolute packageDir to spiral/lib, module vanilla_start after showcase; throws when the anchors move), and runs the Cells gate on the copy (memo off, own MIX_BUILD_PATH); the blog tree stays untouched. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~4 min). Next: after the gate, render the page HTML from the copy (mix spiral.html or the engine's generate) to eyeball the 5-column fixes table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 14u32, "2026-10-19T13:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:58 lap 3 grain: fact-checked the post's branch section against git (The-Spiral-Language clone): merge-base of mrakgr/master and mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend is 850588a2, 98 commits on top, first 2025-02-01, last 2025-11-14, and CodegenCpp.fs is touched by 36 of them: all as stated. One wording fix: the table keeps only .fs files (make-post.ps1:15 filters on \\.fs$), while over all files CHANGELOG (36), base.spi (23), package.json (23) and package-lock.json (21) outrank most of the listed compiler files, so These are the files they touch most was inaccurate; now These are the compiler source files (.fs) they touch most, in make-post.ps1 and in the generated post/vanilla_start.spi (same text, one occurrence each). Next: the queued post-gate.ps1 compiles the post inside a blog engine copy; then render and read the fixes table")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain: fact-checked the fixes table against the raw runs. Of fixes.tsv's 13 rows, the 5 the post calls proven match results-before/-after exactly: macro_splice_before_quote compile-fail -> exit 17, gadt_match_loses_constraint compile-fail -> 0, match_multiline_scrutinee compile-fail -> 0, multiline_type_application_dropped compile-fail -> 0, and c_array_create_reused from its own results-before-array.tsv (ArrayCreate0 false -> true). The out rows hold too: native_closure_recursive_capture exits 42 both before and after on his branch (not reproduced), nominal_phantom_parameter 0 both (already fixed upstream), nominal_union_case_pattern compile-fail both (fork syntax), ebpf_neq 0 both (core change shown as C text, not as a fix). The only proof not in a results file is C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (wrong-exit -1073741571 = stack overflow before; post 2's subject). Table data is publication-ready; the open step is still the queued post-gate compile")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_16 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:16 lap 5 grain: main's 10:57 post-gate counted as passed, but its summary says discovered 0, executed 0 (only the static prelude), so entry was type-checked at most and never evaluated or rendered. make-post.ps1 now generates 2 tests after the entry: the_post_is_a_page_of_the_site (site.files/paths holds vanilla-start/index.html) and the_post_renders_its_tables (page.render_entry has a table and the first proven fix id, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT; with no proven fixes it probes the placeholder note instead). Regenerated with -Date 2026-10-18: the old text is an unchanged prefix (22 core rows, 5 proven fixes). post-gate.ps1 now fails on a non-zero gate exit or when executed is not 2, and sets SPIRAL_LIB. Re-queued after the switch accept (the engine compiles against lib).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_17 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:33Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:33 lap 6 grain: the post's core table is generated from lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/decisions.tsv (22 rows, last written 10-18 18:38: take 9, take+extend 2, drop 6, keep 1, same 1, trim 1, move out 1, move out for now 1), and main is applying the switch now. The switch accept is therefore the point where the post's core section becomes a record of what landed instead of the plan. Step for after the accept: if decisions.tsv changed during the switch, rerun make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18 (the table regenerates, and the 2 render tests stay valid because they do not depend on the core rows), then the queued post-gate. Recorded, so the post does not ship the pre-switch plan.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_18 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 18u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7 grain: post-gate.ps1 now regenerates the post first (make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18) and then compiles that output, so the gate always tests what the generator writes from the current decisions.tsv, fixes.tsv and branch-commits.tsv, never a stale copy (this matters once the switch accept changes decisions.tsv). Parse-checked.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_19 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:29 lap 8 grain: dep CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is Done, so the post's core-decisions table can be final. Checked its input lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/decisions.tsv (10-18) against the shipped fork core (deps/The-Spiral-Language VS Code Plugin/core package.spiproj, 15 modules): 21 of 22 rows match; the types row said trim, but every alias in corebase/types.spi (tensor, partition, partition_offsets, serializer, pu, tuple_list) names a module that moved out, so the shipped core has no top-level types, only compile_time/types. Row corrected to drop with that reason. Next: post-gate.ps1 regenerates and compiles the post after main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_20 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:55 lap 9 grain: ran make-post.ps1 into the scratchpad (text generation only, no compile): 98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 proven fixes; against the stored post/vanilla_start.spi the only difference is the types row (trim -> drop with the corrected reason from lap 8). The post's tables are list literals ([...]) of strings, not ;[...] array literals, so the core v1 switch (array literals become array t) cannot break the post's compile; the blog engine's page/post modules were migrated by main. post-gate.ps1 regenerates and compiles after main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_21 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:30 lap 10 grain: the only dep, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE, is Done (100%, eoie lane 12:57: regen 168 owners 0 changed, eoie reinstalled), so nothing in the backlog blocks this story any more; the 12:55 scratch regeneration of make-post.ps1 already ran on the switched core (98 commits, 22 core rows, 5 proven fixes, only the corrected types row differs from the stored post). Left: main's go for post-gate.ps1 (the heavy proof of the 5 fixes), then store the regenerated post")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_22 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 22u32, "2026-10-19T17:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:50 lap 11 grain: post-gate.ps1 readiness re-checked after the switch: parses with 0 errors and every path it names exists (blog/engine + its package.spiproj, apps/cells, heavy.ps1, lib/spiral, post/vanilla_start.spi, make-post.ps1); it regenerates the post with -Date 2026-10-18 (the post's own date, intended). Ready to run on main's go")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_23 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 23u32, "2026-10-19T22:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:59 post-gate.ps1 PASSED on the installed pack 10 compiler and the core v1 lib (bounded job job-1792450312484, 60 s of work after a 315 s slot wait): make-post.ps1 -Date 2026-10-18 regenerated post/vanilla_start.spi from the current data (98 commits, 22 core rows incl. the corrected types row, 5 proven fixes with before and after), and inside a copy of blog/engine it compiled and ran both post tests, discovered 2, executed 2, passed 2, failed 0 (the post is a page of the site; the rendered entry has its tables and the first proven fix id). Blog tree only read. Everything on our side is done; what remains is outside the lanes: the user decides where the post lives (a blog/engine module, or the fork) and whether the PR is opened. Progress 90")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_24 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 24u32, "2026-10-20T07:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 03:19 main: post 1 is built into the blog. blog/scripts/generate.ps1 (Spiral compile of engine/generate.spi to Rust, 250,960 bytes; cargo release build; run) wrote dist/ with 8 site files: dist/vanilla-start/index.html (8,941 bytes) is the post, and dist/index.html plus dist/articles/index.html link it. Changed for the user to commit: blog/engine/vanilla_start.spi (new), the vanilla_start module in blog/engine/package.spiproj, blog/engine/generate.spi (the post first in the entries), and the regenerated blog/engine/generate.rs. Publishing stays with the user.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_25 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 25u32, "2026-10-22T03:49Z", "main", "23:49 lab drained into the fork (user 23:1x: post in fork too): all 29 files now live in the fork under posts/upstream_consolidation (data/, fixes/, make.ps1; post/vanilla_start.spi is regenerated as upstream_consolidation.spi, post-gate.ps1 became scripts/post.ps1); non-script files byte-identical; the post gate passes from the fork (POST-1-IN-FORK note 2). Removed spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (29 files); the fork changes are uncommitted for the user.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_26 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 26u32, "2026-10-24T05:08Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:08 main grooming: status Active -> Blocked. Every agent step is done (post gate PASS 10-19 22:59, built into the blog 10-20, drained into the fork 10-22). What remains is the user's: commit the fork and blog changes, choose where the post lives, and decide whether and when a PR is opened (outward-facing, so never an agent step). As Active it kept ranking first in agile next (wsjf 153600) with nothing an agent can do.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_27 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 27u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: the user says the post vanished and was to be moved to upstream: heal (UPSTREAM-FORK-START) (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_28 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 28u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 12 14:15 (subagent2): post-gate anchors still hold (blog engine package.spiproj unchanged since 09:06: packageDir and showcase lines present); its mix build dir upstream-post-1-gate already exists, so the run is warm -> next: main's go, or a bounded heavy run if under 2 min | lap 13 14:40 (subagent2): coverage check fixes.tsv vs post/vanilla_start.spi: every proven fix (C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, FOR-D-COMPILER-BUGS x2, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE, core SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ) is in the post; every out row and the unported C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (reproduces, needs the d94dc380 hand port) are absent, as intended -> next: main's go: post-gate.ps1; C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP is the post-2 candidate | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): ready (anchors, coverage); waits main's go for post-gate.ps1 -> next: main's go | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): ready -> next: main's go | lap 19-drive 18:59 (subagent2): post-gate.ps1 PASSED (job-1792450312484: 2 discovered, 2 executed, 2 passed; 60 s work, 315 s gate wait) on pack 10 + core v1; post regenerated from current data; set 90 (not Done: where the post lives and the PR are the user's call) -> next: user: post location + PR")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_29 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 29u32, "2026-10-24T13:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:32 main: found, not vanished. On 10-22 23:49 (the user's \"post in fork too\") the post's 29 files were moved from spiral/lab into the fork checkout C:\\home\\git\\The-Spiral-Language under posts/upstream_consolidation (data/, fixes/, make.ps1, upstream_consolidation.spi, plus scripts/post.ps1; the post gate passes there). They are UNTRACKED in the fork (`?? posts/`, `?? scripts/`, `M .gitignore`), and the blog's rendered copy lives only in blog/dist/vanilla-start (generated output), so nothing committed shows the post: that is why it looked gone. To make it visible: the user commits posts/ and scripts/ in the fork (commits are the user's), or tells me to move it elsewhere. The fork itself (remote mrakgr, HEAD 7423e39e) has had no core work from me yet; the plan for that starts in UPSTREAM-FORK-START note 1.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_1_vanilla_30 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", 30u32, "2026-10-24T13:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:37 main: healed, and a correction to note 1. The checkout that is the real fork is spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language: master, clean before this change, tracking origin i574n/The-Spiral-Language, carrying the user's 10-19 push and the consolidated core. C:\\home\\git\\The-Spiral-Language is a STALE 2023 checkout (branch i574n, HEAD May 2023). My 10-22 lab drain put the post there, which is why the user could not find it in the fork. Moved now: posts/upstream_consolidation (35 files) and scripts/init.ps1 + scripts/post.ps1 copied into the real fork (hash-verified, uncommitted for the user) plus `/deps/` and `/target/` in its .gitignore. The scripts now find spiral and blog by walking up from the checkout (init.ps1 reports blog found at C:\\home\\git\\blog, spiral found at C:\\home\\git\\spiral), so there is no junction and no cycle from inside spiral/deps. The stale checkout is back to its committed state (copies removed after the hash check, its deps junctions and target/ build output removed; .gitignore content equal to HEAD). Its existence as a second checkout is the user's call.")
inl cells_accept_tuples () = Task ("CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", "Cells --accept rewrites only scalar literals and hides why it refused: a tuple _assert_eq (e.g. (a, b, c, d) |> _assert_eq (0i32, 0i32, 0i32, 0i32)) reports accepted 0 with no reason on the CLI (the reason sits in the report row's accept_refused)", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,tibia,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl spiral_literal_alias_specialization () = Task ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION", "a record passed to a let function whose fields share one literal value (two \"\" or two 0u64) collapses into one dyn variable, so the join point gets a second specialization next to the recursive one", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,codegen,size", "current", "current")
inl upstream_post_2_c_self_tail () = Task ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", "Upstream post 2 (bite-sized series after UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA): hand-port C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP (fork commit d94dc380, ~147 CodegenC lines plus its fork-only method_key_of_tag) to upstream CodegenC, so a let rec self tail call becomes a while loop instead of C recursion. Reproduced 2026-10-18 on his compiler with spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/repros/c_self_tail_loop_heap: -O0 dies 0xC00000FD after 1M self calls, accepted exit 24. Same before-and-after proof as post 1", Story, P1, D4, 95u32, Active, "UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA", "blog,upstream,docs,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail () = Planning ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T23:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 19:39 main-thread: patch drafted early, spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/patches/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff (154 changed lines; applies to host_cpp_and_cuda_backend). upstream.py export --since d94dc380~1 --until d94dc380 carried everything but one insertion (CONFLICT at method_key_of_tag.[i] <- box (jp_body, key), a fork-only registration). The patch adds that line at the top of upstream's method_templ, so is_method_tag sees the same (jp_body, key) pairs. Not compiled yet: it gets its own build after post 1's, so a failure can't block post 1's patches")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:37 helper-lane lap 1: found a stacking conflict, checked with git apply --cached on a throwaway index of The-Spiral-Language at mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (no worktree or branch touched): the drafted lab/UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA/patches/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff applies alone, but on top of post 1's patches it fails at CodegenC.fs:470, because it carries post 1's own C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT hunk (TyArrayCreate init true) verbatim. Wrote lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff = the same patch minus that hunk: after C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, FOR-D-OFFSIDE, FOR-D-TYPE-APPLY, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT and SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE it applies cleanly (rc 0; the stack touches CodegenC, BlockParsing, Infer, Tokenize: 190 insertions, 13 deletions). Post 1's patch dir stays as is (main's).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:22 helper-lane lap 2: prepared the before/after proof so post 2 does not wait for tooling: lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/run.ps1 reuses post 1's own scripts in process (build-driver.ps1 -Patch with post 1's five patches plus the rebased C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff, into spiral-bin/upstream-post-2; then run-fixes.ps1 -Only C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP plus three post-1 rows as a guard), and moves results-after-post-2.tsv into this item's lab folder. Called with & instead of pwsh -File because -File would collapse the -Patch and -Only arrays into one string. Post 1's results-after.tsv still shows C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP wrong-exit -1073741571 (stack overflow) vs expected 24, which is what this run should flip. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL/run.ps1 (~15 min).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:52 helper-lane lap 3: pre-checked the queued run's riskiest step without building: a git archive of mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (0cb0fda0, 2025-11-14, the same ref build-driver.ps1 uses) with post 1's five patches applied in order and then the rebased C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff: all six git apply calls exit 0 (scratch tree, removed afterwards). So the queued run.ps1 cannot fail at its patch step; what it proves is the build and the exit-24 flip. Post 1 (UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST) is still Planned 0%, so the post 2 text waits for post 1 either way.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 5u32, "2026-10-22T03:49Z", "main", "23:49 lab drained into the fork (user 23:1x: post in fork too): C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff and run.ps1 now live in the fork under posts/c_self_tail_loop (run.ps1 reaches post 1 tooling at ../upstream_consolidation/fixes and heavy.ps1 through deps/spiral); the post text is still to write. Removed spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL (2 files); the fork changes are uncommitted for the user.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:35 (subagent): blocked: run.ps1 still queued (patch step pre-verified in lap 3) -> next: read results-after-post-2.tsv | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): run.ps1 queued -> next: read results-after-post-2.tsv | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): queued 15-min upstream build left queued while pack 11 builds -> next: after pack 11, under the gate | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: 15-min upstream build left queued while the gate is saturated -> next: idle window")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 7u32, "2026-10-26T09:33Z", "main", "10-26 05:33 main: post 2 resumed. The proof run was never completed (the 10-19 laps left it queued behind the saturated gate), so it's queued now as job-1793007041394-main-upstream-post-2-proof: posts/c_self_tail_loop/run.ps1 in the fork builds the upstream driver from mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend (0cb0fda0) plus post 1's 5 patches plus C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff, then runs C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE and GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT into results-after-post-2.tsv. Expected: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP exit 24 at -O0 (was 0xC00000FD after 1M self calls), and post 1's rows still pass. Post text drafted in post 1's page.entry shape at %TEMP%\\upstream-post-2\\c_self_tail_loop.spi, with The bug and The change sections (147 added, 4 removed, all in CodegenC.fs; loop + continue, arguments through temporaries, releases before continue, a returned self-call let counts, method_key_of_tag ported). It goes into the fork's posts/c_self_tail_loop only with its Fixes table filled from results-after-post-2.tsv, so the post states measured results, not expected ones; then post 1's Next paragraph is already true.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 8u32, "2026-10-26T10:19Z", "main", "10-26 06:19 main: post 2 proof's first run died at its first line (16 s): posts/c_self_tail_loop/run.ps1 dot-sources <fork>/deps/spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1. A standalone fork checkout has that link, but this fork lives at spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language, so its spiral is the enclosing repo, two levels up. post 1's fixes/run-fixes.ps1 had the same assumption for its samples path. Both now try <fork>/deps/spiral first and then the enclosing repo, and throw naming both if neither exists (parse-checked; the enclosing samples path verified). Uncommitted in the fork, for the user. Requeued as job-1793009939399-main-upstream-post-2-proof-2.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 9u32, "2026-10-26T14:07Z", "main", "10-26 10:07 main: proof-2 (job-1793009939399) failed in the driver build, not in the repro, for two causes found in its log. (1) WDiff.fs FS0764: no assignment for field 'continuation_column' of BlockParsing.Env. post 2's run.ps1 applied FOR-D-OFFSIDE.diff (which adds the field) without its companion FOR-D-OFFSIDE-WDIFF.diff (which fills it); run.ps1 now lists it. (2) CodegenC.fs FS0039: 'fns_printing', 'TyDo' and 'TyIndent' are not defined. C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff had been translated from our slim compiler core, which has a fns_printing stack and the TyDo/TyIndent ops, into upstream's file. Neither upstream (mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend 0cb0fda0) nor the fork checkout's CodegenC.fs has them. The patch applied textually and only failed to compile. Ported with the same semantics using upstream's own shape: a `function_depth` int ref, raised around every function-body print (method_templ around binds_start, and the closure body print at upstream's ~604); the loop records it and the four depth checks compare against it. The `TyDo b | TyIndent b` arm of op_self_tail was dropped (no such ops upstream). Tool: TEMP upstream-post-2/port.ps1, which applies the fork-cut diff to pristine upstream in a scratch git repo, makes the anchored edits (each exactly once; 4 for the depth checks), refuses if a fork-only name is left, and regenerates the diff (241 lines). The fork-cut original is kept as TEMP upstream-post-2/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.fork-cut.diff. Requeued: job-1793023556983-main-upstream-post-2-proof-3. Its expectation stays C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP ok at exit 24, -O0 (was a stack overflow), with the post 1 rows still ok.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 10u32, "2026-10-26T23:32Z", "main", "10-26 19:32 main: 10-26 19:32 main: proof-3 (job-1793023556983) never reached the driver build. It TIMED OUT (exit 124 after 8.2 h incl. queue) in a self-deadlock, read from its output. The job was started with --heavy, so kit took heavy slot 1 for it (holder label 'main-upstream-post-2-proof-3 (eoie job ...)', pid 36196). Then posts/c_self_tail_loop/run.ps1 wraps its body in Invoke-Heavy and asked for a second slot. Slot 2 was held by kit's FOLLOWUP release, so the script waited on its own job for 238 min ('Invoke-Heavy: still waiting ... slot 1 held by ... main-upstream-post-2-proof-3') until the wall clock hit. FIX (applied to main's requeue plan, TEMP pause-requeue/plan.json, backup beside it): the row now has Heavy=false, so the script's own Invoke-Heavy is the only gate, as run.ps1 intends. Scan: it was the only heavy plan row whose script also calls Invoke-Heavy. Expectation unchanged: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP ok at exit 24 at -O0, post 1 rows still ok. Generic gap, not fixed here (heavy.ps1 is shared and dot-sourced by running jobs): Invoke-Heavy should pass through when a slot's holder pid is an ancestor of the caller. That makes nested acquisition impossible to deadlock.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 11u32, "2026-10-26T23:46Z", "main", "10-26 19:46 main: 10-26 19:46 main: the generic fix for n10's self-deadlock is LANDED (unstaged) in spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1, hash 5A5F55043DCF008B -> 1D7D5889488BA952 (undo: TEMP heavy-reentry/heavy.ps1.pre-land; apply script TEMP heavy-reentry/apply.ps1, anchored, refuses if already applied). While a slot is held, Invoke-Heavy exports SPIRAL_HEAVY_HELD=<slot>:<pid> (restored afterwards). A nested Invoke-Heavy passes through and runs its command inside the held slot, printing 'Invoke-Heavy: slot N is already held by ancestor pid P; running inside it', only when ALL hold: that slot's holder file records that pid, and the pid is the caller itself or one of its ancestors (process Parent chain, depth 32). So a daemon that merely inherited the variable from a heavy job can't bypass the gate. kit's --heavy goes through its heavy-wrapper.ps1 -> Invoke-Heavy, so its children inherit the variable, which covers proof-3's case. PROOF (TEMP heavy-reentry/proof.ps1, private mutex names and a private queue dir, the real gate untouched): with slot 2 held by a blocker, outer Invoke-Heavy starting a child pwsh that calls Invoke-Heavy. Unpatched copy: did not finish in 90 s, inner never ran (positive control: the deadlock REPRODUCED). Patched copy: finished, inner ran, pass-through line printed. VERDICT GREEN. Before landing: no other slice in TEMP patches heavy.ps1, and the live file was untouched since 10-24 09:05. Running jobs parsed the old file at start, so only new runs get the change.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 12u32, "2026-10-27T00:57Z", "main", "10-26 20:57 main: 10-26 20:57 main: the re-entrant heavy.ps1 (n11, hash 1D7D5889488BA952) has done its first real acquisitions. After the post-rename requeue at 20:53, both slots were taken normally: slot 1 by main-polyglot-alias-heal-proof-4 and slot 2 by main-gate-convert-radix, both at 20:52, holders alive and recorded. 24 main chains are live, 16 running. No pass-through case has occurred in production yet (no nested caller), so that path is still proven only by the private-mutex control. This item's proof-3 is requeued without the job-level --heavy (job-1793062432267).")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 13u32, "2026-10-27T07:21Z", "main", "10-27 03:21 main: 10-27 03:21 main: proof-3 (job-1793062432267) TIMED OUT again at 4 h, this time purely in the queue (no double gate): it waited 238 min for slot 1, held by the hung polyglot alias-heal proof (see GRANULAR-HEAL). It never ran. Requeue with a timeout that covers the queue, after the alias-heal hang is resolved.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 14u32, "2026-10-27T08:14Z", "main", "10-27 04:14 main: 10-27 04:14 main: proof-4 (job-1793086025446) was the first run to reach the driver build, and it FAILED there on the ported patch, not on upstream. Three port bugs, all fixed in TEMP upstream-post-2/port.ps1. (1) The patch annotated its helpers with the fork-only type CodegenEnv (CodegenC.fs 183/184/189/248). Upstream's name is string_builder_env (binds_start/binds at 183/192 of the pristine file). Now Edit 'CodegenEnv' -> 'string_builder_env' (4), and CodegenEnv joins the fork-only-names guard so this can't pass silently again. (2) Line 289, 'op vars s (BindsLocal d) a', is a CONTEXT line of the diff left at upstream's 24-space indent, while the added match arm around it puts its body at 28, so F# read the arm's unit result as applied (FS0003 'not a function'). An anchored re-indent edit fixes it. (3) port.ps1 applied posts/.../C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff, the file its own -Write had overwritten with the ported result, so it wasn't re-runnable. It now always starts from the saved fork-cut original (C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.fork-cut.diff). Result: 0 CodegenEnv left, string_builder_env used 7x, the arm body aligned at 28, and an F# --parseonly of the ported CodegenC.fs exits 0 (types are checked by the real build). The diff is rewritten in the fork's posts/c_self_tail_loop (unstaged), and proof-5 is queued (job-1793088688213). The expectation is unchanged: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP ok at exit 24, -O0, post 1 rows still ok.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 15u32, "2026-10-27T10:43Z", "main", "10-27 06:43 main: proof-5 (job-1793088688213) RED at the upstream F# build: CodegenC.fs(312) FS0039 'UnionRecC' is not defined, plus FS0001 'int * string' vs 'string'. Diagnosis: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.fork-cut.diff carries hunks that are not the self-tail loop. Its 8 hunks, by whether they mention self_loop/self_tail/function_depth/self_let_tail: diff lines 5, 28, 90, 112 and 137 do; lines 104 (+1), 129 (+1) and 187 (+30) do not. Hunk 104 is the fork's stack-union refcount elision ('| UStack when ((ustack t : UnionRecC).free_vars : Map<int * string, TyV[]>) ...'). It depends on two fork-only types (UnionRecC, and free_vars keyed by int * string where upstream keys by string), so it can't build upstream and isn't part of this post's feature. Rule for the next port, fixed now: a hunk stays in the fork cut only if it mentions a self-tail term OR removing it makes a self-tail sample's C output differ. Hunk 104 is dropped; hunks 129 and 187 are read next and kept only by that rule. Then rerun port.ps1 -Write and proof-6.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_16 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 16u32, "2026-10-27T10:48Z", "main", "10-27 06:48 main: rule amended before it was applied (n15's 'output differs' clause would keep nearly any codegen hunk). Final rule: a fork-cut hunk stays only if it names a self-tail term (self_loop, self_tail, function_depth, self_let_tail) or a self-tail hunk uses a name it defines. Applied: hunk 129 stays, because it fills method_key_of_tag, which self-tail hunk 137 reads (TryGetValue tag) and hunk 5 declares. Hunk 104 (fork-only stack-union refcount elision on UnionRecC) and hunk 187 (iterative UHDecref for recursive heap unions, a separate feature and a candidate for its own post) are dropped. TEMP upstream-post-2/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.self-tail-only.diff keeps 6 of 8 hunks with 0 UnionRecC and 0 is_same_union. port.ps1 now applies it, and its fork-only-name guard also refuses UnionRecC. The port applies cleanly to upstream (197 diff lines, 0 fork-only names), and port.ps1 -Write rewrote the fork's posts/c_self_tail_loop/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff (unstaged). proof-6 is queued (heavy), the same run.ps1 as proof-5.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_17 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 17u32, "2026-10-27T11:57Z", "main", "10-27 07:57 main: proof-6 moved to the node (user 07:1x: the node had free CPU). Staged there: a fork clone at C:\\home\\remote\\post2\\The-Spiral-Language (origin plus the mrakgr remote, ref 0cb0fda0, the same as this box), posts pushed by hash-verified zip, deps\\spiral junction, and this box's matrix oracle EXPECTED.tsv, which run-fixes.ps1 reads. proof-6b (job-1793100837920): UPSTREAM-DRIVER-OK, so the self-tail-only port COMPILES against upstream (proof-5's UnionRecC failure is gone), and post 1's rows C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE and GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT are still ok. C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP itself was compile-fail with two causes, found by running the driver by hand on the node. (1) The repro's package.spiproj said 'core-' where its sibling repros say '|core-'. The bare form brought the fork's samples/core sm.spi into scope, and upstream can't parse it (sm.spi:72 'Expected: }', Unbound byte_to_i32). The sample uses nothing from core, so the spiproj now matches its siblings byte for byte. (2) With that fixed, upstream built the sample but gcc failed: 'continue statement not within a loop'. The diff's method_templ hunk adds 'else self_loop.Value <- None', and the line after it, 'binds_start (if is_while ...) x.body', is a CONTEXT line at its old indent. In the fork it sits inside the else, so applied to upstream it ran unconditionally and every self-tail method got its body twice: once in while (1), then once after it with a stray continue. port.ps1 now re-indents that line into the else, as it already did for the let-tail 'op vars' line (198 diff lines, 0 fork-only names), and -Write rewrote the fork's posts diff. proof-7 is on the node (job-1793102277804). Pass rule unchanged: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP ok with exit 24 at -O0, and post 1's rows still ok.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_18 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 18u32, "2026-10-27T12:06Z", "main", "10-27 08:06 main: proof-7 GREEN on the node (job-1793102277804, 6 min). Upstream 0cb0fda0 with post 1's patches plus the self-tail-only port: C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP builds and runs ok with exit 24 at -O0, as expected (upstream was a -1073741571 stack overflow), and post 1's rows C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT (0), SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE (17) and GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT (0) are still ok. Two fixes since proof-6b, both found by running the driver by hand on the node: the repro's spiproj now uses '|core-' like its siblings, and the method_templ else-branch context line is re-indented. The post's diff (posts/c_self_tail_loop/C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP.diff, unstaged in the fork) is the proven one. Next: the post's prose, and fetching results-after-post-2.tsv into the post folder.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_19 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 19u32, "2026-10-27T12:20Z", "main", "10-27 08:20 main: the post is DRAFTED in its proper place (unstaged, fork posts/c_self_tail_loop/): c_self_tail_loop.spi, a page entry in post 1's form (Paragraph/Heading/Table blocks plus the site and render test blocks, with list sugar [entry ()] instead of post 1's Cons/Nil), generated by make.ps1 from results-after-post-2.tsv (fetched from proof-7). make.ps1 refuses to write unless every row is ok at its expected exit, and its output is byte-identical to the hand draft. Sections: the bug (-O0 stack overflow, exit -1073741571 = 0xC00000FD), the fix (some path ending in a self call becomes while (1) with arguments reassigned through temporaries and continue; the other paths return), the port (2 of 8 hunks dropped as separate features; fns_printing.Count replaced by an explicit depth counter; the dependency rule for keeping hunks), checked (the 4 rows), next. Two claims corrected while drafting: 'every path' is 'some path' (self_tail ORs an if's branches), and the next-post line no longer asserts an unmeasured overflow from the iterative-decref hunk, only that a later post will measure it. Remaining: run the post's two test blocks under Cells, as post 1's were.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_20 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 20u32, "2026-10-27T15:04Z", "main", "10-27 11:04 main: blog check RESULT (node job-1793113346812, mirror 'mainpost' of spiral 3f1b7588 and blog 1b84c84f, sync-verified; compiler pinned 4FD712E9): blog's own build is still RED at today's HEAD. Under Cells, engine/post.spi has 8 'ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: html' sites (19:52 in cells, and 28, 29, 31, 33, 36, 38, 42 in render_block), the same break as n22/n23. Second finding: the fork's post gate (scripts/post.ps1) is stale against today's blog layout. It requires blog/engine/package.spiproj ('blog/engine or spiral/lib/spiral missing'), but engine/ is now a module folder inside blog's own package (blog/package.spiproj lists engine/ html post page index rss style site build), not a package of its own. Both share one root: once engine became a folder block, its modules can't see each other by name, because a folder block's namespace binds only after the block closes (n23). FIX PLAN (draft next, proven by the same node job): (1) blog/package.spiproj splits engine/ into an early block holding html (and any other leaf the rest uses) and a later engine/ block with post, page, index, rss, style, site and build, the way lib repeats rust/; the later modules then open the earlier block's namespace (:< engine, or engine.html). That needs one experiment, because n23 measured that ':< rust' resolves once the earlier block is closed. (2) The fork's post.ps1 overlays the post onto blog's root package instead of engine/package.spiproj: it inserts the post into blog/package.spiproj's modules and runs the gate on that copy. Post 2's own tests ride on the same fix.")
inl note_of_upstream_post_2_c_self_tail_21 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL", 21u32, "2026-10-27T15:28Z", "main", "10-27 11:28 main: post 2's own tests PASS; the post is proven end to end. The fork's post gate (scripts/post.ps1) is updated to blog's new layout: it copies blog's root package (package.spiproj, engine/, posts/, app/) into target/post/blog, makes packageDir absolute, puts the post .spi at the overlay root, and lists it as a top-level module right before the posts/ block, i.e. after the engine blocks. The old gate required blog/engine/package.spiproj, which no longer exists. Because a post now sits outside engine's blocks, both posts open engine (as blog's posts/showcase.spi already does), added through BOTH generators (posts/*/make.ps1 templates) and the generated .spi files; each make.ps1 regenerates its .spi byte-identical. Node proof (job-1793114718510; mirrors of spiral 3f1b7588 and the layer-fixed blog, compiler pinned 4FD712E9): upstream_consolidation 2/2 passed and c_self_tail_loop 2/2 passed, VERDICT GREEN. With the self-tail port proven upstream (proof-7) and the post generated from its results, post 2 is complete except for the user's commit of the fork's untracked posts/ and scripts/.")
inl wgsl_run_wgpu_30 () = Task ("WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30", "Upgrade spiral-wgsl (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run) from wgpu 24 / pollster 0.4 to wgpu 30+, the only wgpu pin in our repos; rerun the matrix Wgsl rows and the tibia GPU twins (gpu_sim, hunt_sim) on lavapipe and the local NVIDIA", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,deps,tibia", "current", "current")
inl rust_nightly_everywhere () = Task ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", "Every Rust build in every repo on one recent dated nightly (user 2026-10-19 via the tibia lane: attempt rust nightly for everything, pin a recent date as default, lower only where it still does not work; all repos). Default nightly-2026-10-18 (rustc 1.101, installed cleanly by the tibia lane; wgpu 30 needs >= 1.90). Scope: rust-toolchain.toml files, cargo +X calls in scripts, Kino ensure_rust_toolchain (+1.99.0), the matrix native rustc, CI workflows; install once before parallel jobs (rustup is not concurrency-safe). Every fallback to an older date gets a recorded reason", Feature, P0, D5, 85u32, Paused, "", "rust,toolchain,ci,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_nightly_everywhere () = Planning ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_1 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T06:37Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 02:37 main-thread inventory + first pass. Pins found across the 10 repos: nightly-2025-11-01 (app and tool builds: spiral apps wasm/spiral/plot/lisp, lib rust testing.spi, parsing bench, dir-tree-html, dice lib, alphabet documents, polyglot math), nightly-2025-05-09 (machine default and Kino wasm default in spiral and polyglot init.ps1), nightly-2024-07-14 (NEAR contracts: dice contract, polyglot chat contract, apps/spiral contract build), 1.99.0 (compiler matrix Rust rows and the Cells Rust host), x rust-toolchain.toml channel stable. The tibia lane's share is done (rust-toolchain.toml, gh-pages.yml, apps/map). Applied with lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/apply.ps1: 23 pins in 15 files -> nightly-2026-10-18, NEAR excluded (apply.ps1 -Near later; newer rustc emits wasm features the NEAR VM may reject, which would be the recorded fallback). The new toolchain already has clippy, rust-src, rustfmt and wasm32. Verified: dir-tree-html RUST-OK in 39 s. verify.ps1 (7 more builds, serial) running. Next: the matrix and Cells 1.99.0 pin as a reviewed matrix pack, then NEAR, then rustup default")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_2 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T09:54Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 05:54 paused the verify rerun at the tibia lane's request (GPU twins at one job, hand-back by 06:45). Done so far on nightly-2026-10-18: spiral apps/lisp, apps/plot pass. apps/wasm got past openssl-sys and stopped in near-workspaces; resume with verify.ps1 -Skip 'spiral apps/lisp','spiral apps/plot' -Jobs 2 as an eoie job.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_3 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T10:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:02 paused again for the tibia lane's PR-14 live block (06:05-06:30). verify.ps1 now splits -Skip on commas, because pwsh -File (as eoie job start runs it) cannot take an array (the first eoie job bound 'spiral apps/plot' to -Out). Resume: eoie job start rust-nightly-verify 5400000 pwsh -NoProfile -File verify.ps1 -Skip 'spiral apps/lisp,spiral apps/plot' -Jobs 2.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_4 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T10:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 06:05 CI pins: gh-pages.yml setup-rust channel set to nightly-2026-10-18 in alphabet, dice, dir-tree-html, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral (were nightly-2025-05-09 or 2025-11-01) and blog (was stable; its Rust comes through Cells, which now runs +nightly-2026-10-18), so the toolchain the build scripts name exists on the runner. Only with: changed, no uses:, so actions.lock is untouched. Left to their lanes: x ci.yml says stable but x has rust-toolchain.toml nightly-2026-10-18 (x lane); eoie gh-pages.yml pins 1.88.0 (eoie lane, needs an eoie build on the nightly first). Tibia already on nightly-2026-10-18.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_5 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T10:13Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:13 eoie lane: eoie on nightly-2026-10-18 (rustc 1.101.0-nightly 8006091eb 2026-10-17): cargo check --locked --workspace --all-targets in a fresh target folder checked 198 crates, 0 errors, 8 warnings (unused dependencies plus one manifest warning in patch-domain), 3m29s. eoie .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml now pins channel nightly-2026-10-18 (was 1.88.0); uncommitted for the user. Not run locally on nightly: codegen and the test run (CI covers them on the next push). Local default toolchain is still nightly-2025-05-09; tonight's releases were built with it.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_6 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:05 verification complete on nightly-2026-10-18, every build exit 0: dir-tree-html, spiral apps/lisp, apps/plot, apps/wasm (144 s), apps/spiral (283 s), dice lib (201 s), alphabet apps/documents (282 s), polyglot lib/math (154 s); no build needed a lower date. Pack 5 review single-flight-20261019-072348 (matrix rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, wgpu 30 runner): 7,007 rows, native memo replayed 0 (all 6,508 native rows ran fresh, so every Rust row compiled on the nightly), differing cells 0, DISAGREE 0. Accept running as eoie job pack5-accept. Remaining: NEAR pins (apply.ps1 -Near, contract builds) and Cells test.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_7 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: Linux rustix break reproduced on Windows with cargo check --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p rustix@0.37.28 (rust-std for linux added to nightly-2026-10-18); fixed without lowering the date via rustix's own libc backend cfg: .cargo/config.toml with [target.'cfg(target_os = linux)'] rustflags --cfg rustix_use_libc in spiral/workspace, dice and polyglot/apps/chat (linux-only, Windows and wasm untouched); the check then passes Next: cargo check --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p spiral_wasm and dice_contract_tests with the config (heavy, after pack 5 accept); then NEAR pins")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_8 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:55Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:55 pack 5 ACCEPTED: single-flight-20261019-080524 accepted 7,007 rows into the baseline (matrix rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, wgpu 30 runner, -AcceptToolchain), native 6,499 ran, oracle agree 5,779, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the review 072348, C rows vs the pack 4 toolchain run 0 of 531 differ.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_9 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:05 the whole-tree Linux cargo check from Windows is dropped: cargo check still runs build scripts, and spiral_wasm's vendored openssl-sys compiles C for the target, which needs a Linux cross compiler; the rustix check (the only nightly break seen) passed locally with the cfg, and the user's next push gives the full Linux verdict on CI.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_10 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:22 lap 2 grain: pin inventory across the in-scope repos (grep of rust-toolchain.toml, cargo/rustc/rustup +toolchain calls, CI setup steps): on nightly-2026-10-18 already: tibia and x rust-toolchain.toml, wgsl-run shim, matrix rustc (test.ps1:710), Cells @rust_toolchain (cells.ex:863), spiral apps wasm/spiral/plot/lisp builds, spiral init trunk install, dice lib, polyglot lib/math, alphabet documents, dir-tree-html, eoie CI (moonrepo/setup-rust channel nightly-2026-10-18). Only remaining older pin: the NEAR contract path, dice/contract/build.ps1:50-51 (+nightly-2024-07-14 build and metadata) plus polyglot/scripts/init.ps1:36-37 installing that toolchain for it. Recorded reason for the fallback: since rustc 1.82 wasm32-unknown-unknown enables reference-types and multivalue by default and the precompiled std carries them, which the nearcore 1.79 VM used by the dice sandbox tests rejects. Candidate lift: nightly-2026-10-18 with -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and RUSTFLAGS -C target-cpu=mvp. Prepared lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/near-mvp.ps1 (own CARGO_TARGET_DIR under spiral-bin/runs, then reads the wasm target_features section for +reference-types/+multivalue/+bulk-memory). HEAVY QUEUE row added (~10 min). Next: if the wasm is mvp-clean, switch dice/contract/build.ps1 (and alphabet's mirror if any) to the dated nightly with build-std, drop the 2024-07-14 install from polyglot init, and let the next push's Linux sandbox test decide")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_11 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:58 lap 3 grain: CI side verified: all 11 moonrepo/setup-rust steps across the 10 repos' workflows (spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, blog, dir-tree-html, x twice, eoie, tibia, i574n/.github) set channel nightly-2026-10-18. The one CI path without a setup-rust step is spiral's compiler job (schedule/dispatch only), whose test.ps1 calls rustc +<toolchain> from 2 parallel workers: in today's first Linux run (39258592236) rustup auto-installed +1.99.0 concurrently and 5 array_* Rust rows build-failed (could not rename downloaded component). Fixed in the tree (CI-OFFLOAD note 5): scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler reads the matrix toolchain from test.ps1 and runs rustup toolchain install once before the suite, so the nightly switch in test.ps1 cannot reintroduce the race. Remaining pin: the NEAR contract (near-mvp.ps1 queued; TODO block at dice/contract/build.ps1:50). Progress 70")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_12 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain: audited the other auto-install path for the CI race found in lap 3. Cells (cells.ex:863-899) pins +nightly-2026-10-18 and installs it through ensure_rust_toolchain, which serializes inside one BEAM with :global.trans plus a persistent_term ready flag, so its parallel cells cannot race rustup; two separate BEAMs (two mix runs at once, or the daemon plus a gate) still could on a machine without the toolchain, which only CI hits, and CI's Cells steps run sequentially in one workflow step. test.ps1's matrix rustc is the other parallel caller: covered on CI by the workflow.ps1 pre-install (lap 3), and on this laptop the toolchain is installed. No further change needed; remaining work is only the NEAR pin")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_13 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 near-mvp 10:59 got past build-std and failed at rust-lld: undefined panic_utf8, storage_write, ... This nightly no longer passes --allow-undefined for wasm32-unknown-unknown, and the dice lock pins near-sys 0.2.2 whose extern block has no wasm_import_module; near-sys 0.2.14 (in the local registry) has cfg_attr(wasm32, link(wasm_import_module = env)). near-mvp.ps1 now builds twice: with -C link-arg=--allow-undefined, then with near-sys 0.2.14 in a Cargo.lock copy via -Z lockfile-path (dice lock untouched). Re-queued (12 min). If variant 2 passes, the dice fix is a lock-only near-sys bump plus the toolchain swap in contract/build.ps1 (alphabet mirrors).")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_14 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:09Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 11:09 NEAR contracts on nightly-2026-10-18: a plain dice_contract wasm build fails at rust-lld (undefined NEAR host symbols panic_utf8, storage_remove: this nightly no longer passes --allow-undefined, and dice locks near-sys 0.2.2 without wasm_import_module, per helper 2); with RUSTFLAGS -C link-arg=--allow-undefined it builds (265,665 B, 58 s) and with build-std + target-cpu=mvp + allow-undefined too (variant 1 of near-mvp.ps1, 1m28s). Variant 2 tests the near-sys 0.2.14 bump alone; the lab run hit cargo's --lockfile-path unstable flag (cargo +nightly needs -Z unstable-options before update; helper 2 owns the script). Next: the sandbox test on Linux CI decides whether mvp is needed; then flip the NEAR pins (apply.ps1 -Near) with either the link arg or the near-sys bump.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_15 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T15:10Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:10 main ran near-mvp.ps1 once more: variant 1 (nightly-2026-10-18, build-std, target-cpu=mvp, -C link-arg=--allow-undefined) BUILT in 1m28s, so the toolchain path works with one extra link arg. Variant 2 failed at cargo update: --lockfile-path is gone as a CLI flag on cargo 1.101.0-nightly (stabilized in 1.97 as the config key resolver.lockfile-path, probe warning confirms). Script now passes --config resolver.lockfile-path=<copy> to update (offline) and build, and checks the copy pins near-sys 0.2.14. Re-queued after the switch window. dice Cargo.lock verified unchanged.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_16 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:34 lap 6 grain: swept all repos for nightly pins so lifting the last one is a single change list. Every nightly in our ps1/toml/yml files is nightly-2026-10-18 (dice lib, dice, blog, dir-tree-html, eoie and alphabet workflows, alphabet documents, polyglot init), with these exceptions: (1) dice/contract/build.ps1:53-54 (cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 build and metadata), the pin this item is about; (2) polyglot/scripts/init.ps1:35-37, which installs nightly-2024-07-14 with the wasm32 target, clippy, rust-src and rustfmt only to serve (1), so it goes in the same change; (3) left alone: fc1943s.github's workflow (nightly-2024-10-07, the fc1943s backup repos are kept as they are), rustix and zed (third-party clones). Once near-mvp variant 2 builds, the change is: near-sys 0.2.14 in dice's Cargo.lock, the toolchain plus -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and RUSTFLAGS -C target-cpu=mvp in contract/build.ps1, and polyglot init minus 3 lines. alphabet has no contract (checked), so there is nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_17 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:50 lap 7 grain: (1) the lift is staged as lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/lift-pin.py (dry run: both anchors match once, LF files). It writes dice/contract/build.ps1 build + metadata on nightly-2026-10-18 with -Z build-std=std,panic_abort and CARGO_TARGET_WASM32_UNKNOWN_UNKNOWN_RUSTFLAGS=-C target-cpu=mvp (target-scoped, so dice's .cargo linux rustflags for host build scripts are not overridden the way a global RUSTFLAGS would), drops the TODO block, and removes polyglot init's 3 nightly-2024-07-14 lines. Plus the lock bump: cargo update -p near-sys --precise 0.2.14. (2) near-mvp.ps1 variant 2 now uses exactly those target-scoped flags. (3) The feature check was weak: the linked wasm need not keep a target_features section (and -s strips more), so 'none' could be a false all-clear. Each variant now also runs wasmtime compile -W bulk-memory=n,reference-types=n,multi-value=n on the result (the prebuilt 2024 dist/dice.wasm passes it: exit 0), which validates the code itself without those features.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_18 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T16:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:30 lap 8 grain: checked what the staged lift needs beyond its own edits: dice gh-pages.yml already installs nightly-2026-10-18 with components clippy,rust-src,rustfmt and the wasm32-unknown-unknown target, so -Z build-std has rust-src in CI once polyglot init stops installing nightly-2024-07-14; locally rust-src and rust-std-wasm32 are installed on that nightly. alphabet (mirrors dice) has no contract and is already on nightly-2026-10-18 in CI and apps/documents/build.ps1, so the lift needs no alphabet edit. The only 2024 pins left in dice are contract/build.ps1:53-54, which lift-pin.py covers. Next: near-mvp variant 2 after main's go, then lift-pin --apply and the near-sys 0.2.14 lock bump")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_19 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:55 lap 9 grain: lift-pin.py still matches both anchors once (dice contract/build.ps1, polyglot scripts/init.ps1). Checked the lock bump it relies on: dice/contract pins near-sdk =5.11.0 and crates.io lists that version's near-sys requirement as ^0.2.2, so cargo update -p near-sys --precise 0.2.14 (the latest near-sys, the one with the newer wasm handling) stays inside the sdk's requirement and needs no Cargo.toml edit; Cargo.lock has near-sys 0.2.2 today. Separate finding for latest-of-everything: near-sdk itself is 5.11.0 against 5.29.1 on crates.io; that bump is its own step after the nightly lift (contract API changes possible)")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_20 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T17:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:30 lap 10 grain: the separate near-sdk bump sized from crates.io: latest stable 5.29.1 (2026-09-17, rust_version 1.93, a nightly satisfies it) requires near-sys ^0.2.14, borsh ^1.0.0, near-account-id ^2.3.0; so the near-sys 0.2.14 lock bump staged for this item is exactly what 5.29.1 needs, and the two bumps can land as one cargo update -p near-sdk --precise 5.29.1 (pulls near-sys 0.2.14) after dice's pin =5.11.0 is relaxed to 5.29.1. 5.30.0-rc.1 (today, rust 1.95) is a pre-release: skip. Next: near-mvp v2 after main's go, then lift-pin --apply and the combined sdk + sys bump")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_21 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:50 lap 11 grain: the near-sdk pin =5.11.0 sits in 4 manifests, so the bump is one change across 3 repos: spiral apps/wasm/Cargo.toml:21, dice contract/Cargo.toml:16 (pinned 2025-10-25 in 4e107a1, commit message only 'tests', no stated reason), polyglot apps/chat/contract/Cargo.toml:14 and its tests/Cargo.toml:12; alphabet has none (nothing to mirror). Risk to check in near-mvp v2: newer near-sdk releases target wasm32v1-none-era toolchains, so the contract build flags may need a look, not just the lock. Next: near-mvp v2 after main's go, then the combined sdk 5.29.1 + near-sys 0.2.14 bump in all 4 manifests")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_22 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T18:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:16 lap 12: near-sdk's latest release 5.29.1 (2026-09-17, rust_version 1.93) requires near-sys ^0.2.14, which is exactly one of the two unblocks the dice contract/build.ps1 TODO names (wasm_import_module env); today's locks hold near-sdk 5.11.0 with near-sys 0.2.5 (spiral workspace) and 0.2.2 (dice). So the latest-of-everything bump of the 4 near-sdk pins should also lift the last nightly pin; near-mvp v2 can test the bump instead of a separate near-sys lock")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_23 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T18:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:34 lap 13: the nearcore that rejects post-1.82 wasm comes from the contract test harness: dice and polyglot contract/tests pin near-workspaces =0.11.1 (old sandbox), while near-workspaces 0.23.0 (released 2026-10-19) and near-sandbox 0.3.16 (2026-10-14) are current. Hypothesis for near-mvp v2: bump near-sdk to 5.29.1 and near-workspaces to 0.23.0 together and build on the plain nightly, which may remove the build-std/mvp workaround instead of locking near-sys")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_24 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T23:05Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:05 lap 20 grain, latest-of-everything recheck from the crates.io API (max_stable_version): near-sdk 5.29.1 (published 2026-09-17) is still the newest stable, newest overall 5.30.0-rc.1; near-workspaces 0.23.0 stable, but published 2026-07-09, not 2026-10-19 as the 14:34 note said, newest 0.24.0-rc.2; near-sandbox 0.3.16 stable published 2026-09-04 (not 10-14), newest 0.4.0-rc.2. So near-sdk-bump.ps1's default 5.29.1 is right; the release candidates are not releases and stay out. Next: the near-sdk-bump row (beyond the 2-minute cap, main's queue)")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_25 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 25u32, "2026-10-21T22:26Z", "main", "18:26 pin census (git grep of tracked ps1, yml, ex, exs and toml, plus rust-toolchain files in spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, tibia, eoie and i574n.github): every Rust pin is nightly-2026-10-18 (3 rust-toolchain.toml files: spiral's wgsl-run shim, x, tibia; cargo/rustc +nightly-2026-10-18 in spiral apps/lisp, plot, spiral, wasm and scripts/init.ps1, polyglot lib/math, dice lib, alphabet apps/documents) except one: dice/contract/build.ps1:53-54 cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 for the NEAR wasm32 contract. Its recorded reason is the post-1.82 wasm features that the pinned near-workspaces 0.11.1 sandbox rejects, and its exit is the near-sdk-bump.ps1 row (near-sdk 5.29.1 + near-workspaces 0.23.0 on the plain nightly; cargo-heavy, main's queue). No workflow pins a toolchain by action input. So the item is at its last row: the near-sdk bump.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_26 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 26u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 9 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE until the item lands.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_27 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 27u32, "2026-10-22T07:07Z", "main", "03:07 main: lab trimmed to the open row. apply.ps1 is deleted: the 10-21 18:26 census shows every pin it targeted at nightly-2026-10-18. verify.tsv (the 7-build verification of 10-19, all exit 0) moved to the run cache. Kept for the near-sdk row: lift-pin.py (its dry run still matches dice/contract/build.ps1 and polyglot/scripts/init.ps1, so the lift is not applied; the TODO at dice contract line 50 is the earlier marker), near-mvp.ps1, near-sdk-bump.ps1 and verify.ps1.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_28 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 28u32, "2026-10-22T21:45Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:45 main: gap found. The compiler matrix does NOT run the pinned nightly. C:\\home\\git\\spiral has no rust-toolchain.toml, and the rustup default is nightly-2025-05-09 (rustc 1.88.0-nightly 50aa04180), so test.ps1's native Rust tier (tools.Rustc = the scoop rustup proxy) and export-check-rust compile every Rust row with the 2025-05-09 toolchain. PACK-NEXT-3's export checks showed nightly-2025-05-09 in the process list. x hit the same split today (its SPIRAL_RUSTC change, X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED). Fix, not applied: the rustup default is machine-wide and shared by every lane, so it stays. Instead, test.ps1 and export-check-rust pass +nightly-2026-10-18 when the rustc they resolve is the rustup proxy (the Cells rule, fixed today by step 13 to decide by directory). The Rust tool stamp changes, so it needs its own -AcceptToolchain accept run after PACK-NEXT-3b's, with Rust native results compared row for row (any difference is a toolchain finding, read before accepting).")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_29 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 29u32, "2026-10-22T21:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:54 main: CORRECTION to note 28. The matrix IS pinned: test.ps1's Rust native clause (line 725) calls the proxy with +nightly-2026-10-18, so matrix Rust rows and their accepts use the pinned nightly. The unpinned callers are only the bare-rustc helpers: scripts/export-check-rust.ps1 (the 2025-05-09 rustc seen in PACK-NEXT-3's export checks) and scripts/try.ps1. Fix scope shrinks to those two: pass +nightly-2026-10-18 when the resolved rustc is the rustup proxy. Neither is in Get-NativeSignature, so no -AcceptToolchain run is needed.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_30 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 30u32, "2026-10-22T21:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:54 main: applied in the tree. export-check-rust.ps1 (both rustc calls) and try.ps1 (its Rust run) pass +nightly-2026-10-18 when the rustc on PATH is rustup's proxy (under .cargo\\bin), the same rule Cells step 13 uses. A direct toolchain rustc gets no + argument, avoiding the bug x hit. On this machine rustc resolves to scoop's .cargo\\bin proxy, so both now compile with the pinned nightly. PACK-NEXT-3b's export checks are the first run of the edited script.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_31 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 31u32, "2026-10-23T03:02Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:02 main: audit of what's left (read only). Pinned to nightly-2026-10-18: every CI workflow (blog, dir-tree-html, dice, i574n.github, alphabet, x x2, tibia, eoie, polyglot, spiral x2), Cells (cells.ex:871 @rust_toolchain), the matrix (test.ps1:725), export-check-rust and try.ps1, alphabet documents, dice lib and dir-tree-html builds. Stale: dice/contract/build.ps1 on nightly-2024-07-14 (recorded reason: the near-sdk bump; the contract doesn't type-check on newer nightlies). Main gap: 24 bare cargo calls in tracked scripts (spiral 4: mega bench, apps/zed test.ps1, lib parsing bench, init-wgsl-run; polyglot 3; dice 5; eoie 8; x 2; blog 1) resolve to the machine default nightly-2025-05-09 (rustc 1.88) locally while CI uses 2026-10-18, because no repo of ours has a rust-toolchain.toml. Fix: a rust-toolchain.toml (channel = 'nightly-2026-10-18') per repo, so a bare cargo matches CI. Plan: spiral first (mine), after a quiet-window build of its cargo crates (apps/zed, wgsl-run, the benches) on the pinned nightly; eoie, x and tibia decide for their repos (eoie documents Rust 1.88 and is mid release train), so they get the data by message, not an edit. Third-party clones (zed, atrium, cargo-outdated, dlm) keep their own toolchains.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_32 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 32u32, "2026-10-23T03:03Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:03 x: correction for x. x has a rust-toolchain.toml: tracked, in HEAD 61b8fff, channel = nightly-2026-10-18. In C:\\home\\git\\x, 'rustup show active-toolchain' prints 'nightly-2026-10-18-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc (overridden by C:\\home\\git\\x\\rust-toolchain.toml)' and rustc --version prints 1.101.0-nightly (8006091eb 2026-10-17). So the bare 'cargo build --release' in apps/client/build.ps1:37 and scripts/workflow.ps1:7 already builds with CI's nightly locally, and x needs no change. Cells' rust cells match too, by their own +nightly-2026-10-18. Before calling a repo's toolchain unpinned, run 'rustup show active-toolchain' inside it: it names the override file it applies.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_33 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 33u32, "2026-10-23T03:04Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:04 main: correction to note 31 (x pointed it out). x and tibia already have a tracked rust-toolchain.toml on nightly-2026-10-18. My Grep hit its 80-result cap on third-party clones, so their files never showed. rustup show active-toolchain per repo is authoritative: x and tibia are overridden by their files to nightly-2026-10-18. spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, eoie, blog, fiscal, i574n.github and dir-tree-html fall back to the machine default nightly-2025-05-09. So the bare cargo calls in x are fine; the gap is those 9 repos. The plan stands with x and tibia out of it.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_34 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 34u32, "2026-10-23T03:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:09 main: spiral's rust-toolchain.toml staged in the scratchpad (channel only, x's minimal form, so no component or target installs are forced). It lands after eoie's F6/F7 test batch leaves the spiral tree, then one quiet-window check builds spiral's bare-cargo crates on the pinned nightly: scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1 (wgpu 30 needs >= 1.90, which the 1.88 default can't build), apps/zed test.ps1, the parsing bench. Cells and the matrix pass +nightly-2026-10-18 explicitly, which outranks a toolchain file, so they're unaffected.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_35 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 35u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:11 main: spiral/rust-toolchain.toml written (channel nightly-2026-10-18, x's minimal form) after F9 left the tree. Open: the quiet-window build check of spiral's bare-cargo crates (scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1, apps/zed test.ps1, the parsing bench); the remaining 8 repos still fall back to the machine default.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_36 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 36u32, "2026-10-23T12:34Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:34 main: rule, fixed before writing: a repo with a tracked Cargo.toml and no toolchain file gets x's minimal rust-toolchain.toml (channel nightly-2026-10-18), which is CI parity since every gh-pages workflow already pins that nightly. Written to polyglot, dice, alphabet (which mirrors dice), blog and dir-tree-html. rustup show active-toolchain in each now reports nightly-2026-10-18, overridden by the repo's file. fiscal and i574n.github have no tracked Cargo manifest, so they're skipped. eoie (197 manifests) is the eoie lane's repo and was asked to add the same file. Build proof: the queued main-rustls-check (job-1792753867788, after HP) runs cargo check --locked in dice, spiral/workspace and polyglot/apps/chat, now on the pinned nightly; its result settles this item's open check for those repos. Uncommitted, for the user.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_37 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 37u32, "2026-10-24T03:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:11 main grooming estimate 120 m: remaining milestones: toolchain files are written (10-23 08:34); build each Rust repo once on nightly-2026-10-18, about 20 m each for 6 repos.")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_38 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 38u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 11:07 (subagent2): near-mvp link failure: nightly drops --allow-undefined, dice locks near-sys 0.2.2 without wasm_import_module (0.2.14 has it); script builds both variants, re-queued -> next: if near-sys 0.2.14 builds: lock bump + toolchain swap in dice contract/build.ps1 (+ alphabet); note someone set --allow-undefined on variant 2 too | lap 14 15:01 (subagent2): staged near-sdk-bump.ps1 (near-mvp v2 part a): standalone copy of dice/contract with near-sdk =5.29.1 and the dice release profile, plain nightly build, reports post-mvp wasm features + locked near-sdk/near-sys; parse 0; queue row (about 6 min, over the bounded slot) -> next: main's queue; part b near-workspaces 0.23 sandbox test if features show up | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): note for the CLI slice: rust_runner keeps the monolith's cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 for --wasm/--contract builds (parity), one more pin for this item to lift with the near-sdk bump -> next: near-sdk-bump.ps1 row | lap 19 18:55 (subagent2): unchanged: near-sdk-bump.ps1 (network + cargo wasm build) is beyond the 2-minute cap -> next: main's queue (row 102)")
inl note_of_rust_nightly_everywhere_39 () = Note ("RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE", 39u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl cells_everywhere () = Task ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", "Every Spiral source in every repo runs under Cells (user 2026-10-19 02:19: 100% of the code should eventually become cell compatible, split destructively, coordinate with the main lane without clogging it). Cell compatible = the file opens with a ///- --package line and a static prelude test, every module has test blocks with explicit ///> targets that agree, Rust-only primitives sit behind backend_switch (or a thin crate-glue module) so the domain compiles to more than one backend, and run_notebook.ps1 --path file passes; crates keep their !!!!Export entry. Per repo children: X-CELLS-SPLIT (x lane), eoie via EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION (eoie lane), lib/spiral and compiler samples (main lane, only when idle), tibia already follows it", Epic, P1, D7, 5u32, Active, "", "cells,spiral,x,eoie,tibia,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_everywhere () = Planning ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 4800u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: lib/spiral baseline: 81 .spi files, 40 carry test blocks (401 tests), 41 have none; none open with a ///- --package line because lib is itself the package (package.spiproj), so for lib the criterion is test blocks with explicit ///> targets per module, not the prelude line Next: list the 41 lib files without tests by size, smallest first, as the lib slice's work queue")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:24 lap 2 grain: lib slice work queue, recounted excluding the git-ignored bench/parsing/target copies (otherwise 199 files): 81 files, 41 without test blocks. By kind: 5 declaration-only operator/prototype files (file_system_operators 1, optionm'_operators 1, rust_operators 2, sm'_prototypes 4, iter_prototypes 8, am'_prototypes 10; sm'_operators 29); 19 leptos ui/svg view components plus leptos.spi 893 (browser views: static compile tests at most); 3 bench/parsing (main 14, pure_split 22, bench_kit 157, fparsec 201); browser bindings store 46 and wasm 132; real logic: mapm 87, json_scan 176 (helper lane 1's CONVO-IN-SPIRAL is rewriting it, so not here), backend 248, state_core 591. First slice drafted on mapm: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/mapm_test_block.spi (///> rust, from_array -> get and hash_map_count, expects (Some 2, 2)) and mapm-run.ps1 (inserts it before from_list, gates twice: as-is, then with hash_map_count fixed). Found by reading: hash_map_count is .count() on a std HashMap, which has no count method (E0599 expected; .len() as i32 is the fix), and it has 0 callers in any repo, so it never compiled. The script keeps the test + fix in lib only when the fixed gate passes, else restores. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~6 min). Next: after the run, next slice backend.spi (pure dispatch helpers) test blocks")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_3 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:00 lap 3 grain: second slice drafted on backend.spi (248 lines, no tests). Found by reading: is_rust's backend_switch record has 14 keys and lacks C, Wasm and Bend, while is_zig lists all 17, so is_rust () reached from a C/Wasm/Bend program falls to the no-arm fallback (a build failure there, per the BackendSwitch rule that a missing arm surfaces in the target compiler); it is called from testing.spi:283, runtime, resultm, threading, wasm and rust/testing. Test block lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/backend_test_block.spi (///> rust, zig, c, fsharp, gleam): is_rust/is_zig must agree with run_target's own dispatch (1 for Rust, 2 for Zig, 0 elsewhere). lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/backend-run.ps1 inserts it before indent, gates twice (as-is, expected to fail on c; then with C/Wasm/Bend = false added to is_rust), keeps test + fix only when the fixed gate passes, else restores; then the lib-edit rule applies (apps/spiral build + lib corpus before landing). HEAVY QUEUE row added (~6 min). Next: after mapm and backend, slice 3 = state_core.spi (591 lines, pure model code)")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_4 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:29 lap 4 grain, criterion for the lib slice decided: state_core.spi (591 lines) is not pure model code as planned in lap 3; it opens rust, leptos and wasm and is IndexedDB (rexie) plus leptos signal state (build_database, get_data/set_data futures, use_core_database, use_requests). So the 41 untested lib files split into: 2 with pure logic now drafted (mapm, backend; json_scan is with helper lane 1), 6 declaration-only operator/prototype files that hold nothing to execute, and 33 browser-bound files (leptos.spi, 19 ui/svg views, store, wasm, state_core, bench/parsing runners) whose code only runs inside a wasm page. Decision: the per-module criterion (test blocks with explicit ///> targets) applies to the pure-logic files; declaration-only files are exempt; browser-bound files are covered as a group by one compile gate, the existing apps/wasm build that links them (spiral/apps/wasm/build.ps1, in the CI workflow), not by per-file blocks, because a Cells rust cell would have to build leptos and rexie per test. With the 2 queued slices the lib side of this item is then complete. Next: the apps side (apps/spiral, cells spi) by the same three-way split")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_5 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:59 lap 5 grain, apps side started: apps/cells/spi (Cells' own Spiral domain, 7 modules: cell 435, directives 94, document 707, main 21, notebook 296, targets 65, text 383 lines) has 0 test blocks, as do apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.spi (224) and the monolith (being split by SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR). The spi package is |core- only (no lib testing), and text.spi is Gleam-bound (erlang byte_size/binary.part externals), so its blocks are ///> gleam returning 0/1 like the scaffold's wgsl test. Drafted lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_text_test_blocks.spi (2 blocks: starts_with, drop, slice past the end, trim, parse_i32 42 and the -1 sentinel for 4x, contains; ensure_nl on x, empty and y-newline), expectations taken from the code (parse_i32 has no sign, so -42 is not a case). Runner lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells-text-run.ps1 appends them to apps/cells/spi/text.spi, gates the file and re-runs mix compile (the domain build must skip test blocks), keeps them only if both pass. apps/cells is not a switch consumer root, so this can run any time. HEAVY QUEUE row added (~4 min). Next: same for directives.spi and targets.spi (small, pure)")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_6 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:34 lap 6 grain: second Cells module drafted: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_directives_test_blocks.spi (2 gleam test blocks for apps/cells/spi/directives.spi: prepare of a ///- line with --print-code --timeout 500 and two --package flags gives ok, 1, 500 and the tab-joined packages; --timeout 0 and a #! dib cell are errors per text.is_error) plus cells-directives-run.ps1 (the cells-text-run pattern: append, gate, mix compile, restore unless both pass). Queued after cells-text-run, since both rely on test blocks inside the Cells domain package. targets.spi (1,753 bytes) is next.")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_7 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:51 lap 7 grain: third Cells module drafted, lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_targets_test_blocks.spi + cells-targets-run.ps1 (1 gleam block on apps/cells/spi/targets.spi: target_of, backend and extension agree for rust, c, zig, wgsl, cuda, lean, and an unknown tool maps to empty), queued after cells-text-run. Finding while writing it: Cells' target union lists 13 tools, but the compiler has 7 more backends (kotlin, swift, openqasm, ebpf, calyx, wasm, bend), so a ///> kotlin test block falls to UnknownTarget today. The block pins that (kotlin -> empty), so whoever adds those targets updates the test on purpose. It belongs to the explicit-targets work (CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS).")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_8 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T16:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:31 lap 8 grain: post-switch re-anchor of the two lib runners: mapm-run.ps1 anchors (/// ### from_list, the count() splice) and backend-run.ps1 anchors (inl indent, the single is_rust Swift/Rust tail) all still match today's migrated lib. One real break fixed: the mapm test block piped a ;[...] literal into from_array, which takes array_base, but under core v1 a literal is array t; it now goes through am'.base first (the same rule the migrated lib uses, dropdown.spi:43, state_core.spi:511). backend_test_block and the three cells blocks hold no arrays. Queue rows unchanged")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_9 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T16:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:56 lap 9 grain: staged the cell.spi test block: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/cells_cell_test_blocks.spi checks without_panic_thread_id (drops the (12345) thread id, keeps a non-digit (x1)) and without_cell_run_dir (drops a spiral_cells run directory inside a panic location, keeps src/main.rs), expected strings traced by hand through the helpers (cell.spi after panic_thread_open). Runner cells-cell-run.ps1 is the targets runner retargeted at spi/cell.spi (gate + mix compile, restores unless both pass; parses clean). HEAVY QUEUE row added. These two helpers are also the Spiral form of xsim comparable's two regex rewrites (SIM-IN-SPIRAL slice 2), so that slice can reuse them")
inl note_of_cells_everywhere_10 () = Note ("CELLS-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:31 lap 10 grain: notebook.spi block staged: lab/CELLS-EVERYWHERE/notebook_test_blocks.spi (one ///> gleam test over the pure list and token helpers: add_nb a,b,a -> a\\nb, join_rev x,y -> y\\nx, take_token ab cd 0 -> 2, skip_ws over space-tab-space from 2 -> 5, has_nb; values traced by hand from notebook.spi:15-65); cells-cell-run.ps1 now takes -Module cell|notebook (default cell, so the queued row is unchanged), parse check 0 errors; queue row added for -Module notebook. Next: both rows after main's go, then document.spi")
