inl compiler_pack_speed_ab () = Task ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", "Quiet-box A/B of compile speed, evening pack (tree core A9A71772B249FB8B) vs the previous core (5AA4938BE6339F4A): tonight's review summed 1,940 s of compile time against 398-757 s before, x's exsss gleam cell and the SEFAZ generator cells hit 300 s on the new compiler; decide load vs regression", TaskKind, P0, D3, 80u32, Active, "", "compiler,performance,matrix,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab () = Planning ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", Minutes 150u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 1u32, "2026-10-21T10:21Z", "main", "2026-10-21 06:21 main: plan, fixed before measuring: rebuild the previous core (5AA4938BE6339F4A, the c7 + parse-stack tree) as a Release candidate in a private cache; bench-pair.ps1 -A old -B new on frontier_hello, mega_brzozowski_derivatives and samples/conversation_negative_orphan_result, 5 rounds, order alternating, when no other heavy job runs; plus one Cells gate of SEFAZ generator.spi on each. Verdict rule: B/A median within 1.05 on every row means load explained tonight; above 1.10 on any row means a regression, and the pack's host and core edits get bisected (profile lookup, related-parser-errors, host walks, lazy-expected).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 2u32, "2026-10-21T10:50Z", "main", "2026-10-21 06:50 main: counter-evidence for a regression: tibia reports all 26 tibia test files green on the 03:19 compiler, and x's Cells passed 34 of 35 (the exception timed out at 300 s under load). Both ran beside other heavy work, so the A/B rule stands.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 3u32, "2026-10-21T11:13Z", "main", "2026-10-21 07:13 main: script ready, lab/COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB/ab.ps1: builds the exact pre-pack core (staged by build.ps1 at spiral-bin/core-src/single-flight/5aa4938be6339f4a, hash-checked) as a Release candidate in candidates/speed-ab-old with the current host, then bench-pair old vs installed on frontier_hello, conversation_negative_orphan_result and mega_brzozowski_derivatives (C, 5 rounds, order alternating). It isolates the core edits; if the core is clean and the slowdown persists, the host edits (profile lookup, parse warning) are next. Runs after tibia's PR-17 live block (no compiles during it).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 4u32, "2026-10-21T12:21Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:21 main: first attempt invalid: the old core (5AA4938BE6339F4A) does not build with the current host, which calls pack-added core functions (postability profile lookup), so the Release candidate failed after 272 s. Rerun as job speed-ab-debug with two existing Debug candidates and no build: A = formatter-c9 (old core + formatter patches only, pre-pack host), B = x-profile (the exact pack core + host). The formatter patches do not touch compilation, so this isolates the pack. Same samples, same verdict rule.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 5u32, "2026-10-21T12:41Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:41 main: partial results (speed-ab-debug, Debug A = formatter-c9 old core + pre-pack host, B = x-profile exact pack, 5 alternating rounds, fresh process, C, machine busy): frontier_hello median A 41.9 s, B 36.8 s, B/A 0.879; conversation_negative_orphan_result median A 30.7 s, B 45.0 s, B/A 1.464 (min A 18.6 s, B 45.0 s). Noise is large (same compiler and sample spans 18.6-45 s), but the type-error sample is above the 1.10 regression line and that path runs through the pack's host router change (TypeErrors result waiting for related parser errors), so it gets a targeted look whatever the third sample says. mega_brzozowski_derivatives still running.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 6u32, "2026-10-21T12:42Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:42 main: the minimums tell a cleaner story than the medians: B's fastest round was 44.4 s (frontier_hello) and 45.0 s (orphan_result), A's 25.7 s and 18.6 s. A floor about 20 s higher in every B round of both samples, independent of the sample, points to a fixed per-process cost of B: either a pack edit on the startup path (host: profile lookup, router; core: module parse thread, x-compat entry check) or something about the x-profile candidate itself (its private cache, missing warm package caches). Next, after the mega sample: rerun B against a fresh Debug build of the same pack in another cache, then time one compile with SPIRAL_DIAG timing to see where the 20 s goes.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 7u32, "2026-10-21T12:43Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:43 main: code read for the ~20 s higher floor of B, CPU-light: both candidates' cache dirs hold the same structure (bin/obj/build-cwd/results, no runtime caches), so a cold candidate cache is ruled out; both hosts have the link-safe walk; the profile code is reached only from --x-compat and --check, and xCompatWarnEntry's edition check is a few file probes; the router change only touches error paths, but frontier_hello has no errors and still shows the floor. Nothing found by reading. Deciding control, queued for after tibia's PR-17 block: rebuild the pack as a Debug candidate in candidates/speed-ab-new and bench it against A (B' near A: the x-profile build is the artifact; B' near B: bisect the pack edits).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 8u32, "2026-10-21T13:11Z", "main", "2026-10-21 09:11 main: VERDICT: no regression demonstrated. bench-pair medians (5 rounds, busy machine): frontier_hello 0.879, orphan_result 1.464, mega_brzozowski_derivatives 0.952 but invalid (both sides wrote no output). The one value above 1.10 did not reproduce in direct timestamped runs (lab timeline.ps1, fresh process, the same two Debug candidates): frontier_hello A 6.2 s / B 5.7 s; orphan_result A 11.1 / B 10.5 and A 9.0 / B 12.1. B wins 3 of 6 head-to-heads, inside A's own 9-11 s spread, and the error appears at the same moment of the compile on both. So tonight's slow review (1,940 s), x's exsss timeout and the SEFAZ generator timeouts are explained by load (four sessions compiling at once, up to 23.5 GB taken by one Cells VM). Per the rule's in-between branch, one 9-round rerun on a quiet machine stays open at low priority; nothing to bisect.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 9u32, "2026-10-21T20:49Z", "main", "16:49 grooming, honesty fix: the 09:11 verdict departed from the pre-written rule. orphan_result's bench-pair median B/A was 1.464, above the rule's 1.10 regression line, and I overrode it with 6 direct timeline runs instead of applying the rule mechanically. The verdict therefore stands as PROVISIONAL, not a pass. The deciding measurement is the 9-round rerun, now under a quiet-round filter like HOPACSOTA-P2 note 48: rounds where other load reaches >= 2 cores at a run's start or end are dropped, with at least 5 quiet rounds per sample. Rule, fixed before measuring: B/A <= 1.05 on every valid sample closes the item; any > 1.10 means bisect the pack edits (host profile lookup, router, x-compat entry check, module parse thread); in between, report and keep it open. Box: a quiet one, after tonight's review or in tomorrow's first quiet hour.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 10u32, "2026-10-24T03:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:11 main grooming estimate 150 m: remaining milestones: one quiet-box bench pair run under the pre-written 1.10 rule 120 m heavy, then apply the rule mechanically 30 m.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 11u32, "2026-10-27T19:02Z", "main", "10-27 15:02 main: Re-plan, set before measuring, so the item no longer waits for a quiet box (per the no-quiet-machine rule: make benches robust under load instead). (1) Metric: CPU-seconds per compile from a Job Object over the whole process tree, not wall-clock. Today's E1 bench measured that way at 100% box load, and its rounds agreed within 6%. The Job Object code moved out of bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1 into a shared apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/tree-cpu.ps1 (Measure-TreeCpu) that both benches dot-source; the moved block is byte-identical, the E1 bench parses, and a smoke run measured 8.8 CPU-s with correct output. (2) A can't be the 10-21 core any more: core-src no longer holds its staged source (5AA4938BE6339F4A), the speed-ab-old cache kept only its core DLL without the host, and none of the 18 committed versions of spiral_compiler.fs between 10-15 and 10-24 hashes to it (it was an uncommitted tree). A is therefore commit 33ee6412 (10-20, the last committed core before that pack) built with the current host, and B is the installed compiler. B/A now measures every core change since 10-20, so a RED means bisecting a wider range than the original pack. (3) Runner: lab COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB/ab-cpu.ps1. It extracts A's core from git, builds it in the speed-ab-old cache, then runs 9 rounds per sample with A and B alternating, each compile 'dotnet <dll> --backend C <entry> <out>' (the same call bench-pair makes) in its own Job Object. A sample is valid only if every run exits 0 and writes output. Samples: frontier_hello, conversation_negative_orphan_result, mega_brzozowski_derivatives. (4) Rule, unchanged from n4 but on CPU medians: every valid B/A CPU median <= 1.05 closes the item; any > 1.10 means bisect; in between, report and keep open; no valid sample is VOID. It runs as a local heavy job after kit's SLICES2 'window closed', since it compiles.")
inl autonomous_stop_gate () = Task ("AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE", "Claude Stop hook for autonomous runs (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1) must never block the window: no sleep or poll inside the hook, at most one block per stop, a global disarm file; user 10-21 07:00 via x: 'traps us without waiting for any shells ... idling waiting for shells is never good ... bugged af'", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "claude,hooks,autonomy,main", "current", "current")
inl eoie_autonomous_gate () = Task ("EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE", "The autonomous stop gate becomes an eoie verb (eoie autonomous arm|disarm|gate): returns in about a second, never sleeps or waits, honors stop_hook_active, reports the session's job states in one call, and is versioned and tested like other verbs (user via x and tibia 2026-10-21 07:00: the hook's in-hook sleep loop froze all three windows)", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Paused, "", "eoie,one-call,hooks", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_autonomous_gate_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-21T11:01Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:01 eoie team: state at 07:00. The hook script was rewritten at 06:59:55 (not by eoie): 13 lines, no sleep loop, exits 0 when stop_hook_active, a global disarm file ~/.claude/autonomous/disarmed, immediate block with a fixed reason. Still open: (3) settings.json hooks.Stop timeout is 600 s; a few seconds would cap any regression. That is the user's config, so I left it for the user rather than changing it on a peer relay. (4/5) arm/disarm as an eoie verb with a test notebook, so the gate is dogfooded. eoie's own session file was removed at the deadline.")
inl note_of_eoie_autonomous_gate_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-21T11:01Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:01 eoie team: duplicate of AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE (main lane owns the hook and says nothing is needed from eoie). Paused here; an eoie arm/disarm verb would only come back if main asks for it.")
inl note_of_eoie_autonomous_gate_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T06:05Z", "fork-git", "fork-git team, 2026-10-25 02:1x: the light list (autonomous-gate.ps1:160-166) reads only C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\docs\\backlog with --team <team>, so a team whose items live in a repo-local eoie root (fork-spiral-patch/docs/backlog holds FORK-SPIRAL and FORK-SPIRAL-SPI, moved there at the user request) always gets an empty list and a generic take-the-next-one block. Second trap: team names are matched as backlog tags, so registering 84b53718=fork handed this session UPSTREAM-FORK-START (tag fork, a main item about the language fork) until the team was renamed fork-git. For the eoie verb: read the session team light list from every registered root (agile roots) or a root named next to the team in standing.txt, and reject a team name that already tags another team items.")
inl cells_postability_profile () = Task ("CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE", "Cells (main lane): Spiral.Cells.XCompat reads the nearest postability/*.json like the compiler does since the 10-21 evening pack, instead of its own constants and priv/tlds.txt (the x profile at x/postability/x.json + tlds.txt is byte-equal to them, so the switch changes no finding). Split out of X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE, whose x and compiler halves are done", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,x-compat,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_postability_profile () = Planning ("CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE", Minutes 60u32, LoadUnset, Unassigned)
inl machine_watch () = Task ("MACHINE-WATCH", "Hourly machine watch until 21:00 (user 2026-10-21 07:0x: lanes stuck waiting while dangling processes take 100% CPU; every hour make sure all lanes work and no rogue process steals the CPU): lab/MACHINE-WATCH/watch.ps1 reports per-process CPU over 10 s with owner (claude session or eoie job by ancestry, else orphan) and repo, rogue candidates, running jobs whose output stopped growing for 60+ min, and held windows whose job ended", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "machine,cpu,lanes,monitoring", "current", "current")
inl eoie_experiment_import () = Task ("EOIE-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT", "eoie experiment import <ledger> <ID> --registered-at <iso> --evidence <commit|file@line> plus experiment record-verdict --verdict --at: historical pre-registrations enter the ledger with imported=yes, the claimed time and the evidence pointer, never looking like a live registration (tibia 2026-10-21 08:00: 22 BENCHMARK.md pre-registrations PR-1..PR-17, PR-3a, PC-1..PC-6 for TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,experiments,tibia", "current", "current")
inl eoie_table_where_compare () = Task ("EOIE-TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE", "table query --where accepts col>value, col>=value, col<value, col<=value and col!=value beside col=value, numeric when both sides parse as numbers (tibia 2026-10-21 08:00: arena-summary mean/sd of damage over runs where damage > 0, so crashed runs do not count as zero)", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,table,tibia", "current", "current")
inl fiscal_xsd_patterns_large () = Task ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", "SEFAZ DFA follow-up: (1) one direct-coded state function per distinct pattern per module (scratchpad generator.shared.spi, staged; today each type has its own copy and modules grew 30-110%); (2) the 7 patterns over 128 states (NF-e QR-code URLs with .* prefixes, up to 494 states) get a table-driven or split DFA instead of the derivative matcher; gate: family gate 17/17 + 13 examples 0 messages + module sizes and the Cells VM memory peak per module", Story, P1, D4, 50u32, Active, "", "sefaz,xsd,regex,dfa,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large () = Planning ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", Minutes 390u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 1u32, "2026-10-21T13:16Z", "main", "2026-10-21 09:16 main: two variants staged in the scratchpad; the gate is sefaz-shared-chain.ps1 -Variant <file> (restores the accepted DFA generator 240C6A248B819314 on red). Variant 1, generator.shared.spi: one state function per distinct pattern, 128-state cap. Variant 2, generator.shared512.spi (15CCDE51F60A7723): the same with a 512-state cap, so all 196 patterns are direct-coded, including the 7 QR-code ones (max 494). Accept rule, fixed before measuring: family gate 17/17 and 13 examples at 0 .NET messages; the generated total under run 7's 11,081 KB for variant 1; for variant 2, at most variant 1's total + 15%, and no Cells VM above 16 GB private (MACHINE-WATCH hog line). Order: variant 1, then variant 2, after tibia's PR-17 and PR-18 blocks (about 13:30).")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 2u32, "2026-10-21T17:42Z", "main", "13:42 variant 1 (generator.shared.spi, 3837A07766BC5B79) accepted by the pre-written rule: family gates 17/17 green, 13 examples at 0 .NET validation messages, generated total 10,098 KB (< 11,081 KB, -9%). Job main-sefaz-shared-v1 took 73 min in total. The accepted generator and generated modules are saved in scratchpad sefaz-v1-accepted; a red variant 2 now restores those (the chain's -Fallback). Variant 2 (generator.shared512.spi) runs next, after the sm'.spi lib gate in the same job (they share MIX_BUILD_PATH), with a sampler for the Cells VM private-memory peak.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 3u32, "2026-10-21T18:14Z", "main", "14:14 variant 2 (generator.shared512.spi, 512-state cap) rejected by the pre-written memory rule: 10 min into its family gates, its Cells VM (erl 120700, job main-libgate-sefaz-v2) reached 21,875 MB private (> 16 GB) with 443 MB free, and Claude Code reaped two of my idle watchers. I stopped the job (eoie job stop), the VM was gone after 5 s, and free memory recovered to 8.2 GB. The lab was restored from sefaz-v1-accepted (generator 3837A07766BC5B79, 10,098 KB, 0 files differing). Variant 1 stays. The 7 patterns over 128 states must not be direct-coded at 494 states: next is a table-driven DFA for those 7 (part 2 of this item). Lessons: the chain's peak sampler matched erl by session id and missed this VM (it saw 71 MB), so the cap must follow the job tree, and the 16 GB rule should be enforced inside the chain (stop at 16 GB) instead of read afterwards.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 4u32, "2026-10-21T18:18Z", "main", "14:18 variant 3 staged (part 2: the 7 patterns over 128 states): scratchpad xsd_regex.table.spi adds dfa_table (records = per state an accept char, a 6-hex move count and moves as 6-hex low/high/target; offsets = 6 hex per state) with table_matches and the encoder dfa_table_of, plus tests table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe (all 196 patterns) and table_keeps_xsd_semantics. generator.table.spi = variant 1 + for DFAs over 128 states (within 4096) an inl xsd_table_<fnv>_<len> constant checked with xsd_regex.table_matches, with the derivative matcher only when no DFA exists. Job sefaz-table-job.ps1 runs the chain under lab/MACHINE-WATCH/memory-cap.ps1, which sums private memory over the chain's descendant tree and stops that tree above 16,000 MB (smoke-tested: capped a 335 MB tree at 200 MB). Accept rule, fixed before measuring: 17/17 green incl. both new tests, 13 examples at 0 messages, generated total <= 10,098 KB x 1.15 = 11,613 KB, never capped, and the generated modules have no xsd_regex.pattern_matches left (all 196 patterns DFA-backed). Red or capped: back to sefaz-v1-accepted (now also holds xsd_regex.spi 0A7681A0543F2709). Order: after the night-pack install + review and the control-workers rerun (WSJF: x is blocked on the install).")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 5u32, "2026-10-22T00:42Z", "main", "20:42 pre-gate code review of variant 3 (scratchpad xsd_regex.table.spi lines 288-452, read only): correctness holds by construction. table_target's early exit (c below a move's low gives -1) needs each state's moves ascending and disjoint: interval_starts starts from [0] and insert_point keeps the starts sorted and unique, state_moves walks them in order with high = next start - 1, add_move only merges adjacent ranges with the same target, and reverse_moves restores ascending order; the list matcher move_target uses the same exit, so table and list agree by design (and the 196-pattern .NET test checks it). Start state = index 0 = the compiled pattern (build_states 0 [r] 1). 6-hex fields cover code points to 0x10FFFF and record offsets to 16.7M chars; the encoder writes lowercase and table_hex_value reads only lowercase, consistent. Performance risks for the gated run, not bugs: state_index is a linear rx_compare scan and with_state appends at the end, so building n states is O(n^2) structural compares (16M at the 4096 cap); table_of_rows and table_moves_text build records with ++# appends, quadratic in the record length (fine at about 500 states, about 1.5M chars near the cap). If the gate is slow, a map from rx to index and a list-then-concat encoder are the fixes.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 6u32, "2026-10-22T06:12Z", "main", "02:12 main: variant 3 (DFA tables for the 7 patterns over 128 states) moved out of my session scratchpad, which would have lost it at session end, into the sefaz repo. C:\\home\\git\\sefaz\\candidates\\dfa-table\\{generator.spi, xsd_regex.spi} hold scratchpad generator.table.spi and xsd_regex.table.spi with the lab paths rewritten. The rewrite mapping (lab corpus/ to deps/corpus/, generated/ and samples/ to the repo folders, everything else to src/) reproduces all 15 files of sefaz/src from the scratchpad's accepted-v1 snapshot byte for byte, so sefaz/src IS the accepted v1 and the snapshot is redundant. The chain became one gate, sefaz/scripts/candidate-gate.ps1 -Candidate <dir>. It snapshots src/*.spi to TEMP, copies the candidate modules in, and runs family-gates.ps1 under apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/memory-cap.ps1 (16,000 MB). It passes only with >= 17 green modules, 0 validation messages and no cap, and restores the snapshot otherwise. The accept rule of note 14:18 is unchanged; it adds 'generated total <= 11,613 KB' and 'derivative fallbacks left 0', which the gate prints. Run: candidate-gate.ps1 -Candidate candidates\\dfa-table as a heavy job when the machine has room (not tonight: free memory is about 3 GB with 2 heavy jobs; tibia live block 05:30-06:30). Superseded scratchpad variants (orig, dfa, next, shared, shared512) are recorded in notes 10-20 and 10-21.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 7u32, "2026-10-22T15:49Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:49 main: reconciled with today's FISCAL-GENERIC table work, one record per fact. Two table encodings existed: this item's variant 3 (fiscal/candidates/dfa-table: records + offsets strings, 6-hex fields, dfa_table record type) and today's self-indexing format (lib-xsd copy xsd_regex: dfa_table_text, one string whose move targets are the character offsets of their state lines, plus table_matches, also used for the value-set catalogs via dfa_of_values). Rule, fixed before choosing: keep the encoding with fewer moving parts that serves both uses. That is today's (one string, no separate offset table, no record type), and generator.spi already emits it for patterns between 128 and 4096 states (part 2 of this item). Variant 3's strong part, its test, is ported: fiscal corpus_checks gains table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe (all 196 corpus patterns through dfa_of_pattern -> dfa_table_text -> table_matches against .NET's expected rows). Part 1 (one state function per distinct pattern per module) is already in fiscal/src, since unique_emitted dedupes the hash-named functions. The accept rule of 10-21 14:18 carries over to the fiscal regen after the lib landing: 17/17 green, 0 validation messages, generated total <= 11,613 KB, no xsd_regex.pattern_matches left. fiscal/candidates/dfa-table is retired once the new format lands and passes that rule; it stays as reference until then.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T15:30Z", "fiscal", "11:29 fiscal: the 10-22 20:42 performance risk is real and worse than O(n^2). parse_quant unrolls {n,m} into repeat_n + optional_chain, a chain m nodes long, so every DFA state is an O(m) regex and every rx_compare in the linear state_index scan walks O(m) of shared suffix: building R{1,m} is O(m^3). Measured with a faithful Python mirror of build_states (session scratchpad xsd-regex-repeat/mirror.py): compare steps grow 8x per doubling of m unrolled, 4x with a counted node; for FatturaPA's [\\p{IsBasicLatin}\\p{IsLatin-1Supplement}]{1,1000} that projects to 1.2e10 steps unrolled vs 9e6 counted. Second defect found on the way: \\p{...} block escapes are not parsed (escape_ranges 'p' is a literal p), so that class became the ~18 literal characters of 'p{IsBasicLatin}p{IsLatin-1Supplement}' and FatturaPA's validators reject ordinary text such as 'Mario Rossi'; the BR census (FISCAL-APP n5) had 0 \\p, and patterns.txt holds only BR patterns, so no oracle saw it (FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES). Fix drafted as lib slice xsd-regex-repeat/apply.py (base AB61AE5FA83FDCA5): RxRepeat (atom, low, high) with derivative d(R)R{low-1,high-1} through a counted smart constructor (high <= 0 -> eps, {1,1} -> atom; no nullable normalization, so example_with output is unchanged); {n} and {n,m} go through it, {n,} = R{n,n}R*; block escapes IsBasicLatin, IsLatin-1Supplement, IsLatinExtended-A/B, \\P negated, unknown names fail to compile; tests block_escapes_read_code_point_ranges and bounded_repeats_build_one_state_per_count ({1,1000} -> 1001 states). Differential before compiling (differential.py): 400 random patterns incl. nullable atoms under bounded counts x 255 probes vs Python re, 0 disagreements. Gate job-1792941953660-fiscal-lib-gate-xsd-2. Accept rule, fixed now: xsd_regex tests green AND fiscal corpus_checks' 5 .NET pattern tests (196 BR patterns, derivative + DFA + table) at 0 disagreements against the patched lib -> land with reader guard AB61AE5FA83FDCA5.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "fiscal", "13:59 fiscal: XSD-SLICES LANDED in the tree lib by job-1792950895715 (kit land.ps1 + the staged apply): xsd_regex 528907A279636CC1, xsd_emit 5C364F8DA3287371, xsd_resolve 170A91102936C86C, xsd 5CF595CEF83FE12C, byte-identical to the GREEN candidate (the kit's post-land format was a no-op). The lib now has: counted RxRepeat + XSD block escapes (xsd_regex); match-based enumerations, URI case names and list sugar in emitted code (xsd_emit); namespace-qualified type names with collision tags (xsd_resolve); parenthesized tuple-slot list patterns (xsd); tests interleaved. The same job then applied fiscal-post-land (271-pattern oracle derived from deps, KSeF known_defect None, gitignore and README) and is waiting for a test-suite window for the full fiscal build, which checks KSeF's example: it must now pass with no pin.")
inl note_of_fiscal_xsd_patterns_large_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", 10u32, "2026-10-27T07:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:30 fiscal: estimate set to 390m remaining, from its two milestones: the counted RxRepeat and block escapes landed (XSD-SLICES, note of 10-25 14:00), so what remains is (1) one direct-coded state function per distinct pattern per module: the staged generator.shared.spi predates today's landings and must be re-derived on the live tree, then gated (family gates + examples at 0 + module sizes) 150m; (2) the 7 patterns over 128 states (NF-e QR-code URLs, up to 494 states) get a table-driven DFA: design, implementation, tests against the 271-pattern oracle 240m.")
inl eoie_notebook_workers () = Task ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", "The release notebook phase runs N notebooks at once (EOIE_NOTEBOOK_WORKERS, default 2) instead of one by one (63 notebooks, about 35 of a release's 60-70 minutes): spawn through the jobs host child API (kill-on-close container, tree stop on timeout), print each notebook's logs when it ends, list all failures", Story, P1, D4, 80u32, Paused, "", "eoie,dev,release", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_notebook_workers () = Planning ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_1 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 1u32, "2026-10-21T13:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:20 eoie team, design notes before drafting: (1) notebook.run names its output target/spiral-tests/<file>.spi.ipynb, and file names repeat across crates (two windows_cli.spi, several model.spi), so parallel runs need <crate>_<file> output names; (2) concurrent Mix runs in one project clash on _build (memory heavy-gate-lessons), so each worker gets its own MIX_BUILD_PATH under target/spiral-tests/mix-<worker>, paying one Cells build per worker per release; (3) environment for the children (EOIE_BIN_UNDER_TEST, SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM/MEMO/COMPILER) goes on each child command, not set_var; (4) builds on r28's continue-on-failure runner. Expected: notebook phase 35 -> ~18 min with 2 workers at a ~2-3 GB memory peak.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_2 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 2u32, "2026-10-21T13:30Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:30 eoie team: measured before choosing workers: the 61-notebook live run spent 2135 s with a floor of about 20 s per notebook (median 30 s), so fixed per-notebook cost is about 1200 s (56%): pwsh start, mix/BEAM boot and a fresh .NET compiler per compile (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process). Cells already takes many --path in one call (sequential, all failures collected) and can compile through a daemon on a private SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT that --gate retires. Benchmark lab/EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS/batch-bench.ps1 on 8 notebooks (6 model, 2 CLI, unique basenames): A today's one call per notebook, B one batch call with process compiles, C one batch call with a private warm daemon and --gate; order C, B, A, A again for drift. Rule set now: pick the fastest config whose per-notebook pass/fail equals A's; adopt it only if it saves at least 25% against the mean of the two A runs; within 10% of each other the simpler one wins (B over C; no daemon). Parallel workers stay deferred until this lands, since they add CPU load where notes_cli already hit its timeout.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_3 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 3u32, "2026-10-21T14:17Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:17 eoie team: rule addition, written before any result: the bench runs while r26g's release and the r30 previews load the box, so a run is valid only if its two baseline runs (A1, A2) differ by at most 20%. An invalid run decides nothing and the bench reruns once in a quiet window (no heavy job held, checked with eoie window list), before r31 is composed.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_4 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T15:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:48 eoie team: the first bench run (11:36-11:43) is invalid by construction, so its rule was not applied. One of the 8 listed notebooks (eoie_process/table_model.spi) no longer exists. A1/A2 skipped it (7 ran, A1 147.8 s, A2 145.6 s), but in batch mode Cells aborts the whole batch on the first unreadable path (File.Error in notebook.ex:60), so B (75.4 s) and C (117.0 s) ran only 5 notebooks. Fixed the list (eoie_table_logs/logs_model.spi), and the script now refuses missing paths and basename collisions before measuring. Rerun as eoie-nb-bench2 with the same rule. Side findings: (1) one bad --path kills a Cells batch, which a batch runner must survive (per-path error, keep going); (2) C's private daemon still listened right after --gate and was gone a few minutes later. Old run kept in nb-bench-v1-invalid.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_5 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T16:14Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:14 eoie team: rerun valid and the rule applied as written. A1 214.3 s and A2 218.9 s (2% apart, within 20%). Every config ran all 8 notebooks with 17 passed, 0 failed and no errors, so pass/fail matches A. C (batch, private daemon, --gate) 114.1 s, B (batch, process compiles) 134.0 s. C is fastest and saves 47% against the A mean of 216.6 s, which is at least 25%. B is 17% slower than C, outside the 10% tie band, so C is adopted. The daemon had retired by the end of the run. Drafted for r31 (scratch apply-notebook-batch.ps1): eoie_dev_spiral_tests selects the registered notebooks in order and runs them as one Cells batch (--output-dir target/spiral-tests, --no-html, --gate, private compiler port 20000 + pid % 10000, timeout = per-notebook timeout x count). It streams output as before and attributes failures from each 'spiral-tests file' report: failed > 0 or no report counts as failed, with the batch exit code, so the 'spiral notebooks failed (n): ...' summary stays. Only present files go in, since Cells aborts a batch on an unreadable path. Cargo check running.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_6 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 6u32, "2026-10-22T07:53Z", "eoie", "03:53 groom: the row said Planned 0% but the work is in the live tree: r31 apply-notebook-batch (eoie_dev_spiral_tests), composed into release ABC (regen published 02:36, candidate built). It installs with the R13 tail (now or next session under R14); verify the verb on the installed binary, then close.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_7 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T07:32Z", "eoie", "03:32 eoie: measurement from F8 (batch mode, one Cells call with 81 --path, shared daemon). Cargo tests 00:21-00:53 (32 min); notebook batch 00:53-02:48 (115 min, 1.42 min per notebook) with 2-4 GB free, one daemon retire and cold restart (01:45, free under its 2048 floor), main's review and four Claude sessions on the box. The 2026-10-21 quiet-box figure was 61 notebooks in 2135 s (0.58 min each), so tonight ran about 2.4x slower per notebook. CLI notebooks dominate (notes_cli 7 min, planning_cli 4.5, windows_cli 5), and 26 of the last 40 were CLI. F6's run on the same tree under a 4096 floor managed ~35 notebooks in 99 min (2.8 min each): the floor alone roughly doubled the time. Budget rule for release job limits: cargo 35 min + notebooks x 1.5 min + 30 min tail under load, i.e. 3.5 h for 81 notebooks, inside the 6 h limits now used.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_8 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 80%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_notebook_workers_9 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
