inl c_output_blank_lines () = Task ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", "C backend emits no indentation-only lines: 45,538 of 138,961 lines (32.8%) across 507 samples/*/main.c are whitespace-only, every other backend has 0", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,c,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_output_blank_lines () = Planning ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", Minutes 30u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_output_blank_lines_1 () = Note ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:15Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:15 main: cause found by code read. In CodegenC (core 174375), line' x s = line x (String.concat \" \" s) emits indentation plus an empty string whenever the statement group is empty: the refcount increments and decrements per bind (refc_varc incr/decr at 174633, two calls per statement, hence the paired blank lines), and empty decl_vars. The module already defines lineC, which skips \"\", but line' never used it. Fix staged: %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\c-blank-lines.ps1 routes line' through lineC (1 anchor, matches once on the next3 core). CodegenCpp has its own line' (175586), left alone (an upstream-mapped section, 16 rows). Pack PACK-NEXT-4, after next3's accept; rule fixed now: every C residual change is whitespace-only (git diff --ignore-blank-lines --ignore-space-at-eol is empty for each changed main.c), no other backend changes, DISAGREE 0, and native results equal the baseline row for row.")
inl note_of_c_output_blank_lines_2 () = Note ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T23:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:28 main: status fixed. The change is implemented and staged in PACK-NEXT-4, so it is Active, not Planned. The next4 composite is decided by review-3, requeued in %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\requeue-after-block.ps1 chain B after recompose-stack; this item's review rule (note 1) is applied there.")
inl note_of_c_output_blank_lines_3 () = Note ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", 3u32, "2026-10-28T01:28Z", "main", "10-27 21:28 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. c-blank-lines.ps1's lineC anchor is in the tree core (1 hit).")
inl zig_prelude_on_demand () = Task ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", "Zig output emits only the prelude helpers it uses: today every main.zig carries the whole fixed prelude (~30% of the 121,843 Zig lines across 467 samples)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,zig,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_prelude_on_demand () = Planning ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_1 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:28Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:28 main: measured on the samples tree: about 36,957 of 121,843 Zig lines come before the first program item. The prelude (spiralWrite with the kernel32 externs, the panic handler, print/flush buffers, spiralFloatText, spiralPrintAny, spiralFail, spiralConv, spiralConcat, spiralStringSlice, spiralIndex, spiralNewArray, spiralCreate) is one multi-item text, which UnusedGlobals.prune keeps by design (it only drops single-item globals). Zig analyzes only referenced functions, so this is output size and readability, not compile time. Design: emit each helper as its own single-item global (fn name(...) text), so the existing prune drops unreferenced ones, transitively (spiralFail needs spiralFlush and spiralWrite); keep the panic override and the arena/gpa vars whenever any allocating helper survives. The same approach as Gleam's and Wasm's on-demand preludes. Rule: Zig residuals change only by dropped prelude lines (each changed main.zig minus its prelude equals the old one minus its prelude), every Zig row's native result is unchanged, and DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_2 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:39Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:39 main: patch staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\zig-prelude-on-demand.ps1, 3 anchors, each once on the next3 core). zigUsedPrelude splits zigPrelude into top-level chunks and drops every fn chunk whose name no longer occurs in the program body or in another kept chunk, iterating to a fixpoint. Vars, externs and the panic override always stay (they keep spiralPanic, spiralFlush and spiralWrite alive). codegenZig now builds the body first and returns zigUsedPrelude body + body. A Python mirror over the 467 samples/*/main.zig (the prelude is verbatim at the start of all 467): 121,843 -> 92,192 lines (24.3% fewer). Dropped helpers by file count: spiralPrint, spiralStringSlice, spiralPrintAny and spiralFloatText 459 each, spiralConv and spiralConcat 453, spiralFail 446, spiralNewArray 367, spiralIndex 349, spiralCreate 248. Rule as filed (only dropped prelude lines differ, native results unchanged, DISAGREE 0); the mirror script is scratch zig_prelude_mirror.py.")
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_3 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: pack-next4 patch, reviewed by review-3.")
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_4 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. zigUsedPrelude has 3 hits in the tree core.")
