inl eoie_tree_read_record () = Task ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", "One record for tree leases: release and regeneration scripts hold a tree-read window (eoie window run/request) instead of writing tree-leases/*.lease files; the PreToolUse hook and eoie's write verbs read the window store; the .lease directory goes away", TaskKind, P1, D3, 50u32, Active, "EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "eoie,lease,windows", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_tree_read_record () = Planning ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "eoie-lane", "15:48 rule written before any probe: the cutover (scripts plus hook, no compile) is applied after F installs only if all of these hold on the installed binary, using a scratch folder as the leased root, so no one else's paths are touched: (1) 'window request tree-read 5 --label <scratch>' ok; (2) 'window list' shows it as tree-read with the label; (3) the drafted hook blocks an Edit inside <scratch> and allows one outside; (4) after 'window release' it allows the inside edit; (5) 'window run tree-read 30 --label <scratch> -- <long child>' with only that window-run pid (mine) killed: the next 'window list' prunes it (ProcessHolder orphan), so the hook allows; (6) hook p50 over 10 calls is at most 1.5x the current lease-file hook's p50 on the same inputs. If any fails, the .lease files stay and the failing case is noted here. Known gap found in reading: eoie_window_claim's claim_of reads stored_rows rather than live_rows, so 'window claim' still refuses for an orphaned window until it expires; the hook therefore reads 'window list' (which prunes), and the claim fix (claim over live_rows with an empty kept list) is a slice for the next compile release.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_2 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "eoie-lane", "15:55 drafted in scratch tree-read/ (synced into the lab night folder after F's lease ends): (a) apply-tree-read-scripts.ps1 (no compile): workspace.ps1 gains Start-EoieTreeRead, which starts 'eoie window run tree-read 600 --label <root> -- pwsh -Command Wait-Process -Id <caller pid>', so the window ends with its caller, and if the holder itself is killed, the window-run pid dies and live_rows prunes it as an orphan ProcessHolder. test-regeneration -Publish calls it instead of writing regeneration-<pid>.lease. Dry run on copies: workspace.ps1 +14 lines, test-regeneration.ps1 5 lines -> 1, both parse. (b) the hook draft eoie-tree-lease.ps1: Edit/Write runs 'window claim <full path>'; on refusal it runs 'window list' (prunes) and claims again. Commands with a write verb parse 'window list' tree-read rows (label = root). A binary without claim (exit 2) allows. (c) apply-claim-live-rows.ps1 (compile): claim over live_rows. Anchor present in both the live tree and the GE2+H tree, so it is chained into apply-rH, and eoie_window_claim is added to H's regen filter. Left for the cutover: release-build-job-* call Start-EoieTreeRead instead of writing release-<pid>.lease; the rule's 6 probes after F installs. A trade-off to measure (case 6): the window label must be the bare root for claim to match, so a refusal names the window id, not the holder text ('eoie release F').")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_3 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:34Z", "eoie-lane", "16:34 the claim slice now carries its test: windows_cli gains a_tree_read_window_whose_holder_died_refuses_nothing (a '-run' tree-read row with a dead pid in the fixture store; the claim prints 'edit allowed' and the row is pruned). The row is built from Spiral strings, with no new host macro. It applies on both the live tree and the GE2+H tree. Release H's regen filter adds eoie_machine_windows, and its candidate notebooks add windows_cli.spi (4 instead of 3). job-script-check is ok.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_4 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 4u32, "2026-10-22T20:40Z", "eoie-lane", "16:40 placement for the write-verb half (eoie's own writes honoring the lease), read from the code: 'do edit' writes only through host.spi committed (one transactional host blob over the pending writes), and 'do write' through appended_to, copied_bytes and moved. A per-path claim check belongs in those write points, but do_edit.rs is at 976 lines (981 after GE2), and 'touching a legacy function migrates it' turns committed into a granular rewrite. A root-level check in eoie_do's dispatcher would be wrong the other way: a do root of C:\\work would refuse edits to a sibling repo while one tree under it is leased. Decision: the write-verb half waits for do_edit's boundary migration (EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY), which moves committed into the OS boundary package. There the claim export (a path-taking variant of eoie_window_claim_run) is one call per staged path. The hook half (scripts plus hook) does not depend on it.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_5 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 5u32, "2026-10-22T21:29Z", "eoie-lane", "17:29 the six probes of note 1 are scripted (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1; it parses), one call after F installs. EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME points at a scratch store, so the machine's shared window store is untouched, and the leased root is a scratch folder with a space in its name. Case 5 kills only the window-run process the probe itself started. Case 6 compares 10-call medians of the drafted hook and the installed lease-file hook on the same outside-path Edit payload.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_6 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 6u32, "2026-10-22T21:43Z", "eoie-lane", "17:43 baseline for probe 6, measured while F's real lease is live: the installed lease-file hook on an outside-path Edit has p50 2,003 ms over 10 calls (min 1,780, max 2,617). Almost all of it is pwsh startup, so the bar for the drafted hook is p50 <= 3,005 ms. An inside-tree Edit (eoie/README.md) is refused, exit 2. 'window list' costs ~760 ms, but the drafted hook calls it only on a claim refusal or for commands that contain a write verb.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_7 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 7u32, "2026-10-23T20:54Z", "eoie", "16:54 eoie: the six probes (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1) ran on the installed eoie, 6/6 PASS: request tree-read; list shows it with the leased path; the drafted hook blocks an inside Edit (exit 2) and allows outside (0); a release frees the inside (0); a killed window-run holder is pruned (inside 2 while held, 0 after the kill); hook p50 5,504 ms vs the current lease-file hook's 5,598 ms on a loaded box (bar 1.5x). Order for the swap, set now so protection never lapses: (1) release and regen scripts hold a tree-read window in addition to writing their .lease (both records for one release); (2) once one release ran with both, swap ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1 to the window-reading draft, since every session on the box shares that hook; (3) the next release drops the .lease write and the tree-leases folder. Step 1 goes into the release scripts after GE2's train settles, not into a running release.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_8 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:28Z", "eoie", "18:28 eoie: step 1 drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-tree-read-step1.ps1 (dry run on copies of launch-O and launch-Q: rewritten, then idempotent). Each launch's release job start becomes 'job start --heavy <label> <ms> eoie window run tree-read <ms/60000+1> --label C:\\home\\git\\eoie -- pwsh -File release-build-job-X.ps1'. The window lives exactly as long as the release (window run's child; probe 5 showed a dead holder is pruned), and the exit code passes through. Not applied to tonight's train, by the 18:5x rule (train scripts stay frozen while it runs). Apply after P's acceptance, then step 2 (hook swap) after one release ran with both records.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_9 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 9u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-7 (source freeze on paths while a reader such as a Cells run or a release regen is in flight). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_10 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_11 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 11u32, "2026-10-25T23:22Z", "eoie", "19:22 kit: writer half, requested by main (lib lands starve: CLI-ARGS-STYLE gated GREEN at 15:18, then its land waited 180 min for two clear 20 s samples with no Cells run and gave up; 4-5 lanes gate lib through Cells all day; detail on LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND n12-n13). Design, ships after KIT-RENAME (feature freeze):\n(1) New window class tree-write PATH. A path conflicts with a held or waiting tree-read on that path or any path under or above it.\n(2) Writer preference: while a tree-write waits, new tree-read claims on a conflicting path queue behind it. This is the existing 'an older exclusive waiter keeps newcomers out' rule applied to the tree classes. Existing tree-read leases drain; then the write is granted, the land commits its staged files (seconds) and releases; then the queued reads are granted in order.\n(3) Readers declare themselves: lanes' Cells gate runners claim tree-read on lib for the length of their run (window run tree-read lib -- ...). land.ps1 requests tree-write instead of polling erl processes.\n(4) Bounds so nothing starves: a tree-write waits at most its --wait, and it states the reads it is waiting on by holder and age. A tree-read holder past its declared minutes shows as overdue in window list, so a stuck reader is visible rather than silently blocking a land.\n(5) Tests, pre-registered: a writer waiting behind two reads is granted when both release, and a third read claimed meanwhile waits behind the writer; a writer never waits on a read claimed after it; non-conflicting paths (lib vs eoie src) never block each other.\nPriority: the first post-rename feature slot, ahead of the splits, since every lane's lib lands depend on it.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_12 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 12u32, "2026-10-26T05:54Z", "eoie", "01:54 eoie: input for the tree-write window class, from main (10-26 01:3x). Two of main's lib lands raced at 22:11:30: both staged from the same file and committed inside one 2-second reader gap; each drift check passed because the other had not written yet, so the second write erased the first (file_system.spi lost fs-link-length). Main's interim fix: the lib gate's land.ps1 takes a machine-wide named mutex, Global\\spiral-lib-land, across drift check plus commit; a waiter rechecks and restages; a race test shows both lands survive. Design consequence: tree-write serializes writers by construction (writers queue on the window), which subsumes this mutex. Until it lands, any kit lib-landing tool takes the same mutex name. The window's write must also span check-then-commit as one critical section, not just the commit, since the race is between two checks.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_13 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 13u32, "2026-10-26T07:47Z", "eoie", "03:47 eoie: lease leak found during the rename. The renamed compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 takes its tree lease in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases (its state path renamed with everything else) and does not remove it when the regen ends: regeneration-19520.lease (FASTRENAME, 22:27) and regeneration-73716.lease (RESUME, 00:59) were both still there at 03:4x, and neither holder pid is running. Readers that judge leases by pid liveness are unaffected, but anything that treats a lease file as held is not: kit-state-move refused the move on them until it learned to check the holder pid (tests 12/12, a live lease still refuses). Two follow-ups: (1) the regen must remove its lease in a finally block, success or failure; (2) until the state move, the old eoie lease hook cannot see leases written under kit, so lease readers should look in both folders during the transition.")
inl python_lazy_array_import () = Task ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", "Python output imports cupy/numpy only when the program uses arrays: 357 of 451 samples/*/main.py never touch cp. yet pay the import at startup", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,python,codegen,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_python_lazy_array_import () = Planning ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", Minutes 45u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:50Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:50 main: measured. Every main.py has the fixed head try: import cupy as cp (CUDA probe) except: import numpy as cp; plus dataclass/typing imports. Only 94 of 451 programs use cp. in their body (39 use @dataclass, 255 the typing names). On this box (loaded, cupy installed) python -c pass takes 1,085 ms and import numpy 1,785 ms, so about 700 ms of each row's run is an unused array import, and with cupy present the CUDA runtime probe adds more. Fix: CodegenPython emits the cp block only when the body uses cp., and the dataclass/typing line only for what is used, the same on-demand rule as the other backends' helpers. Rule: Python residuals change only in those head lines, native results unchanged, DISAGREE 0; median Python native_ms before/after published.")
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:00Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:00 main: patch staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\python-lazy-array-import.ps1, 2 anchors once on the next3 core). CodegenPython no longer adds cupy_prelude before the body; after main_defs is written, it inserts the block at globals index 0 (its old position) only when globals, types, functions or main_defs match \\bcp\\. or \\bcuda\\b. Checked first: main_auto.py has no import and no Python cp/cuda use (its 3 cuda hits are inside the kernel string), no main.py body references cuda, and the matrix shim run_main.py does not use cp. The pack review checks that each changed main.py equals the old one minus exactly that block, with 94 of 451 programs expected to keep it.")
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_3 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:02 main: measured the payoff on the matrix path (python run_main.py, SPIRAL_CUDA=0, ts_tuple_mixed, 4 rounds on a loaded box). With the numpy block 731-1,367 ms, without it 259-443 ms; python -X importtime puts import numpy at 570 ms cumulative. Dropping 'from main_auto import *' as well changes nothing measurable (266-511 ms). The reference run's Python run_ms median of 502 ms (22x C) is therefore mostly the numpy import that this pass removes from array-free programs.")
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_4 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:30Z", "main", "10-27 21:30 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. reaches_arrays (pack-next4 python-lazy-array-import.ps1) has 1 hit in the tree core.")
inl eoie_agile_find () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", "eoie agile find <root> <words>: one-call text search over titles, tags and notes (agile list has no text filter; grooming greps state/agile.spi by hand)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 60u32, Paused, "", "eoie,agile,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_find () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "eoie-lane", "15:55 friction while grooming: to find which item owns tree leases, 'agile list' rejected --grep (no text filter exists) and I grepped the 4.3 MB state/agile.spi by hand with a regex window. Verb: 'eoie agile find <root> <words> [--open] [--team <word>] [--notes]' ranks items whose title, tags or (with --notes) notes contain every word (whole-word, case-insensitive), printing id, status, priority and the matching snippet. Typed: match_site Title | Tag | Note of note_number; reuse the whole-word scoring drafted for memory recall (lab/EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS recall coverage) instead of a second matcher.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:01Z", "eoie-lane", "16:01 related, not a duplicate: EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES adds structured filters and named views (team, tag, prefix, open, ready, blocked, sort); this item is free-text search. If a view row can carry words, find becomes a --words filter on list, plus a find view, instead of a separate parser.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:56Z", "eoie-lane", "16:56 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_find_model, model only, HostFree + LibReferenced): backlog_item {id; title; tags; notes}; find_scope TitlesAndTags | WithNotes; match_site InTitle | InTag | InNote n; find_result Found score | Missed word, which names the first query word that hit nothing instead of returning an empty list. Words are whole-word and case-insensitive (lower-case, punctuation to spaces, lib sm'.split plus am'.any); a word scores 3 in the title, 2 in the tags, 1 in note n; blank words are skipped. Two test blocks run on today's lease item. Cells notebook check: pass on the first compile and again after re-laying; eoie standards 0 findings; census ratchet clean. Next (compile release): the agile CLI verb over state rows, sorted by score, with --notes and --open.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 4u32, "2026-10-23T04:39Z", "eoie", "00:39 eoie: verb drafted as note 2 asked: a list/next filter, not a separate parser, since EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES' typed query flags are in the tree. Scratch agile-find/apply-agile-words.ps1, written against the post-GE2+H tree (GE2's heal wave rewrites query.spi; the dry run on that tree is clean). FlagWords :: query_flag string -> WithWords filter; words_hit keeps an item when every comma-separated word is a whole word, case-insensitive, of its title or tags (has_word copied from the Cells-checked agile_find_model). query_passes and the eoie_query_passes export gain the title; task_listing and next ranking pass row.title / task.title; the list and next usage shapes name --words (the usage test requires every flag). New test block words_match_whole_words_in_titles_and_tags (5 cases plus flag read and refusal). Note search (WithNotes) stays out: list rows don't carry notes. Risk, checked before it rides a release: task_listing.rs is at 979 and task_next.rs at 978 of 1000 lines; a compile preview after H decides, and if either crosses the gate, the callers' macro moves into a shared export instead of growing inline. Release: J, after I.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 5u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 6u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl eoie_ci_dev_workflow () = Task ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", "eoie CI is red since 10-18: test-dev-workflow 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used' on both OSes, and the uncommitted lib staging in test-regeneration would add a 'Spiral lib not found' failure for its fixture bundle", TaskKind, P1, D3, 90u32, Active, "", "eoie,ci", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow () = Planning ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:10Z", "eoie-lane", "16:10 found while checking EOIE-PAGES-VERIFY (read-only gh): eoie gh-pages CI has failed on every push since dd48d2a (10-18 17:48, last green). 2e0fb13 and 40e71ad failed in build.ps1 ('Native Spiral developer workflow failed (exit 101)'). a831bad (10-19 22:09, the latest) fails on both ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025 in compiler-contracts/test-dev-workflow.ps1:151, 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used.': the regeneration with -CargoCheck -Test -SkipRelease -Publish -IncludeAuxiliary prints PASS, yet <fixture>/src/target/shared validation/debug/deps does not exist. Cause not yet found by reading; it needs a local run of test-dev-workflow (fake compiler fixture plus a one-crate cargo check, a compile-window job) after F. Second finding, which would break CI again once committed: the uncommitted lib staging in test-regeneration.ps1 throws 'Spiral lib not found' whenever <bundle>/../../../lib/spiral is missing, and test-dev-workflow's synthetic bundle has none. Fix drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-regen-lib-optional.ps1 (stage the lib when present; refuse only when a staged package.spiproj lists spiral- without it); a dry run composed with apply-tree-read-scripts on copies, and both files parse. Rule for the local run: apply both slices to a scratch copy of compiler-contracts plus build.ps1 and src/eoie_dev only (no .cache, no target), run test-dev-workflow.ps1 there under 'eoie job start --compile'; the fix is ready when it exits 0, and otherwise the first failing assertion's cause is recorded here.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:18Z", "eoie-lane", "16:17 cause found and confirmed by measurement: a831bad switched CI from Rust 1.88.0 to nightly-2026-10-18, the same commit where this failure first appears (the two earlier red runs failed differently, in build.ps1). Probe job job-1792700083598 (one-file crate, 'cargo check --target-dir T --all-targets'): nightly-2026-10-18 creates T/debug/build/probe/<hash> and T/debug/incremental, with no debug/deps and no .fingerprint (cargo's build-dir layout); 1.90 creates T/debug/deps. test-dev-workflow.ps1:151 asserted the old internal layout, and it is the only deps-path assertion in compiler-contracts, scripts or src. Fix drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-dev-workflow-layout.ps1: assert the profile folder T/debug, which both layouts create (not debug/incremental, since CI caches may set CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0). Dry run: 1 line changed, parses. With apply-regen-lib-optional this gives two slices; the local test-dev-workflow run (rule in note 1) verifies both after F.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_3 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:31Z", "eoie-lane", "16:31 rule from note 1 met: local run job-1792700833224 (scratch copies of compiler-contracts and build.ps1, existing workflow-driver eoie-dev, RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=nightly-2026-10-18 like CI, 185 s). (a) Current tree: fails at the first regeneration (2 PASS), consistent with the lib-staging throw. (b) lib-optional only: 6 PASS, then exactly CI's 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used.' (reproduced locally). (c) Both slices: exit 0, 12 PASS. No fixture folders were left in TEMP. Ready: apply tree-read/apply-regen-lib-optional.ps1 and tree-read/apply-dev-workflow-layout.ps1 once F's lease ends; CI goes green on the next push if nothing else broke since a831bad (the user commits).")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_4 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 4u32, "2026-10-22T21:17Z", "eoie-lane", "17:17 applied to the live tree in the gap between F's runs (after-F-lease.ps1): test-regeneration lib staging is optional-unless-referenced, and test-dev-workflow asserts T/debug (diff: +19/-1 and +1/-1 against HEAD, including today's earlier range and lease edits). CI turns green on the user's next push if nothing else broke since a831bad.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_5 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:10Z", "eoie", "23:10 groom: no eoie CI run since 2026-10-20 02:10Z (gh-pages workflow 'tests' failure, run 39357467622). The last commit is a831bad (10-19); everything since is uncommitted local work, which is the user's to commit and push. So the 90% can't be confirmed until the next push; check that run before calling this done.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_6 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 6u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 30m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 90%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_7 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 7u32, "2026-10-25T17:39Z", "eoie", "13:39 kit groom: the CI failure changed. Run 40196034538 (i574n/kit tests, push to main 2026-10-25 13:05Z) fails on windows-2025 and Linux with 0 'Explicit Cargo target' and 0 'Spiral lib not found' lines.\n- New cause: native_selected_tests::selected_packages_preserve_native_build_contracts panics at eoie_dev/selected_tests.rs:24 because its fixture test fresh_cli_is_available can't find the CLI ('selected test requires the CLI', NotFound).\n- The fixture (eoie_dev/selected_tests.spi lines 42-55) expects <current_exe>/../../eoie(.exe), i.e. target/debug/eoie next to deps/. The client crate declares test-requires-cli=true, so the selected-test driver should build the eoie-cli bin before running the client's tests.\n- Next step: run the selected-test driver locally on the fixture with CARGO_TARGET_DIR set the way CI sets it, and compare where the bin lands with where current_exe/../.. points. Likely suspect: the driver builds the CLI into a different target dir or profile than the one the test runs from.\n- Progress stays 90% for the old cause; this new cause is a separate milestone (estimate 60m). It is a fix, not a feature, so the freeze allows it, but it rides SLATE's successor because the tree is in release.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_8 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 8u32, "2026-10-25T17:56Z", "eoie", "13:56 kit: likely root cause of the new CI failure (note 7).\n- In CI the test binaries use cargo's build-dir layout. The log shows 'Running selected_tests.rs (target\\debug\\build\\eoie-dev/3915de9c8a6f4b11\\out\\selected_workflow-3915de9c8a6f4b11.exe)', not target\\debug\\deps.\n- The fixture's client test looks for the CLI at current_exe/../../eoie(.exe). That is target/debug only in the old deps layout; under build/<pkg>/<hash>/out it points at a folder with no CLI, so NotFound.\n- The driver is fine: committed and live dev.rs are identical (682 lines, eoie-cli build path present), and it does build <target>/debug/eoie when test-requires-cli=true.\n- Local artifacts still use deps (last built 10-24 10:39), which is why it passes here. CI runs the same nightly-2026-10-18; what makes CI use the new layout is unconfirmed (no CARGO_BUILD_* other than JOBS in the log).\n- Fix draft (fixture only, no product change; the test text is a Rust string written into a temp project): scratch ci-cli-path\\fixture-cli-lookup.do. It finds the first ancestor of current_exe that holds eoie(.exe), which works in both layouts.\n- Next: lands after SLATE (editing the tree now would change measured content), then one CI run shows green or the next cause. A local repro needs a compile and can go to the laptop node.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_9 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "kit2", "13:59 claimed by kit2 (88a146f8) per kit/kit2 split: reproduce the CI cargo build-dir failure on the laptop (full eoie test run = CPU this box lacks); kit's fixture-cli-lookup fix (ancestors().find is_file) lands after SLATE.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_10 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 10u32, "2026-10-25T18:08Z", "kit2", "14:08 kit2: read-only config check toward the reproduce (no compile). No repo/CI setting forces the build-dir layout: src/.cargo/config.toml pins target-dir=target and the only CARGO_* in CI is CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 (gh-pages.yml). So the deps-vs-build-dir split is not an env/config toggle - it is toolchain/cargo-cache state. This box passes because its cached eoie artifacts (built 10-24) use the deps layout; CI fails on a clean build. Therefore the laptop, which has never built eoie, is the correct clean reproduce env (fresh cargo state matches CI). Queued for the fork-idle green light: offload a clean read-only selected-test run of eoie_dev via remote-run.ps1 (sync src minus target/.cache, RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=nightly-2026-10-18, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2), expect the NotFound panic at selected_tests.rs:24, then apply kit fixture-cli-lookup on the mirror only and confirm green. In-tree fix still lands after SLATE.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_11 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 11u32, "2026-10-25T19:53Z", "eoie", "15:53 kit: the fix draft from note 8 was in invalid eoie do syntax; corrected and dry-run ok on a copy (ci-cli-path\\dry-run.ps1: 1 replacement at selected_tests.spi line 54, the ancestors() lookup in place, the two-parent lookup gone). The corrected text went to kit2, who reproduces on the worker node.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_12 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 12u32, "2026-10-25T19:55Z", "kit2", "15:55 kit2: kit's corrected fixture-cli-lookup apply program saved ready-to-run at scratchpad/ci-cli-path-fix.do (eoie do, root=src, via stdin; dry-run ok 1 replacement at selected_tests.spi:54). NOT applied now - eoie source is under the KIT-RENAME freeze and this fix lands after SLATE (editing the tree changes measured release content). The node REPRODUCE is also gated on SLATE: eoie can't build from source on the node yet (8 hand-split crates lack .rs until SLATE's regen, EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS). So: apply the fix + reproduce the build-dir-layout NotFound both after SLATE installs, then one CI run confirms green.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_13 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 13u32, "2026-10-26T20:53Z", "kit", "16:53 kit: kit2's fixture fix (its scratch ci-cli-path-fix.do) no longer applied. The rename moved it to kit_dev/selected_tests.spi and the CLI name to kit, and edition 3 respelled the fixture as ^^ string pieces with escaped quotes, so the old one-line anchor is gone. Rebased as kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\ci-cli-path-fix-e3.do (1 file, 1 replacement, lines 67-68 become three pieces). The joined fixture is: let name=format!(\\\"kit{}\\\",EXE_SUFFIX); let binary=current.ancestors().map(|folder| folder.join(&name)).find(|candidate| candidate.is_file()).expect(...), instead of parent().parent(), so a test exe under an explicit CARGO_TARGET_DIR still finds the CLI. Dry run on a copy: ok, 1 replacement, widest line 119. Lands after the rename is finalized. Proof, set now: on a node mirror, run the kit_dev notebook that writes this fixture with CARGO_TARGET_DIR pointed outside the fixture project; before the fix it must fail with 'selected test requires the CLI' (positive control), after it, pass.")
inl pack_next_4 () = Task ("PACK-NEXT-4", "Compiler pack after PACK-NEXT-3: output size and startup cost (C blank lines, Zig/Bend preludes on demand, Python lazy array import) plus formatter raw-block segments v2; composed by %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\compose.ps1 over the reviewed next3 core 95AF71D2FE8AE0E6", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-2", "compiler,codegen,formatter,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pack_next_4 () = Planning ("PACK-NEXT-4", Minutes 180u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pack_next_4_1 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:24Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:24 main: contents, each with its own pre-written rule. (1) C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES: line' routes through lineC; C diffs whitespace-only. (2) ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND: zigUsedPrelude; changed main.zig = old minus prelude helpers, mirror -24.3% lines. (3) BEND-BACKEND note 31: bendUsedPrelude; changed main.bend = old minus prelude defs, mirror -18.1%. (4) PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT: the cupy/numpy block only when cp. or cuda is used; changed main.py = old minus exactly that block, 94 of 451 keep it. (5) FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS raw-block-segments v2: raw.spi lays out like plain.spi, 0 synthetic lines, formatter gate 0 beyond parentheses/whitespace. review.ps1 checks (1)-(4) mechanically over a before-snapshot of every main.c/zig/bend/py, plus DISAGREE 0, native results equal to next3 row for row, and the median native_ms for Bend and Python published. Starts only after PACK-NEXT-3 is accepted and installed, because compose refuses a changed next3 core.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_2 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:51Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:51 main: rebased onto PACK-NEXT-3b (the or-join-free next3, core B9C5F83974DB245C) and composed end to end: c-blank-lines, zig-prelude-on-demand, bend-prelude-on-demand, python-lazy-array-import, raw-block-segments v2, and macro-continuations-kept (FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS note 51) all apply in sequence. Core CDCF5A72AFA1EC54, pair-drift 0 one-sided, lint G1 596, G2 0. review.ps1 now also checks that sm'.spi formats with 0 over-120 lines and every continuation line kept as written. It starts after next3b is accepted and installed (compose refuses a changed base).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_3 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 3u32, "2026-10-22T22:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:06 main: next-session order for the main lane, written from today's state. (1) Read PACK-NEXT-3b's review (%TEMP%\\pack-next3b\\read.ps1 against pack-after-night's run, rules in PACK-NEXT-2 notes 5, 7, 9, 12). If green, install (adapt %TEMP%\\pack-next3\\install.ps1 to next3b: Base 25D2BE7C94943D8E, core B9C5F83974DB245C) and run a single-flight -Accept that reproduces the review row for row with SPIRAL_CC pinned. (2) PACK-NEXT-4 review (compose refuses anything but next3b's core; its build should reuse >= 20 shared ReadyToRun images, which closes BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE). (3) The Cells pack verdict (targeted baseline of the failing files) and the combined lib landing (25 files) after eoie's past-lib-reads. (4) or-join v3: a Debug candidate with v2 for the stack, then the v3 design with or_pattern_bound_alternatives as its guard. (5) On a quiet box: the hopacsota waits census and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_4 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 4u32, "2026-10-22T22:56Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:56 main: recomposed, core B84CC78DC4162016 (pair-drift 0 one-sided, G1 596, G2 0). Now: next3b core B9C5F83974DB245C, then macro-unit-fit-dropped.ps1 (exact inverse; PACK-NEXT-2 note 18), c-blank-lines, zig and bend prelude on demand, python lazy array import, raw-block segments v2, macro continuations kept, and rust-let-immutable.ps1 (RUST-LET-IMMUTABLE). Since next3b is not installed, next4 is the pack to accept; its matrix compares with next3b's run. Review rules added before the run: Rust outputs differ only by let mut -> let; the formatter gate's automatic part must PASS, and it now fails on joined authored lines.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_5 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 5u32, "2026-10-22T23:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 19:06 main: plus C-FUSE-DECLARATIONS, so the core is now 307C133544754112 (pair-drift 0 one-sided, G1 596, G2 0). The C rule in review.ps1 becomes 'beyond blank lines and declaration fusion 0' (self-tested on closure_curry against the mirror). Queued as job-1792709830384-main-pack-next4-review (heavy, after the next3b review).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_6 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 6u32, "2026-10-22T23:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 19:54 main: next3b's matrix (single-flight-20261022-184215: DISAGREE 0, native 6806 ran) exposed four defects that next4 inherited, all fixed before next4's matrix. (1) Bend: union-w64 re-declared w64_hi/w64_lo, which bendW64Prelude already defines; bend2 rejects the duplicate, so 11 Bend 64-bit rows were build-fail. New step bend-w64-projection-dedupe.ps1 deletes the copy. (2) Rust module split: 6 NATIVE-DIFF rows (main_part1.rs missing), because the host's stale-parts cleanup (Program.fs ~600) ran on every backend's compile of a sample and deleted the Rust parts written moments earlier. The cleanup now runs for backend = Rust only (tree host, before next4's host compile). (3) record_with_expected_base and gadt_growing_index_runtime used + and - under packages core-, which only binds +. and -.; fixed in the tree samples. (4) next4's first build failed on my c-fuse-declarations: the here-string dropped its final newline and glued 'let codegenC' to 'fused.ToString()'. Fixed, and both reviews now run fsc --parseonly under both defines (8 s) before building. Core now 67F2E4064FD4AFDE; requeued as job-1792712913136-main-pack-next4-review.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_7 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 7u32, "2026-10-23T00:15Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:15 main: order fixed before the run. Both matrices write outputs into the tree's samples/ and their mutexes are per cache, so next4 is requeued after the hopacsota promotion (job-1792714506844, --after job-1792711871311). Because the hopacsota matrix replaces next3b's outputs with pack-after-night's, next4's snapshot is pack-after-night's. That keeps the C, Zig and Python text rules exact (next3b touched none of them). For Rust and Bend the text check becomes informational; their acceptance is the matrix compare with next3b's run single-flight-20261022-184215 (DISAGREE 0, native equal row for row, no new build-fail).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_8 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 8u32, "2026-10-23T01:48Z", "main", "2026-10-22 21:48 main: ZIG-CONST-LOCALS rides in next4 (pack-queued-builds: next4 hadn't started). The compose applies %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\zig-const-locals.ps1 after the bend dedupe. Core E1F6B0543BC3A1B2 parses under both defines, and codegenZig already uses Dictionary/HashSet unqualified. The Zig review rule becomes 'beyond the prelude and const locals 0'. zig-const-check.fsx strips the full prelude from each snapshot, applies the same zigConstLocals to the body in one fsi pass, and requires the new output to end with it. Self-test: a correct pair passes and an untransformed pair is flagged (checked 2, bad 1).")
