inl note_of_lua_helpers_on_demand_1 () = Note ("LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:48 unused helpers are 4.3% of 1,321,108 Lua bytes over 448 tree samples (wrap_unsigned unused in 185, mul_mod32 in 167, wrap_signed in 19). Pass pack-next6\\lua-helpers.ps1 (luaUsedHelpers in codegenLua; helperLocals counted after pruning, so the 190-local definitions line can only gain room). Mirror lua_helpers_mirror.py; the F# port equals it on all 200 outputs with helpers (248 have none). In pack-next4, core 6D9273CFCCB09AEB parses under both defines; review rule in locals_check.py.")
inl note_of_lua_helpers_on_demand_2 () = Note ("LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-27T23:07Z", "main", "10-27 19:07 main: Groom 10-27 19:0x: this change is LANDED and live; the 80% from 10-23 is stale. Evidence (read-only): the live core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs contains the pass, and the committed sample outputs show its effect (LUA: luaUsedHelpers present, and only 1 of 40 sampled main.lua still defines wrap_unsigned, against 185 unused copies measured on 10-23; GLEAM: gleamUsedHelpers present; C-STATIC: generated C such as samples/closure_apply/main.c declares its functions static). It reached the core through pack-next4/next6, which were composed into the installed stacks. Closing condition, set now: Done when the running bundle accept (accept-6, job-1793136844465) is GREEN, since that accept blesses the current outputs as the oracle; if it's RED for unrelated reasons, close on the next GREEN accept.")
