inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_1 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:29Z", "eoie", "06:29 why: the adoption sweep says 19,447 of 32,687 shell calls in the last 8 days were habit calls, but not WHICH habits, so the guide cannot target the biggest ones. Today habit_named (eoie_adoption host.spi:69) is one host-macro blob that answers a bool over 18 words (get-content, select-string, get-childitem, set-content, add-content, out-file, foreach-object, measure-object, copy-item, remove-item, move-item, test-path, start-sleep, robocopy, findstr, python, python3, py.exe, plus [io.file]). Design: a typed habit union with one case per habit and its word (union_reflection for the lookup, like agile_task_query's flags), a per-habit tally in model.spi, and 'eoie adoption --habits' printing habit TAB calls TAB share, sorted by calls; the blob migrates into the union (AGENTS.md: touching legacy migrates it), word-boundary matching moves to lib sm' calls. Rule for the guide: its next section covers the habits that together make up 80% of habit calls in the --habits table, largest first. Tests: model test over synthetic commands, adoption CLI notebook with a fixture transcript.")
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_2 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:37Z", "eoie", "06:37 pure model passes (scratch f5/src/eoie_adoption_habits_model, notebook check habits5): habit union with one word per case (19 cases incl. python3, py.exe and [io.file]), whole-word matching on lib code points (python3 is not python, my-get-contents.ps1 is not get-content), one count per habit per call, text table of the habits that occur in union order. Typecheck lessons recorded in memory (precedence of |> not with &&; annotate lambda params for record fields). Next: the --habits flag in eoie_adoption with the blob migrated into this union, after GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_3 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:57Z", "eoie", "07:57 wired: scratch adoption-habits/apply-adoption-habits.ps1 (on the raw chain after G's heal) adds habits.spi (the passing model + habit_any), threads a typed tally_mode (ByDay | ByHabit) through calls_tallied/files_tallied/client_tallied, prints 'habit TAB calls' largest first under --habits with receipt 'mode=habits days=n habits=k', switches classify to habits.habit_any and removes the 18-word host blob habit_named (migration; same word-boundary semantics). Full L dry chain applies; on that tree classify.spi (the migration's regression net) and habits.spi pass. First run hit the here-string trailing-newline trap twice (two lines glued); fixed. Ships in release L with an after-install probe; closes on PASS.")
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_4 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 4u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl sim_x_feature_parity () = Task ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", "x sim: extend the simulator until it covers every X feature x uses, so the whole client can run against the sim (start only after all the sim's Elixir is rewritten in Spiral)", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "SIM-IN-SPIRAL", "x,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_feature_parity () = Planning ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", Minutes 630u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:41 x: user request 2026-10-23 06:3x: 'extend sim so we reach full x feature parity, but only after all the elixir got rewritten in spiral'. Gate: deps SIM-IN-SPIRAL (the remaining Elixir half: World/Runner supervision, mix tasks); do not start while any apps/sim Elixir remains. Starting point for the inventory when it starts: today the sim models post, reply (parent), edit (revisions), delete, like/unlike, and frames (threads split into posts, edits of frames). xc also uses timelines per account with paging, following lists, users lookup by name and id, posts lookup by id with referenced posts (quote, reply, repost), conversation search for threads, OAuth 1.0a / 2.0 auth and token refresh, post and delete writes, and X's text rules (weights, autolinks, HTML escaping, trailing-whitespace trim), which e2e's Python stub covers today. First step: list each xc feature against the sim with a covered/missing column, then add the missing ones as sim slices in Spiral, each with its parity test against the stub's current behaviour, until e2e can run against the sim instead of the stub.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: PARITY TARGET inventory (planning only; work still waits for SIM-IN-SPIRAL). x's model of the X API today is the e2e Python stub (apps/client/e2e/stub.py), so 'full x feature parity' means the sim serves at least what the stub serves. GET: /2/users/by (usernames), /2/users (ids), /2/tweets (id lookup, 100 per call), /2/tweets/:id, /2/tweets/search/recent (7 days) and /search/all (30 days, start_time), /2/users/:id/following (paged by next_token, following_page_size), /2/users/:id/tweets (timeline, exclude=replies,retweets). POST: /2/tweets (create, reply via reply.in_reply_to_tweet_id, assigns ids, conversation_id and replied_to refs), /2/oauth2/token (refresh rotation, client Basic auth). DELETE: /2/tweets/:id (own posts only). Cross-cutting: 4 auth contexts (app bearer, user bearer, OAuth 1.0a signature check, client Basic) and the 403 for app-only on writes; problem+json errors (400/401/403/404); edit_history_post_ids; HTML escaping of & < > on output; public_metrics; url entities with expanded/display/title; referenced posts and their expansions; a request log the e2e asserts on. GAP found while reading: the stub caps text at 280 Python characters (code points), not X's weighted count, so it accepts a 200-CJK post that X would refuse at weight 400. The sim should enforce the weighted rule (glyphs plain_weight + X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's entities) and return X's 400 problem. Its world model (inbox/scheduler) already has posts, edits, likes and deletes; users, following, timelines, search windows and auth are what remains to model.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:21Z", "x", "10-24 07:21 x: estimate by rule, not by guess: 90m per X API endpoint family that the client calls and the sim does not serve (a sim handler, a world-model field, and one e2e row that runs the client against the sim). Code check on HEAD 6ef9c48: apps/client/wire.spi calls 7 families (/2/tweets lookup, /2/tweets create+delete, /2/tweets/search/all, /2/users lookup, /2/users/by, following, user timeline), and apps/sim mentions no /2/ path, so 7 x 90 = 630m. Load is medium: each family needs one sim compile plus one client e2e, not a matrix. Re-size after SIM-IN-SPIRAL lands, because the Spiral world may change the per-family cost.")
inl sim_world_spiral () = Task ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", "x sim: port the runtime world model (apps/sim/lib/xsim/world.ex, 127 lines: delivery dedup, pending buffer, frame gathering, rekeying, demand, dirty set) to a pure Spiral module with test blocks; no supervision involved", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,sim,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_world_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL after the 10-22/23 night retrospective. world.ex is a pure state machine (apply_delivery, drain, gathered, rekey, wanted_key, take_dirty) and has no Spiral counterpart (client world.spi is the posting side). Nothing in it needs gleam_otp actors or KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, so marking all of SIM-IN-SPIRAL Blocked hid this work from the ready list for two nights. Shape: unions for the 7 delivery kinds (log.ex @fields), a record world, exhaustive matches, the oracle being the current Elixir via the existing ExUnit and corpus-commute fixtures until the Spiral module replaces it. Until the user's commit lands, work in a scratch copy with an anchored apply script (memory verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:09 x: grooming, the user's 10-23 directions applied to the first rewrite step. (1) BACKEND-BALANCE: write it backend-neutral and test it on rust AND gleam; it runs inside the Gleam runner and on Rust for batch sweeps. (2) Lib first: build on listm' / sm' / resultm and LIB-PAR-MAP; anything generic it needs (a map/set over keys, a dedup set) goes into lib/spiral first, not into x. (3) No host blobs: pure Spiral domain (unions for the delivery kinds, records), with any Gleam boundary through granular primitives (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2), never global or rust_global code.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:16 x: plan and rules for today's run (until 18:00), written before any code. FACTS: world.ex needs a map (posts, keys, frames, demand, clock), a set (seen, dirty) and SHA-256 (rekey: first 16 hex chars of sha256(parent_key, 0, source)). lib/spiral has none of these backend-neutrally: mapm wraps Rust/F# HashMap/BTreeMap only, crypto's sha256 has F#/Python/Rust arms only, and there is no gleam/dict or gleam_crypto binding. STEPS: (1) draft lib modules in a lib scratch copy, granular per the user's 10-23 direction (one nominal per foreign type, one one-expression primitive per foreign function, no blobs). They are a persistent map (Rust std BTreeMap via persistent copy-on-insert or im-style; Gleam gleam/dict), a set on top of it, and a sha256-hex (Rust sha2 crate; Gleam gleam_crypto hash Sha256 + bit_array base16). Test blocks on rust + gleam, plus a fixed-vector sha test ('abc' -> ba7816bf...). (2) Port world.ex to a new module (name chosen after a clash scan) on top of them, with unions for the 7 delivery kinds and records, plus test blocks ported from xsim_test's World cases. (3) Compile-check both arms (rust crate entry, gleam via simframes-style entry) under eoie compile windows at >= 3.5 GB free. (4) Cells test runs only when no other Cells job is active. RULES: never touch x's staged tree or lib/spiral itself (scratch copies plus anchored apply scripts); hand the lib drafts to main for landing (main owns lib) and keep x's port on them; fallback if the map binding stalls past 13:00: an association-list map behind the same interface, swapped later. Progress is recorded here with timestamps.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:27 x: step 1 done for the map: LIB-DICTM drafted and passing on rust + gleam under Cells. Correction to note 3: SHA-256 is NOT a gap; lib crypto.hash_text is already backend-neutral (Gleam via an @external to erlang crypto:hash, plus Rust, Zig, F# and TS arms, fixed-vector tests). Next: the world.ex port on dictm + crypto.hash_text, before tibia's live block at about 09:40 (no compiles during it).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:46 x: step 2 done in draft: world.ex is ported to Spiral as module 'inbox' (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\inbox.spi; scratch package C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client = x client + dictm + inbox, x untouched). It reuses events.spi's delivery/event unions and splits state into small records (intake, graph, reactions, tallies) so each definition fits one post. Under Cells (process compiler, memo off, no other Cells job active): 6/6 test blocks pass on rust -d sha2 AND gleam (217 s). They cover dedup, a child before its parent waiting then landing, frames in any order rebuilding the post, an older unlike not undoing a newer like, an edit rekeying children, and keys_match_the_elixir_world: both backends give exactly Elixir's keys (022a6979e6dab7aa / 451526e1c47e6023, computed with the same :crypto.hash(:sha256, [parent_key, 0, source]) on elixir 1.20.4). Fixed on the way: 6 name clashes renamed (edited, frame_at, gathered, posted, step, wanted); x-compat autolinks on .social and .parts fields (now reactions / frame_parts); a tuple accumulator in a fold (see LIB-DICTM note 2). Next: a parity run against the Elixir World on real event streams, then hand dictm to main and the module to x's tree after the user's commit.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:52 x: PARITY with the Elixir World on a real event stream, both backends. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\gen_parity.exs loads the actual apps/sim/lib/xsim/world.ex + frames.ex (Code.require_file), replays apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (65 deliveries: 19 frame, 22 edit_frame, 20 like, 4 unlike) through Xsim.World and writes a Spiral test that rebuilds the same deliveries with typed constructors and asserts Elixir's results. Spiral's inbox gives exactly the same on rust -d sha2 and gleam (Cells, 96 s): tallies framed=6 edits=6 dups=14 buffered=0, all 6 post keys (p0=3f5400c8092236b1 ... p5=f86e168f9079f0b8) and the same 6 wanted keys. inbox gained frame_from (a frame with a parent) and edit_frame_at. The generated parity test stays scratch tooling (its fixture literal would not fit x-compat's one-post rule); the generator is the reusable part. Slip: this run overlapped eoie's notebook check by about a minute because my script printed the foreign runner instead of refusing; eoie was told, and the script now refuses (exit 75) while any other run_notebook is active.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:49 x: dependency recorded: LIB-DICTM. inbox.spi (and scheduler.spi on top of it) use dictm, and by lib-first dictm lands in lib/spiral (main's), not as a copy in x. LANDING PLAN once main lands dictm: inbox.spi, scheduler.spi, runner_checks.spi and the parity tests (inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity) join apps/client's package next to invariants/world/scenario, where the sim's Spiral already lives (simframes exports). The parity tests' generators (gen_parity.exs, gen_runner_parity.exs, gen_checks_parity.exs) become mix tasks in apps/sim (xsim.parity), so the fixtures are regenerated from the Elixir oracle rather than hand-kept, until each Elixir module is retired. All sources are in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:51Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:51 x: the generator mix task is drafted and checked: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim.parity.ex (Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Parity, 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out DIR'). It replaces the three loose generators (gen_parity / gen_runner_parity / gen_checks_parity .exs). It reads the stream through the real Xsim.Log.decode, runs the real World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check, and writes inbox_parity.spi, scheduler_parity.spi and checks_parity.spi with their headers, so no PowerShell assembly is left. Checked by loading the task source next to the real xsim modules (check_parity_task.exs, Elixir only) on prelude.events.jsonl: 65 deliveries, 6 posts, 6 visible. The fixture is byte-equal to the hand-verified inbox_parity.spi, and every final expectation equals the Cells-verified files. checks_parity keeps only the real Invariants.check test; the row-text copy test is dropped, because the real oracle supersedes it. Lands into apps/sim/lib/mix/tasks with the Spiral modules (note 7).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:31 x: landing script for note 7's plan: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_modules.ps1 [-Go]. It copies the 10 Spiral sim modules (inbox, inbox_parity, scheduler, scheduler_parity, runner_checks, checks_parity, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run) from x-world\\client into apps/client, lists them after invariants in package.spiproj (dependency order) and in build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests notebook list. Guards: it refuses (exit 75) until lib/spiral has dictm.spi (lib-first: no x copy of dictm) or while x has staged changes, and refuses if any module is missing or already present. Dry run now: refused on the dictm guard, as expected. xsim_entry is left out: as an entry it needs its own crate folder with Cargo.toml and a workspace entry, which come with the X-BACKEND-MAP decision, not this landing.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-23T19:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:25 x: after the formatter pass (inbox 161 and scheduler 219 lines re-laid), the dictm no-host-callback rework, sorted Gleam keys and the sim_run import fix, everything passes again on rust + gleam: inbox 6/6, inbox_parity 1/1, scheduler 5/5 (with a_crashed_task_is_a_visible_crash_result and peak_results), scheduler_parity 1/1, checks_parity 2/2. Parity with the real Elixir World, Runner and Invariants holds on the formatted sources, so the formatter changed no meaning there. Remaining in chain9: tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, xsim_entry and the lib drafts.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:26Z", "x", "10-24 03:26 x: landing state of the 17 Spiral sim modules (apply_sim_modules.ps1). Its 'x has staged changes' refusal is replaced by an unstaged check on package.spiproj/build.ps1 only, per the new rule that work lands as an unstaged layer over the user's staged commit (memory land-drafts-in-the-repo). Dry run now refuses only on the real blocker: lib/spiral has no dictm.spi yet (main's main-lib-dictm-land-2, in main's lib chain, paused for tibia's PR-27 block). After dictm lands: run with -Verdicts <tonight's chain logs> (chain21c passed scheduler_parity, runner_checks, scenario_checks, runner_shell and executors; the rest are in requeue_after_block's chain21d), then -Go.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T10:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 06:24 -04:00 x: xsim.parity LANDED (unstaged) in a new shape. It no longer writes .spi files with the fixture inlined (that broke the 280-weight cell rule: inbox_parity's literal list weighed 10,222). 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out FILE' writes one labelled line per expectation (tallies, keys, wanted, wanted_count, visible, visible_count, checks_fake, checks_wrong) from the real Xsim.World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check. apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates it after xsim.events and compares it with apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.parity.txt via Confirm-Fixture (-Accept rewrites). The Spiral parity modules hold no data: they read ../sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (the one recorded stream; one record per fact) and look up each expectation by label. The row-text test is dropped, as the draft said. A new test, the_prelude_fixture_is_a_canonical_stream, checks that Spiral's encoder re-emits the Elixir stream byte for byte. First run on the current stream: deliveries=61 posts=6 visible=6, dups=12 buffered=1. The old hand-kept expectations were for an older 65-delivery stream, the reason to generate them.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-27T12:41Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:41 x: groomed. LIB-DICTM is Done and lib/spiral/dictm.spi is live. The Spiral sim modules are already in the tree, in apps/sim/spi, not apps/client: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, scenario_checks, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, world, invariants and the rest are listed in apps/sim/spi/package.spiproj, and apps/sim/build.ps1's full pass runs their notebooks. The three *_parity modules were reshaped on 10-25 (xsim.parity writes fixtures/prelude.parity.txt, compared with Confirm-Fixture). So %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_modules.ps1, which copies into apps/client, is obsolete; a dry run today refused on stale Cells verdicts and wrote nothing. What is left is a full sim build with every sim notebook green, which X-WIP-VERIFY's sim part runs (verify16b stopped at mix test under node load, before the notebooks).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:39Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl sim_runner_core_spiral () = Task ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", "x sim: port the runner's pure scheduling core (retarget, release/acquire refcounts, doom/expire/evict, queue, harvest bookkeeping from runner.ex) to Spiral as a state machine over events; the task spawning stays a thin shell", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,sim,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_runner_core_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL with SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL. In runner.ex only start_queued (Task.async), harvest (Task.yield_many) and Task.shutdown touch processes; everything else is pure bookkeeping that can be modeled as 'state + event -> state + commands' (StartTask key, CancelTask key) in Spiral now. The effectful shell that executes those commands is what waits for KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (gleam_otp). Folds in X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES's crash result as a domain case.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:53 x: design and rules, written before code. Module 'scheduler' (name after a clash scan) on top of inbox. STATE (small records): the inbox; running (dict key -> owner ids) and the queue (list key); refs (key -> count), post_key (id -> key), owner (key -> id); results (key -> task_result); doomed (key -> deadline) with a clock; visible (id -> key, result); counters (starts, cancelled, evicted, cancels, rescues, recomputes, peaks). INPUTS: a delivery; a task finished (key, result); a task crashed (key, reason); the drain end. OUTPUTS: commands StartTask {key; sources} and CancelTask key, which a shell executes. The shell is Elixir Task today and gleam_otp later via LIB-OTP-ACTORS; it is not part of this item. Faults as values: cancel on/off, rerun_completed, evict Off | Now | Grace n, naive visibility, max_concurrency. Every runner.ex function maps 1:1 (retarget, release, acquire, doom, expire, rescued, evict, requeue_completed, start_queued, reconcile, settle). TESTS: a synchronous driver that runs StartTask at once with a fake executor (the same digest as Executors.fake: sha256 of the sources joined by NUL, first 12 hex), and a deferred driver that completes tasks only at drain, which exercises cancellation. PARITY RULE: compare against the Elixir Runner on the settled visible map (id -> key and result) and wanted keys, which are deterministic. Do not compare starts/cancels, which depend on Elixir task timing. Compile/test windows: before 09:40 or after tibia's block (about 12:10).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:03 x: drafted as module 'scheduler' (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\scheduler.spi, on top of inbox): every runner.ex function 1:1 as small pure functions over records (task_work, owner_map, life_cycle, run_counts), task_command StartTask/CancelTask as outputs, faults as values (cancel, rerun, eviction NoEviction/EvictNow/EvictAfter n, naive, limit), plus sync_run and deferred_run drivers and 4 test blocks (computed once, withdrawn demand cancels, same-source roots share one task, eviction then relike recomputes). A clash scan renamed command/settled/started (cli, invariants, refs). Type-checks and compiles to Rust (24 s, 304 KB) and Gleam (19 s, 140 KB) via a check entry. Cells runs are waiting for tibia's Cells run to finish (my script refused twice, as it should). Parity test generated from the real Elixir Runner (gen_runner_parity.exs, Code.require_file of frames/world/executors/runner, Executors.fake): on prelude.events.jsonl it settles 6 visible posts with keys and fake outputs (26 starts and 1 cancel there, which by the rule are not compared); scheduler_parity asserts the same settled map on both backends.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: PARITY PASSES. Cells (rust + gleam, process compiler, memo off): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client\\scheduler.spi 4/4 and scheduler_parity.spi 1/1. On the recorded prelude stream (65 deliveries), the Spiral scheduler's settled visible map equals the Elixir Runner's: 6 posts, each with the same source key and the same fake executor digest. Two fixes got there: (1) a test used {plain_faults () with ...}, a parse error, because record update needs a bound name, so it is now inl plain = plain_faults (); (2) sync_run and deferred_run now bind the delivery list dynamically (inl ~deliveries = l). Before that, a static 65-element fixture made the partial evaluator unroll the whole fold with every inl scheduler step inlined, and the cell hit Cells' 300 s timeout. Lesson for SIM-IN-SPIRAL: step functions over recorded streams take dynamic input. Next (in flight): runner_checks.spi, the typed bridge that replaces invariants.ex (scheduler state -> invariants.spi snapshot, rows sorted by id, no text rows), and checks_parity.spi, which compares the bridge's findings with (a) checked_text over the Elixir row text and (b) the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching and a wrong baseline (expected 'sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Both pass on gleam and fail on rust only because of the dictm fold bug (LIB-DICTM note 5).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:44 x: progress set to 60%, by the rule used for every x item today: verified on a scratch copy = 70%, and the last 30% is applying it in the real tree plus the full test run. This one sits 10 points lower until checks_parity passes on rust after the dictm fix (queued for after tibia's block, with the new crash-result test).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:57 x: xsim.run's pure half drafted as C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_report.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). summary_line prints the same 'XSIM seed=.. schedule=.. ... peak_beam_mb=.. ms=.. <findings>' line as Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Run.summary, field for field in the same order, so xsim.diff and the logs keep working when the entry switches to Spiral. commuted_runs folds runs_commute over consecutive schedules (Enum.chunk_every(2, 1)), and verdict_line gives 'XSIM commute=.. result=pass|fail'. The scheduler gained run_counts.peak_results (max results size, updated in reconciled like runner.ex's reconcile). peak_beam_mb and ms come in through run_shape from the shell, since they are host measurements. Caught before compiling: 'x |> listm.append y' is y ++ x, so the fields are now joined by explicit nesting. 2 tests pin the line format and pass/fail.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:56 x: checks_parity PASSES 2/2 on rust + gleam (self-gating chain9, 14:42, after the dictm no-host-callback fix and the formatter pass). On the recorded 65-delivery stream, runner_checks' findings equal both Elixir oracles on both backends: checked_text over the Elixir row text, and the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching baseline and a wrong one ('sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Scheduler parity passed earlier, so the runner core and its invariant bridge are verified on scratch. Progress 60 -> 70 (rule: verified on scratch; landing waits for LIB-DICTM, via apply_sim_modules.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T23:04Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:04 x: PORTING BUG found by desk check (mapping xsim_test.exs case 51 'forgetting finished results recompiles shared prefixes'), fixed in scratch, check queued after tibia's block. Elixir's Runner re-queues completed tasks (rerun_completed) only in the per-delivery reconcile; drain reconciles with rerun: false (runner.ex:166), and harvest doesn't reconcile at all. scheduler.spi's reconciled always read faults.rerun and is also called after every completion batch (executed_all, the crash test helper, runner_shell's Finished handler), so any run with rerun = true would re-queue forever and never settle. The parity tests all use plain faults, which is why it went unseen. Fix: reconciled_with (rerun) as the core, reconciled = per delivery (faults.rerun), drained = after completions (rerun false); executed_all, crash test and runner_shell use drained. With rerun false both are identical, so the parity results are unaffected (scheduler_parity rerun first in the queue). Regression tests added to runner_checks: rerunning_finished_results_recompiles_shared_prefixes (rerun -> one_compile_per_prefix FAIL, plain -> ok) and naive_visibility_without_cancelling_surfaces_a_stale_revision (case 39; a newest-first execution run stands in for Elixir's slow old revision: naive + no cancel -> no_stale_results FAIL[p0], plain -> ok).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:28 x: scheduler.spi gained an executor parameter for the synchronous runner: executed_by exec / executed_all_by exec / sync_run_with exec, with sync_run = sync_run_with fake_result (fake_result = the old inline {Done; fake_out}). Needed by the ported Elixir case 223 (an honest executor that errs on broken sources). Behavior for existing callers is unchanged; scheduler + scheduler_parity rerun first after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:37 x: scheduler.spi PASSES 5/5 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:31-20:36) with both of tonight's changes: the rerun fix (reconciled_with / reconciled per delivery / drained after completions) and the executor parameter (executed_by / executed_all_by / sync_run_with). scheduler_parity (the Elixir oracle) is next in the chain; runner_checks then carries the regression tests for the rerun bug.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:40 x: scheduler_parity PASSES after tonight's changes (chain20, 20:36-20:39): the settled view still equals the real Elixir Runner's on the recorded 65-delivery stream. With rerun = false, reconciled_with is identical to the old reconciled, as predicted. runner_checks (the regression tests for the rerun bug) is next.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T01:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:02 x: runner_checks PASSES 9/9 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:44-20:5x): the 4 existing tests plus tonight's 5 runner-level ports of Elixir cases. Case 51, rerun recompiles shared prefixes, now settles and fails one_compile_per_prefix as Elixir does, which confirms the drain fix. Also case 39 naive stale revision, case 57 withdrawn demand, case 190 grace window and case 143 saved-stream replay. With scheduler 5/5 and scheduler_parity, the fixed core is verified on scratch. scenario_checks and everything after it in the queue didn't start before the 20:50 cutoff; re-run per X-DONE n68.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T03:51Z", "x", "23:51 x: chain21 (job-1792811662170) scheduler.spi: all 5 Rust cells timed out at 300 s. It passed 5/5 at 20:31. The box was saturated (CPU 100%, five heavy/compile windows across main, tibia and eoie), and main's listm-runtime land (23:05) changed listm'/seq runtime paths. RULE, written before the retry: retry_timeouts.ps1 (job x-chain21-retry, after chain21) reruns every notebook whose failures are all timeouts. Pass means contention. A timeout again means a regression, and I bisect against a pre-23:05 lib copy (x-libprog-roundtrip, 21:24, has the pre-tonight state) before anything that depends on scheduler lands.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T04:44Z", "x", "10-24 00:44 x: rule applied (n13). The timeout-only retry of scheduler.spi PASSED (job-1792813911883 attempt 2, 316 s, 00:43), so chain21's five 300 s timeouts were contention, not a regression from main's listm-runtime land. No bisect needed. The rest of chain21 is requeued as x-chain21c on the mutex-guarded compile-class gate.")
