inl eoie_start_under_shell () = Task ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", "eoie process start-under-shell starts a daemon with the user shell as its live parent", Story, P2, D2, 60u32, Paused, "", "eoie,process,parent", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_start_under_shell () = Planning ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_1 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T12:50Z", "eoie", "08:50 requested by main (CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT): one generic verb that starts a long-lived process with a live parent, so Cells' CompilerDaemon (and any other daemon) stops running parentless without a second Win32 launcher. Shape: eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]: CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the user shell (GetShellWindow -> explorer.exe), handle inheritance off, NORMAL priority, TEMP cwd, detached; prints pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> and exits 0; falls back to a plain detached start (reported as parent=none) only when there is no shell window. Reuses the launch primitive HP ships for the job host (one primitive in the eoie_os boundary package per EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY's 08:32 scope), so the host and this verb share code. Test: start a ping under the shell, read its parent (explorer.exe), stop it. Priority P2, after HP-tail and the release chain.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_2 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:04Z", "eoie", "09:04 drafted for release L: new OS boundary crate eoie_os_launch. launch.spi emits the launch function once through Spiral global (top-level Rust: CreateProcessW with the shell as parent attribute, the same fallbacks as the job host, returns pid and parent; unix keeps a Command in its own process group) plus a one-expression primitive; run.spi is lib-only (env args, console). Verb: VerbProcess -> 'eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]' printing 'pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> program=...'. The Rust compiled standalone and started ping under explorer.exe (reported parent 8628 = actual parent explorer.exe); both Spiral files parse with the 5e formatter, which left the global Rust byte-identical. Prelaunch clean after two checker fixes (a triple-quoted global block and a formatter-wrapped macro were read as code); the improved checker still passes GE2's chain. after-install-L probes it (parent must be explorer.exe). Next: the job host calls this function instead of its own copy.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_3 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: user direction 10-23 22:1x is that eoie processes belong to the session that started them, not explorer (EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT). This verb stays for daemons that must outlive any session, but should gain a start-under-owner form on the same eoie_parented_start primitive (owner pid instead of the shell window), and callers should pick it unless outliving the session is the point.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_4 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:34Z", "eoie", "00:34 found and fixed a certain stage-L build failure before L launched. launch.spi put its Rust in a top-level 'global' block of a non-entry module, which codegen drops: the regenerated os_launch.rs was 4,623 bytes with no eoie_shell_parented_start*, so run.spi's call failed E0425 under real cargo (the 09:04 check compiled the Rust by hand, outside Spiral). Fix: start-under-shell\\eoie_os_launch\\launch.spi now puts the global inside shell_parented_started's body (move-global-into-body.ps1; Rust byte-identical; backup .before-global-move). Verified: regen + cargo check of eoie-os-launch + eoie-job-host on the emulated post-R tree, PASS 00:33, os_launch.rs 13,334 bytes. This edits stage L's source while the train runs (GE3 queued); justified because L would otherwise fail deterministically, and the change was emulated and compiled before L launches.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_5 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl eoie_slot_file_settled () = Task ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", "job slot files record the settled state instead of a phantom waiting ticket", TaskKind, P2, D2, 50u32, Paused, "", "eoie,jobs,slots", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_slot_file_settled () = Planning ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_slot_file_settled_1 () = Note ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "eoie", "09:08 found while checking slot tickets before stopping HP-tail: 28 job folders whose job has settled (finished, stopped, lost, expired) still have a slot file saying 'waiting ticket=...'. The supervisor writes 'waiting' while queued and 'ready' on admission, but nothing rewrites it when a job settles before or without admission, and old-binary jobs never wrote 'ready'. Anything that reads slot files (window-slot-order.ps1, a human checking the queue) sees phantom waiters. Fix: the supervisor writes the slot file's final state when the job settles ('settled ticket=... state=<state>'), and window list (or job list --queue) shows the live ticket queue from the queue directory, not from job folders. Test: a queued heavy job stopped before admission leaves 'settled', not 'waiting'.")
inl note_of_eoie_slot_file_settled_2 () = Note ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:12Z", "eoie", "09:12 slice drafted for release L (scratch slot-settled/apply-slot-settled.ps1, on G's healed slot.spi): the slot loop's stop and expiry branches write 'settled ticket=<t> state=stopped|expired-in-queue' to the slot file before returning, so a job that ends in the queue no longer leaves a phantom waiting ticket. Anchored on the two return lines (layout-independent); dry run on the chain's raw slot.spi applies and is idempotent. The other half (a live queue view from the queue directory) stays open.")
inl note_of_eoie_slot_file_settled_3 () = Note ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", 3u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl x_stub_weighted_limit () = Task ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", "x e2e: the stub refuses posts over X's weighted 280 (code-point weights + emoji entities) with X's 400 problem, instead of capping 280 Python characters, so e2e catches a splitter that emits an overweight post", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,e2e,weights", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_stub_weighted_limit () = Planning ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_1 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: found while inventorying the stub for SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY. stub.py create() checks len(text) > 280 in code points, so a 200-CJK post (X weight 400) is accepted, and e2e can never catch an overweight frame. The stub's weight must be an independent implementation (Python, from twitter-text v3's ranges and emoji entities), not a call into x's glyphs, so it stays an oracle. Test: one tutorial or e2e-only step posting a text that is 141 CJK characters gets the 400. Land after X-EMOJI-WEIGHT, so both sides count emoji the same way.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_2 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:43 x: drafted and verified red/green (no compile: release xc.exe + the Python stub). Oracle: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\x_weight.py, an algorithm independent of x's glyphs: NFC first as twitter-text does, then regex-matched emoji entities (flag pairs, keycaps, pictograph or VS16-symbol sequences with skin tones, tags and ZWJ joins) at 2 each, then per-code-point v3 ranges. Its self-test agrees on 14 of 14 cases (thumb + tone 2, ZWJ family 2, heart + VS16 2, keycap 2, JP flag 2, decomposed e 1 after NFC, (c) 1, a + VS16 3, 140 CJK 280, 141 CJK 282). apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines it into stub.py (the stub runs with python -I, so no sibling import), changes create() to x_weight(text) > 280 with the same 400 message, and adds a README step after 'posted 9001': xc post ('日' * 141); $LASTEXITCODE. GREEN (patched stub): the step answers 'xc: X API answered 400 for /2/tweets: Invalid Request: text must be 1 to 280 characters' and 4. RED (same README, original stub): 'posted 9002' and 0. Both copies: accept and verify have 0 failures, so no other step changed and the refused post uses no id. Note: x's counts are >= the oracle's for every case (x doesn't NFC; it counts a decomposed e as 2), so the stub never refuses a post x's splitter produced. Harness: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_stub_check.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_3 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:39 x: known simplification in the drafted oracle (x_weight.py): X weighs every URL as 23 (t.co, twitter-text's transformedURLLength), but the oracle counts a URL's real characters. So it is STRICTER than X for URLs longer than 23 and laxer for shorter ones. No tutorial step hits it today: framed thread posts defuse dots (no links form), and e2e's single posts carry no URLs. If a future step posts a link, add twitter-text's URL rule to the oracle first (its validUrl regex is large; a scheme-and-host subset covers x's cases), or the stub will refuse posts X accepts.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_4 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:07Z", "x", "10-24 00:07 x: n3's known gap CLOSED for scheme URLs. x_weight.py gains twitter-text's transformedURLLength rule: each http(s):// URL weighs 23 whatever its length, and trailing . , ! ? ; : ) ] ' quote stay outside it. Emoji and code-point weights apply to the text between URLs (plain_weight). Seven new self-test cases make 21/21. Positive control: the old rule weighs the same three URL cases 61, 11 and 16, the new one 27, 23 and 25. Still NOT covered: bare domains ('example.com' weighs 11 here, 23 on X, since twitter-text links them through its TLD list). x's own posts defuse dots (defused_dot), so only a human-typed bare domain would hit it. apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines the file up to its __main__ block. A simulated inline into the staged stub.py compiles (py_compile 0), and the new names (plain_weight, URL, TRANSFORMED_URL_LENGTH) don't clash: stub.py's 'url' is a lowercase local.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_5 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:10Z", "x", "10-24 03:10 x: n4 CORRECTED by a cross-check against x's own weighting (X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's Spiral cases agree with the oracle on every shared case). The 00:05 URL regex matched DEFUSED links: x writes dots in posts as ZWSP + '.' (defused_dot), Python's \\s doesn't match U+200B, so 'https://x<ZWSP>.co/abc' was weighed 23, while X doesn't link it (a host containing U+200B isn't a valid domain) and counts its characters. The regex now requires a real host (label(.label)+ over [a-z0-9-], case-insensitive) before an optional path. Two new defused cases make 23/23. Positive control: URL.search finds 'https://x.co/abc' but not its defused form. The simulated inline into the staged stub.py still compiles.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_6 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_7 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_8 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:0x x: e2e evidence; Done waits on a fully green verify. Weighted stub limit in HEAD since 6ef9c48; the README step xc post ('<CJK>' * 141) matched its expected 400 refusal ('same', 1 request) in node verify job-1792988648856-x-verify-remote9 among 36/36 tutorial steps (the node log prints the CJK as '?', an encoding artifact of the log only).")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_9 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:53Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl eoie_job_cells_gate () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", "eoie job start waits, instead of failing, while another team's Cells notebook run is active (a 'cells' slot or --wait-free <process pattern>), so agents stop hand-rolling busy loops around run_notebook.ps1", Story, P2, D3, 20u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs,cells,friction", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_cells_gate () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:52 x: friction measured today. To keep my Cells checks off other teams' runs I wrote three ad-hoc gates: cells_when_free.ps1 (poll every 60 s, give up after N min), cells_chain.ps1 (poll, retry on refusal, and a named mutex after two of my own chains started in the same second at 09:18:40-41), and cells_check.ps1, which refuses with exit 75 when a run_notebook.ps1 outside its own process tree is alive (the 07:52 overlap with eoie's notebook check is why). eoie job start --compile does not know about Cells runs started outside eoie jobs (tibia's and main's run_notebook calls), so a queued job either overlaps them or fails with 75 and stops the --after-ok chain. Wanted: job start waits in its queue while a run_notebook.ps1 / spiral.notebook process not in its own tree is alive (re-checked on each slot grant), shows 'waiting: foreign Cells run pid N (label)' in job status and machine watch, and has a --slot-within deadline as today. Then every agent's Cells check is one 'eoie job start --compile' call, and these scripts are deleted.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T15:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:57 x: measured cost today. I queued 21 compile-class jobs right after tibia's 11:22 block end, each running a Cells notebook through a foreign-run refusal (exit 75). eoie admitted them while other teams' run_notebook.ps1 were alive (eoie's own f6 scratch notebooks among them), so 19 of 21 finished as exit 75 within ~30 min without testing anything. Only the non-Cells xc compile and one early prelude run did real work. Workaround now: a detached self-gating chain (poll until no foreign runner, a named mutex, retry on 75), i.e. the hand-rolled gate this item asks eoie to own. With the requested wait-in-queue behaviour, the 21 jobs would have run in sequence with no resubmission.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "eoie", "14:21 eoie: design chosen and pure model drafted (scratch f6/src/eoie_wait_free; typecheck queued behind main's matrix). Of the item's two options, a box-wide exclusive 'cells' window class would serialize every team's notebook runs, which is a policy change for main and tibia. '--wait-free <pattern,...>' is opt-in and generic, so it comes first: job start writes the patterns to the job file; the supervisor's gate samples processes (eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo rows) every 5 s and stays queued while a process OUTSIDE the job's own tree has a command line containing any pattern; job status shows 'waiting free of a foreign run pid=N (command)' (with EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR's waiting_for); --slot-within keeps its deadline. Model: wait_free_state_of (rows, own root pid, patterns) -> MachineFree | ForeignRun (pid, command), with tree membership by bounded parent walk; tests: a foreign run_notebook.ps1 holds the job, the job's own run never does, comma pattern lists, and empty patterns never wait. Then x's three scripts become 'eoie job start --compile --wait-free run_notebook.ps1,spiral.notebook <label> ...'. Placement: the flag goes into K's typed start-option fold, so this lands after K. Today's cost, from x: 19 of 21 queued Cells jobs ended exit 75 without testing anything.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl eoie_job_rechain () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", "one-call re-chaining of queued jobs: eoie job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok] re-points a queued job's --after (label, timeout, gate and command kept; refused for a running job), and eoie job chain <after-id> -- <label> <timeout> <program> [args] -- <label> ... starts N jobs each after the previous one and prints every id", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_rechain () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:13Z", "eoie", "10:13 from main (10:1x): re-chaining queued jobs costs a stop and a fresh start per job, plus relaunching every job behind it; done 7-8 times on 10-23 (around HP, tibia's block, serializing a chain), each time retyping program and args and re-reading new ids. Builds on job start --after/--after-ok (EOIE-JOBS note 31). Rule: every verb one call (EOIE-ONE-CALL). Shape to decide when drafting: re-point by editing the queued job's after file under the jobs home vs stop+restart with the same command record; the first keeps the id, which is what callers hold.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:34Z", "eoie", "10:34 eoie: part 1 drafted, scratch job-rechain/apply-job-rechain.ps1 (dry run clean on copies). eoie job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok] rewrites the waiting job's after file in place, so the id main holds stays valid. Refusals in a fixed order: unknown job, unknown predecessor, self, not started with --after, no longer waiting, and a cycle (the predecessor's own after chain, walked up to 64 steps, leads back to the job). The waiter (eoie_job_after) re-read nothing before: it read the after file once. It now re-reads every 250 ms beat, and the ok-mode decision uses the predecessor that actually settled; the file is migrated off its two host macros onto lib split/format (AGENTS.md: touching a legacy function migrates it). Model: typed facts record -> rechain_outcome union (HostFree crate eoie_job_rechain, model tests). CLI test in stop_cli: re-point a job from a 20 s predecessor to a 0.5 s one with --ok, finishes within 15 s while the old predecessor still runs, plus the cycle, not-chained, unknown and settled refusals. Part 2 (job chain) not drafted yet. Checks wait for tibia's block.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:38Z", "eoie", "10:38 eoie: part 2 drafted in the same slice. eoie job chain [--ok] [<after-id>] -- <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...] -- ... parses into a typed chain_plan (head: optional --ok and starting job id; links split at --, each needs label, timeout, program) and starts each link as job start --after(-ok) <previous id> through the running binary (lib gap env.current_executable added to lib-gaps), so the launch logic stays in one place and --heavy/--compile inside a link keep working. It prints each start receipt, then 'eoie job chain ok jobs=N ids=a,b', and stops at the first link that does not start, naming the ids started before it. Model test covers head, links, short link, refusals and id parsing; CLI test chains two jobs with --ok and waits on the second. Checks queued with the other f6 drafts for after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): JOB-2 (chains that stop on the first failing prerequisite; per-stage receipts go to EOIE-BUILD-PHASES). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 5u32, "2026-10-26T06:58Z", "eoie", "02:58 eoie: stop_cli's job-ordering tests flake under load. In the RESUME release (02:5x, box saturated, 20 of 53 notebooks after 82 min) after_repoints_a_waiting_job_and_refuses_cycles_and_settled_jobs failed, although it passed in the SLATE and FASTRENAME candidate runs. All four of these tests fail on the installed binary (oldbinary7 run): chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one, after_repoints_a_waiting_job_and_refuses_cycles_and_settled_jobs, after_waits_for_the_named_job_and_after_ok_skips_when_it_failed, a_queued_job_takes_a_new_limit_until_its_child_starts. So by the release gate's pre-registered rule (a row only for a test that also fails on the installed binary) all four are now known-failure rows, added before the gate step reads the table, so a load flake can't fail an otherwise good release. Owed: make them deterministic. They assert on job-state transitions within fixed waits, so under contention a supervisor starts late. Wait on the state, not on a clock, then remove the rows.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 6u32, "2026-10-26T08:08Z", "eoie", "04:08 eoie: the stop_cli timing flakes (note 5), drafted deterministic: kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\stop-cli-deterministic.do, 5 replacements, test-only notebook (not a crate module, so no regen). Each change only removes a race; no assertion changes. (1) started_tree: the sleeper's job timeout 60 s to 600 s (it is always stopped at the end of its test) and the child-beat wait 20 s to 60 s, so a_queued_job_takes_a_new_limit_until_its_child_starts no longer loses its running predecessor to a timeout before amend runs. (2) after_waits_for_the_named_job...: first sleeps 30 s instead of 8 s (second must still be queued at the first status check, which several job calls can delay under load) with a 120 s timeout, and the wait on second gets 120 s. (3) after_repoints...: slow sleeps 600 s with a 600 s timeout (stopped at the end), so 'slow is still running' holds at any load, and the wait on the repointed job gets 60 s. chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one is not a timing flake (the id that chain prints is unknown to job wait), so its row stays until that bug is fixed. Lands with the post-rename batch (clean\\respell\\land-post-rename.ps1); then a stop_cli run on the loaded node is the proof, and the three timing rows leave the known-failures table.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 7u32, "2026-10-26T08:12Z", "eoie", "04:12 eoie: chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one is a TEST HARNESS bug, not a product bug. Direct run of the candidate kit.exe on the worker node with a scratch jobs home: job chain --ok with two links printed both receipts and 'kit job chain ok jobs=2 ids=job-...-one,job-...-two'; both job wait calls returned state=finished exit=0. The test takes the second id with word_after chained ',' and kit_cli_check/cli.spi's word_after splits the rest only at a space (char 32). The ids are the last thing on the line, so the id kept the trailing newline, and job wait looked up 'job-...-two' plus a line ending: unknown job id (invisible in the printed message, and consistent rather than flaky). Every other caller (id=, at=, elapsed_ms=, supervisor_pid=) reads a value followed by a space, which is why only this test failed. Fix drafted: kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\word-after-whitespace.do (split at any whitespace); kit_cli_check is a notebook-only harness package (no Cargo.toml), so no regen. With it and stop-cli-deterministic.do, all four stop_cli known-failure rows can leave once a node run confirms.")
inl lib_listm_runtime_paths () = Task ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", "lib/spiral listm': seq.memoize calls the static try_find on a runtime list (should be try_find_); map4 has no runtime variant; optionally give the static/runtime pairs exists'-style auto-switching so the wrong pick can't happen", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths () = Planning ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl lua_single_scrutinee () = Task ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", "Lua union matches read a single scrutinee directly instead of wrapping it in a fresh table per match (557 of 559 __v tables over the tree main.lua)", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lua,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lua_single_scrutinee () = Planning ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", Minutes 30u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl c_nullary_case_singleton () = Task ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", "C heap unions build each nullary case (UH0_Nil()) by malloc + refcount instead of sharing one static immortal object", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,c,codegen,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_nullary_case_singleton () = Planning ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_1 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:30 main: found reading samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c. UH0 * UH0_Nil() { malloc; tag = 1; refc = 1 }, so every list end and every payload-free case pays a malloc and a free through UHDecref0. A census of the tree main.c files finds 46 nullary heap-union constructors in 19 files. The design must keep the decref path branch-light: (a) a static object per nullary case with an immortal refcount, where UHDecref0 never frees an object whose refc is above a sentinel bound, so the static's refc starts at INT32_MAX / 2 and increments wrap safely below the sentinel; or (b) the tag lives in the pointer for nullary cases (a tagged-pointer null per case), which changes every switch on x->tag. Rule, written before measuring: pick (a) unless the benchmark shows decref overhead above 2% on a refcount-heavy sample (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2); adopt only if allocation-heavy samples speed up by 5% or more and every C row stays bit-identical in exit and stdout. Same question for Zig, which allocates per box.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_2 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:58 main: option (a) drafted, %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\c-nullary-singleton.ps1 (1 anchor in union_tmpl's heap constructor), composed as pack-next9 on next8 (3957189B) -> core 86448170CAD44C75, hopacsota lint G1/G2 pass. A nullary UH case emits 'static UHi x = { .refc = INT32_MAX/2, .tag = t }; return &x;'. No decref sentinel is needed: C refcounts are single-threaded and balanced, so the static's count cannot reach 0; the code read found no refc == 1 reuse path and free only inside UHDecref. Pending (CPU, after tibia's block): fsc --parseonly, then the pre-written rule from the 10:30 note (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt on >=5% on allocation-heavy samples with every C row bit-identical). Zig question still open.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_3 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:42 main: mirror survey across backends on samples/native_closure_union_rec. Rust already shares nullary cases (a thread_local Rc, cloned), and F#, Lean, Gleam and Python use plain constructors or values. Swift's case is a struct, so no allocation. Kotlin (class UH0_Nil instantiated per use) and Lua (function Uh0_Nil() return { tag = ... } end, a new table per call) allocate per nullary case like C did. Mirror drafted as pack-next10 on next9: %TEMP%\\pack-next10\\nullary-mirrors.ps1 has 4 anchors, each matching once on the tree and next9. Kotlin: object instead of class, and the zero and box sites name the object without (). Lua: the heap case becomes NAME = (function() local shared = {...} return function() return shared end end)(), so no new chunk-level local, staying under Lua 5.1's 200-local limit, and the call sites are unchanged. Lua stack unions (Us) and TypeScript are left as they are: whether anything mutates or compares those tables or objects by identity is unverified. Review rule (next10_rules.py, checked with positive and negative controls): a changed main.kt or main.lua must equal its snapshot with exactly these rewrites applied, no other file changes, and native results and DISAGREE 0 as baseline. Compose and review wait for the next9 review.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_4 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 4u32, "2026-10-23T23:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:48 main: Wasm added to the pack-next10 mirror. Its bump allocator never frees, so a nullary case allocated per use (alloc 8, store the tag) was also an unbounded leak in loops. Now each distinct tag gets one 8-byte static data segment [tag][pad], interned like string literals at data_end before heap_start is computed. Objects with the same tag are byte-identical and immutable, so tag alone is the key across unions. Boxing a nullary case returns (i32.const at). The patch has 6 anchors (Kotlin 3, Lua 1, Wasm 2), each matching once on the tree. The patched F# was read on a scratch copy: the arm is if/else with the allocation path under else, and nullary_object sits after intern. The rule for main.wat is that allocation calls must not increase, with the matrix's native check (DISAGREE 0) deciding.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_5 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:51 main: Delphi added to pack-next10 (7 anchors now). Its nullary heap constructor did Result := TUH0.Create per call, and none of the 412 generated main.pas files frees anything, so every Nil leaked an object. Now: var <case>_shared: TUHn = nil; plus a constructor that creates it on first use and returns it. FPC/Delphi allow an initialized global var between routines. Stack unions (TUS, records) are unchanged. next10_rules.py predicts the main.pas rewrite exactly and was checked on the real sample text (1 file within the rule, 0 violations). Order: next10's review compares against the installed outputs, so it runs only after next9 is reviewed and installed; review.ps1 and compose.ps1 are in %TEMP%\\pack-next10.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_6 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:00 main: TypeScript added to pack-next10 (8 anchors). It was held back on an identity risk, now settled by a code read: TS's EQ lowering (a === b, core 174081) serves primitives only. Union equality reaches codegen as a multi-scrutinee unbox comparing tags (v{a}.tag === v{b}.tag at 173971), so no union object is ever compared by identity. A nullary case now emits const <case>_shared: U = { tag: i }; and the function returns it; the types are readonly already. next10_rules.py predicts the main.ts rewrite and was checked on the real sample (1 file within the rule). Python is skipped on purpose: a fieldless NamedTuple per call is cheap, and Python is the slowest target anyway. Swift's nullary case is a struct (no allocation), and Rust already shares a thread_local Rc.")
inl x_per_account_min_1 () = Task ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", "x client: xc timeline --per-account accepts 1..100 (minimum 1 instead of 5); below 5 it still asks X for 5 (the API floor) and keeps only the newest N per account", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,client,cli", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_per_account_min_1 () = Planning ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_1 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:22 x: user request 2026-10-23 ~11:58: 'make --per-account have a minimum of 1 instead of 5'. Today cli.spi validates 5..100 (usage line :81 '5..100 (default 5)'; the test at :422 per_account_below_the_api_minimum_is_rejected expects 'rejected: --per-account expects a number from 5 to 100, got 4'), because X's GET /2/users/:id/tweets takes max_results 5..100, and args.spi:38 passes per_account straight through as max_results. DESIGN: (1) cli accepts 1..100, with the usage line and rejection text saying 1 to 100; (2) the request asks for max(5, N) (a wire-side constant named for the API floor); (3) after fetching, each account's posts are cut to its newest N before merging, so --limit and --per-author keep their meaning; (4) tests: the cli test pins 1 accepted and 0 rejected, and a unit test checks the per-account cut (N=1 out of 5 fetched keeps the newest). e2e gets one tutorial step, 'xc timeline --per-account 1 alice bob', showing one post each. COST NOTE for the README line: below 5, X still bills the 5 posts it returns, so --per-account 1 trims what is shown but doesn't cut paid reads (X-READ-BUDGET's cost line would show it). Lands after X-DONE with the other client drafts; the draft and verification go on a scratch copy while the tree is staged.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_2 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:30 x: drafted as C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_per_account_min_1.ps1 (in land_all and apply_all_check: 12 of 12 drafts apply together, no new name clashes). cli: range 1..100, the usage line says 'posts shown per account, 1..100 (default 5; X sends at least 5)', and the test now rejects 0 and accepts 1 ('accounts alice per=1 ...'). wire: requested_posts n = max(timeline_floor () = 5, n) in posts_path, plus a test that n=1 asks for max_results=5. fetch: each primary entry carries 'keep' = N, and refs are taken only from the kept posts, so a smaller N also means fewer reference lookups. digest: kept_posts cuts each account's primary posts to the first N (X and the e2e stub both return newest first; the stub sorts by created_at descending), and a test checks keep '1' -> 1 of 2, '' -> all. Negative control already in hand: today's stub answers 400 to max_results < 5, so without the floor the e2e step would fail. Cells on cli, wire, fetch, digest queued (self-gating chain8).")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_3 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:27 x: Cells on the scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-permin\\client, x tree + this draft): cli.spi 18/18 PASS, including per_account_takes_one_and_rejects_zero ('0' rejected as 1 to 100, '1' accepted as per=1), and wire.spi 23/23 PASS, including posts_below_the_api_floor_ask_for_five (max_results=5 for N=1). fetch and digest (the keep cut) are next in the same chain.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_4 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:36 x: VERIFIED on the scratch copy: fetch.spi PASS and digest.spi 2/2 PASS (keep_cuts_each_account_to_its_newest: keep '1' gives 1 of 2, '' gives all), alongside cli 18/18 and wire 23/23. Also compiled with all 14 drafts applied together (xc.spi -> 831 lines of Rust, under the 1,000-line gate). Left: landing after the user's commit, then e2e -Accept for the README step (covered by the queued x-landing-trial).")
