inl note_of_x_format_client_1 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:21 x: decision on eoie's question (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS note 4 follow-up): x declares NO LineWidth override. 120 is the formatter's own limit (memory formatter-keep-breaks: it re-lays only what exceeds 120), so x's 136 over-wide client lines are unformatted code, not a different standard. ORDER: after land_all.ps1 -Go, because the 14 drafts are anchored on today's exact text and formatting first would break their anchors (the reformat-peer-repos lesson, applied to my own patch chain). Steps: SpiralCompiler format on each .spi (copy first, read 3 files by hand), byte-compare the generated Rust and Gleam before and after (tibia did that for 15 outputs), then Cells and eoie standards (expect 0 line-width findings, and 0 test-targets findings once eoie's release M ships).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_2 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:43 x: scope update from the 10-23 evening drafts, so the formatting pass covers everything that lands. Post-land scripts touch apps/client args/args.spi, xc/xc.spi, write/write.spi (+ README), wire.spi, thread/thread.spi, paging.spi, prelude.spi. Lib-dependent delegations touch the same files. apply_sim_modules adds 17 sim modules (inbox ... xsim_shell_entry) and apply_xsim_crate adds xsim/xsim.spi. Order: format only after all of them land and pass, then x-compat (XC005) on the formatted cells, because re-laying can move cell weights. Rules from memory formatter-keep-breaks: never join authored lines, re-lay only lines over 120, and apply to unstaged files only.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_3 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "x", "23:08 x grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack at 22:18 (B553EF6B78E6EE29, single-flight): no parens around join heads, 'then' placement follows each file's habit, deep-indent room max 40. Run this item with that compiler (or newer), and compare against a format of the same tree with it, not against earlier 10-23 formats. Still waits for the user to land x's queued drafts.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_4 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: grooming: the condition 'after the drafts land' is MET (HEAD 6ef9c48, user commit 05:20). What's left is the unstaged fix layer: x-compat fixes, chain_json, e2e expectations, plural, and the frames imports. Start when verify-landing5 passes and the user commits that layer. Its anchors are the last chain this pass could break. Use main's newest formatter pack (n3).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_5 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "x", "10-24 23:53 x: measured input for this item. The installed formatter (10:08 build) on x's edition-3 text changes 40 of 44 files and makes 28 cells overweight, mostly records exploded to one field per line and long signatures re-laid with deep indentation. So the format pass here must come with per-cell splits until x-compat and postable are both 0, plus a check that no authored line is joined (0 files lost lines in the measured run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_6 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: MEASURED on a copy (scratch x-simland-0214\\format_measure.ps1; installed 5l'' formatter over apps/client and the new apps/sim/spi). Before: client 35 files / 4,619 lines, 133 lines over 120, 0 cells over one post, x-compat 0; sim/spi 23 files / 4,550 lines, 65 over 120, 0 spilled, x-compat 0. The formatter changes 51 of 58 files. After: client 4,991 lines, 1 over 120, 21 cells over one post (x-compat 21 overweight); sim/spi 4,752 lines, 0 over 120, 8 spilled (x-compat 8). So formatting fixes 197 long lines and creates 29 overweight definitions in 17 files: wire 5, glyphs 3, fields 2, posts 2, assemble 2, personas 2, scenario 2, world 2, and 1 each in api, digest, fetch, locate, refs, token, xc, runner_shell, schedule. Plan, by the formatter-keeps-breaks rule (re-lay only >120): (1) land the formatter result file by file; (2) split each of the 29 by hand into named helpers, as the batch dry-run patch did, gated on x-compat 0 + dry-run 0 spilled + --check per package; (3) glyphs.spi waits for main's job-1792944559767 (it lands the glyphs heal), and the sim/spi files go last, after the parity landing. Size: 29 splits at ~5-10 min each, 3-5 h, light load. || 2026-10-25 17:19 -04:00 x: classified the 29 (scratch classify_overweight.ps1): 5 are RECORD LAYOUT (fields command_fields + filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in), where the formatter puts one field per line (EDITION3-280-SPLIT n2). Those need a formatter record-fill layout (fields filled within 120 columns), which is compiler work x now owns, not hand splits. 24 are code: 16 split by hand on formatted copies (%TEMP%\\fmt-bisect client, %TEMP%\\fmt-measure sim), each with a named helper: wire x5 (post_extras, included_posts, edit_history_in, root_fields, ghost_error), api classified_export, assemble frame_members, digest primary_entry, fetch fetch_filters, locate frame_members, posts loud_posts, refs refs_of + refs_args (the generated Rust is byte-identical, pub fn xc_refs unchanged), token with_tokens, xc credentials_hint, and in sim personas fresh_cast, runner_shell withdrawn_demand, scenario rooted_post + merged_posts, schedule corpus_pacing, world metrics_pair + child_cell. Every split file: x-compat clean except its record-layout def, 0 lines over 120, --check exit 0 (one wrong guess, ghost_error typed json instead of string, was caught by --check and bisected). The 3 glyphs defs wait for job-1792944559767, and gleam_weight goes away with that heal. Tooling gap seen: '--check' rejection prints only 'Package typecheck rejected' with no error, so finding the failing definition took a per-edit bisect; worth an eoie or compiler item (print the first type errors). Landing: after node verify5b, copy the 46 formatted files that carry no record-layout def into the tree and verify again; fields, assemble, posts, personas and glyphs wait for the record-fill layout. || 2026-10-25 17:45 -04:00 x: RECORD FILL drafted and measured (scratchpad\\record-fill\\apply.ps1, compiler formatter). layoutFields packs consecutive simple fields onto one line, separated by '; ', within the width; a field the author started on a new line still starts one (authoredBreaks), so no authored line is joined; fields whose body needs its own layout keep their lines; the formatter's parse guard still falls back to as-written if a layout changes meaning. Proof 2 on the node (job-1792964325355, candidate = tree core + patch, installed vs candidate on the same x copy): overweight client 28 -> 22, sim 8 -> 5, only 12 files differ. Fixed: assemble.frame_of, fields.filter_fields, posts.at, personas.acted_in. NOT fixed: fields.command_fields, a record TYPE: type definitions go through a separate path (3 sites near spiral_compiler.fs:181804, 181811, 181891) that maps each field to its own line. The patch now routes those 3 through layoutFields as well; proof 3 = job-1792964722843. With all 5 records fixed, the 16 hand splits plus the record fill leave only glyphs (main's landing) before x formats cleanly. || 2026-10-25 17:57 -04:00 x: DECIDED by main on FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (rules on record, user 10-20 n1): record fill as a default is rejected (the user corpus template is one field per line at +4, no ';', 224/0), and record TYPES are one field per line by design. Record VALUES keep their authored breaks. So x fixes it at the source: the 4 over-120 record values (fields.filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in) are re-authored as 2 lines within 120 (scratch rewrap.ps1, applied to the tree; x-compat 0 in client and sim/spi). The installed formatter keeps those lines; it only normalizes brace spacing and the +4 continuation, and none is overweight after formatting. Left: fields.command_fields, a 15-field record TYPE, 376 > 273 after formatting by design. It needs a structural change: split it into two record types or nest a sub-record (it carries the CLI parse fields; check its consumers: fields.spi helpers, args, the xc_args export). That's its own small design step before fields.spi can be formatted. The record-fill patch stays as evidence only (4 of 5 fit). || 2026-10-25 18:07 -04:00 x: command_fields SPLIT, applied to the tree (scratch cfsplit.ps1): command_fields = {kind; payload; selection : selection_fields; window : window_fields}, with selection_fields (following, usernames, max_results, limit, per_author, cap) and window_fields (since, until, no_replies, no_reposts, depth, hints, json), written in the canonical one-field-per-line form; blank, source_fields, counts_fields, filter_fields and output_fields go through per-sub-record helpers (record updates on a bound variable); 2 args tests now read f.selection.usernames, f.window.since and f.window.no_replies/no_reposts. Because Spiral exports flatten a record into its leaves in declaration order, the boundary is unchanged: pub fn xc_parse_os_args and xc_timeline_fields keep the same flat 15-tuple (byte-identical signatures), and the xc crate's generated Rust is byte-identical. Checked: --check exit 0; fields.spi 0 lines over 120; formatted with the installed formatter, 0 overweight and 0 long lines; x-compat client 0. So all 5 record definitions are now within 280 after formatting. Landing the format: re-run the formatter on the tree files, then apply the 16 code splits as anchored edits against the formatted text (the scratch copies are older than the tree for fields, posts, assemble and personas). || 2026-10-25 19:37 -04:00 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x (unstaged): the batch dry-run patch (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS M1) and the formatting of 57 files (installed 5l'' formatter, glyphs.spi skipped) plus 23 anchored hand splits (scratch format_land.ps1, rehearsed stacked twice on copies). After landing: x-compat 0 in apps/client (35 files) and apps/sim/spi (23); --check exit 0 for xc and scenario_checks; the only lines over 120 are glyphs.spi's 9. verify7b on the node (pre-format tree) showed e2e 36/36 + 5 extra after accepting the one stale tutorial output (xc thread 3000001, the respelled prelude), postable 1301 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled, and the client notebooks green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF). The README accept is copied into the tree (3 lines, the respelled prelude in the thread output). The formatting changes prelude/posts text again, which the sim worlds embed, so verify8 (job-1792971438120) runs the sim build -Accept, then e2e -Accept, then both full builds; the accepted fixture and README diffs come back for review before they enter the tree. Node gaps reported to kit2 and fixed on the way: rename-folded moves, empty dirs, mirror deps, the stale compiler; the node's spiral rust runner is missing, so Cells rust-with-deps cells are 'unsupported' there (oauth1 thread_run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_7 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T04:23Z", "x", "2026-10-26 00:23 -04:00 x: verify8 on the node (job-1792971438120, after the format + batch landing): sim -Accept regenerated 11 fixtures; e2e -Accept updated 3 of 36 tutorial outputs (the new 'post-thread --dry-run FILE...' usage line in xc --help and in the --per-account usage refusal, plus the thread output of the formatted prelude); then e2e 36/36 + 5 extra; postable 1325 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled; x-compat 0. Reviewed and copied into the tree: the README (only those 3 tutorial blocks beyond my earlier move edits) and the fixtures, which are the formatted sources as the sim renders them (the sim-small program matches the formatted prelude line for line after the notebook header rewrite). Two failures, both fixed or explained. (1) mix test: ':xsim_frames.split_cell/3 is undefined'. The formatter wrapped !!!!Export( so the name sits on the next line, and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 found exports with a per-line regex, so it dropped split_cell from xsim_frames' kept surface (14 of 15). FIXED: it matches '!!!!Export\\(\\s*\"(\\w+)\"' over the whole file (15 of 15); nothing else in x or lib parses exports per line. (2) Cells glyphs rust -d cells: the node's spiral runner rejected --rs-path (stale 2025-03-13 build). It is being rebuilt as node upkeep (job-1792988618221), with verify9 chained after it (job-1792988648856: full client + sim builds in a fresh mirror). Expected result: green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_8 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T09:52Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:52 x: glyphs.spi, the last file, FORMATTED and landed unstaged now that its heal is live (X-CELLS-UNICODE, 05:27): installed formatter, then glyphs_split.ps1 (the two weight-table tests become lists mapped through glyph_weight). Checks on a client copy: 0 lines over 120, x-compat 0 findings, compiles on Rust and Gleam. All x .spi (apps/client + apps/sim/spi) now have 0 lines over 120. Backup: scratch x-simland-0214\\glyphs-before-format-20261026-055150.spi. Done when the next full verify runs glyphs' 8 notebook tests green.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_9 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: DONE by its own condition (the next full verify runs glyphs' notebook tests green): verify24 (job-1793167893768) printed notebook glyphs passed. Since then glyphs.spi changed only its header timeout.")
inl rust_fnmut_capture_move () = Task ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", "Rust codegen: a vec_fold' closure that reuses its seed literal moves a captured variable (E0507 cannot move out of v4, a captured variable in an FnMut closure); regression since 10-22 13:50 (eoie EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES note 20)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 60u32, Paused, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,regression,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move () = Planning ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_1 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T19:38Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:38 main: filed from eoie's report with its repro (session scratch fc1568a6\\f6\\src\\fold_capture_repro\\repro.spi: two rust -d regex tests, named_group/literal_group, both E0507). Hypothesis to test first: the seed '' and the '' inside the closure share one CSE'd variable, which the FnMut closure captures and then moves into optionm'.default_value' instead of cloning. Bisect against the cores installed since 10-22 13:50 (pack-after-night 25D2BE7C is the tree core now); the fix gets the one regression sample (the literal_group shape).")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_2 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T21:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:35 main: narrowing by code read (no build): (1) the E0507 closure is not a Spiral closure. codegenRust emits those as Rc<dyn Fn> with captured variables kept live (closure: live_binds (fun i -> captured.Contains i)), so the FnMut must be the Rust closure written inside lib am'.vec_fold''s macro arm, where a spliced variable is opaque to liveness. (2) live_binds itself is old (git log -S: 76f3989c, 10-06), so it is not the regression. The window is 10-22 13:50 to now, i.e. the pack-after-night install (tree core 25D2BE7C, now 19F5F752 with only the formatter changed since). Bisect next round: compile eoie's repro with the next3 and pack-after-night candidate DLLs (both still in spiral-bin/candidates) and diff the vec_fold' call site's Rust; a plain listm.fold version of the shape compiles fine (scratch fnmut probe), so the macro arm is the place.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_3 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:23Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:2x main: ROOT CAUSE found, and it is lib, not the compiler. A probe package with eoie's literal_group shape, compiled to Rust by night-release (10-21), pack-after-night and the installed compiler, gives byte-identical output (scratch fnmut-bisect), so no compiler change in the window caused it. The generated fold closure reads `let mut v14: Rc<str> = v13.unwrap_or(v3);`: v3 is the fold's seed literal (the inner \"\" default is the same CSE'd value), captured by the FnMut closure, and lib optionm'.default_value''s Rust arm `!x.unwrap_or(!value)` moves it (E0507). Note 1's CSE hypothesis holds. Fix (%TEMP%\\lib-default-clone\\apply.ps1, dry run clean): the Rust arm becomes `!x.unwrap_or_else(|| !value.clone())` (a lazy clone; every Spiral Rust value is Clone), plus the regression test default_value_inside_a_fold_closure_keeps_the_captured_seed in sm'.spi (rust -d regex, eoie's shape). Gate and land after tibia's PR-27 block. It likely surfaced on 10-22 because a lib change began CSE-sharing the literal, not because the arm changed.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_4 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl rust_emit_unit_arg () = Task ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", "A Rust emit snippet given a unit argument keeps its literal $0, so rustc fails (Cells: a print with no value)", Story, P2, D2, 55u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,cells,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_emit_unit_arg () = Planning ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_1 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:19 main: the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs' fails on the tree and in every pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT notes 11, 12, 16): rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0). Root of the literal: inlineRustEmits (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:163964 splitArgs) maps an args text of '()' to no arguments, and the $k evaluator (164012) keeps m.Value when k >= args.Length, so a unit argument leaves $0 as written. Fix A (compiler): splitArgs returns ['()'] for '()', so $0 becomes () (valid Rust; other $k unchanged). Fix A alone does not pass the test: Cells' split_output (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi:226) would read 'SPIRAL_CELLS_VALUE:()' as a value, and the test expects none. Open question B: why does a unit cell reach show_value_main at all? cells.ex compile/12 tries Cell.unit_result? (append 0i32) before value_result? (show_value_main), and unit_pair accepts Got () / Expected i32 with padding skipped. So either the error text no longer matches (restore_type_lines?), or the wrap already emits a show_value main. Rule: B is decided by one probe run after tibia's block (that cell's compile output with attempt numbers). If unit_result? misses, fix its match. In both cases also land A, since any emit snippet given a unit argument hits it.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_2 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:24 main: Fix A staged in pack-next9. Patch: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\rust-emit-unit-arg.ps1 (splitArgs '()' -> ['()']; anchors once on both the tree and next8). compose.ps1 runs it after gadt-index-cap, and next9 now composes to 5124A93187EEF889 (lint G1/G2 clean). Regression sample samples/rust_emit_unit_arg is staged in %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\samples, not in the tree, so review-3 (next4 composite, no fix) never sees it. next9's review.ps1 copies it in after the snapshot (remove it if next9 is rejected) and prints its row: Rust must pass with 42, i.e. a unit's size_of_val is 0. next9_rules.py now accepts a main.rs change only when the old text with $0 replaced by () equals the new.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_3 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: still the only non-lib red in the Cells suite on the tree (cells-suite-tree-2 11:55). Fix A rides in pack 5i (5h is built on pack-next9). Done once 5i is installed and the Cells live test passes on the tree.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_4 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 4u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Root cause found. The core emits `__spiral_emit_rust <args> <code>` placeholders; the HOST post-processes them\nin `rewriteRustEmitExpr` (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:416-491, called at :590 for coreWrites Rust),\nsubstituting `$0,$1,...` in the snippet payload with the args (loop at :484-485).\n\nBug: `splitArgs` (:440-462) has `if value = \"()\" then []` (:442). A UNIT argument renders as Rust `()`, so the\nargs group is `()`, which splitArgs treats as ZERO args -> the substitution loop `for argIndex = args.Length-1 downto 0`\nnever runs -> `$0` stays LITERAL in the emitted Rust -> rustc rejects `$0`. (Cells symptom: \"a print... keeps its literal $0\".)\n\nFix (one line): :442 `if value = \"()\" then []` -> `if value = \"()\" then [\"()\"]`.\nSafe in both cases:\n- unit arg, payload has $0  -> $0 replaced with `()` (correct Rust unit). FIXES IT.\n- genuine no-args, payload has no $0 -> args=[\"()\"] but Replace(\"$0\",\"()\") at :485 is a no-op. HARMLESS.\n\nVerify (needs a compile, deferred under 100% CPU): add/compile a sample with a Rust emit snippet taking a unit\nargument that references $0 (e.g. `$\"do_thing($0)\"  ()`), build -Backend Rust -Native, confirm rustc exits clean and\nthe output has `()` not `$0`. Then -Suite examples -Native stays green (the change only affects the \"()\" args branch).\nCommit the sample's main.rs. Seam: anchored apply + composite hash into pack 5n.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_5 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 5u32, "2026-10-26T05:42Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: a plain `emit ()` unit arg does NOT reproduce the bug on the node single-flight compiler. TDD: added `inl unit_case : i32 = emit () ($'\"{ let _u = $0; 0i32 }\"')` to a copy of rust_emit_tuple_args, compiled -Mode single-flight -Backend Rust on the node (exit 0); generated main.rs = `let v10: i32 = { let _u = (); 0i32 };` -- $0 -> () correctly, NO literal $0, NO __spiral_emit_rust marker anywhere (all three tuple emits also clean). So the core substitutes emit args (incl. a unit arg) directly; the host post-pass rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs:416-491, splitArgs(\"()\")->[] at :442) is NOT exercised by a normal `emit` call. The item real trigger is the specific Cells \"print\" scenario that LEAVES a __spiral_emit_rust marker for the host to rewrite with a unit arg -- need that exact shape to reproduce. The splitArgs(\"()\")->[\"()\"] fix stays correct for the host-rewrite path but is UNVERIFIED until that trigger is found. Next: locate the Cells print/emit that leaves the marker (kept at 55%).")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_6 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 6u32, "2026-10-26T16:47Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (open question from note 5 CLOSED: the Cells marker trigger is located + the host-vs-core distinction explained). Trigger: apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/console.spi.\n- Line 1-2: `:= (~!\\\\) ... = $'__spiral_emit_rust !args !code '` -- `!\\\\` is a MACRO ($'...'), so it emits the LITERAL text `__spiral_emit_rust <args> <code>` into the Rust output; the CORE does not substitute it.\n- Line 4-16: `show_value value` calls `!\\\\(value, <snippet>)` where the snippet (line 7-15) contains `(&SpiralKinoShow(&$0)).spiral_cells_show()` -- i.e. it references $0, and $0 is `value`.\nSo `show_value value` -> emitted marker `__spiral_emit_rust <value-text> <snippet with $0>`, left for the HOST's rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs:416-491) to substitute $0 with the args.\n\nWHY note 5's plain `emit ()` did NOT reproduce but THIS does: a normal core `emit (args)(code)` intrinsic is substituted by the CORE (so a unit arg -> $0 becomes () correctly, no marker survives). Cells' `!\\\\` is a $'__spiral_emit_rust ...' MACRO -> the core emits the marker VERBATIM and the HOST post-pass does the $0 substitution. The host path is `splitArgs` (:440-462) with the `if value = \"()\" then []` bug (:442): a UNIT value renders args group `()`, splitArgs returns [] -> the $0 substitution loop never runs -> literal `$0` survives -> rustc rejects `SpiralKinoShow(&$0)`. That is EXACTLY the \"a print with no value\" Cells live test (note 1: rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0)).\n\nSo: Fix A (host splitArgs \"()\"->[\"()\"], already staged in pack 5i per note 3) is the CORRECT fix for this host-rewrite path; now its trigger + mechanism are pinned. Repro shape = a Cells CELL whose value is unit (e.g. last expr is `console.write_line \"x\"` / a bare `()` -> show_value gets a unit value). It is a CELLS-HARNESS repro (the marker is host-rewritten only in the Cells compile path), NOT a standalone `--backend Rust` sample -- consistent with note 1's gate being the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs', not a matrix sample. Item open question (note 5 \"locate the Cells print/emit that leaves the marker\") = CLOSED. Remaining: 5i install + the Cells live test passes (both build/harness-bound). Open question B from note 1 (why a unit cell reaches show_value_main at all, vs unit_result? appending 0i32) is a separate cells.ex compile/12 ordering question, still needs the probe run.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_7 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 7u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (open question B CLOSED by reading the Cells host). Q: why does a unit cell reach show_value_main vs unit_result? appending 0i32? A: it normally DOESN'T. In apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells.ex compile/13 the recovery cond on a first-attempt compile error of a generated_main cell checks Cell.unit_result? (line ~632-634, retry appending 0i32) BEFORE value_result? (line ~636-638, retry via show_value_main). They are ORDERED, not competing, so a true unit generated_main cell gets 0i32 appended and never reaches show_value_main. The $0 bug is ORTHOGONAL to that routing: show_value DEFAULTS true (cells.ex ~549 `show_value = not prepared.no_value`), so console.spi's SpiralKinoShow show-snippet (!\\(value, ...$0...)) is emitted for any non-:no_value cell, and when that cell's RUNTIME value is unit the host splitArgs(\"()\")->[] drops $0 (the Program.fs:440-462 bug) -> literal $0 survives -> rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0). The compile-retry 0i32 logic only protects generated_main cells caught by unit_result?, NOT every unit value reaching the show-snippet. So pack 5i's splitArgs \"()\"->[\"()\"] is confirmed at the RIGHT layer (host show-path, covers all unit-reaching-show cases). Open question B resolved; remaining = 5i install + the Cells live test \"a print with no value\" passes (build/harness-bound).")
inl lib_gleam_os_arms () = Task ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", "lib/spiral: Gleam arms for portable lib functions BEAM components call (env argv; file_system read/write/append/exists/delete/basename/is_dir; sm'.last_index_of), from a census of 50 portable functions without one", Story, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "lib,backends,gleam", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_gleam_os_arms () = Planning ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_1 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:27 x: found while building Gleam components for BACKEND-BALANCE. The sim's OTP shell, memory_watch and a future Gleam xsim entry (needed for xsim diff/thread_run, which call Cells' evaluate on the BEAM) all hit these five functions having no Gleam arm (Rust/F#/Zig/TS only). memory_watch works around it with an injected thunk. Previously only a mention in X-BACKEND-MAP n9, which main read as already tracked; this item is the record. x will draft the arms in x-world/lib-draft for main to land, as with today's dictm/unicode handoff.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_2 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:30 x: arms DRAFTED as an anchored patch, C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. env: count = list.length(init:get_plain_arguments()), item i = charlist.to_string of the i-th, or '' past the end (Rust returns unwrap_or_default ''). file_system: read_all_text = file:read_file + bit_array.to_string, with let assert (panics on error like Rust's unwrap); write_all_text = file:write_file, asserting ok; file_exists = filelib:is_regular (an existing regular file, the same as Rust's exists && is_file). Helpers are env_gleam_argv () and file_system_gleam_files () (global blocks, as process.spi does). Dry-applied to a lib copy: 5 arms, every anchor unique. Proof set up on a PATCHED lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\lib, real folder, no junction), check package %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\check: write/exists/read round trip + argv. The same test is also run on the LIVE lib (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control) as the positive control, which must fail at the missing Gleam arm. Both queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_3 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:30 x: fixed before any run. The draft put file_system_gleam_files () before read_all_text (~634), but file_exists (572) and write_all_text (611) call it too, and Spiral resolves top-down, so those arms would not have compiled. The helper now goes before file_exists, anchored with its let line, since file_exists is a prefix of file_exists_content. The proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy was rebuilt from the live lib and re-applied: helper at 571, users at 612/654/678; no new lines over 120 in env.spi or file_system.spi. The check + live-lib control still run after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_4 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:02 x: one more missing Gleam arm, same theme (found while making memory_watch incremental): sm'.last_index_of has Zig/Rust arms and an F#-style default (.LastIndexOf), so on Gleam it fails. Draft C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_last_index.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds a Gleam arm returning a BYTE offset, like sm'.index_of's Gleam arm (bit_array.byte_size of the head) and Rust's rfind. It uses Erlang string:find(S, Search, trailing): total bytes minus the suffix's, -1 on nomatch, and the length for an empty search. That's also right for overlapping patterns ('aa' in 'aaa' = 1), where binary:matches' non-overlapping scan would give 0. Applied to the OS-arms proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; the check package gains last_index_of_gives_the_last_byte_offset_on_the_beam (abcb/b = 3, aaa/aa = 1, z = -1, '' = 4), and the live-lib control must fail it.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_5 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:05 x: CENSUS of lib functions with backend arms but no Gleam arm (core modules, Rust-only signatures filtered out): 50 with portable signatures (string/i32/bool/list), mostly file_system (25), env (6), sm' (5: line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at, nonempty_line_at, last_index_of), console colors, date_time, process.try_capture_combined_list. A BEAM component that calls one fails at runtime. Second batch DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms2.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds Gleam arms to get_file_name (filename:basename), directory_exists (filelib:is_dir), file_delete (file:delete, errors ignored like Rust's traced path) and append_all_text (file:write_file with [append], asserting ok like Rust's unwrap). It has its own helper before get_file_name (line 414), because those functions come before the first batch's helper. Skipped get_extension on purpose: its backends already disagree (Rust gives 'spi' and panics without an extension; F# gives '.spi'), so a Gleam arm would have to pick a contract; that needs a decision first. Applied to %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batch 1 + last_index_of: helper 414, users 457/534/827/1386, no new lines over 120. Check test paths_append_and_delete_on_the_beam added (live-lib control must fail).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_6 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:07 x: batch 4 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_line_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It adds Gleam arms to sm'.line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at and nonempty_line_at, whose only arm was Rust's .lines(), with the same semantics: split on '\\n', strip one trailing '\\r' per line, no final empty line after a trailing newline, 0 lines for ''. The helper lines_gleam () sits before line_count (line_count has no heading, so the anchor is its inl line). Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batches 1-3 (helper 683, users from 712). Check test line_helpers_follow_rust_lines_on_the_beam: 'a\\r\\nb\\n' has 2 lines and line 1 = 'b'; '' has 0; nonempty in 'a\\n\\nb' = 2 and line 1 = 'b'.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_7 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T00:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:08 x: batch 5 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_try_capture.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It gives process.try_capture_combined_list a Gleam arm built from process.spi's own port helpers: a missing program gives None (os:find_executable is checked first, because open_port with 'false' would crash, while Rust's output() returns Err), a non-zero exit gives None, and otherwise stdout + stderr (stderr_to_stdout). One known difference: invalid UTF-8 output gives None on Gleam where Rust gives lossy text. Risk stated: the arm returns a Gleam #(Bool, String) typed as Spiral bool * string, which assumes Spiral pairs are native Gleam tuples; the check settles it (if not, switch to a nominal + accessors like x's cells_run). Use case: memory_watch's sampler can run a sampling command per tick on the BEAM. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; check test: a missing program is None.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_8 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T00:10Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:10 x: batch 6 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_stat_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. get_current_directory gets file:get_cwd (asserting ok like Rust's unwrap), and last_write_ms gets file:read_file_info with posix time, mtime x 1000 and 0 when missing (Rust's unwrap_or(0)). Two differences, recorded: Erlang's cwd uses forward slashes on Windows ('c:/...') where Rust prints backslashes, and the mtime is whole seconds where Rust has milliseconds; both are fine for change detection, not for path equality across backends. Skipped on purpose: get_full_path (Rust canonicalize adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix; the contract needs a decision) and get_temp_path (OS-specific env-var rules). Helper file_system_gleam_stat () sits before get_current_directory (494; users 540, 1396). Check test: cwd non-empty, mtime > 0 for a fresh file, 0 for a missing one. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy with batches 1-5.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_9 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:13Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:13 main: get_extension contract decided by rule (x asked). The arms disagree (Rust Path::extension -> 'spi' and a panic when there is none through optionm'.unwrap; F# Path.GetExtension -> '.spi' or ''), and the function has 0 callers in lib/spiral, eoie/src and x/apps, so it can be fixed without breaking anyone. Rule: total (no panic) and the majority of standard libraries (Python os.path.splitext, Node path.extname, Go filepath.Ext and .NET all return the leading dot, and '' when there is no extension; Rust is the outlier). Contract: '.spi', or '' for no extension. So the Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) and the Gleam arm follows the same contract, with a test over 'a/b.spi' -> '.spi', 'a/b' -> '' and 'a.b/c' -> '' (a dot in a directory name only).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_10 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:15 x: get_extension folded into batch 2, per main's 20:1x decision (it has no callers in lib, eoie or x; Python, Node, Go and .NET all give the leading dot or ''). The Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) instead of unwrap (which panicked); the Gleam arm is filename:extension of the basename with one leading dot stripped, because Erlang gives '.bashrc' for a dotfile where Rust gives None. Test on rust + gleam: a/b.spi -> .spi, a/b -> '', a.b/c -> '', .bashrc -> ''. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt from the live lib with all six batches in order: all applied, no new lines over 120. That makes 18 functions covered; get_full_path and get_temp_path still need a contract decision.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_11 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:17 x: get_extension's F# arm aligned too (main's follow-up): an explicit Fsharp arm returns empty for a dotfile (the file name starts with a dot and has no later dot; IndexOf with a string, ordinal), else Path.GetExtension, which by default gives .bashrc. The string overload avoids a quoted char literal, which would close the Spiral macro string early. The test targets rust, gleam and fsharp, so the three arms are pinned together. Proof copy rebuilt with all six batches; no new long lines.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_12 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:22 x: analysis for the two undecided contracts (main decides; evidence from this machine). Both have callers only inside file_system.spi (6 and 8 hits, mostly its own tests), so a contract change is cheap, as with get_extension. (1) get_temp_path: .NET GetTempPath returns WITH a trailing separator (C:\\Users\\...\\Temp\\); Rust std::env::temp_dir follows GetTempPath2 on Windows and TMPDIR or /tmp elsewhere. Proposal: return the directory without a trailing separator on every backend (trim it in the F# arm), and for Gleam look up os:getenv TMPDIR, then TEMP, then TMP, falling back to /tmp. (2) get_full_path: today the Rust arm canonicalizes existing paths, which requires existence, resolves symlinks and adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix, and normalizes lexically only for missing paths; .NET GetFullPath is purely lexical (x/../y -> <cwd>\\y, no filesystem access). Proposal: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\), i.e. the .NET behavior, the same for existing and missing paths. Gleam: filename:absname, then fold the segments to drop . and resolve .. . Either way, a test pinning x/../y and a missing path on all arms.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_13 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:23 main: get_temp_path and get_full_path decided by the get_extension rule (total; the majority of standard libraries; callers only inside file_system.spi). (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend. Python tempfile.gettempdir, Node os.tmpdir and Go os.TempDir omit it; .NET GetTempPath is the outlier. Lookup order on Gleam: TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP, then /tmp (Python's order), so the F# arm trims the trailing separator. (2) get_full_path: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\ prefix), as Python os.path.abspath, Node path.resolve, Go filepath.Abs and .NET GetFullPath are. Rust's canonicalize is the outlier and fails on missing paths. Test: x/../y and a missing path, on rust, gleam and fsharp.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_14 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 14u32, "2026-10-24T00:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:26 x: batch 7 DRAFTED per main's 20:2x decisions: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_path_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend (new pure without_trailing_separator, keeping a lone root); Rust and F# trim, and Gleam looks up TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP (empty counts as unset), then /tmp (Python's order). (2) get_full_path: purely lexical. The Rust arm drops its canonicalize branch and always takes its existing lexical branch (extracted programmatically from the live text and dedented, refusing if the arm's shape drifted). </> is PathBuf::join, so absolute inputs still replace cwd. The Gleam arm is filename:absname, then a fold dropping . and empty segments and resolving .. ; F# keeps GetFullPath (already lexical). Recorded difference: Gleam joins with / on Windows, like get_current_directory. Test on rust + gleam + fsharp: x/../y ends in y with no .., a missing path ends in its name, and the temp path has no trailing separator. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt with all 7 batches in order: all applied; helpers precede users (env 408 < temp 435; paths 450 < full_path 1091); no new lines over 120. 20 functions covered now.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_15 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 15u32, "2026-10-24T01:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:11 x: CORRECTION by user direction (21:0x): \"why we're delegating to main lib work? makes no sense, one of my goals of inventing eoie is the atomic batched patches, so all lanes can go ham, also with automatic backups etc\". The earlier note here saying \"Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main\" was my own unexamined assumption, not a rule. From now on x lands its own lib changes through eoie do atomic batched edits (expect counts, all-or-nothing, sha256 per file) plus the lib Cells gate. Coordination with other lanes is about order and live-tree reads only, not ownership. x takes over landing of its own drafts: the x landing patch (crypto/sm'/listm'/resultm + oauth + cursor_walk) and LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's 7 batches. Main keeps LIB-GAPS-X, which x's patch builds on (take_).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_16 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 16u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "x", "23:06 x: batches 1-7 are LIVE in lib/spiral since 22:06:07 (inside x part A's single eoie do batch; see X-GENERIC-TO-LIB n23). Gate on the new compiler: oauth 2/2 and cursor_walk 2/2 pass (the first oauth timeout was contention); x_lib_check 2/2; gleam_os_check 8/9. The open failure is the Gleam target of full_path_is_lexical_and_temp_path_has_no_trailing_separator: gleam build stops at 'Unknown module gleam/regexp'. Only sm'.replace_regex imports gleam/regexp (its Gleam arm), reached through file_system.normalize_path, and statically neither new Gleam arm calls it. Bisecting with two one-call probes (%TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe, job-1792811193870-x-gleam-regexp-bisect). Rules: if the probe that fails (get_full_path or get_temp_path) shows the regexp import, fix that arm with an atomic patch; if neither fails, it's the combined notebook, so add the gleam regexp dependency to the test.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_17 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 17u32, "2026-10-24T03:35Z", "x", "23:35 x: bisect DONE. probe_temp_path passes on Gleam; probe_full_path fails with the regexp import. Cause, by four compile probes in %TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe: the dead Rust arm of get_full_path calls the top-level let functions get_current_directory and normalize_path. Their Gleam-side globals leak into Gleam builds (normalize_path brings sm'.replace_regex's gleam/regexp), while an inl callee in a dead arm doesn't leak. Compiler finding sent to main. Lib fix (programs-normalize-prefix\\land.eoie + undo): normalize_path strips the literal \\\\?\\ prefix with starts_with + substring instead of replace_regex, plus a test normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix (rust, no -d regex). On a patched copy the probe no longer imports regexp. RULE, written before the Cells results: land when (a) gleam_os_check on the patched copy is 9/9, (b) the new test passes, and (c) file_system.spi's failing tests on the patched copy are a subset of those on an unpatched copy with the same layout (x-normalize-base). Otherwise don't land, and fix first.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_18 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 18u32, "2026-10-24T04:09Z", "x", "10-24 00:09 x: side finding from the bisect. lib sm'.replace_regex's own test runs on '///> gleam' with no dependency directive, but its Gleam arm imports gleam/regexp, which Cells' Gleam build doesn't include by default (probe_full_path's failure). Red/green probes queued, job-1792814947532 (%TEMP%\\x-regexp-directive-probe): the same assertion bare vs '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' (base.spi's -d form). RULE, before results: bare fails on regexp and directive passes -> land the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test via the guarded runner, gated on that test. Both pass -> Cells already provides it, so re-examine tonight's failure. Directive fails -> the -d syntax is wrong; read Cells' directive parser before touching lib.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_19 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 19u32, "2026-10-24T04:59Z", "x", "10-24 00:59 x: ORDERING. main's %TEMP%\\lib-fs-primitives\\apply.ps1 (replace_checked, is_within and find_on_path, appended to file_system.spi; is_within calls normalize_path) waits for the normalize_path land. When x-lib-normalize lands (decide3 job-1792817005040 -> lander), send main a one-liner. main takes the file_system.spi:889 repo-coupled cwd test fix itself (not folded into mine, so my program stays byte-identical to the proof).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_20 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 20u32, "2026-10-24T06:08Z", "x", "10-24 02:08 x: n18's rule applied. The bare probe fails on Gleam with 'Unknown module gleam/regexp' and the same assertion with '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' passes, so landing the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test. It goes via the guarded lander (programs-regexp-directive\\land.eoie + undo, dry-run fits the live lib, job x-regexp-directive-lander), gated on sm'.spi through the mutex + 3.5 GB floor gate. (probe job job-1792817005684-x-regexp-directive-probe3)")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_21 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T06:19Z", "x", "10-24 02:19 x: proof3 result. gleam_os_check is 9/9 on the patched copy (rule a). The patched file_system.spi ran 21 tests and failed 3: find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and trace_file (fsharp). The new normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix test is not among them. PREDICTION, written before the baseline result: all three fail the same way on the unpatched twin. find_parent/_2 walk up from the source or current directory for a 'workspace' folder and README.md, which don't exist above a %TEMP% lib copy, and trace_file writes under get_workspace_root's layout search. None of them reaches the \\\\?\\ prefix branch. If the baseline differs, the decision job's rule (c) refuses the land, and I investigate before any retry.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_22 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T08:47Z", "x", "10-24 04:47 x: TAKING the file_system.spi GRANULAR-HEAL slice at main's request (7,928 violating chars). It's drafted on a lib copy with the normalize_path patch already applied (%TEMP%\\x-fs-heal\\lib), so its anchors match the tree after the normalize land, and it lands after it. The globals with bodies: env (temp_dir_from), paths (lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text), stat (cwd, mtime_ms), files (read_text, write_text), plus walk_dir_filter's poll (Rust), file_exists, and Rust's MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, symlink cfg blocks and mtime. FS-1 (programs-fs-heal-1, 4 replacements) moves the 3 gleam_paths bodies into Spiral over the externs, which stay. gleam_segment_step plus a listm.fold over sm'.split_list of absname gives lexical_full_path; basename, a Spiral leading-dot strip and the extension extern give dotted_extension; append goes through gleam_written + coerce + gleam_atoms_equal, and failwith keeps the old let-assert behavior. Check: gleam_os_check on the copy, job-1792831611429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_23 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T08:54Z", "x", "10-24 04:54 x: FS-1 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792831611429), covering full path, extension and append. FS-2 (programs-fs-heal-2, 10 replacements) is applied to the copy: the shared nominals (gleam_dynamic, gleam_atom, gleam_charlist) move above gleam_env; temp_dir_from becomes a Spiral let rec over one-call getenv/coerce/charlist primitives; cwd and mtime_ms go through resultm.result' + unbox, with the posix-option tuple written as ##( in the macro and * 1000 in Spiral; read_text and write_text go through result' + unbox and the ok-atom check. Its check is job-1792832046429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_24 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 24u32, "2026-10-24T09:01Z", "x", "10-24 05:01 x: FS-2 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792832046429), covering temp path, cwd, mtime and the file round trip. With FS-1 + FS-2 every Gleam global in file_system.spi holds only externs and imports (rule 3): the bodies of temp_dir_from, lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text, cwd, mtime_ms, read_text and write_text are now Spiral over one-call primitives. Ready to land in order after the normalize land (anchored on the post-normalize text): programs-normalize-prefix, then programs-fs-heal-1, then programs-fs-heal-2. Left for FS-3: the F# macros (interpolated messages, REPL let-exports), Rust walk_dir_filter's async filter, the MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, the symlink cfg blocks, and the mtime/append chains.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_25 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 25u32, "2026-10-24T09:02Z", "x", "10-24 05:02 x: land chain queued, all guarded (reader guard + rehearsal on a fresh copy + gleam_os_check on the live lib): normalize lander job-1792828314873 (waits for eoie GE3's live-lib phases) -> --after-ok job-1792832494176-x-fs-heal-1-lander -> --after-ok job-1792832495716-x-fs-heal-2-lander. Each lander's guard also waits for the previous slice's own gate, which reads the lib, so every slice lands on a verified base. Message main at the normalize land (their fs-primitives append follows).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_26 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 26u32, "2026-10-24T09:09Z", "x", "10-24 05:09 x: FS-3 under main's rule readings (GRANULAR-HEAL n5): symlinks use cfg-gated helper fns (same name and signature, one-call bodies); F# REPL export lines are host declarations, so they stay; walk_dir_filter is x's (later, over main's async primitives). FS-3a (programs-fs-heal-3): Rust last_write_ms = metadata/modified/duration_since bridges + ms in Spiral (0 on any error); Rust append_all_text = OpenOptions open + write_all bridges, with failwith keeping the old unwrap panics. Check job-1792832705648. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4, dry run 6/6): directory/file_create_symbolic_link Rust arms call spiral_fs_symlink_dir/_file, cfg-gated globals for windows (symlink_dir/symlink_file) and unix (symlink), behind a rust_link_done bridge + failwith; and the all-string F# interpolations become Spiral ++# (find_parent's message, new_file_uri, trace_file's file name with sm'.obj_to_string guid). Left: read_link's mixed-type messages (ints and exceptions; they need the lib formatter, so not a mechanical swap), trace_file's %A, and walk_dir_filter.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_27 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 27u32, "2026-10-24T09:37Z", "x", "10-24 05:37 x: FS-3a VERIFIED (job-1792832705648). The whole file_system.spi on the healed copy fails exactly the 3 layout tests, the same set as the unpatched baseline (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file); gleam_os_check passes 9/9. Its lander, job-1792834592721, is chained --after-ok the FS-2 lander. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4: symlink cfg helpers + string messages) is applied to the copy; its check is job-1792834585180 (same two notebooks, same baseline rule).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_28 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 28u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: held-files runner (job-1792875049850, exit 0) LANDED normalize-prefix and fs-heal-1..3 on live file_system.spi (18:28; gate %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control\\gleam_os_check.spi passed after each). fs-heal-4 was applied only to the copy %TEMP%\\x-fs-heal-4-recheck-10241828\\spiral: file_system.spi failed 21/21 tests, 0 passed, on every target (rust, fsharp, gleam, zig). That's a whole-file failure, not the baseline subset (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file), so FS-4 stays unlanded. Next (CPU-light first): diff the copy's file_system.spi against live and read the first compile error in that run's per-target output. 21/21 points at a parse/type error the program introduces, not test semantics. main got 'file_system.spi done for this round' for its edition-3 respelling, so FS-4 will be redrafted in edition-3 spelling after main's pass.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_29 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 29u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "x", "10-24 19:46 x: main acked (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n11): listm'/sm' are released from the edition-3 hold, and file_system.spi stays held until tibia frees it. So FS-4 waits on both its 21/21 diagnosis and tibia's release, and gets redrafted in edition-3 spelling then.")
inl eoie_agile_note_stdin () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", "agile note and note-edit read the text from stdin when it is a single dash, so long notes never pass through shell quoting", TaskKind, P2, D1, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,friction", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:31Z", "eoie", "evidence 2026-10-23: three agile note calls from PowerShell failed with 'received 6 operands' because an escaped quote inside a double-quoted argument split the text (pwsh does not treat backslash-quote as an escape), and one more failed in Invoke-Expression; each needed a rewrite into a here-string. Shape: agile note <root> <id> - [--as <author>] reads stdin to EOF, refuses an empty text as today, and the receipt is unchanged; same for note-edit <root> <id> <n> -. A note text that is literally a dash stays possible by quoting it differently, since a lone dash as text has no real use. Test: notes_cli pipes a text with quotes, backslashes and newlines and shows it back byte for byte.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:31Z", "eoie", "18:31 eoie: prerequisite found: lib/spiral has no function that reads the process's own stdin (grep for read_to_end, stdin and read_line across lib: only runtime.spi's child-process stdin writers). eoie do <root> - already reads stdin through a legacy host blob in eoie_do. By lib-first, step 1 is lib console.read_to_end () : string (backend_switch: Rust std::io::read_to_string, plus F#/Gleam arms where they exist, with a lib test), landed in my next lib batch after main lands x's lib_gaps. Step 2 is the agile note/note-edit dash (agile_task_notes and agile_note_edit), and step 3 moves eoie_do's blob onto it.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 3u32, "2026-10-23T22:33Z", "eoie", "18:33 eoie: step 1 drafted as scratch lib-gaps-2/apply-console-read-to-end.ps1. It inserts lib console.read_to_end () : string before console's write section: a Rust arm through std::io::Read into sm'.std_string and sm'.from_std_string, plus F# Console.In.ReadToEnd and Python sys.stdin.read arms. Its test only typechecks the call behind a runtime-false branch, since a test process's stdin may be a terminal. Dry run on a copy is clean and idempotent. Lib gate after tibia's block; landing after main's next lib land (x's lib_gaps), with main told.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 4u32, "2026-10-23T22:35Z", "eoie", "18:35 eoie: step 2 drafted as scratch note-stdin/apply-note-stdin.ps1, on top of release O's note-as-first. note_text_of maps a lone dash to console.read_to_end, trimmed like an argument, at all 4 body sites of note_request_of (refusing if that count changes); note-edit's body gets the same. A guard refuses until lib console.read_to_end exists (control: on the live lib it refused). Dry run on post-O copies applies and is idempotent. Ships in the first release after the lib function lands; its acceptance probe pipes a note with quotes, backslashes and newlines and reads it back with agile show.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_note_stdin_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "eoie", "19:19 eoie: fourth case today: a note quoting a pwsh loop wrote 'while (\\$true)' inside a double-quoted argument, pwsh expanded $true, and the backlog stored while (\\\\True) until a note-edit fixed it. The fix was the same each time: route the text through a literal here-string. A stdin dash makes that the default path.")
inl json_unicode_escapes () = Task ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", "lib json decode keeps \\u escapes as literal text: json_scan.unescaped has no \\u (or \\b, \\f) branch, so decode of {\"s\":\"\\u00e9\"} yields the 6 characters \\u00e9", Story, P2, D3, 95u32, Active, "", "lib,json,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_json_unicode_escapes () = Planning ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_1 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:41 main: found while landing x's json_text.spi (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE). json.decode (json.spi:8) runs json_real.decode in real code, and that emits runtime calls into json_scan (json_scan.spi:78-98 unescaped/unescape_from). unescaped maps n t r quote backslash slash and keeps any other escape as its two source characters, and unescape_from always advances 2. json's tests run on C and Rust (json.spi:2-3). Decision (one record per fact): x's json_text is not landed as a second unescaper; its algorithm (hex4, surrogate pairs joined, a lone surrogate or broken pair -> U+FFFD, broken hex kept) becomes json_scan's \\u branch. unescaped returns (piece, width) so unescape_from advances 6 or 12. That needs a code-point-to-text function with C, Rust and Gleam arms (sm' has none; core sm has only index/length/slice). The Rust arm is char::from_u32 with unwrap_or U+FFFD, and the Gleam arm string.utf_codepoint, both as in the unicode draft. The C arm needs the C backend's string construction convention. Blocker: one small C probe after tibia's 'block end' to read how a C string is built from bytes in generated C. Then draft the arm, gate json.spi (C+Rust) plus the new tests via lib-gate-kit, and land. Regression tests: x's five cases as json decode tests.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_2 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:19 main: correction to note 1: lib/spiral/xml.spi:24 already has code_point_string (u32 -> text), Rust only, and after LIB-UNICODE lands it delegates to unicode.char_of (Rust + Gleam). The missing piece is a C arm for unicode.char_of, which still needs the C probe. json_scan comes before xml in the module order, so its \\u branch calls unicode.char_of, which is listed before xml and after sm'. json_scan is listed after sm' too, so unicode must also come before json_scan: re-check the module order at landing.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_3 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:23 main: C string convention read from samples/managed_string_builder/main.c: String is Array0 { int refc; uint32_t len; char ptr[] } with len counting the trailing NUL, built by StringLit(len, ptr), which copies. A C arm for char_of can therefore UTF-8-encode into a local char[5] and return StringLit(k + 1, buf) inside a GCC statement expression; no lib C arm uses ({ }) yet, so the probe tests exactly that. Probe drafted: scratch c-char-of-probe (main.spi with the C macro; run.ps1 compiles with the installed compiler --backend C, then gcc -std=gnu11 -O2). Expected exit 6 (e-acute 2 bytes + U+1F600 4 bytes + lone surrogate U+D800 0 bytes, matching Rust's from_u32 None -> ''). It runs after tibia's block. Exit 6 means the arm goes into unicode.char_of as C = fun () => (wrapped under 120); anything else means read the generated main.c first.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_4 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:40 main: the C probe passed (job-1792802067040, exit 6: e-acute 2 bytes, U+1F600 4 bytes, lone surrogate 0), so the ({ }) statement expression with StringLit works on the C backend. The C arm is now in the unicode landing draft (%TEMP%\\lib-unicode, unicode.char_of C = ..., every line under 120), with a new test char_of_encodes_utf8_and_rejects_surrogates on rust, gleam and c (2, 4, 0 bytes). Its gate (main-lib-unicode-gate, chain A) now also compiles C. Next: json_scan's \\u branch calling unicode.char_of, which needs unicode listed before json_scan in the lib modules.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_5 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:43 main: json_scan side drafted (%TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\json_scan_u.spi.draft, plain Spiral in json_scan's own style): hex_digit over digit_of; hex4; unicode_escape (a valid pair -> one code point, width 11; a lone or broken surrogate -> U+FFFD, width 5; broken hex keeps the 2 source chars as today); unescaped now returns (piece, width) and also maps \\b and \\f; unescape_from advances 1 + width. Prerequisite found while drafting: every program using json.decode compiles this branch, so unicode.char_of must compile on every backend JSON runs on. Before json_scan calls it, char_of moves to the run_target form with Rust, Gleam, C, F# (System.Char.ConvertFromUtf32 with the surrogate check) and TypeScript (String.fromCodePoint) arms and a default '| _ => failwith', as xml's code-point functions do. Otherwise F#/TS/Zig users of json decode stop compiling. Order: LIB-UNICODE lands, then the char_of arms, then this json_scan patch with x's 5 cases as json tests on c and rust.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_6 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:51 main: status 0 Planned -> 40 Active: the C probe passed, the run_target char_of with every arm is in the gated unicode draft, and the json_scan \\u side is drafted; it lands after LIB-UNICODE.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_7 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 7u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: lib unicode slice LANDED in live lib/spiral at 23:01 (job main-lib-unicode-land, exit 0) after a GREEN gate (xsd_regex 4/4 and every file rows red 0): unicode.spi listed before json_scan, xml code_point_count and code_point_string delegate to it, char_of with Gleam/Rust/C/F#/TS arms. Next milestone: the json_scan_u draft (hex4, unicode_escape with surrogate pairs, lone surrogate to U+FFFD) through the same gate kit.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_8 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 8u32, "2026-10-24T04:02Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:02 main: json_scan_u slice ready as %TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\apply.ps1 (dry run clean on the live lib): hex_digit, hex4, unicode_escape (surrogate pair = width 11, lone surrogate = U+FFFD width 5, invalid hex kept as written), unescaped returning (piece, width) plus \\b and \\f, unescape_from advancing by width, a testing header, and 2 tests on rust/gleam/c (BMP char + surrogate pair + lone surrogate + \\n; malformed \\u12zz kept). No caller outside json_scan uses unescaped (grep of lib, apps, x, tibia, eoie, fiscal). Queued gate then land at the end of main's lib chain. Side finding for EOIE-PRIMITIVES-TO-LIB: eoie has 3 private JSON unescapers (agile_note_model, agile_task_inspection, eoie_memory_model) that could use lib json_scan once this lands.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_9 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 9u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict: (3) KEPT Active: its slice %TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\apply.ps1 is drafted; re-dry-run on the current lib and gate it in main's post-TRAIN2 lib lands.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_10 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 10u32, "2026-10-28T09:47Z", "main", "10-28 05:47 main: Groom 10-28 05:4x: this item's slice has LANDED, as a newer implementation than main's 10-24 draft (TEMP lib-json-unicode, now superseded; its anchors match 0 times). lib/spiral/json_scan.spi (written 10-27 19:53, by another team) has is_high_surrogate, is_low_surrogate, surrogate_pair (code point 65536 + ..., width 11), scalar_text (a lone low surrogate gives U+FFFD, 65533) and unicode_escape, dispatched from the \\u branch at L129, plus the test unicode_escapes_decoded (L240). Set to 95%: the shape asked for is in the tree, but main hasn't seen that test pass. It closes on the next lib gate that runs json_scan's tests GREEN.")
inl eoie_job_retention () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-RETENTION", "settled job folders are pruned after a retention period, so the jobs home and every job list stay fast as the registry grows", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,jobs,stability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_retention () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-RETENTION", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_retention_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RETENTION", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:51Z", "eoie", "measured 2026-10-23 18:5x: the live jobs home holds 1,421 job folders (22.5 MB), growing about 250 a day (10-19: 254, 10-21: 461, 10-22: 303, 10-23: 235), with nothing ever pruned; job list 5 takes 281 ms today because newest_directories lists and sorts every folder, so at this rate it scans tens of thousands of folders within months. Shape: eoie job prune [--older-than <days>] (default 14) removes only folders whose exit file exists and whose exit time is older than the cutoff, never a queued, held or running job and never one named by a live after file; receipt 'eoie job prune ok kept=N removed=M bytes=B'. The job host runs the same prune once per day while idle. 14 days keeps the history that today's audits read (window-slot-order since an install, adoption over 7 days). Test: a fixture home with settled-old, settled-new, running and held jobs, where only settled-old goes.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_retention_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RETENTION", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:56Z", "eoie", "18:56 eoie: drafted. The crate is scratch f6/src/eoie_job_prune: model with prune_verdict (KeepUnsettled | KeepReferenced | KeepRecent | PruneSettled), a cutoff that never underflows, days defaulting to 14, and a test of every verdict. prune.spi reads the exit file's ended field, collects the predecessors named by after files of unsettled jobs (held jobs from release P included), and deletes only PruneSettled folders through lib file_system.directory_delete; the receipt is 'kept=N removed=M older_than_days=D'. The wiring slice is scratch job-prune/apply-job-prune.ps1, built like M's job doctor: JobPrune case and word, usage, route, Cargo dependency, workspace member, a registry notebook (paren balance unchanged), and a crate Cargo.toml without the doctor's sysinfo. Dry run on the post-O tree is clean and idempotent. Typecheck after tibia's block; a release vehicle once that passes. The host's daily idle prune comes as step 2 after the verb proves itself.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_retention_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RETENTION", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "eoie", "19:51 eoie: step 2 design (host housekeeping, after the verb proves itself). The host's served loop already knows when it is idle (no live children, no held jobs, no requests) and has an idle clock. Rule: when idle for 60 s and the file shell-host/pruned (holding the last prune time) is older than 24 h, the host calls eoie_job_prune's export with the default 14 days and writes the time. A prune never touches unsettled, held or referenced jobs, so it can't race a starting job: a job without an exit file is always kept. Cost per day: one directory listing plus exit-file reads, about 0.3 s at today's 1,421 folders. Test: a fixture home with an old settled job and a stale pruned file sees the old job removed after an idle beat; a fresh pruned file sees nothing removed.")
inl eoie_window_announce () = Task ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", "a lane announces an exclusive block in the window store (class, from, until, latest start), so heavy starts, releases and waiters see it instead of relying on a peer message", TaskKind, P1, D3, 60u32, Paused, "", "eoie,windows,coordination", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_window_announce () = Planning ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_window_announce_1 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "eoie", "evidence 2026-10-23: tibia announced a 4-stage live block by message at 18:0x (latest start 20:00, end 21:00). eoie's window store knew nothing of it, so I found out only from the message that my GE2 release (heavy-build window, 228 min left) would block it; I stopped GE2 by hand at 18:02 and wrote a requeue waiter, which shipped with a dead predicate. Shape: 'eoie window announce <class> --from <HH:MM> --until <HH:MM> [--latest-start <HH:MM>] --label <text>' writes an Announced row (typed: announced class, window, latest start, owner label). window list shows it. Admission tells a new request whose declared end passes an announced exclusive window's start ('would overlap tibia block PC-10 from 18:05'). 'release yield' and 'window wait-clear' read the rows (wait until the announced window ended, not a 10-minute guess). From memory window-coordination (read queue plus announced blocks before heavy work; EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES).")
inl note_of_eoie_window_announce_2 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:39Z", "eoie", "00:39 drafted for the next train (scratch next-train\\window-announce.do + -files.do; applies on the emulated post-R tree, 20 replacements + 4 new files). Model: window_state gains Announced ('announced' in the row file); blocks/row_blocks take the request's span (now, now+minutes); an Announced row blocks only a clashing class whose span overlaps [start, until]; Held/Waiting rules unchanged. Admission passes the span. Verb: new crate eoie_window_announce behind the window dispatcher: 'eoie window announce <class> <start-in-minutes> <minutes> --label <text>' writes a lease-held row (not pruned as orphaned, expires at until; 'window release <id>' cancels it). The shape takes minute offsets rather than HH:MM, so no local-clock parsing; latest-start isn't modeled yet. Model test: overlapping compile waits, one ending before or starting after passes, the state reads back. Typecheck queued (job-1792816660444).")
inl note_of_eoie_window_announce_3 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:13Z", "x", "10-24 02:13 x evidence (cross-team, for the owner): 02:05 main's main-cells-pack-land needed two clear 20 s samples with no Cells erl, and x's chain ran notebooks back to back, so the gap never came. Worked around it with an x holder job that keeps 2 compile windows; x's gate waits for room (at most 1 other compile window) after taking its mutex, so x's next notebook waits until main's land settles. Generic shape this suggests, without a new class: a step that needs a quiet box for seconds (an apps/cells or compiler file land) requests a short EXCLUSIVE window, like compiler-install, instead of polling for natural gaps. Running jobs finish, new heavy/compile/test windows queue behind it, and the gap opens deterministically. Possible verb: 'eoie window request quiet <seconds> --until-idle <process pattern>' (exclusive, granted when the pattern has no live process). It would replace land.ps1's '2 clear 20 s samples' loop and the per-lane holder trick.")
inl note_of_eoie_window_announce_4 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T06:33Z", "eoie", "02:33 draft typecheck PASS (02:28, 4/4 notebooks on the post-R tree: window_model with the announced-overlap test, announce, admission, crowding). The first run's Pattern miss was two matches over window_state without an Announced arm (settling_mb, crowding); both now have one. Ready for the next train (PLAN stage S2).")
inl note_of_eoie_window_announce_5 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl eoie_job_start_settle () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", "eoie job start: settle briefly and report a job that already failed (exit, first stderr line, nonzero exit) instead of an ok receipt for a dead job", TaskKind, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_start_settle () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T00:24Z", "eoie", "20:24 found in an audit: a waiter queued with job start from a fresh shell got an empty path variable, pwsh exited 64 in 694 ms ('\\post-block-waiter.ps1' not recognized), the receipt said 'job start ok', and the planned post-block checks would never have run (found 25 min later only because the audit listed running jobs). Proposal: job start waits a settle window (default 2 s, --settle-ms n, 0 disables) for the supervisor's first state; if the job finished nonzero inside it, print 'job start failed id=... exit=n stderr=<first line>' and exit with the job's code; if it is still running or finished 0, print the ok receipt with settled_ms. Spec case: a program that exits 3 at once yields exit 3 and the stderr line; a sleeper yields ok.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: draft job-start-settle\\job-start-settle.do exists (settle loop over the typed job_state, SettleHealthy/SettleFailed, --settle-ms flag, usage text). Known defect before it can be applied: in the Launched branch the old stdout_line receipt and 'if attached' line must be indented under the new '| SettleHealthy =>' arm; the draft leaves them at the outer level. Fix, then apply on a post-train copy and typecheck.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:35Z", "eoie", "23:35 draft typecheck PASS (23:34, drafts-typecheck.ps1 with job-start-settle\\spec.json on the post-train tree, current compiler): eoie_job_launch launch.spi including a probe that reaches start_settle_of, and eoie_jobs model.spi with the new usage text. Two fixes on the way: the SettleHealthy arm indentation, and a nested pattern that needed parentheses (Settled (JobLost)). Ready to ride the train after P, or to join a later stage.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "eoie", "01:36 follow-up agreed with main (EOIE-JOB-EXPECT): one receipt for every early settle, 'eoie job start failed id=<id> state=<state_word> exit=<n> detail=\"<settled_detail>\"', using model.spi's vocabulary so job status/list and the settle receipt agree. R's SettleFailed widens from (exit, stderr line) to (word, exit, detail) read from the exit record. Lands in the next train with job-expect (a missed expectation then needs no special case: its exit record is nonzero). Tonight's R ships as tested.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 5u32, "2026-10-27T22:53Z", "eoie", "18:5x eoie: state check on the installed kit. The settle ships: --settle-ms defaults to 2000, and 'kit job start settle-probe2 60000 cmd /c exit 7' printed 'kit job start failed id=... exit=7 stderr=' and exited 7 after 1.9 s. A pwsh -Command job that failed took 8.6 s to settle under load, so start said ok. That's expected: the window is 2 s. Not done: note 4's agreed single receipt. The live receipt still says exit=/stderr=, and stopped or timed-out jobs that settle inside the window are reported as ok (the match falls through to SettleHealthy). Draft: scratchpad clean\\job-settle\\settle-receipt.do, 4/4 on package copies. SettleFailed now carries job_settled * string, every Settled case reports, and Live alone is healthy. The receipt becomes 'kit job start failed id=<id> state=<settled_word> exit=<settled_exit>' plus ' detail=\"...\"' when there is one, the same shape as job status. The detail is the first stderr line for a finished job and state_detail otherwise; lost keeps its sentence. Exit codes come from settled_exit, so spawn-failed moves from 127 to 125, as job status reports it. The usage text in kit_jobs/model.spi follows. Nothing parses the old 'stderr=' receipt (grep of kit/src and i574n.github/scripts). Left: compile kit_job_launch on the node, then ride the release after TRAIN, then a CLI check that a failed start prints state=finished exit=7.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_settle_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE", 6u32, "2026-10-27T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:3x eoie: node regen (job-1793142539742): kit_job_launch goes 601 -> 714 lines with the one-shape receipt; the settled_word/settled_exit/state_detail functions now get emitted into that crate. kit_jobs and kit_job_start_args differ only by the usage text. 25 other job and window crates that reach kit_jobs regenerate byte-identical. Ready for the release after TRAIN.")
inl eoie_supervisor_no_console () = Task ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", "job supervisors start with DETACHED_PROCESS: no conhost per supervisor or queued job; one hidden console only per running job program", TaskKind, P0, D1, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-NO-SHELL-SPAWN,EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", "eoie,jobs,processes", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console () = Planning ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl eoie_do_concurrent_edits () = Task ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "eoie do edits compose across sessions on the same file: lock + changed-since-read check + anchored re-apply, pre-image backups, and edit+write in one atomic program", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,do,concurrency,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits () = Planning ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits_1 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:20 main (user 2026-10-23 21:1x: 'shouldn't eoie's patching capabilities allow us to edit the same file from all the other sessions?'). Code read: proxy_text_replacement/text_replacement.spi replace_in (74-87) reads the file, computes the updated text and calls workspace_primitives replace_file_checked (files.spi:19), which writes <path>.<pid>.pending, renames it and reads it back. The rename is atomic, but nothing checks that the file is unchanged since the read, so two sessions editing one file can lose an update (the later rename wins). That is the class EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES fixed for agile.spi only. Design: (1) a per-file lock (an exclusive-create <path>.eoie-lock holding pid and start time; stale if the pid is gone) held across re-read, hash compare and rename; (2) on a hash mismatch, re-apply the same anchored statements to the current text; the program's expect counts decide (same counts: merge succeeds; otherwise reject all-or-nothing and name the conflicting anchor); bounded retries; (3) a pre-image backup per touched file (<root>/.eoie/backups/<stamp>/<rel>, bounded retention), as the user expects from 'automatic backups'; (4) edit and write/copy statements in one program, applied as one transaction (x's EOIE-DO-VERB-FAMILY note 5: today 'edit and write statements cannot share one program'). Test: two processes applying disjoint anchored edits to one file 100 times concurrently keep both edits every time; overlapping anchors reject one side with a named conflict.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits_2 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-9 (owner token, compare-and-swap by hash before every write), MERGE-1 (three-way merge keeping conflicts, with a receipt). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits_3 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T20:20Z", "eoie", "16:20 kit: a live occurrence on agile writes, not only do edits. At 16:18 'agile note EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE' failed with 'could not move the replacement file into place' (exit 2). That is RenameFailed from workspace_primitives/files.spi replace_file_checked, which gives up on the first failed rename. Several sessions were writing and reading the 6.6 MB shared backlog at once; on Windows a rename fails while any process has the target open. The same command succeeded on a manual retry seconds later.\n- Proposal, scoped to this item's compare-and-swap:\n  - bounded retry of the rename (for example 5 tries with growing waits, about 1 s in total), with a check before each retry that the target's hash still equals the hash read before the edit;\n  - if the hash changed, re-read and re-apply the anchored edit (this item's plan) instead of overwriting.\n  This fixes the transient reader case without making lost updates possible.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits_4 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T22:33Z", "main", "10-26 18:33 main: 10-26 18:33 main: live evidence of cross-session contention on the backlog store, from today's run. At 22:01Z an agile note by main failed with 'agile note EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG rejected: could not move the replacement file into place'. A note to a different item a second later succeeded, and the retry at 22:02Z succeeded after the file grew about 1.2 KB in between (bytes_before 7,760,531 -> 7,761,768), i.e. another session's write landed first. The failure was safe (atomic replace refused, nothing lost), and a plain retry was correct only because notes are appends that commute. For in-place edits (eoie do, agile edit) a blind retry would not be safe. That is this item's case: lock + changed-since-read check + anchored apply. Suggested acceptance probe: two processes appending notes to the same root in a tight loop, then assert every note is present exactly once, with no lost update.")
inl note_of_eoie_do_concurrent_edits_5 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 5u32, "2026-10-26T22:39Z", "main", "10-26 18:39 main: 10-26 18:39 main: CORRECTION to n4, checked against the session transcript. Confirmed: at 22:01Z 'agile note EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG rejected: could not move the replacement file into place' (eoie's RenameFailed arm of replace_file_checked). In the SAME command, the next note (COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS n46) succeeded, ending at 7,760,531 bytes. The retry at 22:02Z (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG n14) started at 7,761,768 bytes, so another writer landed 1,237 bytes in between. Wrong in n4: 'a second later' (it was the same command), and 'another session's write landed first' as the cause. That write came AFTER the failure, so the cause of the rename failure is unproven. On Windows a rename onto an open file fails when any process holds it without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, so a concurrent reader (or an AV scan) is as likely as a writer. Implication for this item: a lock must cover readers' open handles too, or the replace should retry RenameFailed with backoff. The acceptance probe in n4 stands, plus one variant: a tight reader loop against a writer.")
inl fossil_assimilation () = Task ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", "Study Fossil SCM and list what eoie should absorb (atomic check-ins, the artifact/content-addressed store, built-in tickets and wiki, sync, timeline, undo, backups), with each feature filed as an eoie item with all fields", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "eoie,research,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fossil_assimilation () = Planning ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl lib_generic_audit () = Task ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", "Audit lib/spiral for non-generic code: product, app, team or user specifics move out of lib (to the owning repo) or get generalized", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Paused, "-", "lib,audit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_generic_audit () = Planning ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_1 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:44 main (user 2026-10-23 21:4x: 'also gotta check later if all lib code is really generic, not having products details etc'). Method: grep lib/spiral for product, repo and app names (dice, alphabet, tibia, x, nearblocks, near wallet and leptos ui components, fiscal/SEFAZ, eoie, user paths) and read each hit in place. Verdict per module: generic, generalize (name the generic shape), or move (name the target repo and its consumers). Done when every module has a verdict and every move or generalize has an item.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_2 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:50Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:50 main: audit pass 1 over 107 lib .spi/.spir files, by name scan (tibia, otclient, arena, dice, alphabet, polyglot, fiscal, sefaz, nfe, twitter, oauth1, xsim, hangul, dota, eoie, i574n, fc1943s, near_wallet, nearblocks, sigenius, gerenciador), absolute-path scan (C:/Users, /home, AppData) and team words (lane, team main/x/tibia/eoie). Findings: (1) bench/parsing/bench_kit.spi benchmark command lines carry the checkout path c:/home/git/spiral (5) and a user path C:/Users/i574n/... (1). (2) xsd.spi test schema uses an NFe id pattern. (3) file_system.spi:889 test asserts the current directory is named spiral or polyglot (a repo coupling). Kept as generic: oauth.spi's api.twitter.com URL is the documented OAuth 1.0a signing test vector; near/wallet is a reusable integration package for an external platform, not product code. No team or lane names in lib. Slice %TEMP%\\lib-generic-audit\\apply.ps1 fixes (1) and (2) with neutral text (c:/repo/..., user, ID[0-9]{44}); queued gate (xsd.spi) then land at the end of main's chain. (3) waits for x's normalize_path land on file_system.spi to avoid a concurrent edit; then the test should assert a property that holds in any checkout (current directory exists and is absolute).")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_3 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T05:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:00 main: finding (3) folded into %TEMP%\\lib-fs-primitives\\apply.ps1 (x asked main to take it, so x's normalize_path program stays byte-identical to its proof). Both find_parent tests were coupled: fsharp find_parent asserted the parent is named spiral or polyglot, and rust find_parent_2 asserted spiral. Now both assert only that the workspace dir and README.md share one parent (am'.distinct length 1), and rust asserts a non-empty name. Lands after x's normalize_path land, with the fs primitives.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_4 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl eoie_agile_field_completeness () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", "eoie agile flags items with unset planning fields (estimate, load, assignee, deps) and gains the detail fields tasks keep putting in notes (acceptance rule, verify command, conflict surface)", Story, P1, D3, 10u32, Active, "EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS", "eoie,agile,dogfood,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:44 main (user 2026-10-23 21:4x: 'we also gotta get the discipline of filling all tasks's fields, also when needing more fields for detail, requesting that also'). Today many items show 'estimate=- load=- assignee=-', and the stop gate's light-work list depends on load. Ask: (1) 'agile list --incomplete' plus an agile check that names each open item with an unset field; (2) typed fields for what notes carry today: acceptance (the pre-registered rule), verify (the command that proves it), and touches (files and modules, for concurrent-edit ordering); (3) add/set accept them, and show prints them. Done when the check reports 0 incomplete open items for team main after a backfill pass.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): AGILE-5 (progress and ratings from evidence with the denominator recorded; prompt blocks: 0-1000 ratings with deltas each round). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T02:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:27 main grooming census: of 276 open items, 123 lack an estimate and 111 lack a load (assignee now set on all: 208 filled with agent by rule). Per team: main 104, eoie 69, x 23, tibia 2 had at least one gap. Estimates stay unset until sized from each item's milestones (estimates-from-rules); this verb should list them per team so each lane sizes its own.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main team load backfill by a rule written before applying it: heavy when the remaining work is a compiler pack gate or install (backend codegen items, PACK-*, HOPACSOTA*, compiler and syntax items), light for docs, CI config, cleanup and posts, medium for lib, Cells and Kino items needing targeted compiles. Applied to 85 main items with no load. 65 main items still lack an estimate; they are sized from milestones as each is picked up.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T07:26Z", "main", "10-27 03:26 main: 10-27 03:26 main groom (user 03:2x 'groom the backlog'): planning-field audit of team main's 159 open items. 51 had no estimate. Sized by the estimates-from-rules method (each item's own milestone and stop-rule notes, never its description; estimate = REMAINING effort). SET (5): HOPACSOTA-P6 60m (both promotion gates green; left: decide whether P3 really gates promotion, then the user's gpt5parallel call), SPIRAL-FORMATTER 240m (eoie's two respell gaps, n84 and n85), CI-K3S 300m (Dockerfile, Deployment + Service, k3s CI job from the 10-26 sketch; blocked on the user's placement call), CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT 60m (option B GREEN on the node; left: land), RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE 120m (root cause is lib, one slice). NOT SIZED, by reason. (a) Blocked on unsized work: HOPACSOTA-P3, P4, P5 (PLAN.md forbids starting before P2's engine gate numbers), EDITION3-280-SPLIT (after the symbol migration), HOPACSOTA (umbrella = sum of its parts). (b) Research-scale epics: HOPACSOTA-P2, SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, BEND2-IN-SPIRAL, SPIRAL-UI, PWSH-FREE, GPU-RENTAL-READINESS, LIB-CANON-NAMES, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST. (c) Another lane sizes them: fiscal (FISCAL-APP, FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH, FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE, FISCAL-GENERIC); compiler (HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, HOPACSOTA-P7, COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO, RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, FSX-FROM-SPI, EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION). Both lanes were asked tonight. (d) Parked by user rule: DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD, DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO (no Dota until the user starts it). (e) STALE: the last note is the 10-24 11:49 fold of the 10-19 lap ledger, so nothing has moved since ~10-19, and they need a fresh read before sizing: NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE, NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES, CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG, ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY, SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR, KINO-HOT-PATHS, LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS, LIB-REACTORS, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS. (f) Active with recent notes but no stated remaining milestone (size at their next note): BLOG, DIB-MODERNIZE, CI-OFFLOAD, CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST. Also closed tonight: CELLS-PACK-NEXT (Done; its pack landed 10-24, all leaves closed). Kept open at 90%+: BEND-BACKEND (not-supported list), SPIRAL-FORMATTER (respell gaps), FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (dep COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS at 75%), MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO (done-check needs a matrix run).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_field_completeness_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T22:57Z", "eoie", "18:57 eoie: census on the live backlog. Of 416 open items, 47 have no estimate, 2 no load and 2 no assignee. 17 of the 47 are Epics, which stay unsized by convention (an umbrella is the sum of its parts). 30 non-Epic items have a gap. Team eoie has none: its only gaps are its Epics. Two items without load or assignee: NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER (no estimate, load or assignee) and SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (no assignee). The rest lack only an estimate: HOPACSOTA-P2..P5, EDITION3-280-SPLIT, NEAR-*, CELLS-*, LIB-REACTORS, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, FISCAL-GENERIC, RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, DOTA-*, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS and similar. Most match main's 10-27 03:26 'not sized, by reason' groups. What exists today: list --load none and --assignee none select two of the three gaps. Missing: an estimate gap filter, and a single 'unplanned' view that exempts Epics from estimate. Design for the first half: list/next take --unplanned, meaning open items missing load or assignee, or missing an estimate unless the kind is Epic. Plus a view-add default named 'unplanned', so each lane runs 'agile view <root> unplanned --team <t>'. Placement: agile_task_listing is at 978/1000 generated lines, so the predicate goes in agile_planning_filters (73 lines of Spiral, already holds plan_selected) and the listing gains only one call. The second half (typed detail fields: acceptance rule, verify command, conflict surface) needs EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS (the dep) first. Not drafted tonight; the census is the input for it.")
inl eoie_do_undo () = Task ("EOIE-DO-UNDO", "eoie undo/redo: the last do program can be undone and redone from its pre-images (with --dry-run), as Fossil's undo restores saved file copies oldest-first", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "eoie,do,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_do_undo () = Planning ("EOIE-DO-UNDO", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_do_undo_1 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-UNDO", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: undo.c saves each file's prior content (undo_maybe_save, with a size cap, UNDO_TOOBIG) and the checkout tables (undo_vfile, undo_vmerge, undo_stash) before an undoable command, then undo_all_filesystem replays the copies oldest-first; one level; --dry-run; commit clears the stack. eoie: the pre-image backups from EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS become the undo record per do program (program id, files, pre and post hashes). 'eoie undo [--dry-run]' restores the pre-images only if each file still has the post hash (otherwise it names the file that moved on); 'eoie redo' re-applies. Today x hand-wrote reverse do programs (undo-8..1) for its 21:18 revert. This makes that one call. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_eoie_do_undo_2 () = Note ("EOIE-DO-UNDO", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): EDIT-12 (inverse patch by receipt), CKPT-3 (restore one file by hash), EDIT-3 rollback after a failed post-write gate. Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl eoie_edit_intent_leases () = Task ("EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", "eoie edit intents: a session announces 'editing <files> against base <hash>' and other sessions get a warning before overlapping, like Fossil's ci-lock fork guard", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "eoie,coordination,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_edit_intent_leases () = Planning ("EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_edit_intent_leases_1 () = Note ("EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: xfer.c 1773-1850, 'pragma ci-lock CHECKIN-HASH CLIENT-ID' stores a ci-lock-<hash> config row with login, clientid and time; a second client committing against the same base gets 'ci-lock-fail USER TIME' and is warned it would fork; checkin.c refuses a forking commit without --allow-fork/--override-lock and rechecks before writing. eoie: 'eoie intent <files> [--base <hash>] --as <team>' writes a soft lease. do edits and agile writes print a warning (not a block) when another team holds an overlapping intent; intents expire on land or after N minutes. Tonight's duplicate Kotlin val pass and the listm' ordering conflict with x are exactly what a visible intent would have caught before drafting. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl eoie_draft_stash () = Task ("EOIE-DRAFT-STASH", "eoie drafts: named patch sets saved against a baseline (stash save/list/show/apply/pop/drop/diff), replacing ad-hoc %TEMP% apply scripts", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-DO-UNDO", "eoie,do,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_draft_stash () = Planning ("EOIE-DRAFT-STASH", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_draft_stash_1 () = Note ("EOIE-DRAFT-STASH", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: stash.c, save/snapshot/list/show/pop/apply/goto/drop/diff/rename; a stash is a changeset relative to a baseline check-in, and pop/apply/goto/drop are undoable. eoie: 'eoie draft save <name> <do program> --base <files' hashes>' stores the program plus base hashes; show prints the planned diff against the current tree; apply runs it on a target root (scratch copy or live, with a mandatory target, as x's landing rule requires); rebase re-checks anchors on the current base; list shows drafts with stale-base flags. Tonight main and x kept about 15 drafts as hand-written apply.ps1 files with private anchor checks (lib-gaps-x, lib-unicode, nullary-mirrors...). This makes them one typed store. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_eoie_draft_stash_2 () = Note ("EOIE-DRAFT-STASH", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:50Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:50 main: a needed verb, from tonight's miss. 'stash status <target>': for every saved draft, is it applied to this target (its new text present / an 'already applied' guard trips), applicable (anchors match once), or conflicting (anchors miss)? Main found by hand (a scratch loop dry-running ~56 pack slices against a composed core) that a composite silently lacked 7 reviewed slices (next7/next8), and that one blocked a lib item (LIB-PAR-MAP's closure factory). Drafts whose apply scripts lack an 'already applied' check show as conflicting even when included, so the stash record should keep each draft's post-image anchors to decide 'applied' positively.")
inl eoie_timeline () = Task ("EOIE-TIMELINE", "eoie timeline: one time-ordered view across teams of notes, item changes, job starts and ends, windows, lands and drafts, Fossil's primary view", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "-", "eoie,coordination,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_timeline () = Planning ("EOIE-TIMELINE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_timeline_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TIMELINE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil puts the timeline first (fossil-v-git: 'situational awareness for the whole team'): every check-in, ticket change, wiki edit and forum post in one DAG-aware time view. eoie keeps the same events in separate places (agile notes, job dirs, window log, gate logs). 'eoie timeline [--since 2h] [--team t] [--kind job,window,note,land]' merges them. That answers 'what did the other lanes just do', which tonight took cross-session messages and reads of gate.log and job lists. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl eoie_typed_hooks () = Task ("EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS", "eoie hooks: typed events (after-land, after-job, after-note) run configured commands in sequence, with hook list/test --dry-run, as in Fossil's hook.c", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-TIMELINE", "eoie,automation,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_typed_hooks () = Planning ("EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_typed_hooks_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: hook.c stores hooks as a JSON array setting {type, cmd, seq}, validates the type, substitutes %F/%R/%A, feeds after-receive hooks the received artifact list on stdin, keeps a hook-last-rcvid watermark so each event is processed once, has a hook-embargo, and offers 'hook test --dry-run'. eoie: typed events with a processed watermark, so e.g. 'after a lib land, message the teams that read lib' or 'after review-N green, queue its install' become declared hooks instead of a human relaying them. That serves the user's 'more bouncy' coordination. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl eoie_agile_event_log () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", "agile state as an append-only, content-addressed event log (each add/set/edit/note an immutable event), with item rows derived by replay, so concurrent writers merge by set union like Fossil's G-Set of artifacts", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-STATE-SCALE,LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "eoie,agile,concurrency,fossil", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_event_log () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: the repository is an unordered set of immutable artifacts named by content hash (fileformat.wiki, Z checksum card); every metadata table is derived and 'contains no new information' (fossil rebuild recomputes it); sync is set union (igot/gimme), with no artifact-level conflicts (sync.wiki calls it a grow-only CRDT). eoie today rewrites agile.spi rows in place under a lock (EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES), and EOIE-STATE-SCALE plans append-only note segments. Extension: also make status, progress and field edits events (actor, time, item, field, old, new), with rows derived by replay. Two lanes' writes then never conflict, history answers 'who set this to 60% when', and cross-machine or CI sync becomes a union. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): AGILE-2 (every mirror and report rendered from typed state in one transaction). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 3u32, "2026-10-26T05:17Z", "eoie", "01:17 eoie: user 10-26 01:1x asked whether state as .spi has paid off, and proposed persisting a binary derivative (like Cells memoizes execution) to find new uses for iggy plus typefeatureset. Assessment from measurements.\nPaid off:\n(a) state is typed: the compiler checks the union schema, so a bad row is a type error;\n(b) a real compiler corpus: at 2.6 MB it found COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK (a stack overflow);\n(c) formatter and respell dogfooding: release byte identity runs 8 verbs on a raw and a formatted backlog.\nCosts now, measured tonight on a fully loaded box:\n- 6.66 MB, 8,758 lines (1.15 MB on 10-17);\n- list 6.2 s, next 5.7 s, show 0.19 s;\n- writes rewrite the whole file: note about 17 ms/MB, set about 27 ms + 65 ms/MB (STATE-SCALE note 12), so a set is about 0.46 s now;\n- one lock: a transient 'could not move the replacement file into place' tonight under concurrent writers;\n- the 8 MB threshold arrives in 2-3 days.\nDesign, keeping the strengths:\n1. .spi stays the canonical human-readable projection, typed and checked.\n2. A content-addressed binary derivative: a memo of the parsed typed rows keyed by the state text's sha256, like Cells memo. A read with a matching hash skips the parse, and list and next become loads. Phase 1, local, no infra.\n3. Writes become typed events (TaskAdded, FieldEdited old to new, NoteAppended, StatusMoved) on an iggy agile stream, and the .spi is materialized from them (Fossil rebuild).\n   - Writers never rewrite megabytes, and lanes and machines stop contending on one lock.\n   - Each session's consumer offset answers 'what changed since I last read', which beats show --last.\n   - History answers who set what when.\n4. typefeatureset: event constructors indexed by the item's status typestate make an illegal move unrepresentable in the log. The codec family is the same one as LIB-IGGY-CLIENT (one model, Rust and Gleam).\nDecision rules, written before any measurement:\nR1 (phase 1 memo): accept when a warm list on the live file is under 500 ms and its output is byte-identical to the parse path (the byte identity check covers it); a stale-hash read must fall back to a parse.\nR2 (phase 2 events): accept when a note write's p95 stays under 20 ms at any file size, replaying the log reproduces agile.spi byte for byte, and two machines appending concurrently converge to one identical file.\nR3: until R2 passes, the single file stays canonical, per the 10-17 decision.\nSequencing: phase 1 is the first eoie change after KIT-RENAME lands (feature freeze until then); phase 2 waits for LIB-IGGY-CLIENT M2.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 4u32, "2026-10-26T09:15Z", "eoie", "05:15 eoie: dominant-phase check before building phase 1, with the installed binary on state\\agile.spi (6.87 MB, 803 items). A filtered list still parses the whole file and counts every status, yet takes 155 ms (--prefix KIT-REN, 1 row shown) and 340 ms (Blocked, 17 rows), both from the tool's own elapsed_ms. The full list takes 5,769 ms and 6,767 ms for 803 rows. So parsing is about 150 ms and rendering is about 7 ms per row, over 95% of a full list. A parse memo (phase 1 as designed) can save at most the ~150 ms; it cannot bring a full list under R1's 500 ms. The per-row cost is where list-planning-index points: row_line looked up each row's planning entry by a linear scan, so a full list is quadratic in items. The drafted index makes it one HashMap lookup, byte-identical on the node; the release speed check is still pending. EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC (each string parameter copied at the export boundary) adds a smaller constant. Revised order, decided now by R1's own target: (1) land list-planning-index after the rename and measure a full list in release (5 runs, median); (2) build the memo only if that list still misses 500 ms with parse as the larger share. R1 is unchanged; only the order of work moves.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 5u32, "2026-10-26T12:05Z", "eoie", "08:05 eoie: step (1) of the revised order measured on the worker node (scratch clean\\respell\\node-list-speed.ps1: release builds of kit-cli before and after list-planning-index.do, a copy of the shared backlog, 803 items, 5 full-list runs each, elapsed_ms from the tool). Before: median 12,158 ms (8,519 / 13,753 / 9,879 / 12,158 / 15,276). After: median 2,118 ms (1,624 / 2,118 / 3,315 / 2,976 / 1,230), about 5.7x faster. Output byte-identical: 804 lines. The node was at 100% CPU with other lanes' jobs, so absolute numbers are inflated and noisy; the ratio is the reliable part. By R1 (warm full list under 500 ms), still not met: about 2.6 ms per row remains. Per the rule, the memo is built only if parse is now the larger share. Next measurement, set now: on the same binaries, time a filtered list (--prefix, 1 row, which still parses everything) against the full list; if parse is under half of the full list, the next step is the per-row renderer again (EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC copies, then the note index), not the memo. Script bug, fixed: Say also writes to the pipeline, so Measure-List returned an array and the final ratio line threw after every result was logged.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_6 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 6u32, "2026-10-26T12:10Z", "eoie", "08:10 eoie: parse-share measurement (same two release binaries on the node, 5 runs each, medians): before full 15,057 ms, before filtered (--prefix KIT-REN, 1 row, full parse) 1,024 ms; after full 1,877 ms, after filtered 1,756 ms. So after the index, setup (parse plus building the planning index) is about 94% of a full list, and by the rule the memo is next. Two caveats before building it: (a) the filtered list got slower with the index (1,024 to 1,756 ms), which suggests planning_index_of costs more than a hash fill should, perhaps a string copy per planning line; (b) noise is large at 100% node CPU (after filtered ranged 734 to 3,314 ms). Rule, set now: remeasure with 15 runs when the node's CPU is under 70% (read from node status first). If the filtered list after the index exceeds before-filtered by more than 20%, fix planning_index_of first, and the memo waits.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_7 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 7u32, "2026-10-26T16:14Z", "eoie", "12:14 eoie: the granular planning index (no host flow: dictm plus sm' ops, scratch list-planning-index-granular.do) measured on the node, same method (release builds, 5 runs, 803-item backlog copy, node at 100% CPU). Output identical, 804 lines. Full list before 12,402 ms, after 6,764 ms (1.83x); filtered list after 7,612 ms, more than the full list, so building the index now dominates. The host-flow draft gave 1,877 / 1,756 ms in the 10:0x run. By the granular discipline (style holds at any cost), the granular index is the one that lands; the host-flow draft is withdrawn. Its cost is lib performance, to fix in lib so every user gains. Suspects, to measure in order: (1) sm'.lines over 6.8 MB (one Rc<str> per line, ~60k lines); (2) dictm.insert's unwrap_or_clone copying the whole map when the Rc is shared (quadratic); (3) per-line sm'.starts_with expansion. Measurement, set now: a lib Cells notebook timing each of the three on a 6.8 MB string, with the review box idle; the slowest goes to lib as a fix (likely a lines iterator without per-line Rc, or a dictm insert that keeps the Rc unique).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_8 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 8u32, "2026-10-26T20:50Z", "kit", "16:50 kit: the three-suspect lib measurement is done (scratch clean\\lib-perf\\index-cost.spi, Rust cells on the worker node, job-1793047551305-kit-lib-perf3, both tests pass). The first run timed out at 300 s with one end-of-test print, so the notebook now prints each phase as it ends and runs 6,000 and 12,000 backlog-shaped lines (0.55 and 1.11 MB) so scaling shows directly. Results for 6k then 12k: sm'.lines 1,065 then 3,636 ms (3.4x for 2x the input, superlinear); dictm insert fill 1 then 2 ms; starts_with marking 1 then 1 ms; and, not on the suspect list, sm'.concat_list 2,866 then 13,301 ms (4.6x, quadratic: its Rust arm is concat_right_fold, the trade-off recorded on GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR whose follow-up is a native join). Verdict by the 12:14 rule: suspect (1), sm'.lines, is the one; dictm (2) and starts_with (3) are cleared. The granular index pays for lines over the whole 6.8 MB backlog, so the fix belongs in lib (main's LIB-SM-LINES-GRANULAR, where lines' Rust arm pushes one element at a time into a vec built with am'.vec_push). Kit's side waits for that lib change: after it lands, rerun this notebook as the acceptance check (12k under 2.2x the 6k time for both lines and concat_list), then the node full-list timing.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_9 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 9u32, "2026-10-26T21:00Z", "main", "10-26 17:00 main: 10-26 16:5x main: lib sm'.lines and sm'.concat_list Rust performance, from kit's scaling data (Rust cells on the node, backlog-shaped text 6k then 12k lines, 0.55 then 1.11 MB; baseline job-1793047551305): lines 1,065 / 3,636 ms (3.4x for 2x the input, superlinear); concat_list 2,866 / 13,301 ms (4.6x, quadratic). These are the costs kit's granular planning index pays on the 6.8 MB backlog, so kit's fix waits on lib.\nCauses, by reading sm'.spi: concat_list's Rust arm is concat_right_fold, whose acc = x ++# sep ++# acc copies the whole accumulator every step (quadratic). lines' Rust arm emulates Rust's line rules (split on newline, strip a trailing CR per line, drop a final empty part) through a vec_push loop in a join point.\nSlice (TEMP lib-sm-linear/apply.ps1; anchored, 0 new lines over 120):\n- concat_list Rust: the list becomes an array (listm'.box |> listm'.to_array'), then ONE host call, Rc<str>::from((*items.borrow()).join(&*separator)) (linear).\n- lines Rust: ONE host call, text.lines().map(Rc::from).collect into the array. That is the semantics the existing test lines_follow_rust_line_rules pins ('a\\r\\n\\nb\\n' gives 3 lines).\n- Other backends unchanged.\nAcceptance rule (kit's, adopted before the run): for both functions the 12k time is under 2.2x the 6k time, on kit's own notebook (index-cost.spi, phase lines in the .ipynb). A and B run in the SAME node job for equal load (job-1793048449505-main-sm-linear-ab: A = kit's candlib, B = a copy with the slice), plus the lib gate's lines and concat tests on landing.\nOrdering: sm'.spi also carries main's queued pack (job-1793027588823) and Nil batch 2, so the slice lands after the pack, and lib is read by kit's FOLLOWUP until the rename release ends.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_10 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 10u32, "2026-10-26T21:07Z", "main", "10-26 17:07 main: 10-26 17:0x main: sm' linear slice A/B on the node, both sides in ONE job (job-1793048449505) for equal load, on kit's index-cost.spi. Outputs agree on both sides: equal bytes, line counts and planning counts at 6k and 12k, so the slice changed only time.\n- lines: A 1,181 / 3,956 ms; B 45 / 7 ms. Rule (12k under 2.2x the 6k time): B ratio 0.16, PASS.\n- concat_list: A 3,401 / 15,650 ms; B 3 / 57 ms. That is 275x faster at 12k, but the B ratio is 19x, so by the rule as written it FAILS. The rule is not amended after the fact.\nThe concat verdict is RED by rule. Two readings fit 3 -> 57 ms: single-digit-ms noise, or something non-linear left (for example listm'.box |> listm'.to_array' before the join). To tell them apart, a NEW measurement, pre-registered before its run: kit's notebook with three larger sizes on side B only (A is quadratic and would take hours at 192k); node job-1793048885441, 48k/96k/192k lines. Rule: for both functions, the 192k time under 2.2x the 96k time, with B's times there at least 50 ms so the ratio isn't noise; the planning asserts (640/1280/2560) keep the outputs checked. If concat fails that too, the cause is in the list-to-array step, and the fix moves the join to a host fold over the list itself.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_11 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 11u32, "2026-10-26T21:12Z", "main", "10-26 17:12 main: 10-26 17:1x main: the scaled follow-up (node job-1793048885441, side B only, outputs checked by the planning asserts 640/1280/2560, all passed), scored by n10's rule (192k under 2.2x the 96k time, both at least 50 ms):\n- concat_list: 96k 111 ms, 192k 80 ms. Ratio 0.72, PASS.\n- lines: 96k 191 ms, 192k 430 ms. Ratio 2.25, FAIL by 0.05.\nSo each function has now passed one rule and missed the other. The node is visibly noisy: concat measured 54 ms at 6k and 4 ms at 12k in this very run, an inverted size order, because the node runs other lanes' jobs. That points at single-sample noise, not at either implementation (code reading: box and to_array' are single linear passes on Rust; the join is one host call). I won't add a third ad-hoc reading. ONE tiebreaker, pre-registered now and final: node job-1793049153700, three repetitions each at 96k and 192k; for both functions the MEDIAN 192k time must be under 2.2x the MEDIAN 96k time. Its verdict decides. For scale, against the original costs: at 12k lines B is 7 ms vs 3,956 ms for lines and 4 to 57 ms vs 15,650 ms for concat. At kit's 6.8 MB backlog (about 75k lines) both fall from minutes to about 100 ms.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_12 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 12u32, "2026-10-26T21:18Z", "main", "10-26 17:18 main: 10-26 17:1x main: the FINAL pre-registered tiebreaker (n11; node job-1793049153700; median of 3 runs at 96k and 192k, side B, outputs asserted) FAILS for both functions:\n- concat_list: 96k 42/47/103 ms (median 47); 192k 129/136/222 ms (median 136). Ratio 2.89, FAIL.\n- lines: 96k 58/72/118 ms (median 72); 192k 150/231/247 ms (median 231). Ratio 3.21, FAIL.\nVerdict, as declared final in advance: the slice does NOT meet the linear-scaling acceptance rule, so it is not landed as accepted.\nWhat stands: identical outputs, and a large constant-factor win (12k lines: lines 3,956 to about 7 ms, concat 15,650 to 4-57 ms). Kit's index cost on the 6.8 MB backlog falls from minutes to about 100 ms either way. Medians strip the per-run noise, so the remaining ~3x per doubling at 9-18 MB is real, and may come from the platform rather than lib: str::lines and [Rc<str>]::join both allocate per line (one Rc<str> each), and these sizes cross cache and allocator regimes.\nNext, a diagnostic and not an acceptance test, offered to kit as the rule's owner: a native-Rust program doing exactly the same two operations at 96k and 192k on the node. If native Rust shows the same ratio, the right acceptance shape is 'within a stated factor of native' rather than 'under 2.2x', and kit decides that. If native is linear, the remaining cost is in lib's glue (for example the list box/clone before the join), to be traced phase by phase.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_13 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 13u32, "2026-10-26T21:24Z", "main", "10-26 17:24 main: 10-26 17:2x main: kit's acceptance rule (fixed by kit before any same-job native numbers existed; my standalone native run was disclosed to kit), run as written in ONE node job (job-1793049776329): side B median of 3 at 96k/192k, then native Rust (str::lines into Vec<Rc<str>>, [Rc<str>]::join) median of 3. Per function: (1) slice ratio at most 1.25x native's ratio, (2) slice 192k time at most 3x native's.\n- concat_list: B 44 / 124 ms (ratio 2.82); native 15 / 51 ms (ratio 3.40, limit 4.25), ok; 124 ms against limit 153, ok. ACCEPT.\n- lines: B 72 / 120 ms (ratio 1.67); native 54 / 132 ms (ratio 2.44, limit 3.06), ok; 120 ms against limit 396, ok. ACCEPT.\nBoth halves ACCEPTED. Native Rust itself grows 2.4-3.4x per doubling at 9-18 MB on this node (per-line Rc<str> allocation, cache regime), so the earlier 2.2x rule asked lib to beat native scaling. That is why it failed, and it's exactly what kit's rule was built to check. The verdict history stays on record (n10 RED, n11 split, n12 tiebreaker FAIL). The land is registered in the post-rename requeue (main-serial-sm-linear: kit gate on sm'.spi, which runs lines_follow_rust_line_rules and concat_list's own tests, then land), after main's sm' pack chain.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_event_log_14 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 14u32, "2026-10-26T22:02Z", "main", "10-26 18:02 main: 10-26 18:01 main: compiler base for n9-n13, from x's finding that the node's shared single-flight DLL is stale (499AFC18E49F, 10/25 15:23; this box has 4FD712E9E357, 10/26 08:19). The sm' A/B jobs did NOT use the shared DLL: kit's node-lib-perf.ps1 sets SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\renameproof\\compiler\\SpiralCompiler.dll (kit's rename-proof compiler). A, B and native ran in the same job under that one compiler, so the relative accept verdict (n13) stands. The absolute timings carry that compiler's codegen. The gate on landing (main-serial-sm-linear) re-checks behavior on the installed compiler.")
