inl python_nullary_shared () = Task ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", "Python output shares one instance per nullary union case (UH0_End_shared) instead of building a NamedTuple per construction, mirroring next9/next10's nullary singletons on C, Zig, Kotlin, Lua, Wasm, Delphi and TypeScript", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "PACK-NEXT-2", "compiler,python,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_python_nullary_shared () = Planning ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_python_nullary_shared_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", 1u32, "2026-10-24T06:42Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:5x main: drafted and composed into pack 5g. %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\python-nullary-shared.ps1 (3 anchors, the construction one scoped to module CodegenPython, because the CUDA backend's Python host has the same TyUnionBox text). Every nullary heap/stack union case gets `<Case>_shared = <Case>()` right after its NamedTuple class, and a nullary construction emits `<Case>_shared` instead of `<Case>()`. Nullness is read from the case's payload array (as next9 does), not from the argument text. NamedTuple values are immutable, so sharing is safe, and `case <Case>():` patterns still match the class. Rides review-4 (job requeued after tibia's block); the matrix's Python rows are its behaviour check, and main.py diffs should change only at nullary constructions.")
inl lib_macro_heal () = Task ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", "Heal lib/spiral's logic-in-macros drift (user 10-24: like eoie's rust_global): 69 definitions hold control flow inside \\$'...' text (59,479 chars, 22.6 % of lib is macro text), plus helper preludes kept as global blobs; host code shrinks to named one-call primitives and the logic moves into Spiral over lib", Epic, P0, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,spiral,heal,macros,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_macro_heal () = Planning ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_1 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:08Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:08 main: user direction 2026-10-24 03:0x: \"we started ruining lib's code stuffing a lot of code inside macros, same way eoie drifted with rust_global, unhealable drift that we're still dealing with there. look at async.start_child_rust geez, prioritize healing ALL of this\". Census (scratch macro-census.ps1 over 107 lib .spi/.spir files): macro text is 193,899 of 856,114 definition chars (22.6 %); 962 definitions use a macro; 69 hold LOGIC inside a macro (3+ statements or 2+ control keywords), 59,479 chars (list in scratch macro-logic.tsv). Largest: sm'_real.format_real 12,866 (171 lines), sm'_real rec 4,109, runtime.execute_with_options_rust 2,276, leptos svg loading render 2,168, async.start_child_rust 2,072, date_time.time_span_format 1,908, trace.trace 1,505, unicode.char_of 1,365, trace.init_trace_state 1,184, sm'.index_of 1,141, common.random' 1,114, base.try_finally 1,102, convert.convert_i32_base 1,072, guid.new_raw_guid 1,045, resultm.unwrap_err 996, base.try_unit 989, resultm.get 968, file_system.walk_dir_filter 952, sm'.split 948, async.catch_rust 905. Not counted as logic but in scope: Gleam helper preludes kept as global blobs (process.process_gleam_prelude 5,839, file_system.file_system_gleam_paths 1,550). Out of scope: F# export `main` stubs (one let per exported name) and one-call backend arms ($'Async.Sleep').\nRULE, written before any heal: (1) host code stays only as a named primitive whose macro is ONE host call or expression, at most 120 chars, and does no control flow; (2) everything else (statements, loops, matches, panic and timeout handling, helper preludes) is Spiral over lib, with the primitives as its only host contact; (3) order: async.start_child_rust first (user-named), then by logic chars descending, a file's defs together when they share primitives; (4) acceptance per heal: every test the def had passes on every backend it had, the generated output for its tests is reviewed (no behavior change), the lib Cells gate is green, and no new macro exceeds rule (1); (5) a new lib def with logic in a macro is a review failure from now on. Progress = healed logic chars / 59,479, plus the 2 preludes.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_2 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: under GRANULAR-HEAL; rule extended by the user's cast remark (env.command_line_argument_count Rust arm (std::env::args().count() as i32) - 1): no as-casts and no arithmetic in macro text either. Rule (6): authorship is irrelevant (user 03:3x: don't keep old code just because I wrote it). Census now 715 sites, 60,477 chars; top files sm'_real.spir 44/15,447, file_system 55/7,928, sm' 69/6,033, trace 27/5,649, date_time 43/2,373, base 48/2,331. start_child_rust: 0 callers in every repo since the 10-23 unused removal took start_child/start_child_timeout, so rule (4) deletes it; catch_rust and run_synchronously (used by catch' and start) are healed first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_3 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:17 tibia: process.spi's heal moved to tibia, prelude included, by main's handover (04:0x), and it absorbs LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's slice. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL/process_healed.spi is the whole module rewritten under this item's rule. The Gleam global keeps only imports and @external declarations (coerce, open_port, element, find_executable, halt, port_info, port_close, unlink, os cmd, monotonic_time, get, put, file write_file). Every host contact is a named primitive of one expression: nominals dynamic, port, bits, gleam_strings, port_options, selector, received, plus rust_command, rust_output, rust_try_output, rust_file, rust_instant, started. The verbs follow main's suggested shape (port_open, port_unlink, port_os_pid, port_selector, selector_receive, file_write, os_cmd, rust_command_new and friends, rust_command_spawn, rust_child_exited and rust_child_code). The logic is Spiral: the argv filter, the collect and drain loops, the exit cache, the deadline loop, and kill_tree_of_pid choosing taskkill or kill through platform.is_windows. It carries the module's 6 existing tests plus the handle's 3, on the same backends. Lab run 2 is queued after run 1's two parse and type errors (a != splice and an array type in words_of).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_4 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:32 tibia: rule (4) agreed with main (04:2x). Slice (a) removes lib process.spi: all 6 of its definitions have 0 callers in every repo. Scratch process-delete-apply.ps1: one eoie do edit drops process from package.spiproj, both files are backed up for undo, and the file is removed by literal path. Its guard refuses if any lib code outside macro text still calls process.*; the first runs flagged 15 lines, all host text (TypeScript process.stdout and argv, Zig std.process, Gleam process.*), and the strip now covers single-line and unterminated multi-line macros. Proven on a lib copy: process.spi removed, spiproj lists runtime then file_system. Kit gate job tibia-lib-process-delete-gate (gates runtime.spi to show the package still loads); the land goes after the Cells mounts fix. The lab is trimmed to what lands later with world_port: capture_status_within (now taking list args) plus the handle, 5 tests, with no unused definitions left (two cleanup rounds; a removal that ate 4 adjacent nominals was caught and restored). Lab run 4 is compiling.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_5 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:40 main: heal slices drafted and queued as gate-kit gate+land pairs (land waits for Cells erl, live-gui windows and now running eoie-release-* trains, which read the live lib): (1) LIB-UNICODE-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-unicode-granular (char_of validity + UTF-8 encode in Spiral, one host call per arm; new width test) job-1792829476802; (2) LIB-ENV-ARGV %TEMP%\\lib-env-argv-granular (host_argument_count/host_argument one-call arms, offsets and +/- in Spiral, Gleam fn body gone) job-1792829682792; (3) LIB-ASYNC-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-async-granular (poll/context/waker/panic-hook primitives; run_synchronously via rust.loop, catch_rust via a capture-style poll_fn, ignore/sleep via new_future_move; start_child_rust deleted by rule 4; threading.sleep cast -> conv; 3 new Rust tests) job-1792830257981; (4) RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE %TEMP%\\lib-default-clone (default_value' Rust clones via rust.clone; Lua/Python/TS branch in Spiral over one-op is_none') job-1792830472144; (5) LIB-FLOAT-NATIVE %TEMP%\\lib-float-native (format_real: 11 float arms -> one float_native.shortest call; new module float_native.spi: is_nan/is_infinite/native shortest one-calls, precision search for Lua/C/Delphi and scaling search for Lean in Spiral; composes the float-wire slice; gated on all 10 float-wire files, superseding float-wire gates 2-4) job-1792831183650. Withdrawn: lib-otp-single (Rust mailbox blobs), dictm land (x rebuilt it granular, 0 violations, pending its Cells check), reactor-actor Gleam clock arm.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_6 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T10:27Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:27 main: more slices drafted and queued: LIB-SM-LINES-GRANULAR (line_count/line_at/nonempty_* over split/ends_with/slice, last_index_of and percent_encode in Spiral; Gleam fn bodies gone), LIB-SM-INDEX-GRANULAR (index_of on 6 backends, substring casts; composes sm-lines), LIB-BASE-TRY-GRANULAR (one home for the Rust panic primitives; catch_unwind_rust, try_unit, try_finally in Spiral; async rebased onto it), LIB-CRYPTO-GRANULAR (Gleam fn bodies inlined as call expressions; hmac block as primitives), LIB-TRACE-GRANULAR (TS clock, NEAR chunk cast), LIB-XML-GRANULAR (code_point_at/width; composes unicode). The queue is collapsed into serial runners (eoie's process-pile request): main-serial-old-lib-chain, -heal-gates, -heal-lands (dependency order), -other-heavies, -xml; manifests and logs are scratch serial-*.json/.log. Census correction: my first census read only tracked files; with untracked lib modules it is 738 sites / 69,262 chars (new: process.spi 7,304, which tibia is replacing; unicode 600; xml 149; erlang_process/otp_actor/otp_supervisor are thunks around one call, allowed by rule 2, except erlang_process's ProcessDown/PortDown case bridge). Left for later, each needing a platform test first: trace's Rust local clock and NEAR log helper, env.environment_restored's Rust block, sm's Rust lines/nonempty_lines closures.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_7 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:37 main: next lib target by size (not started; plan written for the next pass). guid.spi: (1) new_raw_guid's Zig and Rust arms do the v4 bit layout and hex formatting in host text. Move it to Spiral as guid_v4_text (a b : u64) : string, with the masks, shifts and version/variant bits in Spiral bit ops and hex text from a Spiral digit loop, reusing sm's hex_digit once LIB-SM-LINES lands. (2) Per backend, only random_u64 stays host. Zig keeps `var spiral_random_state` as a declaration, the xorshift step moves to Spiral over a state get/set primitive pair, and the OS seed becomes a rule-3c platform helper. Rust uses RandomState hasher primitives (new/build_hasher, write_u128, finish), each one call, with the SystemTime nanos through unwrap_or_default. Tests: guid's rust/zig/fsharp/gleam rows plus a format check (8-4-4-4-12, version 4, variant 8-b).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_8 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T10:41Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:41 main: guid plan of note 7 drafted after all (%TEMP%\\lib-guid-granular): guid_v4_text and guid_hex in Spiral; Rust random through one-call hasher primitives (rust_unix_nanos, build_hasher, write_u128/u64, finish); Zig keeps zig_random_u64 for now (its xorshift/seed declaration is the remaining guid site). Serial runner main-serial-guid (gate on guid.spi, then land).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_9 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T15:41Z", "main", "main 12:0x: process.spi (user 06:56 list: the multiline block) heal A queued as job main-serial-process-a (gate files process.spi,runtime.spi). The Rust arms now compose the user's own runtime.spi Command lego (new_process_command, process_command_args, setters, spawn, output/status accessors) plus new one-call pieces in runtime.spi: Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit; process_command_stdout/stderr now use their stdio argument (they ignored it and always piped). Argument filtering, success, UTF-8 decoding and the deadline loop (core loop.while' over try_wait and date_time.stopwatch, no raw-brace injection) are Spiral. Probe on a lib copy: Rust compile + rustc + run gives (true, 2 a b, warned, None, None, 0, -1) = every test expectation. Census note: macro-rule.ps1 missed these arms (it does not follow \\ continuations), so its lib count is low. Heal B next: the 165-line Gleam program in process_gleam_prelude becomes one-call @external primitives with the port/selector logic in Spiral over erlang_process (needs module order: process after erlang_process, or a new erlang_port module).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_10 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T15:47Z", "main", "main 12:1x: correction to note 9: process slice A is withdrawn and its job stopped before it took a slot (no live change). process.spi's heal was handed to the tibia team at 04:0x (note 3) and rule (4) already decided it: 0 callers outside process.spi in every repo, so today's module is deleted (tibia-lib-process-delete-gate) and the GREEN granular module in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL lands with its first caller (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP). I missed note 3 because I searched the backlog for the file name and not the item tree. The slice A runtime pieces (Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit) stay as a draft in %TEMP%\\lib-process-granular-a for whoever lands the lab module's Rust half.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_11 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T16:11Z", "main", "main 12:4x: run-the-binary probes ahead of the queued heal gates (CPU minutes instead of gate slots). base-try (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) had 2 hidden failures, both fixed in %TEMP%\\lib-base-try-granular before its slot: (1) Rust: catch_unwind_rust returned rust_caught (std::thread::Result, not Clone) out of rust_quietly's if, and Spiral clones branch results, so rustc E0599 x4; the conversion now happens inside the quiet thunk. (2) Lua: the healed arms ended in $'nil' : t, a bare nil statement Lua rejects; restored the original IIFE (function() return nil end)() (one expression, so within the rule). Probe results: Rust (Some 7, Some -1, true, true) with 0 stderr lines; Lua (true, true). sm-lines (RED 11:55: lines code used split before its definition, and arraym.length) fixed and requeued as main-serial-sm-lines-2 after Rust+Gleam probes matched every expectation.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_12 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T16:18Z", "main", "main 12:2x: sm-index (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) would have thrown at apply: its lines/nonempty_lines anchor was the pre-format text, but LIB-FORMAT-IDLE (landed 08:55) wrapped nonempty_lines' macro with a \\ continuation; and moving lines/nonempty_lines below the healed helpers stranded the test lines_follow_rust_line_rules above them. The apply now cuts the span by position (inl lines .. that test, checked by content markers) and moves the test with the definitions. Composed with sm-lines on one fresh copy: order split 1389, line_parts 1455, lines 1482, test 1500, line_count 1509. Probe: Rust ((2, 2, -1, 1), (bcd, ), 3, 2, a, b, 0) and Gleam ((2, 2, -1, 1), bcd, ) match every expectation; Lua has no substring arm on the tree either (gap, not a regression: the test runs on gleam/rust/fsharp only).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_13 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T17:43Z", "main", "main 13:4x: date_time.spi heal 1 (rule (4) order after sm') queued as job main-serial-date-time-span: hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds Python, Rust and TypeScript arms are one host call each (seconds/microseconds field, num_hours..num_milliseconds, the ms number) with the /, % and casts in Spiral (conv; annotated : i32 per arm since backend_switch record arms do not unify). Probes per accessor, each in its own program: TypeScript (node) 10/11/12/13 after widening the ms arm to i64 (conv of a 3.4e10 ms span to i32 wrapped to -355); Rust through a chrono cargo crate built --offline 10/11/12/13. Python not run locally (its generated main imports a main_auto harness module), covered by the gate's cuda target. Unified unicode landed 12:51 (GREEN 12:48, live = draft).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_14 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:0x: date_time heal 2 queued (job main-serial-date-time-span-2): Rust tick math as one-call num_seconds/subsec_nanos with the scaling in Spiral (rust_time_delta_ticks, used by total_seconds and days), add_days' cast through conv, stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds from as_secs/subsec_millis (no u128 as i64), F# to_timestamp and time_span_of_micros arithmetic in Spiral. Two new tests (spans_convert_to_seconds_and_days, add_days_moves_the_timestamp; F# and Rust): the first exposed a PRE-EXISTING bug, total_seconds truncated to whole seconds (n / 10000000 in i64 before the f64 conversion: 1.5 s gave 1.0 on Rust and F#); fixed as (conv n : f64) / 10000000f64; its one caller converts to i32 anyway. Probes per body: Rust (chrono 0.4.45 cargo crate, --offline) and F# (dotnet fsi) give (1.5, 1.0), 1792152000000000, (1791892800000000, 0); the stopwatch reads 25..5000 ms after a 30 ms sleep. Lua and Python/Cuda arms left for a slice that can run them.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_15 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-24T18:18Z", "main", "main 14:1x: unicode heal LANDED 12:51 (GREEN 12:48). xml heal run 5 RED 13:57 was my own cross-slice break: the unicode heal changed rust_char_code's argument to the new rust_scalar nominal (Spiral char is u8 in Rust), and the xml slice still annotated option' char. Fixed (rust_scalar in the xml helpers and its unicode-presence check), plus a Zig fix found by the probe: convert.convert i32 -> convert.unativeint is identity on Zig (try_convert has no Zig mapping for the nominal), so the index stayed i32; the Zig helper now indexes with conv to u64 (Zig coerces u64 to usize). Probe: Rust (1, 5) and (2, 1); Zig (2, 1). code_point_count has no Zig arm on the tree either (same error on the baseline; the xml Zig tests do not call it). Requeued as main-serial-xml-6. Lib gap noted: convert.convert to unativeint on Zig.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_16 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-24T18:42Z", "main", "main 14:4x: the post-land document_test (main-document, 14:25) is still 15/17: today's lands broke the Cells livemd round trip of two more files, which the lib gate never checked: async.spi (the async heal's primitives block ends without the blank line before /// ### ignore) and the sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair (a missing blank line before /// ### (~#?) and a .spir heading sequence that no longer mirrors the .spi's format_fixed_digits). Fixes: lib-async-roundtrip queued (token-neutral blank line); the sm' pair waits until the 4 pending sm'.spi slices land (canonicalizing it now would move their anchors). The gate kit now runs %TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\roundtrip.exs on every touched .spi/.spir (candidate and tree): a file that round-trips on the tree and not on the candidate turns the verdict RED. Positive control: tree async.spi differs, fixed copy ok, json.spi ok.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_17 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-24T20:28Z", "main", "main 16:3x, correction to note 16 for the sm' pair: not a regression from today's lands. HEAD fails the same document_test; the lib-pair loop stops at its first failure, so sm' surfaced only after json/routes landed. Cause: sm'_real.spir kept 7 empty mirror headings with no .spi counterpart (index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode; their .spi sections were removed before HEAD). apps/cells/spi/document.spi interleave walks the .spir headings and, when one is absent from the rest of the .spi, appends the remaining .spir cells at the end, so the canonical form was not even a fixed point. Slice %TEMP%\\lib-sm-roundtrip\\apply.ps1: drop only empty unmatched .spir headings (refuses if one has content), then write Cells' canonical pair (a fixed point; code lines unchanged, checked; the installed formatter leaves it unchanged; idempotent). Queued: main-serial-sm-roundtrip (gate + land). Rule for later pairs: every .spir heading needs a .spi twin, or the interleave appends the rest.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_18 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 18u32, "2026-10-24T21:49Z", "main", "main 17:5x: LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP landed (gate GREEN 17:15, land 17:47). Live check: the tree's sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair is a Cells canonical fixed point and round-trips byte for byte through livemd (both hashes equal), which is what the document_test lib-pair loop asserts. The next pair in that loop, if any, is the next thing to look at in the post-land document_test.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_19 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 19u32, "2026-10-25T11:39Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:39 -04:00 x: two observations from x's code_points draft (X-GRANULAR-HEAL n10), both in lib/spiral/unicode.spi after the 10-24 unicode heal. (1) first_scalar's Gleam arm is still one macro chaining four host calls: option.from_result(result.map(list.first(string.to_utf_codepoints(s)), string.utf_codepoint_to_int)). With x's draft primitives gleam_codepoints and gleam_codepoint_code, it becomes Spiral over a host-list head bridge. (2) grapheme_list's Rust arm is a graphemes(..).map(..).collect() chain inside Rc::new; x's rust_scalars uses the same chars().collect() shape, so whichever rule decides that one (iterator chain = one host expression or not) applies to both. x lands code_points and is_white_space into unicode.spi with its gate; it doesn't touch first_scalar or grapheme_list.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_20 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:26 tibia takes date_time.spi's time_span_format, its largest host-text logic (rule 4: chars descending). Its TypeScript and Rust arms (two 6-line host programs computing days, hours, minutes, seconds and 100 ns ticks with sign, c format and hh/mm/ss substitution) become one Spiral time_span_ticks_text over i64 ticks, with one primitive per backend: rust_time_span_nanoseconds (num_nanoseconds) and typescript_time_span_milliseconds (the number itself); ticks = nanoseconds / 100 or milliseconds * 10000 in Spiral. Equivalent for whole milliseconds, which is all TypeScript's Date subtraction gives. New test time_span_format_custom_and_negative (rust, typescript; F# excluded because .NET rejects hh-mm-ss as a custom format) pins hh-mm-ss substitution and a negative c format. Slice scratch time-span-heal-apply.ps1 (2 replacements, round trip byte-exact); kit gate job-1792941907856 on date_time, land job-1792941929670 after it. Its anchors don't overlap LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's monotonic_milliseconds insertion, so either can land first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_21 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 21u32, "2026-10-25T15:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:32 date_time.spi second slice, rule (2) casts: the Rust arms of min_value, max_value, unix_epoch, date_time_milliseconds and date_time_utc chained chrono calls with 'as u32' casts inside host text. They become Spiral over five primitives (naive_date_rust; rust_naive_date year month day; rust_date_at, rust_date_at_milli and rust_date_at_nano, one chrono call each) with the casts as conv in Spiral; 0 'as u32' left. New ///> rust -d chrono test calendar_constructors_agree_on_rust (date_time_utc and date_time_milliseconds at 1970-01-01 equal unix_epoch; min_value and max_value run). Slice scratch date-casts-heal-apply.ps1 (7 replacements, round trip byte-exact; anchors don't overlap the time_span or monotonic slices). Gate then land in one job: job-1792942299778 (kit gate-then-land.ps1, the reusable runner from now on).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_22 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 22u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:14 Both date_time gates went RED for kit reasons, and the cells show what each slice actually did. (1) Kit bug: gate.ps1 piped lib_gate into Select-Object -First 16, which stops the upstream script once 16 lines match, so with many reds lib_gate died before copying tests.tsv (the metrics dirs were left in %TEMP%); fixed by capturing the whole lib_gate output to <raw>.gate.log before filtering. (2) The surviving tests.tsv: time_span slice: time_span_format and _2 'failed' only on typescript, as 300 s cell timeouts (compile 190-209 s of each cell under load), while _3 and the new custom-and-negative test passed on rust and typescript; date-casts slice: its new test had a real type error ('=' needs primitive types; date_time is a backend_switch host type), now rewritten to compare chrono %f text for utc, milliseconds 250, unix_epoch, min_value and max_value. The other reds (unix_epoch cuda, date_time_milliseconds fsharp/cuda, to_timestamp*, hours, minutes) flip between the two runs, i.e. load. New gate rule, written before the next run: a candidate-only red counts only if it is red again when its test runs alone (lib_gate.ps1 gained an optional -Test list for --test; additive). Both slices now run in serial-b (job-1792945250381) after the process steps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_23 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 23u32, "2026-10-25T20:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:02 The two date_time slices and LIB-OBJ-TO-STRING-C now gate on the remote kit worker node (kit2's go at 15:3x; node core 826D67C69119F61E built from the tree source; mirror tibia-gates, REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest FE6BB515A8B9E970; kit in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\tibia-kit). The first node run failed in mix because a mirror worktree has no apps/cells/deps (reported to kit2); after mix deps.get in the mirror the rerun is past mix. The local kit gate now takes -Tree, -Gate and -Candidate, and reuses a GREEN verdict newer than its apply script, so when the node verdicts are fetched into the local kit, serial-b's time_span and date-casts steps and serial-c's obj-to-string-c step go straight to their lands instead of re-gating.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_24 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 24u32, "2026-10-25T20:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:51 Node offload result: not usable for lib gates yet. After mix deps.get the Cells run starts, but LIB-OBJ-TO-STRING-C had 204/204 target rows red or not run (c, fsharp, gleam, lua, rust, zig failing; cuda, typescript, delphi, lean, python not run) and the run ends in a mix dependency error: the node lacks the per-backend toolchains and deps the lib tests target. Reported to kit2 with a readiness check (one small lib file gated on node vs review box, equal rows). Node RED verdicts are ignored (the local kit only reuses GREEN); the two date_time slices and obj-to-string-c stay in the local serials (serial-b steps time-span and date-casts; serial-c obj-to-string-c).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_25 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 25u32, "2026-10-25T21:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 17:12 Node rerun, read for signal (verdicts not used): both date_time slices show 51 baseline reds from the node's environment and exactly one candidate-only red each, the slice's NEW rust test, reported 'rust -d chrono: unavailable on this machine (spiral rust runner not found)'; the node baseline marks 18 rust targets unsupported. So beyond environment gaps, nothing in either slice fails where the node can run it; the time_span slice's typescript arm, which only timed out locally under load, ran there without a candidate-only red. Kit note: a new test that is unavailable (not failed) on a target should count as not-run, not red; the local gate keeps its rule until a real run shows the case. Local gates in serial-b remain the deciding ones.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_26 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 26u32, "2026-10-25T22:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 18:41 Node retry after the rust provisioning (17:57-18:2x): no change. The date_time baseline still marks rust -d chrono 18, cuda 21 and lua 12 targets unsupported in a remote.ps1 session, so the slices' new rust tests read 'unavailable' and the verdicts stay unusable. Reported to the compiler lane as a PATH-visibility gap. The date_time heals stay in tibia-serial-heals (job-1792967843150) locally.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_27 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 27u32, "2026-10-26T07:54Z", "tibia-lane", "07:54Z 07:5xZ Correction to note 26: the remote kit worker node now gates lib for real. Gaps gates of job-1792998526851 (Cells tests.tsv read on the node, not just the verdict line):\n- file_system candidate: 28 rows over fsharp, gleam, lua, rust, rust -d chrono, rust -d regex, rust -d sha2 regex and zig. 24 passed, 4 failed, the same 4 as the 27-row baseline. The new path_join_native_uses_the_platform_separator ran on rust and passed. Rust, chrono and lua no longer read 'unavailable'.\n- env (exit_with): 23 of 23 passed over cuda, fsharp, gleam, lua, rust and typescript.\n- am' (create_base, set_base): 42 of 42 passed over cuda, fsharp, lua, python, rust, typescript and zig.\nThese touched files are byte-identical on both boxes (file_system 2D2FE2B3, env 8A982F53, am' 6D5FD000), so the three GREEN verdicts were imported into the local gate kit, and tibia-serial-gaps goes straight to the anchored lands.\nRule for the next node gates: read the candidate tests.tsv. A new test whose row reads unavailable or not run voids the verdict, even when it is GREEN.\nHeal gates on the node need a lib base with the slices they stack on. Job-1792998723460's REDs (process, time_span, date-casts, obj-to-string-c) were all 'did not apply to the copy', never test reds.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_28 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 28u32, "2026-10-26T08:22Z", "tibia-lane", "08:22Z 08:2xZ resultm.spi slice LIB-RESULT-DEBUG (tibia; nobody had claimed resultm).\nBefore: get's and unwrap_err's Rust arms each inlined the same 6-line host program in one macro: a SpiralDebug struct, two traits and two impls doing autoref specialization (Debug output, or the type name for types without Debug), plus the format!.\nAfter: one :~ rust_debug_message (prefix) (x). Its global \"\"\"...\"\"\" holds only the declarations, which rule 3 allows: the struct, both traits and both impls, written once, in base.spi's SpiralUse form. Its body is one host expression: Rc::<str>::from(format!(\"{}{}\", prefix, (&SpiralDebug(&x)).show())). Both arms become `Rust = \\ () => rust_debug_message \"resultm.get / Error x: \" x` (and the unwrap_err text).\n- Why not sm'.format_debug: resultm is module 12 and sm' is module 26, so neither format_debug nor ++# is reachable, and !!!!StaticStringConcat is compile-time only (PartEval.fs:2611).\n- New test rust_debug_message_shows_debug_values (rust): (\"x: 5\", \"s: \\\"a\\\"\").\nProof on the consumer path (scratch lib-result-debug/probe-run.ps1). The same programs are compiled to Rust against the tree lib and against a patched copy, built with rustc and run.\n- resultm.get on Error \"boom\" panics with `resultm.get / Error x: \"boom\"` on both libs.\n- resultm.get on an Error holding a closure (no Debug) panics with `resultm.get / Error x: &alloc::rcs::rc::Rc<dyn core::ops::function::Fn(i32) -> i32>` on both libs, byte-identical. That proves the type-name fallback.\n- rust_debug_message prints `x: 5` and `s: \"a\"`.\nFormatter clean, 0 lines over 120, 1 SpiralDebug declaration instead of 2. resultm.spi base B5AEA6562895C673 is identical on this box and on the remote kit worker node's mirror.\nGate on the node: job-1793002971950-tibia-result-debug-gate (generic lib-gates-job.ps1, which prints every non-passed tests.tsv row). The land step is packed into the queued tibia-serial-gaps, which reuses a GREEN node verdict once it is fetched.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_29 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 29u32, "2026-10-26T08:39Z", "tibia-lane", "08:39Z 08:3xZ Second resultm slice, LIB-TRY-LUA (base.spi and resultm.spi). resultm.from_try kept its own try in four host arms: Lua as 12 host statements (locals, pcall, if/else), Python as a try/except statement block, TypeScript as an IIFE, and F#. That duplicated base.try, the granular single home from main's LIB-BASE-TRY-GRANULAR.\n- from_try becomes Spiral over base.try: `try (\\ () => Ok (fn ()) : result t exn) caught |> optionm.value`, where caught wraps ex_fn's exn in Some (Error ...). All four from_try host arms are gone. Side effect: Rust gains from_try through catch_unwind_rust. Before, Rust had no arm, and a Rust consumer failed to build with 'backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no Rust arm yet'.\n- base.try's Lua arm called fn without catching. It now uses one-call primitives: lua_pcall ({pcall(fn)}, a :! lua_attempt), lua_attempt_ok ([1]), lua_attempt_value ([2] : t) and lua_attempt_error ([2] : exn). The branching is a Spiral if. On Lua this changes the two lib callers of try, and both intend to catch: testing._assert_fn and common.retry_fn (whose retry loop never retried on Lua, because a failure escaped the try).\n- New tests: base try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values and resultm from_try_keeps_values_and_catches_failures, both on lua, fsharp, cuda, rust and typescript.\nStem check: from_try's only callers are convert (F# and Python arms) and file_system (F#), so no repo reaches the deleted Lua or TS arms. Positive control: the same Grep finds resultm.get in dice.\nProof (scratch lib-try-lua/probe-run.ps1): programs compiled against the tree lib and against a copy patched with LIB-RESULT-DEBUG plus this slice, which composes the two resultm slices. Run on Lua (lua 5.1), Rust (rustc) and TypeScript (node).\n- Tree: from_try prints '5 | caught' on Lua and TS, and fails to build on Rust (no arm). base.try prints '5 | caught' on Rust and TS, and on Lua dies with 'probe.lua:26: boom'.\n- Patched: '5 | caught' for from_try and base.try on all three backends.\nFormatter clean on both files, 0 lines over 120. The bases are identical on the remote kit worker node: base 4E28EB421DBB5F8F, resultm B5AEA6562895C673, common D658CC64AF3DA35A.\nGate on the node: job-1793003995132-tibia-try-lua-gate (files base, resultm, common), after the result-debug gate. Lands go into tibia-serial-gaps in the proven order: result-debug, then try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_30 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 30u32, "2026-10-26T08:50Z", "tibia-lane", "08:50Z 08:4xZ Third resultm slice, LIB-RESULT-UNBOX. It deletes dead arms and heals nothing: rule 4 applied at arm level.\n- unbox's Lua arm (8 host statements: locals, nil test, if/else) and its Python arm (the same as a statement block) never run. result'_union () is !+ on Lua and Python, so unbox takes `$'!x ' : result t u` there, and so does box, whose Lua and Python Ok and Error lines are also unreachable.\n- Found by proof: the Lua probe's generated code for unbox was the identity (local v22 = v21) on the tree, and no 'local r = nil' was emitted.\n- The slice removes those 2 blocks and 4 lines. Its apply refuses unless result'_union still reads !+ for both Lua and Python.\nProof (scratch lib-result-unbox/probe-run.ps1): one copy patched with all three resultm slices (result-debug, try-lua, result-unbox), which also proves the anchors compose. On Lua, box (Ok 5), then map''' (+1), then unbox prints 'ok 6', and a nil result' prints 'nil', byte-identical to the tree. Formatter clean.\nLeft in resultm unbox: the F#, Rust and Gleam arms are single host match/case expressions. Bridging them with is_ok plus value primitives would clone the payload on Rust's hot path. That trade-off gets its own decision rule before any slice.\nGate on the node: job-1793004636955-tibia-result-unbox-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_31 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 31u32, "2026-10-26T08:56Z", "tibia-lane", "08:56Z 08:5xZ listm'.spi slice LIB-LIST-CASTS, rule 2 (no as-casts in host text). The Rust arms of fold', fold_back', filter' and distinct' each held `(!list).len() as i32` and `(!list)[!i as usize].clone()`. They now use two one-call primitives: rust_list'_length, which is `($'(!list).len()' : convert.unativeint) |> convert.convert`, the env.spi:150 shape, and rust_list'_item, which converts i to convert.unativeint in Spiral and then indexes `(!list)[!index].clone()`. convert is module 13 and listm' module 14, so convert is reachable.\n- The apply refuses if the length-cast text appears anywhere besides the four loops.\n- Proof (scratch lib-list-casts/probe-run.ps1): one Rust program runs fold', fold_back', filter' and distinct' over box [3; 1; 3; 2], against the tree and a patched copy (rustc, then run). Output is identical on both: Cons(20,30,10,30) | Cons(4,2,4,3) | Cons(2,3,3) | Cons(2,1,3). The generated Rust goes from 6 'len() as i32' casts to 0.\n- Formatter clean. Base 289CECC552F64E5E is identical on the remote kit worker node.\n- Zig's matching @as/@intCast arms stay as they are, because of the noted gap: convert to unativeint is identity on Zig.\nGate on the node: job-1793005022670-tibia-list-casts-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_32 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 32u32, "2026-10-26T09:09Z", "tibia-lane", "09:09Z 09:0xZ testing.spi slice LIB-THROWS-TRY. This is the third copy of try in lib. testing._throws had an is_rust special case over catch_unwind_rust plus F#, Python (statement block), TypeScript (IIFE) and Zig arms. It is now Spiral over base.try: `try (\\ () => fn (); none) caught |> optionm.value`, where caught ex = Some (Some ex). base.try already routes Rust through catch_unwind_rust, so Rust behaves as before. Lua and Gleam gain _throws: before, a Lua consumer failed at run time with 'backend.backend_switch / record_type_try_find'.\nConsumers by stem: dice lib tests calculate_dice_count_5/6/7 (rust), sm' format_exception (fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript), listm' item_2 (fsharp).\nProof (scratch lib-throws-try/probe-run.ps1): _throws on failwith \"test\", then sm'.format_exception, and _throws on a thunk that doesn't throw.\n- Rust: 'test | none' on both tree and patched.\n- TypeScript: 'Error: test | none' on both.\n- Lua, with LIB-TRY-LUA applied first (the land order): 'probe.lua:16: test | none'. The tree fails at run time; this slice alone lets the error escape, because base.try's Lua arm only catches after try-lua.\nFormatter clean; testing.spi base BF2AADFDACD09E2C is identical on the node.\nGate on the node: job-1793005792320-tibia-throws-try-gate (testing and listm'; sm' gates on the node are pending the lib_gate diagnosis, job-1793004142496). The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_33 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 33u32, "2026-10-26T09:22Z", "tibia-lane", "09:22Z 09:2xZ seq.spi slice LIB-SEQ-LENGTH (rule 2, cast). seq.length''s Rust arm $'(!items.len() as `int)' becomes ($'!items.len()' : convert.unativeint) |> convert.convert : int. The annotation is needed: without it, the conversion target inside the backend_switch is an unbound metavar (ETY-ValueRestriction at seq.spi:345). Proof (scratch lib-seq-length/probe-run.ps1): seq.of_list' over box [3; 1; 3; 2; 7], then length', prints 5 with the tree lib and with the patched lib (Rust, rustc); host 'len() as' casts drop from 1 to 0. Formatter clean; seq.spi base 2AAA221CA673F880 is identical on the node. Gate on the node: job-1793006586197-tibia-seq-length-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_34 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 34u32, "2026-10-26T10:00Z", "tibia-lane", "10:00Z 10:0xZ testing.spi slice LIB-CONTAINS-CHAR (rule 2, cast). __assert_contains' Rust arm for a char needle read $'!a.contains(!b as char)'. Spiral char is u8 on Rust, so that was a u8 to char cast in host text. It is now a one-call primitive, rust_char_of_byte (c : char) : unicode.rust_scalar = $'char::from(!c)' (From<u8>, no cast), and the arm reads := scalar = rust_char_of_byte ($'!b ' : char) followed by $'!a.contains(!scalar)'. The primitive lives in testing.spi to stay clear of x's unicode work. Proof (scratch lib-contains-char/probe-run.ps1, with LIB-THROWS-TRY applied first, which composes both testing.spi slices): a Rust program runs _throws over _assert_contains 'r' and then 'z' on \"troll\" and prints 'false | true' on both tree and patched. 'as char' in the generated Rust drops from 3 to 1; the one left comes from sm'_real's format_debug char arm, which is main's file. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. Gate on the node: job-1793008875316-tibia-contains-char-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after throws-try. The testing.spi Zig loops (for, break) at 252-253 remain; they need a Zig slice length/index primitive pair, a follow-up.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_35 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 35u32, "2026-10-26T10:48Z", "tibia-lane", "10:48Z 10:4xZ Correction to note 29 (LIB-TRY-LUA): its first node gate was RED, correctly.\n- What failed: common's retry_fn and retry_fn_2 failed on fsharp, again when run alone, and passed on the baseline. resultm's map/typescript red passed alone, so that one was load.\n- Reproduced locally (scratch lib-try-lua/retry-repro.ps1, an F# consumer of retry_fn): the patched lib fails to compile. On F#, retry_fn's trace calls file_system.trace_file, which calls resultm.from_try with a thunk whose t is unconstrained outside host text.\n- Cause 1: routing F#'s from_try through base.try gave 'Pattern miss' in convert's `from_try ... |> resultm.ok`. The partial evaluator can't match through base.try's host try/with macro.\n- Cause 2: shared local helpers (caught, through_try) at from_try's top level gave 'Got: i64, Expected: result time_span exn' for a date_time caller.\n- Fix to the cut: only the Lua arm changes, written inline in the arm. The 12 host statements become `try (\\ () => Ok (fn ()) : result t exn) caught |> optionm.value`, with base.try's new pcall primitives underneath. F#, Python, TypeScript and Zig keep their original arms. F#'s is already one host expression; Python's and TypeScript's try blocks wait for a rule on try-as-a-statement hosts, and Python has no expression try. Rust stays without a from_try arm, as on the tree, so the new test drops its rust marker.\n- Re-proof: the F# retry_fn consumer compiles and runs like the tree. from_try and base.try print '5 | caught' on Lua and TypeScript, and base.try also on Rust. LIB-THROWS-TRY on top, now including F#, prints 'System.Exception: test | none' on both libs.\n- Re-gate on the node: job-1793011562381-tibia-try-lua-gate2, with files base, resultm, common and convert (convert added as from_try's F# and Python consumer).\nAlso gated on the node: LIB-RESULT-UNBOX GREEN (11 of 11) and LIB-LIST-CASTS GREEN (45 of 45).\nGate-kit fix found by base checks: lib files changed after their node gates ran: listm'.spi (289CECC5 to C6FA3415, 06:07) and file_system.spi (2D2FE2B3 to ACF6BC84). gate.ps1 used to reuse any GREEN verdict newer than its apply script without looking at the base.\n- Verdicts may now carry a `bases file=hash;...` line, and gate.ps1 reuses a verdict only while every listed base matches (tested: matching base reuses, a forged hash re-gates with a stated reason, no bases line behaves as before).\n- The stale LIB-PATH-JOIN-NATIVE import was removed. LIB-LIST-CASTS wasn't imported. Both re-gate locally in tibia-serial-gaps on the real base (both still apply cleanly). The other imports carry bases lines.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_36 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 36u32, "2026-10-26T11:06Z", "tibia-lane", "11:06Z 11:0xZ Pre-land check of the whole tibia-serial-gaps lib stack, after listm'.spi and file_system.spi moved under it (scratch gaps-compose-check.ps1). (1) Each of the 12 packed slice scripts dry-runs cleanly on today's live lib: path-join-native, exit-with, array-base, result-debug, try-lua (revised), result-unbox, list-casts, throws-try, seq-length, par-gleam, contains-char and obj-to-string-c. (2) The 11 gaps slices, applied in land order to one lib copy, all apply, which proves the resultm and testing slices compose. (3) One consumer exercises every touched module: listm'.fold', seq.length', par'.map, resultm.from_try then ok, testing._throws, file_system.path_join_native and env.exit_with. It prints '12 3 14 a\\b | tried 4 | thrown', exit 0, on F# (dotnet fsi) and on Rust (rustc, with the from_try call swapped for Some, since from_try has no Rust arm on the tree either and fails there with 'no Rust arm' plus E0614). Coordination from main's 07:00 relay (both nodes until 21:00): the probes above ran here, CPU-light in a few minutes; heavy lib gates keep going to the remote kit worker node one at a time, and tree-writing lands stay local.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_37 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 37u32, "2026-10-26T11:19Z", "tibia-lane", "11:19Z 11:2xZ date_time.spi slice LIB-DATE-CASTS2 (rule 2: casts and arithmetic out of host text), on the base left by today's time-span and date-casts lands (6D530E6A98F37ED9).\n(1) time_span x, Rust arm: '(chrono::TimeDelta::microseconds((!x as i64) / 10) + chrono::TimeDelta::nanoseconds(((!x as i64) % 10) * 100))' becomes rust_time_delta_of_ticks (conv x). That is Spiral over three one-call primitives, rust_time_delta_microseconds, rust_time_delta_nanoseconds and rust_time_delta_sum, with /10, %10 and *100 in Spiral.\n(2) get_utc_offset: the offset chain stays one host expression returning i32; 'as i64' becomes conv and TimeDelta::seconds its own call.\n(3) guid_sub_micro_digit: timestamp_subsec_nanos as u32, and '/ 100 % 10 as i64' becomes Spiral arithmetic plus conv.\nProof (scratch lib-date-casts2/probe-run.ps1): a cargo crate on chrono 0.4.45. The cached 0.4.23 predates TimeDelta, so it was pinned >= 0.4.40. The same Spiral program, built against the tree and the patched lib, prints identical output: ticks round-trip 15000037 and -15000037 (the negative case exercises truncating / and %), and guid_sub_micro_digit 0. Host 'as i64' in the generated Rust drops from 5 to 3. Formatter clean; the one over-120 line in date_time.spi (153, a Zig line) is already on the tree.\nGate on the remote kit worker node: job-1793013639091-tibia-date-casts2-gate. Its mirror's date_time.spi was synced to the live file first (hash-verified 6D530E6A98F37ED9). If the node again runs 0 date_time tests, the packed tibia-serial-gaps step gates locally.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_38 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 38u32, "2026-10-26T11:32Z", "tibia-lane", "11:32Z 11:3xZ date_time.spi slice LIB-DATE-LUA-OR (rule 1, no and/or fallbacks in Lua host text): 6 sites.\n- os.date: the date and time fallbacks become one-call os.date macros typed optionm'.option' string, then optionm'.default_value' \" (whose Lua arm already does the nil test in Spiral). The year-below-1000 format choice moves into a Spiral ?, and gsub stays one host call.\n- os.time: min_value, max_value, unix_epoch, date_time_milliseconds and date_time_utc each become a one-call os.time typed optionm'.option' date_time (or int), then default_value' with the old fallback: -62135596800, 253402300799 and 0 as date_time literals, and 0i32. 446 and 488 were the same line, so their anchors include the line after.\nProof (scratch lib-date-lua-or/probe-run.ps1, on a copy with LIB-DATE-CASTS2 applied first, so the two date_time slices compose): lua 5.1 prints '-62135596800 253402300799 14400 | 1793012889 1793012889 | 10-26-26 07:08:09 AM | 2026 | 12-31-69 11:04:05 PM' on both tree and patched. That covers min (the year-1 fallback fires on Windows), max, epoch, both built dates, the default and explicit formats, and the year-999 two-digit path. Host ' or ' on os.time/os.date lines in the generated Lua drops from 12 to 0. Formatter clean; new lines at most 120.\nGate on the node: job-1793014240142-tibia-date-lua-or-gate, after date-casts2's. Lands are packed in tibia-serial-gaps in that order.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_39 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 39u32, "2026-10-26T11:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11:58Z 11:5xZ My mistake on the remote kit worker node's gate mirror (tibia-gaps), found and fixed. At 11:1xZ I copied only the live date_time.spi into the mirror's lib, so LIB-DATE-CASTS2 would gate on its real base. That file already uses erlang_term (today's process split), and the mirror's package.spiproj and process modules were still pre-split. Every Cells run on the mirror that loaded date_time then failed to type-check: 'date_time.spi:1133 ETY-UnboundVariable: erlang_term' in monotonic_milliseconds.\nAffected:\n- LIB-PAR-GLEAM's RED: void, every test cell failed to compile on that error.\n- LIB-CONTAINS-CHAR's GREEN: void; its baseline and candidate were both broken, which is why gleam and fsharp rows read failed or not run.\n- the running LIB-TRY-LUA re-gate (try-lua-gate2): stopped, process tree stopped.\nFix: the whole live lib/spiral (1,147 files, without target, _build, .cache and node_modules) was zipped, uploaded, expanded and robocopy /MIR'd over the mirror's lib. The node now matches this box on package.spiproj 7193C5EF, date_time 6D530E6A, process 72A16CF8 and erlang_term B2BA83E4. The queued date-casts2 and date-lua-or gates now run on the consistent mirror. Requeued after them: try-lua-gate3 (job-1793015876485), contains-char-gate2 (job-1793015919659) and par-gleam-gate2.\nRule from now on: a node gate mirror is refreshed as a whole lib snapshot, never one file, and only between gates. Every bases line still names the files a verdict depends on.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_40 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 40u32, "2026-10-26T12:35Z", "tibia-lane", "12:35Z 12:4xZ First two shims under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8). (1) LIB-SHIM-SHA256 (crypto.spi): hash_text's Zig arm was one 247-char blk (an out-param digest, then hash, bytesToHex and dupe), the file's only line over 120. It becomes zig_sha256_hex, whose one host expression calls global spiral_out_sha256_hex: a var digest, then calls only. The arm is Zig => \\ () => zig_sha256_hex input. New ///> zig test zig_sha256_hex_matches_known_digests ('' and '\\n'). Proof (scratch lib-shim-sha256/probe-run.ps1): crypto.hash_text compiled to Zig against the tree and the patched lib, run with zig run, prints e3b0c442...b855 and 01ba4719...546b on both. Lines over 120 in crypto.spi go from 1 to 0. Gate on the node: job-1793017761158-tibia-shim-sha256-gate; land packed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib. (2) LIB-PAR-RUST (spiral_scope_map), see LIB-PAR-MAP note 15. Left: (3) spiral_cfg_local_fields for trace.spi:328. Its proof needs a wasm32 compile against near_sdk besides the Windows run, so it goes through a cargo crate and comes next.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_41 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 41u32, "2026-10-26T12:42Z", "tibia-lane", "12:42Z 12:5xZ Third shim under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8): LIB-SHIM-CLOCK (trace.spi:328, the 1,214-char Rust clock line). Two cfg-class shims in one global, each defined under every cfg so all targets link. spiral_cfg_local_clock() -> Option<[i64; 3]> returns hour, minute and second: GetLocalTime on Windows, localtime_r on unix (secs through c_long::try_from, fields through i64::from: calls, no casts), None on wasm32 and the fallback. spiral_cfg_epoch_nanos() -> u64: near_sdk block_timestamp on wasm32, SystemTime as_nanos through u64::try_from elsewhere. Spiral side: rust_local_clock and rust_epoch_nanos (one host expression each), three one-call field readers, and local_seconds_of_day, which is h*3600 + m*60 + s via conv, or the epoch fallback nanos / 1e9 % 86400 (equal to the old wasm (secs/3600)%24 split for the time of day). trace's Rust arm becomes := seconds_of_day : u64 = local_seconds_of_day (). New ///> rust test local_seconds_of_day_is_within_a_day. Proof (scratch lib-shim-clock/probe-run.ps1): the shim text, extracted from the patched trace.spi with a near_sdk stub module, type-checks (rustc --emit=metadata) on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and wasm32-unknown-unknown. A Rust consumer of local_seconds_of_day prints 31233, equal to PowerShell's local time of day at that second (diff 0). Formatter clean; lines over 120 in trace.spi go from 3 to 2 (the two left are pre-existing). Gate on the node: job-1793018635336-tibia-shim-clock-gate (trace base 52B2D34F matches); land packed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib. With LIB-SHIM-SHA256 and LIB-PAR-RUST this closes the three sites GRANULAR-LINT rule (8) was accepted for.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_42 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 42u32, "2026-10-26T13:32Z", "tibia-lane", "13:32Z 13:2xZ (1) LIB-DATE-CASTS2's node gate is GREEN but deliberately not imported. Its red rows are red on the node baseline too: rust -d chrono to_timestamp, timestamp_round_trip, hours, minutes and seconds, and gleam's guid round trips. Those are exactly the time_span paths the slice touches. The node's chrono runner fails them either way, so the verdict says nothing about the change (the weak-GREEN rule in the remote-node memory). tibia-serial-gaps-lib re-gates it locally on a working chrono; the local cargo probe on chrono 0.4.45 already showed identical tick round trips. LIB-DATE-LUA-OR gets the same treatment unless its lua rows run on the node. (2) Style fix from the list-sugar rule (updated 10-26: never Nil, use [] and | [] =>): LIB-PAR-GLEAM's par'.spi had 8 Nil and now has 0 (backup par'-with-nil.spi). It re-proved: formatter clean, compiles to Gleam and F#, gleam run prints 338350. The node copy was updated before par-gleam-gate2 started. A grep of my other packed slice scripts finds no Nil or 'Cons (' in code.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_43 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 43u32, "2026-10-26T15:59Z", "main", "10-26 11:59 main: lib census 11:5x (scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 over lib/spiral: 684 flagged; TEMP macro-rule-lib-1026.tsv), triaged by hand on the reliable classes (52 casts, 78 arith; most of the 540 'flow' are the known false positives). date_time.spi (16) is tibia's, in flight (LIB-DATE-CASTS2 / LIB-DATE-LUA-OR), as are crypto, trace and par' (rule (8) shims). Not violations: convert.spi's `(!x as u8)`, `as u16/u64/f32/f64` and round_ties_even are the conversion primitives themselves (the cast is that module's job); base.spi 50-56 `1 + 1` are test literals; file_system's 'import * as' rows are JS module syntax, and MAIN_SEPARATOR as u8 is one host constant. Real and unclaimed: listm'.spi's Rust arms (4x `(!list).len() as i32`, 4x `(!list)[!i as usize].clone()`) and Lua arms (6x `[!i + 1]`, Lua's 1-based index). Slice LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS (TEMP lib-listm-index-casts/apply.ps1) adds three one-call primitives before rev': rust_list_length (len() as convert.unativeint, then convert.convert in Spiral), rust_list_item (index converted in Spiral, then [!at].clone()) and lua_list_item (i + 1 in Spiral, then !list[!at]). It moves all 14 sites, leaves 0 host casts or index arithmetic in those arms and 0 lines over 120. Zig arms (`@intCast(!i ...)`) stay until CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT, since convert.convert from unativeint emits an uncast assignment on Zig. Typechecks on a lib copy, and the negative control (a string as rust_list_item's index) is rejected, so the check sees the generic definitions. Chain queued: job-1793030321888 (gate on listm'.spi, rust + lua among its rows, then land). Remaining real convert.spi sites, next: from_str_radix's `(!base) as u32` and the Delphi StrToInt(##36 + !x) prefixes.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_44 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 44u32, "2026-10-26T16:01Z", "main", "10-26 12:01 main: LIB-CONVERT-RADIX drafted and queued (TEMP lib-convert-radix/apply.ps1): convert_i32_base's Rust arm cast '(!base) as u32' in host text. Now ':= radix : u32 = conv base' in Spiral, and the host call takes !radix. One anchored edit, dry run clean, 0 casts left in that arm. convert_i32_base backs the int-from-string conversions, and convert.spi's try_convert test runs on rust, so the gate exercises it. Left in the same function, written down: the Delphi arm's StrToInt(##36 + !x) prefixes ('$'/'%'/'&' + x concatenated in host text) need a run-time concat that convert.spi can't reach (sm'_operators comes later in the module order), so they wait for a Delphi concat primitive placed before convert.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_45 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 45u32, "2026-10-26T16:03Z", "main", "10-26 12:03 main: LIB-AM-TRY-ITEM drafted and queued (TEMP lib-am-try-item/apply.ps1). am'.spi:724: try_item's Rust arm was '(if !i < 0 { None } else { !a.borrow().get(!i as usize).cloned() })', flow plus a cast in host text. The function already had a pure-Spiral in_bounds_item (i >= 0 && i < length a, then index i a), used by the Zig and Lean arms, so the Rust arm becomes 'Rust = in_bounds_item'. That leaves no host code in it, and one implementation for three backends. try_item had no test; the slice adds try_item_is_none_out_of_bounds (rust, zig, fsharp): indices -1, 0, 1, 2 over [10; 20] give [-1; 10; 20; -1] with None coded -1. Option equality is avoided, since union equality is unproven here. Dry run clean, 0 lines over 120. Other am'.spi slices (main's json_scan length_base, tibia's LIB-ARRAY-BASE) edit other anchors, and the land kit's base check re-gates a moved base.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_46 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 46u32, "2026-10-26T16:07Z", "main", "10-26 12:07 main: LIB-SM-FORMAT-PRECISION drafted (TEMP lib-sm-format-precision/apply.ps1): sm'.format_fixed's Rust arm cast '!decimals as usize' in host text; now ':= precision : convert.unativeint = convert.convert decimals' in Spiral, and the host format! takes !precision. Rust isn't routed through format_fixed_digits (as C, Gleam and Zig are), because format_fixed_avoids_ties pins Rust's rounding. Packed into the queued LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF chain (job-1793027588823, its gate not yet started, waiting for a slot): that slice's apply.ps1 now also runs the format step, dry or -Apply, right after its own edit. One whole-file sm'.spi gate (about 2 h under today's load, and noisy) then covers both, instead of two. Proven on a scratch copy of sm'.spi: both edits present, anchors unique. The pre-pack script is kept as apply.before-pack.ps1.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_47 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 47u32, "2026-10-26T17:12Z", "main", "10-26 13:12 main: sm' pack extended and checked (12:5x-13:1x). The packed LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF apply now carries three sm'.spi edits: the Zig index_of/last_index_of arms; format_fixed's precision (conv in Spiral); and strip_prefix, whose '(!prefix) as char' becomes the infallible conversion call char::from(!prefix), Spiral's char being a byte on Rust, with the result typed as the function's option' (rust.ref str). The lib-copy typecheck is GREEN (exit 0) and the control is rejected (exit 5), so the check sees the edit. That control annotates strip_prefix's host call as i32 against the declared option' return. The first control (convert.convert applied to a string literal) was accepted, because try_convert parses strings, so it proved nothing and was replaced, not counted. The first check run was VOID ('Attestation timed out after 30000 ms', exit 3, load). The chain job-1793027588823 gates and lands all three; its gate hasn't started yet (it waits for a slot, behind kit's FOLLOWUP).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_48 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 48u32, "2026-10-26T17:14Z", "main", "10-26 13:14 main: LIB-PARSING-CONCAT-CASTS drafted, checked and queued (TEMP lib-parsing-concat-casts/apply.ps1). parsing.concat_rev's Rust fast path (4+ pieces) cast three times in host text: 'vec![0u8; !total as usize]' and '[!start as usize..!end as usize]'. All three conversions now happen in Spiral (size, from, upto : convert.unativeint via convert.convert), and the host text keeps only the buffer calls. New ///> rust test concat_rev_keeps_byte_order_past_three_pieces: [\"d\"; \"c\"; \"é\"; \"a\"] gives \"aécd\". A multi-byte piece past three pieces reaches the fast path and checks the byte-length offsets, which matters because the path ends in from_utf8_unchecked. It uses a literal \"é\" because unicode comes after parsing in the module order. Lib-copy typecheck GREEN; control rejected (a string returned where the fold carries the i32 offset, exit 5). Chain job-1793034801882: gate on parsing.spi, then land. With this, the lib census's cheap provable sites are all drafted; what remains needs a new primitive or test first (runtime.spi:51 capacity vector, runtime.spi:638 arg_index on F#).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_49 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 49u32, "2026-10-26T17:24Z", "main", "10-26 13:24 main: LIB-RUNTIME-CASTS drafted, checked and queued (TEMP lib-runtime-casts/apply.ps1). I corrected my own note 48: the two runtime.spi sites didn't need a new primitive, because they are the same cast pattern. (1) split_args: Vec::with_capacity(!n as usize) becomes a Spiral 'capacity : convert.unativeint = convert.convert n'; the host text keeps the one allocation call. Its existing ///> rust test split_args covers it. (2) arg_index: the whole is_rust split (Rust 'as usize', F# type-cast text) collapses into 'index : convert.unativeint = convert.convert value' on every backend. Lib-copy typecheck GREEN; control rejected (index bound as i32, exit 5). Chain job-1793035301036. Census rerun 13:2x: every lib host-text cast is now drafted, a convert primitive body, or owned elsewhere. testing.spi:236 '!b as char' is next (char::from, like strip_prefix). date_time.spi:619 is left to fiscal, which has a pending date_time.spi draft. xml.spi:71 is in fiscal's layout land. sm'.spi:1180 is a whole host block (char_indices/map/unwrap_or) and needs its own rewrite with a test.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_50 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 50u32, "2026-10-26T17:27Z", "main", "10-26 13:27 main: sm'.builder_insert Rust arm (sm'.spi:1180-1181) is a whole host block: clone, char_indices().nth(!i as usize), map, unwrap_or(len), insert_str. The plan, by the granular rule: lift the Spiral helpers that already exist into sm', namely xml.code_point_width (xml.spi:23) and xpath_eval's private byte_offset (xpath_eval.spi:301), which walks code points to a byte offset clamped at the length. Then compute 'at' in Spiral, so the host text keeps one call: clone + insert_str at !at (a unativeint from convert.convert). Add ///> rust to test builder_insert (\"ab\" |> insert 1 \"gh\" = \"aghb\"), plus a multi-byte case (\"éb\" |> insert 1 \"x\" = \"éxb\") and a past-end clamp case. On hold by a pre-written condition, not by guess: xml.spi and xpath_eval.spi are both in fiscal's pending landings (LIB-TARGET-GAP layout; S2d node check job-1793034540282), and moving their helpers now would stale those anchors. Resume when fiscal reports both landed, then re-point xml/xpath_eval callers at the sm' helper in the same slice. Module order allows it: sm' comes before xml and xpath_eval.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_51 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 51u32, "2026-10-26T17:28Z", "main", "10-26 13:28 main: LIB-TESTING-CHAR-CAST queued (chain job-1793035684942; TEMP lib-testing-char-cast/apply.ps1). testing.__assert_contains' Rust char arm '!a.contains(!b as char)' becomes '!a.contains(char::from(!b))', the same lossless byte-to-char conversion as sm'.strip_prefix. The lib-copy typecheck is VOID as proof: its control (the arm annotated i32 instead of bool) was ACCEPTED, exit 0. The arm sits inside a '?: t ?| char =>' type-case of a generic function, and untaken type-case arms aren't checked until instantiation. That is unlike plain generic bodies, which are (sm'/parsing/runtime controls rejected). So the proof moves to execution: the slice adds '///> rust' to test assert_contains_2 (\"abcd\" |> _assert_contains 'b'), which instantiates exactly that arm. The rule for accepting the GREEN, written before the run: the gate log must show assert_contains_2 passed on rust. A GREEN without that row is VOID.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_52 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 52u32, "2026-10-27T02:50Z", "tibia-lane", "02:50Z Status of tibia's slices from notes 28-33, after tibia-serial-gaps-lib5 (job-1793062501895, requeued after the kit rename):\n- Landed, gated GREEN: LIB-RESULT-DEBUG (earlier, in gaps-lib4), LIB-RESULT-UNBOX, LIB-THROWS-TRY and LIB-SEQ-LENGTH.\n- LIB-LIST-CASTS: superseded, nothing to land. Its 'Rust loop 1' anchor matched 0 because listm'.spi changed at 20:29 local, where main's LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS replaced every '(!list).len() as i32' and '[!i as usize].clone()' with rust_list_length plus an index-with-conversion primitive (the same heal under different names).\n- LIB-TRY-LUA: gate GREEN. The land got its clear samples, but the apply-time re-check saw a Cells run appear (erl 70016) and refused safely, with no write. Requeued as tibia-lib-try-lua-land2 (job-1793069335405) at the end of the local chain, reusing the GREEN verdict.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_53 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 53u32, "2026-10-27T06:56Z", "tibia-lane", "06:56Z LIB-TRY-LUA LANDED (tibia-lib-try-lua-land3, about 03:00 local, reusing its GREEN verdict).\nFiles: base.spi 2D3CA4A6D144D776 (lua_pcall present) and resultm.spi 33094A9EC47E8A65; the live hashes equal the land's.\nAttempts 1 and 2 expired the land's 90-minute reader wait. The reader that never left was the long-lived Cells compiler daemon (CompilerDaemon.serve_app), which tibia's guard counted and main's lib-gate-kit guard ignores. tibia's process-handle-kit land.ps1 now uses main's filter: it skips the daemon and any --path outside C:\\home\\git\\, waits up to 600 min, and also skips notebooks whose .spi paths all sit in %TEMP% or spiral-bin\\runs. Attempt 3 landed about 15 min after starting.\nAll of tibia's slices from notes 28-33 are now landed or superseded (LIB-LIST-CASTS by main's LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS).")
inl granular_heal () = Task ("GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal host-text drift in every project once and for all (user 2026-10-24 03:0x-03:3x: lib drift like eoie rust_global, unhealable; look at async.start_child_rust; even casts should go through Spiral's type system; the whole purpose of lib is to be granular; groom the backlog to heal this once and for all in all projects including eoie; make it properly granular even if it takes five times more tokens or calls): every Spiral project ends with host macros as one-call primitives and its logic in granular typed Spiral, with a lint that keeps it there", Epic, P0, D7, 30u32, Active, "GRANULAR-LINT,LIB-MACRO-HEAL,EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION,DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL,ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL,APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL,POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL,X-GRANULAR-HEAL,TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", "granular,macros,lib,eoie,x,tibia,dice,alphabet,polyglot,apps,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_heal () = Planning ("GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 9600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: Rule (pre-written, applied mechanically): (1) a host macro ($'...', $\"...\", global, rust_global) is a named primitive holding ONE host call or expression, at most 120 chars, with no as-casts, no arithmetic, no if/match/loop/let/;/blocks; type text in type position is exempt. (2) casts go through conv and the convert types, arithmetic and control flow are Spiral, a host sum type is bridged to a Spiral union by one primitive per constructor and per test. (3) global/rust_global blocks keep only host declarations Spiral cannot express (imports, extern items); function bodies move to Spiral over lib. (4) order per project: file by violating chars descending; a definition with 0 callers by stem across all repos is deleted instead of healed. (5) slice acceptance: tests pass on the same backends, generated output reviewed, the file's census count drops, the lint baseline only shrinks. (6) authorship is irrelevant: user-era and agent-era code are healed alike, the style is what is kept; extra primitives, lines, calls and model hits are the accepted price.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main census (scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 over tracked .spi/.spir; flow regex also counts -> in type text, so flow is an upper bound): sites/chars - eoie/src 786/1,050,302 (202 blocks, 247 casts); spiral/lib 715/60,477 (40 blocks, 69 casts, 106 arith); dice 79/7,187; spiral/apps 63/2,875; alphabet 34/1,340; x 32/2,916; polyglot 13/3,476; tibia 5/232; fiscal/src and blog 0.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:20Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:20 main: rule (2) refinement, written before its first use (async heal): Rust has no expression that extracts a payload from a host sum type, so a bridge primitive may hold ONE match whose arms only map host constructors to Spiral-visible constructors (e.g. Poll::Ready(v) => Some(v), _ => None), with no other logic. A closure inside a macro is allowed only as a thunk around ONE call that the host API requires as a closure (catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(|| poll)), set_hook(Box::new(|_| ()))). Identity retags between two lib types with the same host type ($'!x' : t) are type bridges, as in the user-era rust.from_ref. Everything else stays under rule (1).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:56 main: rule (3b), written before its first use (base.try_*): when a host offers an exception construct only as syntax (F# try/with/finally, Python try/except/finally), ONE such construct whose every body is a single call is allowed, as an expression where the host has one (F#) or as a one-construct helper def where it does not (Python). Lua pcall and Rust catch_unwind are calls, so their results go through one-call primitives and the branching is Spiral. Known trade-off recorded with the heal: the Rust quiet catch swaps the panic hook per call instead of the old once-installed thread-local counter, so two threads catching at the same moment can leave the silent hook installed; Spiral Rust programs that catch across threads should be checked when they appear.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:07Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:07 main: rule (3c), answering x on FS-3: a cfg-gated host declaration is allowed when Spiral cannot express the platform split (Rust type-checks both arms of a runtime cfg!() branch, so platform-only APIs need #[cfg]): every variant has the same name and signature and a body of ONE call (e.g. #[cfg(windows)]/#[cfg(unix)] spiral_fs_symlink_dir), and the result handling stays in Spiral. REPL/module export bindings (let name x = !name x) are declarations, not flow. Wrapper types (SpiralAssertSend) are declarations if their impls hold no logic.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: process.spi's big multiline block is the user's example of drift: granular even at 3x the time and lines, every lego piece in its proper file (tibia's process replace slice follows this) (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "main", "10-25 03:11 main: LESSON from LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF: a heal that rewrites a backend arm must run a test declaring that backend; sm'.index_of's Zig arm was healed into a Rust-only call and passed the gate because its test lists gleam/fsharp/cuda/rust/typescript only. Rule for heal drafts: for each Zig/Lua/Gleam/C arm touched, grep the nearest test's ///> lines for that target, add it when missing.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-25T15:13Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:13 -04:00 x: x's part of the heal, re-censused on tracked .spi (scratchpad x-heal-census.ps1). Rule (1) leaves 3 sites flagged, and the census missed Gleam 'case' as flow, which adds 3 more. All of them are in glyphs.spi except wire.spi:42, a single serde_json::json! call whose braces are a JSON literal, which is one call under rule (1), so it stays. Rule (3) adds glyphs.spi's Gleam global function bodies: x_light, x_white, x_spaced, plus the binary:split extern and its XSplitScope type. Healed in ONE draft, the code-points patch (scratchpad\\code-points\\apply.ps1, lib unicode plus x glyphs, dry run on copies clean). plain_weight = a Spiral fold over lib unicode.code_points with a Spiral light/code_point_weight. blank = unicode.is_white_space over code points, the union of Rust's trim() set and the Gleam x_spaced set. split_all = lib sm'.split_list on both backends, which replaces the Gleam binary:split bridge, its three case macros and listed_pieces. glyphs.spi goes 190 -> 134 lines with 0 host macros and 0 globals, lines over 120 go 9 -> 4, and nothing outside glyphs calls the removed names (grep over C:\\home\\git, positive control: glyphs.spi itself). Noted, not changed: prelude.split_on skips i+1, so it is only right for 1-byte separators; sm'.split_list uses the separator's width. Folding it into lib is X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE's job. Gate when lib is free of Cells readers: Cells on lib unicode (rust+gleam), then x glyphs, splitter and reading (rust+gleam). After that x has 0 rule-1/rule-3 sites.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-25T15:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:17 lib process.spi heal drafted and queued (GRANULAR-HEAL in lib, after LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's split). Scratch process-heal-apply.ps1 (4 replacements in process.spi and runtime.spi, round-tripped byte-exact on a post-split copy) deletes the Gleam prelude (about 130 lines of Gleam source in a global string: externals, open/collect/captured/try_capture glue) and the four host-text Rust blocks of capture_stdout, capture_stdout_list, capture_combined_list and try_capture_combined_list, and rebuilds them in Spiral over the split's primitives: one captured record from captured_by (Rust: runtime shared command output; Gleam: erlang_port with binary, exit_status, args and optional stderr_to_stdout, collected until exit_status), required exits 3 on failure like before (new exit_with: std::process::exit / erlang:halt), try_capture returns None when the program is missing or fails. argument_separator goes (no callers outside process.spi). runtime's command_output becomes Rc-wrapped so it can be read several times, plus ran, code, stdout and stderr readers (one expression each). process.spi: 413 to 318 lines, host macros 17 to 8 (all one expression), Gleam globals 7 to 0, lines over 120 3 to 0. API unchanged for eoie's try_capture_combined_list callers. Gate and land: job-1792941398810 (kit gate on process and runtime, then land), queued after the split's serial.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-26T00:35Z", "main", "10-25 20:35 main: dir-tree-html census after split4 (5 .spi, 21 host-macro lines). 19 are one host call each (rule 1). The drift is tree.spi's host_listing: an 8-line Rust block in a macro that walks read_dir, skips symlinks, classifies dir and file, and encodes 'flag len\\\\nname' rows that tree.spi decodes again (parse, parse_digits, digit_value). host_file_len carries a map/unwrap_or closure. Lib-first: lib has read_dir_names, directory_exists and file_exists but no symlink test or file length, so the heal is 2 slices. (1) lib (%TEMP%\\lib-fs-link-length\\apply.ps1, gate-kit form): file_system gains rust_metadata_len, rust_symlink_metadata_of and rust_metadata_is_symlink (one-expression primitives), plus rust_file_length and rust_is_symlink with the flow in Spiral, the rust_last_write_ms style. A rust test writes 'abc' and links a target dir, then expects ((3, 0), (false, true, false)): the link is the symlink positive control. file_system.spi 1,647 -> 1,677 lines, no new line over 120. (2) dir-tree-html (%TEMP%\\dir-tree-heal\\apply.ps1, 5 anchored edits, dry-run clean on the repo): listing = read_dir_names folded through child_of (rust_is_symlink skips, directory_exists 1, file_exists 0, otherwise dropped), the 3 decoder functions deleted, and host_file_len replaced by file_system.rust_file_length. tree.spi 150 -> 124 lines, host macros 2 -> 0. Gate: n11's rule (%TEMP%\\dir-tree-heal\\proof.ps1: reference = the current repo, healed = a copy plus the apply; both build.ps1 --self-test; healed units at most 1,000 lines; byte-identical HTML for spiral/scripts, x/scripts and dir-tree-html; 0 host macros in tree.spi). Queued for after tibia's PR-28 block as one serial job: lib gate, lib land, proof.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-26T00:36Z", "main", "10-25 20:36 main: dir-tree-html's second drift site, deliberately left out of the tree heal. dir_tree_html.spi:16-18 host_read_text runs lines().join(newline) inside its macro: CRLF to LF, exactly one trailing newline dropped, a read failure gives an empty string. Spiral equivalent, checked case by case against str::lines: replace CRLF with LF, then drop one trailing LF (the empty string and a lone LF give empty; a lone CR stays, as lines() keeps it). Lib's trim_end_suffix drops EVERY trailing copy, so it doesn't fit; the heal slices one. Kept separate because the root unit dir_tree_html.rs is at 990 of 1,000 lines and sm'.replace/ends_with pull lib code into it: it lands after the tree heal's proof, with its own size check, and if the root crosses 1,000 the planned cut (host I/O out of the root) goes first. The root's other macros stay as they are: each is one host call (rule 1), and lib's read_all_text/write_all_text/create_dir_all panic on failure where dir-tree-html reports exit codes, so swapping them would change error behavior.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-26T01:44Z", "main", "10-25 21:44 main: block-macro census of main's code (rule-(1) violations: a host macro holding a { ... } block). Continued lines alone are not drift: most are single long calls wrapped at 120. lib 36 blocks in 30 files (rust/near 10, leptos 5, sm' 3, date_time 1, file_system 1, ...); spiral/apps 5 (cells console.spi 4, apps/spiral 1); polyglot chat_contract 5 (its dist/ twin is generated); alphabet documents 1; dir-tree-html tree.spi 1 (heal queued); dice 0. Cells' console.spi first (main's app). It is the notebook console prelude, embedded into cell.ex at compile time (@external_resource), so every lane's notebook output passes through it. Of its 4 blocks: (a) write_line's Rust arm (write text, newline, flush in one block) becomes 3 one-call macros, each io::Result .is_ok() bound to a discarded bool (macro text is opaque to dead-code elimination, so none is dropped); the same global stdout, flushed last, so the output is identical. (b, c) both Zig arms '{ spiralPrintAny(x); spiralPrint(newline); }' become two unit macros (statements since pack-1w). (d) show_value's Rust arm is an autoref-specialization trick (Debug when available, else the type name) built from trait impls Spiral can't express; that is a rule-(3) case (declare the trait machinery once as a global, then one call), left for a design pass. Draft %TEMP%\\cells-console-heal\\apply.ps1 (3 anchored edits, each exactly once, 0 lines over 120). Gate main-cells-console-heal-verify (job-1792979017941): mix test cells_test.exs on full copies of unhealed and healed Cells; GREEN = the healed run fails nothing the baseline doesn't, and tests ran. Caveat: if cells_test.exs runs no Zig program, the Zig half is uncovered and needs a Zig console probe before landing.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-26T01:47Z", "main", "10-25 21:47 main: the console heal's gate was strengthened before it ran (the job was still waiting for a heavy slot, so it reads the new script). cells_test.exs has no Zig, and its console tests stub compile, rustc and execute (cells_test.exs:656), so they barely exercise console.spi's real codegen; targets_test's Zig tests stub the toolchain too. Added a direct compiler probe on each side: a package with that side's console.spi as a module plus main = console.write_line \"probe-line\"; console.write_line 42i32, compiled with the installed single-flight compiler to Rust (rustc --edition 2024) and Zig (zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast), both run natively. GREEN now also needs both backends to print exactly probe-line|42 with exit 0 on both sides. That covers the Zig half the caveat named.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_14 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-26T01:50Z", "main", "10-25 21:50 main: alphabet's only block macro (apps/documents/documents.spi:283-289, documents.run's Rust arm: a host stack-based recursive read_dir walk collecting .md files that are not .hangul.md and contain the optional filter) is exactly what %TEMP%\\alphabet-heal\\heal3.ps1 already heals (a Spiral walk over file_system.read_dir_names plus directory_exists, ending in am'.new_vec), blocked since this morning only on new_vec (LIB-NEW-VEC-RUNTIME). Walk order doesn't matter: the files are processed with async.par_map, and the non-Rust arm's async walkdir has arbitrary order anyway. heal3 snapshots documents.spi/.rs and restores both on a RED build; alphabet is clean in git. Queued as main-alphabet-heal3 after main-serial-new-vec-runtime (the lib gate+land of the new_vec fix), and added to quiet.ps1's tree-writer list. GREEN = build.ps1 -SkipNotebook passes the run check with the exact nota.hangul.md. dice mirrors: dice has 0 block macros (census), so nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_15 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-26T01:51Z", "main", "10-25 21:51 main: lib rust/near.spi's 10 block macros classified (the census's largest lib count): they are NOT mechanical heals. 8 of 10 (new_vector, keccak512, random_seed, block_timestamp, block_height, epoch_height, account_balance, predecessor_account_id, signer_account_id) are one pattern: a '#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")] let v = near_sdk::env::...;' real call beside a '#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))] let v = <native stub>;' and then v, inside a block because Rust's cfg attributes attach to statements and items. Both halves are the Rust backend, differing only in rustc's target, so backend_switch can't split them, and moving them into a global fn pair is rule-(3) drift too. The real fix is a design gap: a Rust target-arch dimension that Spiral can select on (e.g. a RustWasm arm, or a cfg-aware backend_switch key), after which each site is two one-call arms. Belongs with the backend-keys work (BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES), not mechanical healing. The 10th, vector_extend ('{ (!vector).extend((!vec).clone()); }'), is a single call in needless braces, mechanically a unit macro '(!vector).extend((!vec).clone())'; it rides the next near.spi lib slice, since alone it is not worth a gate run. Corrected lib target list for mechanical heals: leptos 5, sm' 3, date_time 1, file_system 1, then the rest; near's 9 cfg sites wait on the design.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_16 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-26T01:55Z", "main", "10-25 21:55 main: lib sm'.spi block macros: 2 of 3 healed in one slice, %TEMP%\\lib-sm-heal-blocks\\apply.ps1 (gate-kit form, 2 anchored edits each exactly once, 0 lines over 120). (1) builder_append's Rust arm '{ let mut r = !sb.clone(); r.push_str(&*!item); r }' is one call, '[(!sb).as_str(), &*!item].concat()' : string_builder (Rust string_builder is String, String::from in the constructor, so concat returns the same type); covered by builder_append/builder_replace/builder_clear, which declare rust. (2) byte_slice's Rust arm drops its needless clone binding: 'Box::leak((!s).as_bytes().to_vec().into_boxed_slice())'. It had NO test, so per the 10-25 LESSON a rust test is added: byte_count of \"abc\" is 3 and of e-acute is 2 (UTF-8 bytes, through am'.slice_to_vec and vec_len). (3) builder_insert stays: its Rust arm turns a char index into a byte offset (char_indices().nth().map().unwrap_or()), which is flow, and needs a Spiral char-index helper first. Queued main-serial-sm-heal-blocks after the json-canonical chain: lib gate on sm'.spi, then the staged land.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_17 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-26T02:01Z", "main", "10-25 22:01 main: census refined. A '{ call; }' macro holding ONE statement and no let/if/for/while/match/loop is a unit-type adapter, not flow drift: Effect::new returns an Effect handle and dispatch_local a value, and the braces turn that into the () the macro is typed as, which a bare call would not do on Rust (Rust's unit-macro emission isn't documented as a statement; Zig's is, since pack-1w). Recount: lib 23 real blocks plus 13 unit wrappers (leptos' 4 'blocks' are such wrappers, and its tag_raw '\"{ !children }\"' is a format-string literal, a false positive); spiral/apps 5; polyglot 3 (+1 wrapper, dist/ excluded); alphabet 1; dir-tree-html 1. lib's 23: near 9 (cfg design), sm' 3 (2 queued), trace 2 (cfg design: trace_raw and trace's wasm32 seconds_of_day), lib console 2, date_time 1, runtime 1 (the big Command builder; process-heal territory), others 1 each. Next slice drafted: %TEMP%\\lib-console-trace-blocks\\apply.ps1, 4 anchored edits. console.write's Rust arm '{ use std::io::Write; print!; stdout().flush().unwrap(); }' becomes 2 unit calls (print!, then the fully qualified std::io::Write::flush, so no use is needed); console.write_line's Zig arm becomes 2 statements (as in the Cells heal); trace.get_trace_state_or_init's '{ spiral_trace_hold(&$0) }' loses its needless braces (an expression: same value). console's write/write_line had NO direct test, so one is added after write: write \"a\"; write_line \"b\" on rust and zig (proves both rewritten arms compile and run; stdout isn't asserted). trace's arm is covered by trace_2 (rust). Pending-slice check: lib-trace-arm-lambda and lib-trace-granular refuse on missing anchors (landed 10-24), lone-then finds 0 sites. Queued main-serial-console-trace-blocks after the sm' chain.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_18 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 18u32, "2026-10-26T02:09Z", "main", "10-25 22:09 main: polyglot apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi classified (3 real blocks after the adapter rule; its dist/ twin is a generated copy and is excluded). (1) is_valid_alias:39 is validation logic in host code: 0 < len < 64 (Rust .len(), BYTES), no leading or trailing '-', every char alphanumeric (Unicode, char::is_alphanumeric) or '-'. It heals over lib sm' (length, starts_with, ends_with, a char fold), but the byte-vs-char unit of each lib function must match Rust's exactly, or the contract's alias rule silently changes for non-ASCII input. (2) generate_cid:65 builds a CIDv1: unsigned-varint codec 0x55, sha2 SHA-256 multihash with the 0x12 0x20 prefix, concatenated as version 1 + codec + multihash; it heals over lib crypto's hasher plus am' vec concat, with a known-answer test (a fixed content, its expected CID bytes). (3) account_alias:122-131 holds near storage-map mutations with an ordered re-index (sort_unstable_by_key on the stored index); the heaviest, and it needs the near collections' Spiral API first. The two single-call sites at :126 and the like are unit adapters. Not drafted tonight on purpose: proving any of them needs the contract's own build on its pinned NEAR toolchain (nightly-2024-07-14) plus its ///> rust -c tests, and polyglot is the user's repo. Order when taken: (2) first (pure function, known-answer test), then (1) with an explicit non-ASCII alias test, then (3) after the near collection API.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_19 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 19u32, "2026-10-26T04:35Z", "main", "10-26 00:35 main: Cells console heal is GREEN on reread (node job-1792988600908). The decisive probe: Rust and Zig both print probe-line|42 exit 0, identically on unhealed and healed copies. mix test ran 38 tests per side (Result: 37/38 passed both). The 1 failure is the same test on both sides, cells_test.exs:679, which asserts apps/cells/mix.exs relative to the copy's own location (a copy artifact). The verify said RED because my summary parser expected ExUnit's 'N tests, M failures' while Cells prints 'Result: N/M passed'; parser fixed. Landing through main-cells-console-land (job-1792989196828): it waits for a 2-second Cells-reader gap, then applies if console.spi still hashes as verified.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_20 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 20u32, "2026-10-26T04:55Z", "tibia-lane", "04:55Z tibia lane takes alphabet's real block from main's n17 census (alphabet 1): apps/documents/documents.spi:283, the Rust arm of run's file listing. It is one macro holding a hand-written DFS: let mut out, a stack Vec, while let pop, read_dir, flatten, is_dir push, and a .md / not .hangul.md / filter test. Second site, 746: main's 'if exit_code != 0 { std::process::exit }'. Heal plan, rules written before drafting. (1) Keep the order exactly: a Spiral join point over the stack as a list (head = top, pop = head, subdirectories pushed in read_dir order, so the last pushed comes first), files appended in visit order; downstream does no visible sort. (2) Keep the path text exactly: the original prints PathBuf::join output (backslash on Windows), and lib's (</>) joins with '/' (file_system.spi:375, tests 'dir/file'), so it can't be used. A one-call native join primitive (Path::new(dir).join(name).display()) goes into lib file_system first, as a lib gap with a rust test, unless one exists. (3) Primitives only: read_dir_names (exists, errors skipped like flatten), directory_exists, the new join, and sm' ends_with/contains (argument order as in the '_' arm). The predicate markdown_file and the walk are Spiral. (4) 746 becomes '? exit_code <> 0 ?> exit_with exit_code' over a one-call exit primitive (lib has none; process.spi only inlines std::process::exit inside blocks). (5) Acceptance: alphabet's documents app builds on Rust, and a listing over a fixture tree (nested dirs, .md, .hangul.md, a filter) is byte-identical between the unhealed and healed builds. The build is a node offload once the node core matches (asked the compiler lane 00:5x). Mirror rule: dice has no such site, so nothing mirrors.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_21 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 21u32, "2026-10-26T06:19Z", "tibia-lane", "06:19Z Alphabet documents heal (note 20 plan) is drafted and type-checked; not landed. Lib side: file_system.path_join_native (scratch lib-path-join/lib-path-join-native.ps1: Rust arm Path::new(&*$0).join(&*$1).display() through sm'.from_std_string, F# Path.Combine, default </>, plus a rust test). A scratch lib copy compiles file_system.spi as the entry on Rust and F#. Its gate-then-land is queued in tibia-serial-heals after date-casts-heal (the kit now holds Global\\spiral-lib-land). Alphabet side: scratch alphabet-heal/documents-heal-apply.ps1 (dry run by default; both anchors must match exactly once) replaces the 662-char Rust walk macro with `markdown_walk filter has_filter [ dist_dir ] (am'.new_vec Nil)`, a stack-list join point (head = top, subdirectories pushed in read_dir order, files appended in visit order, read errors skipped like flatten) over read_dir_names, directory_exists, path_join_native and the pure predicate markdown_file. A precedence bug in the first draft was caught before compiling: `has_filter |> not || x` parses as `has_filter |> (not || x)`; it now uses explicit `= !-`. The healed file is formatter-clean, the census shows the real block gone (left: 2 F# export adapters and the exit check at 758, which waits for an exit primitive after process-heal lands, since runtime.spi and process.spi are both in that slice), and it compiles to Rust against the lib copy with the native join in documents.rs. Remaining proof (consumer path): a cargo build of apps/documents plus a fixture-tree listing (nested dirs, .md, .hangul.md, a filter) byte-identical between the unhealed and healed builds, as a node job after path_join_native lands.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_22 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 22u32, "2026-10-26T06:29Z", "main", "10-26 02:29 main: dir-tree-html's second drift site drafted: %TEMP%\\dir-tree-read-text\\apply.ps1 replaces shared.spi's host_read_text (read_to_string + lines().join inside a macro) with read_lines_text in Spiral over lib file_system.read, sm'.string_from_utf8 (both unboxed, so a read or UTF-8 failure gives \"\" as before), sm'.replace CRLF to LF and drop_final_newline (drops exactly one trailing LF; a lone LF gives \"\"). The call site in readme_html is renamed with it. No lib slice is needed: every piece is an existing lib primitive. Pass rule, written before the run: proof.ps1 builds the repo and a healed copy through build.ps1 (incl. --self-test), and the HTML must be byte-identical for spiral\\scripts, dir-tree-html itself and 7 README fixtures (CRLF with non-ASCII, a lone CR, CR CR LF with two trailing LF, a lone LF, empty, no final LF with an emoji, invalid UTF-8); no .rs over 1,000 lines; no read_to_string or lines() left in shared.spi. Queued as job-1792995570428-main-dir-tree-read-text-proof (heavy). It touches only shared.spi, so it composes with the pending tree.spi heal.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_23 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 23u32, "2026-10-26T06:35Z", "tibia-lane", "06:35Z Lib gap for two granular sites, drafted and proven on the consumer path: env.exit_with (scratch lib-exit-with/lib-exit-with.ps1; Rust std::process::exit, F# exit, TypeScript process.exit, Python sys.exit, otherwise failwith; env.spi is not touched by any queued slice, and .NET puts this in Environment). Proof: a probe package against the patched lib copy compiled to Rust, built with plain rustc, printed \"before exit\", exited 3, and never printed \"after exit\". A Cells test cannot exercise exit without killing its own run, so its gate checks that env.spi still compiles and its tests pass, and the consumers prove the behaviour. Queued: gate-then-land LIB-EXIT-WITH (-Files env) in tibia-serial-heals after path-join-native. Consumers ready as dry runs: (1) spiral/apps/spiral/spiral.spi:405 `{ eprintln!(\"{}\", error); std::process::exit(1) }` becomes console.write_error_line error (whose Rust arm is the same eprintln!) plus env.exit_with 1 (scratch lib-exit-with/spiral-cli-heal-apply.ps1, anchor matches once); it lands with the spiral.rs regeneration and a CLI build on the node after exit_with lands. (2) alphabet documents.spi:758 `if exit_code != 0 { exit }` becomes ? exit_code <> 0 ?> env.exit_with exit_code.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_24 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 24u32, "2026-10-26T06:48Z", "tibia-lane", "06:48Z Alphabet walk heal proven behaviourally (scratch alphabet-heal/walk-probe-run.ps1). One probe program holds the ORIGINAL Rust walk macro, copied verbatim from documents.spi, and the healed markdown_walk. It compiles to Rust against the patched lib copy, builds with plain rustc, and runs both over a fixture tree (nested a/b/c, a/bb, e; .md, .hangul.md, .txt). Unfiltered: 6 files each, identical in order (native backslash paths, LIFO directory order, .hangul.md and .txt excluded). Filter \"notes\": 1 file each, identical. The first filter, \"b\", matched the fixture path itself and was replaced by a discriminating one. Left before landing: path_join_native and exit_with land (queued in heals), then documents-heal-apply.ps1 -Apply plus the app's cargo build on the node.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_25 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 25u32, "2026-10-26T07:11Z", "tibia-lane", "07:11Z Lib gates moved to the remote node (user 10-26: use the node better). gate.ps1 reuses a GREEN verdict file newer than its apply script, so the node runs the gates and the local queue only lands (anchored eoie do plus Global\\spiral-lib-land, after the local reader check). Node mirror tibia-gaps (spiral d095f13d, digest-verified at 03:0x). Job 1, job-1792998526851-tibia-gaps-gates: LIB-PATH-JOIN-NATIVE (file_system), LIB-EXIT-WITH (env), LIB-ARRAY-BASE (am'). Job 2, job-1792998723460-tibia-heals-gates, after job 1: LIB-OBJ-TO-STRING-C (sm', with the typed C arm), LIB-TIME-SPAN-HEAL and LIB-DATE-CASTS-HEAL (date_time), LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (process, runtime), LIB-RANDOM-LUA (common). LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT stays local: its apply reads spiral\\lab\\LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT\\shared_command.spi. Next: fetch each node verdict-<Name>.txt into scratch process-handle-kit; a GREEN one skips that gate in tibia-serial-lib, -heals and -gaps, and a RED one gets diagnosed from the node gate logs before its local turn.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_26 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 26u32, "2026-10-26T07:38Z", "main", "10-26 03:38 main: polyglot chat_contract is_valid_alias heal, in 2 slices. (1) lib: unicode.rust_is_alphanumeric (c : char) = one host call (char::is_alphanumeric) plus a Rust test over iter.all (Latin, Hebrew, '-', U+200B). Anchored before gleam_erlang_characters, disjoint from x's code-points anchor (nfc). Gate + land chain job-1792996967739-main-serial-unicode-alphanumeric. (2) %TEMP%\\polyglot-alias-heal\\apply.ps1: the 3-line host block becomes Spiral over lib: sm'.length (Rust emits .len(), bytes, the same unit as alias.len()), starts_with/ends_with \"-\", and sm'.chars |> iter.all (rust_is_alphanumeric || '-'). Two tests added: the 64 limit counts bytes (63 a / 64 a / 31 e-acute = 62 bytes / 32 e-acute = 64 bytes) and chars are Unicode alphanumeric (e-acute ok, '_', U+200B and a leading '-' rejected, an inner '--' allowed). Both pass on the old code by construction, so they guard the heal. Pass rule, written first: build.ps1 -SkipTests on a reference and a healed copy (notebook tests, Spiral -> Rust, nightly wasm build, export check); GREEN = healed exit 0, identical wasm export lists, no host is_alphanumeric() left; VOID if the reference fails on its own or the lib slice isn't landed. Queued as job-1793000241647-main-polyglot-alias-heal-proof after the lib chain. The NEAR sandbox tests (tests/build.ps1) are not run; they need the sandbox binary.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_27 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 27u32, "2026-10-26T09:26Z", "tibia-lane", "09:26Z 09:3xZ lib census ranked by macro length (scratch granular-census.ps1 -Top N, new switch). 25 largest flagged sites, sorted into owners:\n- In tibia's queue: common:140 (random Rust state, next to LIB-RANDOM-LUA), date_time:858 (time_span TS IIFE, LIB-TIME-SPAN-HEAL in tibia-serial-heals), process.spi:185/204/217/232/246 (LIB-PROCESS-HEAL, heals), resultm:79/130 (LIB-RESULT-DEBUG, gating on the node).\n- main's sm' work: sm':1427 (Lua split) and sm':1460 (Zig split).\n- False positives (text inside strings, or a multi-line match across strings): leptos class and svg path strings, xpath_eval:296, json_schema:131.\n- Unclaimed, needing design before a slice:\n  (a) trace.spi:328, a 1.3k-char Rust block. The local seconds of day under four cfg branches (wasm32 NEAR block_timestamp, Windows GetLocalTime FFI, unix localtime_r FFI, fallback SystemTime), with a let tuple and as-casts. Spiral can't express cfg, so the pre-written rule is: the cfg'd extern declarations and #[repr(C)] structs go to global (rule 3). Each platform read becomes one cfg'd one-call primitive returning raw fields (block_timestamp nanos; the St [u16; 8]; the Tm [i32; 16]; unix secs). The h/m/s selection, `as u64` widening and arithmetic move to Spiral over conv. The remaining question is whether one cfg-dispatching host fn counts as a declaration. Decide with main's GRANULAR-LINT 'block' class before slicing. Proof shape: the same Rust program on Windows, plus wasm32 compile-only, against tree and candidate; equal HH:MM:SS within one second.\n  (b) crypto.spi:177, Zig sha256 hex: a blk with an out-param digest, hash and bytesToHex. Zig has no expression form for an out-param call, so it needs either a declared helper (a body in global) or a Spiral array plus a hash-into primitive. Same question as (a).\nThis session's slices (LIB-MACRO-HEAL notes 28-33): LIB-RESULT-DEBUG, LIB-TRY-LUA, LIB-RESULT-UNBOX (dead arms deleted), LIB-LIST-CASTS, LIB-THROWS-TRY and LIB-SEQ-LENGTH. Each is proven on the consumer path and gating on the remote kit worker node, with lands packed into tibia-serial-gaps.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_28 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 28u32, "2026-10-26T09:44Z", "main", "10-26 05:44 main: lost-job audit (10-26 05:4x): 123 main jobs were stopped while waiting (live-block holds), and 42 labels never ran again. Two of those were real losses and are requeued: main-dir-tree-repo-build-2 (post-rename ledger) and main-test-blocks-families (job-1793007377286). Of the 14 lost lib lands, I dry-ran each apply script against a copy of today's lib. 5 anchors match 0 times (json-unicode, generic-audit, whitespace, default-clone, otp-single) and 2 target spans are missing (env-argv, base-try), so they most likely landed through a later route: json-unicode's \\u and surrogate-pair handling is in json_scan (hex4, paired low half, U+FFFD for lone halves); my first keyword check missed it. 3 guard themselves (dictm/dictm-granular: dictm.spi exists; crypto: Gleam heal already applied; async-granular and sm-index: blocked on their base heals). Two still need action: lib-unicode-granular (unicode.spi changed since it was drafted, now also by x's 05:18 land, so rebase it before any requeue) and lib-reactor-actor (needs lib-step-init first). The cause of the losses is gone: pause-main.ps1 snapshots waiting jobs and requeue-paused.ps1 rebuilds them.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_29 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 29u32, "2026-10-26T09:57Z", "main", "10-26 05:57 main: fs re-land failed for lack of a reader gap, not on content: main-reland-fs-link-length staged file_system.spi at 01:35 (rust_file_length, rust_is_symlink + test), then waited 3 h for a gap in lib readers. The kit release train (RESUME) read the live lib continuously from 00:59. It gave up at 04:35, exit 1, nothing committed. So dir-tree-heal-proof-2 went RED for the expected reason: the healed tree.spi calls the missing functions ('File tree has a type error', compile exit 5), while its reference build and self-test passed. Kit stopped RESUME at 04:4x, so the chain is requeued unchanged as job-1793008572392-main-serial-reland-fs-2 (re-land, then the heal proof).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_30 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 30u32, "2026-10-26T10:01Z", "main", "10-26 06:01 main: correction to n28: lib-unicode-granular needs no rebase. It already landed. Its base is commit 0ec501fb's unicode.spi (SHA-256 matched), and a three-way merge of base, today's file and the heal conflicts only on spelling: today's unicode.spi has exactly the healed is_scalar_value, utf8_lead, utf8_continuation and utf8_text in edition-3 spelling (:= and \\), where the heal wrote inl and fun. It landed, then the 10-24 lib respell rewrote it, so its whole-file hash guard no longer matched. Of the 14 lost lib lands, only lib-reactor-actor (waiting on lib-step-init) is left open.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_31 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 31u32, "2026-10-26T10:02Z", "main", "10-26 06:02 main: correction to n28/n30: lib-reactor-actor also landed (step_runtime.spi:130 reactor_actor plus its test at :140, edition-3 spelling). Its guards look for word spelling ('type actor s m = { init', 'inl reactor_actor'), which the 10-24 respell rewrote to ':== actor s m' and ':= reactor_actor', so its dry run misreported 'no init thunk yet'. lib-step-init had landed on 10-24 (land-3 exit 0). Result of the lost-land audit: none of the 14 is missing. The real losses tonight were the dir-tree repo build and the test-blocks run, both requeued. Lesson for drafts: a guard that matches spelled text goes stale across a respell. Anchor on spelling-neutral text (names, string literals) or check with the compiler.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_32 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 32u32, "2026-10-26T10:09Z", "main", "10-26 06:09 main: zig-index-of-2 gate RED (05:51) on a single candidate-only red: show_floats_in_python_repr_layout/delphi 'disagrees'. The slice (%TEMP%\\lib-zig-index-of\\apply.ps1) changes only the Zig arms of sm'.index_of and last_index_of and adds ///> zig to their two tests; no Delphi code changes. Same compiler on both sides: the baseline (35 min later) has that Delphi row passing and Lean failing, the candidate has Delphi disagreeing and Lean passing, a flip-flop on float output tests. Hypothesis: flaky under load. Rule, written before the rerun: rerun the gate once (job main-serial-zig-index-of-4). The same Delphi disagreement again means a real investigation of Delphi float printing; any other pattern means flake, and the slice lands on its GREEN. Also seen: show_floats/zig now passes on the candidate (it failed on the baseline), which is the slice's own fix showing.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_33 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 33u32, "2026-10-26T10:11Z", "main", "10-26 06:11 main: dir-tree-html read-text heal LANDED (unstaged) 06:1x: shared.spi's host_read_text (read_to_string + lines().join inside a macro) is now read_lines_text in Spiral over lib file_system.read, sm'.string_from_utf8, sm'.replace and drop_final_newline. Proof GREEN by its pre-written rule: both builds and --self-test pass; HTML byte-identical on 9 dirs incl. the 7 README fixtures (CRLF with non-ASCII, lone CR, CR CR LF, lone LF, empty, no final LF with an emoji, invalid UTF-8); no unit over 1,000 lines; 0 host read_to_string/lines() left. The queued run (job-1792995570428) had built both sides GREEN and then died on two proof-script bugs, both PowerShell traps: [char]0x1F600 is outside the 16-bit char range (now [char]::ConvertFromUtf32), and GetBytes('') unrolls to null on assignment. The comparison was rerun on the built exes with -SkipBuild. The live shared.spi equals the proof's healed copy by hash, and the proof's reference copy equaled the live file before the apply. Left for TREE-PAGES-V2: the tree.spi heal (reland chain job-1793008572392), then main-dir-tree-repo-build-2.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_34 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 34u32, "2026-10-26T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-26 06:30 x: net.spi's two host-logic macros (local_day_start's and_then closure, local_midnight's map_or closure) healed and LANDED in x: one host call per step (day_of, day_zoned, utc_text) with the branches in Spiral; net notebook passed on the node (job-1793004081457). x's granular census: 0 flagged macros, 0 closures in host text.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_35 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 35u32, "2026-10-26T13:05Z", "main", "10-26 09:05 main: dir-tree-html tree.spi heal LANDED (unstaged) 09:0x. The chain main-serial-reland-fs-2 first landed LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH (lib file_system), then ran main-dir-tree-heal-proof-2 GREEN by its pre-written rule. Both builds plus --self-test pass. HTML is byte-identical on 3 trees (spiral/scripts 52CD6D8E, x/scripts 6F1D9B7B, dir-tree-html B4E1567F). Every generated unit is under 1,000 lines (inline 912, selftest 837, tree 737, markdown 491, root 450). 0 host macros are left in tree.spi. Before the apply, the live tree.spi equaled the proof's reference copy (E291DB48); after it, the file equals the proof's healed copy (E21582FC). Next, already queued: the args/clock heal (%TEMP%\\dir-tree-args-lib, job main-dir-tree-args-lib-proof-2). It drops dir_tree_html.spi's host_arg_count, host_arg and host_error_line for lib env.try_command_line_argument and console.write_error_line, and shared.spi's process id + nanos casts for lib guid. Its proof copies the live repo, so it also builds the tree heal with it, and adds an error-path check (no operands: exit 2, same stderr bytes). dir-tree-html's macro census after both: 0 casts, 0 flow.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_36 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 36u32, "2026-10-26T13:22Z", "main", "10-26 09:22 main: LIB-UNICODE-ALPHANUMERIC redesigned after its second RED (08:15). This was no flake: on the Rust backend Spiral's char is a byte (u8) and sm'.chars yields bytes, so 'rust_is_alphanumeric (c : char) = c.is_alphanumeric()' did not compile (E0599: u8 has only is_ascii_alphanumeric), and the gate reported the test as failed. Reproduced by a probe on a lib copy (TEMP unicode-probe/run.ps1). The drafted polyglot alias heal (n18 item 1) had the same flaw: it iterated sm'.chars (bytes), while the host alias.chars() iterates Unicode scalars. Applied, it would have silently changed the contract's alias rule for non-ASCII input. New design, mirroring unicode.is_white_space (an i32 code-point predicate): unicode.is_alphanumeric (c : i32), whose Rust arm does conv to u32 in Spiral, then rust_char_of_u32, then optionm'.map over rust_scalar_is_alphanumeric (one host call on rust_char), defaulting to false; the other backends fail loudly. Probe on Rust: [a 7 233 1488 - 8203 55296] gives true true true true false false false (55296, an unpaired surrogate, yields None, hence false), and code_points of 'a7' + 233 + 1488 gives 97 55 233 1488. Two ///> rust tests are in the slice. The alias heal now folds over unicode.code_points with 'unicode.is_alphanumeric c || c = 45i32', the same unit as Rust's chars(); its byte-length check stays sm'.length (bytes, as the host's len()). Queued: job-1793020861797-main-serial-unicode-alphanumeric-3, which gates unicode.spi, lands, then runs the alias proof (the wasm exports must stay identical).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_37 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 37u32, "2026-10-26T14:09Z", "main", "10-26 10:09 main: census of main's repos re-run 07:4x (scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 over dice, alphabet, blog, polyglot, dir-tree-html, spiral/apps; 96 flagged rows; TEMP macro-rule-main-1026.tsv), triaged by hand since the regex classifier over-reports. dice 25: all false positives, or allowed: nearblocks/rpc struct-literal constructors ({ field: !x }), dice.spi `let f x = !f` export shims, dice_ui.spi:9 `let _ = ...` (unit adapter, n17 (A)), global item declarations. Its one real site is dice_contract.spi:52 `$0 as u32`, a cast inside an old-style !\\\\ macro with drain(..).collect(); it needs a lib drain wrapper and a wasm contract build proof (queued as the next dice slice, mirrored in alphabet per the dice rule). alphabet 3: documents.spi:746 `if code != 0 { exit }` is real and covered by tibia's alphabet-documents-heal after LIB-EXIT-WITH; the 2 export shims are allowed. dir-tree-html 5 casts: all 5 are covered by the queued args/clock heal and the landed tree heal, leaving 0 after both. polyglot 12: chat_contract is_valid_alias (main, in the unicode-3 chain) and generate_cid (tibia, landing); :126 is a one-statement unit adapter; lib/math math.spi:555 exit (tibia's polyglot-exit-heal); math.spi:276 is a pyo3 attach closure (rule (8) scope class); println! debug lines in math.spi (96/254/263) are one host call each (allowed). spiral/apps 51: 46 are $\"...\" interpolations (false positives: interpolation, not flow); real is apps/spiral spiral.spi:405 `{ eprintln!(...); std::process::exit(1) }`, which heals to console.write_error_line + env.exit_with once LIB-EXIT-WITH lands; cells console.spi `&[_]u8{10}` is a Zig literal (false positive). Classifier fixes for the eoie verb (GRANULAR-LINT): $\"...\" is not flow, a `{ field: !x }` struct literal is not flow, a Zig `&[_]T{...}` literal is not flow.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_38 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 38u32, "2026-10-26T14:15Z", "tibia-lane", "14:15Z 14:3xZ terrain raw-ops conversion packed. The paren-aware converter (scratch lib-array-base/terrain-array-wrappers.ps1) runs on the terrain slice's candidate, since terrain-slice lands first (slices-c2). It converts 23 raw ops (!!!!ArrayCreate, ArrayIndexSet, ArrayIndex) onto am'.create_base, set_base and index_base, with 0 raw ops left and 0 lines over 120. It lands at the end of tibia-serial-gaps-lib3, after LIB-ARRAY-BASE in the same job, behind two guards: am'.spi has create_base, and src/terrain.spi equals the terrain slice's candidate (the control). The land goes through slice-land with the terrain slice's own gate files (terrain, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim, gpu_sim, so C and WGSL rows) and restores on red. Scratch terrain-raw/. Main's json_scan arrays slice (length_base) rides on the same LIB-ARRAY-BASE land.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_39 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 39u32, "2026-10-26T14:15Z", "main", "10-26 10:15 main: dice_contract.spi:52 heal drafted (dice census row; alphabet has no contract, so the dice-mirror rule has nothing to mirror). The old-style macro '!\\\\(seed_excess_len, \\$'\"!seeds.drain(0..$0 as u32).collect::<Vec<_>>()\"')' casts in host text. It becomes 'seeds |> near.vector_drain_front (conv seed_excess_len : u32)', with the cast in Spiral. The new lib wrapper (TEMP lib-near-drain/apply.ps1) sits beside vector_extend and vector_to_vec in rust/near.spi: one host expression drain(0..n).collect(), in the same shape as vector_to_vec's iter().cloned().collect(). Both targets support it: on wasm the store Vector's drain(Range<u32>); off wasm, near.spi's SpiralNearVec stand-in defines drain(Range<u32>) too (near.spi:37). Chain queued (job-1793024094867): kit gate on rust/near.spi (only a static test block, so the gate shows only that the candidate compiles), land, then the consumer proof (TEMP dice-drain-heal/proof.ps1). That proof copies contract/ with members rewritten to contract alone, plus Cargo.lock and LICENSE and a deps junction, then runs build.ps1 -SkipTests 1 on both sides: the notebook incl. contribute_seed, Spiral -> Rust, the nightly wasm build and the 6-method export check. GREEN iff the healed build exits 0, the export lists are identical and 0 host casts are left. On GREEN: apply.ps1 -Contract C:\\home\\git\\dice\\contract -Apply (unstaged).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_40 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 40u32, "2026-10-26T15:13Z", "main", "10-26 11:13 main: zig-index-of-4 gate (the 06:09 rule's one rerun) is RED again at 08:25, and the rule's split applies: this is not 'the same Delphi disagreement'. Candidate reds: 31 rows. Unchanged-tree baseline reds: 22 rows. The two sets are almost disjoint and spread across code the slice never touches (concat_list/fsharp, range/cuda, format/lua, replace_regex/rust), while show_floats/delphi is red on BOTH sides, so it is not candidate-only. The gate had already flagged cell timeouts and compiler-start lock timeouts and rerun the file once. Under 100% CPU on both boxes, a whole-file gate of sm'.spi (hundreds of cells) is noise. Verdict class: VOID by environment, not a slice fault. The slice (Zig arms of index_of and last_index_of only, plus ///> zig on their two tests) still applies cleanly (dry run 11:1x). Requeued as job-1793027588823-main-serial-zig-index-of-5 (gate, then land). It runs when FIFO order reaches it, likely in a lighter window; a GREEN there lands it.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_41 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 41u32, "2026-10-26T17:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:39 fiscal: lib date_time.spi granular heals, in one draft (fiscal session scratchpad bench-ours/micros_transform.py -> draft 989E9F87 over live 6D530E6A, 0 shadow clashes; main left date_time to fiscal to avoid a collision). (1) time_span's Rust arm (main's pointer, :619): the tick arithmetic moves to Spiral ('conv x : i64', '/ 10i64', '(% 10i64) * 100i64') with one host call per macro (TimeDelta::microseconds, TimeDelta::nanoseconds, '!whole + !part'); test time_span_keeps_sub_microsecond_ticks round-trips 15 / 10^7 / 0 ticks via the already-granular rust_time_delta_ticks. (2) timestamp_gleam_of (:25): seconds = x / 1000000i64 and nanos = (x % 1000000i64) * 1000i64 in Spiral, then one host call. Same semantics (Spiral i64 / and % compile to Gleam's own operators), covered by date_time's 11 Gleam test markers through the micros -> calendar path (:379, :1057). (3) New stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds (granular, Rust as_secs + subsec_micros) with its test. Deliberately NOT healed: the Lua arms (:380 math.floor(micros / 1000000), :693, :742), Python :452 and Cuda :661, because whether Spiral i64 '/' compiles to Lua 5.1's floor division there is unverified, and a wrong heal would silently change WoW-target arithmetic; they need a Lua codegen probe first. Gate: date_time suite (rust + gleam rows) when a slot frees.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_42 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 42u32, "2026-10-26T17:42Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:42 fiscal: the date_time Lua arms' boundary resolved by READING the codegen (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:169178-169203, no compile). Spiral i64 on Lua is spiral_w64: a plain number on the fast path, else a two-half table; '/' is divmod = (a - math.fmod(a, b)) / b, i.e. TRUNCATION toward zero (like C / Rust). The host arms floor (:380 'math.floor(!micros / 1000000)'), and floor != trunc for negative operands = pre-1970 instants. So a naive '/' heal would introduce a calendar bug. The faithful heal reuses date_time's own granular floor_div (:161, negative-correct): 'Lua = \\\\ () => floor_div micros 1000000i64 |> convert.unbox : date_time' (the file's convert.unbox idiom for Lua 'number'); same shape for :693 / :742. Left UNAPPLIED: convert.unbox to 'number' is sound only on the w64 plain-number path (current micros ~1.7e15 < 2^53 stay there, but that is an argument, not a proof), and date_time has 0 '///> lua' test rows. Unblock: one Lua test row in date_time (pre- and post-1970 micros -> seconds) for this heal to land against.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_43 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 43u32, "2026-10-26T17:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:46 fiscal: CORRECTION to note 42. date_time has 12 '///> lua' test rows, not 0 (I wrote 0 before running the count; the count was in the same call). None of them sends a pre-1970 instant through to_calendar_utc, so the point stood, and it is now closed with a discriminating test instead of left open. Applied in the date_time draft (AE7BDB78 over 6D530E6A, 0 clashes): (a) :380 Lua 'floor_div micros 1000000i64 |> convert.unbox : date_time' (date_time's own negative-correct floor_div, defined above at :161); (b) :691-692 Gleam timestamp-of-date: 2 host reads (to_unix_seconds_and_nanoseconds .0 / .1), then 'seconds * 1000000i64 + nanos / 1000i64' in Spiral (nanos >= 0, so '/' is exact); (c) new '///> lua' test pre_epoch_micros_floor_to_the_second_below_on_lua: '(to_calendar_utc (timestamp -1i64) = to_calendar_utc (timestamp -1000000i64)) |> _assert_eq true'. Lua date_time has second resolution, so floor maps both to -1 s, while a truncating heal maps -1 us to 0 and FAILS. It passes on today's host math.floor too, so it is a pure regression guard and needs no time-zone-dependent format. Still deliberately unapplied, with the semantics recorded: :742 (Lua '/' is FLOAT division, so time_span keeps fractional seconds; a heal must go through f64, not i64 '/') and :693 (number date -> i64 needs a pinned conversion first).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_44 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 44u32, "2026-10-26T17:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:48 fiscal: the Lua guard test from note 43 is revised before any gate. It compared two date_time values with '=', and nothing in date_time ever compares or asserts on a date_time (an opaque backend_switch type; on Lua a host 'number'), so the equality constraint was an untested assumption. Now it follows the file's own timestamp-assert idiom (to_timestamp tests :709-736, '_assert_eq'' on 'n |> timestamp', timestamp = nominal i64): 'timestamp -1i64 |> to_calendar_utc |> to_timestamp |> _assert_eq'' (-1000000i64 |> timestamp)'. Floor puts -1 us on the -1 s date, and back through to_timestamp's Lua arm that is -1000000; a truncating heal gives 0 and fails. It needs no time zone (to_calendar_utc / to_timestamp on Lua are pure arithmetic; the existing to_timestamp test excludes Lua, likely because its unix_epoch uses local-time os.time). Draft date_time 56C01D79.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_45 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 45u32, "2026-10-26T17:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 13:58 fiscal: date_time heals packaged as a transform-based landing (fiscal session scratchpad landings/date-time-granular: apply.ps1 derives from the LIVE date_time.spi into a temp probe, refuses with nothing written if an anchor moved or the change is already in, then copies; gate-only.ps1 = kit gate LIB-DATE-TIME-GRANULAR on date_time.spi; land.ps1). Proven on a copy of today's live: derives 56C01D79, byte-identical to the draft; a second apply on the healed copy refuses (no double apply). Cells runs each test block on its own '///>' markers, so the gate exercises the new Lua guard on Lua and the stopwatch / time_span tests on Rust. Queue condition: a free local test-suite window (kit's release follow-up and main's serial jobs hold the slots now). date_time stays on main's respell exclusion list until it lands.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_46 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 46u32, "2026-10-26T18:18Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:18 fiscal: date_time draft 56C01D79 TYPE-CHECKS (seconds, no gate). 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp date_time.spi' run in a temp lib copy (lib/spiral plus its deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core): live exit 0 in 21 s, draft exit 0 in 27 s. Positive control: multiplying by the string 'broken' in the unreached timestamp_gleam_of heal FAILS ('date_time.spi:26:35: ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Got: i64 Expected: string In the definition: timestamp_gleam_of'), so the check covers every definition, including arms no entry reaches. All 5 heals (stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds; time_span Rust; timestamp_gleam_of; to_calendar_utc Lua via floor_div; timestamp Gleam) type-check. The new test BODIES are not covered (only the Cells gate parses test blocks), so the kit gate LIB-DATE-TIME-GRANULAR still decides; this removes the risk of a gate run lost to a module type error.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_47 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 47u32, "2026-10-26T18:59Z", "main", "10-26 14:59 main: 10-26 15:0x main: cube-core-math-proof-3 TIMED OUT (exit 124 at its 3 h cap, holding heavy slot 1 since 11:15) with no output: the proof buffered the build log in memory, and BuildSpiral's progress lines are Write-Host (information stream), which 2>&1 misses. Narrowed by measurement: (1) the reference copy's cube.c was never rewritten, so the stall preceded the first compile's write; (2) a direct --backend C compile of the same cube.spi takes 17 s (exit 0), so it isn't the compiler; (3) polyglot/deps/spiral resolves to C:\\home\\git\\spiral and the single-flight dll exists, so it isn't a compiler bootstrap. Remaining suspects, all before the first write and inside the job context: sourcing scripts/core.ps1 or lib.ps1, GetTargetDir, or Ensure-SpiralCompiler's Resolve-SpiralDotnet. Fix to the proof: the build now runs with *>&1 teed to build-<side>.log, so a killed run still names its last step. Requeued as proof-4 in the post-rename requeue (extras).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_48 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 48u32, "2026-10-26T19:16Z", "main", "10-26 15:16 main: 10-26 15:1x main: ROOT CAUSE of cube-core-math-proof-3's 3 h timeout found and fixed. polyglot apps/spiral/temp/cube contains a directory SYMLINK, lib -> C:\\home\\git\\polyglot\\lib. robocopy /E follows directory links, so the proof's copy walked all of polyglot's lib through it; a probe copy reached 386 files against the source's 13 before I stopped it. The copy never finished, so the build never started, which matches the reference copy's untouched files and missing deps junction. Disproved along the way, by measurement: the compiler (a direct C compile takes 17 s), junction creation (6.7 s), and the compiler bootstrap. Fix in TEMP cube-core-math/proof.ps1: robocopy /XJD /R:2 /W:2 with exit checks; the copy recreates each source link dir as a symlink to the same target (lib -> polyglot/lib); cleanup deletes every reparse point, deepest first, before Remove-Item -Recurse. Verified: the bounded copy takes 1.7 s for 13 files, the lib link is recreated, the cleanup leaves polyglot/lib intact (539 files), and the build log is now teed to a file. Other proof sources checked for followed links: dice/ui, dice/contract, blog have 0; dir-tree-html has deps/spiral, which its proofs exclude. proof-4 stays in the post-rename requeue.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_49 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 49u32, "2026-10-26T19:33Z", "main", "10-26 15:33 main: 10-26 15:3x main: the last unclaimed site of the main-repo flow/arith census (13:3x), polyglot lib/math/math.spi:551, is drafted (TEMP math-test-name/apply.ps1). main's test name came from ONE host block holding two concerns: SPIRAL_TEST_NAME.with(...).unwrap_or_else(|| Rc::from(std::env::args().nth(1).unwrap_or_default())). Now: test_name_or_first_argument () makes one host call for the thread-local read, typed optionm'.option' string and unboxed to option, and on None goes to first_argument (), which is lib env.try_command_line_argument 0i32 (lib applies the program-name offset, so it equals args().nth(1)) with \"\" when absent. These are two helpers instead of nested matches, per the parser-trap memory. Anchored, count 1, 0 host argv/fallback code left, 0 new lines over 120. The exit line (555) still waits on LIB-EXIT-WITH. Proof: math-test-name/fix-run.ps1 runs the consumer build itself (lib/math/build.ps1 -SkipNotebook: BuildSpiral, then cargo's 12 Rust tests, each through spiral_test(name), which sets SPIRAL_TEST_NAME, so the Some branch runs 12 times). Rule, written before the run: GREEN iff build exit 0 AND 'test result: ok. 12 passed' is in the teed log; else restore math.spi and math.rs from backups, verified by hash. Queued in the post-rename requeue (main-math-test-name-fix). With this, every unclaimed census site in main's repos is drafted or owned elsewhere.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_50 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 50u32, "2026-10-26T23:59Z", "main", "10-26 19:59 main: 10-26 19:59 main: the dice_contract heal's lib half (LIB-NEAR-DRAIN, near.vector_drain_front, chain main-serial-near-drain, job-1793024094867) gate went RED at 18:16. Read from its log: near.spi has no test rows on either side, so the only check was Cells' livemd round trip, which reported 'candidate differs, tree ok'. Cause, reproduced with a diff variant of the gate's roundtrip.exs on a candidate copy: the round trip adds a blank line before the vector_to_vec heading (line 121). The slice's here-string drops its final newline, so insert + one newline + anchor left no blank line after the new definition. All 12 other closing-brace-to-heading boundaries in near.spi have one. Fix: two newlines (TEMP lib-near-drain/apply.ps1). Proven: a fresh candidate now round-trips byte-identical (279 = 279 lines), and the control (live near.spi) round-trips clean. The land step refused correctly ('gate verdict is not GREEN'). The chain's dice proof step will exit VOID on its own guard (lib not landed), so nothing ran on the wrong base. Requeued: job-1793059114753-main-serial-near-drain-2 (gate -> land -> dice proof). Lesson for every slice script: a definition inserted before an anchor heading needs a blank line between them, or the livemd round trip check fails.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_51 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 51u32, "2026-10-27T00:05Z", "main", "10-26 20:05 main: 10-26 20:05 main: after near-drain's RED, swept 16 of main's queued lib slices through the gate's own Cells livemd round trip (scratchpad slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1: each slice applied to a lib copy, every touched file round-tripped, first differing line printed; control: live near.spi must round-trip, held). Then each differing file's LIVE version was round-tripped, because the gate is RED only when the candidate differs AND the tree is ok. Results: 2 would-be REDs, both the same here-string defect (an insert ends at one newline straight before a heading). (1) lib-crypto-ed25519 (crypto.spi, at 'hash_to_port'; chain main-serial-crypto-ed25519, post-rename requeue). (2) lib-parsing-concat-casts (parsing.spi, at 'string_of'; chain main-serial-parsing-concat-casts, queued, gate not reached yet). Both fixed (insert + newline + anchor) and re-swept: both round-trip ok now. 8 others differ but their live files already fail the round trip, so the gate passes them by rule: json_scan, file_system, runtime, listm', dictm, reflection, xml, xpath_eval, xpath. That pre-existing round-trip drift in 9 lib files is its own finding, since a file that doesn't round-trip loses the gate's livemd check entirely. 10 slices round-trip ok: near-drain (fixed), sm-linear, testing-char-cast, sm-format-precision, zig-index-of, am-try-item, convert-radix, and nil-literal's cli_args, cursor_walk, oauth, resultm, testing. command-line-arguments and unicode-alphanumeric are already landed.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_52 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 52u32, "2026-10-27T00:07Z", "main", "10-26 20:07 main: 10-26 20:09 main: CORRECTION to n51's count: 9 slice-touched lib files differ in the round trip with their live version failing too (json_scan, file_system, runtime, listm', dictm, reflection, xml, xpath_eval, xpath), not 8. One cause found, by printing the round-trip head: xml.spi. Cells' to_livemd stores a cell's source inside an HTML comment (<!-- livebook:{...} -->), and xml.spi's own string literal \"-->\" (the XML comment terminator its parser scans for) closes that comment early. The rest of the metadata JSON then leaks into the parsed document as a stray first line, the round trip gains 4 lines (400 -> 404), and the gate's livemd check is blind for xml.spi. Of the 9 files, only xml.spi contains \"-->\", so the other 8 differ for other reasons: the round-trip drift tracked by CELLS-PACK-NEXT n21's probe and LIB-MACRO-HEAL n16/n18. The fix belongs in Cells' livemd writer: escape \"-->\" inside the livebook attrs JSON (for example as -->, which JSON decoding restores). Cells is shared code; not changed here.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_53 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 53u32, "2026-10-27T00:18Z", "main", "10-26 20:18 main: 10-26 20:16 main: (timestamp fix first: n52's text says 20:09, but it was posted at 20:07; times here come from the clock.) xml.spi's livemd round trip has TWO independent causes, now separated by a 2x2 run (Cells patched in memory via Code.compile_file over the main-tree build; nothing landed). (1) Cells bug: to_livemd puts each smart cell's attrs JSON inside '<!-- livebook:... -->', and xml.spi's literal \"-->\" ended that comment early. Fix drafted: TEMP cells-livemd-arrow/apply.ps1, one pipe after the encode, |> String.replace(\"-->\", \"--\\\\u003e\"), which JSON decoding restores. (2) Source defect: xml.spi line 219 (the end of a test block) runs straight into ':= element_children' with no blank line, and Cells' canonical form inserts one. Results: unpatched Cells + xml as is: false; unpatched + blank line: false; patched + as is: false; patched + blank line: true. Both fixes are needed. No-regression check: over all 77 lib .spi files, the Cells patch changed no file's round-trip state (60 ok before, 60 after). My pre-registered rule ('xml flips and nothing else changes') came out RED, because it assumed a single cause; the 2x2 is the evidence, and the rule is not relaxed. Not landed: the Cells change needs the Cells mix test gate (shared code, read live by other lanes' runs), and the xml blank line is a one-line lib slice for the next lib gate batch. Both are drafts, landing-ready.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_54 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 54u32, "2026-10-27T00:22Z", "main", "10-26 20:22 main: 10-26 20:22 main: two follow-ups to n51-n53. (1) lib-fs-junction (carries directory_create_junction, file_is_read_only and file_set_read_only for PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN; chain main-serial-fs-junction, post-rename requeue) inserted its definitions straight before the file_type heading and its tests straight before the rust heading, with no blank line. Its gate would pass by rule, since live file_system.spi already fails the round trip, but the slice made the canonical form worse. Both inserts now leave exactly one blank line (verified on a copy: blank before each heading, no tripled newlines). (2) The xml.spi blank-line source fix from n53 is a queued slice: TEMP lib-xml-blank-line/apply.ps1 (anchor once, dry run clean), requeue extra main-serial-xml-blank-line (gate -> land) AFTER job-1793022977380-main-serial-nil-literal-2, which also edits xml.spi. With the Cells escape (TEMP cells-livemd-arrow) it makes xml.spi round-trip.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_55 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 55u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "main", "10-26 20:55 main: 10-26 20:27 main: the 8 other lib files that fail Cells' livemd round trip, classified by each first differing line (one batch run). (a) Missing blank line before a block, the same source defect as xml.spi: reflection.spi:113 (before the get_union_fields_untag heading), xpath_eval.spi:546 and xpath.spi:596 (before a ///> rust test). Each is a one-line lib fix like lib-xml-blank-line. (b) An extra blank line right after a top heading that Cells' canonical form drops: json_scan.spi:2, runtime.spi:1157 (after the shared commands heading), dictm.spi:6. file_system.spi shows a mix (1693 -> 1694 lines). (c) A CELLS DATA-LOSS BUG, in listm'.spi:45: the round trip RENAMES 'test map4_' to 'test map4'. Mechanism, read in apps/cells/spi/document.spi: when a test's name differs from its heading-derived name, the writer adds an explicit '#### <name> (test)' heading (line 832) to carry the exact name. On read, heading_test_name (line 306-308) passes that explicit heading through the same strip_underscores as prose headings, so a name's own trailing underscore is cut. Exposure today: 1 lib test (map4_ is the only test name ending in _), but it sits exactly on lib's convention that a trailing underscore marks the runtime-loop form (try_find_, take_, zip_with_). Proposed fix: in heading_test_name, when the line ends in ' (test)' (the explicit-name marker), keep test_name_go's result verbatim and apply strip_underscores only to prose headings. Needs a Cells regeneration plus the Cells gate; not done tonight.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_56 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 56u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "main", "10-26 20:55 main: 10-26 20:51 main: round-trip drift, last classification before wrap (held note: backlog writes are blocked by the wrap and rename guard). Of the 9 lib files that fail Cells' livemd round trip: (1) reflection, xpath_eval and xpath each lacked one blank line before a block. Fix drafted: TEMP lib-blank-line-gaps/apply.ps1 (heading-side anchors, so it's valid before or after the Nil batch, and idempotent). On copies, all three then round-trip clean. It rides the nil-literal-3 requeue chain after the Nil batch and the xml fix. (2) xml needs that chain's xml blank-line slice plus the Cells '-->' escape (TEMP cells-livemd-arrow, proven on all 77 lib files). (3) json_scan, runtime, dictm and file_system differ ONLY in blank lines (verified: identical once empty lines are dropped), so adopting Cells' canonical layout would make them round-trip with no code change. Open question before drafting it: Cells drops a blank line after a top heading (for example after the json_scan title), and the formatter keeps authored breaks. So check that 'SpiralCompiler format --check' accepts the canonical text first, or the change only moves the failure from one check to another. (4) listm' differs only by the Cells test-name bug (map4_ -> map4). The fix is drafted, unproven: TEMP cells-test-name-underscore/apply.ps1. When (1)-(4) land, all 77 lib files round-trip, and the gate's livemd check covers every file again.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_57 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 57u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "main", "10-26 20:55 main: 10-26 20:55 main: two closing facts. (1) The open question in the previous note is answered by execution. 'SpiralCompiler format --check' on the ORIGINAL and on the Cells-CANONICAL text of json_scan, runtime, dictm and file_system flags only file_system.spi, identically on both sides (a pre-existing formatter difference). So for json_scan, runtime and dictm, the canonical blank-line layout is format-neutral and can be adopted with no code change, which leaves the gate's livemd check covering them. file_system first needs its own formatter fix. (2) CORRECTION to my 20:37 report: the 'lease authority blocked ... should_wrap=1' refusals were not this session's wrap window. Per kit's post-install note, kit.exe reads only KIT_* variables, and shells started before the rename lacked KIT_LEASE_POLICY. With the User KIT_* variables loaded into the process, writes go through. All 25 main chains were requeued at 20:53 under kit.exe; json-scan-arrays was re-chained behind the new am-try-item job (job-1793062358131), because its stored After named the stopped old one.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_58 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 58u32, "2026-10-27T06:43Z", "tibia-lane", "06:43Z polyglot apps/chat/contract generate_cid heal LANDED (tibia-polyglot-cid-land7, 10-27 03:xx local, unstaged).\n1. The earlier VOIDs (cid-land5/6) were a baseline red unrelated to the heal: the generate_cid test piped the literal ;[] into am'.to_vec, which has taken array_base since at least 10-24 20:43, so the test failed to type-check from 10-24. (Main's in-place export migration, 20:54-00:14, overlapped cid-land5 and was restored by hash.)\n2. tibia-polyglot-cid-test-fix landed the one-line test fix (;[] |> am'.base |> am'.to_vec, the idiom crypto.spi uses): contract tests 6/6.\n3. cid-land7 then applied the heal on the green baseline: build exit 0, tests 6/6, and identical exports (contract_source_metadata, new, is_valid_alias, generate_cid, generate_cid_borsh, claim_alias, get_account_info, get_alias_map, get_alias_map_borsh).\nMain's export migration is unblocked.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_59 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 59u32, "2026-10-27T07:21Z", "main", "10-27 03:21 main: 10-27 03:21 main: post-rename requeue outcomes, read from each chain's runner summary. LANDED (gate + land exit 0, unstaged in lib): am-try-item, parsing-concat-casts, testing-char-cast, sm-linear, sm-heal-blocks, console-trace-blocks, zig-index-of (x's blocker), near-drain-2 (vector_drain_front, plus its dice drain-heal proof exit 0). GREEN proofs, kept unstaged: cube-core-math (8 backends byte-identical), math-test-name (12 Rust tests), alphabet documents (list sugar + argv). FAILED, next run: (1) convert-radix gate RED on exactly one new row, try_convert/lua, which passes on the unchanged tree: the slice's Lua arm is wrong. (2) runtime-casts gated GREEN, then its land applied cleanly and exited 1 with no reason in the job logs; read the land's own log before requeueing. (3) dice-menu-href RED: the healed dice/ui menu_tabs.spi has a type error (FatalError in dice_ui's path). (4) The polyglot alias-heal proof (proof-4) HUNG: it held heavy slot 1 from 20:52 until the 240-min watchdog killed its tree at 00:52 (the 'no process on the other end of the pipe' error), and it starved everything queued behind slot 1. Rerun it alone, with a short MaxMinutes, and sample its CPU. Obsolete and dropped: the xml blank-line and lib-blank-line-gaps slices; all four sites were already separated in the live tree after the Nil batch landed. Gate kit fix: lib-gate-kit/gate.ps1 now writes 'VOID <stamp> gate crashed before a verdict: <reason>' on any terminating error (a trap after the verdict reset; tested with a failing apply; backup gate.ps1.pre-void-trap-031928). Before, a gate that crashed before its verdict left land.ps1 polling for up to 240 min (nil-literal-3's xml land stalled 45 min that way and was stopped).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_60 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 60u32, "2026-10-27T07:37Z", "main", "10-27 03:37 main: 10-27 03:36 main: follow-up to n59's three failures, all now diagnosed and in flight. (1) runtime-casts: not a slice defect. Its land refused because another land committed resultm.spi while runtime.spi was staged (land.ps1:60, 'the target changed ... nothing was committed'). The GREEN gate predates that change, so it gets a fresh gate + land, not a land retry: main-serial-runtime-casts-2 (job-1793086363604); the slice dry-runs clean on the live lib. (2) convert-radix: the one new red, try_convert/lua, is 'disagrees', not a failure. Gleam printed all 5 asserts; Lua printed one line, and in a different assert form (__assert_eq' { expected = 3.0 }), so the Lua program stops or runs other code after its first output. The slice edits ONLY the Rust arm (from_str_radix's cast becomes conv base : u32), so the suspects are backend_switch evaluating the new conv in the Lua build, or a flaky run. A gate-only repro is queued (job-1793086226993, gate name LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REPRO, no land). (3) The polyglot alias-heal proof that hung 4 h had two defects, both fixed in TEMP polyglot-alias-heal/proof.ps1 (backup beside it). It captured the whole build log in memory, so the hang left no trace; each side now tees its build to <side>-build.log. And it ran Remove-Item -Recurse on a folder holding a deps junction into the real polyglot repo without removing the link first; the dice proof's link-delete line is added. Rerun bounded to 90 min: job-1793086509793. Also requeued: upstream-post-2 proof-4 (job-1793086025446), since proof-3 never left the queue.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_61 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 61u32, "2026-10-27T07:45Z", "main", "10-27 03:45 main: 10-27 03:45 main: dice-menu-href RED, diagnosis and a RULE CHANGE registered BEFORE the rerun. The slice turned menu_tabs.spi's href macro ('!url + \\#/ + !id + empty-string') into 'url ++# #/ ++# id'. Types read from source: url comes from leptos.use_url, built with ++# over sm'.from_std_string, so it's a Spiral string; menu_tab_id returns Spiral literals. So the new expression is a valid Spiral string. The type error (TypeErrors at character 49 of the healed line) is on href's side: the old macro's trailing + empty-string is the Rust idiom for an owned String, so href expects std_string. Fix drafted: (url ++# #/ ++# id) |> sm'.to_std_string (both conversions exist in sm'.spi, lines 26 and 1825). The old pass rule required main.rs byte-identical, which an explicit conversion can't meet, so the rule is amended now, before any run: GREEN iff both dice/ui consumer builds pass AND main.rs differs from the reference only inside the href expression of menu_tabs (diff confined to that one function), AND 0 host concat is left in the source. If href turns out NOT to be std_string (the build still fails at that line), the slice goes back to drafting; no third variant is tried by guesswork.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_62 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 62u32, "2026-10-27T08:50Z", "main", "10-27 04:50 main: 10-27 04:50 main: dice-menu-href proof-2 RED again (same Unification failure, now at character 50). Per n61 that sends the slice back to drafting, and drafting found the ROOT CAUSE with evidence, not a guess. (1) href's type: ui.tab_link is lib/spiral/leptos/ui/tab_link.spi (a module named by its file, which is why text searches for tab_link found nothing), and its render applies 'href >> sm''.to_std_string' itself. So href takes a thunk returning a SPIRAL string, n61's std_string hypothesis was wrong, and proof-2's to_std_string was a double conversion. (2) The real error: menu_tabs.spi opens only ':< leptos', while EVERY other dice/ui file that uses ++# also opens ':< sm''_operators' (content, dice_view, dice_ui, home, settings_view, transaction, transactions_view, use_transactions, rpc), so in menu_tabs ++# did not resolve to sm''_operators' string concat. The error column (49 in proof-1, 50 in proof-2) is the second ++#. Fix (TEMP dice-menu-href/apply.ps1): the original 'url ++# #/ ++# id' plus ':< sm''_operators' after ':< leptos', with guards (the first line must be :< leptos, refuse if already opened); a dry run on a copy is clean. Proof-3 runs under n61's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_63 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 63u32, "2026-10-27T10:39Z", "main", "10-27 06:39 main: dice-menu-href proof-3 (job-1793091052085, exit 0): the n62 root-cause fix WORKS. Both dice/ui consumer builds pass with ':< sm'_operators' plus the original 'url ++# \"#/\" ++# id', and 0 host concat is left. The VERDICT was RED under n61's rule, and that verdict stands for proof-3. Cause: n61's confinement check measured ONE contiguous region from the first to the last changed line of the vN-normalized main.rs, and across 4 href sites that region spans the unchanged closures between them, which contain fn lines. The real change, read from the diff, is exactly the href expressions: where host text emitted format!(\"{}#/{}\", url, id) once, Spiral ++# emits two steps (a \"#/\" literal, then format!(\"{}{}\") twice), with later temporaries renumbered. AMENDED RULE (per hunk; proof.ps1 updated before proof-4): git diff --no-index -U0 of the vN-normalized files; GREEN-CONFINED iff every hunk has #/ on one of its sides, no hunk adds or removes a fn line, and there are at most 2 hunks per folded href site. Disclosure: the 2-hunks-per-site bound and the either-side test were set with proof-3's artifacts in view (8 hunks, 2 per site, 0 outside), so proof-4 reproduces the build; it is not an independent test of the rule. Controls: a renamed fn gives 1 hunk outside (fails), and a body change outside any href gives 5 outside (fails).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_64 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 64u32, "2026-10-27T12:32Z", "main", "10-27 08:32 main: dice-menu-href LANDED (unstaged): proof-4 (job-1793097533446) gave VERDICT GREEN-CONFINED under n61 with its per-hunk amendment. Both dice/ui consumer builds pass, 0 host concat is left, and the vN-normalized main.rs diff has 8 hunks (2 per folded href site, 4 sites), 0 outside an href expression. main.rs hashes equal proof-3's (deterministic build, as n63 disclosed). apply.ps1 then ran on the live dice/ui with its own guards (anchor count 1; refuses unless the first line is ':< leptos'; refuses if sm'_operators is already opened). menu_tabs.spi went E4EE936988EE0F14 -> E555EE7FDE7499F1, git diff 2+/1-: the ':< sm'_operators' open plus 'url ++# \"#/\" ++# id' in place of the host concat. The proof copied this same live file at its start, unchanged since (git status was clean before the land). alphabet mirror check: no alphabet .spi carries the '!url +' concat, so there's nothing to mirror. This slice is closed.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_65 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 65u32, "2026-10-27T12:43Z", "main", "10-27 08:43 main: polyglot-alias-heal proof-5 (job-1793086509793) TIMED OUT at its 91 min bound (exit 124) with no output after taking the slot: its tee'd reference-build.log was never written, so the stall point is unknown. The box was overloaded from 04:15 to 05:46, and the chat build runs spiral_wasm through WSL, which timed out (Wsl/Service/ERROR_TIMEOUT) in the export migration's run at 07:4x. That's a likely suspect, not a proven one. Two decisions. (1) ORDER: this heal and the polyglot chat export migration (job-1793103260833, queued) both rewrite apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi, so whichever lands first moves the other's anchors. The alias heal reruns AFTER the export migration's verdict, re-anchored on whatever is live then. (2) proof.ps1 now prints a timestamped line at each step (copy, build start, build exit) for each side, so a stall shows its step, and its next bound is 180 min for its two chat builds. Reference VOID and the other rules are unchanged.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_66 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 66u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "main", "10-27 09:55 main: LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REPRO (gate GREEN 10-27 03:31 after one load rerun; the first run's reds were cell and compiler-start timeouts) never landed. Its first land job (job-1793105281102) timed out at main's 60 min job cap while land.ps1's reader guard waited for a gap: kit-release-SLICES reads the live lib, and Cells runs kept starting. Nothing was written. The cap was main's sizing error: per the job-timeout-counts-queue-wait rule, a land's cap must cover land.ps1's own 600 min wait. Meanwhile tibia's lib-lua-narrow-parse land changed convert.spi at 09:05 (+38/-3: narrow_parsed and its range table), so live convert.spi no longer equals the gate's base copy, and by rule the 03:31 GREEN doesn't cover the new base, even though the radix anchor still applies once (dry run: 0 host casts left in that arm). Re-gate and land on today's base: chain job-1793109268907 (gate LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REBASE, then land; 11 h cap).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_67 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 67u32, "2026-10-27T19:59Z", "main", "10-27 15:59 main: LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REBASE LANDED (unstaged) at 15:40 by chain job-1793109268907 (2 steps, both exit 0): (1) the gate on the rebased slice: verdict GREEN under the standing rule (every candidate red is also red on the unchanged tree lib, and a touched file that round-trips through Cells livemd on the tree still does); (2) land.ps1, which waited in its reader guard for kit's SLICES2 Cells runs and landed once they cleared: 'landed LIB-CONVERT-RADIX-REBASE (1 lib files touched)'. Live lib convert.spi is now 96F6D2E1B22C9BEB; git diff 41+/4-. The file's mtime reads 14:06 because land.ps1 copies a file it staged then, and the copy keeps that timestamp. This is the radix slice whose first land timed out at a 60-minute cap and whose base moved when tibia reworked convert.spi at 09:05; it was rebased onto that move and re-gated. The land was inside kit's open window (before the TRAIN lib cut-off), so TRAIN's launch-time diff includes convert.spi.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_68 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 68u32, "2026-10-27T20:27Z", "tibia-lane", "20:27Z lib threading.spi:213 heal drafted (tibia-lane, 10-27 16:4x, no backlog claim on threading.spi since 10-25). new_channel was one host block holding two concerns: `{ let (sender, receiver) = std::sync::mpsc::channel(); (sender, std::sync::Arc::new(receiver)) }`. It becomes a one-call primitive, mpsc_channel () : channel_sender std_string * channel_receiver std_string (`std::sync::mpsc::channel()`), plus Spiral: `:= sender, receiver = mpsc_channel ()` then `sender, new_arc receiver`, reusing lib's existing threading.new_arc. Slice: scratch lib-threading/threading-channel-apply.ps1. It is an anchored edit that refuses unless the anchor matches once (checked: dry run 1 match, apply, re-run 0 matches). threading and runtime both compile as Rust entries on a scratch lib copy. The one consumer is runtime.spi:866 (the command runner's output channel). Node lib gate LIB-THREADING-CHANNEL on threading and runtime is queued (job-1793132871203); the land waits for kit's \"TRAIN window closed\". By my broader rule (any $'...' holding let/if/for/while/match/loop plus ; or {) lib has 60 such macros. Most are the \"true; let mut !x = !x\" statement idiom main's census classes as adapters; threading's other 5 are that idiom.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_69 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 69u32, "2026-10-27T20:33Z", "tibia-lane", "20:33Z lib seq.spi:528 heal drafted (tibia-lane, 10-27 16:3x): rev'''s Rust arm `Rc::new({ let mut v = (!items).as_ref().clone(); v.reverse(); v })` becomes one expression, `Rc::new((!items).as_ref().iter().rev().cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>())`. It needs the same T: Clone the old .clone() did. The Zig arm's blk: block stays: it has no single-call equivalent. Slice: scratch lib-threading/seq-rev-apply.ps1, an anchored line that refuses unless it matches once (checked both ways). Proof: seq's own `test rev` (///> rust: [a; b] -> [b; a]) in node lib gate LIB-SEQ-REV (job-1793133178549, after LIB-THREADING-CHANNEL). A local entry compile can't stand in, because seq has no main (\"Cannot find main\", the same on the unchanged control). listm'.spi:511 has the same pattern but is in main's held Nil batch 2, so I left it for main. Not healed, on purpose: async.spi:313 poll_option. Rust has no Poll -> Option conversion, so any primitive still needs the match. It already is a one-expression adapter.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_70 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 70u32, "2026-10-27T21:44Z", "tibia-lane", "21:44Z lib common.spi random' Rust arm healed (drafted; this is the \"common:140 random Rust state\" entry from tibia's queue). Before, it was one host block with use-imports, a static AtomicU64, load, seeding (RandomState hasher, write_u128(nanos), finish | 1), xorshift 13/7/17, store and i128 range math. Now:\n- host primitives, one call each: native_rng_state (a `global` declaring the static), native_rng_load and native_rng_store;\n- Spiral: xorshift (u64 ^^^/<<</>>>); native_rng_seed reuses guid.rust_random_pair, whose first value is exactly the old RandomState + write_u128(nanos) + finish, then `||| 1`; native_rng_next does the lazy seed only when the state is 0; native_random_between (lo hi : i64) returns lo when span <= 0, else lo + x % span.\nThe Rust arm is now `native_random_between (convert.i64 min) (convert.i64 max) |> convert.convert`. Documented difference: the range math is i64 where the old block used i128. They are identical for every in-range i64 input and differ only for u64 bounds above i64::MAX. Proof path: random_draws_stay_in_their_half_open_range gains `///> rust` (200 draws in [3,7), random' 5 5 = 5 and 5 6 = 5), so the gate runs the heal on its consumer path. Type-checks on Rust and F# against the control (0 = 0). Slice: scratch lib-small4/random-heal-apply.ps1 (base-hash guard, checked). Node gate LIB-RANDOM-RUST-HEAL (job-1793137548409). It lands after kit's TRAIN window with the others.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_71 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 71u32, "2026-10-27T21:57Z", "tibia-lane", "21:57Z LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-E (oauth.spi): over cells 7 -> 0, no lines over 120.\nSplits:\n- parameter_string's pair join moved into joined_pairs.\n- protocol_params = consumer_params ++ token_params.\n- authorization_header = protocol_params |> with_signature c method url |> header_text.\n- Each example credential string got its own fixture (consumer secret, token, token secret), as did the status, nonce and url.\n- The two tests share oauth1_example_url, oauth1_example_header and oauth1_example_header_prefix.\nBehavior: pair order, field order and every string literal are unchanged; only names were introduced. Both asserts (the published signature and the header prefix) are unchanged.\nType-check on Rust against the control: base and draft both OK. Negative control: passing a u8 for url fails at authorization_header (Unification failure), so the check bites.\nSlice: scratch lib-small4/small-postable-e-apply.ps1 (base-hash guard C548B936E68F; dry run matches the live lib; a scratch apply reproduces the draft hash). Gate job-1793138208983 runs oauth's rust -d hmac sha1 base64 and gleam tests, queued after the random heal. It is in the land-after-train list.\nEarlier gates: LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-B GREEN (5 rows, 0 red) and LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-C GREEN (3 rows, 0 red).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_72 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 72u32, "2026-10-27T22:03Z", "tibia-lane", "22:03Z Gates back. LIB-RANDOM-RUST-HEAL is GREEN: 16 rows, 0 red, and the Rust row of random_draws_stay_in_their_half_open_range now runs (6/6 tests). LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-D (json) is GREEN: 22 rows, 0 red. B and C were GREEN earlier. All of them sit in the land-after-train list, which waits for kit's TRAIN window to close.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_73 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 73u32, "2026-10-27T23:25Z", "tibia-lane", "23:25Z LAND PROCEDURE for the 13 gated lib slices, to run once kit says 'TRAIN window closed' (kit's ETA is 22:00-24:00, after this run's 21:00 end). Run pwsh -File C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\ac4b4e98-9c58-4dbb-a7f7-3c93d9627107\\scratchpad\\node-kit\\land-after-train.ps1. For each slice in order, it imports the node's GREEN verdict, refuses if any file in the verdict's bases line has moved in the live lib, and starts the guarded land.ps1 kit job (format plus apply, unstaged). Order: LIB-PHYSICS-POSTABLE-3, LIB-THREADING-CHANNEL, LIB-SEQ-REV, LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-B, -C, -F, -D (F before D, because F's verdict lists json.spi as a consumer and D edits json), LIB-RANDOM-RUST-HEAL, -E, -G, LIB-LISTM-REV-POSTNIL, -H, -I. All 18 bases matched the live lib at 18:3x local; reactors (I) and cli_args (H) were gated later on their then-live bases. A refusal is safe (nothing is written); the fix is a re-gate on the new base. After the lands, one combined check is owed: D's json tests ran on the old json_scan and F's on the old json, so json plus json_scan get one node gate together on the landed tree.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_74 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 74u32, "2026-10-27T23:37Z", "tibia-lane", "23:37Z The combined D+F check owed in note 73 is DONE before landing: LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-DF (job-1793143725819) applied D's json.spi and F's json_scan.spi together. GREEN: json_scan 2/2 tests in 6 rows, json 11/11 in 22 rows, 0 red, bases json_scan D767423F and json A1C81150 (both still live). D and F compose, so no post-land gate is needed for them.\nNext heal in flight, LIB-SHA1-HEAL (crypto.spi). sha1_hex's Rust arm was one host string that hashed, then iterated and format!-ed each byte. Now one host call, rust_sha1_digest, returns the digest as array_base u8 (Rc<RefCell<Vec<u8>>>, as parsing.spi builds byte buffers), and the hex lives in Spiral: hex_byte (lowercase nibble table, the same table hex_nibble decodes with) and hex_of_bytes (a join point over am'.index_base). The Gleam arm is unchanged. It type-checks on Rust against its control, and the postable count is unchanged (16 over before and after). The granular census still counts crypto at 5: the new single call uses the <sha1::Sha1 as sha1::Digest>:: qualified path, which the heuristic reads as a cast; the iterator and format logic is gone from host text. Gate job-1793144247514 runs sha1_hex_and_hmac_sha1_base64 (rust -d sha1 hmac base64, gleam). A negative control follows it (job-1793144287250, uppercase table), which must come out RED.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_75 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 75u32, "2026-10-27T23:59Z", "tibia-lane", "23:59Z Rejected heal, recorded so nobody retries it: sm' builder_insert's Rust arm (a let/char_indices/insert_str block) can't just reuse the Gleam/Lua arms' slice + concat_list shape. A Rust probe (main appended, compiled with the pinned core) shows two differences. (1) On Rust, #?sb is format_debug, so a String renders quoted. (2) On Rust, sm' slice is byte-indexed: string_slice aborts when an index lands inside a UTF-8 sequence, exit 3. The current arm inserts at a char index. So the shared shape would break non-ASCII inserts on Rust. A correct heal needs a one-call char-to-byte offset primitive (char_indices().nth(i)), with the insert built in Spiral from it, plus a rust row on builder_insert's test with a non-ASCII case. Not drafted tonight.\nLands: kit closed TRAIN at 19:50 without publishing. All 13 slices plus LIB-SHA1-HEAL passed their base checks at import and are landing (guarded kit jobs serialized by Global\\spiral-lib-land). LIB-SHA1-HEAL's negative control came back RED as required (the uppercase table fails the rust row).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_76 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 76u32, "2026-10-28T00:08Z", "tibia-lane", "00:08Z LANDED 10-27 19:5x-20:0x (live lib, unstaged), each by a guarded land.ps1 kit job after a base-hash import of its node GREEN verdict:\n- LIB-PHYSICS-POSTABLE-3 (physics ABB3C48CC789)\n- LIB-THREADING-CHANNEL (D3DD42EAD0B0)\n- LIB-SEQ-REV (seq 7785D6B40B93)\n- LIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-B (6 files), -C (3 files), -F (json_scan), -D (json), -E (oauth), -G (step_runtime), -H (cli_args)\n- LIB-RANDOM-RUST-HEAL (common)\n- LIB-LISTM-REV-POSTNIL (listm' 568756C7BB74)\n- LIB-SHA1-HEAL (crypto)\nLIB-SMALL-POSTABLE-I (reactors) refused at apply time because a Cells reader appeared; nothing was written. It is requeued (job-1793145876346) and lands once the reader is gone.\nQueued node gate pairs, each with a negative control that must come out RED: LIB-EXT-HEAL (file_system get_extension, plus a new rust/gleam test), LIB-EXE-HEAL (env current_executable), LIB-INSERT-HEAL (sm' builder_insert).\nThe insert heal replaces the rejected draft from note 75. A one-call rust_char_offset primitive returns the byte offset of char i, and the insert is built in Spiral from byte slices (Rust's sm' slice is byte-indexed). Proof: builder_insert gains a rust row, plus a new rust test builder_insert_counts_chars_not_bytes (\"éb\" insert 1 \"gh\" = \"éghb\"). Both probes, the original arm and the healed one, compiled with rustc and exited 0. The negative control is the byte-index mutant (at = i), which must fail on the é case.\nFixed in the push tool on the way: push-pair.ps1 now doubles a ' in the file name (sm'.spi broke the generated slice literal). The first two insert jobs were stopped before they ran.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_77 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 77u32, "2026-10-28T02:18Z", "tibia-lane", "02:0xZ HOLD on lib lands: kit's TRAIN2 runs from about 23:45 to 05:30-06:00, after main's 'cleared'. LIB-INSERT-HEAL-2's land waits for kit's 'TRAIN window closed' even if its verdict arrives earlier. Lands are started by hand, never chained, and the reader guard isn't relied on (train-guard-gap).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_78 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 78u32, "2026-10-28T02:43Z", "tibia-lane", "02:2xZ LIB-INSERT-HEAL negative control is VALID, read from its candidate run (job-1793146139017). The job hit its 60 min cap in the sm' baseline phase (209 rows), after the candidate run had finished. In the byte-index mutant (at = i), builder_insert_counts_chars_not_bytes/rust failed with 'cell exited 3'. That is string_slice's abort inside a UTF-8 sequence, exactly the predicted failure, and not a daemon error. The ASCII builder_insert/rust passed in the same run. The positive's first run timed out in the queue; it re-runs as job-1793153933955 (3 h cap, own Cells port). Its land waits for kit's TRAIN2 'window closed'.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_79 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 79u32, "2026-10-28T03:35Z", "tibia-lane", "03:1xZ Consumer check of tonight's 18 lib lands, on tibia, is GREEN. Re-run job-1793152392179 on the node, with its own Cells compiler port and the post-land lib mirror: pack_sim 39/39, hunt_sim 9/9, creatures 11/11, gpu_sim C oracle 3/3. Together with the first run's terrain 17/17, network 7/7 and hunting_pack 2/2, every executed tibia src test passes, the same as r9's baseline this afternoon. WGSL rows are 'unsupported' (the node has no GPU runner), unchanged. The first run's reds were all ':lock_timeout' daemon contention on port 13905, not lib changes.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_80 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 80u32, "2026-10-28T04:24Z", "tibia-lane", "04:0xZ LIB-INSERT-HEAL-2 (sm' builder_insert) positive gate GREEN (job-1793153933955, its own Cells port 13984): builder_insert/rust and builder_insert_counts_chars_not_bytes/rust pass. sm's float-format and builder reds are each red on the baseline too or pass when run alone ('red again when run alone: 0'). With the valid negative from note 78 (cell exited 3), the heal is proven. Base sm' F7D34CB8 still live. It lands only after kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed', using scratch lib-insert/lib-insert-heal-apply.ps1 through import-and-land with the name LIB-INSERT-HEAL-2.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_81 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 81u32, "2026-10-28T09:28Z", "tibia-lane", "LANDED 10-28 05:3x (live lib, unstaged): LIB-INSERT-HEAL-2, sm' builder_insert. The Rust arm's let/char_indices/insert_str block became one call, rust_char_offset, plus Spiral byte slices and concat. builder_insert gains a rust row and a new test builder_insert_counts_chars_not_bytes. It landed after kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed' (05:25), on the gated base F7D34CB8. Run total: 19 lib changes landed 10-27/28, all gated GREEN with valid negatives, plus the tibia consumer check.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_82 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 82u32, "2026-10-28T10:45Z", "tibia-lane", "10:45Z DESIGN NOTE (not drafted), env environment_snapshot/environment_restored heal. Today the Rust arms are one 2-line snapshot macro and one 12-line block that parses the snapshot, filters hidden '=' keys, removes extras outside kept_prefix and sets changed values. Plan: one-call primitives rust_remove_var key and rust_set_var key value (each an unsafe {} call), with the parse, filter and diff written in Spiral over a pair list. Found blockers:\n(1) package order: env (module 35) loads before unicode (46), so unicode.char_of 0 isn't available for the NUL separator; the NUL text needs a primitive in env or a lower module.\n(2) sm'.split returns array_base string, and am' has no array_base -> list or fold helper. Either a small lib helper (am'.to_list_base) or a join-point walk over index_base.\n(3) Consumers: kit uses both functions (kit_job_create/create.spi, kit_jobs/jobs.spi) for job environments, so the proof must include kit's consumer path, not just env's own test.\n(4) The existing rust test only restores a snapshot to itself, so it exercises no removal or setting. The heal needs a new rust test that adds a variable and changes another before restoring, plus a negative control that skips the removal.\nEdge note: the current arm sets a variable that is missing or differs (var_os map_or true); an empty value on Windows deletes it, so compare against an option, not the \"\" default of get_environment_variable.\nEstimate about 2 h with the kit check; queued after today's park work.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_83 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 83u32, "2026-10-28T10:53Z", "tibia-lane", "10-28 schedule from main: lib FROZEN 09:45 to about 14:00 (kit TRAIN3) and 16:00-20:00 (TRAIN4). Land windows are about 14:00-16:00, on kit's 'land window open' (order: kit's fix-forward, fiscal XSD-ALL-GROUPS, then tibia's POSTABLE/GRANULAR slices), and 20:00-21:00. Node gates can run before. X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE is Done (x), so POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS's dependency is clear.")
inl granular_lint () = Task ("GRANULAR-LINT", "eoie lint macros <paths>: classify every host macro by GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1)-(3) (long, flow, cast, arith, block), per-repo baseline file so only NEW violations fail, JSON/TSV out; wired into the PostToolUse style check and the lib gate kit acceptance, so drift cannot re-enter", Feature, P0, D4, 70u32, Active, "KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL", "granular,macros,eoie,lint,hooks,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_lint () = Planning ("GRANULAR-LINT", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_lint_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: prototype is the scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 classifier (regexes for flow/cast/arith; tracked files via git ls-files; build dirs skipped). Port it to Spiral in eoie as a generic verb (no project names), drop the -> false positive by skipping type-position macros, then baseline each repo with the GRANULAR-HEAL census.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:35Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:35 main: lessons for the verb, from the prototype: (1) count untracked files too. New agent modules are untracked until the user commits, and my tracked-only census missed 23 lib sites / 8.8k chars (process.spi, unicode, xml, the otp modules). (2) Do not walk untracked trees with git ls-files --others in repos whose large caches are not gitignored (eoie's 5.5 GB .cache took over 30 min); take the package manifests' module lists, or skip known cache dirs before listing. (3) The flow regex has false positives on type text (Rc<dyn Fn()>, -> in signatures), data literals ((char[]){ !b, 0 }) and allowed thunks; classify by AST position (type vs term macro) and by the rule 2/3 exceptions instead of regexes. (4) Report per definition and per added line of a diff, so a slice's check covers only what it adds (slice-macro-check.ps1 in scratch does that for apply scripts).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T21:10Z", "main", "main 17:2x: draft one-call case for the verb, in eoie/spec/one-call.md form, offered to the eoie team to adopt (their spec file; nothing edited there). Fixture lib/sample.spi lines: (1) inl ok (x : i32) : i32 = $'!x'  (2) inl widen (x : i32) : i64 = $'!x as i64'  (3) inl pick (b : bool) : i32 = $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'  (4) inl sum (a : i32) (b : i32) : i32 = $'!a + !b'  (5) nominal callback = $'Rc<dyn Fn()>'. Command: eoie lint macros . lib. Expected, one row per violation, then the receipt: 'lib/sample.spi:2  widen  cast   $'!x as i64'' / 'lib/sample.spi:3  pick   flow   $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'' / 'lib/sample.spi:4  sum    arith  $'!a + !b'' / 'failed  files 1  macros 5  violations 3  new 3  <elapsed>  <clock>'. Line 5 is the positive control for lesson 3: -> and dyn in a TYPE-position macro (nominal body) is not flow. Companion case: the same command with --baseline lint/macros.tsv listing rows 2 and 3 prints only row 4 and 'failed ... violations 3  new 1'; with all three listed it ends 'ok ... new 0'. Classes from the prototype (scratch macro-rule.ps1): long = text over 120 chars, flow = if/else/match/for/while/loop/let/return/fn/=>/->/; or a braced statement in a TERM macro, cast = 'as T', arith = an operator between splices or literals, block = a triple-quoted global/macro; rule 2/3 exceptions (named one-expression boundary primitives) come from the package standards.spi, not from regexes.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:46Z", "eoie", "03:46 eoie: verb core built TDD in eoie/src/eoie_lint_macros (HostFree, LibReferenced, standards 0 findings). model.spi holds unions macro_place (term/type) and macro_class (long/block/flow/cast/arith), records host_macro, violation, known_violation and lint_tally, and the pure classifier. lint.spi walks the tree (skipping target, node_modules, _build, deps, bin, obj and dot-dirs; untracked files count, per lesson 1, and no git listing, per lesson 2) and builds the report. Spec cases 48/49 adopted into eoie/spec/one-call.md. Both notebooks pass: main's 5-line fixture gives exactly the 3 expected rows and the receipt head; type-position macros (nominal, after : or ->) are not flow, per lesson 3; string literals and a deref are clean; and a temp-tree e2e run gives new 3, then new 1 against a baseline. Change from main's draft: the baseline is the verb's own output (eoie lint macros . lib > lint/macros.txt, keyed by file+name+class, line numbers ignored), because the style ratchet refuses tab-encoded rows in a new package. Left: a crate plus a CLI route (lint verb, help line), after SLATE; standards.spi exemptions for named boundary primitives (rule 2/3) need a declaration in eoie_standards first; per-diff reporting (lesson 4); wiring into the PostToolUse check. Finding for eoie_standards: host-free counts \\$' inside string literals as a host macro, a false positive that forced fixtures through a marker.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T08:07Z", "eoie", "04:06 eoie: lesson 4 done: report only what a change adds. A file named on the command line counts whatever its extension, and a .diff or .patch contributes only its added lines (+, not the +++ header; context and removed lines are skipped), so a slice check is 'eoie lint macros . change.diff'. Test a_diff_counts_only_its_added_lines passes, and both notebooks and standards are green (0 findings). Spec case 48's note documents it. CLI wiring is drafted (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\apply-lint-verb.ps1, all 4 anchors present once): a crate with entry.spi exporting eoie_lint_macros_run, VerbLint with its help line, the eoie_cli_model route and the eoie_cli dependency. It applies right after SLATE.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_6 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T19:48Z", "eoie", "15:48 kit: lint CLI wiring draft re-verified against the live tree (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\dry-run.ps1, on a copy of the 7 files it touches).\n- All anchors matched exactly once. Result: the eoie_lint_macros crate files (entry.spi export eoie_lint_macros_run, package.spiproj, Cargo.toml), VerbLint plus its help line in direct_verbs.spi, the lint_run runner and route in eoie_cli_model, the eoie_cli dependency, and the workspace member.\n- cargo metadata exits 101 on the partial copy, which is expected; on the full tree it must exit 0.\n- apply-lint-verb.ps1 now checks the tree lease only when -Src is the live tree, so dry runs on copies work during a release.\n- Sequencing: the wiring is a feature, so it lands after KIT-RENAME, and its anchors name eoie_* (eoie_crate_gate, eoie_mirror, eoie_cli_model).\n  Rule: run kit-rename.ps1's replacement rules over the draft script itself before applying it, then rerun this dry run on the renamed tree. Every anchor must again match exactly once.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_7 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T02:08Z", "main", "10-25 22:08 main: three refinements to the verb's 'block' class, from today's GRANULAR-HEAL census (n12-n17), as fixture cases for eoie/spec/one-call.md form (eoie team's file; nothing edited there; eoie source frozen until KIT-RENAME). With the naive rule (any '{' at the start of a macro body) lib reported 36 blocks; with these three, 23 real ones, and leptos' 5 'blocks' all vanish. (A) unit adapter, NOT a violation: a brace body holding exactly one statement and no let/if/for/while/match/loop keyword, e.g. $'{ leptos::prelude::Effect::new(move |_| (!fn)()); }' : (). The braces turn a returned handle into the () the macro is typed as; a bare call would not on Rust, whose unit-macro emission isn't specified as a statement (Zig's is, since pack-1w). Also '{ let _ = f(&$0); }' (a discard: one let of _). Fixture: inl fire (f : () -> ()) : () = $'{ spawn(!f); }' gives 0 violations. (B) format-string literal, NOT a block: a '{' inside a host string literal, e.g. tag_text tag props $'\"{ !children }\"', where the macro body is the Rust string \"{ children }\". Rule: strip host string literals (\"...\", with escapes) before looking for braces. Fixture: inl t (c : string) : string = $'\"{ !c }\"' gives 0. (C) new class target_cfg, reported apart from block: a brace body whose statements carry #[cfg(...)] attributes (near.spi's 9 wasm32/native pairs, trace's 2). They are not mechanically healable (Spiral can't select on rustc's target; that is a backend-key design gap), so the baseline should list them separately and the 'NEW violations fail' gate should apply to them on their own terms. Fixture: inl h () : u64 = $'{ #[cfg(a)] let v = f(); #[cfg(not(a))] let v = 1u64; v }' gives 1 target_cfg and 0 block. Census numbers to check the verb against, today's tree: lib 23 block + 11 target_cfg-ish + 13 adapters; spiral/apps 5; polyglot 3 (dist/ excluded); alphabet 1; dir-tree-html 1; dice 0.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_8 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T11:21Z", "eoie", "07:21 eoie: the wiring draft is redone for the renamed, respelled tree, per note rule (rename rules over the draft, then the dry run on the renamed tree). Scratch clean\\lint-verb\\apply-lint-verb-kit.ps1 replaces apply-lint-verb.ps1. Names are kit_* (crate kit_lint_macros, export kit_lint_macros_run, kit_cli dep, workspace member). The generated entry.spi is edition 3 (':< lint', ':= main', a backslash lambda), and the entry module is appended to the existing package.spiproj instead of overwriting it (the live one already lists kit_receipt). Anchors updated: the union now ends before ':== verb_help =', the help entry is a multi-line record (new VerbLint record laid out the same way, every line within 120 columns, checked), and the runner line is ':= mirror_run'. Refuses if lint.spi lacks ':~ lint_run () : i32'. Dry run on a partial copy (dry-run-kit.ps1): every anchor matched once, all five checks true, and a second run says 'already wired' (cargo metadata 101 is expected on a partial copy). Node proof queued: job-1793013737837-kit-lint-verb (applier, then regen of kit_lint_macros plus the crates that inline command_spec_domain or kit_cli_model: kit_cli, kit_command_help, kit_legacy_operations, command_spec_domain; debug build; smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros'; notebooks lint.spi and command_spec_domain model.spi, strict). Found on the way: kit_lint_macros/lint.spi has a test block but is not in the release test phase's 111 notebooks, so its tests never run in a release. To fix when the verb lands: register it with the dev test registry.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_9 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 9u32, "2026-10-26T12:21Z", "tibia-lane", "12:21Z 12:3xZ tibia proposal for the 'block' class: three open sites (GRANULAR-HEAL note 27, LIB-PAR-MAP note 13) need host code that is neither one host expression nor expressible in Spiral:\n- trace.spi:328, the Rust local clock under 4 cfg branches;\n- crypto.spi:177, Zig sha256 into an out-param digest;\n- par' Rust map_on_threads, std::thread::scope with spawned closures borrowing the scope.\nProposed rule (8), host shims, as fixtures for one-call.md:\n(a) A shim is a host function declared in `global`, allowed only when the construct needs a host-only feature: cfg or conditional compilation, an out-parameter or `var` buffer the host forces, or a lifetime- or scope-bound closure (thread::scope, borrow-scoped iterators).\n(b) A shim body uses only that feature plus calls. No arithmetic, no casts, no branches beyond the cfg/feature selection itself, no loops. Each cfg arm is one call or field read, at most 120 chars per line.\n(c) Its inputs and outputs are raw host values. Everything else stays in Spiral at the call site: unit conversion, h/m/s splitting, chunk sizing and the conv casts.\n(d) Each shim gets one named Spiral primitive that calls it in one host expression. No other Spiral code names the shim.\nWorked shapes:\n- trace: one cfg'd shim per platform, each returning a raw local-time field tuple (wasm block_timestamp secs; windows GetLocalTime [u16; 8]; unix localtime_r [i32; 16]; fallback SystemTime secs), plus Spiral seconds-of-day math over conv.\n- crypto: `fn spiral_sha256(input) -> [32]u8 { var d: [32]u8 = undefined; Sha256.hash(input, &d, .{}); return d; }`; bytesToHex stays its own call.\n- par': `fn spiral_scoped_map<T, U>(chunks, f) -> Vec<Vec<U>>` doing scope, spawn and join only; chunking and flattening in Spiral (par'.chunks, listm'.collect).\nLint check: a global block may declare fn only when tagged with its allowance class: cfg, out-param or scope.\nThis is a proposal for main's GRANULAR-LINT decision; tibia drafts the three slices once a rule is recorded.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_10 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 10u32, "2026-10-26T12:22Z", "main", "10-26 08:22 main: DECISION on tibia's rule (8), host shims (note 12:21Z): ACCEPTED, with 3 amendments. Checked against the pre-written granular rules: a macro is one host call; casts, arithmetic and flow stay in Spiral at any cost; the rules govern style, not authorship. (8) only admits host code for constructs Spiral cannot express (conditional compilation, an out-parameter the host forces, a closure bound to a host scope). Everything else stays at the Spiral call site, so it extends the rules without loosening them. Amendments: (A1) scope class: spawning per chunk needs iteration, which (b) as written forbids. The spawn and the join may each be one iterator map over the chunks, because that iteration is the scope feature itself. No other closure body logic is allowed: the spawned closure only calls the Spiral-provided f. (A2) the allowance class lives in the shim's name, not a comment (no-comments rule): spiral_cfg_<name>, spiral_out_<name>, spiral_scope_<name>. The lint check reads the prefix, and a global fn without one of the three prefixes is a violation. (A3) proof per shim: its single Spiral primitive (rule d) gets a test block on the shim's backend (///> rust for trace and par', ///> zig for crypto), and one-call.md gets a negative fixture: a spiral_cfg_ shim whose arm has arithmetic ('secs / 60') is flagged as arith, and one with two calls chained in an arm passes only if each arm stays a single call or field read. Consequence for the three sites: trace.spi:328 gets spiral_cfg_local_fields per platform, returning raw tuples, with the seconds-of-day math in Spiral; crypto.spi:177 gets spiral_out_sha256 with bytesToHex as its own call; par' gets spiral_scope_map, with chunking and flattening in Spiral. Tibia drafts the three slices; each goes through the gate kit with its backend's tests.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_11 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 11u32, "2026-10-26T13:07Z", "eoie", "09:06 eoie: classifier refinement from the granular migration: a macro whose whole body is one call into another kit crate's export (\\$'other_crate::kit_export(&*!a, ...)' : t) is the hand-modeled build-unit seam, not a violation. Report it as its own class (export_seam), never as flow or block. Fixture: := f (~a : string) : string = \\$'agile_title_tails::kit_agile_title_kept_end(&*!a)' : string gives 0 violations and 1 export_seam.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_12 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 12u32, "2026-10-26T14:59Z", "eoie", "10:59 eoie: node proof of the redone wiring (job kit-lint-verb, runner step 4): the applier wired it (Cargo.lock refresh 0), regen exit 0, debug build 0, smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros' gives 'ok files 4 macros 2 violations 0 new 0', and lint.spi plus command_spec_domain model.spi notebooks pass 2/2. Not landable as is: the generated kit_lint_macros/lint.rs is 2,056 lines against the 1,000-line gate, so the release topology check would refuse it (the other touched crates are 843-911). Next, a hand-modeled split at a crate boundary: a kit_lint_macros_model crate generating the classifier and baseline matching with an export (the report as typed rows), and kit_lint_macros keeping the file walk and CLI and calling that export, each under 1,000. Size the split from lint.rs's function list before drafting.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_13 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 13u32, "2026-10-26T15:10Z", "eoie", "11:10 eoie: split sized from the node-generated lint.rs (2,056 lines, 79 functions). Source: model.spi 351 lines, lint.spi 110; generated, about 300 lines come from lint.spi (lint_run 78, lint_report 40, the walk) and about 1,700 from model.spi (operator_between 96, line_macros 91, definition_name 81, global_macro 81, term_class 75, known_of 57, macros_from 52, words_of 51, the rest smaller). So the model alone is over the gate and needs two crates of its own. Cut by concern: (1) kit_lint_macros_scan (macro discovery: line_macros, macros_from, definition_name, global_macro, words_of); (2) kit_lint_macros_classify (term_class, operator_between, the class union and the violation row); (3) kit_lint_macros (walk, baseline known_of, report, CLI), joined by exports carrying typed rows. Each about 600-800 generated lines by these numbers; confirm with a regen before the wiring draft is rebased onto it.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_14 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 14u32, "2026-10-26T15:12Z", "eoie", "11:12 eoie: design tension in the split, found reading model.spi's structure before drafting. The natural cut (scan: lines 9-153, macro discovery and the diff filter; classify: 156-288, words, term_class, class_of, file_violations, baseline, rows) passes list host_macro and list violation between stages. A crate boundary is an export, and exports carry primitives, strings and tuples, so the lists would cross as joined-string rows, which the census counts as drift (no joined-string rows). Options to measure before choosing, rule set now: (a) cut where the payload is already a primitive, e.g. scan one file to a count tuple plus row text only at the report end, measured by generated lines per crate; (b) keep one crate and cut generated size by healing its own bloat (79 functions, 13 of them over 50 lines; a regen with the lint pass's own fixes may drop it under 1,000); (c) wait for a compiler export of typed rows. Take (b) first: it adds no seam, and the lint verb should not need a dispensation from the rule it enforces.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_15 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 15u32, "2026-10-26T15:32Z", "eoie", "11:32 eoie: option (b) measured and refuted. Hypothesis: 11 small helpers declared with := (inlined at each call site: word_letter, last_letter, first_letter, flow_word, punctuation, definition_word, global_line, declaration_body, macro_open, interpolated_open, hosted) explained the bloat, and :~ (one generated function each) would shrink lint.rs. Node regen with lint-join-points.do on the wired lint1 mirror: lint.rs 2,103 lines against 2,056 before (+47), with build, smoke and notebooks unaffected. So inlining is not the cost; the generated size comes from each string operation's expansion (sm'.trim and sm'.substring each become 10-20 Rust lines with slice bounds and Rc copies, visible in operator_between's 96 lines). Consequences: (1) the lint crate cannot get under 1,000 by declaration tweaks; (2) the remaining options are the export split (a), with its joined-row tension, or compiler-side string codegen, i.e. one Rust helper per lib string op instead of an inline expansion, which would shrink every kit crate. (2) belongs to the compiler lane; I'll send them the measurement. Until then the verb waits; its wiring is proven.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_16 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 16u32, "2026-10-26T15:39Z", "eoie", "11:39 eoie: compiler lane's read of the join-point miss (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE, P3): string_slice is already one shared fn. The bloat is lib wrappers like sm'.trim and sm'.substring being := and partial-evaluated at every call site. A :~ on a large, often-called wrapper becomes one method (a top-level fn called per use), while on tiny helpers the call costs more than inlining, or the result becomes a boxed closure (Rc<dyn Fn> plus thread_local), the heavy path. That is why 11 small helpers grew the crate. Next measurement, set now: route lint's own trim/substring uses through two local :~ wrappers (a method each), regen, check that lint.rs has 'fn' definitions and no 'Rc::new(move |' closures for them, and compare lines.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_17 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 17u32, "2026-10-26T20:11Z", "kit", "16:11 kit: the wrapper measurement is done (job-1793035682149-kit-lint-wrappers2, mirror lint4, checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658; the first try, lint3, was void because its mirror sync failed). Lint's own trim, substring, starts_with and replace uses were routed through four local :~ wrappers (begins, cut, swapped, trimmed). Regen exit 0, debug build 0, both lint notebooks pass (lint.spi, command_spec_domain model). Shape check (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\wrapper-shape.ps1): each wrapper is one fn definition called 6 to 25 times, as intended, not a boxed closure. Size: lint.rs 2,005 lines, against 2,056 with no change and 2,103 with join points. So declaration-level tactics are exhausted: 51 lines, about 2.5%, against the 1,005 still to go. Decision by the 11:32 rule: the crate must be split into hand-modeled build units (option a, the export split: the lint model, the rule tables and the CLI entry as separate packages along the import graph), not tuned further. The lib-wide :~ change for sm'.trim and substring (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE) would give at most what these local wrappers gave inside this crate, at the cost of a full lib oracle gate, so kit does not take it for lint. Also found: the smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros' printed the top-level usage and exited 2, so the verb's CLI route is not wired by apply-lint-verb-kit.ps1. That goes with the split.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_18 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 18u32, "2026-10-26T20:26Z", "kit", "16:26 kit: split plan, written before any measurement. Library packages compile into their consumer's crate, so a split only shrinks lint.rs when it makes separate crates joined by Exports (the way kit_agile_mutation calls kit_handoff_render::handoff_render_owned). The cut must keep the interface narrow and must not cross the boundary as joined-string rows (the census bans them). model.spi (351 lines) has three layers along its own import order: (1) scanning, lines 9-153: macro_place/origin/class, host_macro, line_macros, macros_from, file_macros, global_line, declaration_body, the diff reader added_from/added_text; (2) classification, 156-235: outside_quotes, words_of, operator_between, arith_in, term_class, class_of, file_violations; (3) baseline and rendering, 241-289: known_violation, baseline_of, known, name_width, violation_row, tally_head. lint.spi (110) is the walk and the CLI entry. Candidate cuts: A, two crates. kit_lint_file (layers 1-3) exports one call per file, taking file, text and the baseline text and returning that file's rendered report text plus three counts (logic, known, new) as separate scalar exports; kit_lint_macros keeps the walk, the totals and the entry. B, three crates: as A, but layer 3 stays with the entry, and kit_lint_file returns per-violation scalars by index (count, then name/line/class at i), which is chattier but keeps rendering in one place. Rule: take the cut with the fewest crates in which every generated file is at or under 950 lines, the lint notebook and the smoke ('kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros') pass, and the report text is byte-identical to the unsplit binary on src. Rendered report text is output, not a data encoding, so A's boundary is allowed. The smoke failure (top-level usage, exit 2) is fixed in the same change by routing 'lint' in the CLI dispatch. Starts after the rename lands (the tree is held), measured on node mirrors.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_19 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 19u32, "2026-10-26T20:29Z", "kit", "16:29 kit: refinement of cut A from reading lint_report (lint.spi:46-66). Row alignment is global (width = name_width over every file's fresh violations), so a per-file call cannot render rows on its own. Cut A's kit_lint_file therefore exports scalars and text in two passes: macros_in(text) : i32, found_in(file, text) : i32, fresh_in(file, text, baseline) : i32, width_in(file, text, baseline) : i32, then rows_at(file, text, baseline, width) : string, the file's rendered rows at the given width joined by newlines (display text, not a tab or record-separator encoding). kit_lint_macros walks the files once for counts and the maximum width, then once more for rows, so the output stays byte-identical. Cost: about 5 parses per file instead of 1, so the rule adds a timing check: lint over src may take at most 2x the unsplit binary, otherwise go to cut B (the parsed model kept on the entry side, only scanning moved). Lint's own usage text already exists (lint.spi:86); the smoke's top-level usage means the CLI dispatch lacks a 'lint' route, which this change adds.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_20 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 20u32, "2026-10-27T13:39Z", "kit", "09:4x kit: the lint split is built, proven and staged. Cut A as planned (16:26) was measured first and failed its own rule: the per-file layer alone generated 1,560 lines (flat, 84 items, no hot spot), so per-file exports could not fit. The cut that fits sends typed parallel columns across the Export (arrays cross as Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>; no joined-string encoding). Layout: kit_lint_model (source package, model.spi plus code mappings for place, origin and class; each crate emits only what it reaches); kit_lint_scan (kit_lint_scan(text) returns line, name, text, closed, place and origin columns in one call per file, plus added_text, row and tally rendering: 946 lines); kit_lint_classify (kit_lint_class per macro, plus the baseline check kit_lint_known: 891); kit_lint_macros (the walk, the glue and the kit_lint_run export: 844), against 2,005 to 2,056 unsplit. Arrays are filled with create/set (arraym.fromList returns a different array type). Byte identity: the split and unsplit binaries (same compiler, entries exported the same way) give identical output over the kit src tree (959 lines; 792 files, 1,720 macros, 958 violations) and over lib/spiral (516 lines), and identical output with --baseline (new 306 of 515). Timing over the lib, 3 rounds each: unsplit 1,955/1,321/2,784 ms, split 1,182/2,025/973 ms, inside the 2x rule. The lint CLI route was missing (the 16:11 smoke printed top-level usage); it now exists: VerbLint plus kit_cli_model to kit_lint_macros::kit_lint_run (kit.rs 970). The pure sample test moved to kit_lint_model. Stage: clean/kit-rename/cli-ceremonies-evidence-lint (23 rows including one delete of kit_lint_macros/model.spi, Cargo wiring for 5 new crates), applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES. Next after it lands: the per-repo baseline files and the PostToolUse hook wiring.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_21 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 21u32, "2026-10-27T19:05Z", "kit", "kit 15:1x: the hook wiring is drafted and waits for TRAIN, which ships the public 'lint macros' verb (today's binary answers 'unknown verb: lint'). Scratch clean/granular-lint: eoie-style-check.next.ps1 lints every edited .spi under kit/src and lib/spiral with 'kit lint macros <root> <file> --baseline <rows>', and only fresh violations block (exit 2, rows quoted). Outside kit/src the hook stops there; inside it, the existing pattern and ratchet checks still run, and lint rows join their findings. The baselines live in kit/standards (lint-macros.rows, lint-macros-lib.rows), so nothing is written into the spiral repo. activate.ps1, after install: writes both baselines from the installed verb, proves them (one file must fail with an empty baseline, exit 1, and pass with the real one, exit 0), pipe-tests the new hook with a real payload, then swaps it in and keeps a .bak. The lib gate's kit acceptance is the other half of the wiring, still open.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_22 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 22u32, "2026-10-28T09:36Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: 'kit lint macros' is installed (TRAIN2c). The activate.ps1 dry run gives baselines of kit/src 952 rows (800 files, 1668 macros) and lib/spiral 513 rows (114 files, 3695 macros). I'm holding the hook swap: x's first run found that a string literal containing a dollar sign is misread as a macro opener (KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL), and the live hook would flag that pattern in new text across lanes. Activation goes after that fix ships: rerun activate.ps1 -Go, which writes fresh baselines, proves them with a positive control and swaps the hook with a .bak.")
inl dice_granular_heal () = Task ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal dice host macros to granular Spiral per GRANULAR-HEAL (79 sites, 7,187 chars; 53 arith: transaction.spi 30/2,315, dice_contract.spi 5/1,868, dice_view.spi 13/1,020, rpc.spi, nearblocks.spi); every change mirrored in alphabet", Story, P1, D4, 20u32, Active, "", "granular,macros,dice,alphabet,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dice_granular_heal () = Planning ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T11:24Z", "main", "10-25 07:24 main: classified. The title's '53 arith' is stale. The bulk is 55 Leptos attribute props in ui/src/components (transaction 30, menu_tabs 8, dice_view 5, settings_view 5, content 4, transactions_view 2, home 1), all of the shape $'\"class=\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"\"'. The compiler already folds them into literals (main.rs: Rc::<str>::from(\"<div  class=\\\"flex flex-1 flex-col\\\">{ v54 }</div>\")), so a plain Spiral string \"class=\\\"...\\\"\" is the same input to the fold. Proof job: job-1792927268654 (heal-attrs.ps1) compiles dice_ui before and after one exact-pattern rewrite and requires a byte-identical main.rs; on any difference it restores every ui/src source. The tracked main.rs is restored either way. ui/src/model/near/rpc.spi (82 macro lines) and nearblocks.spi (33) are Rust type names (:! x' = $'crate::model::near::rpc::...') and single field reads (!\\\\(x, \"$0.field.clone()\")): one host call each, which granular style keeps. dist/ui is a published copy and out of scope. alphabet has no Leptos code, so nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T15:35Z", "main", "10-25 11:35 main: pass 1 v2 RED (job-1792931138011), sources restored. With the 55 Leptos attribute macros as plain Spiral strings, the folded string literals of main.rs are NOT the same multiset, and cargo check (wasm32) fails with 293 errors. So my premise ('a plain string is the same input to the fold') is wrong for the leptos view path: the attribute text reaching view! as a host macro is not equivalent to a Spiral string literal there. Next: read the first cargo errors and the literal diff (snapshot folder %TEMP%\\dice-heal\\snapshot-*) to see what a plain string becomes, e.g. an Rc<str> variable spliced where view! needs a token. Until then the attribute sites count as required host text. The likely right shape is a lib helper (leptos.attr name value) that keeps the one macro inside lib, so call sites stop carrying escaped host text.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T15:42Z", "main", "10-25 11:42 main: v2 RED re-read, and the RED is not proven to be the heal. (1) With vN numbering normalized, all 376 view! bodies of main.rs are identical before and after; the only output change is 24 fewer Rc<str> literal lets (duplicate attribute texts: 19->7, 18->7, 2->1), so the sorted-multiset rule was too strict. (2) v2 never cargo-checked the before build, and the current compiler's before main.rs (1,690,148 B) differs from the tracked one (1,733,999 B), so the 293 errors may be the compiler's. (3) The error filter missed every line (--message-format short prefixes the path). Rule for v3, written before the run: GREEN iff the view! bodies are identical as a vN-normalized multiset AND the distinct normalized Rc literal set is equal AND the after build's cargo error set (code + message, vN-normalized, positions stripped) is a subset of the before build's set. If before itself fails cargo check, record it on a compiler item, as a dice build regression on the current compiler.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:44Z", "main", "10-25 14:44 main: v3 GREEN (job-1792943110694, by n3's pre-registered rule): all 55 Leptos attribute macros as plain Spiral strings give identical vN-normalized view! bodies, an identical distinct Rc literal set, and 0 new cargo errors. CORRECTION to v3's own printed NOTE ('the unhealed build already fails cargo check: a compiler-side dice regression'): FALSE. The 9 errors (Heap2/Heap3 lacking serde/Borsh traits, a Debug bound) come from my script, not the compiler. dice/ui/build.ps1 runs Add-RustDerives (rust_derive.ps1) on main.rs after BuildSpiral and appends 'pub mod model;', and v3 called only BuildSpiral, so both builds lacked the derives (tracked main.rs: 66 serde derives; v3's: 0). The compiler emits only #[derive(Clone)] on unions. So GREEN compares like with like and stands; there's no dice regression to report. Landing rule: apply the heal, then run dice/ui/build.ps1 itself (BuildSpiral + Add-RustDerives + the wasm build), which must exit 0; else restore. Separate drift noted: Add-RustDerives is a regex post-pass over generated Rust, where derives should be declared in Spiral (GRANULAR-HEAL scope, the same class as x's retired rust-modules.ps1).")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-25T19:16Z", "main", "10-25 15:16 main: alphabet-mirror check (memory alphabet-mirrors-dice): alphabet's tracked Spiral (git ls-files: 2 .spi files) has 0 sites of the Leptos attribute-macro pattern this heal rewrites, so the dice attribute heal has no alphabet counterpart. alphabet's own heals continue under ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL (heal3 waits on LIB-NEW-VEC-RUNTIME). The dice landing (job-1792953997664) is queued in the heavy queue.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-25T22:40Z", "main", "10-25 18:40 main: the land (job-1792953997664) is RED and restored (15 files, all equal to their pre-land hashes): with the 55 heals, BuildSpiral passed, Add-RustDerives and cargo check for wasm32 passed, then dice/ui/build.ps1 exited 2 in a later step (bun build-css or the trunk bundle). The land script's output filter dropped that step's message. Like v2, a RED without a baseline proves nothing about the heal. Rule, written before the run: an unfiltered baseline build of the UNHEALED sources is queued (main-dice-baseline-build). If it also exits 2 at the same step, the fault is dice's build or toolchain (fixed first, separately), and the land rule becomes 'the healed build's exit and step equal the baseline's, and cargo check passes'. If the baseline exits 0, the heal breaks a post-cargo step, and the full log names it.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-26T01:36Z", "main", "10-25 21:36 main: ROOT CAUSE of the heal-attrs-land RED found, and it is not the heal. The baseline build of the UNHEALED sources (main-dice-baseline-build, %TEMP%\\dice-heal\\baseline-210431.log) exits 2 at the same late step: trunk build fails with \"error: invalid value '1' for '--no-color' [possible values: true, false]\". trunk 0.21.14 binds --no-color to the NO_COLOR variable and accepts only true/false, while the agent shell exports the common NO_COLOR=1 (process env only; not user, machine or settings.json), and every job started from it inherits it. CI doesn't set it, which is why dice CI is green. Positive control in an empty folder: NO_COLOR=1 makes trunk exit 2 with that exact error; NO_COLOR=true gets past argument parsing and fails only on the missing Trunk config. Fixed in dice/ui/build.ps1:44 (unstaged): the trunk block's Invoke-Block -EnvironmentVariables now also passes NO_COLOR = 'true' when NO_COLOR is set at all, else 'false'; Invoke-Block scopes and restores it. alphabet has no trunk step, so nothing to mirror. main.rs was restored to its pre-land snapshot by the baseline script (hash True). Requeued as one serial job (main-serial-dice-heal, job-1792978550250): baseline build again, then heal-attrs-land. By the pre-registered rule the heal lands only if the healed build matches the baseline's exit and step.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-26T05:06Z", "main", "10-26 01:06 main: GREEN and LANDED (main-serial-dice-heal: baseline build 2 then heal-attrs-land 2, 00:59). With dice/ui/build.ps1's NO_COLOR normalization, the baseline build passes, and the heal turns 55 Leptos attribute macros into plain Spiral strings; dice/ui/build.ps1 (BuildSpiral, Add-RustDerives, cargo check for wasm32, trunk bundle) passes on the healed tree. The healed .spi files and the rebuilt main.rs are unstaged for the user. Mirror rule (alphabet mirrors dice): alphabet has no Leptos code (0 'leptos' in its .spi; positive control: 511 in 11 dice files), so nothing to mirror. Remaining dice drift: Add-RustDerives itself (RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, compiler lane, after the repro fix).")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-26T17:55Z", "main", "10-26 13:55 main: 10-26 13:5x main: one dice site the attribute heal didn't cover, found by a GRANULAR-HEAL census of main's repos (dice, alphabet, polyglot, blog, dir-tree-html; 0 host-text casts; flow/arith sites all claimed except this one). ui/src/components/menu_tabs.spi:85, the tab href: $'!url + \"\\#/\" + !id + \"\"' becomes url ++# \"#/\" ++# id. url is a Spiral string (leptos.use_url is a ++# memo), menu_tab_id returns string literals, and tab_link.render takes href : () -> string (then sm'.to_std_string). Reachable: content.spi:121 calls menu_tabs.render. Today's main.rs (00:51, staged by the user) already shows Rc::<str>::from(format!(\"{}#/{}\", v1, v2)) at 3 closures, so the compiler folds the host + chain into format!. The heal should therefore not change the output.\nDraft: TEMP dice-menu-href/apply.ps1 (one anchored edit). Proof: TEMP dice-menu-href/proof.ps1, reference and healed scratch copies running the consumer's steps (BuildSpiral, Add-RustDerives, cargo check wasm32). Rules, written before the run:\n- GREEN: both builds exit 0, the reference carries the host concat (1 site in source, at least 1 folded href in main.rs), the healed source has 0 host concat, and main.rs is byte-identical.\n- GREEN-NORMALIZED: the same, but identical only after vN normalization (n3's precedent).\n- VOID: the reference build fails, or the reference doesn't carry the site.\n- RED: anything else.\nLand: on GREEN, apply to the live dice/ui source only, since main.rs is unchanged. On GREEN-NORMALIZED, apply and run dice/ui/build.ps1 (the 01:06 landing rule). Alphabet mirror: alphabet has no menu_tabs (and no Leptos), so nothing to mirror. Queued after kit's rename finalize (the pause would stop it now).")
inl alphabet_granular_heal () = Task ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal alphabet host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (34 sites, 1,340 chars, all in apps/documents/documents.spi), together with the dice mirror", Story, P1, D3, 85u32, Active, "", "granular,macros,alphabet,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_alphabet_granular_heal () = Planning ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "main", "10-25 03:11 main: 25 of 48 macro sites healed in the repo (escape-free strings + path splices -> plain ++#), 23 left: CSS/TeX escape lines 51-82, crowbook --single quoting 89/103, Rust block 283, git ls-tree/hash-object 357/364, exports/args/exit 739-746. Proof job-1792911393939-main-alphabet-heal-build (build.ps1 -SkipNotebook: Rust compile, cargo, run on README + nota with exact nota.hangul.md). Dice mirror: dice has no documents.spi, nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:01Z", "main", "10-25 07:01 main: heal2 GREEN (job-1792912923374). The 17 escape-heavy sites (CSS/TeX args, the crowbook --single quoting, git ls-tree/hash-object) are plain Spiral strings with ++#. Proof: every folded literal in documents.rs is unchanged (202 before, 208 after; 0 missing, 0 unexpected beyond the 6 allowed concatenation pieces), no format! remains at the healed sites, and RUST-OK passes with the exact nota.hangul.md check. 5 macro lines remain. The Fsharp exports (739/740) and the Rust argv/exit (743/746) are one host call each, so granular style keeps them. The real one left is the Rust directory-walk block at 283 (find .md files, skip .hangul.md), which becomes a file_system call in Spiral.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T11:57Z", "main", "10-25 07:57 main: heal3 (the Rust directory walk at line 283 as plain Spiral: read_dir_names + directory_exists + </> + am'.new_vec) was RED (job-1792926763461): documents.main logged result_len = 0, so no README/nota outputs were produced. heal3 restored documents.spi and documents.rs (macro lines back to 5). directory_exists is right (exists && is_dir), and relative_path strips dist_dir then maps \\ to /, so the separator mix alone shouldn't zero the list. Probe job-1792929390206 checks the building blocks on a temp dir (read_dir_names count, directory_exists on dir and file, ends_with) to find which one returns the unexpected value.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:33Z", "main", "10-25 14:33 main: heal3's RED (result_len 0) root cause found, and it's in lib, not in the walk. walk-probe-2 (job-1792943650492) ran heal3's exact walk on a temp tree: the list has 2 files, but am'.new_vec turns it into a vec of length 0 (actual (2, 0), expected (2, 2)). new_vec's Rust arm pushed into the vec inside a listm.iter closure. For a literal list that iteration unrolls at compile time, which is the only case its existing test covers; for a runtime list the closure pushes into a captured copy. Fix staged as LIB-NEW-VEC-RUNTIME (%TEMP%\\lib-new-vec-runtime\\apply.ps1): fold vec_push over the list, plus a test whose list comes from a runtime recursion. Gate + land queued: job-1792953140278, after the lib chain. Then heal3 reruns unchanged; its rule stays the alphabet RUST-OK run check with the exact nota.hangul.md.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-26T10:06Z", "tibia-lane", "10:06Z 10:1xZ tibia lane: the documents.spi walk and exit heal (GRANULAR-HEAL notes 06:35Z/06:48Z; original and healed walk proven identical on a fixture: 6 files, and 1 with the filter) is packed into tibia-serial-gaps. It applies with documents-heal-apply.ps1 -Apply right after the lib lands. The step is guarded: env.spi must have exit_with and file_system.spi must have path_join_native (both node GREEN, landing in the same job). It lands as an unstaged working-tree change in alphabet; documents.spi is LF and clean in git, so only the healed spans change. Follow-up after the land: the documents app's cargo build on the remote kit worker node.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-26T19:28Z", "main", "10-26 15:28 main: 10-26 15:2x main: alphabet apps/documents argv slice drafted (TEMP alphabet-lib-args/apply.ps1), the CLI-ARGS-STYLE consumer twin of dir-tree-html's. main's Rust arm built argv as one host iterator chain (std::env::args().skip(1).map(...).collect into Rc<RefCell<Vec>>). It becomes ':= args : array_base string = env.command_line_arguments () |> listm'.box |> listm'.to_array'' (the list-to-array idiom lib runtime.split_args uses). env.command_line_arguments starts at the first user argument (try_command_line_argument applies each backend's argument_offset), matching skip(1). Anchored, count 1, 0 host argv code left. The new line is 105 chars; the file's one line over 120 (292, 127 chars) predates this change and is left as authored. Guarded: refuses unless lib env.spi carries command_line_arguments. Its exit line (758) waits on LIB-EXIT-WITH, as before. Proof after the lib chain job-1793024343933 lands: alphabet apps/documents/build.ps1 (the consumer build) on the applied tree, with restore-on-RED like perf-fix-run. dice: no counterpart (dice has no host argv chain; census 13:3x).")
inl apps_granular_heal () = Task ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal spiral/apps host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (63 sites: apps/spiral/spiral.spi 54/1,776, cells/spi/text.spi 5/911, cells console, wasm)", Story, P1, D3, 25u32, Active, "", "granular,macros,apps,cells,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_apps_granular_heal () = Planning ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T11:34Z", "main", "10-25 07:34 main: pass 1 queued (job-1792928007912) with a generic tool, %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\plain-macros.ps1 -Spi -Output -Name [-Livemd]. It rewrites plain macro strings ($'$\"text\"', no braces, escapes, splices or quotes) into Spiral strings, and passes only if (a) with -Livemd, the livemd exported to the spi before healing AND the healed livemd exports to the healed spi byte for byte, and (b) the compiled output is byte-identical before and after. Otherwise it restores. apps/spiral: spiral.livemd is the source of truth (build.ps1 exports it first), and both files have 86 such sites, most of them the generated .fsproj template. Its 216 macro lines also hold splice and escape sites for later passes. The tool is a candidate for an eoie verb next to GRANULAR-LINT's 'eoie lint macros' (rule: generic tools, not per-experiment scripts).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:46Z", "main", "10-25 07:46 main: pass 1 re-planned; the first job (job-1792928007912) was stopped while still queued, with spiral.spi untouched (216 macro lines). Finding: the .fsproj template is dead code. persist_code_project is called only by publish_code (only by publish_file, 0 callers) and persist_file (0 callers); publish_project and read_file are reached only from those. A grep of all 11 repos (excluding generated .rs/.fsx/.fs/.ipynb) finds the names only in apps/spiral/spiral.spi and .livemd, with a positive control; SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 1 (10-17) already called it dead. Step 1, job-1792928699219: drop the 6 definitions (persist_code_project..publish_file, the contiguous block before '## rust') from spiral.spi (151 lines) and spiral.livemd (256 lines); get_package_dir stays, since line 270 uses it. Spiral emits only reachable code, so the proof is byte-identical spiral.rs plus the livemd export reproducing the spi. This removes 41 macro lines. Step 2, job-1792928704408 (after-ok): heal the 59 plain macro strings left. FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER: the dotnet publish / fsproj path in apps/spiral is gone with step 1.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T12:38Z", "main", "10-25 08:38 main: FOUND a drift from the 10-24 edition-3 landing. apps/spiral/spiral.livemd is the build's source of truth (build.ps1 exports it with --export-only before compiling), but respell-repo.ps1 only touches .spi/.spir, so the livemd stayed in edition-2 words. The next apps/spiral build would have silently reverted spiral.spi to edition 2. The same shape applies to spiral/apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.livemd and polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd. Fix: %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\livemd-from-spi.ps1 regenerates each livemd from its edition-3 spi through Cells and applies only if the new livemd exports back to the spi byte for byte. apps/spiral dry run GREEN; the apply is job-1792931767062; wasm and perf dry runs are queued. Decision, by the '.spi as notebook' direction (memory self-checking-samples-direction): the regenerated notebook uses Cells' canonical cell layout (40 smart cells, was 64; headings and prose identical except one), so the hand cell boundaries aren't kept. The one cell dropped is '### escape_cell (test)', a static test cell that defines escape_cell and asserts nothing. It never reached the spi (the old export dropped it too), and a grep of all 9 repos finds it only in that livemd, so it's dead under the same rule as the publish chain. Then the dead-publish drop and the plain-string heal are requeued after it.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T15:35Z", "main", "10-25 11:35 main: step 1 GREEN (job-1792931858307): the 6 dead publish definitions are dropped from spiral.spi (151 lines) and the regenerated spiral.livemd (211 lines); the livemd export reproduces the spi byte for byte, and spiral.rs is byte-identical (B5D5A1E4A436DEDA). Step 2, the plain-string heal on the 59 remaining sites, runs next (job-1792931861319).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-25T17:23Z", "main", "10-25 13:23 main: step 2 (heal-plain-3, job-1792931861319) RED was the byte-identical rule, not a behaviour change. spiral.rs 1,459,426 -> 1,455,245 B. Read: the heal turns 59 plain macro pieces into Spiral strings, and the compiler then (a) shares identical literal constants within a function (the Cargo.toml writer reuses the earlier '[dependencies]' and '' bindings, so the standalone literal count drops 3 -> 2 with the same text written) and (b) folds adjacent plain pieces of ++# chains statically, so the concat trees differ in shape. With literal bindings inlined per function and vN normalized, 128 of 20,6xx lines still differ, all concat-shape. So text equality can't prove this class of heal; dice's attribute heal is the same class. Rule for heal-plain-4, written before the run: plain-macros.ps1 -RunCheck <project build.ps1>. If the output is byte-identical: GREEN as before. Otherwise GREEN iff the project's own release gate passes on the healed tree. For apps/spiral that is build.ps1's run check: --help, export, the export of every livemd compared with the previous CLI, and the rust/cuda/lua/gleam runs, which write Cargo.toml and gleam.toml through exactly the healed writers. build.ps1 ships the CLI only on pass. On any failure, the sources and spiral.rs are restored and the previous CLI stays.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-25T17:32Z", "main", "10-25 13:32 main: the Cells trailing-markdown fix is RED and withdrawn (job-1792933087976; snapshot restored, document.spi has no diff, keep_docs is back). Dropping keep_docs from export_text broke 3 suite tests: 'the export equals spiral export on the dice and alphabet notebooks', 'the export keeps directive lines ... follows the CLI export's markdown rules', and the _real.spir interleave round trip. So 'a trailing markdown cell after the last code cell is not part of the spi' is a rule Cells and the spiral CLI export share, not a Cells bug. Consequence for polyglot Perf.livemd (livemd-from-spi dry RED: 2 inserted lines): its trailing markdown can't be regenerated from the spi by design. The fix is in the notebook: move that trailing text above the last code cell (a heading section's prose), then regenerate. Rule for that edit: livemd-from-spi round-trips byte for byte, and the moved prose is unchanged as a line multiset.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:04Z", "main", "10-25 14:04 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd must NOT be regenerated from Perf.spi. It is a mixed notebook: 0 spiral fences, 5 elixir smart cells holding the Spiral code, and 13 fsharp cells (the original F# perf tests, e.g. returnLettersWithOddCountTests' solutions). The spi export carries only the Spiral cells, so livemd-from-spi would drop every F# cell (47,168 -> 30,026 bytes). Its dry run was RED only on the trailing results block, and only because it compared spi with spi. Rule added to livemd-from-spi: refuse a livemd that has any code fence other than spiral/elixir-smart-cell. Perf's fix is an in-place respell of the edition-2 words inside its Spiral smart cells (respell-repo over .livemd cell bodies), proven by: the livemd exports to an spi equal to today's edition-3 Perf.spi, and every non-Spiral cell is byte-identical.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:21Z", "main", "10-25 14:21 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd respelled IN PLACE and landed unstaged (105+/105-, previous copy in %TEMP%\\perf-respell\\Perf.livemd.previous). Method (%TEMP%\\perf-respell\\respell-cells.ps1): export the livemd (670 lines); respell that export with the pack-spelling candidate's 'format --spelling symbols', which keeps the line count (670 = 670); locate each elixir smart cell's body (first to last non-blank line) in order in the export; put back the same range of the respelled export. 5 Spiral cells, 4 respelled; every fsharp cell, markdown line and cell frame byte-identical (checked by masking the cells). Proof: the respelled livemd exports to an spi byte-identical to the respelled export. The committed Perf.spi differs from it only by the installed formatter's doc-comment re-spacing plus the trailing results block (no code-only edits lost). Side finding for FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS: that formatter collapsed the column alignment inside /// markdown tables (e.g. 'abc        | abc' -> 'abc | abc') and rewrapped table rows over 120; comment interiors should be kept as written (F7).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-25T19:12Z", "main", "10-25 15:12 main: step 2 GREEN and LANDED (heal-plain-4, job-1792949109509): the 59 plain macro strings in apps/spiral/spiral.spi and spiral.livemd are now Spiral strings (unstaged; the livemd export reproduced the healed spi). The output changed only by literal sharing and folding (n5), so by n5's rule the release gate decided: apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check passed (--help, export, the 4-notebook export comparison with the previous CLI, and the rust/cuda/lua/gleam runs that write Cargo.toml and gleam.toml through the healed writers), and the new spiral.exe shipped to workspace/target/release. Next: the remaining host macros in apps/spiral are the manifest pieces with escaped quotes; heal them as escaped Spiral strings, by the same gate.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T19:20Z", "main", "10-25 15:20 main: step 3 queued (heal-escaped). plain-macros.ps1 gains -Mode escaped: a F#-string macro piece with escaped quotes or doubled braces and NO interpolation becomes a Spiral string (escaped quotes kept as Spiral escapes, doubled braces become single). Sites with a single brace or a ! (real interpolation, 53) are untouched; F# formats any type there while ++ needs strings, so they need typed rewrites. 41 sites in spiral.spi, the same 41 in the livemd. The rule is n5's, written before heal-plain-4: byte-identical output is GREEN; otherwise GREEN iff apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check passes on the healed tree (it ships the CLI only on pass); any failure restores the sources and spiral.rs, and the previous CLI stays.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-25T19:36Z", "main", "10-25 15:36 main: step 4 queued after step 3 (heal-interp, same files, so sequential). plain-macros.ps1 -Mode interp rewrites an interpolating F#-string macro piece into Spiral concatenation, e.g. name = spiral_{hash} becomes (literal ++#? hash ++# literal), with parentheses so the grouping inside the surrounding newline-join chain can't matter. Only sites whose every interpolated name is on a verified-string allowlist are rewritten (22 names, each read at its binding: string literals, path joins, read_all_text, sm'.concat, obj_to_string, from_std_string, union_to_string, crypto.hash_text, whose test asserts a string). On a dry run that is 20 of 46 interpolating sites. Excluded: deps, k, v, x, ext (a std_string), runtime_cmd and the two result tuples, since show (#?) formats non-primitive types differently from Rust Display. Same rule as n5 (the build.ps1 run check decides).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T22:49Z", "main", "10-25 18:49 main: CI RED on the user's d095f13d push (run 40222011377, both build legs) is MINE, from the 10-25 08:4x wasm livemd regeneration (n3). apps/wasm/build.ps1 runs the notebook (run_notebook --output-path ipynb) before exporting, and that run failed: spiral_wasm.livemd:277:46 ETY-UnboundVariable run_main. Cause: the spi has ONE '///> _' (line 162) covering the ### main section (run_main and main). livemd-from-spi let Cells split the section into 2 cells, but the target line went only on the first. So in the notebook run the run_main cell is skipped and the main cell is compiled without it. The old livemd (59b477ba) had '///> _' in both cells. My proof (export round trip, byte-equal) can't see this, because export ignores cell targets. FIX in the tree, unstaged: the main cell gets '///> _' (livemd +3/-1, previous copy in %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\spiral_wasm.livemd.before-target-fix), and the spi is re-exported from it (+2 lines: '///> _' at 162 and 217). Verifying with CI's exact notebook run on the node (job-1792968520121, mirror main-wasm, type check). Rule fixes: (1) livemd-from-spi.ps1 must also run the notebook (not just export) before applying; (2) the Cells spi-to-livemd split should copy a section's ///> line into every cell it creates from that section (Cells item to file). spiral.livemd and Perf.livemd: check for the same section split.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-25T22:54Z", "main", "10-25 18:54 main: scope of the n12 split bug: only spiral_wasm.livemd. apps/spiral/spiral.livemd keeps its one '///> _' section (spi 1752: run_main + main) in ONE cell (livemd line 2102, which holds both), so its notebook run is unaffected; polyglot Perf.spi has no target lines. The first node check of the wasm fix (job-1792968520121) was VOID for environment reasons: the mirror lacked gitignored deps/The-Spiral-Language, so Cells' package.spiproj could not load the fork core. Junctioned from the node's main clone; rerun job-1792968763090.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_14 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-25T22:57Z", "main", "10-25 18:57 main: wasm CI fix PROVEN. CI's failing step (run_notebook on apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.livemd with --output-path ipynb) exits 0 on the node with the fixed tree (job-1792968763090, mirror main-wasm at this box's tree, digest FAF8B9167ABF9588, L6 core guard): no FatalError, run_main resolves. The notebook has no test blocks, so discovered 0 is expected. The fix (livemd +3/-1, spi +2) is unstaged for the user to commit; it should turn run 40222011377's red green on the next push. livemd-from-spi.ps1 now also (a) refuses a regeneration whose section has a ///> line on only some of its cells (positive control: it flags '### main' on the original bad wasm regeneration) and (b) runs the regenerated notebook as the build does, requiring exit 0 before -Apply.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_15 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-26T01:36Z", "main", "10-25 21:36 main: heal-escaped GREEN (main-apps-spiral-heal-escaped, finished 21:24): the escaped macro strings in apps/spiral/spiral.spi are Spiral strings, the output changed only in literal sharing and folding, and build.ps1's run check passed on rust, cuda, lua and gleam (each exits 42); RUST-OK apps/spiral, and it shipped the new spiral.exe to workspace/target/release. Healed spiral.spi is in the tree, unstaged for the user. heal-interp (the last of the three modes) is running.")
inl polyglot_granular_heal () = Task ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal polyglot host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (13 sites, 3,476 chars: chat_contract.spi 4/2,076, lib/math/math.spi 9/1,400)", Story, P2, D3, 10u32, Planned, "", "granular,macros,polyglot,main", "current", "current")
