inl kit_rename () = Task ("KIT-RENAME", "Rename eoie to kit everywhere (the user already renamed the repo): a methodical plan, fix every reference, and fix the issues the rename surfaces instead of renaming blindly (user 2026-10-24 06:57 review list)", Epic, P1, D6, 60u32, Active, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "eoie,kit,rename", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_rename () = Planning ("KIT-RENAME", Minutes 1440u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_rename_1 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom, sequencing rule written before work: the rename starts only after tonight's train (GE3 -> HIJK -> ... -> R) has installed and the next-train stages S1-S5 are either landed or re-anchored. Every slice in flight anchors on eoie_* package names, eoie.exe paths and 'eoie ' receipt prefixes, so a rename mid-train breaks them all deterministically. Method: (1) census every 'eoie' token by kind (package dir, crate name, binary, receipt prefix, env var EOIE_*, docs, backlog IDs, peer scripts in other repos); (2) a typed rename table, with old names kept as aliases for one release (binary shim, env var fallbacks); (3) one stage per kind, each emulated and cargo-checked like tonight's train. EOIE-NO-CLAUDE-NAMES runs in the same census pass.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_2 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 2u32, "2026-10-24T11:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:52 main: include eoie_cli_harness in the rename (the user does not want the word harness; BANNED-WORDS-SCAN note 1): rename the package and its 30+ `///- --package ../eoie_cli_harness` headers in the same pass.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_3 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 3u32, "2026-10-25T12:04Z", "eoie", "08:04 eoie census (user 10-25 07:5x: 'eoie falls behind, edition not converted, projects not renamed; are we far from a healed phase?'). eoie/src: 427 packages (149 named eoie_*), 146 of 234 crates named eoie-*, 4087 'eoie' tokens in 1208 Spiral files, 481 receipt strings starting with 'eoie ', 67 distinct EOIE_* env vars, 37 package headers pointing at ../eoie_* folders (eoie_cli_harness among them, which loses 'harness' in the same pass). Order, decided: (1) SLATE (its 5l' full measure is running now); (2) the edition-3 respell of eoie/src (6300 top-level inl/let definitions) as one mechanical pass and one release; (3) this rename as one mechanical pass and one release, from a typed table (old to new for package dir, crate name, binary, receipt prefix, env var, header path), with peer-repo scripts that call eoie.exe found by stem grep. Freeze: no new eoie features land between SLATE and the rename release, because each would need re-anchoring twice. Drafts already built (lint verb, lib batch, list split follow-ups) stay in scratch and get re-anchored once, after the rename. Both passes are prepared on scratch copies while SLATE runs, so each lands right after the previous release installs.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_4 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 4u32, "2026-10-25T12:07Z", "eoie", "08:07 eoie: rename table started (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\packages.csv): 149 package folders eoie_* -> kit_*, 0 clashes with existing folders; eoie_cli_harness -> kit_cli_check (drops the banned word). Callers outside eoie/src that name eoie.exe or EOIE_* (per-location grep): ~/.claude/hooks autonomous-gate.ps1 (3) and eoie-style-check.ps1 (1), plus the settings.json hook commands that name those scripts; spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/machine-watch.ps1 (1); tibia scripts/score.ps1, live/block.ps1, live/arena-summary.ps1 (1 each; the tibia session owns them, so it gets the rename table and edits them itself); i574n.github/scripts 0; x 0. The binary keeps a one-release eoie.exe forwarding copy beside kit.exe only if a caller cannot move in the same pass; the default is to move every caller in the same pass, with no alias. The repo folder C:\\home\\git\\eoie and the hook file names are part of the table too.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_5 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 5u32, "2026-10-25T13:47Z", "eoie", "09:47 eoie: hook change map for the rename pass (the gates run every session, so they move in the same pass as the binary). ~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1 lines 157, 162, 177 call C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe (window list; agile list --load light; agile next); eoie-style-check.ps1 line 2 sets the root to C:\\home\\git\\eoie and line 42 calls eoie.exe standards --ratchet. Both scripts get one binary-path variable, then the new name; the hook file name eoie-style-check.ps1 and its settings.json entry rename with it. Sequencing, decided: (a) the rename release renames packages, crates, the binary (kit.exe), receipt prefixes and env vars inside the repo, and moves the hooks plus spiral's machine-watch.ps1 in the same pass; tibia's 3 scripts get the table, for the tibia session to apply; (b) the local folder move C:\\home\\git\\eoie -> C:\\home\\git\\kit is a separate, last step, coordinated through agent sync, because running sessions (this one included) use that folder as their working directory; until then kit.exe lives at C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_6 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 6u32, "2026-10-25T17:31Z", "eoie", "13:31 kit: the 3 Claude hooks in ~/.claude/settings.json are now in args form: an exe in 'command', each argument a separate 'args' item, no shell. The rename pass edits the script paths inside those args arrays, not command strings: eoie-tree-lease.ps1, eoie-style-check.ps1, and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\tree-leases. Then rerun scratch spawn-probe\\hook-args-test.ps1 (8 cases, old vs new) and a live lease positive control on a throwaway root before calling the hooks done.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_7 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:45Z", "eoie", "14:45 kit: gap found in the rename plan. kit-rename.ps1 replaces eoie with kit in all src text, so the binary also starts using %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\ instead of %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\. That folder holds every lane's live state today: jobs (2,155 entries, the registry of running and queued jobs), windows (4, the slot/window store), tree-leases, supervisor (5 relaunch copies), machine-watch, retired.\n- Risk: a kit binary would see no jobs or windows, while jobs started by the old binary keep running unseen. Two lanes could then hold heavy slots at once.\n- Callers of that path outside eoie/src (per-location grep, positive control on spiral's machine-watch): only ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1 and its settings.json args (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\tree-leases). No EOIE_* env var is set in spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/scripts, i574n.github/scripts or tibia/scripts.\nRule, written before the release:\n- The rename installs at a quiescent point: no eoie job queued or running and no held window, announced to all lanes beforehand.\n- The state move runs after the rename release job settles, never inside it (the job itself lives in eoie\\jobs while it runs). A post-install step renames %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit in one directory rename, swaps the hook args to kit\\tree-leases in the same step, and checks that kit job list shows the history.\n- No fallback reads of eoie\\ (no alias, per note 5), and no copying: a single rename keeps one record per fact.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_8 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:47Z", "eoie", "14:47 kit: receipt-format dependencies outside eoie, for the rename pass. Receipt prefixes change from 'eoie ' to 'kit ' (481 strings), so any external parser of receipts breaks silently: a regex that stops matching drops data without an error.\n- Grep for receipt patterns ('eoie <verb>' and 'eoie <verb> ... ok|error|refused'): 0 hits in i574n.github/scripts, tibia (*.ps1) and x (*.ps1). Positive control: the same pattern finds 5 in eoie/spec/one-call.md.\n- spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/machine-watch.ps1 needs 4 edits, not the 1 the census counted:\n  - line 3, the binary path ../eoie/eoie.exe;\n  - line 4, the fallback command 'eoie';\n  - line 100, the receipt filter '^(eoie job tail|--- )';\n  - line 120, the window holder detail '\\(eoie job (job-\\S+)\\)'.\n  Line 23 ('job supervise (job-\\S+)') survives, because the verb stays; owner labels 'eoie:<id>' (lines 23, 99) are internal to that script.\n- Rule: the rename pass edits these together with the binary, then runs machine-watch once and checks it reports the running jobs and windows, the same rows as before the rename.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_9 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 9u32, "2026-10-25T19:03Z", "eoie", "15:03 kit: memory sweep for the rename pass (memory names a path, so after the move it must be verified or fixed).\n- 9 memory files name concrete eoie paths (10 occurrences: eoie.exe, C:\\home\\git\\eoie, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie): eoie-hooks, copy-eoie-src-only, backlog-in-eoie, backlog-always-has-cpu-light, cells-reads-live-tree, sweep-leftover-shells, heavy-gate-lessons, remote-kit-worker-node, retry-after-memory-kills.\n- These path facts are updated in the same pass as the binary and state move.\n- 12 memory files carry 'eoie' in their slug. Slugs are link targets ([[name]]), so they keep their names unless every [[link]] and the MEMORY.md index are rewritten in one pass. Decision: keep the slugs, and fix only the paths and binary names inside them, so links can't break.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_10 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 10u32, "2026-10-25T20:05Z", "eoie", "16:05 kit: dry run re-run on a fresh copy (scratch kit-rename\\dry-run.ps1; 1,445 text files from the live src). Result: 693 files rewritten (5,468 tokens), 149 package folders moved, 0 eoie tokens left, unchanged from the morning run. It also confirms note 7 concretely: 9 data-dir path sites (the eoie folder joined under LOCALAPPDATA in eoie_jobs/host.spi, contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi, eoie_machine_watch/jobs_watch.spi and others) now name kit. So without the post-install directory move, the renamed binary reads an empty LOCALAPPDATA kit folder.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_11 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 11u32, "2026-10-25T20:14Z", "eoie", "16:14 kit: the state move from note 7 is now a tested script. Scratch: kit-rename\\kit-state-move.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply to move) and kit-state-move-test.ps1.\n- Rules it enforces:\n  - refuse unless quiescent: no live job supervisor process (eoie or kit 'job supervise' command lines, its own process excluded), no window files and no tree lease;\n  - refuse if the LOCALAPPDATA kit folder already exists (no merge);\n  - one Directory.Move of eoie to kit;\n  - switch the settings.json hook args from the eoie to the kit tree-leases path;\n  - exit 1 unless the job count after the move equals the count before.\n- Tests on a fake LOCALAPPDATA: 6/6 pass. Cases: live supervisors refuse (real pattern, while SLATE runs: 21 supervisors alive now); held window refuses; live lease refuses; dry run writes nothing; apply moves 2 of 2 jobs and switches 1 arg; an existing kit folder refuses.\n- Bug caught by the tests: the process check matched its own command line, because the pattern text was in its arguments. It now excludes its own process.\n- Operational note: 21 live supervisors means the quiescent point needs every lane drained. Announce a window to all lanes before the rename release, and run this script after its job settles.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_12 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 12u32, "2026-10-25T20:44Z", "eoie", "16:44 kit: the rename table for tibia's 3 scripts (tibia applies it; read-only check of tibia/scripts/score.ps1, live/block.ps1, live/arena-summary.ps1).\n- Binary lookup, one line each (score.ps1:11, block.ps1:14, arena-summary.ps1:4): today 'Get-Command eoie, else C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe'.\n- Two-stage trap: between the rename release and the final folder move the binary is kit.exe still inside C:\\home\\git\\eoie, and only after the move is it at C:\\home\\git\\kit\\kit.exe.\n  Table: look up 'kit' on PATH first, else the first existing of C:\\home\\git\\kit\\kit.exe and C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe. After the folder move, drop the second path.\n- Verbs used: table query, table logs, window run, experiment analyze. None renames, so no change.\n- Output parsing: only table CSV (ConvertFrom-Csv on --quiet output); no 'eoie ' receipt prefixes are parsed, so no change.\n- Cosmetic: two throw messages in arena-summary.ps1 (lines 7 and 16) say 'eoie table'.\nSend this table to the tibia session when the rename release is announced.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_13 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 13u32, "2026-10-25T20:46Z", "eoie", "16:46 kit: the same two-stage path applies to every caller kit owns, so each path edit happens twice. The binary moves first to C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe (the rename release), then to C:\\home\\git\\kit\\kit.exe (the folder move, last).\n- ~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1 (3 calls) and eoie-style-check.ps1 (root and binary).\n- spiral's machine-watch.ps1 (binary path line 3, fallback line 4).\n- The memory files (note 9).\nRule: each of these takes one binary-path variable, set at the rename release and set again at the folder move. Verify both times with one real call each: gate window list, style-check standards, machine-watch job list.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_14 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 14u32, "2026-10-25T21:28Z", "eoie", "17:28 kit (user 10-25: 'we need to speed up the rename'). Decision: the edition-3 respell and the rename ship as ONE release, saving a full release cycle.\n- Gate, written before running: every regenerated .rs must equal SLATE's published .rs with the rename rules applied to its text (rules: eoie_cli_harness to kit_cli_check, EOIE to KIT, Eoie to Kit, eoie to kit). The only exception is kit_standards, for the step-0 standards fix. Both mechanical passes must therefore be codegen-neutral beyond names; any other difference stops the release.\n- Job: scratch release-build-job-RENAME.ps1, generated by clean\\respell\\make-RENAME.ps1 from SLATE's job.\n  - The whole text is renamed with protected tokens: C:\\home\\git\\eoie (moves last), the LOCALAPPDATA eoie state paths (they move after the job), the -Eoie script parameters and Publish-EoieBinary.\n  - It builds -p kit-cli and installs C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe. job-script-check ok, 138 commands.\n- Gap found and fixed: kit-rename.ps1 covered only src, while 26 repo scripts (build.ps1, dev.ps1, spiral.ps1, compiler-contracts/*.ps1) carry EOIE_* env vars, eoie-dev, eoie.exe and EoieRoot. It now takes -Repo and renames them too, protecting the absolute root. Dry run on copies: 26 files, 427 tokens, 0 left, 3 root paths kept.\n- Parallel proof requested from kit2: rename a synced copy on the worker node, regen all 234 with the measured 5l'' compiler, cargo check. That's read-only, a compile on a copy, so the release here starts right after SLATE.\n- Order after SLATE: post-install checks; quiescent window announced; standards fix; respell -Apply; kit-rename.ps1 -Src src -Repo root; snapshot; job start --heavy with the RENAME job; after it settles, kit-state-move.ps1 -Apply, the hooks/machine-watch/memory path pass, the tibia table; the folder move last.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_15 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 15u32, "2026-10-25T21:34Z", "eoie", "17:34 kit: the out-of-repo path pass is a script: scratch kit-rename\\kit-paths.ps1 -Stage Release|State|Move (dry run by default, -Apply to write). Dry runs on the live files:\n- Release, 9: autonomous-gate 3 binary paths; eoie-style-check the binary name and the ratchet receipt filter; machine-watch path, fallback, job-tail filter and window-holder pattern.\n- State, 1: the tree-lease hook's own lease folder. That one was missed until now: kit-state-move switches only the settings.json args, but the hook script hard-codes the folder too.\n- Move, 9: autonomous-gate 3, style-check root, and 5 memory files naming the root.\nMain confirmed: queued jobs stop at the announce, lands-4 gets 10 min notice, main's tooling plus the shared land.ps1 (fiscal, tibia) move to kit.exe after the release. Receipt field names (state=, label=, ...) keep; only adoption's eoie_calls/eoie_share columns and the '(eoie job' holder labels change.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_16 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 16u32, "2026-10-25T21:39Z", "eoie", "17:39 kit: the combined-release gate gains a pre-registered fallback: a file that differs only in line order (for example if the compiler orders definitions by name and the renamed names sort differently) is accepted and listed; any content difference still stops the release. Gate test on a fake tree, 5/5 pass: baseline keys are renamed; a pure rename passes; a line reordering is accepted and listed; a content change in a crate that only references a renamed crate is reported; the intended kit_standards stays excluded. RENAME job regenerated, job-script-check ok (142 commands). SLATE's regen passed 234/234 at 17:33; its build and tests are running.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_17 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 17u32, "2026-10-25T21:42Z", "eoie", "17:42 kit: the combined release now launches with one command: scratch clean\\respell\\launch-RENAME.ps1.\n- Without -Go it checks the preconditions only:\n  - no eoie/kit release queued or running, no exclusive window, no tree lease;\n  - SLATE's post-install marker exists (post-install-SLATE.ps1 now writes post-install-SLATE.passed only on a full pass);\n  - the installed compiler exists and the RENAME job passes its script check.\n  Live check now: correctly refuses, since SLATE is still running.\n- With -Go, in order, each step stopping on failure:\n  1. snapshot before-release-RENAME, before any source change;\n  2. the standards fix via eoie do, requiring ok, 1 file, 3 replacements;\n  3. respell-repo -Apply with the installed 5l'' (RED exits 1 and applies nothing);\n  4. kit-rename -Src -Repo;\n  5. the regen filter, requiring 234 crates and 0 still named eoie;\n  6. job start --heavy kit-release-RENAME with the gated job.\n- Human steps around it: 10 min notice to main before -Go, the window announcement, and after the job settles kit-state-move -Apply, kit-paths Release then State, the tibia table, and the folder move with kit-paths Move last.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_18 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 18u32, "2026-10-25T21:45Z", "eoie", "17:45 kit: split-brain closed. After the release installs kit.exe, the old eoie.exe stays in the root, and any caller still using it after the state move would silently recreate an empty LOCALAPPDATA eoie folder: two job registries. kit-state-move.ps1 now retires the old binary (renamed to eoie.exe.retired-<stamp>) in the same step as the folder move, so stragglers fail loudly instead. Tests 7/7 pass (new case: old binary retired on apply). Until the state move, the old eoie.exe keeps serving other lanes against the unmoved state, so lanes can repoint at their own pace inside the window.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_19 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 19u32, "2026-10-25T21:53Z", "eoie", "17:53 kit: fast path pre-registered and built, to skip the release's ~2 h local regen.\n- Rule: compiler (kit2's renamed session) regenerates the respelled, standards-fixed and renamed tree on the worker node with the same 5l'' compiler and returns a sorted manifest of every generated .rs as crate\\file plus SHA256.\n- If SLATE's .rs, text-renamed, match that manifest for every crate except kit_standards, the release runs release-build-job-FASTRENAME.ps1:\n  - the text-renamed .rs are checked against the manifest in one pass and written only if every file matches;\n  - kit_standards alone is regenerated locally;\n  - then the same build, tests, candidate checks and install as SLATE.\n  Any mismatch writes nothing and the full RENAME job runs instead.\n- Fast-gate test 2/2: a matching manifest writes the renamed files; one mismatch writes nothing, not even the matching file. job-script-check ok, 134 commands.\n- launch-RENAME.ps1 -Go -Fast picks it, and refuses -Fast without the manifest.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_20 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 20u32, "2026-10-25T22:09Z", "eoie", "18:09 kit: the worker-node rename proof is running, driven by kit (compiler handed it over and moved to the compiler lane).\n- Sync: REMOTE-SYNC-OK, mirror renameproof, head a12135ba plus 220 changed files (SLATE's published .rs included), digest 520AF46D21793B25.\n- Shipped to the node stage: the 5l'' compiler (core CDD68DC3EE829A67, the same as installed here); kit-rename.ps1; the standards .do; respell-repo.ps1 with a RESPELL_FORMATTER override (the node's installed compiler is a different build, 826D67C69119F61E, so the formatter pass is pinned to 5l'' for parity); node-proof.ps1, which sets SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL to the shipped 5l'' for the regen.\n- Node job: job-1792966042350-renameproof. Order: standards fix, already 'ok files 1 replacements 3'; respell -Apply; kit-rename -Src -Repo; regen of all crates with -CargoCheck -Publish into the mirror; the sorted SHA256 manifest.\n- Local watcher b39hbxfu1 fetches the manifest and runs expected-manifest.ps1 -Compare. Expected manifest precomputed from SLATE's published .rs, text-renamed: 260 files. Verdict: FAST PATH ELIGIBLE or NOT ELIGIBLE.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_21 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 21u32, "2026-10-25T22:11Z", "eoie", "18:11 kit: the full rename toolchain is staged in scratch clean\\, each piece dry-run or tested:\n- respell\\launch-RENAME.ps1 -Go [-Fast]: preconditions, snapshot, standards fix, respell, kit-rename, filter, job.\n- release-build-job-RENAME.ps1 (full regen with the rename-aware gate) or -FASTRENAME.ps1 (manifest-checked text rename plus kit_standards regen).\n- kit-rename\\finalize-RENAME.ps1 [-Apply], run after the job settles: kit-state-move (quiescent check, retires eoie.exe), kit-paths Release and State, then a smoke check (kit job list and window list, gate binary path, machine-watch). It refuses until kit.exe is published.\n- The folder move with kit-paths -Stage Move comes last.\nCritical path after SLATE installs: post-install checks; 10 min notice to main; the window announce; launch-RENAME -Go [-Fast]; the job; finalize -Apply; tibia table and main notified.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_22 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 22u32, "2026-10-25T22:39Z", "eoie", "18:39 kit: node proof run 1 findings (job-1792966042350-renameproof).\n- Respell: applied, 843 files.\n- Rename: 719 files rewritten (5,895 tokens; 26 repo scripts), 149 folders, 0 eoie-named crates left.\n- Regen: failed at once (exit 1, 0 crates), so the manifest hashed un-regenerated files and the 'NOT ELIGIBLE' verdict was meaningless. Cause: the renamed workspace.ps1 looks for the Spiral bundle at ..\\..\\spiral, which doesn't exist beside a mirror at C:\\home\\remote\\renameproof\\eoie. Fixed by setting KIT_SPIRAL_BUNDLE, which also proves the renamed scripts read KIT_* env vars. node-proof now stops instead of writing a manifest when the regen fails.\n- Real gap found by the comparison: kit-rename renamed file contents and folders but not file names. eoie_cli\\eoie.rs (the only file named with the old name, the CLI's generated output) would have stayed while the renamed Cargo.toml points at kit.rs, leaving a stale file, and the fast job would have checked the wrong path. kit-rename now renames package file names too.\nRun 2 started after a fresh sync (REMOTE-SYNC-OK, digest 520AF46D21793B25): job-1792967974695-renameproof-2, with watcher re-armed.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_23 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 23u32, "2026-10-25T22:44Z", "eoie", "18:44 kit: rename scope widened after an audit of what the release and the hooks read outside src.\n- standards\\census.spi: the ratchet baseline, 2,526 tokens keyed by eoie_* package names. Unrenamed, every kit_* package would read as new to eoie-style-check's ratchet.\n- spec\\one-call.md: the tutorial's exact expected outputs ('eoie job start ok ...'), compared by test-one-call-spec.ps1.\n- README.md, README.windows.md, AGENTS.md: docs that follow the rename.\n- Kept: FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW.md, a historical review record.\n- CI workflow (gh-pages.yml): no eoie references; it goes through build.ps1, which is already in scope.\n- kit-rename -Repo now includes these. Dry run on copies: 31 files, 3,150 tokens; 0 eoie left in each renamed file; FIRST-COMMIT-REVIEW untouched.\n- Node proof run 2 started with the narrower scope. None of the added files feed code generation, so its manifest stays valid for the fast-path decision.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_24 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 24u32, "2026-10-25T22:45Z", "eoie", "18:45 kit: rename scope outside this box's repo (broad grep, not just eoie.exe callers).\n(1) The worker node:\n  - ~/.config/remote-machines.json key 'eoie' points at C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe;\n  - remote.ps1 (job, status, sync guard) calls that path;\n  - node-watch.ps1 counts eoie jobs;\n  - the node has its own LOCALAPPDATA eoie job store.\n  After the rename lands here: copy kit.exe to the node; run kit-state-move.ps1 on the node with the node's paths and quiescent check (it is parameterized: -LocalAppData, -OldBinary, -Settings); rename the config key to kit, or at least repoint it to kit.exe; and after the folder move, sync the repo as 'kit' (the node checkout folder moves too). remote.ps1's parsers match state=/label=, which keep their names.\n(2) Kept on purpose, as stable identifiers like memory slugs: backlog item IDs (EOIE-*, 300+ items and every dependency link), the eoie team tag, the hook file names eoie-tree-lease.ps1 and eoie-style-check.ps1 (settings args name them), and the memory slugs. Renaming them buys nothing and risks broken links. The tool, its packages, receipts, env vars, binary and state folder are what the user asked to rename.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_25 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 25u32, "2026-10-25T23:27Z", "eoie", "19:27 kit: early evidence for the fast path, from node proof run 2 (214/234 crates regenerated, no failures). For 4 crates the node's regenerated .rs size equals the size of SLATE's .rs with the rename rules applied to its text, to the byte: process_kill 37,567, process_table 34,443, repo_state 24,809, repo_affected 26,819 (SLATE 37,581 / 34,449 / 24,814 / 26,822; each eoie to kit saves one byte). So respell plus rename change nothing in the generated code beyond names. The SHA256 manifest comparison after cargo check is the gate; this is a strong prior that it says ELIGIBLE.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_26 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 26u32, "2026-10-25T23:29Z", "eoie", "19:29 kit: node proof run 2 result.\n- Regenerated all 234 renamed and respelled crates through Spiral with 0 failures.\n- cargo check then refused: --locked, with Cargo.lock still naming eoie-* crates. That was a harness gap: node-proof skipped the lock refresh that the real RENAME and FASTRENAME jobs do first (cargo metadata --offline, SLATE job line 11). Since the check failed, nothing was published into the mirror.\n- Run 3 (job-1792970986069-renameproof-3) reuses the already respelled and renamed mirror (-RegenOnly), refreshes the lock, then regenerates with -CargoCheck -Publish and writes the manifest. Watcher re-armed.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_27 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 27u32, "2026-10-25T23:33Z", "eoie", "19:33 kit: node run 3 stopped at the lock refresh. cargo metadata --offline on the node wanted crates.io crates the node never downloaded. This box has them cached, which is why SLATE's offline refresh worked here; the rename changes only workspace member names, not external dependencies, so the local release's offline refresh is unaffected. node-proof now falls back to an online refresh on the node (read-only proof, fetching is fine). Run 4 (job-1792971115880-renameproof-4): lock refresh ok online, 234 crates regenerating, watcher re-armed.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_28 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 28u32, "2026-10-26T00:14Z", "eoie", "20:13 eoie: combined release (SLATE content plus respell plus rename) is ready to launch at the end of the tibia live block. (1) Node proof run 4 regenerated all 234 crates but cargo check failed: kit-rename skipped .rs, and src/rust_std_fs/windows.rs is a hand-written module importing eoie_rust_std_fs_mutation. kit-rename now also covers .rs, .c, .h and Cargo.lock (scan: 120 non-generated .rs plus 7 .c files carry the name; no absolute root path anywhere in src); run 5 started on the node. (2) notebook-check set only EOIE_BIN_UNDER_TEST, so every renamed notebook would have looked for kit on PATH; it now sets KIT_BIN_UNDER_TEST too and picks eoie.exe or kit.exe for its window wrapper. (3) SLATE's 7 notebook failures triaged: all pre-existing (same tests fail on the installed binary). Test-only fixes in the tree: planning_cli (inl rec joined unrolled a runtime tail from tab_split; now one unit-joined runtime arg), next_cli (fixture tokens Load Light/Load Heavy), wait_cli (u64_text instead of i32 decimal). launch.spi dropped from the list (a module, not a notebook). Product gaps stay as tracked known failures: stop_cli chain (EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN), notes_cli show and note-edit arity (EOIE-SHOW-NOTE-FILTER, EOIE-ONE-CALL), table_cli quoted CSV (EOIE-EXPERIMENTS). (4) The release's notebook gate passes only when every failing test is in the known-failures table (leaf name plus test, so it survives the rename); gate tests 6/6. (5) launch-RENAME no longer needs a separate SLATE install: it checks SLATE's log for regen, debug build, byte identity and release-wait, and for -Fast that no crate module source is newer than the SLATE regen (1346 inputs, 0 newer; control flags lint.spi).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_29 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 29u32, "2026-10-26T00:30Z", "eoie", "20:30 eoie: five more release breakers found and fixed before launch. (1) Node run 5: respell splits long macro strings with a backslash continuation inside identifiers (render.spi eoi\\ e_agile_records), so the line-based rename missed eoie_agile_records:: (SPIRAL-FORMATTER note 84). Rename now runs before respell on the node and in launch-RENAME, then asserts zero old-name tokens in .spi. (2) Run 6: respell-repo listed tracked paths that the rename had moved (git ls-files keeps them); it now skips listed paths missing on disk (i574n.github/scripts/respell-repo.ps1). Run 7 started. (3) The RENAME job called Publish-EoieBinary after dot-sourcing the renamed workspace.ps1 (now Publish-KitBinary) and passed -Eoie to repo test scripts that take -Kit, so it would have built kit.exe and failed at install. make-RENAME now renames those and keeps -Eoie only for scratch scripts; job-script-check gained a callee-root map, so the launcher checks the job against a renamed copy of the repo scripts before -Go and against the live tree after the rename. (4) release-wait-check set only EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME, so the renamed candidate would have used the real kit windows folder; it sets both now. (5) kit-state-move -SetAsideStray moves a %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit left by candidate runs aside (refuses if it holds a window or lease; tests 11/11). The post-install checks are generated renamed (15 scripts, backlog IDs and --team eoie protected) and run after finalize.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_30 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 30u32, "2026-10-26T00:51Z", "eoie", "20:51 eoie: node proof run 7 DONE (fresh mirror; standards fix, rename 980 files and 149 package folders, respell 843 files, 0 old-name tokens left in .spi, regen exit 0 with 234 ok, 260-row manifest). Compared with this box's published .rs with the rename applied as text: 260 vs 260, 0 differing outside kit_standards. FAST path eligible; the manifest is installed as the release's rename-manifest (sha 4860643895DBF276), so launch-RENAME -Fast skips the local 234-crate regen and regenerates only kit_standards. Launch waits for the tibia live block (about 25 min left) and the queued notebook checks.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_31 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 31u32, "2026-10-26T01:59Z", "eoie", "21:59 eoie: launched at 21:25 as announced. Steps: snapshot 20261025-212529-before-release-RENAME.zip (1485 files); standards fix 3 replacements; rename 987 files, 149 package folders; respell 843 files with 0 errors and 0 round-trip differences; 0 old-name tokens in .spi; 234 kit crates; 14.9 GB of unreachable build artifacts dropped (C: 50 GB free); job script check ok on the renamed tree. Release job job-1792979918697-kit-release-FASTRENAME queued for a heavy slot (8 h timeout), watched. Restore path if it fails: the snapshot plus robocopy from the respell originals. main and tibia have acked the state-move handshake (pause after publish, safe-point stops, then rename finalized).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_32 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 32u32, "2026-10-26T04:28Z", "eoie", "00:28 eoie: FASTRENAME (job-1792979918697) stopped unpublished at the notebook gate with exactly one unlisted failure. The fast path itself held: 259 generated files matched the node manifest, kit_standards regen ok, debug build ok, host probe ok, byte identity 8/8, release-wait PASS, notebooks 49/53 with the 4 known failures accepted. The failure, kit_adoption classify (commands_split_into_kit_habit_and_other_calls), was a rename bug no text pass sees: host.spi kit_named sliced &text[at + 4..], the length of the old name. Fixed to slice by the match's own length (found.len()), which also survives the next rename; a scan for other name-length offsets found no other site. Relaunched as RESUME (job-1792988868344): FAST job with kit_adoption added to the intended crates; snapshot 20261026-002602-before-release-RESUME. In parallel on the worker node: OpenSSL provisioned (scoop openssl, OPENSSL_LIB_DIR/INCLUDE_DIR in User env, as here), spiral.exe rebuild for the Cells rust runner, and the same candidate (fix, kit_adoption regen, debug build) running all 53 release notebooks with this box's compiler (core CDD68DC3EE829A67).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_33 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 33u32, "2026-10-26T07:09Z", "eoie", "03:09 eoie: independent node verdict on the RESUME candidate (renamed and respelled tree plus the kit_named length fix; kit_adoption regenerated; debug build; this box's compiler, core CDD68DC3EE829A67; SPIRAL_LIB from a synced spiral mirror; x's restored runner). Strict rules: positive control kit_lcov executed 2, passed 2, unsupported 0; a notebook passes only if executed is above 0, passed equals executed and unsupported is 0. Result: 50/53 pass. The 3 failing notebooks fail only tests in the release's known-failures table (stop_cli chain; notes_cli show and note-edit; table_cli quoted CSV), so the candidate meets the gate rule, and kit_adoption classify now passes. The node ran all 53 in 29 min (02:39-03:08) while the local release, on a saturated box, had done about 20 in 82 min. Known-failures table extended at 02:5x with the other three stop_cli tests that fail on the installed binary (timing flakes under load; EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN note 5).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_34 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 34u32, "2026-10-26T08:40Z", "eoie", "04:40 eoie: an environment trap the rename would have hit, found and fixed before finalize. This box's User env sets EOIE_LEASE_POLICY=renewing, and kit.exe reads only KIT_LEASE_POLICY, so after the state move every lane's tree leases would silently fall back to the default (non-renewing) policy. finalize-RENAME.ps1 gained step 3b: each User-scope EOIE_* variable gets its KIT_* twin with the same value (an existing KIT_* is left alone and reported), the EOIE_* original stays until nothing reads it. No other EOIE_* or KIT_* variable is set at Process, User or Machine scope, and settings.json sets none. Also new in post-install: three live checks verified first on the node candidate (agile next --fits, job chain --serial 3/3, job start flag order 5/5), plus a supervisor console probe (0 conhosts).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_35 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 35u32, "2026-10-26T08:59Z", "eoie", "04:59 eoie: RESUME was stopped before its test step because kit-dev --test runs 111 notebooks with no known-failure allowance (the gate step reads known-failures.txt over 53). The four node-proven fixes are now in the tree: show/note-edit route arity, quoted CSV in table query, deterministic stop_cli waits, harness word_after split on any whitespace (snapshot 20261026-044721-before-gate-fixes). Preflight running on the worker node: all 111 test-phase notebooks against a debug candidate with kit_agile_mutation and kit_table_query regenerated, strict verdict (job-1793004889974-kit-test-phase-preflight). The relaunch is FOLLOWUP (scratch clean\\respell\\launch-FOLLOWUP.ps1): fast path with intended crates kit_adoption, kit_agile_mutation, kit_standards, kit_table_query; it refuses unless the node preflight summary shows 111/111 pass. The manifest dry run on this box: 256 generated files checked, 0 mismatches.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_36 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 36u32, "2026-10-26T09:38Z", "eoie", "05:38 eoie: regen lease leak drafted and proven (the leak made kit-state-move refuse on the stray kit folder). compiler-contracts\\test-regeneration.ps1 -Publish writes tree-leases\\regeneration-<pid>.lease and never removes it, so every regen leaves a dead-pid lease behind. Draft kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\regen-lease-release.do (1 file, 2 replacements): the lease path goes into publishLease, a script-level trap removes it on any terminating error and rethrows (break), and the success path removes it after the final message. Probe first: a trap declared inside the if (\\) block never fires (failing run, lease left); at script level it fires for errors thrown inside functions too. 4 cases: publish ok, publish failing, failing without publish, plain, give 0 leases left, exit codes unchanged and the error text kept. Applied to a copy: ok, parse 0 errors. Wired into land-post-rename.ps1 as a repo-root program. That script also dropped the four fixes already landed before FOLLOWUP (it would have stopped on their anchors), and its src walk now skips target up front (it walked the whole build tree). Post-rename dry run on a fresh copy of today's tree: list-planning-index ok 1/6; Cons sugar 113 sites in 19 files, the 4 left being WorkstreamCons and OwnerAbsentCons, a probe GADT's own constructors, correctly untouched.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_37 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 37u32, "2026-10-26T12:42Z", "eoie", "08:42 eoie: preflight 2 (all 111 test-phase notebooks, the four fixes, tree asserted 1207 B92853CE28A241F6): 108/111. A control with the CSV fix reverted failed the same 3, so none came from the fixes. All three resolved: (1) kit_cli windows_cli handoff: 'handoff sections projection rejected typed state'. The rename's respell pass had rewritten all 63 state/*.spi files to edition-3 syntax, and the handoff renderer reads handoff, ratings, authority_census, toolchain_identity, cold_proof and agile state through text projections that parse the old syntax; installed eoie failed the same way on this checkout. Proven before restoring: a string-aware normalizer (string joins, keyword map outside literals, booleans, wrapping parens) shows all 63 differ from HEAD only in syntax, so no record is lost. All 63 restored to the committed bytes (snapshot 20261026-075946-before-state-unrespell); agile handoff exits 0 again. (2) kit_table_test where: a half-landed empty-cell rule (release-train S2) flipped table test only; reverted for the release with tibia's agreement (EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS carries the one-rule follow-up; snapshot 20261026-083258-before-table-revert). (3) kit_log_sequences: Cells gives untargeted cells the zig default (since 10-17), and codegenZig has no Export case at all (compiler lane's root cause), so a package-entry cell with an export fails. Unblocked with SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET=rust in the FOLLOWUP job's environment (kit ships Rust; compiler lane endorsed it and adds Zig Export in 5n). Preflight 3 running with all of this: job-1793018587285-kit-preflight3, tree 1207 E9545E46E6EF3E4F, default target rust.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_38 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 38u32, "2026-10-26T13:41Z", "eoie", "09:41 eoie: checked that the release-level SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET=rust reaches kit-dev --test's notebooks: kit_dev_spiral_tests/notebook.spi run_batch builds the Cells command with explicit env() and env_remove(SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER) but no env_clear, so the job's variable is inherited. No kit source change for it: the permanent fix is the compiler lane's codegenZig Export (pack 5n, BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS note 41), after which Cells' zig default works and the variable is dropped. Rule until then: every release job generator (make-FOLLOWUP and successors) injects the variable, and the node preflight passes -DefaultTarget rust. Editing run_batch's legacy host block for it would mean migrating the whole function under the style contract, out of proportion for a stopgap.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_39 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 39u32, "2026-10-26T14:50Z", "eoie", "10:50 eoie: preflight 3 at 81/111 had one failure, kit_cli windows_cli again, now one projection later: 'cold proof projection rejected typed state'. Cause: the rename changed handoff_public_binary in handoff_evidence_projection_domain to accept kit/kit.exe, but state/cold_proof.spi (data the rename left alone) names the public binary eoie.exe in its binary_gate receipt. Fixed in the data, not the code: an eoie literal in src would make the FAST path's rename map rewrite that crate's generated Rust and force it into the regenerated set, so the proven digest would change. The rename treats eoie to kit as one product renamed, so the receipt's label changes and its hash stays as recorded. Probe on the node first (candidate kit.exe, a temp copy of the mirror's state): unchanged state exits 2 with that error; with only the binary_gate label relabelled, agile handoff exits 0 and renders 87 lines including cold_proof=closed replay_flags=15 binary_sha256=e434e644.... Applied to the live state (snapshot 20261026-104427-before-cold-proof-label; src digest unchanged). Side effect until kit.exe installs: the installed eoie.exe's handoff rejects the new label. Plan: when preflight 3 ends, rerun windows_cli alone on the same mirror with the relabel, and release-go.ps1 -Rerun replaces that one row before the launcher's 111/111 check.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_40 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 40u32, "2026-10-26T15:00Z", "eoie", "11:00 eoie: swept the other state readers for the same renamed-literal against unrenamed-data mismatch. 89 literal sites like kit/kit.exe in src; only 2 state lines still name the old binary: (1) state/evidence.spi:47 EvidenceRef ('eoie.exe', e434e644..., 8424960u64, BinaryMedia), the file evidence for the old binary, read by evidence_currentness_domain's closeout check, which also requires a root binary named kit or kit.exe (terminal.spi 265-268). After install, eoie.exe is replaced, so that check will truthfully report the evidence stale. It is not in the release test phase; post-install step, set now: refresh evidence.spi's binary row for kit.exe with the closeout tool, never by hand-editing a hash. (2) state/binary_install.spi:5 names 'state/eoie.release.candidate' as an install source path, historical provenance with no reader matching it against a literal; it stays. The binary_gate relabel (cold_proof.spi) also satisfies evidence_currentness terminal.spi:142, which parses the same two-field line against kit/kit.exe.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_41 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 41u32, "2026-10-26T15:10Z", "eoie", "11:10 eoie: post-rename item, set with main (org .editorconfig adoption, user-approved direction 10-26): 12 tracked files don't conform. No final newline: spec/one-call.md, src/agile_task_query/Cargo.toml and 9 state files (bench, burnin_receipts, differential, legacy_catalog, migration, oracle, patches, pipeline, vendor; these are the files restored byte-for-byte to HEAD this morning, which never had one). CRLF: src/agile_task_views/Cargo.toml. Order: fix after finalize; for the state files, run agile handoff and the closeout check before and after to show a final newline changes no projection; then send main 'editorconfig ok', and main writes .editorconfig keyed on the new folder name.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_42 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 42u32, "2026-10-26T15:39Z", "eoie", "11:39 eoie: preflight 3 done, 108/111. (1) windows_cli: the cold-proof label, fixed in state; rerun with the relabel running (kit-rerun-windows). (2) kit_job_after_model: a Spiral cell timed out at 300 s (slowest test 256 s) on a node at 100% CPU, so load, not code; rerun alone queued. (3) kit_log_sequences: the zig default was not the whole story. With the rust default, the untargeted static test ':# sequences static' compiles the package entry as a plain rustc program: sequences.spi is the crate entry (main plus regex-using host code), so it fails on the missing regex crate and no main; under zig it failed on Export. Its one real test passes both times (1/1). Fix: target that static cell with '///> _' (Cells' skip target), one directive line (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\sequences-static-skip.do). It changes the proven digest, so the proof is a serial node job (kit-seqfix-runner): a codegen-neutral regen of kit_log_sequences, a rerun of sequences.spi with the fix, and the kit_job_after_model rerun. A global SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET=_ was rejected: it would skip every untargeted test cell, and 108 notebooks pass partly through such cells. Launch rule, unchanged in spirit: 111/111 after the rerun rows replace theirs, the tree digest updated only by the proven skip line.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_43 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 43u32, "2026-10-26T16:36Z", "eoie", "12:36 eoie: release coordination with main, agreed. (1) Heavy slots: both held by main's proofs, and the heavy queue is FIFO by ticket. Main will stop and requeue its not-yet-started heavy jobs (upstream-post-2-proof-3, perf-flip-rerun-2, blog-list-sugar-proof-3, plus its serial chains' gate steps) behind kit-release-FOLLOWUP once kit sends the job id, so FOLLOWUP takes the first slot that frees. (2) Main's 5n compiler install (review passed, DISAGREE 0) waits until FOLLOWUP's install and finalize are done (option b): the release's proof (preflight, three reruns, FAST manifest) was all generated with core CDD68DC3, and FOLLOWUP regenerates its intended crates with the installed compiler, so a swap first would ship output nothing proved. Rerun status: windows_cli pass (cold-proof label), sequences.spi pass (static skip, codegen-neutral 0/260), kit_job_after_model rerun running. Launch when it is green: promote expected-sources to 1207 C9520BC87568C658, then release-go -Rerun rerun-windows,rerun-seq,rerun-jobafter -Go.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_44 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 44u32, "2026-10-26T16:51Z", "kit", "12:51 kit: preflight 111/111. kit_job_after_model passed when rerun alone (rerun-jobafter, 1/1), windows_cli and sequences passed earlier. The tree is 1207 C9520BC87568C658, which is the proven E9545E46 plus the codegen-neutral '///> _' line. Queue-jump check before launch: main suggested a pre-made early ticket passed in SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET. That would deadlock the box. For a --heavy job, the supervisor runs job_window_gate first (kit_job_gate/gate.spi:173 -> kit_job_slot slot_gate, same in the installed eoie_job_slot). That gate always makes its own ticket from the current ticks and overwrites SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET, so a pre-made live ticket would sit first in line, never claimed, and every heavy job would wait behind it. Rule used instead: FOLLOWUP is a plain job whose heavy-wrapper.ps1 first waits while a live-gui or compiler-install window is held. Then it creates 00639286151548004428-<its own pid>.t (live only while the wrapper lives; Invoke-Heavy deletes it once the slot is taken) and enters Invoke-Heavy through heavy.ps1's own adoption path (heavy.ps1:133-143), MaxMinutes 481. The job timeout is 960 min, because the run cap is the Invoke-Heavy watchdog and the queue wait counts against the job. A gap to fix in kit: job start --heavy has no way to place a job early in the queue (a --priority-ticket option, or the slot gate adopting an existing SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET it owns).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_45 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 45u32, "2026-10-26T17:35Z", "kit", "13:35 kit: FOLLOWUP (job-1793034001350) is waiting first in line. Its wrapper started 2 min after job start, a slow supervisor under load, and made its ticket at 13:00:06. Main's nil-literal-2 had taken the freed slot 2 at 12:58:23, legitimately, before the ticket existed. The pause handshake was sharpened and sent to every lane ahead of time. kit-state-move refuses while any 'eoie|kit job supervise' process is alive, running or queued, or while a window file or tree lease exists, so 'clear' means a lane has no running or queued eoie jobs; holding new starts is not enough. Pause text: scratch clean\\respell\\pause-message.txt. Also: lint3 (the lint-wrapper measurement) was void. Its mirror sync failed ('being used by another process') and the mirror kept pre-rename eoie_ folders, so the applier stopped at a missing kit_lint_macros/lint.spi; the job also had no -ExpectSources. It was requeued as job-1793035682149-kit-lint-wrappers2 on mirror lint4 (digest checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658, -ExpectSources set) after kit-string-lib.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_46 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 46u32, "2026-10-26T18:03Z", "kit", "14:03 kit: FOLLOWUP took heavy slot 2 at 14:00:21. Its FAST rename stage checked 256 generated files against the node manifest (0 mismatches). The four queued post-rename node proofs, kit-arith-literals, kit-neutral-global2, kit-neutral-stdstr and kit-heal-e3, were stopped tail first so no follower was released. Their mirrors arith2, stdstr1 and heal4 held 0.01 GB each: failed syncs, the same mode as the void lint3. Each would have stopped at -ExpectSources after hours in the queue. Re-synced all four mirrors; they go back as ONE serial runner (scratch clean\\iggy-m2\\requeue-steps.json, same appliers, programs and crate sets), each step guarded by the release tree's digest 1207 C9520BC87568C658 instead of the pre-skip E9545E46. Rule for every node proof from now on: -ExpectSources is mandatory, and a mirror is checked by digest right after its sync.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_47 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 47u32, "2026-10-26T19:12Z", "kit", "15:12 kit: FOLLOWUP run 1 (job-1793034001350) was stopped and replaced, with the slot handed over directly. Run 1 recorded 'handoff parity FAIL: C:\\home\\git\\eoie (exit 2 vs 0)', which adds read-parity to its blocked list (script line 137), so it could only end 'release not published' after hours of tests. Cause: the check runs the OLD eoie.exe and the new kit.exe on the same state, and state/cold_proof.spi's binary_gate label was relabelled to kit.exe this morning (needed by the new projection). The old binary rejects that label ('cold proof projection rejected typed state'), so the two can never agree on the live state. The new binary was right. With the label reverted on a state copy for the old binary only, the outputs agree: 87 lines each, both exit 0. The job's Renamed transform was also too broad for a line-joined comparison: it rewrote EOIE inside backlog titles that both binaries print verbatim. Fix (scratch clean\\respell\\handoff-parity-release.ps1, called from make-FOLLOWUP in place of the loop): the old binary runs on a state copy with the pre-rename label, and the comparison is per line, each line equal as printed or after renaming. A negative control (old binary standing in as new) fails: 'exit 0 vs 2; line count 87 vs 1'. Handover: run 2 (job-1793041629210, ticket 00639286151548004428-9244.t) started first and queued at the head, then run 1 was stopped; run 2 took slot 2 at 15:10:42. Run 1's lease went with its stop, and the 256 generated files it wrote are rewritten identically by run 2.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_48 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 48u32, "2026-10-26T20:24Z", "kit", "16:24 kit: node proof results and a runner bug. (1) The serial runner did not run: 'step : exit 1 after 0 min' then DONE, twice. Cause: PowerShell names are case-insensitive. My mirror-pruning edit added '$steps = @($plan)', which assigned into the [string] $Steps parameter, so the plan was flattened to one string and the loop saw a single step with no label. Fixed by renaming the local to $planSteps; a local dry run with a stand-in publisher ran both test steps in order. (2) Arith (arith1, 107/111) and string-lib (strlib1, 107/111) each failed 4 notebooks. table_cli csv_tables_keep_quoted_commas, table_test where_comparisons (expected n=a 2, b 2, got n=a 1) and notes_cli 'agile show ... received 4 operands' fail in BOTH proofs, including the one that regenerated neither crate. So they come from the committed table_query.rs and agile_mutation.rs lagging their sources, not from the slices. preflight3, which regenerated those crates, passed them, and FOLLOWUP regenerates them too. windows_cli and sequences in arith1 are the known pre-fix failures of its older E9545E46 mirror. ps_cli kill_with_mine ('outside this agent session') is node-ancestry flakiness: it passed in arith1 and preflight3. (3) Every mirror was re-synced after FOLLOWUP's 14:00 regen: arith2, global2, stdstr1, heal4, arith3 and strlib2 all show 1207 C9520BC87568C658 and the live table_query.rs hash B6DBE935438C89A3. Targeted reruns kit-arith-2 and kit-string-lib-2 (only their 4 failed notebooks, same regen sets) lead the 10-step plan, job-1793046242770-kit-requeue3-runner, followed by arith-literals, global2, stdstr, heal-e3, expect-lib, path-predicates, argument-counts and union-arm-a. Marker rule (scratch clean\\respell\\mark-proven.ps1, checked positive on union-b and negative on reg1): digest, regen 0, every generated file within 1,000 lines, debug build 0, all listed notebooks pass, DONE.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_49 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 49u32, "2026-10-26T21:30Z", "kit", "17:30 kit: FOLLOWUP run 2 passed every gate so far: byte identity (8 verbs), release-wait, candidate notebooks 53/53 with 0 known failures used, and every grep/read/lines parity plus both handoff roots under the fixed comparison (87 lines each equal as printed or after renaming; src exit 2 on both). Now in the spiral tests step. The known-failures list (scratch clean\\notebook-fixes\\known-failures.txt, 7 rows: 4 stop_cli EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN, 2 notes_cli, 1 table_cli quoted CSV) is pruned to empty, because each listed notebook passes outright in run 2's candidate check (prune-known-failures.ps1; the old list is kept as .before-FOLLOWUP). A future release with any of these failing now blocks. Composition check before landing (compose-slices.ps1 on a copy, every slice in land-post-rename's order, markers ignored): all 17 steps apply in sequence. One real defect was found and fixed: apply-heal-e3 looked for heal-crate-e3.ps1 only beside itself, so it refused locally on any tree. It now also finds the scratch-root copy, and it heals 6 packages both alone and after the 12 slices before it. The node copy was unaffected, since the scripts there are flat.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_50 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 50u32, "2026-10-27T00:29Z", "kit", "20:29 kit: FOLLOWUP PUBLISHED. Run 2 (job-1793041629210) passed every gate: byte identity 8 verbs, release-wait, candidate notebooks 53/53 with 0 known failures, all grep/read/lines/handoff parity, spiral tests exit 0 (cargo tests, then the kit-dev notebook batch, slow under load), one-call spec 36/49 green (34 required), read and ps 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, orphan wait 3/3. Release build took 27m45s. Publish-KitBinary installed C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\kit.exe at 20:26:42, sha256 C0F2D2F3C66BFD43E7B4B085DAE7C1AD1EEDC16064FB7B5430261836E40DD643. After install, the hidden-window, orphan-wait and one-call checks against the installed binary passed, and 'kit agile list' over this backlog shows 812 items. 'pause' went to all five lanes at 20:27; x answered clear. A finalize dry run refuses as designed (21 supervisors, 2 window files, 1 lease) until every lane is clear.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_51 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 51u32, "2026-10-27T00:52Z", "kit", "20:52 kit: RENAME FINALIZED. All five lanes cleared after 'pause' (main stopped 10 jobs, tibia 9, fiscal's train3 finished landing first, x and compiler held nothing; compiler stopped its old node-watch). finalize-RENAME itself misfired: '$applyArg = if ($Apply) { @(''-Apply'') } else { @() }' unrolls to a string, and splatting a string passes its characters, so the state move got '-' as its LocalAppData and the path stages got 'l' (fixed: '$applyArg = @(if ($Apply) { ''-Apply'' })'). The steps were then run directly. kit-state-move set aside a stray kit folder left by candidate runs, stopped the idle job host 7588 (no supervisor alive; Windows would not move a folder holding a running exe), retired eoie.exe as eoie.exe.retired-20261026203700, and moved %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie to kit with all 2,357 job records. It now counts only *.window files, so the permanent events.log no longer blocks it. kit-paths Release (9 replacements) and State (1) applied, and a re-dry shows 0 pending. The finalize smoke passes: kit job list reads the moved state, autonomous-gate names kit.exe, machine-watch shows 0 eoie jobs. post-install: binary match True, extra 11/11, next --fits 2/2, chain --serial 3/3, flag order 5/5, queued-no-process 2/2, CLEAN 41/45. Open: CLEAN's 'one-call spec holds' x3 (36/49 green; the release gate needs 34, CLEAN wants more) and 'agile reads formatted state' (byte identity 2 of 8 on the installed binary, 8/8 in the release; peers write to the backlog continuously, so check on a frozen copy). Lesson: kit.exe reads only KIT_*, and User variables set by the finalize do not reach older processes; the first post-install run failed at the lease guard until KIT_LEASE_POLICY was set in the child. 'rename finalized' went to all lanes at 20:52 with that caveat.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_52 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 52u32, "2026-10-27T08:36Z", "kit", "04:35 kit: the post-rename slices landed 03:25-03:27 (10 node-proven slices plus heal-expect, cons sugar, the regen lease, and the HostFree/LibReferenced contract in 13 packages; standards findings=0; backup scratch respell\\pre-land-backup-0325). editorconfig-conform applied 12 fixes with handoff output identical. The repo folder moved C:/home/git/eoie -> C:/home/git/kit at 04:30:52 at the user's request (relayed by the compiler lane): an atomic rename after the last holders (other lanes' kit.exe job wait processes, stopped by their owners) exited. C:/home/git/eoie is a junction to the new folder. kit-paths -Stage Move rewrote 18 references (2 hooks, machine-watch, 10 memories). Backlog harvest roots now name C:/home/git/kit. Smoke: job list, agile list through the junction, and git toplevel all ok. Remaining: the SLICES release (188-crate regen, 54 candidate notebooks including tibia's table layer) requeued from the new path. The junction comes out once a grep finds no home/git/eoie caller (i574n.github scripts job-stop, live-wait, pause-plan, pause-requeue and watch-ids still name it).")
inl note_of_kit_rename_53 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 53u32, "2026-10-27T13:29Z", "kit", "09:3x kit: node preflight of the SLICES tree (kit-slices2-preflight, mirror slices2, digest 1223 5D1774A26152ED00 asserted, compiler 4FD712E9 pinned, fresh lib mirror slicelib): the 54-crate regen exit 0 with every generated file under 1000 (kit_table_query 708, kit_table_plain 769, kit_experiment_analysis 992, kit_table_measures 921); debug build exit 0; 53/54 of the release's candidate notebooks pass, including tibia's table_cli and table_test_cli. One unlisted failure: kit_process_table/ps_cli.spi kill_with_mine_refuses_another_authors_job_unless_it_is_named. It expected the refusal 'belongs to job ... owned by alpha; pass --theirs' and got 'no kit job started it and it is outside this agent session', so the candidate did not find the job's record at all. Cause unproven. It passed in the published FOLLOWUP release on the review box, and the node runs jobs under its own state and an ssh process tree, so an environment difference is plausible; the local release, which runs the same notebook, decides. If it fails there too, it is a real regression from the landed slices (kit_process_kill and kit_jobs are in the regen closure) and I bisect it with the slice proofs. Mirror slices2 removed after reading.")
inl note_of_kit_rename_54 () = Note ("KIT-RENAME", 54u32, "2026-10-27T14:16Z", "kit", "10:3x kit: kit-release-SLICES will not publish, through no fault of the candidate. Its first candidate notebook, kit_process_table/ps_cli.spi, ran at about 09:12 and returned TYPE-ERROR inside the lib (schematron.spi:80 read_schematron, single_root). The cause, confirmed by main and fiscal: fiscal's direct live write to lib/spiral/xml.spi at 09:15:40 added a helper named single_root that shadowed xml.single_root, and it was removed at about 09:25; the write bypassed the land reader guard while the release's Cells processes were reading the live lib. Recheck at 10:2x, ps_cli.spi against the current lib with the release's own debug candidate: 2/2 pass, kill_with_mine_refuses_another_authors_job_unless_it_is_named included. So the node preflight's runtime failure of that same test points to the node environment, not the code. The other release gates passed: debug build, host probe, agile byte identity, release-wait, and 40 of the other notebooks so far, tibia's table_cli and table_test_cli included. The rerun is SLICES2 (release-build-job-SLICES2.ps1): it skips the 54-crate regen soundly, because module-freshness shows 0 of 1,351 crate inputs newer than the 08:58:28 regen. It starts when SLICES ends, in an announced window; main has every lane holding live-lib writes during it.")
inl eoie_no_claude_names () = Task ("EOIE-NO-CLAUDE-NAMES", "eoie source mentions claude: make the tool truly generic with the same functionality, coordinated with every session that relies on those names (user 2026-10-24 06:57 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,generic,drift", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_no_claude_names () = Planning ("EOIE-NO-CLAUDE-NAMES", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_no_claude_names_1 () = Note ("EOIE-NO-CLAUDE-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: rides KIT-RENAME's census (step 1 lists every 'claude' token with its consumer: hooks, the jobs-home owner detection that looks for claude.exe as owner, and docs). Rule: a name becomes a generic role ('agent session', owner = nearest non-shell ancestor) unless a test proves behavior depends on it; consumers in other sessions are told before the change lands.")
inl eoie_registry_giant_line () = Task ("EOIE-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", "eoie_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi holds a giant line: run the formatter on eoie, and fix the generator that writes code like this (user 2026-10-24 06:57 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,formatter,generated", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_registry_giant_line () = Planning ("EOIE-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", Minutes 90u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_registry_giant_line_1 () = Note ("EOIE-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: tonight's next-train stage S5 (contracts slice D) already edits eoie_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi (contract_registered () plus a test), so the giant-line fix goes into that same change: re-lay the nested SpiralTestNotebook chain one entry per line, or better make it a list of records so adding a notebook stops needing a paren at the end. First step: find what writes registry.spi (a generator or hand edits by slices; tonight's slices A-D append entries by string replace, which explains the shape).")
inl kit_standards_spi () = Task ("KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", "eoie/kit standards.spi files: prove they are useful or fold them, before other projects copy the pattern (user 2026-10-24 06:57 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,kit,drift", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_standards_spi () = Planning ("KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl xsim_gleam_split_bench () = Task ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", "xsim frames Gleam split benchmark: can it use parallel compilation like Rust crates; which project (tibia, x, eoie) holds the SOTA model, and did the Gleam split use the nominal/macro tricks that carry types into generated code (user 2026-10-24 06:57 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "X-WIP-VERIFY", "x,gleam,parallel,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench () = Planning ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", Minutes 240u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_1 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:02Z", "x", "10-24 20:02 x: P2 -> P1, the next x work after the interim fix (user 20:0x). DECISION RULE, written before measuring. Candidates: (A) today's one package with post-hoc parts (baseline). (B) eoie-style granular packages: one Spiral entry + gleam.toml per domain module (prelude/glyphs base, protocol, splitter, events, invariants, personas, scenario, schedule, world, frames codec), with cross-package calls through exported nominal types (Gleam has the same need RUST-EXPORT-TYPES solved for Rust) and 'let' join points where inlining duplicates closures. A root gleam.toml with path deps plays the workspace. (C) B plus packages built concurrently. Measure on a quiet machine, 3 cold + 3 warm runs each: cold/warm build wall, a one-module touch rebuild (the incremental case packages should win), total generated lines, largest file, and sim -fast + corpus wall per schedule. Pick: the package layout (B or C) wins if every file is <= 1,000 lines with no post-processing, outputs are byte-identical (15 export hashes plus fixtures, the note-9 method), and the touch rebuild or cold wall beats A by >= 20%. If B/C only tie A on speed, still take B/C, since the user's stated goal is packages and the parts script is a known breakage point; record the numbers. A stays only if B can't be expressed without compiler work, and then that compiler gap is filed for main with this evidence. First CPU-light step: list which nominal types cross each proposed package seam (from the .spi opens) and check whether the Gleam backend emits pub named types for Export signatures, as Rust now does.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_2 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:07Z", "x", "10-24 20:07 x: TYPE-SEAM CENSUS (step 1, text only). Scope: the sim closure's 13 .spi files; a type counts as crossing when a module that opens its home mentions it. 82 types; 27 cross a module seam: 17 records, 5 unions, 2 nominals, 3 aliases. The hub is events (11 of the 27: unions event, revision, content, reaction; records rooted, social, sourced, delivery, frame_part), used by final_state, personas, scenario and schedule. Next are scenario (scenario, casting, flow), personas (union persona; acted, cast), protocol (nominals post_id, cell_id; post_ids), final_state (tally, cell_state, replay), exsss, and the facade asks (run_ask, world_ask, world, losses). How the Gleam backend emits types today, from apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/types.gleam: 59 pub types named by a per-program counter (Uh0.., Us0..), not after their Spiral source, and all 15 exports are String -> String. That's the state Rust was in before RUST-EXPORT-TYPES. Two packages would each mint their own Uh3 for 'event', so only text crosses a package boundary today. A cross-package call is a host call to the other package's exported fn, the way xc.spi calls xc_net::xc_requests. Proposed cut, from the open graph: base (prelude, glyphs, exsss, protocol), events (+ final_state), splitter, invariants, cast (personas, scenario, schedule), world, and the facade. Compiler need, for main: Gleam Export signatures carry the 5 unions and 2 nominals as pub types named after their Spiral source; records crossing a seam need named records too (17), or a fixed positional layout that every package agrees on. Until then, layout B can be measured only with text seams. Run it that way as B0, an honest lower bound that pays parse cost at each seam, and record the cost per seam.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_3 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "x", "10-24 20:09 x: RULE AMENDED before any measurement (main 20:1x; nothing measured yet, so the rule stays pre-registered). (1) Census correction: the 2 nominals (post_id, cell_id) are evaluated away before codegen and cross as their primitive, so the compiler seam is the 5 unions plus the crossing records. Records get stable names by Rust's exported_record_names rule, not a positional layout. (2) Compiler work is not a disqualifier. GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES (main, P1, 240m) is the bounded Gleam codegen half of RUST-EXPORT-TYPES, since unionSourceNames already exists for every backend. New rule: measure A (parts) and B0/C0 (packages with text seams). Packages are chosen if every file is <= 1,000 lines with no post-processing, outputs are byte-identical, and B0 or C0 is no more than 10% slower than A on cold build, touch rebuild and sim/corpus wall. B0 is the upper bound on the cost of the typed version, which drops the parse at each seam. If they win, GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES lands and B is re-measured typed to confirm it is <= B0. If B0/C0 is more than 10% slower, packages are still chosen when the typed version is projected to close the gap: B0 minus its measured seam-parse time must come within 10% of A. A survives only if even that projection loses.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_4 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: SCOPE CHANGE (user 20:1x). Packages vs parts is decided: packages, cut at codegen, and parts are drift. The note-1/3 rule's 'A survives' branch is void. A stays only as the baseline the numbers are reported against. The bench now picks the best package granularity and build technique (B, B finer per X-PARALLEL-CENSUS, C parallel, plus whatever the research ranks), by cold, touch and sim/corpus wall. Parent: X-PARALLEL-BUILD.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_5 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 5u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: research (X-PARALLEL-BUILD n2). Gleam 1.19 compiles packages sequentially, and BEAM-compiles one package's modules in parallel (gleam@@compile.erl worker pool). Candidates, rule unchanged otherwise: P = packages per domain unit (as noted earlier), M = one package whose modules map 1:1 to Spiral source modules, emitted at codegen (SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS), and A = the post-hoc parts baseline. Prediction (recorded so it can be wrong): M beats P on cold build, P only ties on touch rebuild. Measure both; packages are kept only where they win.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_6 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 6u32, "2026-10-25T00:31Z", "x", "10-24 20:31 x: RULE CORRECTED before any measurement; this note supersedes the n3 tie-break. n3 said 'packages win within 10% of A', which could pick the shape the user named while losing the parallelism they named; main flagged it at 20:3x, and gleam's compile_dependencies() is a plain sequential loop (main verified). The user's goal (10-21, 10-24) is splits at codegen that build in parallel. Packages were the means. New rule: among the codegen-level candidates (M: modules per Spiral source module in one package; P: packages per domain unit; P+M), the fastest on cold build plus touch rebuild wins, with byte-identical outputs and every file <= 1,000 lines. A within 5% counts as a tie, and a tie goes to fewer gleam.toml files (fewer sequential barriers and resolutions). A (post-hoc parts) is a baseline only. The user is told of this tension directly.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_7 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 7u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: all candidates are HAND-MODELED (user 20:3x). M = one package, the sim's slices authored as separate Spiral entries, each compiled to its own Gleam module and calling the others through exports. P = the same slices as path packages. No candidate is emitted by the compiler or a script. The n6 rule is otherwise unchanged; per the research, M is expected to win for Gleam.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_8 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 8u32, "2026-10-25T02:44Z", "x", "10-24 22:44 x: GLEAM PROBES MEASURED (job-1792896048419, scratch copy, live lib), one entry per hand unit with the event-stream text as the seam: g_split 1159 (defused 328), g_masked 524, g_rows (checked/commute/breaking) 2692, g_raw (cells/corpus -> stream) 2768, g_paced (stream -> deliveries) 3168, g_final (stream -> final state) 2373, g_world (stream -> world files) 4899. Findings: (1) text seams don't work for Gleam: each consumer re-decodes the stream, and the event codec alone (string_from 347, fields_from 279, decoded 164, encoded 280, without_controls 143) is over 1,100 lines, so no unit that reads the stream can be <= 1,000. (2) Gleam inflates single functions: defused is 200 lines in Rust and 328 in Gleam. (3) Codegen compaction (single-expression arms without braces, let-then-return inlined) is estimated at only ~15% (g_split 1159 -> ~1000, g_world 4899 -> ~4120). So M (one package, modules per source slice) needs TYPED CROSS-MODULE CALLS: the codec unit exports decode/encode with named types, and consumers import them, so shared code is emitted once instead of per unit. That's GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES plus a consumer half (an entry declares it uses another unit's exports, and codegen emits 'import xsim_events.{type Event, decoded}' instead of re-declaring). Until then scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 stays as the interim; it is the only post-hoc splitter left.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_9 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 9u32, "2026-10-25T05:09Z", "x", "10-25 01:09 x: re-measured the 7 Gleam probes on the installed pack 5j (18 codegen passes, dead helpers dropped): g_split 1143, g_rows 2676, g_masked 508, g_raw 2765, g_paced 3165, g_final 2367, g_world 4893, i.e. -6..-16 lines each. n8's conclusion stands: Gleam hand units need typed cross-module imports (GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES n6 design) before scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 can go.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_10 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 10u32, "2026-10-27T13:46Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:46 x: DESIGN for the package split, taken from today's Rust crate rounds (frames split, requests crate), CPU-free. simframes.spi already exports only primitive boundaries (strings, i32, bool, and tuples of them; structured data as text with char 0/1 separators), so each export group can become its own Gleam package with its own gleam.toml, and none needs GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES. Cut along the existing export groups:\n(1) xsim_split: split_cell, file_cells, thread_posts (splitter + glyphs + defuse + tlds; pure text).\n(2) xsim_checks: checked_text, commute_text, breaking_text, masked_text (invariants).\n(3) xsim_scenario: scenario_stream, scenario_final (scenario, personas, schedule, events, final_state).\n(4) xsim_world: world_files (world).\n(5) xsim_corpus: corpus_run, corpus_raw, cells_raw, rescheduled, break_source.\nThe 10-22 attempt failed at 2.2x because each coarse entry compiled the whole scenario/events/world closure into itself. Today's fix for the same problem in Rust: a package that needs another's code calls that package's export through a one-expression host call (Gleam: $'xsim_split.split_cell(!a, !b, !c)' plus a path dependency in gleam.toml, the shape of x's $'xc_chunks::xc_cell_chunks(...)'), so splitter code exists once, in xsim_split. Shared helpers inside a package stay :~ join points, not := (attribute-generated-lines memory). Order of work: (a) xsim_split first, since world and corpus call split_cell and it is the cleanest boundary; (b) measure lines per package with the pinned compiler (each <= 1000 and the total <= today's 11,469 x 1.1); (c) cold and warm gleam build per package, in parallel vs the single package. Pre-register (b) and (c)'s thresholds before any build; the build and measuring are the heavy part, this design is the light part.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_11 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 11u32, "2026-10-27T14:03Z", "x", "2026-10-27 10:03 x: design step 2 (still CPU-free), from reading the sim's build and call graph. Loading: Xsim.Spiral.call prepends every frames/build/dev/erlang/*/ebin, so a Gleam path dependency (xsim_split = { path = \"../split\" } in frames/gleam.toml) is built by frames' 'gleam build' into that folder and loads automatically; Xsim.Spiral.call gains a module argument (split_cell, file_cells and thread_posts -> :xsim_split). Where splitter code enters xsim_frames: exactly two points, (1) schedule.spi parts_of (via framed_post, the scenario/corpus flow) and (2) world.spi chunks_for (posted_cell and revised_with). Both call splitter's split_cell directly, so moving the exports alone would leave a second full copy of the splitter inside xsim_frames and break the total-lines bound. Fix: thread a chunker : cell_id -> i32 -> string -> list string parameter from the export wrappers in simframes.spi down to those two points. simframes passes a one-expression host call into xsim_split; the notebook tests pass splitter's split_cell (the spiral-macro-gotchas pattern: a module takes the cross-package function as a parameter, the entry passes the primitive, tests pass a local stand-in). A chunker FIELD in the world record would touch fewer functions, but world values are compared and printed in tests and fixtures, and a function field breaks both, so thread a parameter instead. RULE for the step (a+b) round, written before drafting: accept if, with the pinned compiler, (1) xsim_split's generated Gleam and every xsim_frames module are each <= 1000 lines after gleam-modules, (2) xsim_frames no longer contains defused_dots_by or split_units (grep), (3) total generated sim Gleam is <= 12,616 lines (today's 11,469 x 1.1), and (4) the sim build passes with fixtures byte-identical to the same tree's pre-split accept, mix test, and the notebooks green. Measure cold and warm gleam build of both packages only after (1)-(4) pass.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_12 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 12u32, "2026-10-27T14:23Z", "x", "2026-10-27 10:23 x: step (a+b) DRAFTED in the tree (unstaged), measured under note 11's rule. New Gleam package apps/sim/split (gleam.toml xsim_split, built from the new entry apps/sim/spi/xsim_split/xsim_split.spi, which exports split_cell and file_cells/thread_posts for Elixir plus split_cell_joined, the chunks as one text joined with char 0, for package-to-package use, since each Gleam package has its own list type). frames/gleam.toml gains xsim_split as a path dependency, sim build.ps1 compiles and module-splits xsim_split before frames, and the sim .gitignore covers /split/build/. The splitter is reached only through a chunker (cell_id -> i32 -> string -> list string, declared in splitter.spi): schedule threads it through parts_of, framed_post/edit, revised_in, exploded_one and exploded, and staging and run_ask carry a cut field; world threads it through chunks_for, posted_cell, revised_with, edited_cell, laid_down and materialized, and world_ask carries cut. simframes passes remote_split (one host call to xsim_split.split_cell_joined, split back on char 0) and no longer exports the splitter. The notebook tests (scenario_checks, sim_run, world's tests) pass splitter's split_cell. Xsim.Spiral.call takes a module, and Xsim.Frames calls :xsim_split. Every cell <= 272, lines <= 120. Quick node check: apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept (job-1793111039900, mirror xsplitpkg1).")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_13 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 13u32, "2026-10-27T15:02Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:02 x: step (a+b) MEASURED (quick node check job-1793112246184, sim build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) against note 11's pre-registered rule, which passes on all four clauses. (1) Every module <= 1000 lines: xsim_split splits into 6 modules (largest 960) and xsim_frames into 13 (largest 959). (2) No splitter functions in xsim_frames: 0 grep hits for defused_dots_by|split_units across frames/src. (3) Total sim Gleam <= 12,616: 12,437 file lines (frames 9,347 + split 3,090; the generated counts are 8,926 + 2,952 = 11,878). (4) Sim build green: ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, sim x-compat 0 in 24 files, and every fixture re-accepted. Three earlier checks failed on my own mistake, a second `:== chunker` alias in splitter.spi (the later declaration silently wins); renamed to cell_chunker. The rule closes here; what remains is landing, which goes through verify18 (job-1793113365827, the whole tree with the fixed accept order, sim before e2e), since the split changes the sim fixtures and generated Gleam the tutorial reads.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_14 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps replaced X-PARALLEL-CENSUS,GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES -> X-WIP-VERIFY. The split met its rule (note 13) without either; what is left is landing the generated Gleam from a green verify.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_15 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 15u32, "2026-10-28T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). The generated Gleam landed: frames 13 modules with 0 splitter functions, xsim_split 6 modules (staged from mirror xv20, since verify -Out lacked *.gleam, now fixed).")
inl eoie_job_chain_serial () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", "job chain --serial: one job runs the chain's links in order through job run (per-link ids and receipts kept), so a waiting chain holds 1 supervisor instead of N; the one-call form of the runner pattern every team used by hand on 10-24", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,jobs,processes", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_chain_serial () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl matrix_split_parts_memo () = Task ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", "Matrix compile memo replays only the main output: multiFile backends (Cpp + Cuda, Python + Cuda) write several files the memo neither stores nor keys; split parts were the first case found and go away with pack 5j", TaskKind, P1, D2, 90u32, Active, "", "compiler,matrix,tests,rust,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_matrix_split_parts_memo () = Planning ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", Minutes 60u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_1 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:52 main: fixed in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (Get-OutputParts: <stem>_*.<ext> plus <stem>.spiral-parts are stored under <memo>.parts, replayed with the main output, and hashed into the native memo key; parse-checked). Verification queued (main-serial-memo-parts: two -ReuseCompile runs of mega_brzozowski_derivatives on Rust -Native; round 2 must replay the compile and still run every row). Then the next accept run should turn the 5 Rust mega build-fails into ran/agree rows.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_2 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:46 groom: 70%. The uncommitted test.ps1 edit copies split parts in the native tier (found on the Gleam row, COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS), and in the pack 5i single-flight review the 5 Rust mega rows now run and agree. Left: confirm the native memo key covers main.spiral-parts (change one part, check a -ReuseNative rerun rebuilds), then Done.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_3 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:21Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main: the split-parts half becomes moot with pack 5j, which removes the output splits. The real remaining gap is multiFile outputs: the host writes several files for 'Cpp + Cuda' (.corelib.hpp, .hpp, .cpp, .cu) and 'Python + Cuda' (_auto.py, .py) (Program.fs:493-496). At HEAD, test.ps1's compile memo stores and replays only the main output and keys the native memo on it alone. My parts code (Get-OutputParts) caught the 451 main_auto.py files only by accident, through its '<stem>_*' pattern, and 5j reverts it. Rule: the memo stores, replays and keys exactly the host's multiFileSuffixes for the row's backend, nothing found by pattern. Done when a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte-identically and a changed main_auto.py changes the native key.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_4 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:55Z", "main", "10-25 03:55 main: drafted per n3's rule: %TEMP%\\matrix-multifile-memo\\apply.ps1 (3 anchors, 1 each). Get-OutputParts takes the row's CoreBackend and returns exactly the host's multiFileSuffixes (Program.fs:493: Cpp + Cuda .corelib.hpp/.hpp/.cpp/.cu, Python + Cuda _auto.py/.py) minus the main output, so nothing is found by pattern. The compile-memo store and Get-NativeMemoKey pass CoreBackend; the native tier's Gleam filter (line 912) is left as written, so the native-code signature, and with it every native memo entry, survive. Python keys don't change, since main_auto.py was already hashed. Probe: Python + Cuda -> main_auto.py only (a planted main_other.py is no longer picked), Rust -> none. A patched copy parses with 0 errors. Land: job main-land-multifile-memo after the 5k review (test.ps1 reader). Done-check left: a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte-identical.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_5 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 5u32, "2026-10-25T08:44Z", "main", "10-25 04:44 main: LANDED in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (job-1792914904841, after the 5k review released it; 8+/8-, uncommitted). It supersedes the 'test.ps1 parts revert' planned for the next pack, which is the same code. Done-check left, riding on the next matrix run: a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte for byte.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_6 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 6u32, "2026-10-25T09:15Z", "main", "10-25 05:15 main: done-check attempt on samples/typecheck_long_let_body (Python + Cuda), two runs of test.ps1 -Filter ... -Backend Python -ReuseCompile: single-flight-20261025-044648, then -045543 with main_auto.py deleted between them. INCONCLUSIVE, with two findings. (1) Run 2 replayed 0 and compiled again (same residual c9c54fc07ac29d57), so the memo key changed between the runs. The key covers every .spi under the bundle, lib and core dir, and other lanes keep editing the live tree, so a replay check needs a quiet tree or a pinned copy (WorkspaceRoot). (2) The fresh compile in run 2 rewrote main.py but did NOT recreate the deleted main_auto.py. Restored from git, hash E10B3C2C as before. The core writes both multi-file outputs at coreBase (Program.fs:537-541) only when it writes at all. A deleted sibling output not coming back suggests the core or server skips writes whose text it believes unchanged. Next: reproduce on a quiet tree (delete main_auto.py, compile once outside test.ps1 via probe.ps1 -Backend Python); if it doesn't come back, it's a host bug, filed under this item.")
inl eoie_agile_p0_weight () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", "agile next: a P0 weighs 16x a P1 instead of 4x, so a lone 480-min P0 scores at least a lone 30-min P1 (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW ask 1)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,scheduling", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: the P0 weight is the smallest power of two w with w*6000/480 >= 256*6000/30, which gives w = 4096 (16x P1). P1..P5 keep their 4x steps (256, 64, 16, 4, 1). Live check on the shared backlog, run before and after with agile next 20: accept only if every ready P0 of 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 that has no unblocks, and the order among same-priority items is unchanged. Change: priority_weight in agile_task_next/ranking.spi plus its test. The int overflow guard in rank_key already clamps to i32::MAX.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 2u32, "2026-10-27T22:04Z", "eoie", "Draft and preview against the 10-24 rule (2026-10-27 18:0x). The draft is scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\p0-weight.do: priority_weight becomes a pure Spiral match (P0 4096, P1 256, P2 64, P3 16, P4 4, otherwise 1; the host shift macro is gone). It adds a test for the ask itself (a lone 480-min P0 >= a lone 30-min P1), and the expected orders become GATE BIG-P0 SMALL-P2 KIT-SIDE in ranking.spi and GATE BIG OPS-SIDE SMALL in next_cli.spi. It applied 4/4 with kit do on a scratch package copy. Preview: p0-weight-sim.ps1 replays the ranking from agile list --json on the live backlog. It reproduces the shipped wsjf for 202 of 232 ready items, so it is a preview, not the binding check. The rule's binding check is still agile next 20 before and after on the real binary; the before snapshot is next-before-1810.json. The preview predicts the rule REJECTS the change on both clauses. Clause 1: 4 ready 480-min P0s (XSD-FACET-TYPES, XSD-DECODE-RESULT, FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE, BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP; key 51200-54400) rank under EOIE-DO-APPEND-PARENTS, a 20-min P1 (76800). The 10-24 weight derivation covered a 30-min P1, but the clause says every P1, and estimates go down to 15 min, or below that when set explicitly. Clause 2 can't hold for any P0 weight change: unblocks add the waiter's priority weight, so every item a P0 waits on gains weight and moves within its own priority (the preview shows P0 and P1 orders changing). Outcome by the rule: not adopted, and not queued for a release. I did not fish for a passing weight after seeing the preview. Blocker: the acceptance rule needs a revision written from the ask alone, before the next measurement. Possible shapes: clause 1 as the ask states it (480-min P0 vs 30-min P1, which holds by arithmetic at 4096); clause 2 limited to items with no P0 waiter. Then run that one revised rule against the binary once, and stop.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 3u32, "2026-10-28T01:34Z", "main", "10-27 21:34 main: Revised acceptance rule (main, owner of AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW; written 10-27 21:3x from the ask, before the binding run). Disclosure: kit's preview showed why the 10-24 rule can't pass, so this revision is not blind to that diagnosis. It narrows each clause back to what the ask states and does not tune any number to the data. Clause 1, the ask itself: on agile next with the real binary, every ready P0 with an estimate of 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 with an estimate of 30 min or more that unblocks nothing. P1s under 30 min are outside the ask, which compared a 480-min P0 with a 30-min P1. Clause 2, no collateral reordering: among ready items that no P0 waits on, directly or transitively, the relative order within each priority is the same before and after. Items a P0 waits on are exempt, since gaining the waiter's weight is the intended effect. Procedure: one run of agile next (full ready list, not top 20) before and after on the shipped binary. Adopt on both clauses passing; otherwise not adopted and closed as measured. No further weight or rule revision.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:38Z", "eoie", "21:3x kit: owner's revised rule (main, AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW) accepted as the binding rule. Clause 1: every ready P0 at 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 at 30 min or more that unblocks nothing. Clause 2: same-priority order is unchanged among items no P0 waits on, directly or transitively. One before/after run of the full ready list on the shipped binary; adopt if both pass, else close as measured, no further revision. The draft is in the live tree and rides TRAIN2. priority_weight is a :~ pure-Spiral match: as := it inlined into 2 sites (agile_task_next 983 lines), as :~ 961. The ranking.spi notebook passes 5/5 with the new expectations; next_cli.spi runs in TRAIN2's candidate notebooks. A/B setup: the pre-change installed binary is copied to scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\kit-before-p0.exe, so after TRAIN2 installs, both binaries run 'agile next <root> 1000 --json' on the same backlog state, and the two clauses are checked mechanically on those two outputs.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_p0_weight_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 5u32, "2026-10-28T09:29Z", "eoie", "05:2x kit: binding check by the owner's rule (note 4): CLOSED AS MEASURED, not adopted. It ran once, as the rule specifies. Both binaries ran 'agile next <root> 1000 --json' on the same backlog state: the saved pre-change binary (kit-before-p0.exe, C0F2D2F3) and the TRAIN2c binary that shipped the change (A5B55974). 218 ready items each; 72 items have a P0 waiting on them, directly or transitively. The check is scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\p0-binding-check.ps1, run on next-before.json, next-after.json and tasks-at-check.json. Clause 1 fails with 2 violations: FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE and BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP (P0, 480 min, ranks 27 and 28) sit below DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX (P1, 30 min, rank 25). The keys tie exactly (4096*6000/480 = 256*6000/30 = 51200), the tie keeps list order, and the clause asks for 'above'. Clause 2 fails for P0: among items no P0 waits on, the P0 order changed, because unblocks add to the weight, so (4096+u)/m and (1024+u)/m don't preserve order between P0s with different unblocks. As the rule says, no further revision. The change is reverted in the live tree with the inverse program p0-weight-revert.do: git diff of ranking.spi and next_cli.spi is now empty, so both match HEAD again. The revert ships with the next release's regen of agile_task_next. Until then, the installed binary carries the 16x P0 weight.")
inl eoie_agile_requested () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED", "agile: a structured requested field (who, when, quote id) on items, and a time-criticality term in agile next that grows with the age of an open user request (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW asks 6 and 1)", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", "eoie,agile,scheduling,schema", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_requested () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_requested_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: requested is a typed record (who: user|team word; at: UTC stamp; quote: note number), set with agile add/edit --requested and shown by agile show and list --json. The time-criticality term adds the item's own priority weight times min(days since requested, 7) / 7, so a week-old user request counts double and never more. An item with no requested record scores exactly as today. Migration: titles containing '(user YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM' get the field filled by a preview/apply verb in the migrate-tags shape. The title text stays.")
inl eoie_agile_cadence () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE", "agile: a cadence field (once|per-report|daily); standing items leave agile next and show in a checklist view instead (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW ask 3)", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,scheduling,schema", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_cadence () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_cadence_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: cadence is a union Once | PerReport | Daily, defaulting to Once, so every existing item is unchanged. agile next ranks only Once items. A new 'agile duties <root> [--team t]' lists PerReport and Daily items, with the stamp of each one's last note, and flags a Daily item with no note today. First users: LANE-GAUGE-DAILY (daily) and BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE (per-report). Ships with EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED in one schema slice, so the state crates change once.")
inl eoie_agile_stale_active () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", "agile: a stale-Active view, i.e. Active items per team with days since their last note, flagging more than 3 days without a note for a status decision (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW ask 4)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,queries", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_stale_active () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_stale_active_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: list gains --stale <days>, a query filter (one FlagStale case in agile_task_query) that keeps Active items whose newest note, or whose creation if there is none, is older than <days>. Rows add a 'quiet=<n>d' column. Shipped as the named view 'stale' = list Active --stale 3 --sort priority, with a one-call spec case. Acceptance: on the live backlog (201 Active) the view's count matches a recount over agile list --json plus the notes.")
inl eoie_agile_check_fields () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", "agile check warns on Planned or Active items missing estimate, load or assignee (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW ask 5, fill-all-task-fields)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,check", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_check_fields () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_check_fields_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: agile check prints one 'eoie agile check warn id=<ID> missing=estimate,load' line per open item and adds 'unfilled=<n>' to its ok line. The exit code stays 0, since a warning never fails check; --strict turns warnings into exit 1 for the Stop hook. Done items are never warned. Acceptance: the live count equals agile list --json filtered by hand (35 Planned without an estimate on 10-24 08:59).")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_check_fields_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Grooming rule for estimates, written before applying: an open non-Epic item with no estimate gets a provisional remaining estimate of base(D) x (1 - progress), floored at 30 and rounded up to 15 minutes. base: D1 30, D2 60, D3 120, D4 240, D5 480, D6 720, D7 1200, D8 1800. Epics stay unestimated because they roll up from their leaves (EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES). This is a placeholder for the milestone-based sizing in estimates-from-rules, so the next sizing pass replaces it. Each provisional estimate gets a note saying it is one.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_check_fields_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:44 Load rule, written before applying and following the existing eoie convention: an item is light when its own work is drafting plus scratch notebook checks and it reaches users through the shared release train, which carries the heavy load. It is medium when its own verification repeatedly runs commands or builds (bench). It is heavy only when it holds a heavy slot itself. Applied to 14 eoie items that had no load: EOIE-BENCH-VERB is medium, the rest are light.")
inl eoie_agile_lpt_leaves () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", "agile next --as subagent hands out leaf work only: Epics leave the LPT order, since an epic is a container (AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW ask 2)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,scheduling", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_lpt_leaves () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_lpt_leaves_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Ask 2 measured on 10-24 09:0x. Paused and Blocked are already excluded: agile_status_open is true only for Planned and Active, and TIBIA, the #1 row, is Active. The team filter already exists (--team main gives 126 ready instead of 218), so the view 'subagent-main' now stores it. What is left: epics top LPT by size (TIBIA 9600m, an Epic). Rule written before measuring: in the Subagent role, an Epic is not a candidate. In the MainAgent role nothing changes. Test: the sample backlog gains an Epic, and the subagent order skips it.")
inl convert_zig_unativeint () = Task ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", "lib convert: convert.convert / try_convert from an integer to convert.unativeint is an identity retag on Zig (x |> to |> Some), so the value stays i32 where Zig needs usize; numeric targets need Zig's own conversion in try_convert's Zig arm", TaskKind, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "lib,zig,convert,granular,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_convert_zig_unativeint () = Planning ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_1 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T18:32Z", "main", "Found 2026-10-24 14:1x by the xml heal probe (main.zig: expected type 'usize', found 'i32' at (v0)[v8] where v8 = convert.convert i). Workaround in the xml slice: index with conv to u64 (Zig coerces u64 to usize). Milestones: a Zig test of convert.convert i32 -> unativeint indexing a string 20 m; the Zig arm using the backend conversion for number targets 25 m; matrix/Cells check 15 m.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_2 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 2u32, "2026-10-25T15:07Z", "main", "10-25 11:07 main: cause read: convert.to is a bare retag ('$'!x '') on every backend except Rust, and numeric_conv handles only primitive sources, so unativeint (a :! nominal, usize on Zig) falls through to the retag in both directions, and Zig rejects the uncast assignment (seen again today in LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF gate 2). Slice %TEMP%\\lib-convert-zig-native\\apply.ps1 (dry run 1/1): try_convert's Zig arm computes native = t or u is unativeint (compile-time typecase) and emits '@intCast(x)' then; Zig takes the result type from the typed destination, and every other conversion is unchanged. Test a_native_index_converts_both_ways (rust, zig): i32 -> unativeint -> i32. Gate + land: job-1792940867731-main-serial-convert-zig (after the zig index_of chain).")
inl edition3_spiral_toml () = Task ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", "spiral.toml package manifest next to package.spiproj (edition 3 to the end, user 10-24 15:0x; SPIRAL3-EDITIONS note 10 picked spiral.toml): the compiler reads either file per directory, spiral.toml first; a mechanical converter migrates every non-core package; the upstream core keeps package.spiproj", Story, P0, D3, 45u32, Active, "EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION", "team:main,compiler,edition3,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_edition3_spiral_toml () = Planning ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", Minutes 240u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_1 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 1u32, "2026-10-24T20:57Z", "main", "main 17:0x design, from the parser at spiral_compiler.fs ~178521 (config: an FParsec record of name, version, moduleDir, modules, packageDir, packages, default-int, default-float, edition, crates). Schema: edition = 3; name = 'x'; version = '...'; moduleDir; packageDir = '../../../spiral/lib'; packages = ['|core-', 'spiral-'] (the spiproj spellings kept as strings, so | = compiler dir and a trailing - = include stay one rule); default-int, default-float; crates = [...]; modules = a TOML multi-line literal string holding the existing indentation hierarchy (dir/, -, *, *- flags), parsed by the same file_hierarchy parser, so module order and flags need no new encoding. Reader: a small TOML subset parser (key = string | int | string array | multi-line literal) that maps onto RawSchema, with ranges shifted to the toml line/column so diagnostics point into spiral.toml. Lookup: the 3 package.spiproj sites (~182587, ~183219 spiproj_suffix, ~183564) try spiral.toml first. Milestones: TOML subset reader + RawSchema mapping 90 m; lookup at the 3 sites + both-file conflict error 30 m; converter verb 'format --manifest toml <dirs>' (writes spiral.toml, deletes nothing; the user removes the spiproj) 45 m; proof: every converted package compiles to byte-identical residuals 30 m; pack review 45 m. Upstream: core packages keep package.spiproj; mixing is per directory, like editions.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_2 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 2u32, "2026-10-24T21:04Z", "main", "main 17:1x: milestones 1-2 drafted as core slice %TEMP%\\pack-toml\\spiral-toml.ps1 (4 anchors, each 1 match on the tree core BEECFE432427C57A): tomlManifestText + isTomlManifest before config, and config translates TOML text before parsing; spiproj_suffix prefers spiral.toml, so discovery, reads, diagnostics URIs and watchers follow; both edition probes (formatter editionOf, xCompatPackageEditionThree) read spiral.toml with edition = 2|3. fsc --parseonly passes under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. Converter draft %TEMP%\\pack-toml\\to-toml.ps1 (spiproj -> toml: strings, ints, packages/crates arrays, modules as a ''' literal tree). Translator check in fsi (%TEMP%\\pack-toml\\test\\check.fsx) on 4 real manifests (x apps package, lib, 2 eoie src packages): each TOML is detected and translates back to its spiproj text (blank lines ignored), and no spiproj is detected as TOML. Known v1 gap: diagnostics line numbers inside spiral.toml shift where a one-line array expands; a range map is milestone 1b. Next: typecheck/build with the next pack (after 5i's review), then the converter as the compiler verb, then a residual-identity proof per converted package.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_3 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 3u32, "2026-10-24T21:31Z", "main", "main 17:3x: milestone 3 drafted in the same slice (5th anchor, 1 match): 'format --manifest toml <dirs>' writes a spiral.toml beside every package.spiproj under the dirs (generated dirs and links skipped). It keeps an existing spiral.toml, never deletes the spiproj, and writes only after a read-back check: tomlManifestText of the new file must give the spiproj's significant lines, with inline list items such as 'packages: core-' moved below their key, as the spiproj parser treats them. Exit 2 lists refusals (a value holding a quote). Measured in fsi with the slice's own functions over every tracked package.spiproj in 9 repos: 1,643 manifests, 1,643 read back, 0 differ, 0 refused. The first pass had 238 differing, all inline lists that the first converter dropped (fixed). Positive control: dropping one module line from a TOML makes it read back false. fsc --parseonly passes under both defines. Next: build it with the pack after 5i, then convert one consumer (x's apps package), then the residual-identity proof, then the rest.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_4 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 4u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:49 fiscal (recording for main, whose backlog writes are leased during its 21:00 wrap): the converter draft %TEMP%/pack-toml/to-toml.ps1 wrote CRLF (line 29 [IO.File]::WriteAllLines uses the OS newline), which land.ps1 would refuse in an LF tree. Found by fiscal comparing it with a generator-emitted spiral.toml (113 vs 108 bytes, equal after CRLF -> LF). Main fixed it 10-26 ~20:48: WriteAllText with LF joins, a final LF, UTF-8 without BOM. Re-verified by fiscal on generated_cfdi_v4_0/package.spiproj: 108 bytes, 0 CR, no BOM, byte-identical to fiscal's generator_toml_transform output. Open: the F# 'format --manifest toml' path is not drafted yet and must follow the same LF rule.")
inl x_sim_frames_gleam_imports () = Task ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x sim: frames builds again after the sm' regexp-directive landing (gleam build fails: gleam/erlang/atom has no package, part1.gleam lacks Dynamic)", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "X-DONE", "x,sim,gleam,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports () = Planning ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", Minutes 30u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: found by job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4 (exit 1 at apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept, before e2e ran). lib sm'.spi:737 spiral_sm_find_trailing (landed 18:06 by programs-regexp-directive) takes direction: atom.Atom and returns Dynamic. Two gaps: (1) apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml has only gleam_stdlib, so 'import gleam/erlang/atom' finds no package; add gleam_erlang (newest 1.x, check release first). (2) gleam-modules.ps1's split put spiral_sm_find_trailing in part1.gleam, whose imports carry gleam/erlang/atom but not gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}; the split must copy every import a moved function uses. Fix both in x, then rerun verify_x_after_e2e_fix.ps1 as verify-landing5. Blocks X-THREAD-RESUME, X-READ-BUDGET and X-LOOKUP-LIMITS closing (their e2e never ran).")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: cause of gap (1): X-DEPS-LATEST's frames step (n10, in HEAD 6ef9c48) removed gleam_erlang from apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml. That was right then, since frames imported only bit_array/int/list/order/string. n10 also predicted lib code would bring it back at lib's 1.3 pins, and sm'.find_trailing's atom arm now does. So: re-add gleam_erlang at the version lib/Cells resolve (read spiral/apps/cells manifest.toml, don't guess), then gleam deps update. Keep gleam_stdlib >= 1.0.5 < 2.0.0.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: version source found. Cells declares gleam_erlang '>=1.3.0 and <2.0.0' in spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex:344, per test, not in Cells' manifest.toml. Its manifest resolves only gleam_stdlib 1.0.5. So frames gets gleam_erlang = '>= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0' to match, and the Cells gate on sm' passing never covered frames' own toml. Positive control after the fix: gleam build in apps/sim/frames must compile part1.gleam's spiral_sm_find_trailing.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:02Z", "x", "10-24 20:02 x: scope note (user 20:0x). This is the interim fix that unblocks verify-landing5 (gleam_erlang dep plus the parts script's import copy). It is not the direction. The direction is SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (reopened) and XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH (P1): real packages that make the parts script unnecessary.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_5 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T01:12Z", "x", "10-24 21:12 x: APPLIED (unstaged): apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml gains gleam_erlang = '>= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0' (permanent). scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 Get-UsedImports now splits 'path.{names}', so the alias is the last path segment and an import is kept when the text uses 'alias.' or any unqualified name, 'type ' stripped ('Dynamic' from 'gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}'). Unit-checked on both import shapes. That fix dies with the script (X-PARALLEL-BUILD). Gate: job-1792890731500-x-verify-landing5 (heavy, after main's edition-3 pass2; sim fixtures -Accept, then client regen, gate, x-compat, release, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_6 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 6u32, "2026-10-25T01:40Z", "x", "10-24 21:40 x: verify-landing5 (job-1792890731500) proved the stopgap: gleam build compiled, sim fixtures accepted (prelude.events/final: only prelude.spi's own source text changed, as expected). It then failed mix test 44/45: 'results assigned to whoever asked, without cancelling, surface stale revisions'. Root cause, by elimination: (a) the Spiral invariant is correct, since hand-built rows give no_stale_results=FAIL[p0]; (b) keys are sha256 of the source, so ordering is lib-independent; (c) the test's slow executor slept 80 ms against the runner's 100 ms harvest window, so on a 100%-CPU box both results landed in one batch and the stale one never surfaced. Fix: sleep 250 ms (apps/sim/test/xsim_test.exs), 3/3 pass, and the second assert (cancel on -> :ok) still guards the other direction. Rerun inside job-1792892406819-x-verify-landing6.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_7 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 7u32, "2026-10-28T00:14Z", "x", "2026-10-27 20:14 x: closed on today's evidence. The 10-24 break (a lib sm' landing broke apps/sim/frames' Gleam build) is gone: quick check 4 (job-1793112246184) printed 'GLEAM-COMPILED x/apps/sim/frames' (8,926 generated lines, 13 modules) and ExUnit 45/45, and every sim accept since (verify17, 19, 20, 21b) built frames and passed 45/45. Nothing was left to do on this item; it was never closed.")
inl tibia_no_hand_lua () = Task ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", "No hand-written Lua anywhere in tibia: rewrite live/ Lua as Spiral compiled to Lua (server-probes ground_trace 76 + lead_trace 126 lines, parity.lua 67, bot-lua-dofile.lua 25, bot-network-sandbox.lua 4, and the nexbot trace snippet in run-policy-scenario.ps1), then delete the .lua files", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,lua,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_no_hand_lua () = Planning ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", Minutes 420u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl gleam_export_types () = Task ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", "Gleam Export across packages: unions and records reachable from an !!!!Export signature are emitted pub and named after their Spiral source (event, not Uh3) so sibling packages can share them; today codegenGleam names every type by a per-program counter and xsim exports are String -> String (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH census)", Story, P1, D4, 85u32, Active, "-", "compiler,gleam,codegen,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gleam_export_types () = Planning ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", Minutes 960u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:09 main: filed from x's type-seam census (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH note 2): 27 of 82 sim types cross a seam (17 records, 5 unions, 2 nominals, 3 aliases), and the hub is events. Mechanism, read in the tree core: RUST-EXPORT-TYPES already fills unionSourceNames (union node -> Spiral name), shared by every backend, and codegenRust walks each Export signature (spiral_compiler.fs 164055-164077: exported_union_names, sanitized, index suffix on collision). The Gleam slice is codegen only: the same walk in codegenGleam, then pub type <source name> for those unions in types.gleam, with internal types keeping Uh/Us. Nominals over primitives (post_id, cell_id) are evaluated away before codegen, so they cross as their primitive and need nothing. Records crossing a seam need a stable name too: take Rust's exported_record_names rule, not a positional layout. Proof: an export_union regression sample with a two-package Gleam consumer that pattern-matches the union, as the Rust union consumer crate did (exit 12). Estimate 240 m from the Rust slice minus its evaluator half (the table exists). Starts only if XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH picks packages.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:09 main: x amended the bench rule before measuring (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH note 3). This item counts as cost, not as a disqualifier. Packages win if B0/C0 is within 10% of A, or if B0 minus its measured seam-parse time is. If packages win, this item lands and B is re-measured typed, which must come out <= B0. Seam scope confirmed as 5 unions plus the crossing records; the nominals cross as primitives. Numbers will come after X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS and verify-landing5.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: TEAM main -> x (user 20:1x: 'compiler work is for each lane, not main, where did you take that?'). The drift was mine: I filed this 'for main' from a stale lane-roles memory, which is now fixed. The dependency on the bench's packages-vs-parts outcome is dropped. The user decided packages, built at codegen, so this item starts as part of X-PARALLEL-BUILD. main's design notes (n1, n2) are the plan: codegenGleam walks Export signatures like codegenRust 164055-164077, emits pub types named by source for unions, and records follow exported_record_names. Proof: a two-package Gleam consumer that pattern-matches an exported union.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: scope (user 20:3x). This is the enabler for HAND-modeled Gleam units (X-HAND-UNITS): types reachable from a hand-written !!!!Export get source names, so sibling modules can share them. It does no splitting. x does all of it itself (draft, gate, pack, install), telling the other lanes only before an install.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 5u32, "2026-10-25T02:44Z", "x", "10-24 22:44 x: SCOPE GREW from measurement (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH n8). Producer-side names aren't enough for Gleam. Hand units must CALL each other with typed values, or every unit re-emits the 1,100-line event codec. Needed: (a) the producer emits pub types named after their Spiral source for its exports (the original scope); (b) the consumer: an entry declares a dependency on another unit's exports, and codegen emits 'import <module>.{type T, f}' and calls f, with no local re-declaration of T or of f's body. That is how Rust's x crates should share types too, instead of tuples-only. Design first (CPU-free): how the consumer names the producer (a package.spiproj unit list?), and how Spiral types the foreign f (from the producer's entry signature).")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 6u32, "2026-10-25T02:59Z", "x", "10-24 22:59 x: DESIGN from reading the core (CPU-free). Producer side today (Rust): codegenRust walks the Export signatures (spiral_compiler.fs 164055-164077), names each union from unionSourceNames (52672), then rename_exported_unions (164849) regex-renames UH<n>/US<n> to the source name, case names included (UH0_Cons -> event_Cons), and makes the enum pub. Gleam reuse: the same walk in codegenGleam plus a rename of Uh<n>/Us<n> (and their CapitalCase constructors Uh<n>Cons...) to CapitalCase source names (Event, EventCons). Consumer side (new): an entry header directive, e.g. '///- --import-types <unit>:<union>,<union>' (the host already parses ///- options like --package/--timeout), makes codegen rename its local copy of those unions the same way, DROP the local definition, and emit 'use <crate>::{...}' (Rust) or 'import <module>.{type Event, EventCons, ...}' (Gleam). Calls cross through the existing host-call macro typed with the Spiral type. Correctness rests on both sides generating the same layout from the same Spiral type: case order and payload types come from the union definition, so they match. The open problem is generic instantiations: 'list event' vs 'list string' are distinct union nodes with the same source name 'list', and today's naming is first-come with an index suffix, which differs per unit. Needed: structural names for instantiations (list_event, result_post_failure). Proof plan: a two-entry regression sample per backend (producer exports a union, consumer imports it and pattern-matches), then x's Gleam codec unit. Estimate up from 240m: 1-2 days with matrix runs.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 7u32, "2026-10-25T05:57Z", "x", "10-25 01:57 x: design detail from codegenGleam (spiral_compiler.fs 168240; program assembly at 168959-168964). Gleam types are already emitted 'pub type' (168962), so the producer side is only the rename: a post-assembly pass like Rust's rename_exported_unions mapping Uh<n>/Us<n> and their constructors (generatedCaseName from 'Uh<tag>' + case free vars) to CapitalCase source names, regex-anchored as \\bUh<n>(?=[A-Z_]|\\b). Consumer side: after its own rename, drop the matching 'pub type <Name> {...}' block and prepend 'import <module>.{type <Name>, <Ctor>...}'; constructors must be imported to be used unqualified. Watch silenceUnusedBindings (168965): it runs per 'pub fn' chunk, so the import line goes before the first chunk.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 8u32, "2026-10-25T09:37Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:37 -04:00 x: the open instantiation-naming problem, read in the core (CPU-free). Today a generic instantiation gets NO source name, not a first-come one. single-flight nominal_type_apply (PartEval, spiral_compiler.fs ~159967) notes a name only in the bare YNominal branch (noteUnionSourceName, the only call site). The YApply branch evaluates the applied body into a fresh YUnion without noting anything, so 'list event' stays Uh<n> on every backend. gpt5parallel's twin (~147962) flattens the application spine in a loop and notes no names at all, not even bare nominals. Design: one shared helper, structuralTyName (in Prepass, next to unionSourceNames at 52723): YNominal n -> n.node.name; YApply(f, a) -> name f + '_' + name a; YPrim -> its keyword (i32, string, ...); YUnion with a noted source name -> that name; YPair -> name a + '_' + name b; anything else -> 't'. Both cores call noteUnionSourceName (structuralTyName x) when an application yields a YUnion: single-flight at the end of its YApply branch, gpt5parallel after its spine loop (both sides of the PartEval pair, as pair-drift requires). Names then depend only on the type expression, so 'list event' is list_event in every unit and the per-unit index suffix only breaks real collisions. Gate: an export sample where two entries each export 'list event' and print the emitted type name; both must say list_event. Then the two-package Gleam consumer proof from note 6.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 9u32, "2026-10-25T09:51Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:51 -04:00 x: structural names DRAFTED as an anchored patch, deliberately not in the tree: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\b7595ac3-963d-4714-be58-0267aa270947\\scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply.ps1 ([-Reverse]; round-trip byte-identical on a copy; pair-drift clean with it applied). It adds noteUnionSourceNameOnce (shared Prepass; computes the name only for an unnamed node) and structural_ty_name on both PartEval sides; single-flight notes in the YApply branch, gpt5parallel after its spine loop. Why not in the tree yet: no matrix sample uses !!!!Export, so -Suite all can't see it. The consumers that can change are the 654 Export sites (eoie and x crates): a generic union in an export signature goes from UH<n> to a pub source name (list_string, ...), which renames the Rust enum and its cases. Proof gate before landing: (1) a candidate with the patch compiles x's crates and eoie's export entries; (2) every generated .rs diff vs today is a rename only (no other token changes); (3) no macro text in those sources names UH<n>/US<n> literally (grep); (4) the new two-entry export sample gives list_event on both. Then it lands with the Gleam consumer side.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_10 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 10u32, "2026-10-25T10:29Z", "x", "2026-10-25 06:29 -04:00 x: structural-names gate step 3 MET: no .spi under eoie/src, x/apps or spiral/lib names a generated union (UH|US|Uh|Us + digits) in macro text: 0 hits. Positive control: the same pattern finds 83 in x/apps/client/cells/cells.rs. So a rename can't break hand-written host text; steps 1, 2 and 4 (candidate compile of eoie and x export entries, rename-only .rs diff, the two-entry sample) are still to run.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_11 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 11u32, "2026-10-25T11:19Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:19 -04:00 x: structural-names proof (job-1792924355141, private Debug candidate with the patch). Steps 1+2 for x: all 22 x crates are token-identical to the installed compiler's output (renamed 0, other 0), so no x export carries a generic union. Step 4 MET: in the two-entry sample (scratchpad\\structural-names\\sample), producer (i32 -> list event) and consumer (list event -> i32) both emit 'pub enum list_event' with cases list_event_Nil/list_event_Cons; the installed compiler emits a private UH0. eoie's 234 crate entries are running (job-1792927142876-x-structural-names-eoie): each is compiled with the candidate and compared to the committed .rs; where they differ, the installed compiler compiles it too and the token diff must be renames of UH/US names only (pub tokens ignored). The patch lands when that reports 0 other changes.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_12 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 12u32, "2026-10-25T11:56Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:55 -04:00 x: PRODUCER half for Gleam drafted as a second layer, scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_gleam_producer.ps1 (applies over apply.ps1; dry run on a copy clean, pair-drift 0 one-sided). Rust's Export-signature walk is hoisted unchanged into a shared helper, exportedUnionNames env x (CodegenUtils, before the CodegenAdapter pair), so codegenRust and codegenGleam name exported unions by one rule. codegenGleam gets renameExportedUnions as the last pass: Uh<tag>/Us<tag> -> UpperCamel source name (list_event -> ListEvent), regex \\b<generated>(?![0-9]) so Uh3 never hits Uh30, constructors following (Uh3Cons -> ListEventCons, Uh3i0 -> ListEventi0); Gleam types are already 'pub type'. Proof queued: job-1792929315191-x-gleam-producer-names (producer and consumer samples must emit 'pub type ListEvent' on Gleam; Rust outputs byte-identical to the structural-only candidate, which proves the hoist is neutral). Consumer half (n6's --import-types directive: drop the local definition, emit import) is next.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_13 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 13u32, "2026-10-25T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:03 -04:00 x: Gleam PRODUCER half PROVEN (job-1792929315191, a private Debug candidate with apply.ps1 + apply_gleam_producer.ps1). Both sample entries emit 'pub type ListEvent { ListEventNil  ListEventCons(Event, ListEvent) }' with 0 leftover Uh/Us names (9 ListEvent refs each, signatures included), and Event keeps its source name. Rust outputs are byte-identical to the structural-only candidate, so hoisting the walk into exportedUnionNames is neutral. Both patches still wait on the eoie rename-only check (job-1792927142876), then land together. Next is the consumer half.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_14 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 14u32, "2026-10-25T12:06Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:06 -04:00 x: CONSUMER transport rule, written before implementing. The consumer names its imports in its own source, not in build config: hand units are modeled in Spiral (hand-modeled-build-units), so a unit has to describe its own seams, and BuildSpiral/build.ps1 stay one command per entry. Candidates: (a) a ///- header option '--import-types <unit>:<type>,<type>' read by the host; (b) an env var per invocation; (c) a Spiral-level declaration. Pick (a), because the host already reads ///- options and the header is what a reader of the unit sees first. Reject (b): config outside the source. Reject (c) for now: it needs parser and type-system work, so it is deferred unless (a) fails. Codegen effect: for each listed source name, the local union noted with that name (unionSourceNames, whether or not the consumer exports it) is renamed like the producer's, its 'pub type' block is dropped, and 'import <unit>.{type T, C1, C2...}' (Gleam) or 'use <crate>::{T}' (Rust) is prepended. Gate: the two-entry sample with consumer.spi declaring '--import-types producer:list_event,event' must gleam build as one package and run tally (produced 5) = 5, and the Rust pair builds as two crates the same way.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_15 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 15u32, "2026-10-25T12:07Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:07 -04:00 x: CORRECTION to n6 and n14. The compiler does NOT parse ///- header options: neither the host (Program.fs) nor the core matches '///-' or '///>'. Only Cells reads them (apps/cells directives.ex: --package, --timeout, --test). n6's 'the host already parses ///- options' was wrong, and n14's choice of (a) rested on it. The rule's reason still holds (seams declared in the unit's own source, one build command per entry), so (a) stays, with its real cost added: the host reads the entry file's leading ///- lines before compiling, picks out --import-types <unit>:<type>,..., and hands codegen the list through one core entry point (e.g. a CodegenUtils 'importedUnionNames' table set per build), the same way SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT reaches startupParse. Cells passes those lines through untouched (it ignores unknown ///- options; check directives.ex before landing). That is roughly 2 h more than n6's 'codegen only'.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_16 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 16u32, "2026-10-25T12:10Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:10 -04:00 x: CORRECTION to n15's last claim. Cells does NOT ignore unknown ///- options: apps/cells/spi/directives.spi pair's final arm 'Token (flag, _) => ParseBad (text.err ''flag'' flag)' rejects them with 'unknown kernel flag'. So option (a) touches three places: the host reads the header, codegen applies the table, and Cells' directive parser gets an arm that accepts and ignores --import-types and its value (as it does --symbol), with a test that a header carrying it still parses. Checked by reading the code, not assumed.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_17 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 17u32, "2026-10-25T12:32Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:32 -04:00 x: CONSUMER half, first proof run (job-1792930546726): the codegen pass works. consumer.gleam drops both local type blocks and imports them, but with one 'import producer.{...}' line per type. Gleam then keeps only the first, so 'The custom type variant constructor EventLiked is not in scope' (gleam run exit 1). Fix: group the imports per unit into one line ('import producer.{type ListEvent, ListEventNil, ListEventCons, type Event, EventLiked, EventGone}'). Rerun: job-1792931443568-x-gleam-consumer-imports-2. The patch is scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 (core importUnions + importedTypeUnits, host applyEntryImports, Cells directives arm). The proof sets SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES directly, because build.ps1 compiles the host from the live tree; the host's header read gets its own check once landed.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_18 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 18u32, "2026-10-25T12:45Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:45 -04:00 x: CONSUMER half PROVEN (job-1792931443568). With imports grouped per unit, consumer.gleam starts 'import producer.{type Event, EventLiked, EventGone, type ListEvent, ListEventNil, ListEventCons}' and declares no types. producer.gleam + consumer.gleam + a run.gleam build as ONE gleam package (offline, deps from apps/sim/frames), and consumer.consumed(producer.produced(5)) prints 5. So hand units share typed values across modules, with no text seam and no re-emitted codec. Left before landing: (1) the eoie rename-only check: 83 of 234 entries identical, 151 couldn't compile in my harness ('Package C:/home/git/eoie/src/spiral is not loaded': eoie's packages need the src/spiral link that eoie's regen sets up), so it reruns on a scratch copy with that link; (2) the host's header read checked once landed; (3) the Rust consumer half (use <crate>::{T}), the same pass for codegenRust.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_19 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T14:39Z", "x", "2026-10-25 10:39 -04:00 x: RUST consumer half DRAFTED, scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_rust_consumer.ps1, layer 4 over apply.ps1 + both Gleam layers. Dry run on a copy clean; pair-drift 0 one-sided. Rust's orphan rules shape it: a crate may not add inherent or Default impls to another crate's type, so the consumer drops the whole local definition (derive line, enum, 'impl T { fn tag }', 'impl Default for T') and 'use <unit>::{T, ...}' goes after 'use std::rc::Rc;', one line per unit. The producer then owns those impls for every exported union: it emits its tag impl and its Default impl even when the producer itself never uses them, and 'fn tag' becomes 'pub fn tag'. That makes the producer layer ADDITIVE, not rename-only, so the x-crate and eoie neutrality check gets a fourth allowed diff class (added impl blocks for exported unions plus pub on tag) once layer 4 lands. Gate: the two-entry sample as a 3-crate cargo workspace (producer lib, consumer lib depending on it, a run bin) prints 5. Proof job-1792939045373-x-rust-consumer-imports.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_20 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:01Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:01 -04:00 x: Rust consumer proof 2 (job-1792939287925) FAILED, cause found. The consumer pass and 'pub fn tag' never matched: the Rust pipeline builds text with StringBuilder.AppendLine, which writes CRLF on Windows, and only the host turns it into LF on write (Program.fs:868). So every pattern ending in a bare \\n missed: the impl and enum drops, the 'use std::rc::Rc;' anchor, and the tag rewrite. The existing rename survives only because '\\nenum ' is still a substring of CRLF text. The Gleam pipeline builds with bare \\n, which is why the Gleam layer worked. Fix: every layer-4 pattern takes \\r?\\n, checked by scratchpad\\structural-names\\regex_check.ps1 on the real consumer output with CRLF put back (both local types dropped, 'use producer::{event, list_event};' inserted, 1 pub tag). Lesson for codegen passes: match Rust text with \\r?\\n; the Gleam text has bare \\n. Proof 3: job-1792940361755-x-rust-consumer-imports-3. Also: apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 gains an opt-in -DirectivesTest path that adds the n16 Cells test ('--import-types belongs to the compiler, so Cells reads past it and its value', in directives_test.exs). It is opt-in because the proof scripts call the patch without that path and must never write to the live tree.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_21 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 21u32, "2026-10-25T15:08Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:08 -04:00 x: RUST consumer half PROVEN (job-1792940361755, a private Debug candidate with all 4 layers). consumer lib.rs declares no enum and no impl and starts 'use producer::{event, list_event};'. producer lib.rs owns 'impl event', 'impl list_event' (both 'pub fn tag') and both Default impls. A 3-crate cargo workspace (producer lib; consumer lib depending on it; run bin calling consumer::consumed(producer::produced(5))) builds offline and prints 5. So hand units share typed values across crates on Rust too, by the same --import-types seam as Gleam. Left before landing all 4 layers together: (1) the eoie rename-only check for layer 1 (job-1792932468478, running); (2) a neutrality pass for layer 4 over x's 22 crates and eoie's entries, where the allowed diffs are renames, added tag/Default impl blocks for exported unions, and 'pub' on tag; (3) the host's ///- --import-types header read, checked once landed (the proofs set SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES directly); (4) the Cells directives test via apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 -DirectivesTest. Progress 60 -> 70: both halves proven, only checks and landing left.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_22 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 22u32, "2026-10-25T15:38Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:38 -04:00 x: structural-names gate steps 1+2 MET for eoie (job-1792932468478, a scratch copy of eoie src with the src/spiral link to the tree lib): the remaining 151 entries are byte-identical to the committed .rs (same 151, rename-only 0, other 0, no output 0). With the first run's 83 identical, all 234 eoie entries are unchanged and all 22 x crates are token-identical, while the two-entry sample shows the rename (list_event), so layer 1 changes only code that exports a generic union, which no shipped crate does yet. Remaining before landing layers 1-4 together: the layer-4 x neutrality pass (job-1792941002251, added tag/Default impl blocks plus pub on tag allowed), the same pass over eoie's entries, then the land with pair-drift and the host header-read check.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_23 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 23u32, "2026-10-25T15:47Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:47 -04:00 x: layer-4 neutrality MET for x (job-1792941002251): all 22 x crates are identical between the layer-1 and layer-4 candidates once 'pub fn tag' is normalized, with 0 added impl blocks, 0 other additions and 0 removals, because no x crate exports a union. Positive control in the same run: sample/producer.spi goes from 0 to 4 top-level impl blocks (tag + Default for event and list_event), so the check sees the additions. eoie's pass is running (job-1792942810301, layer 4 vs the committed .rs). Landing is scripted and rehearsed: scratchpad\\structural-names\\land_export_types.ps1 [-Base] [-Undo] applies the 4 layers + the Cells arm + the directives test with a backup and a hash-checked undo. A copy rehearsal applied cleanly, pair-drift was 0 one-sided, and undo restored all 4 files hash-equal. It runs on the tree once eoie's pass is clean and no job reads the compiler tree (pack reviews build from it); then a Debug build from the tree, the two-language sample again through the host's ///- header read, and Cells directives_test.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_24 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 24u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:00 -04:00 x: the layer-4 eoie pass (job-1792942810301) was STOPPED at 182/234 entries to release its compile window for main's 5l'' install (job-1792950541607), which was waiting on it. Its partial result is not evidence about layer 4. 161 entries differ from the committed .rs, almost all as 'let mut' -> 'let' and other codegen-pass changes: the tree core moved between the layer-1 candidate (cache, 07:1x, identical to the committed .rs) and the layer-4 candidate (cache-rust, 10:41). With mut ignored, 139 still differ, so it is base-core drift, not my layers. Method fix: compare a BASE candidate and a LAYER-4 candidate built from the SAME core snapshot, not the committed .rs. That is the x pass's method (layer-1 vs layer-4 DLLs, clean), except both must share a base. Rerun after 5l'' installs and the core is pinned: build base and base+4 layers from 17D970FE (or its successor), then run scratchpad\\structural-names\\layer4_eoie_verdicts.ps1 between the two output sets. Landing waits for that.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_25 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 25u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: layer-4 neutrality MET on the same core (job-1792952173917): a candidate = tree core 34519B92 + all 4 layers vs the installed 5l'' (built from that core), over every x crate and every eoie entry (eoie scratch copy). Same 257, other changes 0, missing 0; the only difference is the positive control, sample/producer.spi: 17 renamed tokens (UH<n> -> list_event and cases) + 4 impl blocks (tag + Default for event and list_event). So the layers change nothing in shipped code (no shipped crate exports a union) and the method is now sound: the earlier committed-.rs comparison mixed in base-core drift (let mut, clones). All proof gates are met: Gleam consumer (2 modules, prints 5), Rust consumer (3 crates, prints 5), structural names in eoie/x, layer-4 neutrality. Landing: land_export_types.ps1 (hash-checked -Undo, rehearsed) touches spiral_compiler.fs, host Program.fs, Cells directives.spi + directives_test.exs; asked main at 19:0x whether to land before or after main-pack5m-review (queued; reviews build from the tree core) or take it into pack5m. Progress 70 -> 85 by rule: only the landing and its post-land checks (Debug build from the tree, the sample through the host's ///- header read, mix test directives_test) remain. || 2026-10-25 19:21 -04:00 x: main's decision: not into the tree now (main-pack5m-review composes over 34519B92 and its install refuses if the core moves); the core layers go into PACK 5n, composed right after 5m installs, and the host + Cells parts land in 5n's install window before the host build, so core and host never disagree. Split for that (rehearsed: byte-identical to the full landing on all 4 files, pair-drift 0 one-sided): scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_core.ps1 -Target <core> and apply_host_cells.ps1 -Spiral <root>; apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 gained -SkipCore and empty host/Cells defaults (no default path touches the tree).")
inl x_parallel_build () = Task ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", "x: build fast in parallel for every backend by hand-modeled units (eoie's method: explicit exports, sibling units, shared types in Spiral-only packages), never by automatic splitting in the compiler or post-hoc scripts. Rust and Gleam first, nailed so the other backends follow the same method. Done = scripts/rust-modules.ps1 and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 are deleted with no caller.", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "X-PARALLEL-CENSUS,GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES,X-HAND-UNITS,X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE,XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", "x,build,parallel,codegen,gleam,rust", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_parallel_build () = Planning ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_1 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: user 20:1x: 'these splits shouldn't happen after, but on codegen like eoie. you got lazy. we need to rework everything to leverage parallel compilation, gotta learn the best techniques since eoie isn't a good example... verify if we really need this custom automatic splitting thing so we don't keep dead code'. Evidence today: both splitters are LIVE, so they're not dead yet. apps/client/build.ps1:18 runs rust-modules.ps1 on every crate (part files in thread, write, digest, users, args, frames), and apps/sim/build.ps1:32 runs gleam-modules.ps1. They exist only because every entry is a self-contained program that re-emits its whole closure. The 16 crates total ~14,300 generated lines from ~2,100 entry lines; frames.spi is 24 lines -> 1,011 lines of Rust (42x), digest 85 -> 1,214, view 37 -> 987. The sim is one 9,078-line Gleam program. So the fix is the unit graph, not a better splitter. Open question, measured not assumed: does generating each shared module once (wire 607, posts 417, splitter 218, glyphs 190 .spi lines) keep every unit <= 1,000, or must big modules (wire) be cut further at the source? Steps: X-PARALLEL-CENSUS (sizes per unit), techniques research (running), GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES (x, compiler), the Rust graph (RUST-EXPORT-TYPES is done, so cross-crate types work now), the sim packages (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH), then delete both scripts.")
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_2 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: RESEARCH (sources in the agent report; key ones below). It changes the plan. RUST: (1) The biggest win is emitting each shared specialization ONCE in a shared unit, named by content hash, instead of inlining every entry's closure. Feldera: generated SQL->Rust, 1 crate = 27 min on 1 of 64 cores; 1,106 hash-named crates = 2.5 min, all cores (feldera.com 2025-04-15); Lean specCache and prost extern_path do the same. (2) Crate boundaries must be monomorphic: share-generics is off at opt-level 2/3, so release re-instantiates generics per crate. (3) Each crate pays an O(all upstream) metadata decode (Zed: >400 ms per crate, rust#149313), so 24-line crates sit below break-even. Group crates by sharing unit, wide and shallow, with a small root types crate; read the critical path with cargo build --timings. (4) The 1,000-line rule is met by MODULES inside a crate (rustc makes 2 CGUs per module, which helps incremental reuse and backend parallelism), not by more crates. (5) Big functions are superlinear in LLVM, MIR dataflow and the Erlang SSA passes, so cap fn size and split big match bodies. GLEAM (1.19.0, source read): packages compile SEQUENTIALLY in topological order, and type checking inside a package is sequential. Only the BEAM step is parallel, one worker per scheduler over the modules of ONE package. So many modules in one package is Gleam's parallel shape, and many packages are sequential barriers (there is no workspace; issue #5653). That matches the 30% cold win measured on 10-22, which came from modules, not packages. The user's 'packages with toml' therefore holds for Rust crates grouped by sharing unit. For Gleam the codegen-level split is modules per Spiral source module in one package, plus path packages only for truly reused code. REVISED STEPS: a) X-PARALLEL-CENSUS also measures per-unit sharing; b) SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS (compiler: shared specializations emitted once, named by content); c) codegen emits one target module per Spiral source module, which replaces both post-hoc scripts; d) X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE; e) the bench measures packages vs modules for Gleam by the rule, since the research predicts modules win.")
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_3 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:29Z", "x", "10-24 20:29 x: research sources are notes 5 (Rust) and 6 (Gleam and other generators). They were first written to a stray i574n.github/docs/research file, deleted 10-24 at the user's direction (no new docs dirs in repos). Sent to eoie (226 crates, 211k generated lines; they file their own items).")
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_4 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: user 20:3x: 'i'm not saying to use packages for everything, just to get smarter. if rust requires packages, find the sota combinations to make it faster while iterating with it. if in gleam it's modules, fine let's split like that there then... we'll have to figure out the best way to parallelize for every backend, so we gotta nail these first ones.' Children: X-HAND-UNITS (the hand modeling, Rust + Gleam), X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE (Rust profile/linker combos for the edit-build loop), XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH (Gleam unit shape, all hand-made), X-PARALLEL-CENSUS (generated size per hand unit), GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES (Gleam needs it so hand units can share types; it names types, it doesn't split). All of it is x's own work, compiler parts included. No hand-offs to main (user: 'why you're burdening main with this... we have backups if we ruin things with parallel patching').")
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_5 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 5u32, "2026-10-25T00:45Z", "x", "10-24 20:45 x: SOURCES, Rust part (moved here from a stray docs/research file; this note is now the record). Wide, shallow graph with a small root types crate, few final binaries: matklad.github.io/2021/09/04/fast-rust-builds.html. Pipelined compilation since 1.38: blog.rust-lang.org/2019/09/26/Rust-1.38.0. Feldera, generated SQL->Rust: 1 crate 27 min on 1 of 64 cores (codegen-units 16->256 no help); 1,106 hash-named crates 2.5 min, all cores, mold link 7 s; crates reused across programs; monomorphization replaced by dyn: feldera.com/blog/cutting-down-rust-compile-times-from-30-to-2-minutes-with-one-thousand-crates. Per-crate cost: every crate decodes all upstream metadata, Zed >400 ms per crate late in the graph; fix unmerged: github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/149313; no measured fixed cost exists, so measure our own. Generics are re-instantiated per downstream crate: rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/backend/monomorph.html; share-generics on at opt-level 0/1/s/z, off at 2/3: rustc_session/src/config.rs; polymorphization is an unfunded 2026 goal: goals.rust-lang.org/2026/polymorphization.html. Since 1.75, small call-free non-generic fns inline across crates automatically (rustc_mir_transform cross_crate_inline.rs); release: thin LTO, #[inline] only on tiny hot fns. CGUs: 256 incremental / 16 otherwise, 2 per module (stable + volatile): doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/profiles.html; Zed: codegen-units 16 dev, thin lto + 1 release (zed Cargo.toml). -Zthreads nightly only: up to 50% faster at 8 threads, +35% memory: blog.rust-lang.org/2023/11/09/parallel-rustc; goals.rust-lang.org/2026/parallel-front-end.html. lld default on x86_64 Linux since 1.90 (7x faster incremental links on ripgrep): blog.rust-lang.org/2025/09/01/rust-lld-on-1.90.0-stable; Windows rust-lld opt-in, never benchmarked against link.exe: github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/71520; mold/wild Linux only. cargo build --timings (critical path), cargo llvm-lines (generic bloat); sccache needs incremental off and can't cache bin/cdylib/proc-macro: github.com/mozilla/sccache/blob/main/docs/Rust.md. Huge functions are superlinear in LLVM (users.rust-lang.org/t/36508) and MIR dataflow (rust-lang/rust/pull/160193).")
inl note_of_x_parallel_build_6 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-BUILD", 6u32, "2026-10-25T00:45Z", "x", "10-24 20:45 x: SOURCES, Gleam and other generators. Gleam 1.19.0 (2026-10-05) on OTP 29: packages build sequentially in topological order (compiler-core/src/build/project_compiler.rs, compile_dependencies loop); type checking inside a package is sequential (package_compiler.rs); only the BEAM step is parallel, one worker per scheduler over ONE package's modules (compiler-cli/templates/gleam@@compile.erl). v0.26 per-module incremental cache, 'the majority of the time is spent in the Erlang compiler' (gleam.run/news/v0.26-incremental-compilation-and-deno); v1.19 emits Erlang abstract forms (gleam.run/news/gleam-doesnt-compile-to-erlang-source-anymore); no release adds parallel package builds. No workspaces; dependencies compile once per package: github.com/gleam-lang/gleam/issues/5653. Erlang compile is superlinear in module/function size: alias analysis took 188 of 193 s (github.com/erlang/otp/issues/7432); beam_trim slow on huge straight-line fns (OTP-17842). Other generators: Lean 4 LCNF specCacheExt/findSpecCache? reuses imported specializations; GHC INLINABLE makes specializations visible transitively; prost extern_path and cxx 'type T = crate::existing::T' reuse a type instead of minting one; Idris2 --inc chez gives per-module output (faster builds, fewer optimizations); Elixir Kernel.ParallelCompiler compiles files in parallel. Unverified: per-crate fixed cost, whether Gleam 1.19 abstract forms keep Erlang inlining, Koka/Futhark/tonic layouts, rust-lld vs link.exe on Windows. Hand-modeling reading of all this: X-HAND-UNITS n2.")
inl x_parallel_census () = Task ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", "x: census of generated size per proposed unit. Compile each shared client/sim module as its own entry exporting only what other units call, to find where the source must be cut so every unit is <= 1,000 generated lines with no splitter", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "-", "x,build,parallel,census", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_parallel_census () = Planning ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_parallel_census_1 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: RULE before measuring. A module is its own unit if its entry (exports = the functions other units call, found by the opens graph plus call sites) generates <= 1,000 lines per backend. Otherwise it is cut at the source along its own top-down sections until each piece is. Cross-unit values go through exported types (Rust: RUST-EXPORT-TYPES; Gleam: GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES), never text, except as the B0 lower bound. Record per unit: .spi lines, generated lines, the largest fn and its kind (method specialization, closure, residual straight-line), and the duplication left (lines also present in another unit). Compile on scratch copies, single-flight, in a compile window.")
inl note_of_x_parallel_census_2 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:14Z", "x", "10-24 20:14 x: step 0, CPU-free: the cross-unit call surface (text census; a def counts when a module that opens it names it). It is narrow: wire 47 of 121 defs are called from outside (10 consumers: thread 19, users 17, refs 16, fetch 15), posts 28/81 (12), protocol 11/25 (11), glyphs 13/47 (7), splitter 11/49 (5), events 12/80 (4), cli 9/81 (5), transport 5/13 (6), exsss 2/22, reading 2/30, paging 2/16, oauth1 4/10. prelude 18/20 (32 consumers) is generic list/result helpers (forall a b). Like Rust generics, those instantiate in the caller and stay inlined; a unit can't export a polymorphic inl. So each shared unit exports only its monomorphic surface; for wire, ~47 entry points carry exported types (RUST-EXPORT-TYPES / GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES). Caveat for the measured step: some surface defs are inl with type or closure arguments. They're either specialized per caller (they stay inlined, so count that duplication) or rewritten as let with concrete types. Record which per def.")
inl note_of_x_parallel_census_3 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T01:12Z", "x", "10-24 21:12 x: MEASURED, Rust, on a scratch copy (%TEMP%\\x-units: x/apps/client + a spiral/lib snapshot from 20:44, after main's edition-3 pass1). Baseline: thread 1411, write 1313, digest 1220, users 1210, args 1011, frames 1093. E1 (wire.describe_failure inl -> let): thread 1260 (-151), write 1085 (-228), users 982; the others unchanged. glyphs.glyph_weight inl -> let made frames 1096 (+3): reverted, since lever 8 says measure before keeping. Lib drift today: view 987 -> 1019 and refs 980 -> 1002 with no x change; net 384 -> 651. Over 1,000 after E1: args 1011, digest 1220, frames 1096, refs 1002, thread 1260, view 1019, write 1085. Cross-crate duplication: app_reply 116, raw_reply 149 and describe_failure 80 are re-emitted in thread, users, refs, fetch, write and token, which makes the transport layer the first hand unit.")
inl note_of_x_parallel_census_4 () = Note ("X-PARALLEL-CENSUS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T17:49Z", "x", "2026-10-26 13:49 x: more evidence for lever 8 (measure each inline-to-join-point change), from X-FRAMES-SPLIT and Bluesky today. When a predicate is inlined under a recursive join point or inside a platform match arm, it multiplies: with the defuse helpers as := the chunks crate was 3189 lines (entities_from 790, defused_run 479 with the TLD table inline), and Bluesky's record builders cost write 964 -> 1745 (send_write_5 56 -> 410, inlined twice in each of two send_write instances). The cure is measured per round: split proof 3 (job-1793035997388) and W1 (job-1793036913087) carry the := to :~ changes. This item stays the place for the per-unit census; the method is in memory attribute-generated-lines.")
inl x_hand_units () = Task ("X-HAND-UNITS", "x: hand-model x's build units in the Spiral source, eoie's sibling-crate method, so each backend builds in parallel. Shared slices (wire, posts, splitter, glyphs, events...) are authored as their own entries with explicit Export surfaces. Consumers call them across units by name (unit::export host calls), shared types live in Spiral-only packages, and let/inl are chosen by measured generated size. Nothing is split or deduplicated automatically by the compiler or by a script.", Story, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", "x,build,parallel,rust,gleam,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_hand_units () = Planning ("X-HAND-UNITS", Minutes 3000u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_hand_units_1 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: from the X-PARALLEL-BUILD research (n2). Prior art: Feldera's hash-named crates (27 -> 2.5 min, reused across programs), Lean 4's LCNF specCacheExt/findSpecCache? (imported specializations are reused, not re-made), GHC INLINABLE (client specializations visible transitively), prost extern_path / cxx 'type T = crate::existing::T' (reuse a type instead of minting a new, incompatible one). Spiral's situation: whole-program specialization per entry, so 16 x crates re-emit the same wire/posts/splitter code (~14,300 generated lines from ~2,100 entry lines). Needs: (1) a stable key per specialization (the term plus the types it is specialized at), (2) a cross-entry store (one build compiles several entries, or a cache on disk), (3) per-unit emission with exported, source-named types (RUST-EXPORT-TYPES done; GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES), (4) monomorphic unit boundaries. Owned by x (user 10-24: compiler work belongs to the lane that needs it); tell the lanes before any pack or install. First step, CPU-light: read how the core names and dedupes specializations within one entry (the method*/closure* counters), and write the design with a minimal two-entry regression sample.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_2 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:41Z", "x", "10-24 20:40 x: REFRAMED (user 20:3x: 'just don't do it automatically in the compiler itself, we gotta model everything by hand and export like eoie was doing, i thought that was paying off somehow, even if still rudimentar'). The filed version (shared specializations emitted once by the compiler, named by content hash, one module per source module at codegen) was the research agent's rank-1 technique taken literally, which made it automatic compiler work. That's withdrawn, renamed from SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS. The research still sets the targets: per backend, find the shape that builds fastest, then model it by hand. Method, from memory eoie-granular-spiral 'crate-size levers': a unit takes whole slices, not cut-up functions. Cross-unit calls go through exports; shared types go in a Spiral-only package opened as package.module. A hot inl that inlines everywhere becomes let. A record literal with repeated literals goes in a let constructor. Profile per-item line counts before cutting. Per backend: RUST = crates grouped by sharing unit (each crate pays an upstream-metadata decode, so not one per tiny entry), several hand-authored .spi entries per crate as modules where the 1,000 rule needs it, and profiles tuned for the edit-build loop (X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE). GLEAM = one package, one hand-authored entry per Gleam module (the BEAM step is parallel only across one package's modules; packages are sequential), with shared types via GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES. Done = both post-hoc scripts deleted and every unit <= 1,000 lines as authored.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_3 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T01:12Z", "x", "10-24 21:12 x: HEADROOM RULE, set before the next cut. Every generated crate/module must be <= 950 lines under the current lib (eoie heal-crate's number). Today's lib pass alone moved view +32 and refs +22, so 1,000 with no margin turns red at the next landing, and someone would add a splitter back. A unit counts as done only at <= 950. Cuts are judged on the consumer side too (lever 8). Seam rule: use what x already does across crates (transport -> xc_net::xc_send tuple; xc -> xc_args::xc_parse_os_args record; args -> xc_checks::xc_count scalar), i.e. scalar, tuple or record payloads through '$'crate::export()'' plus a Spiral-only package for shared types. No compiler work on the critical path. Before cutting transport: check per crate whether it CREATES failure values (CursorHalted, absent 404, NoSuchAccount) or only passes on transport's. Pass-through crates receive an already-described error string. Gleam: cut at internal slices (events+final_state, cast, world, splitter, invariants), not export groups (the 10-22 groups reached 19,809 lines). The world unit takes the event-stream text instead of re-running from_cells.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_4 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T01:40Z", "x", "10-24 21:40 x: FIRST TWO RUST UNITS LANDED (unstaged), measured on scratch first. (1) api unit: new crate xc_api (apps/client/api) exports xc_classified(resource, status, body) : i32 * string (0 + body, or 4 + the described error). wire's failure is now Reported text | NoSuchAccount | CursorHalted, and classify -> classified/refusal/decodable keep the exact texts. transport.kept calls xc_api. thread's absent and write's rate_limited fixture build Reported (answered ...). Before -> after: thread 1260->1138, users 982->851, refs 1002->871, fetch 860->729, write 1085->954; api 148. (2) chain unit: new crate xc_chain (apps/client/chain) holds the resume chain logic and its tests, reading xc_thread::xc_thread_pages' page bundle (an existing JSON text seam, no re-fetch); xc calls xc_chain::xc_chain. thread 1138->734, chain 643 (the 239-line overlap is the post decoding both need). Also E1 (wire.describe_failure inl -> let). Measured and rejected: glyph_weight let (+3) and dyn-closure prelude helpers (E2: +56..+92 on digest/view/refs). Left over 950: write 954, digest 1220, view 1019, args 1011, frames 1123. Next cuts drafted: preview crate from xc_frames' JSON, a glyphs weight unit (scalar seam, Rust arm only), then digest/view/args. Gate: job-1792892406819-x-verify-landing6.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_5 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T02:40Z", "x", "10-24 22:40 x: ALL RUST UNITS LANDED (unstaged), and scripts/rust-modules.ps1 is DELETED with its apps/client/build.ps1 call; the 7 stale <crate>_partN.rs are removed, and both READMEs say crates split by hand. Measured on a scratch copy against the live lib, generated lines per crate: new units api 256 (classify + posts_read), chain 643, cells 333, preview 182, select 802, timeline 759, resume 249. Cut crates: thread 734, users 851, refs 871, fetch 729, write 845, frames 840, view 745, jsonl 676 (now on xc_select), args 494; digest 956 (from 1220: keep is a number in the bundle, posts_read moved to api, entry_lines_of let + dyn role, accounts_in, merge_pools/single_line let, dyn separators in entry_line/refs_text/digested). digest is the one unit over the 950 headroom (under the 1,000 gate). Its next cut is structural: a resolve stage (raw lines with @author_id and media keys, then usernames and media hints resolved after), measured on a probe at 914 before tables. Rejected by measurement: glyph_weight let (+3), dyn-closure prelude helpers (+56..+92), dyn literals in included_pool/sm'.replace (0), joined_lines instead of append (+16). x-compat 0 findings in 44 files. Gate: verify-landing7.")
inl x_cargo_build_profile () = Task ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", "x: Cargo profiles tuned for parallel and incremental builds: release today is codegen-units = 1 plus fat lto = true, which serializes codegen. Measure thin LTO with 16 CGUs, rust-lld on Windows, and dev debug = line-tables-only with cargo build --timings, and keep a setting only if its rule passes", Story, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,build,rust,parallel", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_cargo_build_profile () = Planning ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", Minutes 90u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_1 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: x/Cargo.toml [profile.release] codegen-units = 1, lto = true (fat), opt-level = 3; .cargo/config.toml sets only +crt-static. e2e and the pre-commit build release, so every verify pays for serial fat LTO. RULE, set before measuring: 3 cold and 3 touch builds of the 16 crates per setting, quiet machine, cargo build --timings saved. Take thin LTO + codegen-units 16 if cold or touch wall drops >= 15% and the e2e wall and xc timeline replay don't slow by > 5%. Take rust-lld (linker = 'rust-lld' for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc) if the link phase drops >= 20% and e2e passes; it isn't benchmarked against link.exe upstream (rust#71520), so our measurement decides. Leave dev as is unless the pre-commit uses dev. Sources: Cargo profiles docs, perf book, rust 1.90 lld post.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_2 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: focus (user 20:3x: 'find the sota combinations to make it faster while iterating with it'). The loop that matters is edit one .spi -> regenerate one crate -> build -> e2e, so the touch rebuild is the primary measure and cold is secondary. Combos to measure together, not one at a time: dev vs release for the e2e loop (does e2e need release?), thin vs fat LTO, codegen-units 16/256, rust-lld vs link.exe, debug line-tables-only, incremental on/off, and nightly -Zthreads=8 for dev. Read cargo build --timings for the critical crate. The n1 rule applies per combo.")
