inl note_of_eoie_open_shadow_lint_1 () = Note ("EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-26T13:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:54 fiscal: request from the field. Shadowing a name from an opened module is a recurring silent bug class: fiscal F1 'census_of' shadowed xsd_regex.census_of (an existing caller broke, far from the edit); R5's 'node_name' / 'node_text' shadowed xml's functions (latent trap); TIBIA-AGENT's 'index' counter shadowed the array index function; PACKAGE-REGISTRY's ':< materialize' resolved to a type alias. Stopgap used today: session scratchpad shadow-check/shadow_check.py <base>=<draft> ... (names the draft adds vs its base; each checked against every ':<' module's top-level definitions; 0 false positives across 10 drafts). Wanted: one eoie verb, e.g. 'eoie spiral shadows <file> [--base <file>]', exit non-zero on a clash, usable in the PostToolUse style hook. Done = the verb + a Spiral test block per clash kind (value, nominal, union case, alias).")
inl rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Task ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", "lib string wrappers (trim/substring) inline per callsite in codegenRust, bloating generated Rust past the 1k gate; make hot wrappers join-point methods so each emits one shared fn called per use instead of inlining", Story, P3, D3, 0u32, Paused, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,size,lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Planning ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_1 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 1u32, "2026-10-26T15:37Z", "compiler", "filed from kit's measurement 10-26: kit_lint_macros 461 Spiral -> 2,056 Rust (gate 1,000). Mechanism (spiral_compiler.fs): the StringSlice intrinsic is ALREADY one shared fn string_slice (164434/164749); the bloat is lib WRAPPERS (trim/substring, sm'.spi:1321) being plain := functions that Spiral inlines whole (bound casts, Rc clones, empty-string thread_local) at every callsite. codegenRust already has the shared-helper mechanism: a METHOD join point emits one top-level fn method_N(...) called per use (164966, self-tail -> loop). Fix is LIB-LEVEL + no compiler build: put :~/join on the big, frequently-called wrappers so they land as methods. VERIFY the regen shows top-level fn <name>(...) {} (method, win) NOT Rc::new(move|...| ) / fn ->Rc<dyn Fn> (closure path at 165003, heavy: box + thread_local, WORSE). kit's :~ experiment grew because it was applied to 11 tiny 1-3 line helpers (inlining those is cheaper) and/or they became closures not methods. operator_between is kit-local, not lib. Distinct from EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC (that's export signatures). Reject: making trim/substring F# intrinsics (duplicates logic across ~15 backends, violates granular-style). Full analysis: scratchpad/lib-string-op-codegen-size.md.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_2 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 2u32, "2026-10-26T17:09Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (concrete lib edit for the codegen-size win, build-independent analysis of lib/spiral/sm'.spi). The bloaty string wrappers kit measured are OUTER `:=` functions that inline their whole chain per callsite:\n- trim (sm'.spi:1321): `:= trim (input:string):string = input |> trim_with []`\n- trim_with (:1289): `:= trim_with (chars)(input):string = input |> trim_start chars |> trim_end chars`\n- trim_start (:1217) / trim_end (:1253): `:=` outer; their INNER recursive loops are ALREADY `:~ ^ 루프` (so the loops already emit as shared methods -- good). Only the outer wrappers inline.\n- substring (:988): `:= substring (start:i32)(len:i32)(str:string):string = run_target \\| | Rust => \\ () => ... slice ...` (a backend_switch via run_target).\n\nEDIT (draft for kit to try, NOT yet applied -- lib is live-tree, Cells reads it, so land as a separate gated change): change the OUTER wrappers from `:=` to `:~` so each emits ONCE as a shared method called per use, instead of inlining the chain at every callsite: trim, trim_with, trim_start, trim_end, substring. All are monomorphic (string->string; substring i32->i32->string->string) and called in statement position, so they should land as METHODS (shared `fn`), not boxed closures. VERIFY on regen: `fn <name>(...)` emitted once + `<name>(...)` at each call (WIN); if you see `Rc::new(move|...)` / `fn ... -> Rc<dyn Fn>` it became a closure (misfire -- back it out). substring caveat: its body is a `run_target` backend_switch with `\\ () =>` arms; a `:~` wraps the resolved dispatch as a method -- should be fine but is the one most likely to misfire, check it first.\n\nSCOPE CAVEAT (important): sm'.spi is SHARED lib -- making these `:~` changes codegen for EVERY consumer across ALL backends, not just kit_lint_macros. So this is NOT a kit-crate-local change: it needs the full `-Suite all -Native` oracle-parity gate + a corpus check (lib + tibia + Kino compile, per lib-smoke-before-syntax) and must accept the perf trade-off (a method call vs inlining) on any HOT trim/substring use elsewhere. For cold lint/verb crates the size win dominates; for a hot inner loop the call overhead is a (tiny) regression. If the all-consumers trade-off is unwanted, the alternative is a crate-local wrapper kit marks `:~` rather than changing lib. Build-blocked (node committed ~8h to kit-release-FOLLOWUP); kit owns the call on whether the lib-wide change is worth it vs a crate-local wrapper.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_3 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 3u32, "2026-10-26T20:15Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (DECISION recorded; item parked). kit decided: NO lib-wide :~ change to sm'.trim/trim_with/trim_start/trim_end/substring for kit's sake. kit measured the crate-local alternative on the node (kit-lint-wrappers2, lint4 mirror): lint's OWN trim/substring/starts_with/replace put through four local :~ wrappers -> each emitted as ONE fn called 6-25x, NOT a boxed closure (confirms this item's note-1/note-2 method-vs-closure prediction empirically). But lint.rs went 2,056 -> 2,005 lines = only 2.5%. So the structural bloat is not wrapper-inlining; kit splits the lint crate into hand-modeled build units instead (GRANULAR-LINT note 17), which is the real size fix.\n\nCompiler CONCURS with parking the lib-wide change: >=2.5% best case (crate-local ceiling; the lib change can't beat it inside one crate) does NOT justify a full -Suite all -Native oracle-parity gate + a per-consumer hot-path call-overhead trade-off on every trim/substring use across all backends. The draft (scratchpad/rswi-lib-draft.txt) is PARKED, not deleted: the lib-wide :~ edit is proven-sound and ready, but is NOT blocked by kit and should only be revived if a DIFFERENT consumer wants the size win for its own reasons and accepts the all-consumers perf trade-off. Status -> parked (resolve-as-wont-do for the lib-wide scope; crate-local/build-unit split is the chosen path). Pairs still with EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC only on the shared annotation mechanism, which is a separate open item.")
inl kit_job_front_ticket () = Task ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", "job start --heavy --front: queue a job first in the heavy line with a ticket owned by its own supervisor", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Active, "KIT-RENAME", "kit,jobs,slots", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_front_ticket () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 1u32, "2026-10-26T17:05Z", "kit", "13:05 kit: Found while launching kit-release-FOLLOWUP (KIT-RENAME note 44). The coordinator agreed it should go first, but job start --heavy can't do that. The supervisor's slot gate (kit_job_slot slot_gate) always stamps its ticket with the current ticks and overwrites SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET. A pre-made ticket owned by another live pid would be first forever and stall the box. The workaround was a plain job plus a hand-written heavy-wrapper.ps1 (scratch clean\\respell\\launch-FOLLOWUP.ps1 -Ticket), which also skips the window gate's bookkeeping. Shape: job start --heavy --front. ticket_created takes a stamp one tick before the current live head, still owned by the supervisor's pid, so the ticket dies with the job. Every other gate step is unchanged. The job's slot file and job list say 'front'. Milestones: (1) slot.spi stamp parameter, with a test block where a front ticket sorts first and a dead-owner front ticket is skipped; (2) the --front flag through job_start_args and start_shape; (3) one-call spec case + release. Rule for using it: only after the lane coordinator agrees the order, never by default.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 2u32, "2026-10-26T19:42Z", "kit", "15:42 kit: design read against the tree (no edits; the tree is held by FOLLOWUP). The options travel as positional values through four packages: kit_job_start_args start_args (a 14-tuple Export), kit_job_launch (re-splats them into kit_job_create's 14-argument Export), kit_job_create (writes job files such as slot_by_ms), then kit_job_slot/kit_job_gate read the files. The style contract says touching a legacy function means migrating it, so --front is not a one-flag change. Plan: (1) carry start_options as one typed record from start_args through launch into create (a record Export instead of 14 positionals), with a front field set by a width-1 flag FrontStart, refused unless the shape is HeavyStart; (2) create writes job file 'front'; (3) slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists, owner still the supervisor pid; (4) tests: a windows_cli fixture with two queued heavy jobs where the --front one is admitted first, and a dead-owner front ticket that gets skipped. Re-estimated from these milestones: 300m (the record migration of the four Export boundaries is most of it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 3u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "kit", "09:5x kit, design correction before building (learned tonight from three crate splits): note 2's 'carry start_options as one typed record ... a record Export instead of 14 positionals' cannot work. A !!!!Export signature carries only FFI shapes: scalars, strings, arrays (Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>) and tuples of them. Spiral records and unions are private generated types (US0, UH1 ...). Revised plan: inside each of start_args, launch and create, migrate the legacy positional handling to one local typed record (start_options) and keep the Export boundary as the positional tuple, widened by one bool 'front' (the boundary stays scalar, as kit's style contract asks of boundaries). create writes job file 'front'; slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists; the owner is still the supervisor pid. The tests are unchanged from note 2. Current sizes (headroom for the record and one field): start_args.rs 578, launch.rs 593, create.rs 341, slot.rs 358. Starts after kit-release-SLICES lands, so the four crates rebuild once with the other staged changes.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 4u32, "2026-10-27T14:06Z", "kit", "10:1x kit: built on a probe copy and staged (clean/kit-rename/job-front-ticket, 4 files with base hashes; applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES). start_args: FrontStart is a width-1 flag '--front' that sets start_options.front (already a typed record inside the package); it is refused unless the shape is HeavyStart ('--front applies to --heavy jobs: it queues the job first in the heavy line'); the Export tuple gains one bool. launch threads front into create through both job_launched callers (launched_text passes false). create takes front and writes job file 'front' (folded into the existing write-error refusal). slot: front_name (pure) builds {head_ticks-1:020}-{pid}.t from the live head ticket, so it sorts first; one-expression primitives ticket_ticks, own_pid and ticket_written; slot_gate takes front_ticket when the job's 'front' file exists, and falls back to a normal ticket when the queue has no live head. A dead-owner front ticket is skipped by the existing first_live_ticket (pid liveness). The owner stays the supervisor pid. Test block: a_front_ticket_sorts_one_tick_before_the_head. Sizes: start_args 616 (was 578), launch 594 (593), create 356 (341), slot 448 (358). Not yet proven: the live two-job ordering (note 2's windows_cli fixture) needs the built candidate; it runs after the stage lands and the next release builds.")
inl rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Task ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", "Rust codegen: skip the let vN = vN.clone() for union-arm fields the arm body never reads (bind them as _ in the match pattern)", TaskKind, P3, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,line-gate,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Planning ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_1 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 1u32, "2026-10-26T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-26 x: measured in the Bluesky-stacked write.rs: 112 of 1778 lines (6.3%) are clones of pattern fields that the arm never uses. Most come from ThreadReply's 7 fields bound in each platform arm of path_on, verb_on and decoded_on. Count: a let vN = vN.clone() whose vN appears at most three times in its fn (pattern, binding, clone). This differs from X-DONE note 16's dead literal lets (1.6%). Done when: codegenRust emits _ for unread arm fields, the corpus agrees, and x's generated line counts drop with no rustc warnings added.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_2 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:02Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:02 x: sized on today's split-tree client outputs (proof 7, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357, 23 crates, 16,279 lines). The generated code has 935 match-arm re-binding clones (let vN: T = vN.clone();), but only 102 are never read in their arm (a brace-depth scan to the end of the arm), i.e. 0.6% of all lines. Worst crates: write 29 (pre-R1), chain 9, users/locate/thread 7 each. The 6.3% measured earlier was the Bluesky-stacked write before R1. For x this fix is a polish item, not a line-gate lever; P3 stands. The remaining arm clones are read; they are the &-match-then-own pattern, which only a borrow-aware codegen could drop.")
inl kit_evidence_refresh () = Task ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", "kit evidence refresh: rewrite one EvidenceRef row from the file on disk (sha256, size), so binary evidence never needs a hand edit", Story, P2, D3, 70u32, Active, "KIT-RENAME", "kit,evidence,release", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_evidence_refresh () = Planning ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_1 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 1u32, "2026-10-26T21:02Z", "kit", "17:02 kit: Found while preparing KIT-RENAME's post-install step (note 40: 'refresh evidence.spi's binary row for kit.exe with the closeout tool, never by hand-editing a hash'). No such verb exists. state/evidence.spi:47 is core.EvidenceRef ('eoie.exe', sha256, 8424960u64, core.BinaryMedia, core.BundlePath), which evidence_currentness_domain's closeout check reads and also requires a root binary named kit or kit.exe (terminal.spi 265-268). The only writers in src are register_coverage_evidence (coverage rows only, terminal.spi:297) and kit_toolchain_proxy's identity_line (portable toolchain rows, ExternalRequired). So after install, the check will truthfully report the binary evidence stale, and nothing can refresh it without a hand edit. Shape: 'kit evidence refresh <root> <relative path>' rewrites one EvidenceRef row from the file on disk (name, sha256 via the existing portable_sha256, byte length), keeps the media and custody fields, refuses a path outside the root or not already listed, and supports a dry run. Renaming a row (eoie.exe to kit.exe) is the same verb with --as <new relative path>. Test block: a fixture root with a two-row evidence file; refresh one, the other stays byte-identical, and the closeout check passes.")
inl note_of_kit_evidence_refresh_2 () = Note ("KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:55Z", "kit", "09:0x kit: built and proven on a probe copy, staged with the ceremony verbs (one CLI stage: clean/kit-rename/cli-ceremonies-evidence, 11 files with base hashes, applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES). 'kit evidence refresh <root> <relative path> [--as <new relative path>] [--dry-run]' lives in a new granular crate, kit_evidence (evidence.rs 428 lines): a typed refresh_outcome; pure rewritten_row and refreshed_text; one-expression host primitives for args, the existing kit_proxy_search::inspection_file_sha256 and the file length; the write goes through workspace_primitives' replace_file_checked. It is a direct verb (VerbEvidence, help line; kit.rs 958, command_spec.rs 862). Test block: one row rewritten (rename), the other byte-identical; an unlisted path refused; a path escaping the root refused. End-to-end on the generated Rust (fixture root with a two-row evidence.spi; sha256 via a stub with the same signature): --dry-run writes nothing; refresh --as kit.exe rewrites only that row, with sha256 equal to Get-FileHash and length 12, and the other row byte-identical; a missing file and ../escape exit 2 with their reasons. After the next install, 'kit evidence refresh <kit root> eoie.exe --as kit.exe' fixes evidence.spi:47 without a hand edit.")
