inl x_json_collect_dedup () = Task ("X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP", "x: the JSON-to-list collect loops (generated fn from_N) cost 841 lines across 15 client crates; design one shared shape (fewer element types, or a host collect) and draft it", Story, P3, D3, 40u32, Paused, "", "x,lines,census", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_json_collect_dedup () = Planning ("X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_json_collect_dedup_1 () = Note ("X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP", 1u32, "2026-10-27T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-27 06:51 x: census on today's base regen (pinned compiler): digest 165, chain 113, thread 98, refs 79, fetch 67, assemble 58, users 55, locate 55, write 52 and more. Analysis and draft are CPU-free; the measuring round needs a compile slot.")
inl note_of_x_json_collect_dedup_2 () = Note ("X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP", 2u32, "2026-10-27T11:18Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:18 x: analysis, lower value than the census suggested. wire's collect is := with an inner :~ ^ from join point, so every (element type, decoder) pair at a call site emits its own from_N, with the decoder f inlined in the loop. digest's 165 lines are 7 loops of 15, 49, 24, 16, 16, 24 and 21 lines: about 12 lines of fixed loop scaffold each, plus the inlined decoder. Collapsing the scaffold (a host json-array-to-list-of-json call, then listm.map per type) saves at most about 12 lines per instance, and map loops come back per element type. The decoders are the real cost, and they are needed. Recommendation: drop to P3; if a crate nears the bound, turn its biggest inlined decoder (digest from_7, 49 lines) into a :~ join point instead.")
inl note_of_x_json_collect_dedup_3 () = Note ("X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP", 3u32, "2026-10-27T15:10Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:09 x: groomed to Paused, so the light view lists only pullable work. Resume condition (from this item's own analysis): a touched crate's regen nears 900 generated lines, or a change has to grow it. Checked against every regen until then.")
inl x_failure_text_dedup () = Task ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", "x: describe_failure (48 generated lines) is re-emitted in fetch, write, thread, refs and users (240 lines); give it one home crate with a text export, or shrink it", TaskKind, P3, D2, 30u32, Paused, "", "x,lines,census", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_failure_text_dedup () = Planning ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_1 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 1u32, "2026-10-27T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-27 06:51 x: same census. The requests crate already owns failures for writes; reads could call one export too.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_2 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 2u32, "2026-10-27T11:17Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:15 x: design. describe_failure is only the visible part. The base-regen census shows the whole read transport re-emitted in every read crate: describe_failure 48 lines in fetch/thread/refs/users (plus write before R1), app_reply 23 in fetch/thread/refs/users, string_slice 49-75 in each (through clipped in problem_detail), and each crate's raw_reply instance. Proposal for a measured round: extend the requests crate with xc_read(site JSON, path) -> (code, body, failure text), so a read crate keeps only its decoding and calls one host function for the HTTP, auth, classification and failure text. Expected about 150-200 lines less per read crate (fetch, thread, refs, users), and requests grows by about one read path (it already holds raw_reply, describe_failure and string_slice). Pre-register the rule before drafting: each touched crate <= 900 and none grows; requests <= 900; notebooks and e2e green through x/scripts/verify.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_3 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 3u32, "2026-10-27T12:05Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:05 x: analysis, lower value than first thought. transport.spi already delegates status classification to one host call (xc_api::xc_classified), so a read crate repeats only app_reply (23 lines) and describe_failure (48). Moving reads into requests with a text boundary would save about 70 lines per read crate (fetch, thread, refs, users), and the crates would lose the typed failures they branch on (thread's CursorHalted via halted_in, users' NoSuchAccount). None of these crates is near the gate (users 842, refs 873, thread 727, fetch 718). Dropped to P3; revisit only if one nears 900.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_4 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 4u32, "2026-10-27T15:10Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:09 x: groomed to Paused, so the light view lists only pullable work. Resume condition (from this item's own analysis): a touched crate's regen nears 900 generated lines, or a change has to grow it. Checked against every regen until then.")
